window.JOURNAL_INDEX = [{"id": "paper-2611-14038", "arxiv": "2611.14038", "title": "Ultra-high-energy cosmic rays require metal-rich sources", "authors": "E. Podlesnyi, F. Oikonomou, D. Ehlert and M. Unger", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Ultra-high-energy cosmic rays", "Cosmic-ray composition", "Pierre Auger Observatory"], "label": "New paper · model-dependent source-composition inference", "teaser": "Fixed-rigidity comparisons to Galactic cosmic rays leave a robust silicon-group excess in fits to Auger spectrum and shower-depth data, but the phenomenological fit quality and propagation systematics limit the inference.", "path": "2026/11/11/index.html#paper-2611-14038", "reportDate": "11 November 2026", "searchText": "Ultra-high-energy cosmic rays require metal-rich sources E. Podlesnyi, F. Oikonomou, D. Ehlert and M. Unger 2611.14038 Fixed-rigidity comparisons to Galactic cosmic rays leave a robust silicon-group excess in fits to Auger spectrum and shower-depth data, but the phenomenological fit quality and propagation systematics limit the inference. 2026-11-11 11 November 2026 Ultra-high-energy cosmic rays Cosmic-ray composition Pierre Auger Observatory #### Fixed-rigidity abundances point to metal-rich UHECR sources\n\n**Paper:** *Ultra-high-energy cosmic rays require metal-rich sources* — **E. Podlesnyi, F. Oikonomou, D. Ehlert and M. Unger**. [arXiv:2611.14038v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14038v1), [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2611.14038v1). Submitted **10 November 2026 at 13:05:49 UTC** and first listed **11 November**; primary category **astro-ph.HE**. No journal publication is listed. **Classification: model-dependent source-composition inference, not a direct elemental-abundance measurement.**\n\n**Physical question.** Air-shower data indicate that the ultra-high-energy cosmic-ray mixture becomes heavier above the ankle, but a source abundance must be compared with a candidate seed population at the same magnetic rigidity \\(R=E/Z\\), because acceleration and confinement depend on rigidity. For a source spectrum \\(dN_i/dE\\propto\\varepsilon_i E^{-\\gamma}\\), the rigidity-space fraction is\n\n\\[\n\\rho_i=\\frac{\\varepsilon_i Z_i^{1-\\gamma}}{\\sum_j\\varepsilon_j Z_j^{1-\\gamma}}.\n\\]\n\nThis conversion matters enormously: changing the spectral index from a Galactic-like \\(\\gamma\\simeq2.6\\) to the very hard UHECR value \\(\\gamma\\simeq0.8\\) can move the hydrogen-to-iron ratio at fixed energy by roughly \\(26^{1.8}\\simeq350\\), even when the fixed-rigidity fractions are unchanged.\n\n**Method and data.** The authors fit the Pierre Auger Observatory all-particle spectrum, mean shower maximum \\(\\langle X_{\\max}\\rangle\\) and shower-to-shower dispersion \\(\\sigma\\(X_{\\max}\\)\\) above \\(10^{18.7}\\) eV, together with the inferred hydrogen spectrum between \\(10^{17.8}\\) and \\(10^{18.7}\\) eV. Sources inject five groups—H, He, CNO, Si and Fe—with a common power law and a super-exponential rigidity cutoff \\(E_{i,\\rm cut}=Z_iR_{\\rm cut}\\). CRPropa3 propagates the nuclei through photon backgrounds; an MCMC profiles the spectral slope, cutoff, cutoff shape and composition.\n\nThe main fit uses EPOS-LHC-R shower modeling and star-formation-rate-like source evolution, with alternatives spanning \\((1+z)^m\\), \\(m=-4\\) to 5, and Sibyll 2.3e. The Galactic comparison uses \\(\\gamma_{\\rm GCR}=2.6\\) and a deliberately broad ±30% abundance band.\n\n**Results.** For the nominal star-formation-history fit, the posterior modes are\n\n\\[\n\\gamma=0.81^{+0.06}_{-0.05},\\qquad\nR_{\\rm cut}=8.1^{+0.3}_{-0.5}\\times10^{18}\\ {\\rm V},\\qquad\n\\lambda=2.9^{+0.3}_{-0.2},\n\\]\n\nwith fixed-rigidity fractions \\(\\rho_{\\rm H}=0.38^{+0.05}_{-0.08}\\), \\(\\rho_{\\rm CNO}=0.41^{+0.07}_{-0.05}\\), \\(\\rho_{\\rm Si}=0.160\\pm0.018\\) and \\(\\rho_{\\rm Fe}=0.0041^{+0.0027}_{-0.0024}\\). Helium is negligible at the posterior mode. The authors define metallicity as \\(Z_\\rho=\\rho_{\\rm CNO}+\\rho_{\\rm Si}+\\rho_{\\rm Fe}\\); every source-evolution scenario examined requires at least about three times the Galactic value, with a factor near four for star-formation-rate evolution and more than an order of magnitude for strongly positive evolution.\n\nTwo fit classes survive. In the nominal EPOS solution, secondary protons from photodisintegration dominate the hydrogen flux at Earth, while CNO and silicon source fractions exceed the Galactic fixed-rigidity values by about factors 30 and 50. If the Auger \\(X_{\\max}\\) scale is shifted downward by one quoted systematic standard deviation, a primary-hydrogen-rich branch appears: hydrogen and CNO can then resemble the Galactic mixture, but silicon remains about 50 times larger and iron about three times larger. The silicon excess remains above an order of magnitude across the EPOS/Sibyll and \\(X_{\\max}\\) variations studied.\n\n**Limitations.** The nominal fit has deviance per degree of freedom about **3.34**; the shifted-\\(X_{\\max}\\) solution gives about **2.69**. These are phenomenological fits, not statistically pristine descriptions. Results depend on hadronic-interaction models, the absolute \\(X_{\\max}\\) scale, extragalactic background light, assumed homogeneous identical sources, common source evolution, five broad mass groups and one-dimensional propagation. Negative source evolution fits poorly because it produces too few secondary protons. The analysis identifies a robust heavy-seed tension inside this model family, but it does not identify the accelerator or explain how the metal-rich material is supplied.\n\n##### History\n\nThe Pierre Auger Collaboration's [2017 combined fit](https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.07155) already established the modern baseline: above \\(5\\times10^{18}\\) eV, a simple homogeneous-source model prefers a hard injection spectrum, low maximum rigidity and a mixed, relatively heavy composition. Later [two-population fits extending toward \\(10^{17}\\) eV](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.10806) retained a hard, heavy high-energy component while adding a softer, lighter low-energy population. Magnetic-horizon models can soften the inferred injection spectrum, but still require an additional low-energy component under their field and source-density assumptions.\n\nThe new step is the fixed-rigidity comparison with measured Galactic seed abundances and the inclusion of the sub-ankle hydrogen spectrum to separate primary from secondary protons. It strengthens the composition problem into a chemical one: ordinary Galactic cosmic-ray material does not reproduce the inferred silicon-group fraction. It neither proves that every UHECR source is metal-rich nor excludes structured environments, multiple source classes, magnetic horizons or altered propagation. The main unresolved uncertainty remains whether shower-model and source-population systematics can mimic the apparent heavy-seed excess."}, {"id": "paper-2611-14028", "arxiv": "2611.14028", "title": "Constraints on Lorentz Invariance Violation from PeV Gamma Rays of Cygnus X-3 Observed by LHAASO", "authors": "LHAASO Collaboration", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Lorentz invariance", "PeV gamma rays", "LHAASO", "Cygnus X-3"], "label": "New paper · photon-stability constraints", "teaser": "A 3.735 PeV photon candidate and a 3.05 PeV lower limit on the spectral cutoff constrain superluminal photon decay and splitting, with the intrinsic source spectrum dominating the systematic uncertainty.", "path": "2026/11/11/index.html#paper-2611-14028", "reportDate": "11 November 2026", "searchText": "Constraints on Lorentz Invariance Violation from PeV Gamma Rays of Cygnus X-3 Observed by LHAASO LHAASO Collaboration 2611.14028 A 3.735 PeV photon candidate and a 3.05 PeV lower limit on the spectral cutoff constrain superluminal photon decay and splitting, with the intrinsic source spectrum dominating the systematic uncertainty. 2026-11-11 11 November 2026 Lorentz invariance PeV gamma rays LHAASO Cygnus X-3 #### A 3.7 PeV photon tests superluminal photon decay and splitting\n\n**Paper:** *Constraints on Lorentz Invariance Violation from PeV Gamma Rays of Cygnus X-3 Observed by LHAASO* — **LHAASO Collaboration**. [arXiv:2611.14028v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14028v1), [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2611.14028v1). Submitted **10 November 2026 at 12:58:14 UTC** and first listed **11 November**; primary category **astro-ph.HE**, cross-listed in hep-ph. No journal publication is listed. **Classification: photon-stability constraints; no Lorentz-invariance violation is detected.**\n\n**Physical question.** A superluminal photon term in a modified dispersion relation can make normally forbidden processes kinematically possible. Linear or quadratic corrections can permit \\(\\gamma\\rightarrow e^+e^-\\); a quadratic term can also permit photon splitting. Detecting photons above the corresponding threshold constrains the Lorentz-violation energy scale \\(E_{\\rm LIV}^{(n)}\\). These are photon-survival tests and should not be equated with arrival-time tests, which constrain energy-dependent propagation delays under different assumptions.\n\n**Data and method.** LHAASO analyzes **692.8 days** of KM2A exposure across four Cygnus X-3 flare windows from **26 December 2019 to 31 July 2024**, adopting a distance of **8.95 kpc**. KM2A's stated energy resolution improves from about 17% at 0.1 PeV to 10% at 1 PeV, while hadron rejection is about \\(10^4\\) at 100 TeV. A binned three-dimensional spatial–spectral likelihood and an unbinned cross-check fit the Cygnus region. The baseline intrinsic spectrum is a physically motivated \\(p\\gamma_X+p\\gamma_{\\rm UV}\\) model; a power law probes spectral-model systematics. Low-count coverage is calibrated with Monte Carlo \\(CL_s\\), rather than a naive asymptotic cutoff test.\n\n**Results.** The highest reconstructed photon candidate has\n\n\\[\nE_\\gamma=3.735\\pm0.411\\ {\\rm PeV}.\n\\]\n\nThe spectral fit places a **95% confidence lower limit** on an exponential cutoff of\n\n\\[\nE_{\\rm cut}>3.05^{+0.29}_{-0.29}\\ {\\rm PeV},\n\\]\n\nwith the unbinned analysis giving 3.06 PeV. Interpreted in the paper's superluminal photon-sector effective model, the corresponding limits are\n\n\\[\nE_{\\rm LIV}^{(1)}>2.72^{+0.85}_{-0.70}\\times10^{34}\\ {\\rm eV},\n\\]\n\n\\[\nE_{\\rm LIV}^{(2)}>9.09^{+1.81}_{-1.65}\\times10^{24}\\ {\\rm eV}\n\\]\n\nfor photon decay, and\n\n\\[\nE_{\\rm LIV,split}^{(2)}>1.73^{+0.33}_{-0.30}\\times10^{26}\\ {\\rm eV}\n\\]\n\nfor photon splitting. The latter is roughly \\(10^{-2}M_{\\rm Pl}\\). The quoted total systematic uncertainty is about **9.6%**, dominated by the intrinsic-spectrum choice at 9.5%; atmosphere contributes about 1.5%, and high-energy background is negligible in the fitted PeV tail.\n\n**Limitations.** The bounds apply to the particular superluminal photon dispersion and effective photon-splitting framework used. They assume that the intrinsic Cygnus X-3 spectrum has no astrophysical cutoff below the inferred limit and that the spatial/spectral source model correctly separates the crowded Cygnus emission. Energy scale and sparse-event statistics matter directly. A surviving PeV photon does not constrain every Lorentz-violating operator, and it is not evidence for a quantum-gravity scale.\n\n##### History\n\nLHAASO's first PeV-source analysis, published as [*Physical Review Letters* 128, 051102](https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.12350), used photons up to 1.4 PeV and reported photon-stability limits around \\(E_{\\rm LIV}^{(2)}>10^{-3}M_{\\rm Pl}\\), alongside a linear scale above about \\(10^5M_{\\rm Pl}\\). The present Cygnus X-3 data push the quadratic photon-decay/splitting reach toward \\(10^{-2}M_{\\rm Pl}\\), close to an order of magnitude within the same class of photon-stability tests.\n\nTime-of-flight analyses are complementary. LHAASO's [GRB 221009A timing study](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.133.071501) constrained linear and quadratic dispersion from energy-dependent delays, but those values cannot be divided into the present limits to claim a universal improvement: source distance, observables, operator sign and threshold physics differ. The remaining uncertainty is whether intrinsic PeV spectral structure in Cygnus X-3 can be separated cleanly enough from the propagation effect the stability test is designed to exclude."}, {"id": "paper-2611-13090", "arxiv": "2611.13090", "title": "First constraints on axion-like particles with SPHEREx data", "authors": "Connor Hainje, Elena Pinetti and David W. Hogg", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Axion-like particles", "SPHEREx", "Dark matter", "Infrared line searches"], "label": "New paper · power-constrained upper limits", "teaser": "The first SPHEREx data search places competitive 95% limits on decaying eV-mass dark matter, reaching 2.10×10⁻¹¹ GeV⁻¹, while foreground structure leaves large room for methodological improvement.", "path": "2026/11/11/index.html#paper-2611-13090", "reportDate": "11 November 2026", "searchText": "First constraints on axion-like particles with SPHEREx data Connor Hainje, Elena Pinetti and David W. Hogg 2611.13090 The first SPHEREx data search places competitive 95% limits on decaying eV-mass dark matter, reaching 2.10×10⁻¹¹ GeV⁻¹, while foreground structure leaves large room for methodological improvement. 2026-11-11 11 November 2026 Axion-like particles SPHEREx Dark matter Infrared line searches #### SPHEREx places its first real-data limits on decaying eV-mass dark matter\n\n**Paper:** *First constraints on axion-like particles with SPHEREx data* — **Connor Hainje, Elena Pinetti and David W. Hogg**. [arXiv:2611.13090v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13090v1), [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2611.13090v1). Submitted **9 November 2026 at 19:00:02 UTC** and first listed **11 November**; primary category **hep-ph**, cross-listed in astro-ph.CO. No journal publication is listed. **Classification: 95% power-constrained upper limits under the assumption that all dark matter is the decaying axion-like particle; no unknown decay line is detected.**\n\n**Physical question.** An axion-like particle of mass (m_a) coupled to two photons decays at rate\n\n\\[\n\\Gamma_{a\\to\\gamma\\gamma}=\\frac{g_{a\\gamma\\gamma}^2m_a^3}{64\\pi},\n\\]\n\nproducing a narrow line at photon energy (m_a/2). SPHEREx covers 0.744–5.002 μm at resolving power (R=35)–128, corresponding to (0.496<m_a<3.32) eV. The analysis asks whether the Milky Way dark-matter column produces such a line in the mission's first deep-field data.\n\n**Data and method.** The study uses the first **16.435 months** of Quick Release data over **213 deg²** around the North and South Deep Fields. A 6.3-trillion-row pixel table is filtered to **\\(4.70\\times10^{11}\\)** valid, source-masked measurements; the known-source flag removes about half the measurements. The signal assumes an NFW Milky Way halo with \\(r_s=14.46\\) kpc and \\(\\rho_s=0.566\\ {\\rm GeV\\,cm^{-3}}\\), an isotropic Maxwellian velocity width of 160 km s⁻¹, and that ALPs constitute all dark matter.\n\nFor each sky-pixel/detector pair, an independent fourth-order polynomial models the continuum inside a window of ±5 instrumental resolution elements. The analysis profiles those nuisance coefficients analytically at **408 trial masses**. One-sided 95% limits use \\(\\Delta\\chi^2=2.71\\), a power-constraint threshold of 0.1587 to prevent over-exclusion after downward fluctuations, and three data-driven error inflations that account for local scatter and coherent residual structure.\n\n**Results.** The strongest coupling limit is\n\n\\[\ng_{a\\gamma\\gamma}<2.10\\times10^{-11}\\ {\\rm GeV^{-1}}\n\\]\n\nat \\(m_a=0.606\\) eV; the median over the scanned range is \\(4.16\\times10^{-11}\\ {\\rm GeV^{-1}}\\). Recast without the axion coupling relation, the median lower limit on the two-photon dark-matter lifetime is **\\(\\tau>6.5\\times10^{25}\\ {\\rm s}\\)**. These are 95% power-constrained limits for the adopted halo and full-dark-matter fraction.\n\nFourteen wavelength regions have nominal two-sided (z>1.96), but every excursion above about 4σ aligns with known atmospheric He I, PAH 3.3 μm, Paα, Brα or He-I leakage. Sixteen regions fluctuate below −1.96, consistent with substantial structured residuals and look-elsewhere expectations. The authors therefore report no unidentified line. Scaling exposure from 16.435 to 25 months improves the coupling forecast by only about 10%, whereas the median gap to the Cramér–Rao bound is a factor **39**, pointing to foreground and calibration modeling as the main opportunity.\n\n**Limitations.** Coupling limits scale with the assumed dark-matter fraction and halo column; a subdominant ALP component weakens them. The independent local continua are conservative but discard spatial coherence. Detector boundaries and the bright 1.083 μm atmospheric He I line create sensitivity holes, and the current quick-release calibration lacks some planned foreground templates. Different published line searches use different halos and confidence constructions, so point-by-point comparisons are safer than a single improvement factor.\n\n##### History\n\nThe [2025 SPHEREx forecast](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.12286) by Regis, Taoso and Terol Calvo predicted strong eV-mass sensitivity from Milky Way, dwarf-galaxy and Magellanic-Cloud sightlines. The new work is the first search using actual SPHEREx measurements. It also corrects a channel-width mismatch in the journal-version forecast: after discussion, the forecast's arXiv v3 and the data analysis agree.\n\nEarlier real-data baselines include optical MUSE-Faint dwarf-galaxy searches, the [WINERED first result](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.134.051004) and several JWST infrared analyses, including [Janish and Pinetti](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.134.071002). Globular-cluster energy-loss bounds constrain the same photon coupling but do not assume the particle is dark matter; conversely, decay-line limits depend directly on its abundance and halo distribution. SPHEREx is competitive across parts of 0.5–3.3 eV, not uniformly the strongest at every mass. The unresolved issue is whether all-sky morphology and improved calibration can recover the order-of-magnitude statistical potential without foreground mismodeling."}, {"id": "paper-2611-13629", "arxiv": "2611.13629", "title": "Where does the neutrino Mass Ordering preference come from?", "authors": "Pedro A. N. Machado and João Paulo Pinheiro", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Neutrino mass ordering", "JUNO", "Daya Bay", "T2K", "NOvA", "IceCube"], "label": "New paper · diagnostic global-data reanalysis", "teaser": "A disappearance-only combination disfavors inverted ordering at 1.9σ by CLs, but exposes opposing T2K and NOvA pulls and a reactor–accelerator comparison ultimately limited by neutrino energy reconstruction.", "path": "2026/11/11/index.html#paper-2611-13629", "reportDate": "11 November 2026", "searchText": "Where does the neutrino Mass Ordering preference come from? Pedro A. N. Machado and João Paulo Pinheiro 2611.13629 A disappearance-only combination disfavors inverted ordering at 1.9σ by CLs, but exposes opposing T2K and NOvA pulls and a reactor–accelerator comparison ultimately limited by neutrino energy reconstruction. 2026-11-11 11 November 2026 Neutrino mass ordering JUNO Daya Bay T2K NOvA IceCube #### The present neutrino-ordering hint is a balance of small, partly opposing pulls\n\n**Paper:** *Where does the neutrino Mass Ordering preference come from?* — **Pedro A. N. Machado and João Paulo Pinheiro**. [arXiv:2611.13629v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13629v1), [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2611.13629v1). Submitted **10 November 2026 at 06:16:18 UTC** and first listed **11 November**; primary category **hep-ph**. No journal publication is listed. **Classification: diagnostic reanalysis of published likelihood information, not a new oscillation measurement.**\n\n**Physical question.** Reactor disappearance measures \\(\\Delta m^2_{ee}\\), while muon-neutrino disappearance measures \\(\\Delta m^2_{\\mu\\mu}\\). Their relation to the fundamental atmospheric splitting depends weakly on whether \\(m_3\\) lies above or below the solar pair. The paper asks which data sets actually generate the current normal-ordering preference after JUNO's first 207 days, while deliberately removing standalone ordering information from accelerator appearance and matter effects.\n\n**Data and construction.** The fit combines JUNO with Daya Bay and one-dimensional \\(\\Delta\\chi^2(|\\Delta m^2_{31}|)\\) profiles from T2K, NOvA, MINOS and 9.3 years of IceCube/DeepCore data as compiled in NuFIT 6.1. The accelerator and atmospheric profiles have \\(\\theta_{23}\\) and \\(\\delta_{CP}\\) marginalized. Super-Kamiokande is not included. For every ordering, the external curves are re-zeroed at their own minima, removing their direct ordering preference and isolating consistency of the measured splittings. JUNO confidence behavior is treated with Feldman–Cousins and pseudo-experiments because its likelihood has multiple local minima.\n\n**Results.** JUNO alone contributes \\(\\Delta\\chi^2=1.84\\) toward normal ordering, but its preferred splitting is in tension with Daya Bay; after combining the two, the ordering statistic falls to **0.21**, effectively no reactor-only preference. Adding accelerator and IceCube disappearance yields\n\n\\[\nT_{\\rm MO}=5.91.\n\\]\n\nFrom 12,000 toys per ordering, the inverted-ordering tail probability is \\(p_{\\rm IO}=0.013\\), or **2.2σ one-sided**. Because the observed statistic is not unusually central under every normal-ordering toy, the \\(CL_s\\) value is 0.027, or **1.9σ**. T2K and NOvA prefer atmospheric splittings differing by about **3.5%**, comparable to the ordering-dependent offset; reactor+T2K and reactor+NOvA therefore pull toward opposite orderings.\n\nThe same non-Gaussianity weakens apparent cross-experiment tensions. For the full combination, naive \\(\\sqrt{T}\\) gives 2.4σ tension under normal ordering and 3.1σ under inverted ordering, whereas pseudo-experiments give only **1.0σ** and **1.8σ**. In an Asimov projection with 1,500 additional JUNO days but today's external data, inverted-ordering rejection reaches only about **2.8σ**; even perfect reactor precision gives about 2.9σ. The limiting error then moves to accelerator disappearance and percent-level neutrino-energy reconstruction.\n\n**Limitations.** This combination intentionally discards appearance and matter-effect ordering information, so it should not replace a full global fit. It inherits NuFIT's one-dimensional profiles and their nuisance marginalization, approximates some external fluctuations by shifted profiles, and omits Super-Kamiokande. The projection freezes non-JUNO data rather than forecasting DUNE, Hyper-Kamiokande or updated cross-section constraints.\n\n##### History\n\nThe mass ordering was first probed indirectly through atmospheric and long-baseline matter effects; the recent change is JUNO's precise reactor spectrum. JUNO's [first oscillation measurement](https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.14593), published in *Nature* in 2026, made the \\(\\Delta m^2_{ee}\\) comparison competitive after only 207 days. T2K, NOvA and IceCube/DeepCore already measured \\(\\nu_\\mu\\) disappearance precisely, while their full analyses also contain ordering-sensitive appearance or Earth-matter information.\n\nThe new paper explains rather than strengthens that experimental record. Its 1.9σ \\(CL_s\\) preference is weaker and conceptually narrower than some full global-ordering summaries because direct appearance and matter-effect evidence is removed by construction. The key historical advance is diagnostic: a few-percent difference between T2K and NOvA central values can reverse the disappearance-only ordering pull. The outstanding uncertainty is experimental control of reconstructed neutrino energy, including neutrino–nucleus modeling, alongside statistically independent combination of future reactor, accelerator and atmospheric likelihoods."}, {"id": "paper-2611-09037", "arxiv": "2611.09037", "title": "Probing flavor-dependent neutrino-dark matter interactions with the flavor composition of high-energy cosmic neutrinos", "authors": "Ki-Young Choi and Ankhbayar Tugssaikhan", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Neutrino-dark matter interactions", "Astrophysical neutrinos", "TXS 0506+056"], "label": "New paper · conditional phenomenological constraints", "teaser": "Matter-induced flavor conversion in a benchmark TXS 0506+056 dark-matter spike can either tighten or relax neutrino-dark-matter attenuation limits, producing a two-state survival plateau at strong potential.", "path": "2026/11/09/index.html#paper-2611-09037", "reportDate": "9 November 2026", "searchText": "Probing flavor-dependent neutrino-dark matter interactions with the flavor composition of high-energy cosmic neutrinos Ki-Young Choi and Ankhbayar Tugssaikhan 2611.09037 Matter-induced flavor conversion in a benchmark TXS 0506+056 dark-matter spike can either tighten or relax neutrino-dark-matter attenuation limits, producing a two-state survival plateau at strong potential. 2026-11-09 9 November 2026 Neutrino-dark matter interactions Astrophysical neutrinos TXS 0506+056 #### A dark-matter potential can reshape both neutrino flavor and attenuation limits\n\n**Paper:** *Probing flavor-dependent neutrino-dark matter interactions with the flavor composition of high-energy cosmic neutrinos* — **Ki-Young Choi and Ankhbayar Tugssaikhan**. [arXiv:2611.09037v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09037v1), [full text](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.09037v1). Submitted **6 November 2026 at 06:12:47 UTC** and first listed **9 November**; primary category **hep-ph**. No journal publication is listed. **Classification: model-dependent phenomenological constraint; no interaction or source association is detected.**\n\n**Physical question.** High-energy neutrinos crossing a dense dark-matter spike can scatter and lose flux. If the forward-scattering amplitude is flavor dependent, the same medium also changes the neutrino mixing eigenstates. The paper asks how those two effects combine in an observable flavor triangle, using TXS 0506+056 as a benchmark rather than claiming that every IceCube neutrino originates there.\n\nThe interaction is written as a diagonal matter potential\n\n\\[\nV_\\alpha(r)=A_\\alpha\\,\\rho_\\chi(r),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(A_\\alpha\\) has units of cm³ and only one flavor is coupled at a time. The attenuation is parameterized by the cross section per dark-matter mass, \\(\\sigma_{\\nu\\chi}/m_\\chi\\), in cm² GeV⁻¹. These are distinct quantities: \\(A_\\alpha\\) controls coherent flavor evolution, while \\(\\sigma_{\\nu\\chi}\\) controls incoherent removal from the beam.\n\n**Benchmark and method.** The source is placed at **0.023 pc** from a \\(3\\times10^8M_\\odot\\) black hole. The adopted spike has radius **1.28 pc**, density \\(6.4\\times10^{11}\\ {\\rm GeV\\,cm^{-3}}\\) at the emission point and integrated column \\(X_\\chi=3.6\\times10^{28}\\ {\\rm GeV\\,cm^{-2}}\\). The Milky Way column, about \\(10^{22}\\ {\\rm GeV\\,cm^{-2}}\\) in the same calculation, is negligible for attenuation. The propagation is solved adiabatically at a fixed neutrino energy of **100 TeV**.\n\nThe standard \\(p\\gamma\\) source composition is split into neutrinos \\((1/3,1/3,0)\\) and antineutrinos \\((0,1/3,0)\\), giving total \\((1:2:0)\\); a muon-damped \\((0:1:0)\\) source is also tested. The Earthly fractions are compared with IceCube's broad flavor contours around the approximate best fit \\((0.30,0.37,0.33)\\). The authors require that the local Galactic density, taken as **0.4 GeV cm⁻³**, not appreciably alter the measured PMNS mixing at 100 TeV, giving a working upper scale \\(A_\\alpha\\lesssim10^{-30}\\ {\\rm cm^3}\\).\n\n**Results.** Matter resonances occur near\n\n\\[\nA_\\alpha\\sim10^{-38}\\left(\\frac{100\\ {\\rm TeV}}{E_\\nu}\\right){\\rm cm^3}.\n\\]\n\nAt smaller potentials, propagation is essentially vacuum-like and the flux/flavor constraints reproduce attenuation bounds around \\(\\sigma_{\\nu\\chi}/m_\\chi\\lesssim10^{-29}\\)–\\(10^{-28}\\ {\\rm cm^2\\,GeV^{-1}}\\), depending on the coupled flavor, potential sign and source composition. These are conditional limits for the adopted spike and source normalization.\n\nAt \\(A_\\alpha\\gg10^{-36}\\ {\\rm cm^3}\\), the interacting flavor becomes nearly isolated in one matter eigenstate. Scattering can then remove that eigenstate while two others survive, producing a **survival plateau** instead of exponential extinction of the whole flux. Under the paper's 90%-attenuation criterion, the allowed cross section per mass can consequently weaken by as much as four orders of magnitude, reaching about **\\(1.4\\times10^{-25}\\) to \\(5.6\\times10^{-25}\\ {\\rm cm^2\\,GeV^{-1}}\\)** at \\(A_\\alpha=10^{-30}\\ {\\rm cm^3}\\). This is not a universal loosening of the bound: the result changes with flavor, sign and production channel, and resonant trajectories can move outside the IceCube flavor region before strong attenuation occurs.\n\nFor a muon-damped source with \\(V_\\mu\\), the unattenuated flavor ratio is already outside the adopted IceCube contour once \\(A_\\mu\\gtrsim9.52\\times10^{-39}\\ {\\rm cm^3}\\). In part of that parameter space, attenuation can move the ratio back into the contour, so the plotted curve becomes a **minimum** scattering strength rather than an upper limit. By then the total flux has lost more than 90%, underscoring that flavor agreement alone is insufficient.\n\n**Model mapping.** For a benchmark vector mediator the paper uses the approximate relation \\(\\sigma_{\\nu\\chi}/m_\\chi\\sim(g_\\nu g_\\chi/4\\pi)A_\\alpha\\), illustrating coupling products \\(10^{-6}\\), \\(10^{-9}\\) and \\(10^{-12}\\). These curves are benchmark mappings, not confidence intervals on the couplings.\n\n**Limitations.** The calculation uses one energy, one source, a fixed spike profile, an adiabatic approximation and one nonzero flavor coupling. It removes scattered neutrinos without treating energy redistribution or regeneration. It also does not fit a source luminosity and spectral model jointly with the IceCube data. The flavor contours are still broad. A weaker attenuation bound in the survival plateau therefore reflects the assumed coherent propagation model, not evidence that large interactions are present.\n\n##### History\n\nThe baseline idea predates this flavor calculation. **Ferrer, Herrera and Ibarra** used the same broad strategy—high-energy neutrinos escaping the dark-matter spike around TXS 0506+056—to constrain scattering from flux survival ([arXiv:2209.06339](https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.06339), *JCAP* 05 (2023) 057). **Cline et al.** independently derived blazar constraints and emphasized their dependence on the spike and energy scaling ([arXiv:2209.02713](https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.02713)). Those works largely treated attenuation without solving a flavor-dependent matter Hamiltonian.\n\nFlavor evolution in a dark background has also been used to constrain nonstandard neutrino potentials through oscillation data and astrophysical flavor ratios; for example, **Capozzi, Shoemaker and Vecchi** showed how dark backgrounds can perturb the effective Hamiltonian ([arXiv:1804.05117](https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.05117), *JCAP* 07 (2018) 004). The new paper's substantive addition is to evolve the flavor eigenstates through a compact spike and apply attenuation in that matter basis, revealing the two-state plateau and sign-dependent resonances.\n\nIts bounds should not be described as uniformly stronger than cosmological limits. CMB and large-scale-structure constraints integrate interactions in the early Universe and depend on temperature or energy scaling; the blazar constraint applies at 100 TeV and assumes a large local column. The strongest comparison is therefore internal: at fixed TXS profile, flavor-dependent propagation can move a nominal \\(10^{-28}\\ {\\rm cm^2\\,GeV^{-1}}\\) attenuation scale by orders of magnitude. The remaining uncertainty is dominated by the dark-matter spike, the intrinsic neutrino spectrum and composition, and whether the TXS association represents the diffuse IceCube population."}, {"id": "paper-2611-09106", "arxiv": "2611.09106", "title": "Probing Dark Matter via the Migdal Effect in LUX-ZEPLIN Data", "authors": "Yang Yu and Lei Feng", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Migdal effect", "LUX-ZEPLIN", "Sub-GeV dark matter"], "label": "New paper · Bayesian sensitivity forecast", "teaser": "An Asimov reinterpretation of LZ's exposure projects Migdal sensitivity between 350 MeV and 10 GeV, but it is not an official LZ limit or an analysis of the observed event counts.", "path": "2026/11/09/index.html#paper-2611-09106", "reportDate": "9 November 2026", "searchText": "Probing Dark Matter via the Migdal Effect in LUX-ZEPLIN Data Yang Yu and Lei Feng 2611.09106 An Asimov reinterpretation of LZ's exposure projects Migdal sensitivity between 350 MeV and 10 GeV, but it is not an official LZ limit or an analysis of the observed event counts. 2026-11-09 9 November 2026 Migdal effect LUX-ZEPLIN Sub-GeV dark matter #### A projected LZ Migdal reach extends below a GeV, but it is not an observed-data limit\n\n**Paper:** *Probing Dark Matter via the Migdal Effect in LUX-ZEPLIN Data* — **Yang Yu and Lei Feng**. [arXiv:2611.09106v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09106v1), [full text](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.09106v1). Submitted **6 November 2026 at 07:38:27 UTC** and first listed **9 November**; primary category **astro-ph.HE**. No journal publication is listed. **Classification: Bayesian Asimov sensitivity forecast based on a published LZ exposure, not an official LZ exclusion and not a fit to observed LZ counts.**\n\n**Question and signal.** A sub-GeV dark-matter particle transfers too little recoil energy to xenon for a conventional nuclear-recoil search. In a Migdal event, the sudden nuclear acceleration ionizes or excites the atom, supplying a detectable electronic energy. The paper asks how far LZ's **4.2 tonne-year** exposure could reach in spin-independent dark-matter–nucleon cross section if analyzed with Migdal templates.\n\n**Method.** The calculation uses a Standard Halo Model with \\(v_0=220\\ {\\rm km\\,s^{-1}}\\), \\(v_{\\rm esc}=544\\ {\\rm km\\,s^{-1}}\\) and Earth speed \\(232\\ {\\rm km\\,s^{-1}}\\). Xenon ionization probabilities for the **1s through 5p shells** are taken from the standard atomic tables. The reconstructed electronic-equivalent window is **1–4 keVee**, and templates are generated at 20 logarithmically spaced masses from **100 MeV to 10 GeV**.\n\nCrucially, the authors do not use the unblinded LZ event histogram. They set each bin to the expected background, \\(n_i=\\mathcal{E}b_i\\), producing an Asimov data set. The likelihood includes a **20% Gaussian signal-normalization nuisance** and **30% independent background uncertainties per bin**, integrated numerically with 16th-order Gauss–Hermite quadrature. Markov-chain sampling uses a 4,000-step burn-in followed by 20,000 samples. The reported bound is the **90th percentile of the marginalized Bayesian posterior** under the paper's stated priors.\n\n**Results.** Below about **350 MeV**, the assumed halo cannot place an event in the 1-keVee window, so the analysis gives no finite constraint. From **350 MeV to 10 GeV**, the projected 90% upper limits span\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{\\chi N}^{\\rm SI}=6.5\\times10^{-37}\\;\\text{to}\\;9.6\\times10^{-42}\\ {\\rm cm^2}.\n\\]\n\nThe curve improves rapidly as more xenon shells and halo velocities become available, then approaches conventional recoil sensitivity toward the multi-GeV range. Halo variations matter most near threshold and shift the low-mass reach by tens of percent to factors of order unity in the authors' tests.\n\nThe paper also maps the cross-section sensitivity into a light-mediator combination\n\n\\[\n\\alpha_{\\rm med}=\\frac{g_Ng_\\chi}{4\\pi}\n\\]\n\nfor scalar and vector mediators, retaining the full momentum dependence. At the benchmark \\(m_\\chi=1\\ {\\rm GeV}\\), the projected light-mediator sensitivity reaches roughly **\\(10^{-12}\\)**. Comparisons to kaon invisible-decay, horizontal-branch cooling and SN 1987A regions are useful orientation, but they involve different mediator couplings, production assumptions and trapping regimes. The claimed one-to-two-order gain is therefore only meaningful within the matched simplified models.\n\n**Limitations.** This study forecasts median sensitivity from a background-only pseudo-data set. It neither accounts for the actual locations of LZ events nor reproduces the collaboration's detector response, event selection and correlated systematics. The per-bin background priors are a simplified stand-in for a full detector likelihood. Atomic Migdal probabilities and liquid-xenon response remain uncertain near threshold. A future collaboration analysis could be stronger or weaker, and the projected curve must not be cited as an LZ exclusion of dark matter.\n\n##### History\n\nThe Migdal effect has already extended direct searches below the ordinary nuclear-recoil threshold. **XENON1T** used scintillation-plus-ionization and ionization-only data to probe masses down to about **85 MeV** ([arXiv:1907.12771](https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.12771), *PRL* 123, 241803). **DarkSide-50**, using a **12,306 ± 184 kg-day** argon exposure, reached **40 MeV** and reported the strongest spin-independent bounds below 3.6 GeV within its adopted model ([arXiv:2207.11967](https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.11967), *PRL* 130, 101001). **PandaX-4T** published a low-mass ionization search in *PRL* 130, 261001 ([arXiv:2212.10067](https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.10067)). These are observed-data collaboration results and are methodologically stronger baselines than an outside Asimov forecast.\n\nThe experimental status of Migdal ionization is nuanced. A gaseous detector reported **six candidates among nearly one million events**, a **5σ** excess over background and a Migdal-to-nuclear-recoil cross-section ratio \\((4.9^{+2.6}_{-1.9})\\times10^{-5}\\) for recoils above 35 keVee with 5–10 keV electrons ([Nature 649, 580–583 (2026)](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09918-8)). In contrast, a tagged-neutron search in liquid xenon did not observe the predicted M- and L-shell signal ([arXiv:2307.12952](https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.12952), *PRD* 109, L051101). Thus, validation in one gas regime does not eliminate the liquid-phase response uncertainty relevant to LZ.\n\nThe 4.2 tonne-year LZ paper itself is a conventional WIMP search: it used 280 live days, observed no significant excess and set a 90% confidence spin-independent limit of **\\(2.1\\times10^{-48}\\ {\\rm cm^2}\\)** at 36 GeV ([arXiv:2410.17036](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.17036), *PRL* 135, 011802). The new forecast borrows its exposure, not its low-energy likelihood. Its contribution is a concrete target for an official sub-GeV analysis; the dominant remaining questions are detector response, atomic ionization modelling and background control below 4 keVee."}, {"id": "paper-2611-09788", "arxiv": "2611.09788", "title": "Possible Solution to the Gallium Anomaly? Nuclear Transition Densities", "authors": "D. Castillo, D. Frycz and J. Menéndez", "primary_category": "nucl-th", "tags": ["Gallium anomaly", "Nuclear transition densities", "Neutrinos"], "label": "New paper · nuclear-structure calculation", "teaser": "Nine shell-model calculations find that realistic transition densities leave factorized and nonfactorized gallium capture cross sections within one percent, rejecting a recent nuclear-profile solution to the anomaly.", "path": "2026/11/09/index.html#paper-2611-09788", "reportDate": "9 November 2026", "searchText": "Possible Solution to the Gallium Anomaly? Nuclear Transition Densities D. Castillo, D. Frycz and J. Menéndez 2611.09788 Nine shell-model calculations find that realistic transition densities leave factorized and nonfactorized gallium capture cross sections within one percent, rejecting a recent nuclear-profile solution to the anomaly. 2026-11-09 9 November 2026 Gallium anomaly Nuclear transition densities Neutrinos #### Realistic nuclear transition densities do not remove the gallium anomaly\n\n**Paper:** *Possible Solution to the Gallium Anomaly? Nuclear Transition Densities* — **D. Castillo, D. Frycz and J. Menéndez**. [arXiv:2611.09788v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09788v1), [full text](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.09788v1). Submitted **6 November 2026 at 17:45:30 UTC** and first listed **9 November**; primary category **nucl-th**. No journal publication is listed. **Classification: nuclear shell-model calculation testing a recently proposed Standard Model resolution.**\n\n**Question.** Calibration runs in GALLEX, SAGE and BEST observe about 20% fewer \\(^{71}{\\rm Ga}(\\nu_e,e^-)^{71}{\\rm Ge}\\) captures than the standard cross section predicts. A September 2026 *Physical Review Letters* paper proposed that the usual factorization of lepton and nuclear wavefunctions fails: specially fitted Gamow–Teller radial transition densities could reproduce the precisely known \\(^{71}{\\rm Ge}\\) electron-capture half-life while lowering the inverse reaction's cross section. Castillo and collaborators test whether such densities arise in realistic many-body nuclear calculations.\n\n**Method.** The authors solve continuum and bound electron wavefunctions with a Dirac–Hartree–Fock–Slater treatment and keep the lepton functions inside the nuclear radial integral. They calculate nine shell-model Gamow–Teller transition densities using five Hamiltonians, bare and effective operators, and both (pf) and extended (pf+0g_{9/2}) spaces. Every calculation is normalized to the weighted electron-capture half-life\n\n\\[\nt_{1/2}(^{71}{\\rm Ge})=11.465\\pm0.003\\ {\\rm d}.\n\\]\n\nThis normalization absorbs leading quenching and small weak/radiative corrections. The shell-model densities are then folded with the electron wavefunctions for \\(^{51}\\)Cr and \\(^{37}\\)Ar source neutrinos, once using factorization and once without it.\n\n**Why factorization works here.** Across the nuclear radius, the relevant electron Fermi function changes by only about **3%**. The nine shell-model density profiles are also remarkably similar despite the different Hamiltonians, spaces and operators. The calculation therefore finds no large radial lever arm with which the bound and continuum electron integrals could differ.\n\n**Quantitative result.** For ground-state capture, the experimental weighted values used are\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(^{51}{\\rm Cr})=(4.44\\pm0.33)\\times10^{-45}\\ {\\rm cm^2},\\qquad\n\\sigma(^{37}{\\rm Ar})=(5.21\\pm0.72)\\times10^{-45}\\ {\\rm cm^2}.\n\\]\n\nThe shell model gives, beyond factorization,\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(^{51}{\\rm Cr})=(5.26\\pm0.01)\\times10^{-45}\\ {\\rm cm^2},\\qquad\n\\sigma(^{37}{\\rm Ar})=(6.30\\pm0.01)\\times10^{-45}\\ {\\rm cm^2}.\n\\]\n\nThe corresponding factorized values are \\(5.21\\times10^{-45}\\) and \\(6.24\\times10^{-45}\\ {\\rm cm^2}\\): agreement at about **1%**. Experiment-to-theory ratios remain near **0.83**, so the roughly 20% deficit survives.\n\nThe earlier fitted modified-double- and modified-triple-Gaussian profiles behave very differently. Their factorized capture cross sections exceed their nonfactorized values by factors of about **4** and **8**, and the factorized electron-capture half-lives collapse to **3.505 d** and **1.687 d**. Their positive and negative radial lobes cancel so that the net matrix element is only **2.5%** and **0.25%** of the integral of the absolute density. When the new authors fit a combination of shell-model-like single-particle radial functions freely, a solution requires coefficients roughly **3,000 times larger** than the computed values plus percent-level cancellation.\n\n**Interpretation and limitations.** This is strong evidence against that particular nuclear-profile explanation, not a proof that the gallium anomaly is new physics. The shell model is not an ab initio solution for (A=71); two-body currents and more complete configuration spaces could modify the density. The authors argue that known corrections are too small to generate the required thousand-fold oscillating profile, but propose valence-space in-medium similarity-renormalization-group and other many-body calculations as independent tests. Experimental source activity, excited-state strength, cross-section inputs and sterile-neutrino interpretations remain separate uncertainties.\n\n##### History\n\nThe modern anomaly is anchored by **BEST**, which placed a \\(^{51}\\)Cr source at the center of two gallium volumes and found consistent deficits in both zones ([arXiv:2109.11482](https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.11482), *PRL* 128, 232501; see also *PRC* 105, 065502). Combined with the earlier GALLEX and SAGE source runs, the discrepancy is about 20% and exceeds 5σ under the adopted capture cross section. A sterile-neutrino oscillation can suppress both zones, but much of the preferred mixing region is disfavored by reactor, solar, accelerator and beta-spectrum data; those comparisons are not identical because their energies, baselines and systematics differ.\n\n**Cadeddu et al.** shifted attention back to nuclear theory. Their peer-reviewed proposal, published **24 September 2026**, abandoned factorization and fitted radial Gamow–Teller profiles constrained by the electron-capture half-life ([arXiv:2512.20560](https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.20560), *PRL* 137, 131805). It showed that a sufficiently structured profile could lower neutrino capture without changing electron capture. That was a mathematical existence result using phenomenological profiles, not a many-body derivation.\n\nThe new paper provides the missing nuclear-structure test. Its nine calculations agree that realistic densities are smooth enough for detailed balance to hold at the percent level. The disagreement is therefore physical and direct: the profile required by the proposed resolution is orders of magnitude larger and finely cancelling compared with the shell model. Remaining uncertainty lies in many-body truncation and weak currents, but the size of the required deformation makes this specific conventional explanation implausible."}, {"id": "paper-2611-09488", "arxiv": "2611.09488", "title": "Constraints on Cosmic-Rays and the MeV Emission from Jupiter's Atmosphere", "authors": "Dimitris Tsatsis, Thomas Siegert, Volodymyr Savchenko, Saurabh Mittal, Laura Eisenberger, Mika A. Gelowicz, Rudi Reinhardt, Manja L. Zimmerer, Tristan Bouchet, Tomohiko Oka and Manuel R. H. W. Skalka", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Jupiter", "Cosmic rays", "MeV gamma rays", "Dark matter"], "label": "New paper · observational upper limits", "teaser": "Twenty-two years of serendipitous INTEGRAL/SPI pointings yield no Jovian MeV detection; present sensitivity remains two orders above the predicted cosmic-ray signal and four above reflected background emission.", "path": "2026/11/09/index.html#paper-2611-09488", "reportDate": "9 November 2026", "searchText": "Constraints on Cosmic-Rays and the MeV Emission from Jupiter's Atmosphere Dimitris Tsatsis, Thomas Siegert, Volodymyr Savchenko, Saurabh Mittal, Laura Eisenberger, Mika A. Gelowicz, Rudi Reinhardt, Manja L. Zimmerer, Tristan Bouchet, Tomohiko Oka and Manuel R. H. W. Skalka 2611.09488 Twenty-two years of serendipitous INTEGRAL/SPI pointings yield no Jovian MeV detection; present sensitivity remains two orders above the predicted cosmic-ray signal and four above reflected background emission. 2026-11-09 9 November 2026 Jupiter Cosmic rays MeV gamma rays Dark matter #### INTEGRAL finds no Jovian MeV emission, setting a benchmark for COSI\n\n**Paper:** *Constraints on Cosmic-Rays and the MeV Emission from Jupiter's Atmosphere* — **Dimitris Tsatsis, Thomas Siegert, Volodymyr Savchenko, Saurabh Mittal, Laura Eisenberger, Mika A. Gelowicz, Rudi Reinhardt, Manja L. Zimmerer, Tristan Bouchet, Tomohiko Oka and Manuel R. H. W. Skalka**. [arXiv:2611.09488v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09488v1), [full text](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.09488v1). Submitted **6 November 2026 at 14:11:17 UTC** and first listed **9 November**; primary category **astro-ph.HE**. Accepted for publication in *Astronomy & Astrophysics*. **Classification: moving-source gamma-ray analysis with 2σ flux limits and a model-dependent 95% dark-matter exclusion.**\n\n**Question and data.** Jupiter can reflect the cosmic gamma-ray background, produce atmospheric gamma rays when cosmic rays strike its atmosphere, radiate auroral bremsstrahlung, or emit photons from dark-matter annihilation into long-lived mediators. The paper searches the **30 keV–8 MeV** band in 22 years of serendipitous INTEGRAL/SPI pointings.\n\nThe authors cross-match SPI pointings with Jupiter's ephemeris and correct every model for the changing Earth–Jupiter distance. After excluding difficult Galactic-center fields, the usable exposure is **4.08 Ms**, compared with **11.6 Ms** in the full archive. They build a 1,000-km Jovian atmosphere in one-kilometre shells from Galileo profiles and simulate photons, protons, alpha particles, electrons and positrons. A forward model separates instrumental background, fixed celestial sources and a moving Jupiter component. The quasar 3C 273 is used as a calibration check.\n\n**Results.** Every Jovian spectral point is consistent with zero, so the paper reports **2σ upper limits** rather than a detection. A cutoff-power-law fit restricts the MeV bremsstrahlung luminosity to about\n\n\\[\nL_{\\rm brem}\\lesssim10^{18}\\ {\\rm erg\\,s^{-1}},\n\\]\n\nnear the scale inferred indirectly from Juno/JEDI energetic electrons, but still far above the expected auroral flux at Earth. The authors estimate an intrinsic auroral continuum near \\(10^{-10}\\ {\\rm ph\\,cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}\\,keV^{-1}}\\) at 20 keV, about five orders below their 30-keV limit.\n\nFor reflected background photons, the fitted amplitude has a 2σ upper limit **\\(9.2\\times10^2\\)** relative to the model normalization, whereas the expected value derived from the Swift/BAT cosmic background is \\((10.15\\pm0.80)\\times10^{-2}\\). Current sensitivity is therefore about **four orders of magnitude** short. For cosmic-ray-induced emission the fitted 2σ scale is **\\(2.4\\times10^2\\)**, compared with unity for the adopted cosmic-ray spectrum: about **two orders of magnitude** short. These nondetections constrain instrument sensitivity; they do not show that the cosmic-ray or reflected components are absent.\n\n**Dark matter.** The model captures dark matter in Jupiter and lets \\(\\chi\\bar\\chi\\to\\phi\\phi\\to4\\gamma\\), with mediators escaping before decay. SPI is combined with published Fermi-LAT limits, producing a continuous 95% exclusion for two mass ratios, \\(m_\\phi=0.1m_\\chi\\) and \\(m_\\phi=m_\\chi/1.01\\). SPI extends the Jovian search to roughly \\(m_\\chi\\sim10^{-4}\\)–\\(10^{-2}\\) GeV, where its best standalone proton-scattering reach is around **\\(10^{-35}\\ {\\rm cm^2}\\)**. The exact curve depends strongly on mediator lifetime, annihilation equilibrium, evaporation and the width of the box spectrum. It is not a generic direct-detection bound.\n\n**Limitations and outlook.** Jupiter subtends only 30–50 arcsec and is never a dedicated SPI target. Bright Galactic fields were discarded, losing most of the available exposure. The atmospheric yield inherits the assumed heliospheric cosmic-ray spectra and an 11-year solar modulation. The dark-matter limits assume efficient capture, long-lived mediators and specific branching. The planned COSI survey should accumulate **31–62 Ms** near the ecliptic with substantially better continuum sensitivity; the authors estimate that its exposure and response could reduce statistical uncertainties by at least an order of magnitude, but that is a forecast.\n\n##### History\n\nThe first dedicated Jovian gamma-ray search used **12 years of Fermi-LAT data** and found no robust emission, setting flux limits near \\(10^{-9}\\ {\\rm GeV\\,cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}\\) and, under long-lived-mediator assumptions, dark-matter–proton limits as low as about \\(10^{-40}\\ {\\rm cm^2}\\) ([Leane and Linden, *PRL* 131, 071001](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.131.071001), [arXiv:2104.02068](https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.02068)). That stronger number occurs at different masses and in Fermi's GeV band; it must not be compared as a uniform five-order improvement over SPI.\n\nThe MeV work adds a new energy window and a moving-source analysis, rather than a more sensitive limit everywhere. Its cosmic-ray calculation also builds on planetary gamma-ray albedo studies and modern heliospheric spectra. The result is valuable precisely because the expected conventional components remain below reach: it establishes an empirical MeV baseline and validates a pipeline that COSI can reuse. The largest remaining instrumental uncertainty is background modelling near the Galactic plane; the largest physical uncertainty in the dark-matter interpretation is evaporation and mediator decay geometry."}, {"id": "paper-2611-07426", "arxiv": "2611.07426", "title": "First broadband search for fixed-polarization dark photon dark matter with GigaBREAD", "authors": "Jialin Yu, Stefan Knirck, Gabe Hoshino, Rakshya Khatiwada, Alex Lapuente, David W. Miller and Andrew Sonnenschein", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["Dark photons", "Dark matter", "GigaBREAD"], "label": "New paper · experimental reanalysis and 90% limits", "teaser": "A sidereal-modulation likelihood applied to two GigaBREAD runs finds no fixed-polarization dark-photon signal and improves the conservative reinterpretation by about 20% in kinetic mixing.", "path": "2026/11/06/index.html#paper-2611-07426", "reportDate": "6 November 2026", "searchText": "First broadband search for fixed-polarization dark photon dark matter with GigaBREAD Jialin Yu, Stefan Knirck, Gabe Hoshino, Rakshya Khatiwada, Alex Lapuente, David W. Miller and Andrew Sonnenschein 2611.07426 A sidereal-modulation likelihood applied to two GigaBREAD runs finds no fixed-polarization dark-photon signal and improves the conservative reinterpretation by about 20% in kinetic mixing. 2026-11-06 6 November 2026 Dark photons Dark matter GigaBREAD #### GigaBREAD uses Earth's rotation to search for a fixed dark-photon polarization\n\n**Paper:** *First broadband search for fixed-polarization dark photon dark matter with GigaBREAD* — **Jialin Yu, Stefan Knirck, Gabe Hoshino, Rakshya Khatiwada, Alex Lapuente, David W. Miller and Andrew Sonnenschein**. [arXiv:2611.07426v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07426v1), [full text](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.07426v1). Submitted **5 November 2026 at 04:20:25 UTC** and first listed **6 November**. v1 Fermilab preprint, report **FERMILAB-PUB-26-0792-ETD-PPD**; no journal publication is listed. **Classification: experimental reanalysis with Bayesian credible and frequentist confidence limits; no dark-photon detection.**\n\n**Parameter and signal.** A dark photon of mass (m_{\\rm DP}) kinetically mixed with the ordinary photon by the dimensionless parameter (chi) can convert into a radio photon at a conducting surface. Unlike axions, this conversion needs no applied magnetic field. If vector dark matter was produced by a misalignment-like mechanism, its polarization may remain approximately fixed in celestial coordinates. The signal in a detector sensitive along the axis \\(\\hat D(t)\\) is then\n\n\\[\nP_{\\rm det}(t)=P_0\\left|\\hat X\\!\\cdot\\!\\hat D(t)\\right|^2,\n\\]\n\nso Earth's sidereal rotation supplies a predictable modulation rather than the constant factor \\(\\langle\\cos^2\\vartheta\\rangle=1/3\\) used for random polarization.\n\n**Data and analysis.** GigaBREAD converts dark photons on a vertical cylindrical conductor of effective area about **0.5 m²** and focuses the emitted 10.7–12.5 GHz radiation into a horn. The reanalysis combines a **24-day**, zenith-aligned University of Chicago run with **12,970 spectra** and a **27-day**, horizontally aligned Argonne run with **12,481 spectra**. Each spectrum contains 230,392 bins at **7.8 kHz** resolution. The reference conversion assumes a local dark-matter density of **0.45 GeV cm⁻³**.\n\nThe likelihood retains each spectrum's time stamp and matches both the frequency profile and the sidereal polarization pattern. Because absolute timing was not preserved across the 2023 sub-runs, the polarization azimuth is marginalized independently for each sub-run, while the celestial latitude remains common. Bayesian evidence and a profile-likelihood statistic are calibrated with **300 full noise simulations**, covering **1,151,965 mass templates**. The effective frequentist trials factor is about \\(5\\times10^5\\). Limits incorporate a **23% power systematic** inherited from the detector calibration.\n\n**Result.** The observed global Bayesian statistic is \\(k_0^{\\rm global}=-2.5\\), below the paper's 90% candidate threshold of \\(-2.3\\); the frequentist scan likewise contains no globally significant mass. The authors report conservative limits by taking the weaker of their Bayesian 90% credible and frequentist 90% confidence upper limits at every mass. Over **44–52 μeV**, the resulting fixed-polarization limit is at the level of\n\n\\[\n\\chi \\sim 10^{-12}.\n\\]\n\nIt is approximately **20% lower in \\(\\chi\\)** than a naive fixed-polarization rescaling of the earlier GigaBREAD result and is comparable to, or slightly stronger than, the earlier random-polarization limit. The improvement comes from using the timing information and a second detector orientation. It applies to a fixed but unknown polarization; it is not a general improvement for every dark-photon production model.\n\n**Limitations.** The result assumes that polarization stays coherent and fixed over the observation span and that the Standard Halo Model describes the spectral line. The missing absolute phase across 2023 sub-runs removes some directional information. Instrumental baselines are removed with a Savitzky–Golay filter, and the analysis relies on Monte Carlo calibration of a non-Wilks statistic. The constraint also scales with the assumed local density and conversion efficiency. No mass exceeded the candidate threshold, so the work supplies exclusions rather than evidence for a modulated signal.\n\n##### History\n\nThe original GigaBREAD search used the 24-day 2023 data set and treated dark-photon polarization as random. It excluded roughly \\(\\chi\\gtrsim10^{-12}\\) at **90% confidence** over the same 44–52 μeV interval and improved previous bounds by about two orders of magnitude below 49 μeV ([Knirck et al., arXiv:2310.13891](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.13891), *Physical Review Letters* **132**, 131004). The new paper does not gain an order of magnitude or open a new mass range. Its substantive advance is a statistically consistent reinterpretation for fixed polarization, plus the combination of vertical and horizontal runs.\n\nDish searches such as SHUKET, QUALIPHIDE, DOSUE and BRASS-p constrain overlapping or neighboring masses with different receivers, geometries and polarization conventions. Resonant haloscopes including HAYSTAC, ADMX and ORGAN can also constrain dark photons after a polarization assumption is chosen, but their scan strategy and resonant response are different. Improvement factors should therefore be quoted only against the previous GigaBREAD fixed-polarization rescaling. The main remaining uncertainty is physical: structure formation may partially randomize a primordial vector direction, in which case neither the perfectly fixed nor perfectly isotropic limit is the complete description."}, {"id": "paper-2611-07189", "arxiv": "2611.07189", "title": "Systematic Study of Cosmic Rays in Star-Forming and Starburst Galaxies using Fermi-LAT and Multiwavelength Data", "authors": "Hinako Ochi, Tsunefumi Mizuno, Yasushi Fukazawa, Noemi Barnier, Susumu Inoue, Wataru Iwakiri, Junling Chen and Tomonori Totani", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Gamma rays", "Starburst galaxies", "Fermi-LAT"], "label": "New paper · observational analysis", "teaser": "Fifteen years of Fermi-LAT data revise several starburst fluxes downward and indicate high but non-super-calorimetric cosmic-ray efficiencies, with source confusion remaining important.", "path": "2026/11/06/index.html#paper-2611-07189", "reportDate": "6 November 2026", "searchText": "Systematic Study of Cosmic Rays in Star-Forming and Starburst Galaxies using Fermi-LAT and Multiwavelength Data Hinako Ochi, Tsunefumi Mizuno, Yasushi Fukazawa, Noemi Barnier, Susumu Inoue, Wataru Iwakiri, Junling Chen and Tomonori Totani 2611.07189 Fifteen years of Fermi-LAT data revise several starburst fluxes downward and indicate high but non-super-calorimetric cosmic-ray efficiencies, with source confusion remaining important. 2026-11-06 6 November 2026 Cosmic rays Gamma rays Starburst galaxies Fermi-LAT #### Fifteen years of Fermi data put starbursts at or below the cosmic-ray calorimetric limit\n\n**Paper:** *Systematic Study of Cosmic Rays in Star-Forming and Starburst Galaxies using Fermi-LAT and Multiwavelength Data* — **Hinako Ochi, Tsunefumi Mizuno, Yasushi Fukazawa, Noemi Barnier, Susumu Inoue, Wataru Iwakiri, Junling Chen and Tomonori Totani**. [arXiv:2611.07189v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07189v1), [full text](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.07189v1). Submitted **4 November 2026 at 22:25:01 UTC** and first listed **6 November**. The manuscript is accepted by the *Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan* following peer review. **Classification: observational gamma-ray analysis with conditional one-zone cosmic-ray modelling.**\n\n**Question and data.** Star-forming galaxies accelerate cosmic rays, whose hadronic interactions with gas produce gamma rays and neutrinos. A proton calorimeter loses nearly all of the relevant cosmic-ray power through collisions before escape. The authors ask how close ordinary star-forming galaxies and compact starbursts come to this limit, and whether their radio-to-TeV spectra require unusual electron-to-proton ratios.\n\nThe analysis uses **15 years of Fermi-LAT data** and updated Galactic diffuse and point-source models for seven galaxies: M82, NGC 253, M31, Arp 220, NGC 2146, Arp 299 and M33. M31 is decomposed into central and southeast components. Spectra are fitted from 50 MeV to about 100 GeV, then combined with published radio and TeV measurements in NAIMA-based one-zone models.\n\n**Results.** M82 and NGC 253 are detected with test statistics **1702** and **899** and show mild curvature. Their 0.1–100 GeV energy fluxes are \\(8.19\\times10^{-12}\\) and \\(6.31\\times10^{-12}\\ {\\rm erg\\,cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}\\). Both spectra turn down below a few hundred MeV, reducing the electron bremsstrahlung contribution relative to earlier models. The inferred electron-to-proton ratios are **1/25–1/30 at 1 GeV** and **1/120–1/150 at 10 GeV**, close to canonical Milky-Way values.\n\nThe proton energy densities are about **190 eV cm⁻³ in M82** and **470 eV cm⁻³ in NGC 253**, respectively around eight and 2.5 times below the magnetic energy densities used in the fits. This argues against cosmic-ray–magnetic equipartition inside these one-zone starburst models.\n\nFor M31, a compact central component has a soft curved spectrum and a **95% upper limit of 0.33° on its radius**. Unresolved sources can therefore contribute substantially, so its luminosity is treated as an upper limit on interstellar emission. M33 is similarly complicated by an offset toward the young region NGC 604.\n\nRevised fluxes for NGC 2146 and Arp 220 are approximately a factor of two lower than in the 2020 comparison analysis. Across the starburst sample, the observed ratio of gamma-ray luminosity to the calorimetric prediction is **0.33–1.20**; the value slightly above unity is within the combined modelling and source uncertainties, rather than proof of super-calorimetry. Ordinary star-forming galaxies lie around the **10% level**, except for the SMC, where point sources are likely important.\n\n**Limitations.** Calorimetric efficiency depends on infrared luminosity, gas density, source subtraction and the assumed supernova-to-cosmic-ray conversion. Several weak galaxies have \\({\\rm TS}\\sim50\\), making their spectra sensitive to the diffuse foreground. M31 and M33 contain unresolved or localized components that are not clean measurements of diffuse cosmic-ray interactions. The multiwavelength fits assume one-zone geometry, adopted magnetic fields and published gas masses; the apparent departure from equipartition is consequently model-dependent. The work characterizes detected emission but does not directly measure the neutrino output or isolate hadronic photons event by event.\n\n##### History\n\nFermi-LAT first reported M82 and NGC 253 at **6.8σ and 4.8σ** with 11 months of data in 2010, establishing a connection between massive-star formation and GeV emission. A later uniform study using about ten years of LAT data detected **11 bona-fide galaxies and two candidates**, plus a stacked below-threshold population, and estimated that star-forming galaxies contribute about **5% of the extragalactic gamma-ray background** and **3% of the IceCube astrophysical-neutrino flux** ([Ajello et al., arXiv:2003.05493](https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.05493)). Those population fractions are model estimates, not direct source associations.\n\nThe new analysis updates individual nearby systems rather than remeasuring the total background. Its most consequential changes are the sub-GeV downturn in M82 and NGC 253, the factor-of-two downward revisions for NGC 2146 and Arp 220, and explicit source-confusion cautions for M31 and M33. It supports the established picture that compact starbursts are efficient hadronic targets, while removing the need for several systems to sit clearly above the calorimetric relation. Remaining uncertainty is dominated by gas, magnetic-field and unresolved-source modelling, which also propagates into forecasts of the diffuse neutrino intensity."}, {"id": "paper-2611-07022", "arxiv": "2611.07022", "title": "General-relativistic neutrino-radiation hydrodynamics of crust ejection in magnetar giant flares", "authors": "Tetyana Pitik, David Radice, Jacob Fields and Yi Qiu", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Magnetars", "r-process", "Neutrinos", "Nucleosynthesis"], "label": "New paper · numerical simulation", "teaser": "General-relativistic radiation-hydrodynamic simulations sharply reduce the heavy-element yield expected from magnetar giant flares and leave free-neutron decay as the main radioactive transient power source.", "path": "2026/11/06/index.html#paper-2611-07022", "reportDate": "6 November 2026", "searchText": "General-relativistic neutrino-radiation hydrodynamics of crust ejection in magnetar giant flares Tetyana Pitik, David Radice, Jacob Fields and Yi Qiu 2611.07022 General-relativistic radiation-hydrodynamic simulations sharply reduce the heavy-element yield expected from magnetar giant flares and leave free-neutron decay as the main radioactive transient power source. 2026-11-06 6 November 2026 Magnetars r-process Neutrinos Nucleosynthesis #### General-relativistic simulations sharply reduce magnetar-flare r-process yields\n\n**Paper:** *General-relativistic neutrino-radiation hydrodynamics of crust ejection in magnetar giant flares* — **Tetyana Pitik, David Radice, Jacob Fields and Yi Qiu**. [arXiv:2611.07022v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07022v1), [full text](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.07022v1). Submitted **4 November 2026 at 19:00:06 UTC** and first listed **6 November**. v1 preprint; no journal publication is listed. **Classification: one-dimensional numerical simulation and conditional nucleosynthesis forecast, not an observed yield.**\n\n**Question and method.** The radio afterglow of the 27 December 2004 giant flare from SGR 1806−20 implies at least about \\(10^{-9}M_\\odot\\) of baryonic ejecta. Previous hydrodynamic and analytic calculations proposed that a magnetospheric fireball drives a shock into the neutron-star crust, ejecting neutron-rich matter that undergoes r-process nucleosynthesis. This paper tests that chain with general-relativistic hydrodynamics, a tabulated HS(DD2) nuclear equation of state, M1 neutrino transport and a SkyNet reaction network.\n\nThe simulations use a fixed spherical neutron-star spacetime and a one-kilometre, whole-surface pressure layer. Its peak pressure is labelled by the equivalent field \\(B=10^{15}\\)–\\(10^{16.5}\\) G through \\(P_{\\rm GF}=B^2/8\\pi\\). Every model is repeated without neutrino interactions, and the layer width, position and shape are varied. These labels describe the imposed pressure; they are not measurements of an actual flare field.\n\n**Results.** The models unbind approximately\n\n\\[\n10^{-10} - 5\\times10^{-8} M_\\odot\n\\]\n\nof outer crust with mass-weighted asymptotic velocities **0.44–0.52 c**. Only about \\(10^{-3}\\) of the imposed fireball energy becomes ejecta kinetic energy. Neutrino cooling matters strongly only at the most extreme \\(10^{16.5}\\)-G pressure: it removes about a tenth of the layer energy while the shock is active and halves the ejecta mass. The ejecta retain \\(Y_e\\simeq0.44\\)–0.47.\n\nThe rapid, high-entropy expansion makes very few seed nuclei. Roughly **90%** of the ejecta becomes alpha particles, while **7–10%** remains as free neutrons. Even the strongest model produces at most **\\(3.5\\times10^{-11}M_\\odot\\)** in nuclei with \\(A\\ge90\\), three to four orders of magnitude below the earlier analytic calculation. Lanthanides and actinides remain below about \\(3\\times10^{-5}\\) of the ejecta mass.\n\nFor the extreme model at 10 kpc, the authors forecast about **two Super-Kamiokande and 15 Hyper-Kamiokande inverse-beta events**, but this flare is far more energetic than the 2004 event. Flares with observed energies should yield no detectable neutrino count. Free-neutron decay instead powers a predicted ultraviolet/optical flash peaking after roughly **40–55 seconds**; this is a model forecast, not an observed nova brevis.\n\n**Connection to the 2004 flare.** Models with \\(B=10^{15.5}\\)–\\(10^{16}\\) G eject enough mass at about 0.45 c to resemble the radio afterglow, but their whole-surface fireball energies are **60–600 times** the roughly \\(3\\times10^{46}\\) erg prompt spike. Scaling to that observed energy gives only \\((2–3.5)\\times10^{-11}M_\\odot\\), **50–100 times less** than the radio afterglow requires. The authors therefore conclude that a one-dimensional crust shock cannot by itself supply the observed baryons unless the flare released far more total energy or the true magnetic geometry couples energy much more efficiently.\n\n**Limitations.** Spherical symmetry, instantaneous whole-surface heating and omission of magnetic forces probably overestimate ejection. Real flares are localized, laterally expanding and threaded by open and closed field lines. The deepest, marginally unbound shells dominate the already small heavy-element yield, leaving it uncertain by about a factor of two for \\(A\\ge90\\) and more for lanthanides. The fixed spacetime, one-millisecond evolution and analytic continuation of trajectories add further approximations. Multidimensional general-relativistic magnetohydrodynamics is required before treating these values as physical rate predictions.\n\n##### History\n\n**Cehula, Thompson and Metzger** modelled the same shock-ejection mechanism with special-relativistic hydrodynamics and a simplified crust. For pressures around \\(10^{30}\\)–\\(10^{31}\\ {\\rm erg\\,cm^{-3}}\\), they obtained \\(10^{25}\\)–\\(10^{26}\\) g of ejecta at **0.3–0.6 c**, sufficient for the 2004 radio afterglow, and estimated a Galactic r-process contribution near \\(10^{-9}M_\\odot\\,{\\rm yr^{-1}}\\) ([arXiv:2311.05681](https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.05681), *MNRAS* **528**, 5323).\n\n**Patel et al.** then applied an analytic ejecta profile and reaction network, predicting moderate third-peak production, a stronger light r-process and a **10–15 minute**, \\(10^{39}\\)–\\(10^{40}\\ {\\rm erg\\,s^{-1}}\\) nova brevis for an SGR 1806−20-like ejecta mass ([arXiv:2501.17253](https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.17253)). A separate analysis interpreted delayed MeV emission from the 2004 event as decay of roughly \\(10^{-6}M_\\odot\\) of fresh r-process material ([arXiv:2501.09181](https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.09181)).\n\nThe new calculation directly conflicts with that high-yield interpretation within its tested mechanism: its largest mass in nuclei heavier than alpha particles is about \\(8\\times10^{-11}M_\\odot\\), four orders of magnitude below the delayed-MeV requirement. The discrepancy comes mainly from hydrodynamics: deep, cooler crust assumed to escape in the analytic profile falls back in the general-relativistic simulations. This does not disprove that the 2004 MeV component was real or radioactive; it shows that the proposed spherical crust-shock model cannot produce the inferred mass under the new transport and equation-of-state treatment. Geometry, magnetic stresses and an alternative baryon-loading channel remain open."}, {"id": "paper-2611-05783", "arxiv": "2611.05783", "title": "Insights on the mass composition of ultrahigh-energy cosmic rays from small-scale anisotropy with large-scale structure simulations", "authors": "Ryo Higuchi, Eiji Kido and Shigehiro Nagataki", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Ultra-high-energy cosmic rays", "Anisotropy", "Composition"], "label": "New paper · conditional composition inference", "teaser": "Two close pairs among 35 Auger events above 100 EeV are consistent with isotropy; pure-proton mock skies are difficult to reconcile with that result under the tested source and field models.", "path": "2026/11/05/index.html#paper-2611-05783", "reportDate": "5 November 2026", "searchText": "Insights on the mass composition of ultrahigh-energy cosmic rays from small-scale anisotropy with large-scale structure simulations Ryo Higuchi, Eiji Kido and Shigehiro Nagataki 2611.05783 Two close pairs among 35 Auger events above 100 EeV are consistent with isotropy; pure-proton mock skies are difficult to reconcile with that result under the tested source and field models. 2026-11-05 5 November 2026 Ultra-high-energy cosmic rays Anisotropy Composition #### Isotropy above 100 EeV disfavors a pure-proton sky—but only inside a demanding propagation model\n\n**Paper:** *Insights on the mass composition of ultrahigh-energy cosmic rays from small-scale anisotropy with large-scale structure simulations* — **Ryo Higuchi, Eiji Kido and Shigehiro Nagataki**. [arXiv:2611.05783v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05783v1), [full text](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.05783v1). Submitted **4 November 2026 at 00:42:11 UTC** and first listed **5 November**. The arXiv record identifies a 2026 publication in *Physical Review D* **114**, 083040. **Classification: composition and source-population inference from public arrival directions plus simulated propagation; not a direct mass measurement.**\n\n**Question and data.** If particles above 100 EeV are mainly protons, their short energy-loss horizon and small magnetic deflections should make nearby large-scale structure visible as close arrival-direction pairs. The authors take the **35 Pierre Auger Phase-I catalogue events above 100 EeV** and count pairs separated by at most (3^circ). They find **two pairs**. In (10^5) isotropic skies with the same event count and Auger directional exposure, that value lies inside the expected distribution; the data therefore show no significant small-scale anisotropy by this statistic.\n\n**Propagation and mock skies.** The interpretation compares deliberately pure proton, iron and uranium injections. CRPropa supplies proton and iron losses; uranium photodisintegration is calculated with TALYS 2.0 on cosmic-microwave and infrared backgrounds. Sources are sampled from the Millennium Run semi-analytic galaxy catalogue, repeated to cover distances to roughly **500 Mpc**. The source density is scanned from (10^{-6}) to (10^{-3} {\\rm Mpc^{-3}}). Turbulent deflection is summarized by\n\n\\[\n\\alpha=\\left(\\frac{\\lambda}{1\\ {\\rm Mpc}}\\right)^{1/2}\n       \\left(\\frac{B}{1\\ {\\rm nG}}\\right),\n\\]\n\nwhere (B) is the rms extragalactic field and (lambda) its coherence length. For each grid point and composition, **1,000 mock catalogues of 35 events** are tested with the same pair count. A point is called allowed at the paper's one-sided 95% criterion when its median pair count lies inside the corresponding isotropic range. This construction is a simulation acceptance rule, not a likelihood-based confidence interval on composition.\n\n**Result.** With no magnetic deflection, none of the tested proton or iron source densities reproduces the observed isotropy; uranium can do so for high densities around (10^{-3})–(10^{-4} {\\rm Mpc^{-3}}), because nuclei with smaller Lorentz factor can arrive from a smoother, more distant source distribution. With fields, iron and uranium acquire broad allowed regions. A uranium example becomes compatible near (\\rho_{\\rm source}sim10^{-4.5} {\\rm Mpc^{-3}}) for a sufficiently strong turbulent field. Pure protons remain rejected over almost all scanned parameter combinations and survive only for the paper's extreme (alpha=10) field case.\n\nThe inference is therefore conditional: the observed statistic is simply consistent with isotropy, while the preference against protons emerges after fixing pure compositions, injection spectra, a galaxy-to-source prescription, energy losses and magnetic transport. It does not measure a uranium fraction or exclude mixed compositions.\n\n**Limitations.** Thirty-five events give only two close pairs, so the statistic has little resolving power once strong fields erase angular structure. Uranium is an intentionally extreme proxy for nuclei beyond iron; its abundance and acceleration were not fitted. The mock universe uses repeated simulation boxes, one observer realization, a simplified exposure mapping and turbulent fields not tied to filaments, clusters and voids. The regular Galactic field changes inferred source-density limits by about an order of magnitude in the authors' test, and modern Galactic-field models still disagree. Energy-scale uncertainty, hadronic shower modelling and unmodelled mixed composition add further ambiguity.\n\n##### History\n\nThe absence of clustering has constrained source populations before. The Pierre Auger Collaboration's [2013 analysis, arXiv:1305.1576](https://arxiv.org/abs/1305.1576) used events above 60–80 EeV and obtained **95% confidence lower bounds of roughly ((0.06\\text{–}5)\\times10^{-4} {\\rm Mpc^{-3}})** for equal-luminosity, uniformly distributed sources, depending strongly on magnetic smearing; local-matter-tracing cases gave a similar ((0.2\\text{–}7)\\times10^{-4} {\\rm Mpc^{-3}}) range. Those are not directly interchangeable with the new allowed regions because the event threshold, propagation horizon, catalogue and composition hypotheses differ. The new work's useful addition is to ask how much of the isotropy can arise from the longer propagation distance of nuclei heavier than iron, rather than only from a high source density or large deflection.\n\nIndependent composition measurements point in the same qualitative direction without identifying uranium. Auger's deep-learning reconstruction of about **50,000** surface-detector showers found the mean mass becoming heavier and its fluctuations narrowing up to 100 EeV ([arXiv:2406.06319](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.06319)), subject to hadronic-interaction models. The Telescope Array's large-scale-structure comparison also inferred a very heavy injected composition above 100 EeV even when allowing strong extragalactic fields ([arXiv:2406.19287](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.19287)). The present pair-count study is consistent with those baselines, but is less composition-direct than (X_{max}): its remaining uncertainty is the degeneracy among charge, magnetic field, source density and the actual nearby source distribution."}, {"id": "paper-2611-06284", "arxiv": "2611.06284", "title": "A search for sterile neutrinos at ultrahigh energies at the Pierre Auger Observatory", "authors": "Pierre Auger Collaboration", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Sterile neutrinos", "Pierre Auger Observatory", "Dark matter"], "label": "New paper · model-dependent flux limit", "teaser": "Existing upward-going and Earth-skimming air-shower samples are reinterpreted as limits on a MeV-scale sterile-neutrino flux and on a conditional superheavy-dark-matter scenario.", "path": "2026/11/05/index.html#paper-2611-06284", "reportDate": "5 November 2026", "searchText": "A search for sterile neutrinos at ultrahigh energies at the Pierre Auger Observatory Pierre Auger Collaboration 2611.06284 Existing upward-going and Earth-skimming air-shower samples are reinterpreted as limits on a MeV-scale sterile-neutrino flux and on a conditional superheavy-dark-matter scenario. 2026-11-05 5 November 2026 Neutrinos Sterile neutrinos Pierre Auger Observatory Dark matter #### Auger turns Earth-skimming air-shower data into a limit on MeV sterile neutrinos\n\n**Paper:** *A search for sterile neutrinos at ultrahigh energies at the Pierre Auger Observatory* — **Pierre Auger Collaboration**. [arXiv:2611.06284v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06284v1), [full text](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.06284v1). Submitted **4 November 2026 at 10:23:29 UTC** and first listed **5 November**. v1 collaboration preprint; no journal reference is listed. **Classification: model-dependent reinterpretation and 90% confidence flux limit; no sterile-neutrino candidate or detection.**\n\n**Parameter and signal.** The minimal (3+1) benchmark contains a predominantly sterile mass eigenstate (N_1) with mass from the MeV scale to **100 MeV**, mixing only with the tau flavour through (sin^2\\theta_N). Its weak interaction cross section is suppressed by that factor. At ultrahigh energy, an (N_1) can therefore cross more Earth than an active neutrino, interact through its active component, create a tau lepton, and yield an upward or Earth-skimming air shower when the tau exits and decays.\n\n**Method and observations.** A Monte Carlo propagates the sterile state through Earth, samples neutrino interactions, follows tau energy loss and decay, and folds the resulting shower through the fluorescence-detector upward-going and surface-detector Earth-skimming exposures. The upward-going sample, covering **1 January 2004–31 December 2018**, contains **one candidate** against (0.27\\pm0.12) expected misreconstructed cosmic-ray showers. The Earth-skimming search through **31 December 2021** contains **zero candidates**. The corresponding 90% signal-count limits are 4.09 and 2.44 events. Tau energy loss dominates the exposure systematic at **(+34%/-28%)**; the published limit incorporates the conservative exposure treatment.\n\nFor (sin^2\\theta_N\\lesssim0.1), an assumed isotropic (E^{-2}) spectrum gives\n\n\\[\nE^2\\Phi_N \\le 2\\times10^{-9}\\,\\sin^{-2}\\theta_N\n\\quad {\\rm GeV\\,cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}\\,sr^{-1}}\n\\]\n\nat **90% confidence** over **0.3–200 EeV**. Thus the numerical bound is (2\\times10^{-7}) in these units at (sin^2\\theta_N=10^{-2}), and (2\\times10^{-6}) at (10^{-3}). The inverse mixing dependence reflects transparency: weaker mixing lets more particles cross Earth but makes the final interaction less likely. The Earth-skimming channel supplies most of the sensitivity; sterile fluxes generated only by standard source neutrinos oscillating into (N_1) remain several orders below it.\n\n**Conditional dark-matter interpretation.** The paper also assumes a (10^{13})-GeV superheavy particle (chi) that decays exclusively into one or several sterile states. At (m_N=10) MeV and (sin^2\\theta_N\\sim10^{-2}), the (k=1) benchmark gives (\\tau_\\chi\\gtrsim10^{16}) yr at 90% confidence. This is far weaker than typical (sim10^{23})-yr limits on visible superheavy-dark-matter channels because the assumed products are mostly sterile and have low multiplicity. At (m_N=100) MeV, sterile decays transfer energy into active tau neutrinos before arrival. These curves are conditional exclusions in ((sin^2\\theta_N,\\tau_\\chi)), not general dark-matter lifetime bounds.\n\n**Limitations.** The reported flux bound fixes isotropy and an (E^{-2}) spectrum; differential limits are less assumption-heavy but weaker. The model allows only tau mixing, uses vacuum mixing and ignores additional sterile interactions. The upward-going event is background-compatible, while the zero-event Earth-skimming sample drives the result. Cosmogenic predictions depend on the UHECR source evolution and composition; the dark-matter result additionally fixes mass, halo profile, decay channel and multiplicity. For (m_N) above the pion threshold, photon searches can dominate once (N_1\\to\\pi^0\\nu_\\tau) opens.\n\n##### History\n\nAuger's air-shower technique was established for active neutrinos. Using 2004–2013 data, the collaboration found no candidates and set a single-flavour **90% confidence** (E^{-2}) limit of (E_\\nu^2\\Phi_\\nu<6.4\\times10^{-9} {\\rm GeV,cm^{-2},s^{-1},sr^{-1}}) over (10^{17})–(2.5\\times10^{19}) eV ([arXiv:1504.05397](https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.05397)). The new sterile limit reuses the same physical signatures but recalculates transmission and conversion for a suppressed cross section. Its larger numerical normalization at small mixing is therefore not a weaker repeat of the active-neutrino result; it constrains a different incident particle and explicitly carries the (sin^{-2}\\theta_N) penalty.\n\nSterile propagation through Earth was proposed as an explanation of anomalous upward ANITA showers by **Cherry and Shoemaker**, who calculated the acceptance for EeV sterile states and showed how reduced mixing could evade Earth opacity ([*Physical Review D* 99, 063016 (2019)](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.99.063016)). The present collaboration analysis moves from an event-motivated scenario to detector-specific Auger exposure and observed-count limits over a defined energy interval.\n\nOther bounds probe different assumptions. CHARM's direct beam-dump reinterpretation constrains tau-mixed heavy neutral leptons only for roughly **290 MeV–1.6 GeV**, above the main mass range here ([*Physical Review D* 104, 095019 (2021)](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.104.095019)). Big-bang nucleosynthesis, CMB and supernova bounds can be much stronger at 10–100 MeV, but depend on reheating temperature, early-Universe production and stellar-core transport. The Auger limit is less restrictive yet astrophysically independent; the open question is whether nature supplies any ultrahigh-energy sterile flux near its reach."}, {"id": "paper-2611-05518", "arxiv": "2611.05518", "title": "DESI DR2 Results III: The Mass of the Milky Way", "authors": "DESI Collaboration", "primary_category": "astro-ph.GA", "tags": ["Milky Way", "Dark matter", "DESI", "Stellar dynamics"], "label": "New paper · dynamical measurement", "teaser": "Four mock-calibrated dynamical methods applied to 10,036 halo stars give a virial mass near 1.2 trillion solar masses, while exposing strong method-dependent systematics.", "path": "2026/11/05/index.html#paper-2611-05518", "reportDate": "5 November 2026", "searchText": "DESI DR2 Results III: The Mass of the Milky Way DESI Collaboration 2611.05518 Four mock-calibrated dynamical methods applied to 10,036 halo stars give a virial mass near 1.2 trillion solar masses, while exposing strong method-dependent systematics. 2026-11-05 5 November 2026 Milky Way Dark matter DESI Stellar dynamics #### DESI's halo stars put the Milky Way near 1.2 trillion solar masses\n\n**Paper:** *DESI DR2 Results III: The Mass of the Milky Way* — **DESI Collaboration**. [arXiv:2611.05518v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05518v1), [full text](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.05518v1). Submitted **3 November 2026 at 19:15:56 UTC**, after the relevant cutoff, and first listed **5 November**. v1 DESI Key Project preprint, submitted to *The Astrophysical Journal*. **Classification: dynamical mass measurement with mock-calibrated systematic uncertainties.**\n\n**Parameter, data and methods.** (M_{200c}) is the mass inside the radius where the mean enclosed density is 200 times the critical density. DESI DR2 supplies six-dimensional phase space for **5,198 blue horizontal-branch stars** and **4,838 RR Lyrae**, **10,036 tracers** in total, spanning about **10–95 kpc** from the Galactic centre. Known globular clusters, dwarf galaxies and the Sagittarius stream are removed, and angular selection functions are modelled separately for the two tracers.\n\nFour dynamical estimators—emPDF, GME and two variants of the non-parametric Jeans method NIMBLE—fit the enclosed mass profile or halo parameters. Their biases and correlations are tested on **24 AuriDESI mock surveys**: six cosmological Milky-Way analogues viewed from four observer positions, populated and selected to resemble the real samples. The joint hierarchy corrects the measured biases, imposes method-dependent error floors and retains residual correlations rather than treating eight method–tracer estimates as independent.\n\n**Result.** The joint **68% intervals** are\n\n\\[\nM(<80\\ {\\rm kpc})=6.36^{+0.53}_{-0.48}\\times10^{11}\\ M_\\odot,\n\\qquad\nM_{200c}=1.19^{+0.14}_{-0.12}\\times10^{12}\\ M_\\odot.\n\\]\n\nRR Lyrae alone and blue horizontal-branch stars alone both give (M_{200c}\\simeq1.20\\times10^{12} M_\\odot), an important tracer cross-check. The enclosed mass continues rising from 20 to 80 kpc. Within that range the joint uncertainty stays below about 8%, but the virial value extrapolates beyond most tracers and is more model-sensitive.\n\nThe apparently tight virial interval needs context. GME receives about **75% of the joint (M_{200c}) weight** because it performs especially well on the mocks. Removing it shifts the combination to (1.52^{+0.35}_{-0.30}\\times10^{12} M_\\odot) and doubles the relative uncertainty from roughly 11% to 22%. The paper therefore treats method disagreement as part of the result, rather than presenting the central value as a model-free census.\n\n**Limitations.** Every estimator assumes approximate equilibrium and spherical symmetry. The six mock haloes sample only a small set of assembly histories, and transfer of their bias calibration to the real Galaxy is untestable. The mocks do not contain an LMC analogue at its present close position; published experiments indicate that the LMC's reflex motion and halo distortion can bias equilibrium estimates by up to about **20%** over the radial range used. Proper-motion errors and sparse tracers become limiting beyond 70 kpc. The analysis does not infer the local dark-matter density, which would require a large inward extrapolation and assumptions about baryonic contraction.\n\n##### History\n\nThe trillion-solar-mass scale is consistent with several independent tracers. A simulation-calibrated analysis of ten classical satellite galaxies found (M_{200}=1.17^{+0.21}_{-0.15}\\times10^{12} M_\\odot) at 68% confidence ([Callingham et al., arXiv:1808.10456](https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.10456)). A joint Gaia DR2 rotation-curve and outer-tracer fit, including baryonic contraction, obtained (M_{200}^{\\rm total}=1.08^{+0.20}_{-0.14}\\times10^{12} M_\\odot) ([Cautun et al., arXiv:1911.04557](https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.04557)). DESI does not dramatically move that established baseline; its advance is the size of the distant standard-candle samples, the use of four methods on common mocks, and an explicit correlated combination.\n\nThe live dispute is with low masses inferred by extrapolating the declining Gaia DR3 disc rotation curve. **Ou et al.** fit a cored Einasto halo to data reaching about 30 kpc and obtained a dark-halo virial mass (1.81^{+0.06}_{-0.05}\\times10^{11} M_\\odot), while warning that the low value is driven by the chosen extrapolating profile ([arXiv:2303.12838](https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.12838)). **Jiao et al.** reported a roughly 30 km s⁻¹ decline from 19.5 to 26.5 kpc and a total mass (2.06^{+0.24}_{-0.13}\\times10^{11} M_\\odot) ([arXiv:2309.00048](https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.00048)). DESI's mass continues rising well beyond that disc range and strongly favours (M_{200c}\\gtrsim10^{12} M_\\odot), suggesting that disequilibrium, asymmetric-drift or extrapolation systematics affect the low-mass solutions. It does not by itself identify which correction is responsible; LMC dynamics and non-spherical structure remain the central uncertainties."}, {"id": "paper-2611-03785", "arxiv": "2611.03785", "title": "Cosmic-Ray Signals from Dark Matter Annihilation into Scalars with Baryon Number", "authors": "Mattia Di Mauro, Caleb Gemmell, Dan Hooper and Fabrizio Vassallo", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Antinuclei", "Dark matter"], "label": "New paper · conditional signal forecast", "teaser": "Baryon-carrying mediator decays can yield detectable antideuterons under AMS-02 antiproton limits, while antihelium remains marginal.", "path": "2026/11/04/index.html#paper-2611-03785", "reportDate": "4 November 2026", "searchText": "Cosmic-Ray Signals from Dark Matter Annihilation into Scalars with Baryon Number Mattia Di Mauro, Caleb Gemmell, Dan Hooper and Fabrizio Vassallo 2611.03785 Baryon-carrying mediator decays can yield detectable antideuterons under AMS-02 antiproton limits, while antihelium remains marginal. 2026-11-04 4 November 2026 Cosmic rays Antinuclei Dark matter #### Can baryon-carrying dark-sector particles make cosmic antinuclei observable?\n\n**Paper:** *Cosmic-Ray Signals from Dark Matter Annihilation into Scalars with Baryon Number* — **Mattia Di Mauro, Caleb Gemmell, Dan Hooper and Fabrizio Vassallo**. [arXiv:2611.03785v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03785v1), [full text](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.03785v1). Submitted **2 November 2026 at 19:00:01 UTC**, one second after the relevant cutoff; first listed **4 November**. v1 preprint, with no journal publication listed. **Classification: phenomenological dark-matter model, antiproton constraint and conditional antinucleus forecast; no antinucleus detection.**\n\n**Question and method.** Standard hadronic dark-matter annihilation has difficulty making heavy antinuclei without overproducing antiprotons. The authors study self-conjugate dark matter \\(\\chi\\) annihilating into color-singlet scalars \\(\\phi\\) with baryon and lepton numbers \\(B=L=3\\). Each scalar then decays schematically to three copies of \\(udd\\nu_e\\), distributing energy among many quarks and placing more antinucleons close enough in phase space to coalesce.\n\nPythia 8.309 generates the shower and hadronization. Antideuterons and antihelium-3 form when all constituent pairs satisfy calibrated momentum and spatial cuts: \\(p_{\\rm coal}=0.219\\) GeV and \\(d_{\\rm coal}=3\\) fm for antideuterons, and \\(0.206\\) GeV and 3 fm for antihelium-3. The spectra are propagated through a stationary two-zone Galactic model with a 4.2-kpc halo, NFW density profile normalized to \\(\\rho_\\odot=0.4\\ {\\rm GeV\\,cm^{-3}}\\), convection at **13 km s⁻¹**, and force-field solar potential **0.736 GV**. Continuous energy losses, reacceleration and charge-sign-dependent heliospheric transport are omitted.\n\nAt each dark-matter mass \\(m_\\chi\\) and mediator mass \\(m_\\phi\\), the authors derive a **one-sided 95% confidence-level upper limit** on the velocity-averaged annihilation cross section \\(\\langle\\sigma v\\rangle\\) from the seven-year AMS-02 antiproton spectrum. They profile the secondary-antiproton normalization, a spectral-index shift limited to ±0.1, and the Fisk potential. The bound spans approximately **1.5 × 10⁻²⁹ to 6.5 × 10⁻²⁵ cm³ s⁻¹** over the scanned plane; the familiar **3 × 10⁻²⁶ cm³ s⁻¹** thermal reference is excluded for roughly \\(m_\\phi\\lesssim20\\) GeV in this setup. This is a model- and propagation-specific cross-section bound, not a generic limit on dark matter.\n\n**Quantitative forecast.** Saturating that antiproton upper limit, broad parts of the plane yield at least one expected antideuteron event at AMS-02, and a region around \\(m_\\chi,m_\\phi\\sim50\\)–100 GeV also reaches GAPS. A benchmark with \\((m_\\chi,m_\\phi)=(66,53)\\) GeV and \\(\\langle\\sigma v\\rangle=6.3\\times10^{-26}\\ {\nm cm^3\\,s^{-1}}\\) predicts **9.9 AMS-02 antideuterons** and **1.2 GAPS antideuterons**. By contrast, antihelium-3 remains difficult: the favorable \\((43,5)\\)-GeV benchmark, using \\(9.8\\times10^{-28}\\ {\nm cm^3\\,s^{-1}}\\), gives **3.2 antideuterons and 0.5 antihelium-3 events** at AMS-02, a ratio **0.16**. Those event numbers are forecasts under assumed future acceptances, not measured excesses or confidence limits.\n\nThe same cascade fits the *shape* of the Galactic-centre gamma-ray excess near \\((m_\\chi,m_\\phi)=(35,27)\\) GeV, but requires \\(\\langle\\sigma v\\rangle\\simeq1.14\\times10^{-25}\\ {\nm cm^3\\,s^{-1}}\\), above the paper's antiproton limit. Even a larger allowed inner-Galaxy J-factor only reduces it to about \\(5.6\\times10^{-26}\\ {\nm cm^3\\,s^{-1}}\\). The authors therefore disfavor a simultaneous explanation under their reference halo and transport assumptions.\n\n**Limitations.** The mediator decay is sampled with isotropic massless phase space rather than a specified ultraviolet completion. Coalescence universality, antiproton production, Galactic transport and solar modulation dominate the systematics; the plotted ±20% coalescence band does not encompass all of them. Most importantly, AMS-02 has not published a conclusive antideuteron or antihelium flux, and the analysis uses a privately communicated antihelium acceptance estimate. The result identifies testable parameter regions; it does not explain established antinucleus events.\n\n##### History\n\nThe production mechanism was introduced by **Gemmell, Hooper, Koren and Vassallo** in [*Dark Matter, Baryon Number, and Cosmic-Ray Antinuclei*, PRL 137, 171802 (2026), arXiv:2603.12314v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.12314v2). That paper used event-level coalescence to show that a \\(B=L=3\\) mediator could enhance antideuteron and antihelium multiplicities by factors of roughly 10–100 relative to direct \\(b\\bar b\\) annihilation. The new work's substantive step is to propagate those particles, constrain the accompanying antiprotons with AMS-02, fold detector acceptances and test gamma rays. It consequently narrows the earlier qualitative claim: antideuterons can be observable in viable regions, while antihelium is only marginal under the more conservative sensitivity prescription.\n\nThe experimental baseline is still a null result. BESS-Polar II's published search gives \\(\\Phi_{\\bar d}<6.7\\times10^{-5}\\ ({\\rm m^2\\,s\\,sr\\,GeV/n})^{-1}\\) over **0.163–1.100 GeV/n**; AMS-02 candidate reports have not become a published flux measurement. Conventional WIMP studies, such as [Cholis, Linden and Hooper, *PRD* 102, 103019 (2020), arXiv:2001.08749](https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08749), already found that antideuterons may approach experimental reach while antihelium generally does not. The baryon-carrying cascade changes that hierarchy, especially the antihelium-to-antideuteron ratio, but uncertainties in formation and acceptance remain large enough that confirmation requires published events, backgrounds and efficiencies."}, {"id": "paper-2611-03813", "arxiv": "2611.03813", "title": "Presupernovae Strongly Constrain Electrophilic MeV Bosons", "authors": "Yu Cheng, Damiano F. G. Fiorillo, Chui-Fan Kong and Edoardo Vitagliano", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Supernovae", "Dark photons", "Axion-like particles"], "label": "New paper · model-dependent constraint", "teaser": "Years of heavy-element burning can outproduce the explosion in MeV bosons, allowing diffuse 511-keV data to bound their couplings.", "path": "2026/11/04/index.html#paper-2611-03813", "reportDate": "4 November 2026", "searchText": "Presupernovae Strongly Constrain Electrophilic MeV Bosons Yu Cheng, Damiano F. G. Fiorillo, Chui-Fan Kong and Edoardo Vitagliano 2611.03813 Years of heavy-element burning can outproduce the explosion in MeV bosons, allowing diffuse 511-keV data to bound their couplings. 2026-11-04 4 November 2026 Supernovae Dark photons Axion-like particles #### The years before core collapse may beat the supernova as a MeV-particle laboratory\n\n**Paper:** *Presupernovae Strongly Constrain Electrophilic MeV Bosons* — **Yu Cheng, Damiano F. G. Fiorillo, Chui-Fan Kong and Edoardo Vitagliano**. [arXiv:2611.03813v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03813v1), [full text](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.03813v1). Submitted **2 November 2026 at 19:00:16 UTC** and first listed **4 November**. v1 preprint, with no journal publication listed. **Classification: model-dependent astrophysical exclusion from an existing gamma-ray dataset.**\n\n**Parameter and physical question.** A dark photon \\(A'\\) kinetically mixed with the photon has dimensionless coupling \\(\\epsilon\\) and, above \\(2m_e\\), can decay to \\(e^+e^-\\). Supernova explosions are standard factories for MeV particles, but their dense plasma has a frequency of order **10 MeV**, suppressing in-medium mixing for lower-mass dark photons. Massive stars during carbon, oxygen and silicon burning are cooler but remain hot for years, with plasma frequencies only **30–70 keV**. The paper asks whether their much longer exposure produces more escaping particles than the explosion.\n\n**Method.** MESA r26.4.1 evolves solar-metallicity stars of **9–60 solar masses** to the onset of core infall. For a representative 20-solar-mass star, the core advances from approximately **80 keV** during carbon burning, through **190 keV** in oxygen burning, to **320 keV** in the final silicon-burning days. The authors calculate in-medium dark-photon production, decay and escape throughout these profiles, then average the positron yield over a Salpeter initial-mass function. A Galactic core-collapse rate of **2.3 per century** and a spiral-arm source distribution are convolved with an intentionally broad **1-kpc** positron-smearing scale.\n\nThe predicted disk-like 511-keV intensity is compared with INTEGRAL/SPI longitude bins at \\(|l|<20^\\circ\\). The one-sided statistic counts only model flux above the data and imposes \\(\\chi^2\\le4\\), a **2σ** construction without fitting or subtracting the bulge. It limits the number of locally annihilating positrons to **2.0 × 10⁵² per progenitor** for the 1-kpc benchmark.\n\n**Result.** For dark-photon masses approximately **1–3 MeV**, the excluded lower-coupling boundary reaches \\(\\epsilon\\sim10^{-14}\\), up to **one to two orders of magnitude stronger** than the paper's supernova comparisons. This improvement is genuinely comparable because it constrains the same kinetic-mixing parameter and mass interval, using presupernova rather than explosion emission. The authors also calculate an analogous electron-coupled axion-like-particle limit near \\(g_{ae}\\sim10^{-14}\\). At larger couplings, decay inside the star, trapping and energy loss comparable to the neutrino luminosity delimit the calculation; above the \\(L_{A'}=L_\\nu\\) curve the unperturbed stellar tracks cannot support a reliable exclusion.\n\n**Limitations.** The bound assumes that massive-star evolution, the Galactic core-collapse distribution, positron escape and propagation, and the SPI disk profile are described well enough by the adopted models. Varying the source distribution has little effect in the authors' tests, while changing the smearing length from 1 kpc to 100 pc produces a visible but modest shift. Dense circumstellar material weakens the 3-MeV bound by about **3%** for the stated benchmark and less below 2.5 MeV. The upper boundary is deliberately conservative because sufficiently strong dark-photon cooling would modify the progenitor itself. The calculation is therefore an exclusion within a specified stellar and Galactic model, not a laboratory bound.\n\n##### History\n\nThe observable builds on [Calore et al., *PRD* 105, 063026 (2022), arXiv:2112.08382](https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.08382), which used the same Galactic 511-keV line to constrain positrons from **core-collapse supernova** production of sterile neutrinos and dark photons. That work demonstrated that the line could exceed the reach of the SN 1987A energy-loss argument for particles that escape and decay. The new paper moves the production epoch before collapse and gains exposure time plus weaker plasma screening; this is why its improvement applies specifically around 1–3 MeV rather than uniformly across mass and coupling.\n\nPresupernova dark-photon emission was also calculated by [Sieverding, Rrapaj, Guo and Qian, *ApJ* 912, 13 (2021), arXiv:2101.08672](https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.08672). They evolved massive stars with extra cooling and proposed changes to the presupernova neutrino signal; for a 15-solar-mass star at 500 pc, the illustrative sensitivity was around \\(\\epsilon\\lesssim10^{-12}\\) and depended on a nearby, identified progenitor. The present diffuse method integrates the entire Galactic population and counts decay positrons, reaching smaller coupling without waiting for a nearby star. Remaining uncertainty lies in the nonperturbative region: both studies indicate that new cooling can alter advanced burning, so standard stellar profiles cannot be extrapolated through the upper-coupling edge without self-consistent evolution."}, {"id": "paper-2611-04227", "arxiv": "2611.04227", "title": "Fermi-LAT Detection of Extended GeV Emission toward the Middle-aged Supernova Remnant G156.2+05.7", "authors": "Wei-Liang Xu, Qing-Wen Tang and Long Li", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Cosmic rays", "Supernova remnants"], "label": "New paper · observational analysis and emission modelling", "teaser": "Seventeen years of LAT data reveal extended, curved GeV emission; a hadronic component is favoured but its share remains model-dependent.", "path": "2026/11/04/index.html#paper-2611-04227", "reportDate": "4 November 2026", "searchText": "Fermi-LAT Detection of Extended GeV Emission toward the Middle-aged Supernova Remnant G156.2+05.7 Wei-Liang Xu, Qing-Wen Tang and Long Li 2611.04227 Seventeen years of LAT data reveal extended, curved GeV emission; a hadronic component is favoured but its share remains model-dependent. 2026-11-04 4 November 2026 Gamma rays Cosmic rays Supernova remnants #### Extended GeV emission appears around the evolved remnant G156.2+05.7\n\n**Paper:** *Fermi-LAT Detection of Extended GeV Emission toward the Middle-aged Supernova Remnant G156.2+05.7* — **Wei-Liang Xu, Qing-Wen Tang and Long Li**. [arXiv:2611.04227v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04227v1), [full text](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.04227v1). Submitted **3 November 2026 at 03:06:07 UTC** and first listed **4 November**. Accepted by **Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society** on 2 November; arXiv v1. **Classification: observational gamma-ray detection plus conditional one-zone emission modelling.**\n\n**Data and detection.** The analysis uses **17 years** of Fermi-LAT Pass 8 Source-class data from 4 August 2008 to 3 August 2025 over **100 MeV–1 TeV**, with standard Galactic and isotropic diffuse models and 4FGL-DR4 sources. Replacing two catalogue point sources with an extended template gives extension test statistics \\({\\rm TS}_{\\rm ext}=74.9\\) for a uniform disk and **75.7** for a two-dimensional Gaussian. The adopted Gaussian has \\(R_{68}=0.84^\\circ\\pm0.09^\\circ\\); at the reference distance **2.7 kpc**, this is a physical radius near **40 pc**. The Gaussian and disk are effectively tied under the Akaike criterion, while a foreground CO template fits worse.\n\nThe integrated **100 MeV–1 TeV** photon flux is **1.41 × 10⁻⁸ ph cm⁻² s⁻¹**. A log parabola is preferred to a power law by \\({\\rm TS}=11.429\\), nominally **3.4σ for one extra parameter**, with spectral parameters \\(\\Gamma=2.101\\pm0.089\\) and \\(\\beta=0.187\\pm0.081\\). This is evidence for curvature, not a separate component detection. A 17-bin annual light curve has \\({\\rm TS}_{\\rm var}=16.1\\), below the paper's 99%-confidence variability threshold **34.0**.\n\n**Emission modelling.** Radio points and the LAT spectrum are fitted jointly with NAIMA and MCMC. The baseline fixes \\(B=14\\ \\mu{\\rm G}\\), \\(d=2.7\\) kpc and proton cutoff \\(E_{p,{\\rm cut}}=1\\) TeV; that cutoff is not constrained by the LAT data. The mixed leptonic–hadronic model has **BIC 38.62**, versus **68.84** for a single-electron leptonic model and **45.40** for a proton-dominated gamma-ray model with separate synchrotron electrons. Thus a hadronic contribution is strongly preferred to the deliberately minimal one-population leptonic fit, but the **ΔBIC = 6.8** separation from the proton-dominated alternative is not decisive. The baseline finds electron cutoff about **1.18 TeV** and steep proton index about **3.23**.\n\nThe inferred proton energy above 1.22 GeV is\n\\[\nW_p\\simeq1.4\\times10^{50}\\left(\\frac{n}{1\\ {\\rm cm}^{-3}}\\right)^{-1}\n\\left(\\frac{d}{2.7\\ {\\rm kpc}}\\right)^2\\ {\\rm erg}.\n\\]\nAt the Suzaku-scaled preshock density \\(n=0.036\\ {\\rm cm}^{-3}\\), the one-zone requirement is roughly **3.7–4.0 times** a canonical \\(10^{51}\\)-erg supernova energy across the tested magnetic fields. Denser clumps, a multiphase medium or re-acceleration of existing cosmic rays are therefore needed if the hadronic interpretation is correct. This is an energy-budget argument, not a measured gas density.\n\n**Limitations.** The result depends on the diffuse foreground, spatial template and assumed distance. The northeast and southwest quadrants are brighter, but their spectral difference is only about **1.6σ**; association with Hα filaments is suggestive. The model fixes the proton cutoff, magnetic field and one-zone geometry and uses broad priors, while target density and proton normalization are degenerate. The LAT detection of extended GeV emission is robust within the performed template tests; its division between inverse Compton and pion decay is not.\n\n##### History\n\nG156.2+05.7 was discovered as a large X-ray shell with unusually faint radio emission. [Uchida et al., *PASJ* 64, 61 (2012), arXiv:1205.4188](https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.4188) used six Suzaku pointings to separate shocked interstellar gas and metal-rich ejecta. Once a background galaxy cluster was removed, the hard emission was consistent with the cosmic X-ray background: there was **no robust nonthermal X-ray component**. The present electron cutoff is therefore merely compatible with that null result, not measured by the X-rays.\n\nThe distance and age baseline changed with [Katsuda et al., *ApJ* 826, 108 (2016), arXiv:1605.07271](https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.07271). Comparing Hα filaments over about 12 years gave a **1σ proper-motion upper limit of 0.06 arcsec yr⁻¹**. Combined with an approximately 500-km-s⁻¹ shock, that implied **distance >1.7 kpc** and an age of at least a few \\(10^4\\) years, disfavoring an association with nearby 0.2–0.3-kpc clouds. The new paper adopts 2.7 kpc rather than measuring distance anew. Its actual advance is the first detailed extended GeV characterization and conditional particle budget. Whether it reveals freshly accelerated hadrons, re-accelerated Galactic cosmic rays or mixed electron/proton emission remains open."}, {"id": "paper-2611-03846", "arxiv": "2611.03846", "title": "SS 433 Eastern Jet Turbulence Revealed by Spatially-Resolved IXPE Data Analysis", "authors": "Ningyue Fan, Jack T. Dinsmore, Roger W. Romani", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Microquasars", "Polarization", "Particle acceleration"], "label": "Observational reanalysis", "teaser": "Spatially resolved IXPE modelling probes field ordering in SS 433; the proposed sheath-turbulence contrast remains weak.", "version": "v1", "listing_date": "2026-11-04", "path": "2026/11/04/index.html#paper-2611-03846", "reportDate": "4 November 2026", "searchText": "SS 433 Eastern Jet Turbulence Revealed by Spatially-Resolved IXPE Data Analysis Ningyue Fan, Jack T. Dinsmore, Roger W. Romani 2611.03846 Spatially resolved IXPE modelling probes field ordering in SS 433; the proposed sheath-turbulence contrast remains weak. 2026-11-04 4 November 2026 Microquasars Polarization Particle acceleration #### SS 433 Eastern Jet Turbulence Revealed by Spatially-Resolved IXPE Data Analysis\n\n**Authors:** Ningyue Fan, Jack T. Dinsmore and Roger W. Romani. **Paper:** [arXiv:2611.03846v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03846v1), submitted 2 November 2026; first announced in the **4 November UTC listing**. **Status:** observational reanalysis; arXiv reports acceptance by ApJ. This study concerns v1, not the 9 November replacement.\n\n**Question and method.** How ordered is the magnetic field where SS 433's eastern jet accelerates X-ray-emitting electrons? The authors reanalyse the 2023 IXPE exposure using Chandra-derived spatial templates and an event likelihood that accounts for polarization leakage between neighbouring regions. They separate the bright head, spine and surrounding sheath, and compare moment-based and neural-network event reconstruction.\n\n**Results.** For the neural-network reconstruction, the fitted polarization degrees are **25 ± 12% in the head, 26 ± 8% in the spine and 14 ± 12% in the sheath**, with quoted one-standard-deviation errors. A multizone model improves the fit over the single-zone description at approximately **3.5σ**, rising to about 3.8σ for overlapping templates. The electric-vector position angles remain compatible with a magnetic field along the jet. This supports spatial modelling of the emission, but the ordering of the three polarization fractions is much less significant: the inferred excess of sheath turbulence is only about **1–1.8σ**.\n\nFor isotropic Gaussian field fluctuations, the conversion is\n\n\\[\n\\tau\\equiv\\frac{\\delta B}{B_0}\n=\\left[\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{\\Pi_0-\\mathrm{PD}}{\\mathrm{PD}}\\right]^{1/2}\\sin i,\n\\]\n\nusing inclination \\(i=79^\\circ\\) and the synchrotron maximum polarization set by the X-ray photon index. It suggests \\(\\tau\\lesssim1.5\\) in the head/spine and \\(\\tau\\gtrsim1.8\\) in the sheath. These are model-dependent characterizations, not confidence bounds. Unresolved field geometry, template choice and low polarized counts can also reduce the measured polarization. Kelvin–Helmholtz shear is a possible interpretation, not a detected instability.\n\n##### History\n\n[Kaaret et al. (2024)](https://doi.org/10.3847/2041-8213/ad103b) analysed the same IXPE observation and obtained a background-corrected, single-region spectropolarimetric fraction of **52 ± 14%**, with EVPA **−3.7 ± 7.8°**. The new analysis reproduces a compatible result when using a comparable single-zone extraction; its lower multizone values therefore do not establish temporal depolarization. The advance is treatment of spatial mixing and leakage in an existing data set.\n\n[H.E.S.S. (2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.16019) independently used energy-dependent TeV morphology to locate acceleration and constrain downstream transport. Its preferred decelerating-flow model inferred an initial outer-jet speed of **\\((0.083\\pm0.026)c\\)**, with statistical error; alternative flow geometries also required advection. Polarimetry supplies complementary field information, but neither study uniquely fixes the turbulence spectrum or separates shear ordering from shock-related ordering."}, {"id": "paper-2611-03829", "arxiv": "2611.03829", "title": "XMM-Newton and NuSTAR quasi-simultaneous follow-up of the very high energy flare of PKS 1725+123 in August 2025", "authors": "L. Marcotulli, A. Gokus, L. Heckmann, E. Visentin, I. Jaroschewski, J. Otero-Santos, M. Böttcher, M. Zacharias, R. Takeishi, S. Nozaki, Y. Suda, I. Sadeh, P. Boorman, E. de Oña Wilhelmi, D. Berge", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Blazars", "Gamma rays", "X-rays"], "label": "Observations and spectral modelling", "teaser": "Post-flare X-ray spectra separate soft and hard components in PKS 1725+123, with limited simultaneous coverage.", "version": "v1", "listing_date": "2026-11-04", "path": "2026/11/04/index.html#paper-2611-03829", "reportDate": "4 November 2026", "searchText": "XMM-Newton and NuSTAR quasi-simultaneous follow-up of the very high energy flare of PKS 1725+123 in August 2025 L. Marcotulli, A. Gokus, L. Heckmann, E. Visentin, I. Jaroschewski, J. Otero-Santos, M. Böttcher, M. Zacharias, R. Takeishi, S. Nozaki, Y. Suda, I. Sadeh, P. Boorman, E. de Oña Wilhelmi, D. Berge 2611.03829 Post-flare X-ray spectra separate soft and hard components in PKS 1725+123, with limited simultaneous coverage. 2026-11-04 4 November 2026 Blazars Gamma rays X-rays #### XMM-Newton and NuSTAR quasi-simultaneous follow-up of the very high energy flare of PKS 1725+123 in August 2025\n\n**Authors:** L. Marcotulli, A. Gokus, L. Heckmann, E. Visentin, I. Jaroschewski, J. Otero-Santos, M. Böttcher, M. Zacharias, R. Takeishi, S. Nozaki, Y. Suda, I. Sadeh, P. Boorman, E. de Oña Wilhelmi and D. Berge. **Paper:** [arXiv:2611.03829v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03829v1), submitted 2 November 2026; first announced **4 November**. **Status:** submitted to A&A; X-ray observations and spectral interpretation.\n\n**Question and data.** Did the high synchrotron peak of this \\(z=0.586\\) flat-spectrum radio quasar persist after its August 2025 TeV flare? The study analyses approximately **50 ks of XMM-Newton** and **180 ks of NuSTAR** observations beginning on **22 August**, three days after the VHE announcement. Bayesian spectral comparisons use absorbed single- and double-power-law models, with Galactic absorption fixed and calibration constants allowed to vary. Solar-background filtering leaves only about an hour of useful overlap between the instruments: the coverage is quasi-simultaneous and samples the aftermath of the flare.\n\n**Results.** In the first four XMM-Newton time intervals, two power laws are strongly preferred, with differences in log evidence of roughly **16–58**. In the first interval their photon indices are **\\(1.79^{+0.09}_{-0.13}\\)** and **\\(2.87^{+0.30}_{-0.17}\\)**, with 68% highest-density intervals; their crossover is near **2 keV**. NuSTAR sees a comparatively stable hard slope, about **1.6–1.8**, while its 3–79 keV flux rises by approximately **50%** over four days.\n\nThe soft branch is consistent with a synchrotron tail retreating through the X-ray band, while the hard branch can be inverse Compton emission. Individual soft-index changes have limited significance, and a two-zone interpretation remains possible. The paper does not perform a broadband physical fit that selects leptonic over hadronic emission. Nor does the previously discussed positional connection with IceCube-201021A establish a neutrino association.\n\n##### History\n\n[Liu et al. (2026)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.21966) reported SVOM-VT observations of the same outburst, including minute-scale optical variability and a high synchrotron peak. Their broadband interpretation used a compact spine plus an extended sheath, allowing photons from one region to be scattered in the other. Thus, an unusually high synchrotron peak in this FSRQ and multizone modelling predate the present paper.\n\nThe new contribution is the soft-to-hard X-ray decomposition and its evolution during the post-flare interval. It supplies a useful test of that earlier SED interpretation, rather than a separate flare discovery. Connecting these observations to the TeV peak still requires time-dependent modelling that respects the observing gaps; a single simultaneous SED cannot be assumed across the whole interval."}, {"id": "paper-2611-05322", "arxiv": "2611.05322", "title": "Electromagnetic signatures of gradual magnetic dissipation in jets from supermassive black holes", "authors": "Stavros Dimitrakoudis, Maria Petropoulou, Stamatios I. Stathopoulos, Dimitrios Giannios", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Jets", "Magnetic reconnection", "Gamma rays"], "label": "Theoretical radiation calculation", "teaser": "Coupled jet segments enhance inverse-Compton radiation but smooth striped-jet models remain strongly synchrotron dominated.", "version": "v1", "listing_date": "2026-11-04", "path": "2026/11/04/index.html#paper-2611-05322", "reportDate": "4 November 2026", "searchText": "Electromagnetic signatures of gradual magnetic dissipation in jets from supermassive black holes Stavros Dimitrakoudis, Maria Petropoulou, Stamatios I. Stathopoulos, Dimitrios Giannios 2611.05322 Coupled jet segments enhance inverse-Compton radiation but smooth striped-jet models remain strongly synchrotron dominated. 2026-11-04 4 November 2026 Jets Magnetic reconnection Gamma rays #### Electromagnetic signatures of gradual magnetic dissipation in jets from supermassive black holes\n\n**Authors:** Stavros Dimitrakoudis, Maria Petropoulou, Stamatios I. Stathopoulos and Dimitrios Giannios. **Paper:** [arXiv:2611.05322v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05322v1), submitted 3 November 2026; first announced **4 November**. **Status:** submitted to A&A; theoretical radiation calculation.\n\n**Question and method.** Can smooth dissipation of alternating magnetic-field stripes produce the gamma-ray-bright SEDs of blazars? The authors couple an accelerating, conical striped jet to the LeHaMoC kinetic code, following synchrotron emission, self-absorption, inverse Compton scattering and pair production along many jet segments. An iterative calculation includes photons exchanged between segments. The flow contains electron–proton plasma, but the radiation calculation is leptonic; it does not predict a hadronic neutrino spectrum.\n\n**Results.** A representative model uses a **\\(10^8M_\\odot\\)** black hole, jet power **\\(5\\times10^{45}\\ \\mathrm{erg\\,s^{-1}}\\)**, asymptotic Lorentz factor **50**, opening angle **3°** and minimum stripe width **\\(400R_g\\)**. Dissipation peaks around **24 pc**. Intersegment illumination increases the inverse-Compton output by about **a factor of three**, yet the Compton-to-synchrotron luminosity ratio remains only about **\\(10^{-3}\\)** in the baseline model. Magnetic energy density dominates the radiating-electron energy density by roughly three orders of magnitude even near their closest approach.\n\nThe central finding is a difficulty for this particular smoothly dissipating, strongly magnetized jet: it does not naturally produce Compton-dominated blazar emission. External broad-line-region and torus photons, and variations of the tested parameters, do not generally remove that difficulty. This is neither an observational exclusion of reconnection nor a bound on all striped jets. The treatment is steady, uses approximate photon angular distributions, and omits full electron transport between zones in its default implementation; intermittent plasmoids or localized dissipation can behave differently.\n\n##### History\n\n[Giannios and Uzdensky](https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.09343) developed the link between a distribution of magnetic-stripe widths, gradual reconnection, bulk acceleration and a broad dissipation maximum. [Zhang and Giannios](https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.15189) then argued that a striped flow could accommodate large-scale jet emission and the blazar zone within a common model, including substantial external-Compton radiation for suitable conditions.\n\nThe new work revisits that radiative conclusion with explicitly local energy injection and coupled kinetic calculations along the jet. The authors identify the conversion from a dissipation profile to locally injected particle power as an important difference from earlier estimates. Photon exchange does enhance Compton emission, but in their calculation it cannot compensate for the low electron energy density. The disagreement is therefore about radiative implementation and physical assumptions, not evidence that magnetic energy cannot power jets."}, {"id": "paper-2611-05307", "arxiv": "2611.05307", "title": "Radiative relativistic reconnection powering the hard-state emission of X-ray binaries: synchrotron-driven pair loading, MeV tail, and seed photons", "authors": "Valeriia Rohoza, Maria Petropoulou, Hayk Hakobyan, Despina Karavola, Lorenzo Sironi", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Magnetic reconnection", "X-ray binaries", "Pair cascades"], "label": "Kinetic simulations and radiation modelling", "teaser": "Three-dimensional reconnection provides a possible coronal MeV tail and secondary-pair seed photons.", "version": "v1", "listing_date": "2026-11-04", "path": "2026/11/04/index.html#paper-2611-05307", "reportDate": "4 November 2026", "searchText": "Radiative relativistic reconnection powering the hard-state emission of X-ray binaries: synchrotron-driven pair loading, MeV tail, and seed photons Valeriia Rohoza, Maria Petropoulou, Hayk Hakobyan, Despina Karavola, Lorenzo Sironi 2611.05307 Three-dimensional reconnection provides a possible coronal MeV tail and secondary-pair seed photons. 2026-11-04 4 November 2026 Magnetic reconnection X-ray binaries Pair cascades #### Radiative relativistic reconnection powering the hard-state emission of X-ray binaries: synchrotron-driven pair loading, MeV tail, and seed photons\n\n**Authors:** Valeriia Rohoza, Maria Petropoulou, Hayk Hakobyan, Despina Karavola and Lorenzo Sironi. **Paper:** [arXiv:2611.05307v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05307v1), submitted 3 November 2026; first announced **4 November**. **Status:** submitted to ApJ; kinetic simulations and radiative modelling.\n\n**Question and method.** Can reconnection supply the hard X-rays, MeV tail, electron–positron pairs and soft seed photons of a black-hole corona within one physical picture? Three-dimensional particle-in-cell simulations follow radiatively cooled, magnetically dominated pair plasma. A separate kinetic radiation calculation propagates synchrotron and Compton photons through a compact corona and includes photon–photon pair production.\n\n**Results.** In the simulated configuration, about **40%** of the incoming magnetic power goes into inverse-Compton radiation and **10–20%** into synchrotron radiation. Particles escaping the flux ropes can reach energies inaccessible to the confined population and generate a synchrotron MeV tail. For a Cyg X-1-inspired example with **\\(B=10^8\\ \\mathrm G\\)**, radius **\\(R=2.5\\times10^6\\ \\mathrm{cm}\\)** and injected synchrotron/Compton luminosity ratio **0.5**, photon–photon absorption becomes important near **1.5 MeV**. The escaping tail has approximately **\\(\\epsilon L_\\epsilon\\propto\\epsilon^{-1.2}\\)**. Secondary pairs radiate near **0.13 keV**, supplying a substantial soft-photon population.\n\nThese numbers are simulation outputs and chosen physical examples, not fitted confidence intervals. The PIC runs use magnetization **\\(\\sigma=10\\)**, far below the value estimated for the illustrative real corona. Pair creation is handled in post-processing rather than fed back self-consistently into the reconnection simulation; the homogeneous radiation model also omits pair annihilation. The study establishes a plausible mechanism, not a unique observational identification of the corona or a statistically preferred fit over jet-origin MeV models.\n\n##### History\n\n[Sridhar, Sironi and Beloborodov (2021)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.00263) showed that Comptonization by reconnection plasmoids can reproduce a hard-state spectrum peaking near **100 keV**. Their two-dimensional calculations emphasized bulk motions in a cooled plasmoid chain. The new study adds a three-dimensional route to much more energetic particles and examines how their synchrotron photons feed pair production and seed-photon supply.\n\nSynchrotron seed photons and self-absorption as a thermalizing “boiler” are older ideas: [Poutanen and Vurm (2009)](https://doi.org/10.1088/0004-637X/690/2/L97) solved coupled photon/electron kinetic equations and modelled hard-state spectra without requiring disk feedback. Their particular SSC fits preferred relatively weak magnetization. The present proposal changes the acceleration mechanism and attributes much of the soft synchrotron component to secondary pairs in a strongly magnetized corona. A fully coupled calculation is still needed to determine whether this feedback reaches a stable state with the required optical depth."}, {"id": "paper-2611-04677", "arxiv": "2611.04677", "title": "Multimuon Events from Cosmic Rays with the ALICE Detector", "authors": "Mario Sitta, for the ALICE Collaboration", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Muon bundles", "Hadronic interactions"], "label": "Proceedings · previously published measurement", "teaser": "ALICE proceedings summarize Run-2 muon bundles and their dependence on primary composition and hadronic modelling.", "version": "v1", "listing_date": "2026-11-04", "path": "2026/11/04/index.html#paper-2611-04677", "reportDate": "4 November 2026", "searchText": "Multimuon Events from Cosmic Rays with the ALICE Detector Mario Sitta, for the ALICE Collaboration 2611.04677 ALICE proceedings summarize Run-2 muon bundles and their dependence on primary composition and hadronic modelling. 2026-11-04 4 November 2026 Cosmic rays Muon bundles Hadronic interactions #### Multimuon Events from Cosmic Rays with the ALICE Detector\n\n**Author:** Mario Sitta, for the ALICE Collaboration. **Paper:** [arXiv:2611.04677v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04677v1), submitted 3 November 2026; first announced **4 November**. **Status:** ISVHECRI 2026 conference proceedings, summarizing an already published measurement; not a new independent data set.\n\n**Question and data.** Can contemporary hadronic-interaction models reproduce underground muon bundles, and what does the comparison imply for the primary cosmic-ray composition? ALICE accumulated **62.5 live days** during 2015–2018, recording approximately **165 million** events with a reconstructed muon and **15,702** events with at least five muons. The overburden imposes a vertical muon threshold near **16 GeV**. Detector-level multiplicity distributions are compared with CORSIKA showers using QGSJET-II-04, EPOS-LHC and SIBYLL 2.3d, with pure proton and pure iron as limiting compositions.\n\n**Results.** Thirteen events contain more than **100 muons**, corresponding to a rate of **\\((2.4\\pm0.7)\\times10^{-6}\\ \\mathrm{Hz}\\)**; the uncertainty combines statistical and systematic contributions. The largest bundle contains **287 reconstructed muons**. Simulations associate these high-multiplicity events predominantly with primaries around **\\(10^{17}\\ \\mathrm{eV}\\)**. Iron predictions from QGSJET-II-04 and SIBYLL 2.3d are compatible with the rate, while EPOS-LHC is low by more than about two standard deviations. QGSJET-II-04 gives the best overall description across the lower/intermediate multiplicities studied.\n\nThe result favours a heavy composition within these model comparisons. It is not a measurement of a pure-iron flux: composition and hadronic modelling remain entangled, and thirteen extreme events provide limited statistical discrimination. These underground muons and primary energies also differ from those driving the highest-energy air-shower muon discrepancy.\n\n##### History\n\nThe underlying Run-2 analysis is [ALICE, arXiv:2410.17771](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.17771), published in [JCAP 04 (2025) 009](https://doi.org/10.1088/1475-7516/2025/04/009). It already reported the same exposure, multiplicity sample, high-multiplicity rate and hadronic-model comparisons. The November preprint is a new arXiv entry conveying those results at ISVHECRI, rather than a new resolution of the muon puzzle.\n\nThe important baseline is a comparison performed at reconstructed detector level, including overburden and tracking, rather than directly comparing an observed muon count with a ground-level shower prediction. The proceedings offer a concise account of this complementary hadronic test; improved composition inference requires both larger samples and control of interaction-model differences."}, {"id": "paper-2611-05123", "arxiv": "2611.05123", "title": "First application of the optimised next neighbour cleaning to data from imaging atmospheric Cherenkov telescopes", "authors": "Maria Kherlakian, Gernot Maier", "primary_category": "astro-ph.IM", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Cherenkov telescopes", "Instrumentation"], "label": "Instrument-analysis method", "teaser": "Charge–time image cleaning lowers the VERITAS threshold, with gains that depend on retrained background rejection.", "version": "v1", "listing_date": "2026-11-04", "path": "2026/11/04/index.html#paper-2611-05123", "reportDate": "4 November 2026", "searchText": "First application of the optimised next neighbour cleaning to data from imaging atmospheric Cherenkov telescopes Maria Kherlakian, Gernot Maier 2611.05123 Charge–time image cleaning lowers the VERITAS threshold, with gains that depend on retrained background rejection. 2026-11-04 4 November 2026 Gamma rays Cherenkov telescopes Instrumentation #### First application of the optimised next neighbour cleaning to data from imaging atmospheric Cherenkov telescopes\n\n**Authors:** Maria Kherlakian and Gernot Maier. **Paper:** [arXiv:2611.05123v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05123v1), submitted 3 November 2026; first announced **4 November**. **Status:** accepted by A&A; instrument-analysis method validated on VERITAS data.\n\n**Question and method.** How much faint shower information can be retained by using pixel timing as well as charge? Optimised next-neighbour cleaning selects adjacent groups of two to four pixels using charge–coincidence-time contours tied to an allowed accidental-noise rate. The analysis compares it with standard charge-threshold cleaning in simulations spanning zenith angle and night-sky background, and in **121 hours** of Crab observations. Gamma/hadron classifiers are trained separately for the two cleaning methods.\n\n**Results.** Depending on observing conditions, the simulated energy threshold—defined at **10% of maximum effective area**—falls by **5.1–22.3%**. In a **19.8-hour** Crab comparison using boosted-decision-tree selection, the gamma-ray excess rises from about **13,400 to 15,500 events**, or roughly **16%**, while detection significance changes from **189.1σ to 194.6σ**. The lowest reconstructed energy represented in that comparison falls from about **90 to 71 GeV**.\n\nThe gain is not uniform across analysis choices. Keeping simple box cuts increases residual background enough to reduce significance by about **12%**, despite retaining more gamma rays. Angular resolution can worsen at low energies, and the very large effective-area ratios occur near threshold where the original acceptance is small. Consequently, a threshold reduction or an excess-count ratio should not be advertised as the same fractional improvement in point-source sensitivity. Recovered spectra are consistent within the stated simulation/calibration limitations.\n\n##### History\n\n[Shayduk (2013)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.4939) introduced this charge–time neighbour optimization and tested it with CTA simulations. The principle is that short, locally coherent Cherenkov flashes can be separated from night-sky and detector noise without enforcing an unnecessarily restrictive time window across the entire image.\n\nThe new contribution is its detailed application to an operating telescope array, including real source data, reconstruction and retrained background rejection. It is not the invention of timing-assisted image cleaning. The mixed outcomes with box cuts and machine learning make the practical lesson clear: retaining additional pixels changes both signal and background populations, so the downstream classifier and instrument response must be recalibrated together."}, {"id": "paper-2611-05170", "arxiv": "2611.05170", "title": "Constraining tau-sterile neutrino mixing with high-energy astrophysical neutrinos", "authors": "Mauricio Bustamante, Qinrui Liu", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Sterile neutrinos", "Flavour"], "label": "Constraint and conditional forecasts", "teaser": "Astrophysical flavour data provide a weak tau–sterile bound whose source and spectral assumptions are decisive.", "version": "v1", "listing_date": "2026-11-04", "path": "2026/11/04/index.html#paper-2611-05170", "reportDate": "4 November 2026", "searchText": "Constraining tau-sterile neutrino mixing with high-energy astrophysical neutrinos Mauricio Bustamante, Qinrui Liu 2611.05170 Astrophysical flavour data provide a weak tau–sterile bound whose source and spectral assumptions are decisive. 2026-11-04 4 November 2026 Neutrinos Sterile neutrinos Flavour #### Constraining tau-sterile neutrino mixing with high-energy astrophysical neutrinos\n\n**Authors:** Mauricio Bustamante and Qinrui Liu. **Paper:** [arXiv:2611.05170v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05170v1), submitted 3 November 2026; first announced **4 November**. **Status:** preprint; reanalysis of a measured flavour likelihood plus future forecasts.\n\n**Question and method.** Can the flavour composition of cosmic neutrinos constrain the poorly tested tau component of a sterile mass eigenstate? The baseline is a unitary 3+1 model with **\\(U_{e4}=U_{\\mu4}=0\\)**, while \\(u=|U_{\\tau4}|^2\\) varies. Oscillations are averaged over cosmic baselines. The unknown source electron fraction is profiled over, with the source composition restricted to \\((f_e,1-f_e,0,0)\\); standard oscillation parameters have NuFIT-based constraints. In this limit the active-normalized tau fraction is\n\n\\[\nf_{\\tau,\\oplus}=\\frac{(1-u)f^{(3\\nu)}_{\\tau,\\oplus}}\n{1-u f^{(3\\nu)}_{\\tau,\\oplus}}.\n\\]\n\n**Current result.** Using the IceCube **11.4-year**, **9,888-event**, **5 TeV–10 PeV** medium-energy starting-event flavour analysis, the paper obtains **\\(u<0.88\\) at 90% confidence**, using a broken-power-law astrophysical spectrum and \\(\\Delta\\chi^2=2.71\\). It finds no evidence for mixing. The bound becomes **0.65** with a single-power-law spectrum, illustrating its spectral dependence. Allowing a source tau fraction as small as **5% removes the current bound**.\n\nThe mass-splitting independence applies only when oscillations are averaged, approximately \\(\\Delta m^2_{41}\\gg8\\times10^{-17}\\ \\mathrm{eV^2}(E/\\mathrm{PeV})(\\mathrm{Gpc}/L)\\). Earth-matter effects are neglected in the baseline and can matter in part of the eV-scale range. Forecasts using assumed future detector volumes and operation dates give limits of **0.29/0.24 in 2040** and **0.14/0.12 in 2050** for pion-decay/muon-damped sources. These are conditional projections, not measurements or guaranteed detector performance.\n\n##### History\n\nSterile-neutrino effects on astrophysical flavour ratios were already explored by [Brdar, Kopp and Wang (2017)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.04598), including the possibility of altered source production. The present advance is a quantitative tau-only limit using a substantially improved IceCube flavour measurement, with explicit source and spectral tests.\n\nIt does **not** improve the strongest terrestrial constraints in their applicable mass ranges. [IceCube DeepCore's 7.5-year analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.01314) used 5–150 GeV atmospheric events and obtained **\\(|U_{\\tau4}|^2<0.0574\\)** at 90% confidence for \\(\\Delta m^2_{41}\\gtrsim1\\ \\mathrm{eV^2}\\), allowing muon mixing and nuisance parameters to vary. [ORCA6](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07360) obtained **0.076** at 90% confidence at \\(\\Delta m^2_{41}=1\\ \\mathrm{eV^2}\\), profiling the other mixing element; [NOvA's dual-baseline search](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.08900) covers complementary smaller splittings. These conditions differ from the tau-only astrophysical fit.\n\n[Hagstotz et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.02289) found a marginalized 95% cosmological bound **\\(\\log_{10}|U_{\\tau4}|^2<-3.18\\)** for their logarithmic mass-splitting prior on \\(10^{-2}\\)–\\(10^2\\ \\mathrm{eV^2}\\), using CMB+BAO, standard sterile production and an extended ΛCDM model, with logarithmic priors on each mixing between \\(10^{-6}\\) and \\(10^{-1}\\). That much stronger result depends on thermal history and priors. The astrophysical method's value is its different baseline, energy and assumptions, not a competitive present-day upper limit."}, {"id": "paper-2611-03751", "arxiv": "2611.03751", "title": "Electroweak Constraints on Nuclear Neutron Distributions and the Low-Energy Weak Mixing Angle", "authors": "Xu-Run Huang, Tong-Gang Yue, Zhen Zhang, Lie-Wen Chen", "primary_category": "nucl-th", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "CEvNS", "Nuclear structure"], "label": "Nuclear-model inference and electroweak fit", "teaser": "Electron-scattering information constrains model-dependent nuclear responses for a joint COHERENT weak-angle fit.", "version": "v1", "listing_date": "2026-11-04", "path": "2026/11/04/index.html#paper-2611-03751", "reportDate": "4 November 2026", "searchText": "Electroweak Constraints on Nuclear Neutron Distributions and the Low-Energy Weak Mixing Angle Xu-Run Huang, Tong-Gang Yue, Zhen Zhang, Lie-Wen Chen 2611.03751 Electron-scattering information constrains model-dependent nuclear responses for a joint COHERENT weak-angle fit. 2026-11-04 4 November 2026 Neutrinos CEvNS Nuclear structure #### Electroweak Constraints on Nuclear Neutron Distributions and the Low-Energy Weak Mixing Angle\n\n**Authors:** Xu-Run Huang, Tong-Gang Yue, Zhen Zhang and Lie-Wen Chen. **Paper:** [arXiv:2611.03751v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03751v1), submitted 2 November 2026; first announced **4 November**. **Status:** preprint; nuclear-model inference and CEνNS reanalysis. The arXiv record states that supplemental material will accompany the published version; it is not included in this v1.\n\n**Question and method.** Can parity-violating electron scattering constrain the nuclear response sufficiently to sharpen weak-interaction tests with coherent elastic neutrino–nucleus scattering? An extended Skyrme energy-density-functional family maps the measured CREX and PREX-II charge–weak form-factor differences in calcium and lead onto neutron distributions in other nuclei. Regression and experimental uncertainties are propagated, and the inferred form factors enter a joint fit to COHERENT **CsI, Ge and Ar** data.\n\n**Results.** The inferred neutron rms radii include **3.417 fm for argon**, **4.118 fm for germanium-74**, **4.879 fm for xenon-132** and **4.880 fm for cesium-133**, with quoted relative uncertainties of approximately **0.46%, 0.60%, 0.72% and 0.68%**, respectively. These are conditional nuclear-model inferences, not independent subpercent measurements of each nucleus. Varying the symmetry-energy slope within the selected functional family gives only a small residual spread; that does not establish equally small uncertainty across all possible nuclear models.\n\nThe combined CEνNS extraction yields **\\(\\sin^2\\theta_W=0.231^{+0.016}_{-0.015}\\)** at one standard deviation, at momentum transfer of order **50 MeV**, with electroweak radiative corrections. It agrees with the Standard Model. The new germanium information is an important part of the precision gain. The available article does not supply its promised supplemental detail, so the full nuisance-parameter implementation and all auxiliary nuclear fits cannot yet be independently audited from v1.\n\n##### History\n\n[Piekarewicz (2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.03645) already used the calcium–argon correlation and CREX to infer an argon **weak-charge radius**, **\\(3.452\\pm0.028\\,\\mathrm{(stat)}\\pm0.022\\,\\mathrm{(syst)}\\ \\mathrm{fm}\\)**. That is not the same observable as a neutron point radius, so the numerical difference from 3.417 fm is not a discrepancy. The November paper extends this strategy to more targets within a different nuclear framework. The [CREX/PREX modelling tension](https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.03134) remains a reason to test functional dependence rather than treating one regression family as a model-independent error budget.\n\nEarlier [combined germanium work](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.27121) profiled the Lindhard quenching parameter and obtained **\\(0.237^{+0.027}_{-0.022}\\)** at 1σ using a Klein–Nystrand form factor and radiative corrections. [CONUS+ run 2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37318) reported **\\(0.225\\pm0.022\\)** at roughly **10 MeV**. These results use different data and nuclear/quenching assumptions; their error-bar ratios are not a clean measure of the gain due solely to the new nuclear priors. Electron-sector precision tests remain more precise, while CEνNS supplies a complementary neutrino-sector test and better response modelling for dark-matter experiments."}, {"id": "paper-2611-03904", "arxiv": "2611.03904", "title": "Constraints on a Long-Lived X17 Vector Boson with FASER", "authors": "FASER Collaboration", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["BSM", "Dark sectors", "Collider searches"], "label": "Model-dependent exclusion", "teaser": "FASER tests a remaining long-lived X17 window under specified vector couplings and visible branching ratio.", "version": "v1", "listing_date": "2026-11-04", "path": "2026/11/04/index.html#paper-2611-03904", "reportDate": "4 November 2026", "searchText": "Constraints on a Long-Lived X17 Vector Boson with FASER FASER Collaboration 2611.03904 FASER tests a remaining long-lived X17 window under specified vector couplings and visible branching ratio. 2026-11-04 4 November 2026 BSM Dark sectors Collider searches #### Constraints on a Long-Lived X17 Vector Boson with FASER\n\n**Authors:** FASER Collaboration. **Paper:** [arXiv:2611.03904v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03904v1), submitted 2 November 2026; first announced **4 November**. **Status:** preprint; reinterpretation of existing displaced-decay searches.\n\n**Question and data.** Does a long-lived vector interpretation of the nuclear X17 anomaly survive FASER's forward LHC searches? The analysis combines orthogonal selections from its **27 fb⁻¹ dark-photon** and **57.7 fb⁻¹ axion-like-particle** searches at **13.6 TeV**. The former contains zero candidates against **\\(0.0023\\pm0.0023\\)** expected background; the latter has one against **\\(0.44\\pm0.39\\)**. The integrated luminosities overlap in time and must not simply be added.\n\n**Model and result.** Fermion couplings are defined by \\(e\\varepsilon_f X_\\mu\\bar f\\gamma^\\mu f\\). In the principal benchmark, the proton coupling is zero, the neutron coupling is fixed to **\\(\\varepsilon_n=0.01\\)**, neutrino couplings vanish and **\\(\\mathrm{Br}(X\\to e^+e^-)=1\\)**. At **\\(m_X=17\\ \\mathrm{MeV}\\)**, a profiled **90% CLs** analysis excludes approximately\n\n\\[\n2\\times10^{-8}<\\varepsilon_e<3\\times10^{-4}.\n\\]\n\nThis covers the remaining low-electron-coupling window in that benchmark. The larger-coupling region decays too promptly for the same reach. Production includes meson decays and nucleon bremsstrahlung, with a substantial uncertainty from the off-shell treatment. A zero static proton coupling does not eliminate finite-momentum-transfer bremsstrahlung. Changing the fixed neutron coupling, decay branching ratio or vector structure changes the exclusion; this is not a model-independent rejection of every X17 interpretation.\n\n##### History\n\nThe nuclear anomaly concerns unexpected electron–positron angular distributions, which motivated a roughly 17 MeV mediator. The key recent development was [Celentano, Marini and Marsicano's E141 reanalysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.22102), which treated production and experimental acceptance more precisely. In its revised analysis, a remaining interval around **\\(6.5\\times10^{-5}\\lesssim\\varepsilon_e\\lesssim1.1\\times10^{-4}\\)** survived electron-coupling searches. FASER's proton-collision production supplies a complementary test, conditional on its specified quark couplings.\n\n[MEG II](https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.07994) found no significant X17 evidence in excited-beryllium decays and placed 90% limits on the ratios of X17 to photon emission, **\\(R_{18.1}<1.2\\times10^{-5}\\)** and **\\(R_{17.6}<1.8\\times10^{-6}\\)** across **16.5–17.1 MeV**. Those nuclear-transition limits are not directly comparable to a FASER electron-coupling interval without a production model. Together these studies constrain interpretations of an unresolved anomaly; none of the FASER candidates constitutes an X17 detection."}, {"id": "paper-2611-04904", "arxiv": "2611.04904", "title": "Search for heavy neutral leptons in top quark decays with same-sign, same-flavor dilepton final states in proton-proton collisions at √s = 13 TeV", "authors": "CMS Collaboration", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Heavy neutral leptons", "Collider searches"], "label": "Collider search", "teaser": "Same-sign dileptons in top decays constrain prompt Majorana heavy neutral leptons with single-flavour mixing.", "version": "v1", "listing_date": "2026-11-04", "path": "2026/11/04/index.html#paper-2611-04904", "reportDate": "4 November 2026", "searchText": "Search for heavy neutral leptons in top quark decays with same-sign, same-flavor dilepton final states in proton-proton collisions at √s = 13 TeV CMS Collaboration 2611.04904 Same-sign dileptons in top decays constrain prompt Majorana heavy neutral leptons with single-flavour mixing. 2026-11-04 4 November 2026 Neutrinos Heavy neutral leptons Collider searches #### Search for heavy neutral leptons in top quark decays with same-sign, same-flavor dilepton final states in proton-proton collisions at √s = 13 TeV\n\n**Authors:** CMS Collaboration. **Paper:** [arXiv:2611.04904v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04904v1), submitted 3 November 2026; first announced **4 November**. **Status:** submitted to JHEP; collider search and exclusion limits.\n\n**Question and method.** Can top-pair events reveal a Majorana heavy neutral lepton through \\(t\\to bW\\to b\\ell N\\), followed by a same-sign lepton from the decay of \\(N\\)? The search uses **138 fb⁻¹** of CMS data at **13 TeV**, selecting same-sign electron or muon pairs with jets and a b tag. Boosted decision trees separate signal from top-related backgrounds; nonprompt leptons are constrained from data. Rate and shape systematics enter a simultaneous profile-likelihood fit across the data-taking years.\n\n**Results and assumptions.** No significant excess is found. For a **prompt Majorana** state with **only one flavour mixing nonzero**, the observed **95% CLs** branching-fraction limits span **\\(1.1\\times10^{-5}\\)–\\(4.8\\times10^{-5}\\)** for \\(t\\to beN\\) and **\\(3.7\\times10^{-6}\\)–\\(2.4\\times10^{-5}\\)** for \\(t\\to b\\mu N\\), over the mass hypotheses between **20 and 100 GeV** that are tested. The corresponding mixing limits range from **\\(1.6\\times10^{-4}\\) to 0.11** for \\(|V_{eN}|^2\\), and **\\(4.2\\times10^{-5}\\) to 0.039** for \\(|V_{\\mu N}|^2\\).\n\nThese are ranges along mass-dependent curves, not uniform bounds across the entire interval. The analysis omits **75–85 GeV**, where kinematics near the W mass strongly reduce acceptance; interpolation across that gap would be misleading. Prompt-decay and single-flavour assumptions also prevent direct application to displaced particles, Dirac states or arbitrary multi-flavour models. The sensitivity is limited by nonprompt-lepton backgrounds and by the small fraction of signal events in which all decay jets can be reconstructed.\n\n##### History\n\n[ATLAS's earlier top-decay search](https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.05000), using **140 fb⁻¹**, established this channel over **15–75 GeV**. Its best observed electron and muon mixing limits were **\\(2.0\\times10^{-4}\\)** and **\\(6.8\\times10^{-5}\\)** at 95% confidence. Those are minima of mass-dependent curves; comparing only the minima does not define a universal improvement factor. CMS provides an independent implementation and extends the tested mass range above the W threshold.\n\nThe stronger direct-production baseline is [CMS's trilepton search](https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.00100), which examined **10 GeV–1.5 TeV** and considered separate Majorana and Dirac hypotheses. Below the W mass it is generally more sensitive to the same single-flavour mixing. The top channel therefore broadens the experimental test and supplies a complementary production environment, rather than setting the strongest overall heavy-neutral-lepton limit."}, {"id": "paper-2611-04890", "arxiv": "2611.04890", "title": "DAMA/LIBRA dark matter signal: a global analysis", "authors": "Matti Heikinheimo, Sebastian Sassi, Alex Stendahl, Kimmo Tuominen, Niklas Zimmermann", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Direct detection", "Annual modulation"], "label": "Multi-experiment reanalysis", "teaser": "A broader EFT scan reinforces DAMA tension, with important likelihood, halo and covariance limitations.", "version": "v1", "listing_date": "2026-11-04", "path": "2026/11/04/index.html#paper-2611-04890", "reportDate": "4 November 2026", "searchText": "DAMA/LIBRA dark matter signal: a global analysis Matti Heikinheimo, Sebastian Sassi, Alex Stendahl, Kimmo Tuominen, Niklas Zimmermann 2611.04890 A broader EFT scan reinforces DAMA tension, with important likelihood, halo and covariance limitations. 2026-11-04 4 November 2026 Dark matter Direct detection Annual modulation #### DAMA/LIBRA dark matter signal: a global analysis\n\n**Authors:** Matti Heikinheimo, Sebastian Sassi, Alex Stendahl, Kimmo Tuominen and Niklas Zimmermann. **Paper:** [arXiv:2611.04890v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04890v1), submitted 3 November 2026; first announced **4 November**. **Status:** preprint; multi-experiment phenomenological reanalysis.\n\n**Question and method.** Can the DAMA/LIBRA annual modulation be reconciled with xenon, fluorine, iodine and germanium null results by broadening the interaction and halo model? The study scans nonrelativistic effective operators in separate leading-, next-to-leading- and next-to-next-to-leading-order groups, allows inelastic scattering and variable Na/I quenching, and compares with LZ, PICO and CDMSlite. Halo freedom consists of a colder rescaling of a Maxwellian distribution: the velocity dispersion is \\(\\alpha\\,220/\\sqrt2\\ \\mathrm{km\\,s^{-1}}\\), with **\\(0.07\\leq\\alpha\\leq1\\)** and escape speed fixed to **544 km s⁻¹**. This is not an arbitrary halo reconstruction.\n\n**Results.** Within the tested families, fitting DAMA tends to overproduce events in other detectors. For example, one next-to-leading-order fit using the reconstructed-count likelihood predicts approximately **316 LZ events** where the background expectation used in that analysis is **18**, and approximately **877 PICO-CF₃I events** in its zero-background treatment. These are illustrative incompatible model predictions, not measured excesses.\n\nThe statistical treatment is a central limitation. A Skellam likelihood built from reconstructed DAMA counts gives errors smaller than DAMA's published errors and overweights the modulation data. A Gaussian likelihood using the published errors instead drives the allowed dark-matter signal towards zero. Modulation amplitudes and time residuals are treated as independent despite unavailable covariance information. The paper therefore reinforces tension within its specified model space, but its nominal discrepancy significance should not be interpreted as a universal exclusion of every dark-matter explanation. Operator groups are fitted separately and the full halo/model space is not exhausted.\n\n##### History\n\n[Catena, Ibarra and Wild (2016)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.04074) already tested general Galilean-invariant **elastic** interactions, including operator interference and several target materials, and found strong tension under the standard halo model. The November study revisits the problem with newer null data and additional inelastic, quenching and cold-halo freedom; the general observation that changing a single operator rarely reconciles all targets is not new.\n\nAn especially useful independent comparison comes from the same detector material. The [ANAIS-112/COSINE-100 combined study](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.19559) reported, for a simple combination of their six-year results, a **1–6 keV** modulation amplitude of **\\(0.0005\\pm0.0019\\ \\mathrm{counts\\,day^{-1}\\,kg^{-1}\\,keV^{-1}}\\)** with one-year period and phase fixed to 2 June. It differs from the DAMA amplitude by **4.7σ** in that comparison. This tests the modulation more directly than translating between xenon and NaI interaction models, although background treatment, calibration and quenching remain relevant to physical interpretation. It is not the same statistic as the global EFT fit."}, {"id": "paper-2611-05367", "arxiv": "2611.05367", "title": "SBi3PCF: cosmology with Dark Energy Survey Year 3 cosmic shear", "authors": "David Gebauer, Anik Halder, Zhengyangguang Gong, Cora Uhlemann", "primary_category": "astro-ph.CO", "tags": ["Cosmology", "Weak lensing", "Dark energy"], "label": "Cosmological data analysis", "teaser": "An integrated three-point shear statistic adds information to DES Y3 cosmology with simulation-dependent inference.", "version": "v1", "listing_date": "2026-11-04", "path": "2026/11/04/index.html#paper-2611-05367", "reportDate": "4 November 2026", "searchText": "SBi3PCF: cosmology with Dark Energy Survey Year 3 cosmic shear David Gebauer, Anik Halder, Zhengyangguang Gong, Cora Uhlemann 2611.05367 An integrated three-point shear statistic adds information to DES Y3 cosmology with simulation-dependent inference. 2026-11-04 4 November 2026 Cosmology Weak lensing Dark energy #### SBi3PCF: cosmology with Dark Energy Survey Year 3 cosmic shear\n\n**Authors:** David Gebauer, Anik Halder, Zhengyangguang Gong and Cora Uhlemann. **Paper:** [arXiv:2611.05367v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05367v1), submitted 3 November 2026; first announced **4 November**. **Status:** preprint; cosmological inference from observed shear data.\n\n**Question and method.** How much information beyond two-point cosmic shear can be extracted from the correlation between aperture mass and locally measured shear correlations? The study applies an integrated three-point statistic to the **DES Year 3** catalogue, containing approximately **100 million galaxies over 4,143 deg²**. It compresses a **1,440-component** joint data vector into 17 components and learns its likelihood from CosmoGrid simulations. The analysis fits flat **wCDM**, with baryonic-feedback and intrinsic-alignment parameters and marginalized shear/photo-z uncertainties; the summed neutrino mass is fixed to **0.06 eV**.\n\n**Results.** The joint posterior gives\n\n\\[\n\\Omega_m=0.272^{+0.038}_{-0.033},\\qquad\nS_8=0.784^{+0.024}_{-0.021},\\qquad\nw=-1.04^{+0.25}_{-0.19},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(S_8=\\sigma_8\\sqrt{\\Omega_m/0.3}\\) and the intervals are marginalized **16th–84th percentiles**. Relative to its own two-point-only analysis, the joint \\((\\Omega_m,S_8,w)\\) figure of merit improves by **1.59**. This is a result from survey data, rather than the forecast of that improvement.\n\nThe posterior depends on the simulation domain and priors, including **\\(-2<w<-1/3\\)**, and on a restricted intrinsic-alignment model. Coverage tests on held-out simulations pass, but validation against an independent N-body implementation is absent. Full-data posterior-predictive checks give a marginal **p = 0.021**, and a test omitting the third redshift bin gives **p = 0.001**. The authors trace these difficulties to nuisance-related compressed directions and base their consistency criterion on cosmological components, which pass. Removing that bin shifts \\(S_8\\) by about 2.2 times the original posterior half-width; this is not itself a 2.2σ inconsistency test. These qualifications matter when interpreting the modest differences from other cosmological analyses.\n\n##### History\n\n[Secco et al.'s DES Y3 two-point analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.13544) established the cosmic-shear baseline and its sensitivity to intrinsic alignments, baryons and scale cuts. [Gomes et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.14018) subsequently combined two-point shear with aperture-mass skewness, obtaining **\\(S_8=0.749^{+0.027}_{-0.026}\\)** and **\\(w=-1.39\\pm0.31\\)** in their wCDM analysis. These use the same underlying DES catalogue, different third-order summaries and different modelling choices; their posteriors are correlated and cannot be treated as independent measurements in a simple tension calculation.\n\n[The first SBi3PCF paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.13805) developed and tested the likelihood-learning pipeline on simulations, showing why the integrated three-point likelihood need not be Gaussian. The November paper advances from that validation to real-data cosmology. It demonstrates additional non-Gaussian information without establishing evolving dark energy or a resolution of the clustering-amplitude discrepancy. Its quoted comparison with Planck+BAO is a multidimensional wCDM comparison, not a standalone significance for an \\(S_8\\) discrepancy."}, {"id": "paper-2611-04596", "arxiv": "2611.04596", "title": "A New Precise Measurement of the $e^+e^-\\to\\pi^+\\pi^-(\\gamma)$ Cross Section with the Initial-State Radiation Method at BaBar", "authors": "J. P. Lees et al. (BaBar Collaboration)", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["Particle physics", "Muon g-2", "Hadronic vacuum polarization"], "label": "Experimental measurement", "teaser": "Angular separation of ISR pion and muon pairs provides a new BaBar two-pion cross section and a systematic cross-check for hadronic vacuum polarization.", "version": "v1", "listing_date": "2026-11-04", "companion_arxiv": "2611.04621", "path": "2026/11/04/index.html#paper-2611-04596", "reportDate": "4 November 2026", "searchText": "A New Precise Measurement of the $e^+e^-\\to\\pi^+\\pi^-(\\gamma)$ Cross Section with the Initial-State Radiation Method at BaBar J. P. Lees et al. (BaBar Collaboration) 2611.04596 Angular separation of ISR pion and muon pairs provides a new BaBar two-pion cross section and a systematic cross-check for hadronic vacuum polarization. 2026-11-04 4 November 2026 Particle physics Muon g-2 Hadronic vacuum polarization #### A New Precise Measurement of the e⁺e⁻ → π⁺π⁻(γ) Cross Section with the Initial-State Radiation Method at BaBar\n\n**Authors:** J. P. Lees et al. (BaBar Collaboration). **Paper:** [arXiv:2611.04596v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04596v1), submitted 3 November 2026; first announced **4 November**. **Status:** preprint; experimental cross-section measurement. The [detailed companion, 2611.04621v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04621v1), reports the same measurement and is linked here rather than counted as a second independent result.\n\n**Question and method.** Can an alternative separation of pion and muon pairs clarify the inconsistent cross-section inputs to the Standard Model prediction for the muon magnetic anomaly? BaBar uses **460 fb⁻¹** collected near the Υ(4S), selecting initial-state-radiation events to measure the lower-energy two-pion cross section. Unlike its earlier particle-identification analysis, it statistically separates pions and muons through their different angular distributions. Their ratio cancels common luminosity, photon-efficiency and vacuum-polarization effects. Data-derived corrections, unfolding and bootstrap covariance propagation are applied before integrating the bare cross section, including final-state radiation.\n\n**Results.** Over **0.3–1.4 GeV**, the inferred leading-order two-pion hadronic-vacuum-polarization contribution is\n\n\\[\na_\\mu^{\\pi\\pi}=\\bigl(525.9\\pm3.5_{\\rm stat}\\pm3.8_{\\rm syst}\\bigr)\\times10^{-10}.\n\\]\n\nCombining the new and 2009 BaBar measurements, accounting for correlations and enlarging uncertainties where local disagreement warrants it, gives **\\((517.8\\pm2.4_{\\rm stat}\\pm3.3_{\\rm syst})\\times10^{-10}\\)** over the same interval. These are contributions to \\(a_\\mu=(g_\\mu-2)/2\\), not measurements of the complete anomaly. Near the ρ resonance the new cross section is about **2% higher** than BaBar's earlier result. In the shared **0.6–0.8832 GeV** comparison interval, the new integral is compatible with CMD-3 at **0.9σ**, but differs from KLOE by **4.2σ** and BESIII by **3.0σ**, using the paper's stated comparison uncertainties.\n\nThe alternative method provides a valuable systematic cross-check, but it loses statistical discrimination where muons overwhelm pions near threshold and at higher masses. The overall integral is not simply more precise because the luminosity doubled. The muon-to-QED validation agrees at **0.69% total precision**; it does not eliminate every relative pion/muon systematic. This one channel cannot by itself establish the significance of any full Standard Model–experiment discrepancy.\n\n##### History\n\n[BaBar's 2009 measurement](https://arxiv.org/abs/0908.3589) used **232 fb⁻¹**, an ISR pion/muon ratio and particle identification. It already reduced several common systematics, so the new ingredient is primarily angular separation, fuller data usage and treatment of additional radiation—not the ratio method itself. [KLOE's ratio measurement](https://arxiv.org/abs/1212.4524) supplied another independent implementation, while [CMD-3](https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.08834) used an energy scan and obtained a higher two-pion cross section than several earlier experiments.\n\nThe new BaBar result moves toward the higher CMD-3 normalization while leaving disagreement with lower measurements unresolved. Earlier published integrals often use different energy limits and must not be subtracted directly. The collaboration's matched-range comparisons and explicit correlation study are therefore more informative than a comparison of headline \\(a_\\mu^{\\pi\\pi}\\) numbers."}, {"id": "paper-2611-00205", "arxiv": "2611.00205", "title": "Lightest and heaviest nuclei as keys to the origin of ultrahigh energy cosmic rays", "authors": "Anna M. Suliga and Glennys R. Farrar", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "UHECRs", "Neutron-star mergers"], "label": "Source-model inference", "teaser": "Extra protons improve an Auger fit, but neither those protons nor permitted ultraheavy nuclei uniquely identify neutron-star mergers.", "path": "2026/11/03/index.html#paper-2611-00205", "reportDate": "3 November 2026", "searchText": "Lightest and heaviest nuclei as keys to the origin of ultrahigh energy cosmic rays Anna M. Suliga and Glennys R. Farrar 2611.00205 Extra protons improve an Auger fit, but neither those protons nor permitted ultraheavy nuclei uniquely identify neutron-star mergers. 2026-11-03 3 November 2026 Cosmic rays UHECRs Neutron-star mergers #### UHECR origins: a stronger case for an extra light component, not a unique identification of neutron-star mergers\n\n**Paper:** *Lightest and heaviest nuclei as keys to the origin of ultrahigh energy cosmic rays* — **Anna M. Suliga and Glennys R. Farrar**. [arXiv:2611.00205v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00205v1), [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.00205v1). Submitted **30 October 2026, 18:00:03 UTC**; **3 November listing**. Preprint, described as under review at *Science*. **Classification: source-model inference.**\n\nThe authors extend source processing and propagation to nuclei heavier than iron, fitting Auger's spectrum and composition with EPOS-LHC-R shower interpretation. A separate high-energy proton component improves the binary-neutron-star-evolution benchmark from **χ²/dof = 260.5/58 to 118.8/55**. The reported **7.6σ** comes from a rescaled χ² difference with three extra parameters; it is not a significance for identifying the astrophysical source class.\n\nThe preferred maximum rigidity is approximately **7.2 EV**, with a **563 K** source photon field. The best-fit **A = 80 injection number fraction is about 7% at fixed energy**. Figure 2 also permits a solution without nuclei heavier than iron within the authors' 1σ region: ultraheavy nuclei are **allowed, not detected**. The proton component supplies approximately **5% of the injected energy above 10 EeV** in the benchmark; this is a different fraction.\n\nThe fit assumes a common source population and prescribed evolution. Energy-scale and shower-depth shifts are discrete refits, not a marginalization over all systematics. The remaining reduced χ² of **2.16**, source geometry and air-shower modeling limit the interpretation.\n\n##### History\n\nThe central physical problem is how one source population could produce light particles below the ankle and progressively heavier composition above it. [Unger, Farrar and Anchordoqui (2015)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1505.02153) already showed how photon interactions and rigidity-dependent escape create a high-pass filter: lower-energy nuclei fragment before escape, while higher-energy nuclei survive. That mechanism did not require a unique source class.\n\nCrucially, [Muzio, Unger and Farrar (2019), §IV and Fig. 10](https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.06233v2) already found that an extra proton component could substantially improve Auger fits. The new paper updates the data and shower treatment, extends the nuclear network and develops the merger interpretation; the extra-component idea itself is established. [Farrar's merger proposal, published in 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.12004), supplied the physical motivation involving magnetized outflows and r-process material.\n\nAn independent comparison is [Zhang, Murase, Ekanger, Bhattacharya and Horiuchi](https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.17409), published in *PRL* on [7 May 2026](https://doi.org/10.1103/221m-gvs3). It propagated ultraheavy nuclei and considered **both mergers and collapsars**. Its injection fractions and allowed energy-generation ranges use different source assumptions and fit criteria, so they cannot be converted into a clean improvement factor here. **Assessment:** the new fit is an interesting test of the merger hypothesis, but neither its extra protons nor its permitted ultraheavy component establishes mergers uniquely. Its statistical calibration and competing source models remain important open tests."}, {"id": "paper-2611-00198", "arxiv": "2611.00198", "title": "Hidden Neutrino Active Galaxy Candidates: Prospects for Catalog and Stacking Searches with IceCube, IceCube-Gen2, and KM3NeT", "authors": "Mainak Mukhopadhyay, Jose Alonso Carpio, Heer Patel, Ali Kheirandish and Kohta Murase", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Active galaxies", "Multimessenger astronomy"], "label": "Catalog and forecast", "teaser": "An absorption-corrected AGN catalog supplies conditional detector forecasts, building on existing population evidence.", "path": "2026/11/03/index.html#paper-2611-00198", "reportDate": "3 November 2026", "searchText": "Hidden Neutrino Active Galaxy Candidates: Prospects for Catalog and Stacking Searches with IceCube, IceCube-Gen2, and KM3NeT Mainak Mukhopadhyay, Jose Alonso Carpio, Heer Patel, Ali Kheirandish and Kohta Murase 2611.00198 An absorption-corrected AGN catalog supplies conditional detector forecasts, building on existing population evidence. 2026-11-03 3 November 2026 Neutrinos Active galaxies Multimessenger astronomy #### X-ray-selected AGN: a practical neutrino target catalog, with conditional discovery forecasts\n\n**Paper:** *Hidden Neutrino Active Galaxy Candidates: Prospects for Catalog and Stacking Searches with IceCube, IceCube-Gen2, and KM3NeT* — **Mainak Mukhopadhyay, Jose Alonso Carpio, Heer Patel, Ali Kheirandish and Kohta Murase**. [arXiv:2611.00198v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00198v1), [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.00198v1). Submitted **30 October, 18:00:01 UTC**; **3 November listing**; submitted to *ApJ*. **Classification: catalog, emission modeling and forecast.**\n\nXHiNAC selects **28 AGN** using intrinsic **2–195 keV** fluxes, hard-X-ray surveys and NuSTAR-based absorption corrections. Its **10⁻⁷ GeV cm⁻² s⁻¹** flux threshold is a catalog cut, not a confidence limit. AMES calculations model cosmic-ray acceleration and interactions in compact coronae; turbulence, coronal radius and magnetic field define alternative benchmarks.\n\nFor favorable benchmarks, stacking sources other than NGC 1068 reaches nominal **5σ in roughly 5–10 years** with future detectors. The selected nearby objects supply only **about 1–3% of the modeled diffuse intensity at 10 TeV**; that is not a limit on all AGN.\n\nThese are counting forecasts using design/scaled effective areas, atmospheric backgrounds, an assumed **0.4°** angular resolution and one broad energy bin. They omit a full reconstructed-energy/direction likelihood and detector-systematic treatment. X-ray–neutrino scaling, absorption corrections and uncertain black-hole masses also affect rankings. The quoted times are conditional exposures, not scheduled discovery dates.\n\n##### History\n\nThe motivation is that an opaque AGN corona can convert proton energy into neutrinos while reprocessing its gamma rays. The observational anchor is [IceCube's 2022 NGC 1068 analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.09972), which reported **4.2σ** evidence in its predefined source search. That supports studying obscured AGN; it does not uniquely identify coronal acceleration.\n\nPopulation evidence already exists. The [13.1-year northern-sky analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.13403), published in 2026, obtained a **3.3σ binomial excess involving 11 X-ray-bright sources**, excluding NGC 1068. The [southern-sky starting-track analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.10208) found **6.7⁺⁴·⁰₋₃·₂ fitted signal events**, a **3.0σ** stacked excess under its disk-corona spectral model. Its strongest individual source, Circinus, had only **1.8σ after trials**. These are distinct analyses and statistics, not significance values to add.\n\nThe advance is therefore an absorption-corrected target selection and explicit detector forecasts, rather than the first evidence for an AGN neutrino population. The forecast significance should be recalculated with collaboration-level response models before interpreting it as a discovery probability."}, {"id": "paper-2611-03248", "arxiv": "2611.03248", "title": "A Multi-messenger Study of the Cygnus bubble", "authors": "Abhijit Roy, Giovanni Morlino, C Rishi, Jagdish C. Joshi, Soebur Razzaque and Neelam Panwar", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Gamma rays", "Neutrinos"], "label": "Transport fit and forecast", "teaser": "A collective-wind model confronts the Cygnus bubble spectrum and morphology, with poor absolute fit and disputed shock geometry.", "path": "2026/11/03/index.html#paper-2611-03248", "reportDate": "3 November 2026", "searchText": "A Multi-messenger Study of the Cygnus bubble Abhijit Roy, Giovanni Morlino, C Rishi, Jagdish C. Joshi, Soebur Razzaque and Neelam Panwar 2611.03248 A collective-wind model confronts the Cygnus bubble spectrum and morphology, with poor absolute fit and disputed shock geometry. 2026-11-03 3 November 2026 Cosmic rays Gamma rays Neutrinos #### Cygnus: can collective stellar winds account for the gamma-ray bubble?\n\n**Paper:** *A Multi-messenger Study of the Cygnus bubble* — **Abhijit Roy, Giovanni Morlino, C Rishi, Jagdish C. Joshi, Soebur Razzaque and Neelam Panwar**. [arXiv:2611.03248v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03248v1), [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.03248v1). Submitted **2 November, 14:57:56 UTC**; **3 November listing**; preprint prepared for *JCAP*. **Classification: transport-model fit and neutrino forecast.**\n\nThe model accelerates protons at a spherical collective-wind termination shock in Cygnus OB2, then propagates them through a three-dimensional gas model. Spectra from Fermi, HAWC, ARGO and LHAASO are combined with LHAASO radial profiles; Bohm, Kraichnan and Kolmogorov diffusion are compared.\n\nThe preferred Bohm benchmark has wind power **2.62 × 10³⁸ erg s⁻¹**, cosmic-ray efficiency **2.0%**, shock radius **26.0 pc**, bubble radius **185 pc** and acceleration scale **Emax = 3.28 PeV**. However, its combined **reduced χ² = 6.16**: being best among three choices does not make it an adequate overall fit. Aperture conversions, cloud positions and unresolved sources matter.\n\nFor **ten years**, the model predicts approximately **95 signal/530 background events** in IceCube and **58/338** in KM3NeT/ARCA within **6°**, over **1–1000 TeV**. The quoted **4.11σ/3.18σ** are **S/√B estimates**, not likelihood-calibrated discoveries. The fitted acceleration scale is not a measured proton cutoff.\n\n##### History\n\n[LHAASO's Cygnus-bubble study](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.10100), published in *Science Bulletin* in 2024, established the extended ultrahigh-energy gamma-ray target. Today's paper does not discover the bubble or its PeV photons. [Morlino, Blasi, Peretti and Cristofari (2021)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.09217) developed the wind-termination-shock transport framework used here. The new contribution combines updated gas information, spectral data, spatial profiles and neutrino expectations.\n\nA significant counterpoint is [Vieu and colleagues' 2024 hydrodynamic simulation of Cygnus OB2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.13589). Resolving individual stellar winds, they did **not** obtain the coherent spherical termination shock assumed by the new fit; the association's extended stellar distribution instead generated individual shocks and structured flows. That does not exclude collective acceleration generally, but it directly challenges this particular geometry.\n\n**Assessment:** the multiwavelength comparison is useful, yet the poor absolute fit and disputed accelerator geometry prevent a source identification. A fixed electron-injection benchmark in the appendix cannot exclude the full range of leptonic or mixed scenarios. A measured neutrino morphology and spectrum would provide a stronger test than a forecast event count."}, {"id": "paper-2611-00230", "arxiv": "2611.00230", "title": "Evidence for the decay B⁰ → μ⁺μ⁻ and improved measurements of the decay B⁰ₛ → μ⁺μ⁻", "authors": "LHCb Collaboration, R. Aaij et al.", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["Flavor physics", "Rare decays", "BSM"], "label": "Measurement and evidence", "teaser": "New LHCb data support B0 to dimuons and improve Bs observables; the significance and decay-time assumptions remain explicit.", "path": "2026/11/03/index.html#paper-2611-00230", "reportDate": "3 November 2026", "searchText": "Evidence for the decay B⁰ → μ⁺μ⁻ and improved measurements of the decay B⁰ₛ → μ⁺μ⁻ LHCb Collaboration, R. Aaij et al. 2611.00230 New LHCb data support B0 to dimuons and improve Bs observables; the significance and decay-time assumptions remain explicit. 2026-11-03 3 November 2026 Flavor physics Rare decays BSM #### LHCb: evidence for B⁰ → μ⁺μ⁻, with improved Bₛ observables\n\n**Paper:** *Evidence for the decay B⁰ → μ⁺μ⁻ and improved measurements of the decay B⁰ₛ → μ⁺μ⁻* — **LHCb Collaboration, R. Aaij et al.** [arXiv:2611.00230v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00230v1), [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.00230v1). Submitted **30 October, 18:11:34 UTC**; **3 November listing**; submitted to *Physical Review X*. **Classification: measurement and evidence.**\n\nA simultaneous mass-fit analysis uses **18 fb⁻¹ of 2024–2025 data at 13.6 TeV**, then combines it with earlier LHCb data. The B⁰ significance is **3.0σ in the new sample and 3.2σ combined**: evidence, below discovery convention.\n\nCombined results, with statistical then systematic uncertainties, are:\n\n- **B(Bₛ⁰ → μ⁺μ⁻) = (3.25⁺⁰·²⁶₋₀·₂₅ ⁺⁰·¹³₋₀·₁₂) × 10⁻⁹**;\n- **B(B⁰ → μ⁺μ⁻) = (1.14⁺⁰·⁴²₋₀·₃₉ ⁺⁰·⁰⁶₋₀·₀₅) × 10⁻¹⁰**;\n- **effective Bₛ lifetime = 1.60 ± 0.16 ± 0.01 ps**.\n\nBackgrounds, normalization channels, efficiencies and constrained nuisance parameters enter the fits. The branching-fraction extraction assumes **AΔΓ = +1** for Bₛ decay-time acceptance; changing it to **0 or −1** shifts the Run-3 result upward by **4.0% or 9.2%**. Thus these are not assumption-free bounds on arbitrary new scalar interactions. Results remain compatible with the Standard Model.\n\n##### History\n\nThese decays are flavor-changing neutral currents, loop- and helicity-suppressed in the Standard Model. Their small branching fractions make extra scalar or pseudoscalar amplitudes potentially visible, but extracting a coupling bound requires a specified effective Hamiltonian and correlated inputs.\n\nThe [CMS–LHCb combination published in *Nature* in 2015](https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.4413) established Bₛ → μμ and already reported roughly **3σ evidence for B⁰ → μμ**. That earlier combined hint must not be erased when describing the new single-experiment evidence. LHCb's [Run-1/2 analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.09283) subsequently set **B(B⁰ → μμ) < 2.6 × 10⁻¹⁰ at 95% CL** rather than establishing the decay. A former upper limit and today's asymmetric measurement are different statistical products; their ratio is not a sensitivity-improvement factor.\n\nThe new data make the B⁰ mode accessible to a direct measurement and strengthen the joint branching-fraction/lifetime test. They do not yet establish a departure from the Standard Model or resolve the CP structure of Bₛ decay."}, {"id": "paper-2611-00135", "arxiv": "2611.00135", "title": "Search for GeV-scale axion-like particles in PbPb collisions", "authors": "LHCb Collaboration, R. Aaij et al.", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["Axion-like particles", "Heavy ions", "BSM"], "label": "Exclusion search", "teaser": "Forward exclusive diphoton limits extend photon-fusion coverage; newer Belle II bounds qualify the global comparison.", "path": "2026/11/03/index.html#paper-2611-00135", "reportDate": "3 November 2026", "searchText": "Search for GeV-scale axion-like particles in PbPb collisions LHCb Collaboration, R. Aaij et al. 2611.00135 Forward exclusive diphoton limits extend photon-fusion coverage; newer Belle II bounds qualify the global comparison. 2026-11-03 3 November 2026 Axion-like particles Heavy ions BSM #### LHCb searches for photon-coupled GeV-scale axions\n\n**Paper:** *Search for GeV-scale axion-like particles in PbPb collisions* — **LHCb Collaboration, R. Aaij et al.** [arXiv:2611.00135v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00135v1), [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.00135v1). Submitted **29 October, 18:32:11 UTC**; **3 November listing**; submitted to *JHEP*. **Classification: exclusion search.**\n\nCoherent photon fields in ultraperipheral lead collisions can produce **γγ → a → γγ**. LHCb analyzes **0.228 ± 0.010 nb⁻¹** of 2018 PbPb data at **√sNN = 5.02 TeV**, selecting exclusive forward diphotons. It observes **zero candidates**, with approximately **0.2 background events** expected.\n\nFor fixed masses **3–10 GeV**, the **95% CLs** upper limits on photon coupling **gₐγ**, in the conventional **−gₐγaFμνF̃μν/4** normalization, span **3.2 × 10⁻⁴ to 9.5 × 10⁻⁴ GeV⁻¹**. The interpretation assumes a photon-only ALP with prompt diphoton decay. Systematics are profiled; a **31% acceptance uncertainty** from differing production generators dominates the approximately **32% total normalization uncertainty**.\n\nThe result extends exclusive photon-fusion coverage to lower masses in the forward region. It is neither an ALP signal nor a bound valid for arbitrary additional decay channels or long lifetimes.\n\n##### History\n\n[ATLAS's 2020 ultraperipheral analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.05355) and [CMS's full Run-2 PbPb analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.15413), published in *JHEP* in 2025, established this production channel at higher accessible masses. LHCb's claim of leading **exclusive photon-fusion** limits in **3–5 GeV** is narrower than a claim of the world's strongest photon-coupling bound.\n\nAn essential independent update is [Belle II's July 2026 search](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.07800): **408 fb⁻¹**, **e⁺e⁻ → γa → 3γ**, masses **0.17–9.80 GeV**, with excluded resonance windows. Its **95% frequentist profile-likelihood limits reach order 10⁻⁴ GeV⁻¹** and are stronger across much of LHCb's low-mass interval under the same prompt, photon-dominated assumption. LHCb's comparison plot uses the older Belle II result, so that plot alone is insufficient for the current global comparison.\n\nBeam-dump and supernova limits probe different mass/lifetime/production regimes; stellar cooling limits at much lower masses are not comparable GeV exclusions. No universal improvement factor follows. Additional ALP couplings can change both production and branching fraction, requiring reinterpretation of every curve."}, {"id": "paper-2611-01274", "arxiv": "2611.01274", "title": "Measurement of the |Vcb| element of the CKM matrix using tt̄ events in pp collisions at √s = 13 TeV", "authors": "CMS Collaboration", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["CKM matrix", "Top quarks", "Weak interactions"], "label": "Measurement", "teaser": "CMS extracts Vcb from on-shell W decays in top events, independently of B-decay hadronic inputs but with substantial heavy-flavor uncertainties.", "path": "2026/11/03/index.html#paper-2611-01274", "reportDate": "3 November 2026", "searchText": "Measurement of the |Vcb| element of the CKM matrix using tt̄ events in pp collisions at √s = 13 TeV CMS Collaboration 2611.01274 CMS extracts Vcb from on-shell W decays in top events, independently of B-decay hadronic inputs but with substantial heavy-flavor uncertainties. 2026-11-03 3 November 2026 CKM matrix Top quarks Weak interactions #### CMS measures |Vcb| using real W bosons in top-quark events\n\n**Paper:** *Measurement of the |Vcb| element of the CKM matrix using tt̄ events in pp collisions at √s = 13 TeV* — **CMS Collaboration**. [arXiv:2611.01274v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01274v1), [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.01274v1). Submitted **31 October, 21:17:30 UTC**; **3 November listing**; submitted to *JHEP*. **Classification: complementary CKM measurement.**\n\nUsing **138 fb⁻¹** of Run-2 data, CMS reconstructs semileptonic top pairs and separates the rare **W → cb** channel with jet-flavor information, boosted decision trees and a neural-network discriminant. A dilepton control region constrains extra-heavy-flavor backgrounds in the simultaneous fit.\n\nThe result is **|Vcb| = [4.59⁺⁰·⁴⁴₋₀·₄₈(stat) ⁺⁰·⁶⁴₋₀·₇₆(syst)] × 10⁻²**, or **(4.59⁺⁰·⁷⁸₋₀·₉₀) × 10⁻²** in total at approximately **68% CL**. Heavy-flavor production modeling dominates the uncertainty; flavor tagging also matters.\n\nThis probes an on-shell weak vertex without B-decay form factors or heavy-quark-expansion inputs. It still assumes **B(t → Wb) = 1**, Standard-Model couplings and CKM row unitarity, neglecting |Vub|². The PDG reference value normalizes the fitted signal-strength parameter; it does **not** constrain the result as a prior. Agreement with both inclusive and exclusive B-decay determinations leaves their discrepancy unresolved.\n\n##### History\n\nTraditional inclusive B → Xcℓν and exclusive B → D(*)ℓν determinations use different nonperturbative inputs. A useful independent measurement therefore changes the physical process and systematic uncertainties, rather than merely collecting more of the same B decays.\n\n[Harrison and Vladimirov (2019)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.09424) proposed extracting |Vcb| from hadronic W decays in top events. [ATLAS's first measurement](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16414v2), published **23 July 2026**, used **140 fb⁻¹ at 13 TeV** and obtained **(5.0⁺¹·¹₋₁·₄) × 10⁻²**. CMS therefore provides a more precise independent realization of an already demonstrated method, not its first measurement.\n\nThe two experiments agree within their broad uncertainties. Their precision is still insufficient to distinguish the much closer inclusive and exclusive B-decay values. The main opportunity is to improve heavy-flavor control and tagging while preserving independence from low-energy hadronic inputs; the present result is not evidence for CKM nonunitarity, which its extraction assumes."}, {"id": "paper-2611-03537", "arxiv": "2611.03537", "title": "Baryonic feedback and intrinsic alignments in profile: a frequentist view of DES Y3 cosmic shear", "authors": "Anoma Ganguly, Elisabeth Krause, Tanvi Karwal and Vivian Miranda", "primary_category": "astro-ph.CO", "tags": ["Cosmology", "Weak lensing", "Statistical inference"], "label": "Inference reanalysis", "teaser": "Profile likelihoods expose DES Y3 feedback and alignment degeneracies; fixed neutrino mass and parameter boundaries matter.", "path": "2026/11/03/index.html#paper-2611-03537", "reportDate": "3 November 2026", "searchText": "Baryonic feedback and intrinsic alignments in profile: a frequentist view of DES Y3 cosmic shear Anoma Ganguly, Elisabeth Krause, Tanvi Karwal and Vivian Miranda 2611.03537 Profile likelihoods expose DES Y3 feedback and alignment degeneracies; fixed neutrino mass and parameter boundaries matter. 2026-11-03 3 November 2026 Cosmology Weak lensing Statistical inference #### DES Y3: how much of the S₈ constraint comes from parameter boundaries?\n\n**Paper:** *Baryonic feedback and intrinsic alignments in profile: a frequentist view of DES Y3 cosmic shear* — **Anoma Ganguly, Elisabeth Krause, Tanvi Karwal and Vivian Miranda**. [arXiv:2611.03537v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03537v1), [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.03537v1). Submitted **2 November, 18:01:34 UTC**; verified **3 November listing**; preprint, no journal publication listed. **Classification: inference-method reanalysis.**\n\nThe authors profile nuisance parameters instead of marginalizing them in **KiDS-overlap-excised DES Y3 cosmic shear**, using the Hybrid modeling framework. For **S₈ = σ₈√(Ωm/0.3)**, their baseline gives **0.809⁺⁰·⁰⁴³₋₀·₀₂₂** from **Δχ² ≤ 1**, versus posterior **0.813 ± 0.020**. It fixes **Σmν = 0.06 eV**, uses NLA intrinsic alignments with **a₁ ≥ 0**, and bounds **log₁₀(TAGN/K)** to **7.3–8.0**.\n\nExtending the feedback boundary to **8.3** stretches the high-S₈ profile. Compressed Planck information on **ωb, ns and h** breaks degeneracies, giving profile **S₈ = 0.808 ± 0.019**. It does not supply an independent shear-only resolution of the S₈ tension.\n\nCalibration likelihoods and parameter boundaries remain part of the analysis. Only **ten noisy realizations** test the intervals; calibrated frequentist coverage is explicitly left for future work. “Profile likelihood” therefore does not mean assumption-free inference or guaranteed 68% coverage here.\n\n##### History\n\nWeak lensing mixes the clustering amplitude with baryonic suppression of small-scale power, galaxy intrinsic alignments and redshift calibration. Those degeneracies were already central to the [DES Y3 modeling analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.13544) and the [joint DES Y3–KiDS-1000 Hybrid analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.17173). The latter explicitly distinguished its **joint-survey** marginal mean **S₈ = 0.790⁺⁰·⁰¹⁸₋₀·₀₁₄** from its maximum-posterior value **0.801**, discussing skewness and neutrino-mass projection effects. Those joint-survey numbers are not directly comparable to the new paper's DES-only, fixed-mass baseline.\n\nThe new work exposes the degeneracy through the best likelihood available at each S₈ rather than the nuisance-volume-weighted average. Figure 4 makes the boundary dependence particularly clear. Adding shear ratios also reduces the freedom introduced by richer intrinsic-alignment models.\n\n**Assessment:** this is a useful robustness test, not new lensing data and not a demonstration that all low-S₈ measurements were prior artifacts. Its message is to state the feedback range, alignment model, neutrino treatment and external information alongside any reported tension."}, {"id": "paper-2611-01435", "arxiv": "2611.01435", "title": "World average charged-current inclusive neutrino cross section from 6 to 200 GeV", "authors": "Richard Gran", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Cross sections", "Oscillations"], "label": "Data synthesis", "teaser": "A historical cross-section audit revisits shared normalization errors, with inconsistent printed v1 uncertainties explicitly flagged.", "path": "2026/11/03/index.html#paper-2611-01435", "reportDate": "3 November 2026", "searchText": "World average charged-current inclusive neutrino cross section from 6 to 200 GeV Richard Gran 2611.01435 A historical cross-section audit revisits shared normalization errors, with inconsistent printed v1 uncertainties explicitly flagged. 2026-11-03 3 November 2026 Neutrinos Cross sections Oscillations #### A neutrino cross-section audit with a numerical caveat in v1\n\n**Paper:** *World average charged-current inclusive neutrino cross section from 6 to 200 GeV* — **Richard Gran**. [arXiv:2611.01435v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01435v1), [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.01435v1). Submitted **1 November, 02:19:08 UTC**; **3 November listing**; preprint, no journal publication listed. **Classification: historical data synthesis and generator assessment.**\n\nGran reconstructs the inclusive muon-neutrino/antineutrino charged-current **σ/Eν per isoscalar nucleon**, treating shared beam-flux uncertainties in nine high-energy measurements. Central values for **30–200 GeV** are **0.677 and 0.335**, respectively, in **10⁻³⁸ cm² GeV⁻¹**. For **6–30 GeV**, they become **0.698 and 0.316**. These are averages of existing data, not new events.\n\n**Numerical caution:** the abstract gives high-energy uncertainties **±0.013/±0.007**, while §II gives **±0.012/±0.008**. The low-energy neutrino uncertainty is **±0.018 in the abstract but ±0.016 in §VI A**. These discrepancies should be resolved before adopting a precision normalization; they are not different confidence levels stated by the author.\n\nThe lower-energy average inherits the high-energy normalization through low-ν flux determinations. Its covariance treatment is approximate, including incomplete interexperiment correlations. The modest energy trends do not establish a significant departure from a constant σ/Eν, and extrapolation below **6 GeV** is outside the analysis.\n\n##### History\n\nHere ν in “low-ν” means **energy transfer**, not neutrino energy. At sufficiently small transfer, the selected cross section varies weakly with incident energy, permitting a flux-shape measurement; an external cross section still supplies the absolute scale.\n\nThe original [MINOS measurement, Adamson et al. (2010), Eq. 7 and normalization discussion](https://arxiv.org/abs/0910.2201), explicitly anchored its **30–50 GeV** neutrino cross section to **(0.675 ± 0.009) × 10⁻³⁸ cm² GeV⁻¹**, propagating that uncertainty to all energies. This independently confirms why a low-energy point cannot be counted as a wholly independent absolute normalization. Gran traces the established high-energy benchmark to Seligman's 1997 synthesis and revisits earlier revisions to CERN/Fermilab flux calibrations.\n\nThe value of the new paper is the provenance audit, correlation accounting and comparison with GENIE implementations. It does not overturn the standard high-energy central values. Nuclear corrections, generator definitions of DIS versus non-DIS, and the unresolved printed uncertainties limit how literally its precision can be transferred into oscillation analyses."}, {"id": "paper-2610-41476", "arxiv": "2610.41476", "title": "Enhanced Detectability of Very-High-Energy Gamma Rays from Off-Axis Short Gamma-Ray Bursts: Impacts of Structured Jets and External Inverse-Compton Scattering", "authors": "Yuri Sato, Bing Theodore Zhang, Kohta Murase", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Gamma-ray bursts", "Gravitational waves"], "label": "theoretical forecast", "teaser": "External X-ray seed photons brighten off-axis TeV emission in structured-jet benchmarks; joint detection rates remain strongly model dependent.", "path": "2026/11/03/index.html#paper-2610-41476", "reportDate": "3 November 2026", "searchText": "Enhanced Detectability of Very-High-Energy Gamma Rays from Off-Axis Short Gamma-Ray Bursts: Impacts of Structured Jets and External Inverse-Compton Scattering Yuri Sato, Bing Theodore Zhang, Kohta Murase 2610.41476 External X-ray seed photons brighten off-axis TeV emission in structured-jet benchmarks; joint detection rates remain strongly model dependent. 2026-11-03 3 November 2026 Gamma rays Gamma-ray bursts Gravitational waves #### Can prolonged X-rays make off-axis short bursts visible at TeV energies?\n\n**Paper:** *Enhanced Detectability of Very-High-Energy Gamma Rays from Off-Axis Short Gamma-Ray Bursts: Impacts of Structured Jets and External Inverse-Compton Scattering* — **Yuri Sato, Bing Theodore Zhang and Kohta Murase**. [arXiv:2610.41476v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.41476v1), [full text](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.41476v1). Submitted **30 October 2026, 09:28:06 UTC**; verified listing **2 November**; v1 preprint, with no journal publication listed. **Classification: theoretical emission model and detection forecast.**\n\n**Question and method.** Short gamma-ray bursts can show extended or plateau X-ray emission after their prompt flash. Those photons can be scattered to very high energies by electrons in the external forward shock: external inverse-Compton emission, or EIC. This differs from synchrotron self-Compton (SSC), whose seed photons are generated by the same shock electrons. The authors use the AMES radiation calculation, including Klein–Nishina effects and integration over equal-arrival-time surfaces, to compare Gaussian, power-law and uniform top-hat jets viewed away from their axes.\n\nThe fiducial Gaussian jet has core angle **3°**, core Lorentz factor **100** and core isotropic-equivalent kinetic energy **6 × 10⁵² erg**. The uniform external density is **6.3 × 10⁻³ cm⁻³**; electron and magnetic energy fractions are **εₑ = 0.04** and **εᴮ = 2.1 × 10⁻⁵**, with electron index **p = 2.17** and accelerated-electron fraction **1**. These are fixed model choices, not measured confidence bounds. Extended and plateau seed components have characteristic durations **300 s** and **10⁴ s**, respectively, and luminosity normalizations **10⁴⁹** and **10⁴⁷ erg s⁻¹** at their spectral breaks.\n\n**Quantitative result.** At **z = 0.01**, approximately **43 Mpc**, the modeled **1 TeV** EIC peak exceeds the adopted CTAO medium-sized-telescope sensitivity for a **6°** viewing angle in both seed-emission cases, but not for **15°**. Gaussian and power-law jets give brighter off-axis EIC emission than the selected top-hat benchmark. This is a comparison of the adopted jet models, not a universal ordering at fixed total jet energy. The EIC peak follows the end of the seed-photon supply, providing a possible temporal discriminator from SSC; SSC can dominate later.\n\nUsing a sky-averaged binary-neutron-star gravitational-wave range of **300 Mpc** for the assumed LIGO O5 configuration, and merger-rate density **10–1700 Gpc⁻³ yr⁻¹**, the predicted joint GW–TeV rates are **0.003–0.5 yr⁻¹** for extended-emission EIC and **0.004–0.7 yr⁻¹** for plateau EIC, compared with **0.001–0.2 yr⁻¹** for SSC. The ranges propagate the adopted merger-rate normalization; they are **not confidence intervals or measured yields**. In the Gaussian benchmark, the TeV horizon drops rapidly beyond viewing angles of roughly **8°**, so gravitational-wave detectability alone does not imply TeV detectability.\n\n**Limitations.** The result depends on prolonged central-engine emission actually occurring, its luminosity and duration, the angular jet structure, ambient density and shock microphysics. The quoted forecasts do not marginalize over these population uncertainties. Peak-flux sensitivity comparisons also do not establish the yield of a real campaign with pointing delays, sky visibility and localization coverage. A nondetection would therefore not, by itself, exclude a structured jet or a long-lived engine.\n\n##### History\n\nThe established physical question is whether short-burst TeV emission is supplied mainly by shock-generated synchrotron photons or by an additional central-engine radiation field. EIC itself is not new here. In [Zhang, Murase, Yuan, Kimura and Mészáros, *ApJ Letters* 908, L36 (2021), arXiv:2012.09143v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.09143v2), extended and plateau photons were already shown to enhance the high-energy afterglow, with Klein–Nishina effects and the end of seed injection shaping the light curve. Their application to GRB 160821B illustrated how EIC could exceed SSC under suitable parameters. The present advance is the explicit dependence on **off-axis angular structure**, followed through to joint GW–CTAO horizons and conditional rates; it is not the first proposal of this radiation mechanism.\n\nThe observational baseline also needs care. [Acciari et al., MAGIC Collaboration, *ApJ* 908, 90 (2021), arXiv:2012.07193](https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.07193) reported a low-significance VHE excess for GRB 160821B. At the known burst position, the quoted **3.1σ pre-trial significance becomes 2.9σ after accounting for the two analysis cuts**. That is not a secure short-burst TeV detection and does not validate EIC. The new calculation supplies testable light-curve and viewing-angle expectations, while both the radiation mechanism and the population rate remain uncertain. Its forecast rates cannot be interpreted as an improvement in an existing experimental bound."}, {"id": "paper-2610-41163", "arxiv": "2610.41163", "title": "HAWC, Fermi-LAT and Chandra Detections of High Energy Particles in the S106 Star Forming Region", "authors": "Rishi Babu, Stephen DiKerby, Tulun Ergin, Shuo Zhang, Mehr Un Nisa and the HAWC Collaboration", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Gamma rays", "Star formation"], "label": "New paper · observations and emission models", "teaser": "A local infrared field allows electron-only gamma-ray modeling of S106, while a hybrid model remains viable.", "path": "2026/11/02/index.html#paper-2610-41163", "reportDate": "2 November 2026", "searchText": "HAWC, Fermi-LAT and Chandra Detections of High Energy Particles in the S106 Star Forming Region Rishi Babu, Stephen DiKerby, Tulun Ergin, Shuo Zhang, Mehr Un Nisa and the HAWC Collaboration 2610.41163 A local infrared field allows electron-only gamma-ray modeling of S106, while a hybrid model remains viable. 2026-11-02 2 November 2026 Cosmic rays Gamma rays Star formation #### 1. S106: gamma rays do not uniquely identify a proton accelerator\n\n**“HAWC, Fermi-LAT and Chandra Detections of High Energy Particles in the S106 Star Forming Region” — Rishi Babu, Stephen DiKerby, Tulun Ergin, Shuo Zhang, Mehr Un Nisa and the HAWC Collaboration** (arXiv author listing; the PDF gives the full collaboration roster). [arXiv:2610.41163v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.41163v1), [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.41163v1). Submitted **30 October 2026, 02:38:48 UTC**; listed **2 November**; primary category **astro-ph.HE**. **Observational analysis plus competing emission models; preprint.**\n\nCan a star-forming region's very-high-energy gamma rays be produced by electrons, or do they require accelerated protons and accompanying neutrinos? The authors combine approximately **2,900 days of HAWC data**, Fermi-LAT observations from **4 August 2008 to 20 July 2024**, and a **45 ks archival Chandra exposure** of Sharpless 106, at an adopted distance of **1.4 kpc**. They fit neighboring sources and Galactic diffuse emission, then model the broadband spectrum with synchrotron radiation, bremsstrahlung, inverse-Compton scattering and, alternatively, pion decay.\n\nThe HAWC fit using a Planck dust spatial template gives **TS = 31** and photon index **2.61 ± 0.11 statistical, with systematic uncertainty +0.21/−0.19**. The fitted differential flux at **7 TeV** is **4.8 (+1.1/−0.9 statistical; +2.3/−1.4 systematic) × 10⁻¹⁵ TeV⁻¹ cm⁻² s⁻¹**. These are the reported fit errors, not a separately calibrated confidence interval for source association. The Fermi analysis favors an extended source, with Gaussian radius **0.39° ± 0.07°** and a **200 MeV–1 TeV** photon index **2.18 ± 0.06**.\n\nThe important modeling ingredient is S106's own infrared radiation:\n\n\\[\nu_{\\rm IR}(r)=\\frac{L_{\\rm dust}}{4\\pi r^2c}\n\\simeq60\\left(\\frac{r}{\\mathrm{pc}}\\right)^{-2}\\mathrm{eV\\,cm^{-3}}.\n\\]\n\nWith gas density fixed at **250 cm⁻³**, one electron population can reproduce the broadband spectrum. Its representative fit has **B = 2.38 μG**, electron cutoff **1,753 TeV**, electron energy above 1 GeV **4.11 × 10⁴⁵ erg**, and characteristic inverse-Compton distance **3.55 pc**. These correlated model values are **not independent measurements or confidence limits**. Figure 7 demonstrates spectral compatibility, not a unique identification of the particles.\n\nA hybrid model also works: its magnetic field is about **18.2 μG**, with a proton component carrying about **3.1 × 10⁴⁶ erg above 1 GeV**. The proton cutoff reaches the imposed **1,000 TeV upper boundary**. A boundary-hitting fit is not a measured cutoff, and does not by itself establish a proton PeVatron.\n\nThe diffuse-background treatment matters: using the unmasked diffuse template gives a reported **4.8σ post-trial** result, whereas the adopted analysis masks the dense S106 cloud out of that background and fits its contribution separately. The electron-only fit also uses a field below the **10–500 μG** range cited for the core; different emission zones may explain this, but that spatial interpretation is not independently established. Unresolved stellar X-rays can contaminate the nominal diffuse component, and the Chandra snapshot is not contemporaneous with the long gamma-ray integrations. No neutrino detection from S106 is reported.\n\n##### History\n\n**Giardino, Favata and Micela (2004)**, [astro-ph/0406344](https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0406344), already analyzed the **3 November 2001 Chandra observation**, identifying **87 X-ray sources** within 4.4 arcmin and examining the central young massive object. The new paper reuses this exposure but masks resolved sources to investigate diffuse emission. Its contribution is the broadband connection to GeV–TeV emission, rather than discovery of S106's X-ray activity. The earlier source census makes unresolved-source contamination a concrete concern.\n\n**Aharonian, Yang and de Oña Wilhelmi (2019)**, [arXiv:1804.02331v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.02331v2), argued for hadronic emission around massive clusters using spatially resolved gamma rays and gas maps. Their approximately **1/r cosmic-ray density profiles** supported sustained injection; cooling and the absence of a central inverse-Compton brightening disfavored electrons for those extended systems. Their argument was morphological and source-specific, not a theorem that every star-forming region must be hadronic.\n\nS106 therefore does not overturn the cluster evidence. It illustrates why a compact region with a strong local infrared field needs its own transport and radiation model. The decisive unresolved issue is whether a physically consistent spatial model can reproduce the X-ray, gas and gamma-ray morphologies together. The current spectral degeneracy leaves both electron-dominated and hybrid interpretations open."}, {"id": "paper-2610-40782", "arxiv": "2610.40782", "title": "Missing Mergers: On the Unexpected Delay-Time Distribution of Binary Neutron Star Mergers", "authors": "Ryan J. Foley", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Neutron stars", "Gravitational waves", "Population inference"], "label": "New paper · conditional population inference", "teaser": "An old host favors long merger delays under a restricted model, with strong normalization-prior dependence.", "path": "2026/11/02/index.html#paper-2610-40782", "reportDate": "2 November 2026", "searchText": "Missing Mergers: On the Unexpected Delay-Time Distribution of Binary Neutron Star Mergers Ryan J. Foley 2610.40782 An old host favors long merger delays under a restricted model, with strong normalization-prior dependence. 2026-11-02 2 November 2026 Neutron stars Gravitational waves Population inference #### 2. “Missing mergers”: a long-delay claim driven by one old host\n\n**“Missing Mergers: On the Unexpected Delay-Time Distribution of Binary Neutron Star Mergers” — Ryan J. Foley.** [arXiv:2610.40782v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40782v1), [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40782v1). Submitted **29 October 2026, 18:00:10 UTC**; listed **2 November**; primary category **astro-ph.HE**. **Population inference under restrictive assumptions; preprint.**\n\nThe delay-time distribution, or DTD, describes the interval between star formation and a binary-neutron-star merger. For gravitational-wave inspiral, the strong dependence of merger time on orbital separation motivates a broad distribution close to **t⁻¹**. GW170817's host, NGC 4993, instead presents a particularly old stellar population.\n\nFoley fits ultraviolet-to-infrared photometry and an integrated VLT/MUSE spectrum with a 20-bin, nonparametric star-formation history. The nominal model uses a Chabrier initial mass function, MIST stellar tracks, MILES spectra and a continuity prior between adjacent star-formation bins, while fitting metallicity, dust and nuisance parameters. It yields a **mass-weighted stellar age of 12.4 (+0.5/−0.7) Gyr, at 90% posterior credibility**. This is a host-population inference, not a direct clock reading for the merging binary.\n\nThe population model is\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{DTD}(t)=A t^\\alpha,\\qquad\n t_{\\min}\\leq t\\leq13.68\\;\\mathrm{Gyr}.\n\\]\n\nIt combines the host history with a cosmic star-formation history and two GW-detected BNS events, GW170817 and GW190425. Only the first supplies host-age information. The nominal priors are uniform in **−10 < α < 0** and **0.01 < t_min < 13.45 Gyr**; the maximum age is fixed. Metallicity-dependent binary formation is ignored.\n\nUnder the paper's **formation-efficiency normalization treatment**, marginalizing the normalization and slope gives **t_min > 7.5 Gyr at 90% credibility** and **>5.6 Gyr at 95%**, with approximately **0.2 Gyr run-to-run scatter** in each threshold. That scatter is not an additional observational confidence interval. A fixed model with **(t_min, α) = (12.6 Gyr, −1)** has a normalization-marginalized likelihood **40.6 ± 9.4** times that of **(10 Myr, −1)** in the efficiency treatment, or **138 ± 32** in the rate treatment. These are comparisons of specified DTD shapes, not Bayes factors integrated over their shape parameters or probabilities that a formation channel is correct.\n\nA crucial limitation appears in the likelihood itself. In the rate-normalized treatment, the Poisson count, surveyed volume-time and rate prior factor out of the DTD-shape inference. Its remaining dependence is proportional to\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\int_{t_{\\min}}^{t_{\\rm univ}}\n\\mathrm{SFH}_{4993}(t)t^\\alpha\\,dt}\n{\\int_{t_{\\min}}^{t_{\\rm univ}}\n\\psi_{\\rm cosmic}(t)t^\\alpha\\,dt}.\n\\]\n\nThe posterior then accumulates at its upper prior boundary. Changing to the efficiency treatment changes the implied shape prior and produces the quoted finite bounds. Thus the result is driven chiefly by **one host relative to cosmic star formation**, rather than by several independently dated mergers. The stellar fit also leaves abundance-ratio systematics unmodeled, and the paper acknowledges that a mixture of formation channels is more plausible than its single truncated power law. “Missing mergers” is a model-dependent tension, not evidence that LVK overlooked a measured population.\n\n##### History\n\n**Zevin et al. (2022)**, [arXiv:2206.02814v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.02814v2), inferred a DTD from **68 short-GRB hosts**, obtaining **α = −1.83 (+0.35/−0.39)** and **t_min = 184 (+67/−79) Myr**, both 90% credible intervals. They marginalized host-history and DTD uncertainties, fixed the binary formation efficiency, and assumed detection selection did not depend on DTD parameters. Their minimum-delay prior covered **5–500 Myr**. Faint hosts, large offsets and afterglow selection were explicit limitations. Those results concern a selected GRB population and cannot be translated directly into a universal BNS bound.\n\nAn independent, more recent comparison is **Pracchia and Salafia**, [arXiv:2601.03861v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.03861v2), revised **5 August 2026**. Their hierarchical population analysis uses **210 flux-selected Fermi short bursts** and a joint Fermi/Swift sample of **18 bursts, 16 with redshifts**, explicitly modeling detection and redshift-selection cuts. Both empirical luminosity-function and structured-jet treatments favor short delays. Their power-law models give mean-delay medians of **77 Myr** and **105 Myr**, respectively, with broad 90% intervals extending to hundreds of Myr. These are population means, not minimum-delay bounds, and their slope convention has the opposite sign to Foley's.\n\nThe new paper identifies a potentially important conflict between an old nearby GW host and selected cosmological GRBs. It does not yet resolve that conflict: host-age modeling, selection, neutron-star–black-hole contamination of the GRB sample, and multiple BNS channels remain alternatives. The new conditional lower bound is numerically much larger than earlier estimates, but calling that an “improvement” would be misleading because the populations, priors and likelihoods differ."}, {"id": "paper-2610-40766", "arxiv": "2610.40766", "title": "Hints of self-interacting dark matter from strong gravitational lensing", "authors": "Daniel Gilman, Kalliopi Petraki, Thomas Pignard, James Gurian, Alex Kusenko, Anna Nierenberg", "primary_category": "astro-ph.CO", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Gravitational lensing"], "label": "Lensing inference and particle-model interpretation", "teaser": "Quasar lenses favor collapsed SIDM halos—but not a unique particle model", "review_date": "2026-11-11", "listing_date": "2026-11-02", "version": "v1", "submission_date": "2026-10-29T18:00:06Z", "path": "2026/11/02/index.html#paper-2610-40766", "reportDate": "2 November 2026", "searchText": "Hints of self-interacting dark matter from strong gravitational lensing Daniel Gilman, Kalliopi Petraki, Thomas Pignard, James Gurian, Alex Kusenko, Anna Nierenberg 2610.40766 Quasar lenses favor collapsed SIDM halos—but not a unique particle model 2026-11-02 2 November 2026 Dark matter Gravitational lensing #### 3. Quasar lenses favor collapsed SIDM halos—but not a unique particle model\n\n**“Hints of self-interacting dark matter from strong gravitational lensing” — Daniel Gilman, Kalliopi Petraki, Thomas Pignard, James Gurian, Alex Kusenko, Anna Nierenberg.** [arXiv:2610.40766v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40766v1) · [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40766v1) · [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40766v1). Submitted **29 October 2026, 18:00:06 UTC**; UTC listing **2 November 2026**; primary category **astro-ph.CO**. **Lensing inference and particle-model interpretation.** Preprint submitted to Physical Review Letters.\n\nThe paper interprets **29 quadruply imaged quasars** using gravothermal collapse in self-interacting dark matter (SIDM). Both long- and short-mean-free-path heat conduction are included: very strong scattering can slow collapse rather than accelerate it indefinitely. The example particle model contains symmetric Dirac dark matter and a light scalar mediator.\n\nThe relevant statistic is the viscosity cross section, \\(\\sigma_V=(3/2)\\int\\sin^2\\theta\\,(d\\sigma/d\\Omega)d\\Omega\\), averaged over the relevant particle populations, and \\(\\sigma_{10}=\\sigma_V(10\\,\\mathrm{km\\,s^{-1}})/m_\\chi\\). Two halo prescriptions peak near **1,000 and 200 cm² g⁻¹**; they differ in subhalo abundance, inner slope and tidal enhancement. With **αχ fixed to 10⁻³**, likelihood features favor **mχ ≈ 20–80 GeV** and **mφ below 20 MeV**. These are conditional preferred regions, not standalone confidence bounds.\n\nThe **8:1–24:1 Bayes factors** compare specified collapsed-halo scenarios with their CDM reference. They do not independently exclude CDM, and the underlying lensing data are reused from a companion analysis. Cluster-scale scattering penalties and thermal-relic/kinetic-decoupling requirements are imposed inputs. Halo evolution and abundance assumptions remain decisive; the figures demonstrate this dependence rather than a unique particle solution.\n\n##### History\n\nGravothermal SIDM can first form cores and later dense collapsed centers. The direct observational predecessor is the [companion lensing analysis, 2609.35974v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.35974v2), not an independent second data set.\n\nIndependent constraints deserve comparison. [Andrade et al., 2012.06611](https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.06611), inferred **σ/m < 0.13 cm² g⁻¹ at 95% confidence** from eight cluster lenses at characteristic relative speed about **1,458 km s⁻¹**, conditional on their cluster-evolution model. [Ando et al., 2503.13650v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.13650v1), used eight classical and 23 ultrafaint dwarfs, Jeans modeling and SIDM subhalo evolution. With a log-uniform cross-section normalization prior, their 95th-percentile limits on **σ₀/m** are **0.8 cm² g⁻¹ at fixed turnover speed w = 10 km s⁻¹**, or **0.04 cm² g⁻¹ for velocity-independent scattering**; a high-cross-section mode can reappear for smaller w. Those normalization definitions, velocity dependences and galaxy-formation assumptions differ from σ₁₀ here. Potential dwarf-scale tension remains important, but a universal improvement factor or model-independent exclusion would be unjustified."}, {"id": "paper-2610-40770", "arxiv": "2610.40770", "title": "When precise inference is wrong: Diagnosing model misspecification with applications to cosmic dawn and reionisation", "authors": "Daniela Breitman", "primary_category": "astro-ph.CO", "tags": ["Cosmology", "21-cm cosmology", "Statistical methods"], "label": "Statistical diagnostic tested on forecasts and galaxy data", "teaser": "When a good 21-cm fit is a bad astrophysical model", "review_date": "2026-11-11", "listing_date": "2026-11-02", "version": "v1", "submission_date": "2026-10-29T18:00:08Z", "path": "2026/11/02/index.html#paper-2610-40770", "reportDate": "2 November 2026", "searchText": "When precise inference is wrong: Diagnosing model misspecification with applications to cosmic dawn and reionisation Daniela Breitman 2610.40770 When a good 21-cm fit is a bad astrophysical model 2026-11-02 2 November 2026 Cosmology 21-cm cosmology Statistical methods #### 4. When a good 21-cm fit is a bad astrophysical model\n\n**“When precise inference is wrong: Diagnosing model misspecification with applications to cosmic dawn and reionisation” — Daniela Breitman.** [arXiv:2610.40770v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40770v1) · [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40770v1) · [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40770v1). Submitted **29 October 2026, 18:00:08 UTC**; UTC listing **2 November 2026**; primary category **astro-ph.CO**. **Statistical diagnostic tested on forecasts and galaxy data.** Preprint; no journal reference was shown on the checked version record.\n\nBreitman tests whether disagreement between subsets of data can reveal missing astrophysics despite an acceptable overall fit. The mock signal includes atomic- and molecular-cooling galaxies; an intentionally incomplete 21cmEMU inference model includes only the former. Bayesian suspiciousness removes an information-volume term from the evidence ratio: \\(\\log S=\\log R-(D_A+D_B-D_{AB})\\), with D the posterior-to-prior Kullback–Leibler divergence.\n\nFor a **1,080-hour SKA mock**, the incomplete model fits the power spectrum while shifting the logarithmic X-ray luminosity per star-formation rate from injected **40.5 to approximately 41.5**, and the escape-fraction slope from **0 to −0.5**. A representative **log S = −7.45** is more extreme than all **150 calibration mocks**, giving a calibrated lower significance of **2.72σ**; stronger extrapolations depend on distributional assumptions. In another test, suspiciousness flags **48/50** misspecified fits at a 5% threshold, compared with **15/50** for the chosen χ² diagnostic.\n\nSplitting JWST luminosity functions below and above **z = 10** yields roughly **5σ** tension within the restricted galaxy model and Gaussian calibration, falling to **2.1σ** after doubling high-redshift errors. This is not a ΛCDM falsification. Cosmic variance, interlopers, Eddington bias, emulator error and partition selection matter. The relevant setup fixes **σ₈ = 0.8118** and samples astrophysical parameters with stated uniform or informative priors. HERA upper limits offer much weaker consistency discrimination.\n\n##### History\n\n[Handley and Lemos, 1902.04029](https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.04029), introduced suspiciousness to reduce arbitrary prior-volume dependence in Bayesian consistency tests. It is not a posterior probability that a model is true. Its invariance to prior-width changes assumes that those changes leave the posteriors essentially unchanged. The advance here is controlled calibration for 21-cm model misspecification and a galaxy-data application, not a new statistic. The false-alarm interpretation remains conditional on the simulated model and tested partition; searching many partitions creates an additional selection issue."}, {"id": "paper-2610-40781", "arxiv": "2610.40781", "title": "Leaking Sound Waves: Gravitational Waves From Dark Sectors Weakly Coupled to the Standard Model", "authors": "Marco Finetti, Helena Kolešová, Daniil Krichevskiy", "primary_category": "gr-qc", "tags": ["Gravitational waves", "Dark sectors", "Phase transitions"], "label": "Phenomenological gravitational-wave prediction", "teaser": "Hidden-sector sound waves can transfer their energy to visible matter", "review_date": "2026-11-11", "listing_date": "2026-11-02", "version": "v1", "submission_date": "2026-10-29T18:00:10Z", "path": "2026/11/02/index.html#paper-2610-40781", "reportDate": "2 November 2026", "searchText": "Leaking Sound Waves: Gravitational Waves From Dark Sectors Weakly Coupled to the Standard Model Marco Finetti, Helena Kolešová, Daniil Krichevskiy 2610.40781 Hidden-sector sound waves can transfer their energy to visible matter 2026-11-02 2 November 2026 Gravitational waves Dark sectors Phase transitions #### 5. Hidden-sector sound waves can transfer their energy to visible matter\n\n**“Leaking Sound Waves: Gravitational Waves From Dark Sectors Weakly Coupled to the Standard Model” — Marco Finetti, Helena Kolešová, Daniil Krichevskiy.** [arXiv:2610.40781v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40781v1) · [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40781v1) · [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40781v1). Submitted **29 October 2026, 18:00:10 UTC**; UTC listing **2 November 2026**; primary category **gr-qc**. **Phenomenological gravitational-wave prediction.** Preprint; no journal reference was shown on the checked version record.\n\nThe usual acoustic gravitational-wave calculation treats the fluid excited by a first-order transition as effectively isolated. This work couples two ultrarelativistic fluids through an energy-momentum exchange current. For common temperature and sound speed squared **1/3**, the relative velocity decays while the total-fluid mode survives. The assumed interaction time is longer than bubble growth but can be shorter than the acoustic-source lifetime.\n\nThe unequal-time gravitational-wave source acquires factors \\(F(\\tau)=[1+(w_v/w_d)e^{-2\\Gamma\\Delta\\tau}]/[1+w_v/w_d]\\), with w the enthalpy density and Γ the interaction rate. For benchmark dark transition strength **0.1**, wall speed **0.9c**, visible/dark enthalpy ratio **10**, and **Γ/H = 100**, the peak power can fall to about **4%** of the isolated-fluid result for acoustic lifetime **0.1 H⁻¹**; a lifetime of **0.01 H⁻¹** gives a ratio around **0.3**. These are model examples, not measured suppression or confidence limits.\n\nThe infrared **k³** behavior survives, but the spectral modification is not generally one constant rescaling. Γ remains phenomenological. No full nonlinear two-fluid transition simulation or model-independent portal-coupling bound is supplied; a particle model must also explain why its interactions did not already change bubble growth.\n\n##### History\n\nThe [sound-shell model of Hindmarsh and Hijazi, 1909.10040](https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.10040), derives acoustic spectra from velocity correlations and bubble-scale flow profiles, controlled by transition strength, wall speed and bubble separation. Its limitations from nonlinear evolution and particular flow regimes were already recognized. The new ingredient is post-transition energy exchange between fluids. This modifies the isolated-source assumption, rather than invalidating acoustic production or establishing the same suppression for every hidden-sector transition."}, {"id": "paper-2610-40785", "arxiv": "2610.40785", "title": "Model-independent and reconstruction-free evidence for cosmic acceleration from DESI baryon acoustic oscillations", "authors": "Laura Herold, Graeme E. Addison, Adam G. Riess", "primary_category": "astro-ph.CO", "tags": ["Cosmology", "Dark energy", "BAO"], "label": "Kinematic observational tests", "teaser": "DESI BAO tests acceleration without an absolute ruler calibration", "review_date": "2026-11-11", "listing_date": "2026-11-02", "version": "v1", "submission_date": "2026-10-29T18:00:13Z", "path": "2026/11/02/index.html#paper-2610-40785", "reportDate": "2 November 2026", "searchText": "Model-independent and reconstruction-free evidence for cosmic acceleration from DESI baryon acoustic oscillations Laura Herold, Graeme E. Addison, Adam G. Riess 2610.40785 DESI BAO tests acceleration without an absolute ruler calibration 2026-11-02 2 November 2026 Cosmology Dark energy BAO #### 6. DESI BAO tests acceleration without an absolute ruler calibration\n\n**“Model-independent and reconstruction-free evidence for cosmic acceleration from DESI baryon acoustic oscillations” — Laura Herold, Graeme E. Addison, Adam G. Riess.** [arXiv:2610.40785v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40785v1) · [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40785v1) · [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40785v1). Submitted **29 October 2026, 18:00:13 UTC**; UTC listing **2 November 2026**; primary category **astro-ph.CO**. **Kinematic observational tests.** Preprint; no journal reference was shown on the checked version record.\n\nThe authors construct three inequalities that a never-accelerating universe must obey, using **DESI DR2 BAO** and FLRW geometry without calibrating the absolute sound horizon or imposing a dark-energy equation of state. One involves \\(S_F=(1+z)\\ln(1+z)-F_{\\rm AP}\\), where positive values violate the corresponding flat-universe non-acceleration inequality. Others compare distances across redshifts, with the BGS measurement as a low-redshift reference.\n\nFor anisotropic bins around **z = 0.510, 0.706 and 0.934**, the three constructions give maximum-bin significances **2.39σ, 3.04σ and 4.06σ** after the stated three-bin correction; average-based versions give **2.19σ, 2.85σ and 3.22σ**. These share data and must not be combined as independent discoveries. Scanning **|Ωk| ≤ 1** leaves two tests near **2.4–3.5σ** over the explored cases; that scan is not Bayesian curvature marginalization.\n\nThis is kinematic evidence for acceleration under geometric assumptions, not a measurement of its physical cause. FLRW distance relations and a common BAO ruler still enter. Removing an absolute r_d or H₀ calibration does not remove all early-universe, geometry or observational-systematics assumptions.\n\n##### History\n\n[Sutherland's cosmic speed-trap, 1105.3838v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/1105.3838v2), already used BAO distance ratios and one-sided inequalities to test acceleration with limited dependence on gravity and the absolute ruler scale. The present advance is the DESI DR2 implementation, complementary statistics and explicit redshift-bin and curvature checks. Its correlated tests strengthen the assessment of one physical question; they should not be advertised as three independent confirmations or the first model-light BAO acceleration test."}, {"id": "paper-2610-40793", "arxiv": "2610.40793", "title": "Layered Wire-Grid Haloscope for Millielectronvolt Axions and Dark Photons", "authors": "Andrea Caputo", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Axions", "Dark photons", "Dark matter instrumentation"], "label": "Instrument concept and sensitivity forecast", "teaser": "MADGRID: a plasma haloscope aimed at meV dark matter", "review_date": "2026-11-11", "listing_date": "2026-11-02", "version": "v1", "submission_date": "2026-10-29T18:00:25Z", "path": "2026/11/02/index.html#paper-2610-40793", "reportDate": "2 November 2026", "searchText": "Layered Wire-Grid Haloscope for Millielectronvolt Axions and Dark Photons Andrea Caputo 2610.40793 MADGRID: a plasma haloscope aimed at meV dark matter 2026-11-02 2 November 2026 Axions Dark photons Dark matter instrumentation #### 7. MADGRID: a plasma haloscope aimed at meV dark matter\n\n**“Layered Wire-Grid Haloscope for Millielectronvolt Axions and Dark Photons” — Andrea Caputo.** [arXiv:2610.40793v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40793v1) · [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40793v1) · [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40793v1). Submitted **29 October 2026, 18:00:25 UTC**; UTC listing **2 November 2026**; primary category **hep-ph**. **Instrument concept and sensitivity forecast.** Preprint; no journal reference was shown on the checked version record.\n\nMADGRID proposes stacked copper-wire grids that convert axions or dark photons into electromagnetic power, combining a dispersive wire-medium response with discrete Maxwell solutions and extraction through the stack's end face. Five cartridges with approximately **8–15 grids** span **0.25–8 meV**. The projections assume **0.1 m²** aperture, **10 T** for axions, **0.1 K**, local dark-matter density **0.4 GeV cm⁻³**, and the candidate comprising all local dark matter.\n\nFor **one live year per octave** and local **S/N = 5**, an illustrative **1 meV** threshold is **g_aγ ≈ 1.3 × 10⁻¹³ GeV⁻¹**, or dark-photon mixing **χ ≈ 3.4 × 10⁻¹⁶** with random-polarization factor 1/3. These are forecasts, not 95% confidence exclusions. The kinetic-inductance option assumes **NEP = 6 × 10⁻¹⁹ W Hz⁻¹/²**. A more ambitious photon-counter option assumes **50% efficiency**, **1 mHz dark counts per channel**, and 100 or 400 channels; it forecasts KSVZ coverage over roughly **0.8–3.9 meV**, and DFSZ over **0.8–1.2 meV**.\n\nThe combined magnet, detector, terahertz material response and cryogenic performance have not been demonstrated here. Coupling losses and adopted aperture efficiency around **0.65** directly affect reach. This is a technically specified proposal, not an operating dark-matter search.\n\n##### History\n\n[Lawson et al., 1904.11872v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.11872v2), established tunable wire-plasma haloscopes, with illustrative coverage at **35–400 μeV**. MADGRID develops a layered finite geometry and a higher-mass reach. Independently, [CAST, 1705.02290v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.02290v2), measured **g_aγ < 6.6 × 10⁻¹¹ GeV⁻¹ at 95% confidence for m_a ≲ 0.02 eV** using solar axions. CAST does not require axions to make up Galactic dark matter, whereas this forecast does. Their numerical difference is therefore neither an achieved improvement nor an assumption-matched comparison. QCD-axion lines are theoretical benchmarks, not observed confidence regions."}, {"id": "paper-2610-40866", "arxiv": "2610.40866", "title": "Phenomenological Chiral Effective Field Theory for Neutrino Event Generators", "authors": "Zara Graham-Jones, Mareen Hoppe, Joshua Isaacson, Shirley Weishi Li, Miša Toman", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Neutrino interactions", "Event generators"], "label": "Interaction-model and software development", "teaser": "Automating coherent pion production for neutrino generators", "review_date": "2026-11-11", "listing_date": "2026-11-02", "version": "v1", "submission_date": "2026-10-29T18:41:31Z", "path": "2026/11/02/index.html#paper-2610-40866", "reportDate": "2 November 2026", "searchText": "Phenomenological Chiral Effective Field Theory for Neutrino Event Generators Zara Graham-Jones, Mareen Hoppe, Joshua Isaacson, Shirley Weishi Li, Miša Toman 2610.40866 Automating coherent pion production for neutrino generators 2026-11-02 2 November 2026 Neutrinos Neutrino interactions Event generators #### 8. Automating coherent pion production for neutrino generators\n\n**“Phenomenological Chiral Effective Field Theory for Neutrino Event Generators” — Zara Graham-Jones, Mareen Hoppe, Joshua Isaacson, Shirley Weishi Li, Miša Toman.** [arXiv:2610.40866v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40866v1) · [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40866v1) · [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40866v1). Submitted **29 October 2026, 18:41:31 UTC**; UTC listing **2 November 2026**; primary category **hep-ph**. **Interaction-model and software development.** Preprint; no journal reference was shown on the checked version record.\n\nThis work exports chiral-theory amplitudes through **FeynRules/UFO to Sherpa/Comix** and connects them to **Achilles**, combining coherent Δ and nonresonant Born contributions with spectral-function initial states and nuclear final-state interactions. It addresses interaction modeling needed by oscillation experiments, but is a tree-level phenomenological implementation rather than a complete EFT truncation-error calculation.\n\nResonance conventions matter strongly. With a constant Δ pole width, free-nucleon cross sections for **W < 1.4 GeV** overshoot reference data by about **80%, 50% and 15%** in the three displayed charge channels. A running width proportional to \\((k/k_0)^3m_\\Delta/W\\), together with a revised Breit–Wigner parametrization, improves agreement. The illustrated nuclear excesses change from about **50% to 5–10% for T2K**, and **60–70% to 10–20% for MicroBooNE**.\n\nThose improved running-width results use reweighting: they are not yet a complete implemented generator feature. The axial normalization changes from **C₅ᴬ(0) = 1.27** to **1.18** in the revised treatment. Unitarization, higher resonances, two-body and medium effects remain unresolved. Better agreement in these samples does not establish full phase-space validity or a measured reduction in oscillation-parameter bias.\n\n##### History\n\n[Hernández, Nieves and Valverde, hep-ph/0701149v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/0701149v2), already included coherent Δ-pole and chiral-background amplitudes and showed their impact on extracting the axial transition form factor from ANL data. Interference is established physics, not the novelty. The advance is the automated amplitude-to-nuclear-generator workflow and demonstrations of sensitivity to width conventions. C₅ᴬ values must be compared within their fitted amplitude and resonance prescriptions, rather than as independently measured constants."}, {"id": "paper-2610-40895", "arxiv": "2610.40895", "title": "Stars Can Quench Superradiance Around Supermassive Black Holes", "authors": "Yu Cheng, Andrea Caputo, Giovanni Maria Tomaselli, Samuel J. Witte", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Ultralight dark matter", "Black holes", "Superradiance"], "label": "Theoretical environment calculation", "teaser": "How orbiting stars erode superradiant boson clouds", "review_date": "2026-11-11", "listing_date": "2026-11-02", "version": "v1", "submission_date": "2026-10-29T19:17:01Z", "path": "2026/11/02/index.html#paper-2610-40895", "reportDate": "2 November 2026", "searchText": "Stars Can Quench Superradiance Around Supermassive Black Holes Yu Cheng, Andrea Caputo, Giovanni Maria Tomaselli, Samuel J. Witte 2610.40895 How orbiting stars erode superradiant boson clouds 2026-11-02 2 November 2026 Ultralight dark matter Black holes Superradiance #### 9. How orbiting stars erode superradiant boson clouds\n\n**“Stars Can Quench Superradiance Around Supermassive Black Holes” — Yu Cheng, Andrea Caputo, Giovanni Maria Tomaselli, Samuel J. Witte.** [arXiv:2610.40895v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40895v1) · [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40895v1) · [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40895v1). Submitted **29 October 2026, 19:17:01 UTC**; UTC listing **2 November 2026**; primary category **hep-ph**. **Theoretical environment calculation.** Preprint; no journal reference was shown on the checked version record.\n\nThe authors calculate stellar perturbations that transfer bosons from gravitationally bound cloud states into continuum states. They combine hydrogenic wavefunctions, relativistic growth rates and orbital averages, with evolution \\(\\dot M_c/M_c=\\tau_{\\rm sr}^{-1}-\\tau_{\\rm loss}^{-1}\\). A Sgr A*-like benchmark fixes **M = 4 × 10⁶ M⊙** and spin **0.9**; the latter is not an observed spin determination.\n\nGravitational encounters generally do not quench cloud growth in the studied stellar populations. For **α = GMμ = 0.45** in natural units, an illustrative 433 state has loss/growth-time ratio about **2**, halving net growth; a 322 case has a ratio near **8**, slowing growth by roughly **13%**. Encounters may still dominate over gravitational-wave depletion after saturation.\n\nQuadratic matter couplings make stars more effective scatterers. The convention is \\(-d_Nm_N\\phi^2\\bar NN/(2M_{\\rm Pl}^2)\\), with positive d_N for the repulsive case studied. Threshold statements such as negligible effects below **d_N ≈ 900** for one cusp or **50** for a mass-segregated cusp are conditional benchmarks, not observational bounds. Stellar distributions, orbital geometry, omitted bound-to-bound mixing and cloud self-interactions limit extrapolation. The analysis does not deliver one environment-independent corrected superradiance exclusion.\n\n##### History\n\n[Baumann, Chia and Porto, 1804.03208](https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.03208), established tidal mixing and resonant cloud depletion in binaries. Environmental disruption is thus a known issue. The new calculation isolates bound-to-continuum ionization by a stellar population and adds quadratic matter interactions. A loss process that beats late-time gravitational-wave emission need not outrun initial superradiant growth; those comparisons have different implications for spin constraints."}, {"id": "paper-2610-40907", "arxiv": "2610.40907", "title": "The Importance of Curium-250 to the $\\gamma$-Ray Signature of $r$-Process Remnants", "authors": "Axel Gross, Samuel Cupp, G. Wendell Misch, Matthew R. Mumpower", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["r-process", "Gamma rays", "Neutron-star mergers"], "label": "Nuclear-network and emission forecast", "teaser": "Curium-250 as a gamma-ray beacon of old merger remnants", "review_date": "2026-11-11", "listing_date": "2026-11-02", "version": "v1", "submission_date": "2026-10-29T19:27:38Z", "path": "2026/11/02/index.html#paper-2610-40907", "reportDate": "2 November 2026", "searchText": "The Importance of Curium-250 to the $\\gamma$-Ray Signature of $r$-Process Remnants Axel Gross, Samuel Cupp, G. Wendell Misch, Matthew R. Mumpower 2610.40907 Curium-250 as a gamma-ray beacon of old merger remnants 2026-11-02 2 November 2026 r-process Gamma rays Neutron-star mergers #### 10. Curium-250 as a gamma-ray beacon of old merger remnants\n\n**“The Importance of Curium-250 to the $\\gamma$-Ray Signature of $r$-Process Remnants” — Axel Gross, Samuel Cupp, G. Wendell Misch, Matthew R. Mumpower.** [arXiv:2610.40907v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40907v1) · [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40907v1) · [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40907v1). Submitted **29 October 2026, 19:27:38 UTC**; UTC listing **2 November 2026**; primary category **astro-ph.HE**. **Nuclear-network and emission forecast.** Preprint; no journal reference was shown on the checked version record.\n\nThe paper follows long-lived r-process ejecta with **15,625 PRISM trajectories**, varying density, expansion time and electron fraction with specified mass, fission and de-excitation models. It includes prompt fission photons and radioactive daughters, asking which isotopes remain bright thousands of years after a merger.\n\n**²⁵⁰Cm** has adopted spontaneous-fission partial half-life **11,300 ± 500 years** and inferred total half-life about **8,300 years**, including competing branches; some branch inputs are estimates rather than measurements. Its mass fraction is about **0.35%** in a standard example and reaches **2%** within the grid. In an illustrative composition its fission products supply over half the **0.2–5 MeV** flux for approximately **2,200–22,000 years**, peaking near **67%**. The predicted spectrum includes potentially useful daughter features such as **1,079 keV ²⁴⁶ᵐAm**.\n\nDetectability examples use **2 × 10⁻⁴ M⊙ of ²⁵⁰Cm at 1 kpc**. That yield and a corresponding nearby remnant are not observed. Fission yields, neutron-rich nuclei, isomers and the merger ejecta distribution remain major uncertainties. Instrument comparisons are forecasts, not a detection or a measured Galactic merger rate.\n\n##### History\n\n[Korobkin et al., 1905.05089](https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.05089), already calculated gamma-ray signatures of fresh ejecta and old Galactic merger remnants, emphasizing nuclear-yield uncertainty. The strategy therefore predates this paper. The addition is a potentially important long-lived fission contribution and its daughter emission, which can change the useful isotope and age windows. It does not remove actinide-production uncertainties or guarantee an observable remnant within a kiloparsec."}, {"id": "paper-2610-40911", "arxiv": "2610.40911", "title": "Search for long-lived heavy neutral leptons in signatures with one prompt muon and a secondary vertex formed by two non-prompt muons in proton-proton collisions at $\\sqrt{s}$ = 13 and 13.6 TeV", "authors": "CMS Collaboration", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["Heavy neutral leptons", "Neutrinos", "BSM"], "label": "Collider exclusion analysis", "teaser": "CMS searches farther from the beam line for long-lived heavy neutrinos", "review_date": "2026-11-11", "listing_date": "2026-11-02", "version": "v1", "submission_date": "2026-10-29T19:30:10Z", "path": "2026/11/02/index.html#paper-2610-40911", "reportDate": "2 November 2026", "searchText": "Search for long-lived heavy neutral leptons in signatures with one prompt muon and a secondary vertex formed by two non-prompt muons in proton-proton collisions at $\\sqrt{s}$ = 13 and 13.6 TeV CMS Collaboration 2610.40911 CMS searches farther from the beam line for long-lived heavy neutrinos 2026-11-02 2 November 2026 Heavy neutral leptons Neutrinos BSM #### 11. CMS searches farther from the beam line for long-lived heavy neutrinos\n\n**“Search for long-lived heavy neutral leptons in signatures with one prompt muon and a secondary vertex formed by two non-prompt muons in proton-proton collisions at $\\sqrt{s}$ = 13 and 13.6 TeV” — CMS Collaboration.** [arXiv:2610.40911v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40911v1) · [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40911v1) · [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40911v1). Submitted **29 October 2026, 19:30:10 UTC**; UTC listing **2 November 2026**; primary category **hep-ex**. **Collider exclusion analysis.** Preprint submitted to JHEP.\n\nCMS targets \\(W\\to\\mu N\\), followed by a displaced \\(N\\to\\mu\\mu\\nu\\) decay, using **138 fb⁻¹ at 13 TeV (2016–2018)** and **34.6 fb⁻¹ at 13.6 TeV (2022)**. Two displaced standalone muons without tracker requirements extend the useful transverse decay radius to approximately **50–400 cm**. The interpretation assumes a **Majorana** heavy neutral lepton with **mass 1–5 GeV**, mixing only with muon neutrinos; electron and tau mixings are fixed to zero.\n\nAn ABCD construction estimates instrumental backgrounds from data, with transfer factors and uncertainties propagated through the signal-region bins. The observed sample shows no significant excess. Profile-likelihood **CLs limits at 95% confidence** exclude regions in the mass–\\(|V_{\\mu N}|^2\\) plane, rather than supplying one mass-independent mixing bound. The expected sensitivity improves over earlier CMS searches over roughly **2.5–4.2 GeV**; this does not establish a global observed record throughout that interval.\n\nThe result is particularly sensitive to lifetime and detector acceptance. Changing flavor admixtures, whether N is Majorana or Dirac, or the decay model changes both production and branching/lifetime relationships. The figures' exclusion contours should therefore be used with their specified assumptions, not quoted as generic bounds on every sterile-neutrino model.\n\n##### History\n\n[CMS, 2402.18658](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.18658), previously searched for HNL decays through showers in the muon system. Its pure-muon **Majorana** interpretation reached **|VμN|² = 5.0 × 10⁻⁶ at mN = 2.8 GeV**, at **95% confidence**. The often nearby quoted **4.6 × 10⁻⁶ at 3.3 GeV** belongs to its **Dirac** interpretation and must not be substituted. Beam-dump constraints are particularly relevant at lower masses, while collider strategies sample different lifetimes and final states. The new displaced-dimuon reconstruction fills a complementary acceptance region. A single improvement factor would hide those differences and the mass dependence of the observed contours."}, {"id": "paper-2610-40912", "arxiv": "2610.40912", "title": "Incorporating deep neural networks into lepton-nucleus interaction modeling", "authors": "Hemant Prasad, Jan T. Sobczyk, Krzysztof M. Graczyk, Beata E. Kowal, Rwik Dharmapal Banerjee, J. Luis Bonilla", "primary_category": "nucl-th", "tags": ["Neutrino interactions", "Nuclear physics", "Machine learning"], "label": "Neural-network and event-generator method", "teaser": "A learned inclusive carbon cross section does not determine exclusive final states", "review_date": "2026-11-11", "listing_date": "2026-11-02", "version": "v1", "submission_date": "2026-10-29T19:30:25Z", "path": "2026/11/02/index.html#paper-2610-40912", "reportDate": "2 November 2026", "searchText": "Incorporating deep neural networks into lepton-nucleus interaction modeling Hemant Prasad, Jan T. Sobczyk, Krzysztof M. Graczyk, Beata E. Kowal, Rwik Dharmapal Banerjee, J. Luis Bonilla 2610.40912 A learned inclusive carbon cross section does not determine exclusive final states 2026-11-02 2 November 2026 Neutrino interactions Nuclear physics Machine learning #### 12. A learned inclusive carbon cross section does not determine exclusive final states\n\n**“Incorporating deep neural networks into lepton-nucleus interaction modeling” — Hemant Prasad, Jan T. Sobczyk, Krzysztof M. Graczyk, Beata E. Kowal, Rwik Dharmapal Banerjee, J. Luis Bonilla.** [arXiv:2610.40912v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40912v1) · [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40912v1) · [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40912v1). Submitted **29 October 2026, 19:30:25 UTC**; UTC listing **2 November 2026**; primary category **nucl-th**. **Neural-network and event-generator method.** Preprint; no journal reference was shown on the checked version record.\n\nThe authors connect an empirical electron–carbon deep-neural-network cross section to **NuWro/eWro** event generation. A **50-network bootstrap ensemble** describes inclusive data; quasielastic, meson-exchange-current and pion components are assigned using generator fractions \\(f_c=\\sigma_c^{MC}/\\sum_c\\sigma_c^{MC}\\), so that \\(\\sigma_c^{new}=f_c\\sigma_{\\rm DNN}\\). This preserves the generator's exclusive decomposition rather than learning that decomposition from inclusive data.\n\nThe intended domain extends from energy transfer **ω > 26 MeV** through the right half-width of the Δ region, with training coverage reaching **Q² = 10 GeV²**; deep-inelastic scattering is not modeled. SLAC comparisons within training are useful closure checks. More diagnostic are independent **CLAS measurements at beam energy 1.159 GeV and angle 37.5°**: discrepancies remain around **15% in the quasielastic region** and **30% near the Δ**. In the illustrated zero-pion reconstructed-energy distribution, deficits are about **20% at the peak** and **45% in a tail**; the Δ-associated discrepancy can remain near **40%**.\n\nThe procedure provides a controlled way to propagate inclusive empirical information into simulations, but cannot determine unseen channel composition or final-state interactions uniquely. Electron scattering also does not fix the neutrino axial response. The reported bootstrap spread is not a complete uncertainty on neutrino energy reconstruction or oscillation parameters.\n\n##### History\n\n[Kowal et al., 2312.17298](https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.17298), already developed the empirical carbon cross-section network and compared bootstrap and dropout uncertainties. The advance here is its coupling to exclusive event generation and the resulting reconstruction tests. The history matters because inclusive agreement was the starting point, not a new demonstration that every generated channel is correct. Independent exclusive data expose precisely the assumptions that an inclusive fit cannot identify."}, {"id": "paper-2610-40958", "arxiv": "2610.40958", "title": "Optimizing Prompt Radio Counterpart Searches for Gravitational-Wave Events with the Deep Synoptic Array", "authors": "Nikita Kosogorov, Max Melching, Gregg Hallinan, Jonah Kanner, Alan J. Weinstein, Kevin Lacker, Vikram Ravi", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Multimessenger", "Gravitational waves", "Fast radio bursts"], "label": "Observing-strategy simulation and hardware demonstration", "teaser": "Steering a radio array toward mergers before coalescence", "review_date": "2026-11-11", "listing_date": "2026-11-02", "version": "v1", "submission_date": "2026-10-29T20:37:22Z", "path": "2026/11/02/index.html#paper-2610-40958", "reportDate": "2 November 2026", "searchText": "Optimizing Prompt Radio Counterpart Searches for Gravitational-Wave Events with the Deep Synoptic Array Nikita Kosogorov, Max Melching, Gregg Hallinan, Jonah Kanner, Alan J. Weinstein, Kevin Lacker, Vikram Ravi 2610.40958 Steering a radio array toward mergers before coalescence 2026-11-02 2 November 2026 Multimessenger Gravitational waves Fast radio bursts #### 13. Steering a radio array toward mergers before coalescence\n\n**“Optimizing Prompt Radio Counterpart Searches for Gravitational-Wave Events with the Deep Synoptic Array” — Nikita Kosogorov, Max Melching, Gregg Hallinan, Jonah Kanner, Alan J. Weinstein, Kevin Lacker, Vikram Ravi.** [arXiv:2610.40958v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40958v1) · [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40958v1) · [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40958v1). Submitted **29 October 2026, 20:37:22 UTC**; UTC listing **2 November 2026**; primary category **astro-ph.HE**. **Observing-strategy simulation and hardware demonstration.** Accepted for publication in The Astrophysical Journal, according to the arXiv record.\n\nThe study tests prompt radio follow-up with a rapidly steerable DSA system. It simulates an O5-like **HLVA** network, binary neutron stars within **250 Mpc** and selected neutron-star–black-hole systems within **400 Mpc**, comparing peak-probability pointing with reinforcement learning as early-warning maps evolve. The nominal field is **18 deg²**, minimum elevation **20°**, and slew speed **1.5° s⁻¹**; the observing window spans roughly **60 seconds before to 20 seconds after merger**.\n\nPeak-probability pointing performs as well as reinforcement learning for most cases. A classifier routes only about **2%** of events to a learned strategy; the quoted **3.15% improvement** applies to that selected subset, not the full population. About **37%** of the simulated events are above the horizon. Conditional neutron-star-binary acquisition is around **90%** for the zero-error pointing case and **75%** with the modeled Gaussian pointing error. Acquisition of the true position is not detection of a radio counterpart.\n\nA **12 June 2026** two-antenna prototype exercise tests preplanned tracking, including winds around **8 m s⁻¹**, delays up to roughly **1 s** and an initial pointing error around **1°**. It is not a real merger-alert detection. A projected **20 mJy ms** fluence threshold at **700–1,025 MHz** depends on the future signal-processing chain, gain and interference environment. The simulation's requirement for complete early-warning sequences also favors relatively detectable events.\n\n##### History\n\n[Tian et al., 2309.16383](https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.16383), already investigated prompt merger-radio searches with MWA subarrays, exploiting wide fields and dispersive delay. Counterpart existence, beaming and viewing angle remain uncertain independently of telescope pointing. This paper adds a DSA-specific fast-steering strategy and an early hardware demonstration. Its positional-acquisition fractions cannot be translated into a counterpart yield without an emission population model."}, {"id": "paper-2610-41739", "arxiv": "2610.41739", "title": "Gamma-Ray Emission from LS 5039: A 30-500 GeV Excess and Phase-Resolved Broadband Spectral Modeling", "authors": "Xiaozhen Zhao, Jiandong Zhang, Pengfei Zhang", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Cosmic rays", "Particle acceleration"], "label": "Gamma-ray analysis and competing emission models", "teaser": "LS 5039 has an extra GeV component—but the proton model faces an energy-budget problem", "review_date": "2026-11-11", "listing_date": "2026-11-02", "version": "v1", "submission_date": "2026-10-30T13:47:48Z", "path": "2026/11/02/index.html#paper-2610-41739", "reportDate": "2 November 2026", "searchText": "Gamma-Ray Emission from LS 5039: A 30-500 GeV Excess and Phase-Resolved Broadband Spectral Modeling Xiaozhen Zhao, Jiandong Zhang, Pengfei Zhang 2610.41739 LS 5039 has an extra GeV component—but the proton model faces an energy-budget problem 2026-11-02 2 November 2026 Gamma rays Cosmic rays Particle acceleration #### 14. LS 5039 has an extra GeV component—but the proton model faces an energy-budget problem\n\n**“Gamma-Ray Emission from LS 5039: A 30-500 GeV Excess and Phase-Resolved Broadband Spectral Modeling” — Xiaozhen Zhao, Jiandong Zhang, Pengfei Zhang.** [arXiv:2610.41739v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.41739v1) · [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.41739v1) · [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.41739v1). Submitted **30 October 2026, 13:47:48 UTC**; UTC listing **2 November 2026**; primary category **astro-ph.HE**. **Gamma-ray analysis and competing emission models.** Preprint; no journal reference was shown on the checked version record.\n\nUsing **17.3 years of Fermi-LAT data**, from **4 August 2008 to 25 November 2025**, the authors fit **0.1–500 GeV** emission from LS 5039 and combine it with phase-resolved HESS and HAWC spectra. Adding a log-parabola to a broken power law gives **ΔTS = 72.34**, interpreted as **7.90σ** using the adopted three-parameter χ² prescription. This is a local, model-conditional extra-component significance—not evidence for hadrons at 7.9σ.\n\nThe physical fits assume a one-zone pulsar-wind-shock geometry, distance **2.5 kpc**, inclination **60°**, a **1.4 M⊙** compact object and spin-down power **2 × 10³⁶ erg s⁻¹**. Representative fields are **2–4.5 G**, electron cutoffs **28–41 TeV**, and proton maximum energy **0.346 PeV**. The hybrid model is favored over the tested leptonic model by **ΔBIC = 10.8** in superior conjunction and **4.27** in inferior conjunction, yet reduced χ² values remain about **2.96 and 2.78**.\n\nA decisive caveat is energetics: fitted proton reservoirs and short escape times imply powers around **2.08 × 10³⁹** and **5.99 × 10³⁹ erg s⁻¹**, roughly **1,000–3,000 times** the assumed spin-down power. The model also underpredicts X rays. Noncontemporaneous phase-folded data, excluded upper limits and a restrictive one-zone geometry further weaken a unique interpretation. The spectral feature is interesting; a physically self-consistent hadronic explanation is not established.\n\n##### History\n\n[HAWC, 2503.20947v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.20947v2), had already found orbital modulation over **2–118 TeV** at **4.7σ** in **2,859 days** and evidence of emission extending to very high energies; the modulation above **100 TeV** was only **2.2σ**. Such photons motivate extreme particle acceleration but do not alone distinguish electrons from protons. The new LAT analysis addresses the GeV bridge and phase-resolved modeling. Its formal preference between two tested spectra does not solve the pre-existing leptonic/hadronic degeneracy, especially when the favored proton model exceeds its own power budget."}, {"id": "paper-2610-41749", "arxiv": "2610.41749", "title": "Inferring dominant acceleration mechanisms using the longitudinal spread of solar particle intensities: 28 May 2021 event", "authors": "Jaclyn T. Lang, R. Du Toit Strauss, Jan Gieseler, Stefan Jensen, J. Grant Mitchell, Rami Vainio, Nina Dresing", "primary_category": "astro-ph.SR", "tags": ["Particle acceleration", "Cosmic rays", "Solar energetic particles"], "label": "Heliospheric particle observations and transport modeling", "teaser": "A multi-spacecraft SEP event separates electron and proton transport", "review_date": "2026-11-11", "listing_date": "2026-11-02", "version": "v1", "submission_date": "2026-10-30T13:52:40Z", "path": "2026/11/02/index.html#paper-2610-41749", "reportDate": "2 November 2026", "searchText": "Inferring dominant acceleration mechanisms using the longitudinal spread of solar particle intensities: 28 May 2021 event Jaclyn T. Lang, R. Du Toit Strauss, Jan Gieseler, Stefan Jensen, J. Grant Mitchell, Rami Vainio, Nina Dresing 2610.41749 A multi-spacecraft SEP event separates electron and proton transport 2026-11-02 2 November 2026 Particle acceleration Cosmic rays Solar energetic particles #### 15. A multi-spacecraft SEP event separates electron and proton transport\n\n**“Inferring dominant acceleration mechanisms using the longitudinal spread of solar particle intensities: 28 May 2021 event” — Jaclyn T. Lang, R. Du Toit Strauss, Jan Gieseler, Stefan Jensen, J. Grant Mitchell, Rami Vainio, Nina Dresing.** [arXiv:2610.41749v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.41749v1) · [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.41749v1) · [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.41749v1). Submitted **30 October 2026, 13:52:40 UTC**; UTC listing **2 November 2026**; primary category **astro-ph.SR**. **Heliospheric particle observations and transport modeling.** Preprint; no journal reference was shown on the checked version record.\n\nThis is a new analysis of the **28 May 2021** solar energetic-particle event, not an event that occurred in November 2026. The associated **C9.4 flare at N18W67 began at 22:19 UTC**. Five spacecraft at four longitudinal viewpoints—Parker Solar Probe, Solar Orbiter, STEREO-A and near-Earth instruments—provide cross-calibrated particle measurements, with radial scaling between roughly **0.69 and 1 au**.\n\nFifteen-minute Gaussian longitude fits show a relatively stable **∼0.1 MeV electron** distribution centered near **51°**, with width about **34°**, while **14 and 32 MeV protons** broaden and shift westward. For 14 MeV protons the fitted width changes from **32.4° ± 12° to 40.8° ± 1.9°** between **03:00 and 12:00 UTC on 29 May**. Contaminated electron data after 08:00 and insufficiently sampled proton intervals are treated separately.\n\nA two-dimensional transport model can reproduce the proton trend using finite injection, convection and perpendicular diffusion: representative 14 MeV mean free paths are **λ∥ = 3.03 au** and **λ⊥ = 0.005 λ∥**. These are fitted model choices, not universal turbulence measurements. A shock-related proton contribution is favored, but four-viewpoint Gaussian fitting, assumed Parker Solar Probe cross-calibration and the absence of a comprehensive anisotropy fit leave injection–transport degeneracies.\n\n##### History\n\n[Dresing et al., 1802.04722](https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.04722), studied the **26 December 2013** event with multi-spacecraft, time-dependent longitudinal distributions and long-lasting anisotropies. They already found that different particle species and energies need not fill interplanetary space identically, considering shock acceleration and trapping in interacting CMEs. The present event adds more favorable spacecraft coverage and a quantitative transport comparison. A westward-moving fitted center is therefore not, by itself, a unique signature of one acceleration mechanism."}, {"id": "paper-2610-41775", "arxiv": "2610.41775", "title": "First Light with the Dark100 Array of PANOSETI Telescopes at Palomar Observatory", "authors": "Nikolas Korzoun, Aaron Brown, Stephen Fegan, Luca Fiedler, Benjamin Godfrey, Jamie Holder, Paul Horowitz, Andrew W. Howard, Alyssa Johnson, Wei Liu, Jérôme Maire, Yuriy Popovych, Elisa Pueschel, Nicolas Rault-Wang, Sruthiranjani Ravikularaman, Dan Werthimer, Shelley A. Wright", "primary_category": "astro-ph.IM", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Instrumentation"], "label": "Gamma-ray instrument commissioning report", "teaser": "Dark100 is a promising array under commissioning—not a Crab detection yet", "review_date": "2026-11-11", "listing_date": "2026-11-02", "version": "v1", "submission_date": "2026-10-30T14:15:02Z", "path": "2026/11/02/index.html#paper-2610-41775", "reportDate": "2 November 2026", "searchText": "First Light with the Dark100 Array of PANOSETI Telescopes at Palomar Observatory Nikolas Korzoun, Aaron Brown, Stephen Fegan, Luca Fiedler, Benjamin Godfrey, Jamie Holder, Paul Horowitz, Andrew W. Howard, Alyssa Johnson, Wei Liu, Jérôme Maire, Yuriy Popovych, Elisa Pueschel, Nicolas Rault-Wang, Sruthiranjani Ravikularaman, Dan Werthimer, Shelley A. Wright 2610.41775 Dark100 is a promising array under commissioning—not a Crab detection yet 2026-11-02 2 November 2026 Gamma rays Instrumentation #### 16. Dark100 is a promising array under commissioning—not a Crab detection yet\n\n**“First Light with the Dark100 Array of PANOSETI Telescopes at Palomar Observatory” — Nikolas Korzoun, Aaron Brown, Stephen Fegan, Luca Fiedler, Benjamin Godfrey, Jamie Holder, Paul Horowitz, Andrew W. Howard, Alyssa Johnson, Wei Liu, Jérôme Maire, Yuriy Popovych, Elisa Pueschel, Nicolas Rault-Wang, Sruthiranjani Ravikularaman, Dan Werthimer, Shelley A. Wright.** [arXiv:2610.41775v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.41775v1) · [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.41775v1) · [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.41775v1). Submitted **30 October 2026, 14:15:02 UTC**; UTC listing **2 November 2026**; primary category **astro-ph.IM**. **Gamma-ray instrument commissioning report.** Gamma 2026 conference proceedings contribution.\n\nDark100 develops a seven-telescope optical air-shower array using **0.46 m Fresnel lenses**, **1,024 SiPM pixels per telescope**, roughly **0.32° pixels** and a **10° × 10°** field. Four units had been installed by **December 2025**, but the outlying unit was not part of the three-telescope coincidence configuration in the reported initial observations.\n\nApproximately **50 hours of Crab observations in January 2026** yield **2.03σ**, which is not a source detection. The paper discusses focus and gain-calibration issues that limit that commissioning data set. For a future seven-telescope configuration, simulations forecast angular resolution around **0.12° above 100 TeV** and collection area near **0.3 km² before gamma/hadron selection**.\n\nThe distinction between pre-selection collection area and final sensitivity is essential: background rejection, effective exposure and reconstruction performance still determine whether a source is detectable. The report is a proceedings contribution associated with **Gamma 2026, 31 August–4 September**, rather than a new November observing campaign or a demonstrated final sensitivity curve.\n\n##### History\n\nThe related [PANOSETI air-shower demonstration, 2210.01356](https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.01356), reported **6.2 hours** of joint observations with VERITAS on **7 November 2021**, including **10,540 coincident showers** and three VERITAS-identified gamma rays at approximately **15, 36 and 51 TeV**. Thus compact Fresnel/SiPM telescopes already had an air-shower proof of concept. Dark100 advances the array implementation and commissioning, but its 2.03σ Crab excess does not constitute a new established gamma-ray detection."}, {"id": "paper-2610-41842", "arxiv": "2610.41842", "title": "The multiple origins of spurs in stellar streams: a challenge for dark matter diagnostics", "authors": "Claire Guillaume, Alicia Rivero, Titouan Le Guillois, Florent Renaud, Guillaume F. Thomas, Nicolas F. Martin", "primary_category": "astro-ph.GA", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Stellar streams", "Galactic dynamics"], "label": "Controlled dynamical simulations", "teaser": "Stellar-stream spurs are not unique dark-subhalo fingerprints", "review_date": "2026-11-11", "listing_date": "2026-11-02", "version": "v1", "submission_date": "2026-10-30T14:58:34Z", "path": "2026/11/02/index.html#paper-2610-41842", "reportDate": "2 November 2026", "searchText": "The multiple origins of spurs in stellar streams: a challenge for dark matter diagnostics Claire Guillaume, Alicia Rivero, Titouan Le Guillois, Florent Renaud, Guillaume F. Thomas, Nicolas F. Martin 2610.41842 Stellar-stream spurs are not unique dark-subhalo fingerprints 2026-11-02 2 November 2026 Dark matter Stellar streams Galactic dynamics #### 17. Stellar-stream spurs are not unique dark-subhalo fingerprints\n\n**“The multiple origins of spurs in stellar streams: a challenge for dark matter diagnostics” — Claire Guillaume, Alicia Rivero, Titouan Le Guillois, Florent Renaud, Guillaume F. Thomas, Nicolas F. Martin.** [arXiv:2610.41842v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.41842v1) · [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.41842v1) · [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.41842v1). Submitted **30 October 2026, 14:58:34 UTC**; UTC listing **2 November 2026**; primary category **astro-ph.GA**. **Controlled dynamical simulations.** Preprint; no journal reference was shown on the checked version record.\n\nThe study compares stream morphology for **36 star clusters evolved for 5 Gyr** in four Galactic environments: an axisymmetric baseline, a **1:10 merger**, a rotating bar and a dark-subhalo population. The subhalo setup places **10% of the dark matter mass** in **10⁵–10⁹ M⊙ Plummer subhalos**, with mass function proportional to **M⁻¹·⁹**. The bar example extends to **8 kpc** and slows from about **27 to 17 km s⁻¹ kpc⁻¹**.\n\nSpur-like features occur in roughly **50% of merger**, **25% of barred** and **28% of subhalo** simulations. These fractions describe the chosen simulation ensemble; they are not posterior probabilities that an observed spur was made by a merger, bar or dark subhalo. The bar effects are particularly relevant to streams with pericenters below approximately **11 kpc**.\n\nThe phase-space comparisons suggest that line-of-sight velocities and along-stream proper motions can help distinguish histories, but no actual GD-1 likelihood fit or dark-matter cross-section/mass bound is derived. Giant molecular clouds, spiral structure and other perturbers are omitted, the subhalos are Plummer rather than NFW systems, and the modeled cluster/stream histories are restricted. Morphology alone is therefore an incomplete inverse problem.\n\n##### History\n\n[Bonaca et al., 1811.03631](https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.03631), modeled GD-1's gap and spur with a dense perturber. Its acceptable perturber regions are conditional on that encounter model, not a model-independent posterior proving a dark subhalo. The new controlled comparison demonstrates alternative spur-producing environments and motivates additional kinematic information. It does not disprove the specific GD-1 encounter interpretation or establish that all observed spurs have non-dark-matter origins."}, {"id": "paper-2610-41846", "arxiv": "2610.41846", "title": "A multi-wavelength view of PSR J0437-4715 with NICER and other missions: Radius measurements from far-ultraviolet to hard X-ray pulse profile modeling", "authors": "Pierre Stammler, Sebastien Guillot, Denis González-Caniulef, Isak Svensson, Anna L. Watts, Tuomo Salmi, Yves Kini, Mariska Hoogkamer, Devarshi Choudhury, Bas Dorsman, Hannah Göttling, Kai Hebeler, Daniela Huppenkothen, Christine Kazantsev, Lucien Mauviard, Melissa Mendes, Jean-François Olive, Daniel J. Reardon, Nathan Rutherford, Achim Schwenk", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Neutron stars", "Dense matter", "X rays"], "label": "Joint neutron-star mass–radius inference", "teaser": "PSR J0437−4715: a narrower radius conditional on a richer emission model", "review_date": "2026-11-11", "listing_date": "2026-11-02", "version": "v1", "submission_date": "2026-10-30T15:01:07Z", "path": "2026/11/02/index.html#paper-2610-41846", "reportDate": "2 November 2026", "searchText": "A multi-wavelength view of PSR J0437-4715 with NICER and other missions: Radius measurements from far-ultraviolet to hard X-ray pulse profile modeling Pierre Stammler, Sebastien Guillot, Denis González-Caniulef, Isak Svensson, Anna L. Watts, Tuomo Salmi, Yves Kini, Mariska Hoogkamer, Devarshi Choudhury, Bas Dorsman, Hannah Göttling, Kai Hebeler, Daniela Huppenkothen, Christine Kazantsev, Lucien Mauviard, Melissa Mendes, Jean-François Olive, Daniel J. Reardon, Nathan Rutherford, Achim Schwenk 2610.41846 PSR J0437−4715: a narrower radius conditional on a richer emission model 2026-11-02 2 November 2026 Neutron stars Dense matter X rays #### 18. PSR J0437−4715: a narrower radius conditional on a richer emission model\n\n**“A multi-wavelength view of PSR J0437-4715 with NICER and other missions: Radius measurements from far-ultraviolet to hard X-ray pulse profile modeling” — Pierre Stammler, Sebastien Guillot, Denis González-Caniulef, Isak Svensson, Anna L. Watts, Tuomo Salmi, Yves Kini, Mariska Hoogkamer, Devarshi Choudhury, Bas Dorsman, Hannah Göttling, Kai Hebeler, Daniela Huppenkothen, Christine Kazantsev, Lucien Mauviard, Melissa Mendes, Jean-François Olive, Daniel J. Reardon, Nathan Rutherford, Achim Schwenk.** [arXiv:2610.41846v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.41846v1) · [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.41846v1) · [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.41846v1). Submitted **30 October 2026, 15:01:07 UTC**; UTC listing **2 November 2026**; primary category **astro-ph.HE**. **Joint neutron-star mass–radius inference.** Preprint submitted to The Astrophysical Journal Letters.\n\nThe analysis combines **2.56 Ms of NICER data from 2017–2023**, **113 ks of XMM-Newton data from 2009**, **221 ks of NuSTAR data from 2014–2015**, and **14,150 s of HST/STIS ultraviolet observations from 2001**. XPSI models hydrogen-atmosphere hot spots, a partially ionized warm stellar surface and a pulsed power-law component. Ultraviolet emission supplies information on apparent emitting area, complementing the pulse-profile compactness constraint.\n\nWith radio priors **M = 1.418 ± 0.044 M⊙**, **d = 156.98 ± 0.15 pc** and a tightly constrained inclination, a radius prior uniform over **4.43–16 km**, and sampled absorption, reddening and calibration nuisance parameters, the preferred model gives **M = 1.377 (+0.038/−0.034) M⊙** and equatorial radius **R = 11.35 (+0.40/−0.39) km**, at **68% posterior credibility**.\n\nThe smaller error bar is conditional on the surface/emission family and informative external constraints. A simpler spot model gives a smaller radius and poor residuals; the richer model has a large evidence advantage, but the warm-temperature parameter reaches a boundary, lowering that boundary produces problematic multimodality, and a more flexible model did not converge. Removing the mass prior can drive the fit toward its lower mass boundary. The result is not a fully model-marginalized neutron-star radius uncertainty.\n\n##### History\n\n[Choudhury et al., 2407.06789](https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.06789), obtained **R = 11.36 (+0.95/−0.63) km at 68% credibility** using NICER and radio information. The new central value agrees closely; the principal change is narrower conditional uncertainty from extra spectral bands and a revised emission model. Because data, priors and surface assumptions differ, the reduction should not be treated as a universal precision improvement independent of modeling. The joint paper itself exposes the remaining surface-temperature and geometry sensitivities."}, {"id": "paper-2610-40925", "arxiv": "2610.40925", "title": "Equipartition between Alfvénic turbulence and energetic ions as the origin of the -5 common spectrum in the heliosphere", "authors": "Stanislav Boldyrev, Daniel Humphrey, Vadim Roytershteyn", "primary_category": "physics.plasm-ph", "tags": ["Particle acceleration", "Plasma turbulence", "Cosmic rays"], "label": "Analytic acceleration model with observational comparisons", "teaser": "A proposed equipartition origin for suprathermal ion tails", "review_date": "2026-11-11", "listing_date": "2026-11-02", "version": "v1", "submission_date": "2026-10-29T19:50:26Z", "path": "2026/11/02/index.html#paper-2610-40925", "reportDate": "2 November 2026", "searchText": "Equipartition between Alfvénic turbulence and energetic ions as the origin of the -5 common spectrum in the heliosphere Stanislav Boldyrev, Daniel Humphrey, Vadim Roytershteyn 2610.40925 A proposed equipartition origin for suprathermal ion tails 2026-11-02 2 November 2026 Particle acceleration Plasma turbulence Cosmic rays #### 19. A proposed equipartition origin for suprathermal ion tails\n\n**“Equipartition between Alfvénic turbulence and energetic ions as the origin of the -5 common spectrum in the heliosphere” — Stanislav Boldyrev, Daniel Humphrey, Vadim Roytershteyn.** [arXiv:2610.40925v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40925v1) · [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40925v1) · [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40925v1). Submitted **29 October 2026, 19:50:26 UTC**; UTC listing **2 November 2026**; primary category **physics.plasm-ph**. **Analytic acceleration model with observational comparisons.** Preprint; no journal reference was shown on the checked version record.\n\nStrong Alfvénic turbulence is modeled scale by scale with a parallel magnetic spectrum proportional to **k∥⁻²**. Cyclotron resonance connects a particle speed to a turbulent scale, while the accessible pitch-angle cone changes with speed. Equating energy in resonant fluctuations and suprathermal particles yields a phase-space distribution \\(F(v)=B_0^2/(16\\pi^3m_i v^5)\\), or an energy intensity proportional to **B₀² T⁻³ᐟ²**. The normalization includes order-unity modeling choices; this is not an exact universal coefficient.\n\nThe authors compare the prediction with digitized **ACE observations on 12 August and 25 October 2001**, and Voyager measurements. Ratios of measured to predicted intensity span roughly **0.6 and 1.4** in the August examples and **0.5 and 4.5** in October. For Voyager, the predicted intensity at **1 MeV** changes from about **0.98** to **2.5 (cm² sr s MeV)⁻¹** as the adopted field rises from **1.5 to 2.4 μG**, whereas the fitted observed values are about **2.1 and 1.15**. The opposite temporal trend is an important limitation, potentially involving escape.\n\nThis is a physically motivated normalization argument across varied environments, not a statistical demonstration of a universal acceleration law. Quiet-time tails can be orders of magnitude lower, multiple ion species complicate the shared energy budget, and escape and time dependence are not fully closed. Nothing here establishes extrapolation to UHECR acceleration.\n\n##### History\n\n[Boldyrev et al., 2605.02219](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.02219), already developed a scale-by-scale turbulent/particle balance producing the corresponding suprathermal power-law behavior. The present paper sharpens the magnetic-field-dependent normalization and observational comparison. The longstanding empirical power-law tail is the baseline to explain, not a newly discovered population. Agreement within factors of order unity in selected active intervals is encouraging, while the Voyager trend and quiet intervals show why a full transport and multi-species energy treatment remains necessary."}, {"id": "paper-2610-40774", "arxiv": "2610.40774", "title": "Surprisingly normal? GW241011 and GW241110 in the context of hierarchical merger classification", "authors": "Cailin Plunkett, Michael Zevin, Salvatore Vitale", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Gravitational waves", "Black-hole populations"], "label": "Population classification and injection study", "teaser": "Which black-hole mergers can be identified as hierarchical?", "review_date": "2026-11-11", "listing_date": "2026-11-02", "version": "v1", "submission_date": "2026-10-29T18:00:09Z", "path": "2026/11/02/index.html#paper-2610-40774", "reportDate": "2 November 2026", "searchText": "Surprisingly normal? GW241011 and GW241110 in the context of hierarchical merger classification Cailin Plunkett, Michael Zevin, Salvatore Vitale 2610.40774 Which black-hole mergers can be identified as hierarchical? 2026-11-02 2 November 2026 Gravitational waves Black-hole populations #### 20. Which black-hole mergers can be identified as hierarchical?\n\n**“Surprisingly normal? GW241011 and GW241110 in the context of hierarchical merger classification” — Cailin Plunkett, Michael Zevin, Salvatore Vitale.** [arXiv:2610.40774v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40774v1) · [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40774v1) · [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40774v1). Submitted **29 October 2026, 18:00:09 UTC**; UTC listing **2 November 2026**; primary category **astro-ph.HE**. **Population classification and injection study.** Preprint; no journal reference was shown on the checked version record.\n\nThe paper asks whether gravitational-wave spin measurements can identify black holes made in an earlier merger. It uses cluster population simulations, a grid of binary injections and O4-like parameter estimation. The grid spans detector-frame primary masses **10–120 M⊙**, mass ratios **0.25–1**, varying spin tilt, inclinations and **S/N = 12 or 30**. The representative primary spin is **0.7** and secondary spin zero.\n\nWithin **755 simulated detected events**, a classification using precession alone selects **182 (24%)** at the adopted **ln likelihood-ratio > 3** threshold; combining effective aligned spin and precession selects **265 (35%)**. These fractions are conditional on the cluster population, assumed low first-generation natal spins and detection/measurement model. The likelihood-ratio threshold is not a posterior probability of hierarchical origin. A test with 1,000 modeled first-generation events produces no threshold-crossing cases, which does not establish zero false positives in nature.\n\nApplying the classifier to the examined GWTC-5 sample produces candidates, but classifications depend on waveform treatment. For example, GW241011's precession-only statistic changes across waveform/posterior combinations. Mass ratio, inclination and signal strength affect identifiability; primary mass alone is not a clean independent discriminator. High natal spins in first-generation systems or a different formation channel can mimic the diagnostic.\n\n##### History\n\n[Payne, Kremer and Zevin, “Spin Doctors,” 2402.15066](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.15066), already investigated hierarchical-merger classification through precession, obtaining weak/strong identification fractions around **8%/2%** for their O3 setup. The new work expands the injection mapping and combines spin information under an O4 population/detector model. Comparing 24% directly with 8% as a universal improvement factor would ignore changed assumptions. The advance is a conditional selection-function study, not definitive formation-channel assignments for individual observed binaries."}, {"id": "paper-2610-41229", "arxiv": "2610.41229", "title": "More Eccentric, Not Always Less Detectable: High Mass Holds Its Ground, Low Mass Loses Reach", "authors": "Elwin K. Y. Li, Juno C. L. Chan, Alvin K. Y. Li, Otto A. Hannuksela", "primary_category": "gr-qc", "tags": ["Gravitational waves", "Eccentric binaries", "Search methods"], "label": "Search-pipeline injection study", "teaser": "Circular-template searches can retain surprising sensitivity to eccentric mergers", "review_date": "2026-11-11", "listing_date": "2026-11-02", "version": "v1", "submission_date": "2026-10-30T04:06:12Z", "path": "2026/11/02/index.html#paper-2610-41229", "reportDate": "2 November 2026", "searchText": "More Eccentric, Not Always Less Detectable: High Mass Holds Its Ground, Low Mass Loses Reach Elwin K. Y. Li, Juno C. L. Chan, Alvin K. Y. Li, Otto A. Hannuksela 2610.41229 Circular-template searches can retain surprising sensitivity to eccentric mergers 2026-11-02 2 November 2026 Gravitational waves Eccentric binaries Search methods #### 21. Circular-template searches can retain surprising sensitivity to eccentric mergers\n\n**“More Eccentric, Not Always Less Detectable: High Mass Holds Its Ground, Low Mass Loses Reach” — Elwin K. Y. Li, Juno C. L. Chan, Alvin K. Y. Li, Otto A. Hannuksela.** [arXiv:2610.41229v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.41229v1) · [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.41229v1) · [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.41229v1). Submitted **30 October 2026, 04:06:12 UTC**; UTC listing **2 November 2026**; primary category **gr-qc**. **Search-pipeline injection study.** Preprint; no journal reference was shown on the checked version record.\n\nEccentric **TEOBResumS-Dalí** injections are passed through the quasicircular **GstLAL** bank in real O3 H1/L1 data, with eccentricity defined at **15 Hz** and explored up to **0.99**. Campaigns include fixed **30+30 M⊙** and **12+12 M⊙** systems and a broader chirp-mass/spin/inclination sample. The adopted recovery threshold is false-alarm rate **12.1 yr⁻¹**, approximately one per 30 days; it is not a high-confidence discovery threshold.\n\nFor chirp mass **30.44–40.44 M⊙**, sensitive-distance changes remain below roughly **20%** over much of **e ≤ 0.9**. Lower chirp-mass bins fare worse: distance falls around **50%** in **10.44–20.44 M⊙**, and **40%** in **20.44–30.44 M⊙**, near **e = 0.75**. Highly inclined, highly eccentric systems are important exceptions even at high mass.\n\nA same-parameter waveform overlap can exaggerate losses because a search bank recovers signals at different template parameters and because short high-mass signals interact differently with the consistency test. Nevertheless, equal-mass/aligned-spin assumptions, one data interval, the permissive FAR threshold and limited numerical-relativity validation at extreme eccentricity prevent blanket claims that circular searches are insensitive to eccentricity or fully complete for it.\n\n##### History\n\n[Gadre et al., 2405.04186](https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.04186), compared O3 PyCBC and coherent WaveBurst recovery with SEOBNRv4EHM injections, finding about **10–20% sensitive-volume losses above e₁₀Hz ≈ 0.2** in the explored range and advantages for unmodeled searches in heavier systems. The new results concern another pipeline, waveform family, eccentricity reference frequency, parameter distribution and higher-e range. Differences are not automatically a contradiction or an improvement factor. Both establish that pipeline-level recovery is more informative than a single overlap calculation."}, {"id": "paper-2610-41119", "arxiv": "2610.41119", "title": "MAGIKS: Multimodal Alignment and Gravitational-Wave-Conditioned Interaction for Kilonova Search", "authors": "Bingzhou Gao, Weichangfeng Guo, Jingqi Zhang, Alistair McLeod, Zhengyan Liu, Linqing Wen, Wen Zhao", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Multimessenger", "Kilonovae", "Machine learning"], "label": "Multimessenger machine-learning forecast", "teaser": "MAGIKS uses gravitational-wave context to rank kilonova candidates", "review_date": "2026-11-11", "listing_date": "2026-11-02", "version": "v1", "submission_date": "2026-10-30T01:19:55Z", "path": "2026/11/02/index.html#paper-2610-41119", "reportDate": "2 November 2026", "searchText": "MAGIKS: Multimodal Alignment and Gravitational-Wave-Conditioned Interaction for Kilonova Search Bingzhou Gao, Weichangfeng Guo, Jingqi Zhang, Alistair McLeod, Zhengyan Liu, Linqing Wen, Wen Zhao 2610.41119 MAGIKS uses gravitational-wave context to rank kilonova candidates 2026-11-02 2 November 2026 Multimessenger Kilonovae Machine learning #### 22. MAGIKS uses gravitational-wave context to rank kilonova candidates\n\n**“MAGIKS: Multimodal Alignment and Gravitational-Wave-Conditioned Interaction for Kilonova Search” — Bingzhou Gao, Weichangfeng Guo, Jingqi Zhang, Alistair McLeod, Zhengyan Liu, Linqing Wen, Wen Zhao.** [arXiv:2610.41119v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.41119v1) · [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.41119v1) · [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.41119v1). Submitted **30 October 2026, 01:19:55 UTC**; UTC listing **2 November 2026**; primary category **astro-ph.HE**. **Multimessenger machine-learning forecast.** Preprint; no journal reference was shown on the checked version record.\n\nMAGIKS combines simulated gravitational-wave maps and source information with irregular Rubin-like optical light curves. It uses O5a-like detector noise, POSSIS/SFHo-based kilonova models, observing-strategy simulations and ELAsTiCC contaminants. A sky-map encoder, light-curve encoder and attention-based fusion are trained with event-disjoint samples: **21,890 positive and 19,814 negative training/validation examples**, versus **1,843 positive and 2,158 negative test examples**.\n\nIn artificial galleries containing exactly one counterpart among **5,000** sky/time-compatible candidates, top-rank recovery is **39.8%**, compared with **4.8%** for optical information alone, **0.2%** for the sky map alone and **3.8%** for the adapted Fink baseline; top-ten recovery reaches **66.6%**. These are benchmark retrieval fractions, not live-stream purity. The reported **94.2% binary accuracy** uses an artificial class mixture. A GW170817 exercise assumes counterfactual Rubin observations; it is not a new retrospective observational identification.\n\nThe inputs include injected inclination information. Perturbing it by about **±20°** is a useful robustness test but does not substitute for a real, correlated inclination posterior. Training and testing within closely related ejecta-model families, Gaussian detector noise, contamination choices and absence of a live alert-stream demonstration limit generalization. Physical GW conditioning is promising, but must be tested with operational latency and out-of-distribution contaminants.\n\n##### History\n\n[Biswas et al., 2210.17433](https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.17433), developed simulated-light-curve features and random-forest filtering for the Fink kilonova module, which was already operating on real ZTF alerts and producing candidates for follow-up. Thus machine-assisted fast-transient selection predates MAGIKS. The new contribution is fusion of GW physical and spatial context with optical time series. The adapted Fink benchmark does not by itself establish superiority to the live broker under its own observing conditions or selection objectives."}, {"id": "paper-2610-41473", "arxiv": "2610.41473", "title": "Probing dark sector with decihertz gravitational wave observations of compact binary inspirals in IndIGO-D", "authors": "Divya Tahelyani, Abhishek Sharma, Anand S. Sengupta, Arpan Bhattacharyya", "primary_category": "gr-qc", "tags": ["Dark sectors", "Gravitational waves", "Neutron stars"], "label": "Proposed-detector Bayesian forecast", "teaser": "IndIGO dark-force precision is a conditional forecast, not a new exclusion", "review_date": "2026-11-11", "listing_date": "2026-11-02", "version": "v1", "submission_date": "2026-10-30T09:24:16Z", "path": "2026/11/02/index.html#paper-2610-41473", "reportDate": "2 November 2026", "searchText": "Probing dark sector with decihertz gravitational wave observations of compact binary inspirals in IndIGO-D Divya Tahelyani, Abhishek Sharma, Anand S. Sengupta, Arpan Bhattacharyya 2610.41473 IndIGO dark-force precision is a conditional forecast, not a new exclusion 2026-11-02 2 November 2026 Dark sectors Gravitational waves Neutron stars #### 23. IndIGO dark-force precision is a conditional forecast, not a new exclusion\n\n**“Probing dark sector with decihertz gravitational wave observations of compact binary inspirals in IndIGO-D” — Divya Tahelyani, Abhishek Sharma, Anand S. Sengupta, Arpan Bhattacharyya.** [arXiv:2610.41473v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.41473v1) · [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.41473v1) · [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.41473v1). Submitted **30 October 2026, 09:24:16 UTC**; UTC listing **2 November 2026**; primary category **gr-qc**. **Proposed-detector Bayesian forecast.** Preprint; no journal reference was shown on the checked version record.\n\nThe model gives neutron stars effective dark charges, parametrized by \\(\\alpha=(\\hbar c/G)(q_1/m_1)(q_2/m_2)\\), \\(\\gamma=(\\hbar c/G)(q_1/m_1-q_2/m_2)^2\\), and \\(\\mu=G(m_1+m_2)m_A/(\\hbar c)\\). A perturbative, long-range inspiral waveform includes dark-force and radiation effects, matched across mediator-emission thresholds. The proposed heliocentric decihertz IndIGO detector is assumed to observe a GW170817-like binary for **three months over 0.364–10 Hz**, at **40.4 Mpc** and **S/N ≈ 77.9**.\n\nFor the nonzero injection **α = 0.035, γ = 4.1 × 10⁻⁴, μ = 5 × 10⁻⁵**, the reported mediator energy is **m_Ac² = [2.42 (+0.0000208/−0.0000209)] × 10⁻¹⁵ eV**, at **90% credibility**. That strikingly small interval is local precision around an injected signal, not an observed mass or an upper limit. The uniform priors are narrowly centered on the injection: approximately **α ± 1.5 × 10⁻⁴**, **γ ± 5 × 10⁻⁷**, and **μ ± 1.5 × 10⁻⁸**. Distance, sky position and spins are sampled, but this is not a broad global discovery search.\n\nFor a GR injection the mediator mass is unconstrained, and weak-coupling examples also lose identifiability. Threshold modeling, the perturbative waveform domain and astrophysical formation of sufficiently charged stars require validation. Abrupt emission-threshold approximations could contain more apparent information than a refined physical transition.\n\n##### History\n\n[Owen et al., 2503.04916](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.04916), developed the related waveform treatment and confronted ground-based neutron-star-containing events. Its restrictions on mediator parameters include waveform-validity conditions, which must not be reclassified as observational exclusions. The present paper extrapolates to a proposed lower-frequency detector and a nonzero injected signal; its local precision cannot be divided by a ground-based upper bound to claim an experimental improvement.\n\n[Kopp et al., 1807.02527](https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.02527), independently examined how neutron stars could acquire dark charges and showed that ordinary halo capture can make effects extremely small, while more exotic formation scenarios allow larger ones. The benchmark charge is therefore a physical assumption to justify, not a measured or automatically viable population property."}, {"id": "paper-2610-42056", "arxiv": "2610.42056", "title": "Tests of equivalence principle violation in doubly coupled dark energy", "authors": "Yannick Kegel, Ziyang Zheng, Luca Amendola", "primary_category": "astro-ph.CO", "tags": ["Cosmology", "Dark energy", "Equivalence principle"], "label": "Large-scale-structure forecast", "teaser": "Galaxy dipoles probe dark-sector forces—but coupling degeneracies dominate", "review_date": "2026-11-11", "listing_date": "2026-11-02", "version": "v1", "submission_date": "2026-10-30T17:18:53Z", "path": "2026/11/02/index.html#paper-2610-42056", "reportDate": "2 November 2026", "searchText": "Tests of equivalence principle violation in doubly coupled dark energy Yannick Kegel, Ziyang Zheng, Luca Amendola 2610.42056 Galaxy dipoles probe dark-sector forces—but coupling degeneracies dominate 2026-11-02 2 November 2026 Cosmology Dark energy Equivalence principle #### 24. Galaxy dipoles probe dark-sector forces—but coupling degeneracies dominate\n\n**“Tests of equivalence principle violation in doubly coupled dark energy” — Yannick Kegel, Ziyang Zheng, Luca Amendola.** [arXiv:2610.42056v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.42056v1) · [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.42056v1) · [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.42056v1). Submitted **30 October 2026, 17:18:53 UTC**; UTC listing **2 November 2026**; primary category **astro-ph.CO**. **Large-scale-structure forecast.** Preprint; no journal reference was shown on the checked version record.\n\nThe model combines a universal scalar–gravity coupling with a distinct dark-matter coupling, \\(f_1=e^{-2Q\\phi}\\) and \\(f_2=e^{2Q_{\\rm dm}\\phi}\\), defining \\(\\beta=-\\sqrt6 Q/2\\) and \\(\\beta_{\\rm dm}=-\\sqrt6 Q_{\\rm dm}/2\\). Dark-sector nonuniversality can violate the equivalence principle; the universal coupling alone does not. The proposed observable uses the relativistic imaginary cross-spectrum of bright and faint galaxies to compare their motion and gravitational redshift.\n\nAn **SKA2 forecast over z = 0.2–0.8** uses six redshift bins, a freely sampled matter spectrum in **12 bins over k = 0.01–0.12 h Mpc⁻¹**, and fitted bias/damping terms. It assumes a **1% Gaussian distance prior**, fixed evolution bias and accurately known magnification effects. At fiducial **βdm = 0.2, β = 0.1**, allowing both couplings gives marginalized **1σ uncertainties σ(βdm) ≈ 0.26 and σ(β) ≈ 0.43**. In the simpler dark-coupling-only model with the universal coupling fixed to zero, **σ(βdm) ≈ 0.044**, or **22%** relative precision.\n\nThe large degradation is the scientific point: a precise projected observable need not yield a precise unique coupling. These are Fisher forecasts around specified models, not new experimental bounds. The chosen coupling benchmark can conflict with constraints on a coupling assumed constant throughout cosmic history; the paper's late-time setting and growth/quasistatic approximations must be kept explicit.\n\n##### History\n\n[Castello et al., 2412.08627](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.08627), already introduced a multitracer, model-light equivalence-principle statistic and forecast redshift-dependent SKA2 precision around **7–25%**. The new work projects such information onto a doubly coupled dark-energy model, exposing parameter degeneracy. It does not invent the relativistic-dipole test or supersede laboratory and cosmological equivalence-principle limits that constrain different species, scales and time dependences."}, {"id": "paper-2610-40787", "arxiv": "2610.40787", "title": "Cosmic merger rates and population evolution of binary black hole mergers in AGN disks", "authors": "Maria Paola Vaccaro, Alessandro Trinca, Michela Mapelli, Lumen Boco, Raffaella Schneider, Rosa Valiante, Dominika Wylezalek", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Black-hole populations", "Gravitational waves", "AGN"], "label": "Population synthesis and detection-rate forecast", "teaser": "AGN-disk mergers connected to the evolving cosmic AGN population", "review_date": "2026-11-11", "listing_date": "2026-11-02", "version": "v1", "submission_date": "2026-10-29T18:00:14Z", "path": "2026/11/02/index.html#paper-2610-40787", "reportDate": "2 November 2026", "searchText": "Cosmic merger rates and population evolution of binary black hole mergers in AGN disks Maria Paola Vaccaro, Alessandro Trinca, Michela Mapelli, Lumen Boco, Raffaella Schneider, Rosa Valiante, Dominika Wylezalek 2610.40787 AGN-disk mergers connected to the evolving cosmic AGN population 2026-11-02 2 November 2026 Black-hole populations Gravitational waves AGN #### 25. AGN-disk mergers connected to the evolving cosmic AGN population\n\n**“Cosmic merger rates and population evolution of binary black hole mergers in AGN disks” — Maria Paola Vaccaro, Alessandro Trinca, Michela Mapelli, Lumen Boco, Raffaella Schneider, Rosa Valiante, Dominika Wylezalek.** [arXiv:2610.40787v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40787v1) · [full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.40787v1) · [PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40787v1). Submitted **29 October 2026, 18:00:14 UTC**; UTC listing **2 November 2026**; primary category **astro-ph.HE**. **Population synthesis and detection-rate forecast.** Preprint; no journal reference was shown on the checked version record.\n\nARISE connects FastClusterAGN calculations of capture, migration, gas hardening, encounters and hierarchical mergers to evolving AGN abundances and supermassive-black-hole populations. Disk examples span central masses **10⁵–10⁹ M⊙**, Eddington ratios **10⁻³–10**, and viscosity parameters **α = 0.01 or 0.1**, with alternative disk/cluster and high-redshift population prescriptions.\n\nAt **z = 0.2**, representative merger-rate ranges are **12–37** or **0.5–6 Gpc⁻³ yr⁻¹** across different model families. These are between-model spreads, not statistical confidence intervals. Rate peaks shift from around **z = 1.8** in a conservative abundance model to **3.3 or 3.9** in alternative growth histories; one model remains high to **z ≈ 10**.\n\nFor a **10 km triangular Einstein Telescope**, **85% duty cycle** and **S/N > 12**, forecasts reach approximately **0.9–1.6 × 10⁵ detections yr⁻¹** for α = 0.01 and **1.1–2.3 × 10⁴ yr⁻¹** for α = 0.1. These large rates depend on disk lifetimes, replenishment, migration/hardening prescriptions and high-redshift AGN demographics. Low-viscosity models can overproduce the allowed overall rate. The paper's redshift-integrated AGN fractions are not detector-weighted observed branching fractions; luminosity-selected AGN-association bounds cannot be applied indiscriminately to every modeled AGN.\n\n##### History\n\n[Bartos et al., 1602.03831](https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.03831), already showed how dense AGN disks can accelerate mergers of pre-existing stellar-mass black-hole binaries and discussed uncertain electromagnetic counterparts. The contribution here is the explicit connection between local disk dynamics and cosmic AGN/black-hole evolution, rather than the original proposal of an AGN merger channel. The very broad forecast range demonstrates how much the cosmological extrapolation depends on disk physics and population history. Neither present GW data nor these simulations establish a unique AGN contribution."}, {"id": "paper-2610-38061", "arxiv": "2610.38061", "title": "Cosmic rays in galaxy halos I: the circumgalactic transport rate and a dynamically significant cosmic-ray reservoir", "authors": "Roland M. Crocker and Mark R. Krumholz", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Gamma rays", "Galaxy halos"], "label": "New paper · phenomenological transport model", "teaser": "Joint gamma-ray, X-ray and dynamical checks admit a long-lived halo cosmic-ray reservoir, but rapid escape remains viable and earlier transport constraints require careful comparison.", "path": "2026/10/30/index.html#paper-2610-38061", "reportDate": "30 October 2026", "searchText": "Cosmic rays in galaxy halos I: the circumgalactic transport rate and a dynamically significant cosmic-ray reservoir Roland M. Crocker and Mark R. Krumholz 2610.38061 Joint gamma-ray, X-ray and dynamical checks admit a long-lived halo cosmic-ray reservoir, but rapid escape remains viable and earlier transport constraints require careful comparison. 2026-10-30 30 October 2026 Cosmic rays Gamma rays Galaxy halos #### 1. Can galaxy halos retain a cosmological reservoir of cosmic rays?\n\n**“Cosmic rays in galaxy halos I: the circumgalactic transport rate and a dynamically significant cosmic-ray reservoir” — Roland M. Crocker and Mark R. Krumholz.** [arXiv:2610.38061v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.38061v1), [full text](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.38061v1). Submitted **27 October 2026, 23:23:28 UTC**; first listed **30 October**. **Preprint**, explicitly circulated for feedback before journal submission; primary category astro-ph.HE.\n\nMost cosmic-ray protons accelerated in ordinary star-forming discs escape before colliding. The question is whether they then leave the galaxy's gaseous halo, heat that gas, radiate through pion production, or accumulate for billions of years. The distinction matters for both galaxy evolution and the origin of the isotropic gamma-ray background, or IGRB.\n\nThe authors evolve a spherical transport model from redshift 8 to 0, including halo growth, star-formation-dependent injection, diffusion, hadronic losses and streaming heating. They compare reflecting virial-shock boundaries with free escape, while varying the gas-density slope and the dimensionless transport coefficient \\(\\mathcal K\\):\n\n\\[\nD=\\frac{\\mathcal K}{3}r_{\\rm vir}c_s,\n\\qquad t_{\\rm diff}=\\frac{r_{\\rm vir}^2}{6D}.\n\\]\n\nThe independent checks are the **1–10 GeV Fermi IGRB**, outer-Milky-Way gamma-ray emissivities, **0.5–2 keV eROSITA halo luminosities**, hydrostatic support and the pressure that an accretion shock can confine. Figures 5–6 show how these requirements remove different parts of parameter space.\n\nOne surviving regime has **\\(\\mathcal K\\simeq0.05–0.2\\)**, corresponding to **\\(D\\simeq(2–7)\\times10^{29}\\;\\mathrm{cm^2\\,s^{-1}}\\)** for a present-day **\\(10^{12}M_\\odot\\)** halo. The implied crossing time is approximately **0.5–2 Hubble times**. In this regime a halo retains roughly **\\(10^{58}\\) erg** in cosmic-ray protons; the retained fraction spans approximately **27–59% of the energy injected into the CGM** across the allowed slow-transport regions. This is different from the fraction of all cosmic-ray production, because disc losses occur first. Halo streaming can also supply the heating associated with the observed X-ray luminosity. **Rapid free escape remains an allowed alternative.**\n\nThese are **conditional model ranges, not confidence intervals or a detected reservoir**. The scan fixes the cosmic-ray yield at \\(10^{50}\\) erg per core-collapse supernova, the injection spectral index at 2.2, \\(v_A/c_s=0.1\\), and an energy-averaged transport prescription. It does not marginalize over these assumptions. Its IGRB rejection threshold, **\\(1.82\\times10^{-8}\\;\\mathrm{erg\\,cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}\\)** integrated over the sky in 1–10 GeV, combines three statistical errors with an upward foreground systematic; it is not a calibrated joint “3σ” bound. A radius-dependent diffusion coefficient can trade against the gas profile. The X-ray heating condition also depends on streaming losses and the adopted bolometric correction.\n\n##### History\n\nThe reservoir idea predates this paper. **Feldmann, Hooper and Gnedin**, [arXiv:1205.0249](https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.0249), published in *ApJ* **763, 21 (2013)**, coupled a simulated ionized-gas halo to cosmic-ray diffusion and estimated a **3–10%** Milky Way contribution to the high-latitude gamma-ray background for their illustrated transport choices. Reading their Section 4 reveals an important qualification to the new paper's comparison: the earlier calculation already used a **time-dependent source term over 10 Gyr**, including stronger past star formation. It should not simply be described as a steady-state calculation. The new work's advance is the coupled halo evolution, losses and simultaneous energetic/dynamical tests; its larger halo contribution cannot be attributed to adding time dependence alone. Different gas profiles, energy bands and background definitions also prevent a clean improvement factor.\n\nA sharper comparison is **Butsky et al.**, [arXiv:2210.14232](https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.14232), *MNRAS* **521, 2477 (2023)**. Their COS-Halos analysis derives a lower limit on the radially averaged **total effective transport rate**, including advection, from hydrogen columns, star formation and circular velocity. It favors **\\(\\kappa_{\\rm eff}\\gtrsim10^{30}–10^{31}\\;\\mathrm{cm^2\\,s^{-1}}\\)** at **50–100 kpc and beyond**, for pressure-dominating approximately GeV protons. This is not the same parameter as a constant diffusion coefficient relative to the gas. Its equilibrium/injection assumptions and column-density uncertainties differ, and the plotted limits are not a uniform 95% confidence construction. Nevertheless, the tension with a low, radius-independent coefficient is substantive; it remains unresolved by a simple numerical comparison.\n\nFinally, **Chen and Totani**, [arXiv:2502.19879](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.19879), independently found that star-forming galaxies supply only about **50–60%** of the unresolved background near 1–10 GeV under their calibration. They examined stellar-initial-mass-function consistency and other normalization issues. The new paper explicitly applies related corrections in Appendix B and assigns its disc component about 60%. Its remaining halo contribution is therefore a testable interpretation, rather than proof that other populations such as unresolved active galaxies are unnecessary."}, {"id": "paper-2610-39042", "arxiv": "2610.39042", "title": "Measurements of transverse kinematic imbalance in muon neutrino charged-current interactions on argon with a single proton and no pions in the final state with SBND", "authors": "R. Acciarri et al. (SBND Collaboration)", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Argon", "Cross sections", "Oscillation experiments"], "label": "New paper · cross-section measurement", "teaser": "SBND selects 12,804 candidates in its first neutrino-argon cross-section measurement; no tested generator describes all three transverse-imbalance distributions.", "path": "2026/10/30/index.html#paper-2610-39042", "reportDate": "30 October 2026", "searchText": "Measurements of transverse kinematic imbalance in muon neutrino charged-current interactions on argon with a single proton and no pions in the final state with SBND R. Acciarri et al. (SBND Collaboration) 2610.39042 SBND selects 12,804 candidates in its first neutrino-argon cross-section measurement; no tested generator describes all three transverse-imbalance distributions. 2026-10-30 30 October 2026 Neutrinos Argon Cross sections Oscillation experiments #### 2. SBND's first argon cross sections expose complementary nuclear-model failures\n\n**“Measurements of transverse kinematic imbalance in muon neutrino charged-current interactions on argon with a single proton and no pions in the final state with SBND” — R. Acciarri et al. (SBND Collaboration).** [arXiv:2610.39042v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39042v1), [paper and supplement](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39042v1). Submitted **28 October 2026, 23:15:33 UTC**; first listed **30 October**. **Preprint**, with no journal reference stated; primary category hep-ex.\n\nOscillation experiments infer neutrino properties through interactions inside nuclei. Fermi motion, two-body currents and the rescattering of outgoing particles can change reconstructed energies and imitate changes in the incoming neutrino spectrum. SBND tests these effects using a particularly clean final state: one muon and one proton, with no detectable pions.\n\nThe exposure is **\\(8.8\\times10^{19}\\) protons on target**, at mean neutrino energy **0.75 GeV**. The analysis selects **12,804 candidates**, with **91% simulated signal purity**. Its signal definition requires muon momentum **0.22–1.0 GeV/c** and proton momentum **0.3–1.0 GeV/c**; extra protons above 0.3 GeV/c, charged pions above 70 MeV/c, neutral pions and heavier mesons are excluded. Neutrons are unrestricted. This topology includes non-quasielastic processes that produce the same final state; the simulation estimates an 80% quasielastic component.\n\nThe central observable is\n\n\\[\n\\delta p_T=\\left|\\mathbf p_T^\\mu+\\mathbf p_T^p\\right|.\n\\]\n\nFor scattering from a stationary free nucleon, transverse momentum balances. Nuclear motion and rescattering broaden the distribution. The angles \\(\\delta\\alpha_T\\) and \\(\\delta\\phi_T\\) add sensitivity to the direction of the imbalance and departure from muon–proton coplanarity.\n\nSBND unfolds differential cross sections with **Wiener-SVD regularization** and supplies covariance and regularization matrices. Theory must be folded with the supplied \\(A_C\\) matrix before comparison. The integrated systematic uncertainty is **8.7%**, dominated by the **6.5% flux** component; detector and interaction-model contributions are 3.7% and 3.4%. Differential shapes remain more discriminating than the normalization.\n\nFor \\(\\delta p_T\\), Figure 2 gives full-covariance **\\(\\chi^2/\\mathrm{ndof}\\)** values **33.6/12** for GENIE AR23, **63.6/12** for GiBUU 2025 and **62.4/12** for NEUT 6.1.4. None describes that distribution satisfactorily. GENIE tends to undershoot the peak/intermediate region and overshoot the high tail. Yet GENIE describes the angular distributions substantially better: **3.9/8** for \\(\\delta\\alpha_T\\) and **11.6/11** for \\(\\delta\\phi_T\\). Hartree–Fock variants improve the momentum imbalance but worsen the angular slope. No tested configuration succeeds across all three observables.\n\nThis is a **cross-section measurement**, not a new-physics or oscillation signal. Acceptance corrections depend on simulation, although the supplement tests alternate-generator fake data, smooth deformations and injected spectral bumps. A model's failure does not uniquely identify whether its initial nuclear state, primary interaction or final-state transport needs revision.\n\n##### History\n\nThe direct predecessor is **MicroBooNE's first double-differential kinematic-imbalance measurement**, [arXiv:2301.03706](https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.03706), *Physical Review Letters* **131, 101802 (2023)**. With **\\(6.79\\times10^{20}\\) protons on target**, it selected **9,051 events** with approximately **70% purity**. Its double-differential distributions separated regions with weak and strong final-state interactions: the high-\\(\\delta p_T\\), high-\\(\\delta\\alpha_T\\) region strongly disfavored predictions with rescattering switched off. Nuclear effects were already established; SBND makes their detailed modeling harder to evade with a cleaner independent argon sample.\n\nThe samples are not interchangeable. MicroBooNE accepted muons at **0.1–1.2 GeV/c**, versus SBND's 0.22–1.0 GeV/c, and allowed additional protons above 1 GeV/c outside its selected-proton window; SBND vetoes extra protons above 0.3 GeV/c. Generator versions and tunes also changed. A purity or uncertainty ratio is therefore not a universal improvement factor, and the different exposures do not compare fluxes or target masses directly.\n\nSBND confirms the earlier difficulty with the momentum-imbalance peak and the tendency of GiBUU to predict too steep an angular-imbalance slope. It also finds that some form-factor modifications that improved comparisons with MicroBooNE do not achieve comparable agreement here. The practical advance is an additional, precisely specified benchmark for neutrino–argon modeling relevant to SBN and DUNE. The remaining question is whether one common interaction model can describe both experiments after applying their distinct signal definitions and response matrices."}, {"id": "paper-2610-37318", "arxiv": "2610.37318", "title": "High significance measurement of reactor coherent elastic neutrino-nucleus scattering on germanium by CONUS+", "authors": "N. Ackermann et al. (CONUS Collaboration)", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Particle physics", "CEvNS"], "label": "New paper · 10.2σ measurement", "teaser": "CONUS+ reports 1555 ± 152 reactor CEνNS events in 1296 kg·days, a 10.2σ measurement consistent with the Standard Model prediction.", "path": "2026/10/28/index.html#paper-2610-37318", "reportDate": "28 October 2026", "searchText": "High significance measurement of reactor coherent elastic neutrino-nucleus scattering on germanium by CONUS+ N. Ackermann et al. (CONUS Collaboration) 2610.37318 CONUS+ reports 1555 ± 152 reactor CEνNS events in 1296 kg·days, a 10.2σ measurement consistent with the Standard Model prediction. 2026-10-28 28 October 2026 Neutrinos Particle physics CEvNS #### 1. CONUS+ turns reactor coherent scattering into a 10.2σ precision measurement\n\n**N. Ackermann et al. (CONUS Collaboration) — [arXiv:2610.37318v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37318v1).** Submitted **27 October, 14:32:54 UTC**; preprint, with a [simultaneous detailed analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37345v1), and no journal status stated.\n\nCoherent elastic neutrino–nucleus scattering (CEνNS) tests the weak neutral current at low momentum transfer. CONUS+ uses three low-threshold high-purity germanium detectors 20.7 m from the 3.6-GW thermal core of the Leibstadt reactor. The blind run-2 likelihood fits reactor-on and reactor-off spectra together with muon-, neutron-, radon-, cosmogenic and material-background components. The useful nuclear-recoil thresholds are **140–170 eV electron-equivalent**, and the reactor-on exposure is **1296 kg·days**.\n\nThe fit gives\n\n\\[\nN_{\\rm CE\\nu NS}=1555\\pm116\\;({\\rm stat})\\pm98\\;({\\rm syst})=1555\\pm152,\n\\]\n\nwith a quoted **10.2σ** signal significance. The Standard Model prediction is \\(1701\\pm198\\) events, so the measured-to-predicted ratio is \\(0.91\\pm0.14\\), only **0.6 standard deviations** from expectation. The three detectors independently give 7.2σ, 7.8σ and 2.4σ results. Removing the reactor-off data still gives 3.1σ; adopting the alternative background likelihood reduces the combined significance to 9.3σ.\n\nInterpreting the rate through the weak nuclear charge, \\(Q_W=N-(1-4\\sin^2\\theta_W)Z\\), gives\n\n\\[\n\\sin^2\\theta_W(Q\\simeq10\\,\\mathrm{MeV})=0.225\\pm0.022,\n\\]\n\nconsistent with the low-energy Standard Model value 0.2387. This is a measurement, not a new-physics signal. Its precision is limited by the predicted rate: energy-scale and sub-keV germanium-quenching uncertainties contribute 8.0% and 7.5% to the prediction, while background modeling is the largest systematic on the fitted signal. The weak-angle extraction fixes the Standard Model interaction form apart from \\(\\sin^2\\theta_W\\); it is not a model-independent constraint on all possible neutrino interactions.\n\n##### History\n\nCEνNS was predicted by Freedman in [1974](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.9.1389), but the tiny recoil energy delayed observation for four decades. COHERENT first detected it at **6.7σ** in a 14.6-kg CsI[Na] detector at the pulsed Spallation Neutron Source in [2017](https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.01294). That established the process at tens-of-MeV neutrino energies and with timing information. Reactor antineutrinos probe lower energy and momentum transfer, where coherence is more complete, but demand much lower recoil thresholds and continuous-background control.\n\nCONUS+ run 1 supplied the first direct reactor observation: [395 ± 106 events in 327 kg·days at 3.7σ](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09322-2), against \\(347\\pm59\\) expected. Run 2 has about four times the exposure, lowers the relative signal-count uncertainty by almost a factor of three, and changes the status from first observation to a rate measurement. The 2026 [COHERENT germanium result](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.17951) provides a complementary high-momentum-transfer measurement with a different source and background environment. Dresden-II's earlier reactor excess depends on a sub-keV quenching response larger than the Lindhard model and corresponds to a higher rate than CONUS+ and other germanium reactor results, so it is not a directly comparable precision baseline.\n\nThe main remaining uncertainty is detector response to sub-keV nuclear recoils and the reactor-flux prediction. Better neutron calibrations and background rejection can turn the large event sample into stronger tests of neutrino magnetic moments, non-standard interactions and light mediators, but those bounds require their own parameter definitions and nuisance treatment."}, {"id": "paper-2610-36282", "arxiv": "2610.36282", "title": "Reconstruction of very inclined air showers with radio data and application to the first GRANDProto300 cosmic-ray candidates", "authors": "Marion Guelfand et al. for the GRAND Collaboration", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "UHECR", "Neutrinos", "Radio"], "label": "New paper · commissioning result", "teaser": "GRANDProto300 applies an inclined-shower radio reconstruction to commissioning data and retains 51 high-quality cosmic-ray candidates, with performance still simulation-calibrated.", "path": "2026/10/28/index.html#paper-2610-36282", "reportDate": "28 October 2026", "searchText": "Reconstruction of very inclined air showers with radio data and application to the first GRANDProto300 cosmic-ray candidates Marion Guelfand et al. for the GRAND Collaboration 2610.36282 GRANDProto300 applies an inclined-shower radio reconstruction to commissioning data and retains 51 high-quality cosmic-ray candidates, with performance still simulation-calibrated. 2026-10-28 28 October 2026 Cosmic rays UHECR Neutrinos Radio #### 2. GRANDProto300 reconstructs 51 inclined cosmic-ray candidates from raw radio voltages\n\n**Marion Guelfand, Valentin Decoene, Olivier Martineau, Simon Prunet, Sebastian Castro-Isern and Nathan Lebas, for the GRAND Collaboration — [arXiv:2610.36282v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36282v1).** Submitted **26 October, 21:07:25 UTC**; proceedings contribution to Moriond VHEPU 2026, not a refereed journal result.\n\nGRAND's eventual neutrino program depends on autonomously recognizing very inclined air showers in a sparse radio array. This paper combines a spherical radio-wavefront fit with an angular distribution function (ADF) for the 50–200 MHz amplitude pattern. Simulations of cosmic-ray showers above 60° zenith give better than **0.07° directional resolution** and about **15% primary-energy resolution** when the calibrated electric field is used.\n\nThe commissioning application is less mature. Sixty-five of the planned 289 GP300 antennas were deployed in the described configuration. From roughly **18,000 preliminary candidates** collected between July and October 2025, the authors apply a zenith cut below 80°, require all triggered antennas to lie within 3° of the shower axis, and retain events with reduced ADF \\(\\chi^2<20\\). That leaves **51 high-quality cosmic-ray candidates**. One example is reconstructed at \\(\\theta=75.1^\\circ\\pm0.03^\\circ\\), \\(\\phi=358.7^\\circ\\pm0.02^\\circ\\), with a first-pass energy of \\(2.08\\times10^{18}\\) eV and an emission point about 49 km from the shower core.\n\nThe 51 events validate a useful reconstruction chain, but they are not yet a measured cosmic-ray spectrum or a neutrino sample. The quoted 0.07° and 15% resolutions are simulation results. The data analysis acts directly on voltage traces before full electric-field reconstruction, assumes a 5-ns timing error and 7.5% amplitude error, and still has unfinished timing, amplitude and energy calibration. The retained rate is far below the roughly 20 events per day expected over about 1500 hours because the run prioritized hardware commissioning and used deliberately restrictive selections. Primary mass is not reconstructed, the horizon region is cut, and no data-derived selection efficiency or residual-background rate is reported.\n\n##### History\n\nRadio calorimetry is already established for ordinary cosmic-ray showers. The Pierre Auger Engineering Radio Array measured the coherent radio energy and obtained [22% energy resolution for its full sample and 17% for a high-quality subset](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.93.122005). GRAND targets a different geometry: huge footprints from nearly horizontal cosmic rays and upward-going showers initiated by Earth-skimming neutrinos. The collaboration's staged design therefore emphasizes autonomous triggering, sub-degree pointing and rejection of anthropogenic radio transients.\n\nGP300's [ICRC 2025 analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06695) used 46 antennas and a multi-stage pipeline to report an initial set of **26** candidates from December 2024 to May 2025. The present work adds 51 candidates from a later data interval and applies the ADF reconstruction directly to raw voltages, giving per-event directions and a provisional energy scale. The underlying inclined-wavefront and ADF performance was developed in earlier simulation work, including [arXiv:2507.04324](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.04324).\n\nWhat changes is the demonstration that the reconstruction behaves sensibly on commissioning data: the sky distribution has the expected geomagnetic north–south asymmetry and the energy distribution agrees with an independent preliminary reconstruction. What remains uncertain is the absolute efficiency, purity and energy scale. Until those are measured with calibrated fields and cross-checked against a mature particle detector or a well-validated radio selection, the result supports the detection concept but does not establish GRAND's projected UHE-neutrino sensitivity."}, {"id": "paper-2610-36486", "arxiv": "2610.36486", "title": "Three-flavor neutrino Boltzmann transport and flavor instabilities in long-lived neutron-star merger remnants", "authors": "Harry Ho-Yin Ng et al.", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Neutron-star mergers", "Multimessenger"], "label": "New paper · transport calculation", "teaser": "Six-species Boltzmann transport shows that muon physics reshapes fast and collisional flavor instabilities in a merger remnant and makes two-flavor equilibration prescriptions unreliable.", "path": "2026/10/28/index.html#paper-2610-36486", "reportDate": "28 October 2026", "searchText": "Three-flavor neutrino Boltzmann transport and flavor instabilities in long-lived neutron-star merger remnants Harry Ho-Yin Ng et al. 2610.36486 Six-species Boltzmann transport shows that muon physics reshapes fast and collisional flavor instabilities in a merger remnant and makes two-flavor equilibration prescriptions unreliable. 2026-10-28 28 October 2026 Neutrinos Neutron-star mergers Multimessenger #### 3. Three-flavor transport changes where merger-remnant neutrinos become unstable\n\n**Harry Ho-Yin Ng, Jiabao Liu, Arthur Offermans, Shoichi Yamada, Elias R. Most and Luciano Rezzolla — [arXiv:2610.36486v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36486v1).** Submitted **27 October, 01:43:36 UTC**; preprint, with no journal status stated.\n\nNeutrino absorption sets the electron fraction of neutron-star-merger ejecta and therefore its r-process nucleosynthesis and kilonova opacity. Most merger calculations combine muon and tau neutrinos into one heavy-lepton species. Here a three-dimensional grey-M1 merger simulation is handed off at about **38 ms after merger** to axisymmetric general-relativistic Boltzmann transport of six distinct neutrino and antineutrino species. The calculation includes muons, charged-current muonic reactions, pair processes and multienergy transport, then diagnoses fast flavor instability (FFI) and collisional flavor instability (CFI) through linear stability analysis. Local three-flavor quantum-kinetic-equation calculations test selected points beyond the linear regime.\n\nThe calculation finds widespread CFI in all three flavor sectors. Ordinary CFI reaches roughly \\(10^8\\)–\\(10^{10}\\ \\mathrm{s^{-1}}\\), while e–τ fast modes reach about \\(10^9\\)–\\(10^{10}\\ \\mathrm{s^{-1}}\\). Muonic reactions move the e–τ FFI from an extended low-density atmosphere in the electron-only model into denser outer-disk material below about \\(10^{11}\\ \\mathrm{g\\,cm^{-3}}\\). Continued disk demuonization creates a large \\(\\nu_\\mu\\) excess and mostly one-signed muon-lepton-number angular distributions, suppressing e–μ and μ–τ fast modes.\n\nAt three nearby test points, initial CFI growth rates span \\(3.68\\times10^6\\) to \\(6.27\\times10^8\\ \\mathrm{s^{-1}}\\). Nonlinear coupling activates coherence in a sector that was linearly stable. Including diagonal collision terms changes the asymptotic flavor populations; omitting them drives the calculation too close to flavor equipartition. Although coherence can grow rapidly, relaxation takes **100–1000 μs**, comparable to local dynamical times.\n\nThis is a transport and stability calculation, not a kilonova prediction. It remains axisymmetric after the handoff, evolves only an 8-ms remnant interval, uses closure approximations in parts of the stability analysis and evaluates quantum kinetics locally without feedback on global hydrodynamics. Narrow resonance-like layers are not fully resolved. The work therefore demonstrates failure modes of two-flavor subgrid recipes but does not yet quantify the change in ejecta abundance or observable light curves.\n\n##### History\n\nFast flavor conversion arises when the angular distribution of one flavor's lepton number crosses another; neutrino self-interactions can then amplify coherence on scales far shorter than vacuum oscillations. Earlier merger studies found such crossings with moment transport, including a systematic two-flavor stability framework in [Froustey et al. 2024](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.109.043046). A 2026 multienergy moment analysis found fast modes over much of a remnant but also regions where only collisional or slow instabilities survive ([Froustey et al. 2026](https://doi.org/10.1103/htq2-d1t7)). Separate numerical-relativity work showed that imposed flavor conversion can alter ejecta composition and nucleosynthetic yields ([Qiu et al. 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.11758)), but that did not derive the conversion self-consistently from six-species Boltzmann transport.\n\nMuon physics is independently known to matter in merger remnants. Earlier equilibrium calculations found net muon fractions **30–70%** of the electron fraction and pressure changes up to **7%** ([Loffredo et al. 2023](https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202244927)). Recent grey-M1 simulations with muonic rates found more modest bulk-structure changes but altered thermal and compositional evolution ([Gieg et al. 2026](https://doi.org/10.1103/qmb8-q3cx)). The new paper connects these classical muon populations to three-flavor quantum stability and shows that μ–τ degeneracy is not harmless: it changes both the location of FFI and the nonlinear endpoint of CFI.\n\nThe remaining uncertainty is the feedback loop. Only a self-consistent 3D quantum-transport merger calculation can determine whether the identified instabilities persist, how they saturate in an evolving angular field, and whether they materially change ejecta \\(Y_e\\), r-process yields, neutrino signals or post-merger dynamics."}, {"id": "paper-2610-37604", "arxiv": "2610.37604", "title": "Cosmological Constraints on Interacting Dark Matter with SPT-3G D1", "authors": "Cynthia Trendafilova et al.", "primary_category": "astro-ph.CO", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Cosmology", "CMB"], "label": "New paper · 95% CL constraints", "teaser": "SPT-3G D1 small-scale lensing sharpens CMB limits on dark-matter–baryon and dark-matter–dark-radiation interactions under fixed model assumptions.", "path": "2026/10/28/index.html#paper-2610-37604", "reportDate": "28 October 2026", "searchText": "Cosmological Constraints on Interacting Dark Matter with SPT-3G D1 Cynthia Trendafilova et al. 2610.37604 SPT-3G D1 small-scale lensing sharpens CMB limits on dark-matter–baryon and dark-matter–dark-radiation interactions under fixed model assumptions. 2026-10-28 28 October 2026 Dark matter Cosmology CMB #### 4. SPT-3G small-scale lensing sharpens cosmological limits on interacting dark matter\n\n**Cynthia Trendafilova, Fei Ge, Ali Rida Khalife and Christian L. Reichardt — [arXiv:2610.37604v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37604v1).** Submitted **27 October, 17:35:34 UTC**; preprint, with no journal status stated.\n\nDark matter scattering can shift CMB acoustic peaks and suppress small-scale matter growth. The authors fit three phenomenological extensions of ΛCDM using Planck PR3, ACT DR6 and SPT-3G D1 temperature/polarization spectra; Planck, ACT and SPT-3G lensing; and, where stated, DESI DR2 baryon-acoustic oscillations. Final chains use CLASS-family Boltzmann solvers and Cobaya. All quoted upper bounds are marginalized **95% confidence limits** under flat interaction priors and the paper's cosmological priors.\n\nFor elastic dark-matter–baryon scattering,\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(v)=\\sigma_{\\rm DM-b}v^{n_b},\\qquad n_b\\in\\{-4,-2,0,2\\},\n\\]\n\nthe dark-matter mass is fixed at **1 GeV**, all dark matter is assumed to interact with all baryonic components, and the relative bulk velocity is fixed to 30 km s⁻¹ for the negative-power cases. Planck+ACT previously gave \\(\\sigma_{\\rm DM-b}<7.9\\times10^{-26}\\ \\mathrm{cm^2}\\) for \\(n_b=0\\). Adding SPT-3G D1 primary spectra and small-scale MUSE lensing yields\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{\\rm DM-b}<4.6\\times10^{-26}\\ \\mathrm{cm^2}\\quad(n_b=0),\n\\]\n\nand \\(1.4\\times10^{-41}\\ \\mathrm{cm^2}\\) for \\(n_b=-4\\). Adding DESI DR2 tightens the \\(n_b=0\\) number to \\(3.5\\times10^{-26}\\ \\mathrm{cm^2}\\), partly because DESI prefers a lower matter density than the CMB combination. These are not mass-independent direct-detection limits; smaller interacting fractions or another mass rescale the allowed cross-section.\n\nFor dark-matter–dark-radiation scattering, with \\(m_{\\rm DM}=1\\) GeV and a fully interacting component, the CMB combination gives\n\n\\[\n\\Gamma_{0,\\rm nadm}<5.2\\times10^{-8}\\ \\mathrm{Mpc^{-1}},\\qquad \\Delta N_{\\rm eff}<0.11.\n\\]\n\nWith DESI these become \\(2.5\\times10^{-8}\\ \\mathrm{Mpc^{-1}}\\) and 0.17. For annihilation, the deposited-power parameter is constrained to \\(p_{\\rm ann}<3.7\\times10^{-28}\\ \\mathrm{cm^3\\,s^{-1}\\,GeV^{-1}}\\); it combines the annihilation cross-section, mass and deposition efficiency rather than directly bounding \\(\\langle\\sigma v\\rangle\\). Its limit changes with the optical-depth prior: omitting low-ℓ polarization gives \\(2.7\\times10^{-28}\\) in one tested combination. The authors flag a greater-than-3σ inconsistency between SPT+ACT and DESI in the dark-radiation model and do not quote that joint fit.\n\n##### History\n\nThe CMB has long constrained interactions through drag before recombination and the resulting loss of small-scale power. ACT DR4 set dedicated dark-matter–baryon limits in [Li et al. 2023](https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.08985); ACT DR6 then broadened the extended-model analysis ([Calabrese et al. 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.14454)). The new ingredient is SPT-3G D1's low-noise lensing reconstruction out to \\(L=3000\\), based on [2019–2020 SPT-3G data](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20707). For the same fixed 1-GeV, fully interacting, velocity-independent model, the CMB-only comparison moves from \\(7.9\\times10^{-26}\\) to \\(4.6\\times10^{-26}\\ \\mathrm{cm^2}\\); this is a comparable improvement because the parameter definition and confidence level are held fixed.\n\nFor dark-radiation drag, the Planck+ACT combination gives \\(\\Gamma_{0,\\rm nadm}<4.3\\times10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{Mpc^{-1}}\\), while including SPT-3G yields \\(5.2\\times10^{-8}\\ \\mathrm{Mpc^{-1}}\\). The gain comes mainly from small-scale lensing rather than the primary D1 field, which covers only about 4% of the sky. Cosmological large-scale-structure probes can already give numerically stronger bounds for some \\(n_b=0,2\\) models—such as Lyman-α forest and Milky Way satellite analyses ([Rogers et al. 2022](https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.10386))—but those sample other redshifts and rely on nonlinear, thermal and astrophysical modeling. They should not be converted into a single improvement factor against the CMB limit.\n\nRemaining uncertainties include fixed dark-matter mass and interacting fraction, nonlinear power modeling, optical-depth priors and dataset tension. The DESI-assisted tightening is especially model- and consistency-dependent: it can arise because an extended model is forced to accommodate discrepant preferred matter densities, not because DESI directly measures dark scattering."}, {"id": "paper-2610-37287", "arxiv": "2610.37287", "title": "The First Deep Learning all-sky List of Fermi-LAT Gamma-ray Sources", "authors": "Saptashwa Bhattacharyya et al.", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Fermi-LAT", "Machine learning"], "label": "New paper · source-list method", "teaser": "A simulation-trained deep-learning pipeline extracts 3821 sources from ten years of Fermi-LAT data; 816 lack a 4FGL-DR2 match, but most require conventional source validation.", "path": "2026/10/28/index.html#paper-2610-37287", "reportDate": "28 October 2026", "searchText": "The First Deep Learning all-sky List of Fermi-LAT Gamma-ray Sources Saptashwa Bhattacharyya et al. 2610.37287 A simulation-trained deep-learning pipeline extracts 3821 sources from ten years of Fermi-LAT data; 816 lack a 4FGL-DR2 match, but most require conventional source validation. 2026-10-28 28 October 2026 Gamma rays Fermi-LAT Machine learning #### 5. A deep network builds an all-sky Fermi-LAT source list, with source confusion as the main caveat\n\n**Saptashwa Bhattacharyya, Giacomo Principe, Dmitry Malyshev, Roberto Ruiz de Austri, Gabrijela Zaharijas, Sascha Caron, Fiorenzo Stoppa and Guðlaugur Johannesson — [arXiv:2610.37287v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37287v1).** Submitted **27 October, 14:11:29 UTC**; preprint, styled as an *Astronomy & Astrophysics* manuscript but with no journal status stated on arXiv.\n\nThe standard Fermi-LAT catalogs iteratively fit sources and diffuse emission with likelihood methods. This paper trains a modified U-Net to segment source-like photon patterns, then separate networks refine positions, estimate flux above 1 GeV and assign a probability that each candidate is real. Training uses Monte Carlo skies matched to ten years of LAT exposure, instrument response, source populations and two interstellar-emission models. The pipeline is then run on the same ten-year photon interval used by 4FGL-DR2.\n\nAfter merging detections separated by less than 0.1°, the pipeline finds more than 5100 candidates; its final classifier retains **3821** in the 1FDL list. Of these, **816** have no match within 0.25° in 4FGL-DR2. At high Galactic latitude, both effective precision and recall exceed **90%** above \\(2\\times10^{-10}\\ \\mathrm{ph\\,cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}\\). The network recovers more than **98%** of high-latitude 4FGL-DR2 sources with catalog significance above 20σ, and more than 75% of sources above 10σ over the full sky.\n\nThe unmatched count is not a discovery count. Only ten unmatched entries are both outside the Galactic plane and brighter than \\(5\\times10^{-10}\\ \\mathrm{ph\\,cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}\\). Seven lie on dense gas where diffuse-emission residuals are plausible, and one lies about 0.5° from the bright blazar PKS 1502+106. Two others later appear independently in the 16-year Fermi list. The current network was trained for point sources, performs poorly for close pairs below about 0.1°, misses some bright sources in crowded Galactic regions and depends on how simulations represent diffuse emission. The manuscript also says the source-list repository and Zenodo record are in preparation, so the paper's claim of immediate public availability was not independently reproducible at cutoff.\n\n##### History\n\nThe collaboration's likelihood-based [4FGL catalog](https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.10045) used eight years of 50-MeV–1-TeV data and extensive diffuse modeling; the ten-year DR2 reference list contains **5787 sources** and applies approximately \\(TS>25\\) for new entries, with quality flags for confusion and diffuse residuals ([Fermi Science Support Center](https://fermi.gsfc.nasa.gov/ssc/data/access/lat/10yr_catalog/)). Machine learning has previously classified known unassociated sources or inferred unresolved populations, but those tasks start from catalog entries or statistical source counts rather than producing a full-sky point-source list directly from photon maps.\n\nA closer precursor is the 2025 neural-ratio analysis of 14 years of high-latitude LAT data ([Eckner et al.](https://doi.org/10.1103/p4gf-d2zt)). Above \\(3\\times10^{-10}\\ \\mathrm{cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}\\), it recovered more than 98% of 4FGL sources but found no additional source in that restricted bright range. Under an equivalent bright, unflagged, high-latitude selection, 1FDL also recovers about 98%; its change is end-to-end all-sky segmentation plus per-source position, flux and reliability estimates.\n\nThe [FL16Y list](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22148), an early 16-year precursor to 5FGL, contains **7220 sources**. Its independent inclusion of two bright 1FDL-only candidates is a useful validation, but does not validate the other 814 unmatched entries. Conventional likelihood refits with alternative diffuse templates, localization checks and multiwavelength associations remain necessary before any 1FDL-only object is treated as an astrophysical gamma-ray source."}, {"id": "paper-2610-35619", "arxiv": "2610.35619", "title": "Indication of Electron Antineutrinos from the Diffuse Supernova Neutrino Background", "authors": "Super-Kamiokande Collaboration", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Multimessenger", "Supernovae"], "label": "New paper · 2.6σ indication", "teaser": "Super-Kamiokande's combined pure-water and gadolinium data give a 2.6σ model-dependent indication of the diffuse supernova antineutrino background, still below discovery significance.", "path": "2026/10/27/index.html#paper-2610-35619", "reportDate": "27 October 2026", "searchText": "Indication of Electron Antineutrinos from the Diffuse Supernova Neutrino Background Super-Kamiokande Collaboration 2610.35619 Super-Kamiokande's combined pure-water and gadolinium data give a 2.6σ model-dependent indication of the diffuse supernova antineutrino background, still below discovery significance. 2026-10-27 27 October 2026 Neutrinos Multimessenger Supernovae #### 1. Super-Kamiokande reports a 2.6σ indication of the diffuse supernova antineutrino background\n\n**Super-Kamiokande Collaboration — [arXiv:2610.35619v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.35619v1).** Submitted **26 October, 15:35:48 UTC**; preprint, with no journal status stated. This is an indication from a model-dependent spectral fit, not a discovery.\n\nThe diffuse supernova neutrino background (DSNB) integrates electron antineutrinos from past core-collapse supernovae. Super-Kamiokande analyzes **5003.0 live days** from SK-IV through SK-VIII: 3349.3 days of pure water and 1653.7 days with gadolinium. Inverse beta decay supplies a positron plus neutron. The fit uses six Cherenkov-angle/neutron-multiplicity regions, improved neutral-current rejection, updated neutron tagging and nuisance-constrained background components. The one-neutron threshold falls from 15.5 to **11.5 MeV**, while the analysis conservatively raises the other-neutron-multiplicity threshold to **21.5 MeV** because of residual spallation; that choice removes **13.9%** of the expected signal.\n\nFor the Horiuchi et al. 6-MeV-temperature spectrum, the fit gives\n\n\\[\n\\Phi_{\\bar\\nu_e}(E_\\nu>13.3\\,\\mathrm{MeV})=3.6^{+1.6}_{-1.5}\\ \\mathrm{cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}},\n\\]\n\nor (4.8^{+2.2}_{-2.0}) fitted events per year. The null is disfavored at **2.6σ** (one-sided 99.5%); two alternative DSNB shapes give 2.6–2.7σ. Pure-water and Gd phases separately give **1.4σ** and **2.2σ**, with mutually consistent fluxes. A model-independent event-count analysis over 11.5–29.5 MeV reaches only **1.8σ**. Above 17.3 MeV, the fitted flux is (1.8^{+0.8}_{-0.7}\\ \\mathrm{cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}).\n\nThe inference assumes one of several fixed spectral shapes and fits its normalization. Signal-normalization systematics are about 2.9–3.1%, but background-shape errors, residual spallation, atmospheric-neutrino modeling and the core-collapse emission spectrum remain material. The result sits at the high end of the paper's representative (2.1)–(3.9\\ \\mathrm{cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}) model range and is below the conventional evidence threshold.\n\n##### History\n\nThe DSNB has been a long-standing prediction, but backgrounds above reactor energies and below atmospheric-neutrino energies kept searches in an upper-limit regime. Super-K's [2021 pure-water analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.11174) combined 5823 days and set a roughly **2.7 cm⁻² s⁻¹ 90% CL upper limit above 17.3 MeV**. The first [956.2-day SK-Gd search](https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.02222) used enhanced neutron capture and found only a **~1.2σ** spectral excess. The new work combines later Gd exposure with the earlier phase, changes the background rejection and fit, and moves the best-fit preference to 2.6σ rather than merely tightening a limit.\n\nThe comparison is energy-window dependent. KamLAND's [full 9.02–9.42 kton-year analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.29381) found seven candidates against (16.2\\pm9.4) expected background events and set model-dependent **90% CL limits of 38–43 cm⁻² s⁻¹** over a lower-energy window; it does not conflict with Super-K and supplies complementary low-energy information. The remaining uncertainty is whether more exposure and independent detectors preserve the fitted excess with the same spectrum and cross-phase consistency."}, {"id": "paper-2610-33140", "arxiv": "2610.33140", "title": "Search for nonresonant production of strongly coupled dark matter in proton-proton collisions at sqrt(s) = 13 TeV", "authors": "CMS Collaboration", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Collider physics", "Machine learning"], "label": "New paper · 95% CL exclusion", "teaser": "CMS finds no nonresonant semivisible-jet excess in 138 fb⁻¹, while supervised and unsupervised taggers probe complementary hidden-shower regimes.", "path": "2026/10/27/index.html#paper-2610-33140", "reportDate": "27 October 2026", "searchText": "Search for nonresonant production of strongly coupled dark matter in proton-proton collisions at sqrt(s) = 13 TeV CMS Collaboration 2610.33140 CMS finds no nonresonant semivisible-jet excess in 138 fb⁻¹, while supervised and unsupervised taggers probe complementary hidden-shower regimes. 2026-10-27 27 October 2026 Dark matter Collider physics Machine learning #### 2. CMS searches for nonresonant semivisible jets with supervised and anomaly-detection taggers\n\n**CMS Collaboration — [arXiv:2610.33140v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33140v1).** Submitted **23 October, 16:11:58 UTC**; submitted to *JHEP*. Experimental search with 95% confidence exclusions.\n\nA confining hidden sector can produce jets containing visible dark-hadron decays and stable invisible hadrons, so missing transverse momentum aligns with a jet. CMS searches **138 fb⁻¹** of 13-TeV proton-proton data for nonresonant production through a bifundamental scalar mediator (Phi). The benchmark parameters are (m_\\Phi), a quark–dark-quark Yukawa coupling (lambda), dark-hadron mass (m_\\mathrm{dark}), and invisible-hadron fraction (r_\\mathrm{inv}). A supervised ParticleNet graph network and an unsupervised Wasserstein-normalized autoencoder target complementary jet structures; multijet backgrounds are estimated with a data-driven ABCD construction using decorrelated discriminants.\n\nNo significant excess appears. With (m_\\mathrm{dark}=20\\) GeV and (lambda=1), the supervised analysis excludes (m_\\Phi=0.5)–**2.4 TeV** at 95% CL for its most favorable (r_\\mathrm{inv}=0.2)–0.5 region. The unsupervised analysis reaches (0.5)–**2.1 TeV**, strongest at (r_\\mathrm{inv}=0.6)–0.9. For (r_\\mathrm{inv}=0.3), (m_\\mathrm{dark}=20\\) GeV, the supervised search excludes all tested couplings for (m_\\Phi<1.6) TeV; the anomaly detector excludes (lambda>1.8) through the tested (m_\\Phi=3.0) TeV endpoint. Limits use asymptotic modified-frequentist (CL_s).\n\nSensitivity falls near (r_\\mathrm{inv}=0), where missing momentum is small. The supervised tagger also weakens as (r_\\mathrm{inv}\\to1), where visible substructure disappears; the unsupervised tagger performs better there but its signal response depends on training and reconstruction choices. These exclusions apply to the stated shower, flavor, mass and coupling assumptions. They are not generic mass limits on strongly coupled dark matter.\n\n##### History\n\nCMS's [2021 resonant search](https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.11125) targeted a leptophobic (Z') and excluded (Z') masses from **1.5 to 4.0 TeV**, reaching 5.1 TeV with a boosted-decision-tree tagger for a fixed SM-quark coupling of 0.25. That established the semivisible-jet signature, but its s-channel resonance and parameters are not directly comparable to the new bifundamental t-channel mediator.\n\nATLAS then performed the first [nonresonant t-channel semivisible-jet search](https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.18037) in 139 fb⁻¹ and excluded mediator masses up to **2.7 TeV at 95% CL** for unit coupling. CMS now adds two modern jet-tagging strategies and maps how they exchange sensitivity across (r_\\mathrm{inv}), but the two experiments use different hidden-flavor and cross-section conventions; quoting an improvement factor would be misleading. A common signal prescription and combined reinterpretation would be required for a clean comparison."}, {"id": "paper-2610-33548", "arxiv": "2610.33548", "title": "PeV Diffuse Neutrinos from Magnetically Arrested Disks in Low-Luminosity Active Galactic Nuclei", "authors": "Fan Wu and Benzhong Dai", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Cosmic rays", "AGN"], "label": "New paper · population-model prediction", "teaser": "A magnetically arrested LLAGN population can peak near 1.5 PeV without overshooting IceCube, but the normalization constrains a product of uncertain source and acceleration efficiencies.", "path": "2026/10/27/index.html#paper-2610-33548", "reportDate": "27 October 2026", "searchText": "PeV Diffuse Neutrinos from Magnetically Arrested Disks in Low-Luminosity Active Galactic Nuclei Fan Wu and Benzhong Dai 2610.33548 A magnetically arrested LLAGN population can peak near 1.5 PeV without overshooting IceCube, but the normalization constrains a product of uncertain source and acceleration efficiencies. 2026-10-27 27 October 2026 Neutrinos Cosmic rays AGN #### 3. Magnetically arrested LLAGN disks could add a hard PeV neutrino component\n\n**Fan Wu and Benzhong Dai — [arXiv:2610.33548v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33548v1).** Submitted **24 October, 09:01:41 UTC**; accepted for publication in *The Astrophysical Journal*. Population-model prediction, not a source identification or flux measurement.\n\nThe authors model cosmic-ray acceleration in magnetically arrested disks (MADs) inside low-luminosity active galactic nuclei (LLAGNs). They solve single-source particle transport across black-hole mass and accretion rate, include (pp) and (p\\gamma) pion/kaon production, charm, secondary cooling and three-flavor propagation, then integrate an Hα luminosity function weighted by a black-hole mass function. The estimated MAD fraction, derived from the ratio of low-excitation radio-galaxy to LLAGN number densities, is (f_\\mathrm{MAD}^{\\rm LF}\\simeq1.6\\times10^{-3}).\n\nAt that normalization, the all-flavor diffuse intensity peaks near **1.5 PeV** at\n\n\\[\nE_\\nu^2\\Phi_\\nu\\simeq2.6\\times10^{-9}\\ \\mathrm{GeV\\,cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}\\,sr^{-1}}.\n\\]\n\nPhotomeson production on inverse-Compton optical/UV photons dominates the peak; (pp) pions dominate below about 0.1 PeV, and kaons dominate the cooled high-energy tail. The predicted Earthly flavor ratio near the peak is (0.296:0.361:0.344), with mild muon damping. Comparison with IceCube's segmented diffuse spectrum permits at most\n\n\\[\nf_\\mathrm{MAD}^{\\max}\\simeq2.3\\times10^{-3}\\left(\\frac{0.0495}{\\epsilon_{\\rm NT}\\epsilon_{\\rm dis}}\\right),\n\\]\n\nand the fiducial spectrum stays below IceCube's 90% CL extremely-high-energy differential limit while approaching IceCube-Gen2 sensitivity near 30 PeV.\n\nThis is a normalization ceiling inside the model, not a frequentist confidence limit on the astrophysical MAD fraction. IceCube constrains the product (f_\\mathrm{MAD}\\epsilon_{\\rm NT}\\epsilon_{\\rm dis}); the source fraction, nonthermal efficiency and dissipation efficiency are degenerate. Neglected redshift evolution, the uncertain Hα and black-hole mass functions, hadronic yields, acceleration microphysics and the assumption that low-excitation radio galaxies trace neutrino-luminous MADs dominate the interpretation.\n\n##### History\n\nThe foundational LLAGN-RIAF calculation by [Kimura, Murase and Toma](https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.3588) showed that stochastic proton acceleration could reach beyond 10 PeV and, if about 1% of accretion power entered nonthermal ions, yield as much as (E_\\nu^2\\Phi_\\nu\\sim3\\times10^{-8}\\ \\mathrm{GeV\\,cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}\\,sr^{-1}}). A later [thermal-photon plus neutrino population model](https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.01934) connected LLAGN hot flows to both the MeV gamma-ray and TeV–PeV neutrino backgrounds. Those studies established plausibility but allowed broad source and acceleration normalizations.\n\nThe new calculation specifically isolates the MAD state, averages sources over a black-hole mass function and uses the observed low-excitation radio-galaxy abundance to set (f_\\mathrm{MAD}). Its fiducial peak is about an order of magnitude below the early maximal RIAF estimate, so it adds a high-energy component rather than claiming to explain the entire IceCube flux. Updated IceCube data already disfavor some higher-intensity earlier MAD curves. The decisive uncertainty remains population normalization and whether the assumed acceleration efficiencies occur in real LLAGN disks."}, {"id": "paper-2610-35379", "arxiv": "2610.35379", "title": "Revisiting supernova constraints on dark gauge bosons", "authors": "Zun Wang and Yongchao Zhang", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["BSM", "Supernovae", "Dark photons"], "label": "New paper · astrophysical constraint calculation", "teaser": "Realistic supernova profiles and low-energy explosion energetics extend model-dependent exclusions for dark photons and two light U(1) gauge bosons to several hundred MeV.", "path": "2026/10/27/index.html#paper-2610-35379", "reportDate": "27 October 2026", "searchText": "Revisiting supernova constraints on dark gauge bosons Zun Wang and Yongchao Zhang 2610.35379 Realistic supernova profiles and low-energy explosion energetics extend model-dependent exclusions for dark photons and two light U(1) gauge bosons to several hundred MeV. 2026-10-27 27 October 2026 BSM Supernovae Dark photons #### 4. Realistic supernova transport extends dark-gauge-boson exclusions, with large model dependence\n\n**Zun Wang and Yongchao Zhang — [arXiv:2610.35379v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.35379v1).** Submitted **26 October, 13:03:10 UTC**; preprint, with no journal status stated. Astrophysical constraint calculation.\n\nThe paper recalculates production, absorption, decay and geometric escape for a kinetically mixed dark photon, a (U(1)_{B-L}) boson and a (U(1)_{L_\\mu-L_\\tau}) boson. For dark photons and (B-L), it retains full momentum and mediator-mass dependence in one-pion-exchange nucleon bremsstrahlung and includes in-medium transverse/longitudinal modes. It evaluates conventional SN1987A cooling with three proto-neutron-star profiles and a separate low-energy-supernova (LESN) condition: exotic energy deposited in the mantle must not exceed the small observed explosion energy. The headline values use the SFHo-18.8 (18.8M_\\odot) profile.\n\nFor transverse dark photons, SN1987A cooling excludes approximately (1.7\\times10^{-10}\\lesssim\\epsilon\\lesssim2\\times10^{-7}) and masses to **230 MeV**; no longitudinal region survives as an exclusion with the realistic profiles. The LESN calculation excludes (5.8\\times10^{-12}\\lesssim\\epsilon\\lesssim6\\times10^{-7}) for masses to **450 MeV**. For (B-L), combining modes gives LESN coverage around (1.9\\times10^{-12}\\lesssim g_{BL}\\lesssim3.1\\times10^{-7}) up to **440 MeV**; the smallest quoted longitudinal SN1987A coupling is (2.9\\times10^{-16}). For (L_\\mu-L_\\tau), SN1987A gives roughly (1.4\\times10^{-10}\\)–(6.7\\times10^{-7}) to 300 MeV, while LESNe give (6.4\\times10^{-12}\\)–(9\\times10^{-7}) to **600 MeV**.\n\nThese are exclusion regions under specified supernova profiles, transport prescriptions and energy-budget criteria, not laboratory confidence intervals with a single probabilistic coverage. The OPE nuclear treatment carries known factor-level uncertainty, the historical SN1987A neutrino sample is sparse, and LESN explosion energies and progenitor structures are model dependent. Time-dependent stellar evolution and feedback of exotic transport on the explosion are not simulated self-consistently.\n\n##### History\n\nThe classic SN1987A argument excludes couplings that let a new light particle free-stream enough energy to shorten the observed neutrino burst; trapping reopens the large-coupling region. [Chang, Essig and McDermott (2017)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.03864) incorporated plasma effects and realistic profiles for dark photons below roughly 100 MeV, finding profile and transport uncertainties of order factors of a few. Later work emphasized that weakly coupled particles can also alter the shock or deposit energy in unusually faint supernovae. The recent [Heavy Dark Photon Handbook](https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.15785) assembled cooling, LESN, fireball, gamma-ray, cosmological and other constraints across (0.1)–(10^3) MeV.\n\nThe new work is valuable because it applies a common geometry and profile treatment to three vector models and extends the mass reach of its LESN bands. It does not erase earlier limits: laboratory bounds dominate at larger couplings, cosmology can dominate elsewhere, and earlier SN analyses use different nuclear and dynamical approximations. The very low quoted couplings should therefore be read as profile-dependent astrophysical exclusions rather than precision determinations."}, {"id": "paper-2610-34325", "arxiv": "2610.34325", "title": "Near-exact Upper Confidence Limits for Axion Haloscopes", "authors": "Andrea Gallo Rosso and Jan Conrad", "primary_category": "physics.data-an", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Axions", "Statistics"], "label": "New paper · statistical method", "teaser": "A likelihood construction that includes adaptive rescans gives calibrated 90% upper limits and can materially outperform geometric or Bayesian-power-measured exclusions under the tested protocols.", "path": "2026/10/27/index.html#paper-2610-34325", "reportDate": "27 October 2026", "searchText": "Near-exact Upper Confidence Limits for Axion Haloscopes Andrea Gallo Rosso and Jan Conrad 2610.34325 A likelihood construction that includes adaptive rescans gives calibrated 90% upper limits and can materially outperform geometric or Bayesian-power-measured exclusions under the tested protocols. 2026-10-27 27 October 2026 Dark matter Axions Statistics #### 5. Adaptive axion rescans can retain frequentist coverage without the usual loss in power\n\n**Andrea Gallo Rosso and Jan Conrad — [arXiv:2610.34325v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34325v1).** Submitted **25 October, 14:33:45 UTC**; preprint, with no journal status stated. Statistical-method paper with a projected experimental example.\n\nAxion haloscopes rescan frequencies when a power excess crosses a threshold. Because the decision to acquire more data depends on the data already observed, an upper limit must average over the complete stopping tree. The authors model the standardized power measurements as correlated Gaussian cumulative statistics, write the likelihood on each possible stopping branch, and calibrate a one-sided upper confidence limit across all branches. They also derive a practical “mix” rule that reduces sensitivity to detector settings for rescans that never occur.\n\nFor a representative two-scan problem at **90% CL**, the calibrated Bayesian-power-measured (BPM) limit is about **20% higher**—weaker—than the likelihood limit; without frequentist calibration it can be about **80% higher**. In an ALPHA Phase-I projection over **10–20 GHz (40–80 μeV)** with rescan thresholds (3\\le\\beta\\le5), uncalibrated BPM and the older geometric construction give coupling limits **30% and 115% higher**, respectively, than the likelihood method. The mix rule worsens the likelihood limit by only about **0.5%** in that benchmark.\n\nThese are expected limits under Gaussian noise, specified thresholds and an idealized signal model; they are not measured exclusions on (g_{a\\gamma}). Explicit numerical comparisons are shown only for at most two scans, even though the formal construction extends further. Persistent spectral lines, non-Gaussian tails, calibration drift and frequency correlations require experiment-specific validation.\n\n##### History\n\nEarly HAYSTAC analyses used a geometric pass/fail protocol calibrated to a discovery benchmark. That approach is simple but discards measured excess information and can confuse conditional false-negative rates with unconditional coverage over an adaptive sequence. The [Bayesian power-measured framework](https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.08510) reanalyzed HAYSTAC Phase I, tightened coupling constraints and estimated a **36% scan-rate improvement** over the threshold method, while explicitly adopting Bayesian evidence rather than frequentist confidence coverage.\n\nThe new paper shows that a fully specified frequentist likelihood can recover much of that statistical efficiency while preserving nominal coverage, and it quantifies the cost of BPM calibration. Its ALPHA example uses the collaboration's [Phase-I 40–80 μeV design projection](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.10292), so the quoted percentage gains are analysis forecasts, not added hardware sensitivity. The open question is whether the advantage persists under each experiment's real vetoes, line cleaning, rescans and changing noise environment."}, {"id": "paper-2610-31758", "arxiv": "2610.31758", "title": "Collapsars Are Not Heavy r-Process Factories", "authors": "Ore Gottlieb, Danat Issa and Jonatan Jacquemin-Ide", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Compact objects", "Nucleosynthesis", "Gamma rays"], "label": "New paper · physical no-go argument", "teaser": "Magnetized collapsar disks face a tension: fields strong enough to eject gas also shorten neutronization, leaving the most favorable modeled outflow above the heavy-r-process electron-fraction threshold.", "path": "2026/10/26/index.html#paper-2610-31758", "reportDate": "26 October 2026", "searchText": "Collapsars Are Not Heavy r-Process Factories Ore Gottlieb, Danat Issa and Jonatan Jacquemin-Ide 2610.31758 Magnetized collapsar disks face a tension: fields strong enough to eject gas also shorten neutronization, leaving the most favorable modeled outflow above the heavy-r-process electron-fraction threshold. 2026-10-26 26 October 2026 Compact objects Nucleosynthesis Gamma rays #### 1. A magnetic tension challenges heavy r-process production in collapsars\n\n**Ore Gottlieb, Danat Issa and Jonatan Jacquemin-Ide — [arXiv:2610.31758v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31758v1).** Submitted **22 October, 18:00:04 UTC**. Theoretical physical-bound argument, not a yield measurement.\n\nHeavy r-process nuclei require neutron-rich ejecta, conventionally (Y_e\\lesssim0.25), but collapsar gas must first neutronize in the disk and then escape. The authors evolve (Y_e) down an inflow trajectory while choosing, at every radius, the temperature that maximizes net neutronization. That deliberately favorable construction gives a thermal-history-independent lower bound. They then impose magnetic-flux and jet-power conditions needed to unbind material, and compare the analytic result with two general-relativistic MHD simulations.\n\nFor an optimistic (3M_\\odot) black hole, universal unbinding radius (R_{\\rm unb}=10r_g), spin 0.3 and upper representative GRB accretion rate \\(\\dot M_{\\rm BH}\\lesssim0.4M_\\odot\\,{\\rm s}^{-1}\\), the minimum permitted electron fraction is **(Y_e\\gtrsim0.33)**. At dimensionless horizon fluxes ϕ = 20, 30, 40 and 50, the corresponding bounds are about **0.33, 0.44, 0.48 and 0.49**. Weak fields permit neutronization but struggle to eject against fallback; strong fields unbind gas while thickening the disk and shortening the weak-interaction time. Rare extreme engines, including direct-horizon collapsars, remain possible exceptions. [Full methods and simulation comparison](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.31758v1).\n\nThe bound assumes midplane gas, temperature optimized for neutronization and an optimistic ejection radius. It does not calculate a population-integrated abundance and does not apply to neutron-star-merger ejecta. A future direct-horizon GRMHD calculation with composition transport is the relevant stress test.\n\n##### History\n\n[Siegel, Barnes and Metzger (2019)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.00098) simulated neutron-rich collapsar-disk outflows and argued that collapsars might supply **more than 80%** of cosmic r-process material. [Brauer et al. (2021)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.15837) showed that stochastic yields around (0.07M_\\odot) per event could reproduce metal-poor stellar [Eu/Fe] scatter. Those were yield and chemical-evolution claims conditional on ejecting neutron-rich disk material. The new paper attacks that physical prerequisite and therefore conflicts with the standard heavy-yield premise rather than with the abundance bookkeeping.\n\nNeutron-star mergers already have direct multimessenger evidence through GW170817 and remain sufficient in principle, while collapsar optical/near-infrared searches have not established a heavy-r-process component. The remaining uncertainty is concentrated in unusual engine geometries and high accretion rates, not in the authors' claim of a universal mathematical no-go theorem."}, {"id": "paper-2610-32716", "arxiv": "2610.32716", "title": "Magnetic Reconnection Modifies Cosmic-Ray Transport in the Interstellar Medium", "authors": "Antoine Baldacchino-Jordan, Michal Hanasz, Vinod Pisharody, Dominik Wóltański and Artur Gawryszczak", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Gamma rays", "Galactic astrophysics"], "label": "New paper · numerical transport study", "teaser": "Resistive-MHD boxes show that reconnection-driven magnetic topology can raise disk residence times and the B/C peak even when the microscopic diffusion law is held fixed.", "path": "2026/10/26/index.html#paper-2610-32716", "reportDate": "26 October 2026", "searchText": "Magnetic Reconnection Modifies Cosmic-Ray Transport in the Interstellar Medium Antoine Baldacchino-Jordan, Michal Hanasz, Vinod Pisharody, Dominik Wóltański and Artur Gawryszczak 2610.32716 Resistive-MHD boxes show that reconnection-driven magnetic topology can raise disk residence times and the B/C peak even when the microscopic diffusion law is held fixed. 2026-10-26 26 October 2026 Cosmic rays Gamma rays Galactic astrophysics #### 2. Reconnection changes the magnetic routes by which Galactic cosmic rays escape\n\n**Antoine Baldacchino-Jordan, Michal Hanasz, Vinod Pisharody, Dominik Wóltański and Artur Gawryszczak — [arXiv:2610.32716v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32716v1).** Submitted **23 October, 14:35:33 UTC**; submitted to ApJ Letters. Numerical transport study.\n\nGravity-stratified MHD boxes contain thermal gas, spectrally resolved carbon and boron nuclei in CRESP, and spectrally unresolved protons. Cosmic rays diffuse anisotropically with (D_\\parallel=3\\times10^{28}\\ {\nm cm^2\\,s^{-1}}) at 10 GV and rigidity slope δ = 0.3. Resistive MHD supplies a subgrid description of unresolved turbulent magnetic dissipation, with magnetic diffusivity η = 0, 30, 60 or 100 pc² Myr⁻¹. The comparison averages 210–500 Myr.\n\nNonzero η regenerates a more horizontal halo field, inhibiting vertical escape and increasing disk cosmic-ray energy. The simulated B/C maximum is about **0.17** near 1 GeV per nucleon in ideal MHD, **0.19–0.24** at η = 30, and **0.24–0.27** at η = 60–100 pc² Myr⁻¹. Greater confinement raises cosmic-ray pressure and outflows; hadronic gamma-ray luminosity becomes nonmonotonic at the largest η because the outflow also lowers gas density. [Equations and diagnostics](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.32716v1).\n\nThis is qualitative evidence that topology and local dynamics affect the effective escape time. The boxes omit a full Galactic geometry, cosmic-ray streaming, thermal/stellar feedback and spectrally resolved protons, so they do not predict a gamma-ray spectrum. The adopted δ = 0.3 is also shallower than values near 0.5 often favored by global fits. η is a subgrid control parameter, not a measurement of the Milky Way reconnection rate.\n\n##### History\n\nThe B/C ratio became a standard grammage and diffusion diagnostic because boron is mainly secondary. [Strong and Moskalenko's GALPROP framework](https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/9807150) fitted B/C and radioactive ratios with smooth, Galaxy-scale diffusion and reacceleration, while modern AMS-02 precision data expose departures from a single featureless power law. Those approaches infer transport coefficients from local spectra under a chosen global model.\n\nThe new simulations keep the microscopic diffusion law fixed and change magnetic topology, showing that different B/C curves can emerge from the same input (D_\\parallel). This does not overturn AMS constraints or supply a stronger diffusion measurement; it identifies an environmental degeneracy. Whether the effect survives in a full galaxy with resolved feedback and streaming is still open."}, {"id": "paper-2610-32845", "arxiv": "2610.32845", "title": "The Galactic Center Gamma-Ray Excess and the Look-Elsewhere Effect", "authors": "Isabel S. Sands and Philip F. Hopkins", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Cosmic rays", "Dark matter"], "label": "New paper · simulation proof of concept", "teaser": "Time-dependent cosmic-ray simulations produce transient diffuse gamma-ray residuals resembling the Galactic-center excess, but do not yet quantify how often a Milky-Way-like observer should see one.", "path": "2026/10/26/index.html#paper-2610-32845", "reportDate": "26 October 2026", "searchText": "The Galactic Center Gamma-Ray Excess and the Look-Elsewhere Effect Isabel S. Sands and Philip F. Hopkins 2610.32845 Time-dependent cosmic-ray simulations produce transient diffuse gamma-ray residuals resembling the Galactic-center excess, but do not yet quantify how often a Milky-Way-like observer should see one. 2026-10-26 26 October 2026 Gamma rays Cosmic rays Dark matter #### 3. A time-dependent diffuse foreground can mimic the Galactic-center excess\n\n**Isabel S. Sands and Philip F. Hopkins — [arXiv:2610.32845v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32845v1).** Submitted **23 October, 16:19:38 UTC**. Simulation proof of concept.\n\nTwo FIRE Milky-Way analogues with MHD and time-dependent, spectrally resolved cosmic-ray transport are converted into gamma-ray maps. The authors subtract a low-flux time-averaged baseline from instantaneous snapshots, using 10° and 20° regions and several radial cuts. The simulated interval extends from roughly 460 to 20 Myr lookback; the central-kiloparsec transport time is of order **1 Myr**, comparable with changes in the interstellar medium.\n\nResiduals from **0.1–100 GeV** sometimes reach several times the measured Galactic-center excess and, in selected snapshots, resemble its spectrum and morphology. The result demonstrates that temporally fluctuating ordinary-source emission can generate a GCE-like residual even when the baseline model is internally consistent. [Maps, spectra and transport setup](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.32845v1).\n\nIt is not a statistical look-elsewhere calculation: only two galaxies are used, no global probability is reported, and Fermi-LAT data are not refitted with simulation-derived templates. The simulated centers form stars **one to two orders of magnitude faster** than the Milky Way's Central Molecular Zone, making their absolute gamma-ray spectra about an order of magnitude too bright. The study expands the diffuse-model uncertainty budget; it does not identify the real excess as a transient or exclude dark matter or millisecond pulsars.\n\n##### History\n\nThe excess has long admitted dark-matter-annihilation and unresolved-source interpretations. Statistical claims for point-source structure were weakened when [Leane and Slatyer (2020)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.12370) showed that unmodeled north–south asymmetry can create a spurious point-source preference. Conversely, [Song et al. (2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.05449) found a robust preference for a boxy bulge template over dark-matter templates across improved diffuse models.\n\nBoth lines already made foreground modeling central. Sands and Hopkins add a missing dimension: time variability in cosmic-ray sources and transport. The decisive next comparison is to pass simulated skies through the actual Fermi template pipeline and ask, with a Milky-Way-matched central star-formation history, how often a residual as spectrally and spatially similar as the observed GCE occurs."}, {"id": "paper-2610-32935", "arxiv": "2610.32935", "title": "Three subpopulations of binary black holes in mass, spin magnitudes and tilts following GWTC-5", "authors": "Asad Hussain, Maximiliano Isi and Aaron Zimmerman", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Gravitational waves", "Compact objects"], "label": "New paper · hierarchical population inference", "teaser": "A flexible analysis of 259 GWTC-5 binaries resolves low-, medium- and high-spin components whose mixture changes with primary mass; formation-channel labels remain interpretive.", "path": "2026/10/26/index.html#paper-2610-32935", "reportDate": "26 October 2026", "searchText": "Three subpopulations of binary black holes in mass, spin magnitudes and tilts following GWTC-5 Asad Hussain, Maximiliano Isi and Aaron Zimmerman 2610.32935 A flexible analysis of 259 GWTC-5 binaries resolves low-, medium- and high-spin components whose mixture changes with primary mass; formation-channel labels remain interpretive. 2026-10-26 26 October 2026 Gravitational waves Compact objects #### 4. GWTC-5 favors three mass-dependent black-hole spin populations\n\n**Asad Hussain, Maximiliano Isi and Aaron Zimmerman — [arXiv:2610.32935v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32935v1).** Submitted **23 October, 17:31:43 UTC**. Hierarchical Bayesian population inference.\n\nThe analysis uses **259 binary-black-hole events** in GWTC-5 with false-alarm rate below 1 yr⁻¹. Three flexible components have separate mass-ratio, component-spin-magnitude and tilt distributions, with mixing fractions that vary with primary mass. Injection campaigns encode selection effects. Truncated Gaussian mixtures stabilize event likelihoods when a population component becomes very narrow, and NUTS samples the hierarchical posterior.\n\nThe low-spin component has χ₁ and χ₂ near **(0.02^{+0.03}_{-0.02})**, dominates around (35M_\\odot) with fraction **(0.8\\pm0.1)**, and is favored over a nonzero-spin version by (log_{10}B=6.1^{+0.2}_{-0.3}). That Bayes factor tests zero spin *inside this component*; it is not evidence for three versus two populations. A medium component near χ ≈ 0.2 is aligned and matters mainly below (20M_\\odot). A broad, high-spin component with χ₁ around **(0.7^{+0.3}_{-0.4})** dominates above (60M_\\odot). A fourth component becomes degenerate rather than distinct. [Model, priors and posterior checks](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.32935v1).\n\nFormation labels remain hypotheses. Low aligned spins may indicate efficient angular-momentum loss in isolated binaries; broad tilts and high spins can arise dynamically or through hierarchical mergers, but the population fit does not prove either channel. Above (100M_\\odot) the result is prior-sensitive and only two events carry much information.\n\n##### History\n\n[GWTC-5](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.27223) enlarged the catalog and changed the statistical base for population work. On GWTC-4, the same authors found [two mass-dependent spin components](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.24281), with a low-spin regime below a broad (35–70M_\\odot) transition and a rapidly spinning high-mass component. The new medium-spin population is the main structural addition; the low/high trend is a confirmation with more data and a richer model.\n\nComparisons of Bayes factors across these papers are not simple because the catalogs, priors, mixture parameterizations and likelihood estimators differ. The astrophysical uncertainty is whether the statistical components map one-to-one onto formation channels; metallicity, common-envelope physics, dynamical assembly and hierarchical mergers can overlap in mass and spin."}, {"id": "paper-2610-32747", "arxiv": "2610.32747", "title": "Heavy Sterile Fermions from the Atmosphere: LZ Limits on Neutrino Up-Scattering", "authors": "D. Aristizabal Sierra, V. De Romeri, D. K. Papoulias and I. M. Shoemaker", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "BSM", "Dark matter detectors"], "label": "New paper · phenomenological reinterpretation", "teaser": "A two-bin reinterpretation of LZ's extended recoil data probes atmospheric-neutrino up-scattering above roughly 50 MeV; the lone 248-keV event is not compellingly explained.", "path": "2026/10/26/index.html#paper-2610-32747", "reportDate": "26 October 2026", "searchText": "Heavy Sterile Fermions from the Atmosphere: LZ Limits on Neutrino Up-Scattering D. Aristizabal Sierra, V. De Romeri, D. K. Papoulias and I. M. Shoemaker 2610.32747 A two-bin reinterpretation of LZ's extended recoil data probes atmospheric-neutrino up-scattering above roughly 50 MeV; the lone 248-keV event is not compellingly explained. 2026-10-26 26 October 2026 Neutrinos BSM Dark matter detectors #### 5. LZ's high-recoil window probes atmospheric-neutrino up-scattering\n\n**D. Aristizabal Sierra, V. De Romeri, D. K. Papoulias and I. M. Shoemaker — [arXiv:2610.32747v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32747v1).** Submitted **23 October, 14:57:33 UTC**. Independent phenomenological reinterpretation, not an LZ Collaboration result.\n\nThe input is LZ's **2.84 tonne-year** extended nuclear-recoil sample from **5.4–270 keV**, including one event at (248\\pm23_{\\rm stat}\\pm23_{\\rm sys}) keV. The authors approximate it with two bins: 5.4–225 keV, where 1,712 background events are expected and no excess appears, and 225–270 keV, where the reported background is 0.0106 events and one event occurred. Standard atmospheric coherent scattering contributes only about **0.11** and **(10^{-6})** events, respectively.\n\nThey calculate νN → χN for a sterile fermion of mass (m_\\chi), using universal scalar, vector or axial mediator couplings (g_a) with mediator mass (m_X), plus a photon transition dipole (μ_\\nu). The 90% confidence contours use Δχ² = 4.61 in each two-parameter plane, with other model parameters fixed as shown. The null lower-energy bin opens parameter space above roughly **50–60 MeV** and reaches final-state masses near or above **1 GeV**, beyond low-energy solar, reactor and stopped-pion kinematics. [Operators, likelihood and exclusion figures](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.32747v1).\n\nFor scalar, vector and axial models, fitting the lone high-recoil event pushes the effective scale (m_X/g_X) to about **1.41 GeV**, the authors' perturbativity boundary; the dipole gives a poor fit. Up-scattering is therefore not compelling. The 248-keV recoil lies near a xenon nuclear-form-factor minimum, and omitted incoherent nucleon scattering can matter. A two-bin public reconstruction cannot replace LZ's event-level S1c/S2c likelihood.\n\n##### History\n\nThe [LZ Collaboration's extended-window search](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.02823) reported the exposure, background model and single high-recoil event while setting dark-matter limits; it did not claim a sterile-fermion signal. Earlier neutrino-up-scattering bounds come from solar-neutrino direct detection, COHERENT and reactor CEνNS at low masses, accelerator beams such as LSND/DUNE, and LEP at collider momentum transfer. These constrain different coupling combinations and mediator assumptions.\n\nFor the dipole portal, the paper's photon-only LZ curve can exceed LSND or photon-only LEP reach above (m_\\chi\\sim100) MeV, while a gauge-complete LEP photon-plus-Z treatment is stronger. For scalar/vector/axial mediators there is no single earlier number to divide into an improvement factor: the result is a two-dimensional (g_a)-versus-(m_X) contour that shifts with (m_\\chi), quark/lepton universality and fixed mediator choices. The robust advance is access to heavier kinematics; the constraint remains model-dependent."}, {"id": "paper-2610-31881", "arxiv": "2610.31881", "title": "Semi-Analytic Predictions for Strong-Lensing Perturbers in Cold and Self-Interacting Dark Matter", "authors": "Jonathan Tekverk, Duncan K. Adams, Simon Birrer, Rouven Essig and Daniel Gilman", "primary_category": "astro-ph.CO", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Cosmology", "Strong lensing"], "label": "New paper · model comparison", "teaser": "A semi-analytic SIDM model produces three unusually dense lensing perturbers more often than CDM, but baryons, tidal survival and lens-model systematics prevent a parameter constraint.", "path": "2026/10/26/index.html#paper-2610-31881", "reportDate": "26 October 2026", "searchText": "Semi-Analytic Predictions for Strong-Lensing Perturbers in Cold and Self-Interacting Dark Matter Jonathan Tekverk, Duncan K. Adams, Simon Birrer, Rouven Essig and Daniel Gilman 2610.31881 A semi-analytic SIDM model produces three unusually dense lensing perturbers more often than CDM, but baryons, tidal survival and lens-model systematics prevent a parameter constraint. 2026-10-26 26 October 2026 Dark matter Cosmology Strong lensing #### 6. Can self-interactions explain unusually dense lensing perturbers?\n\n**Jonathan Tekverk, Duncan K. Adams, Simon Birrer, Rouven Essig and Daniel Gilman — [arXiv:2610.31881v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31881v1).** Submitted **22 October, 20:22:44 UTC**. Semi-analytic model comparison, not a dark-matter detection or exclusion.\n\nCDM merger trees are coupled to a gravothermal SIDM evolution model and projected into enclosed masses and deflection-density slopes. The fiducial velocity-dependent interaction has normalization σ₀/(m_\\chi=147.1\\ {\nm cm^2\\,g^{-1}}) and turnover speed (w=120\\ {\nm km\\,s^{-1}}). Predictions are compared with perturbers inferred in PJ011646, SDSS J0946+1006 and JVAS B1938+666.\n\nAcross an entire assumed (10^{13}M_\\odot) host halo, the mean numbers matching the published posterior regions are approximately:\n\n| Lens perturber | CDM | cored SIDM | core-collapsed SIDM |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| PJ011646 | 0.002 | 0.6 | none matching |\n| SDSS J0946+1006 | none in 900 realizations | 0.004 | 3 |\n| JVAS B1938+666 detection V | 0.2 | 20 | 220 |\n\nThese are model-predicted abundances anywhere in the virial volume, not probabilities of observing perturbers near the lensed images. Among tested sets, reproducing all three requires roughly (w\\gtrsim100\\ {\nm km\\,s^{-1}}) and σ₀/(m_\\chi\\gtrsim100\\ {\nm cm^2\\,g^{-1}}). [Semi-analytic machinery and sensitivity tests](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.31881v1).\n\nThe calculation is dark-matter-only. Baryonic contraction is especially relevant to the (5.6\\times10^{10}M_\\odot) J0946 progenitor, which would plausibly contain stars not included in the lens model. Late core collapse, tidal survival, host mass and the lens-inference posteriors all carry large uncertainty. Thus the preferred interaction region is a benchmark, not a confidence interval.\n\n##### History\n\nStrong-lensing imaging first established sensitivity to otherwise dark substructure, but individual inferences can depend strongly on the macromodel and line-of-sight treatment. [Minor et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.10627) found J0946 unusually concentrated: less than 1% of comparable CDM mock views achieved its steep slope, and those analogues were too luminous. [Amvrosiadis et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.21212) inferred the PJ011646 subhalo at about 5.8σ relative to a multipole lens model, with (M_{200}=2.78^{+0.43}_{-0.66}\\times10^{10}M_\\odot) and concentration (30^{+5}_{-7}).\n\nThe new work asks whether one SIDM microphysics choice can populate all three posterior regions; it finds a qualitative yes more often than CDM. It neither reanalyzes the imaging nor marginalizes over baryons. A joint forward analysis of lens images, luminous structure, line-of-sight halos and cosmological hydrodynamics is needed before treating these counts as evidence for σ/(m_\\chi)."}, {"id": "paper-2610-30968", "arxiv": "2610.30968", "title": "Search for Ultra-Relativistic Magnetic Monopoles with LHAASO-KM2A", "authors": "Zhen Cao et al.", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["BSM", "Cosmic rays"], "label": "New paper · experimental null search", "teaser": "Four years of KM2A data constrain monopoles at Lorentz factors 100,000–10,000,000, filling a different speed regime from existing IceCube and Auger searches.", "path": "2026/10/23/index.html#paper-2610-30968", "reportDate": "23 October 2026", "searchText": "Search for Ultra-Relativistic Magnetic Monopoles with LHAASO-KM2A Zhen Cao et al. 2610.30968 Four years of KM2A data constrain monopoles at Lorentz factors 100,000–10,000,000, filling a different speed regime from existing IceCube and Auger searches. 2026-10-23 23 October 2026 BSM Cosmic rays #### 1. LHAASO searches for ultra-relativistic magnetic monopoles\n\n**Zhen Cao et al. — [arXiv:2610.30968v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30968v1).** Submitted **22 October, 09:13 UTC**. Experimental null result.\n\nUsing **1,438.81 live days** of full-array KM2A data, the authors seek the compact, muon-poor air showers produced by monopoles. A neural classifier combines shower morphology; a likelihood fit uses data control regions for the background and profiles normalization and shape uncertainties.\n\nFor one Dirac magnetic charge and reference mass \\(10^5\\) GeV, the observed **90% confidence CLs flux limits** are:\n\n| Lorentz factor \\(\\gamma\\) | Flux limit, \\(\\mathrm{cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}\\,sr^{-1}}\\) |\n| --- | --- |\n| \\(10^5\\) | \\(2.6\\times10^{-16}\\) |\n| \\(10^6\\) | \\(2.24\\times10^{-18}\\) |\n| \\(10^7\\) | \\(8.8\\times10^{-19}\\) |\n\nThere is no significant excess. Transferring these limits to other masses requires negligible atmospheric deceleration and the assumed energy-loss regime. Shower simulation and the portability of background control templates remain important dependencies. [Methods, flux table and supplement](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.30968v1).\n\n##### History\n\n[IceCube's relativistic-monopole search](https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.13719) covered speeds \\(0.75<\\beta<0.995\\), with 90% limits below \\(2\\times10^{-19}\\) over much of that interval. [Auger's ultra-relativistic search](https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.04451) reached approximately \\(10^{-19}\\) at \\(\\gamma=10^9\\), improving to \\(2.5\\times10^{-21}\\) at \\(10^{12}\\), also at 90% confidence. Those numbers concern different speeds and detector responses; they cannot be ranked against KM2A without aligning the physical regime. The advance is coverage between existing search regimes, not a universal strongest monopole bound. Likewise, the Parker argument concerns survival of Galactic magnetic fields and is an astrophysical model constraint, not an experimental confidence interval."}, {"id": "paper-2610-30378", "arxiv": "2610.30378", "title": "Chandra and Multiwavelength Study of Pevatron Candiate HESS J1702-420A", "authors": "Hui Yang, Yichao Lin, Jeremy Hare, Seth Gagnon, Oleg Kargaltsev", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "X rays", "Cosmic rays"], "label": "New paper · candidate counterpart", "teaser": "Chandra resolves a faint point source and diffuse lobes near the PeVatron candidate, supporting a possible pulsar-wind nebula without establishing its TeV association.", "path": "2026/10/23/index.html#paper-2610-30378", "reportDate": "23 October 2026", "searchText": "Chandra and Multiwavelength Study of Pevatron Candiate HESS J1702-420A Hui Yang, Yichao Lin, Jeremy Hare, Seth Gagnon, Oleg Kargaltsev 2610.30378 Chandra resolves a faint point source and diffuse lobes near the PeVatron candidate, supporting a possible pulsar-wind nebula without establishing its TeV association. 2026-10-23 23 October 2026 Gamma rays X rays Cosmic rays #### 2. A possible pulsar-wind nebula near HESS J1702–420A\n\n**Hui Yang, Yichao Lin, Jeremy Hare, Seth Gagnon and Oleg Kargaltsev — [arXiv:2610.30378v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30378v1).** Submitted **21 October, 19:40 UTC**; submitted to A&A. Observational candidate identification.\n\n**50.42 ks of Chandra observations** resolve the previously extended XMM source into a faint point source, CXO J170144.5−421415, and asymmetric diffuse lobes. The combined diffuse emission has photon index **\\(1.8\\pm0.5\\)** and observed 0.5–7 keV flux **\\((1.5\\pm0.1)\\times10^{-13}\\ \\mathrm{erg\\,cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}\\)**; quoted spectral uncertainties are 68% intervals.\n\nThe morphology and spectrum support a pulsar/pulsar-wind-nebula interpretation. However, the point source contains only **17 photons**, no pulsations establish its identity, and its roughly seven-arcminute displacement from the TeV centroid complicates association. Distance and motion assumptions enter a relic-nebula scenario. This is a useful counterpart candidate, not proof that HESS J1702–420A is leptonic or hadronic. [Imaging, spectroscopy and interpretation](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.30378v1).\n\n##### History\n\nThe [H.E.S.S. component analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.06405) isolated a hard component extending toward 100 TeV. In a hadronic interpretation, its proton cutoff exceeded 0.5 PeV at 95% confidence; this was conditional on the radiation model, not direct identification of accelerated protons. [Giunti et al.'s 2022 XMM study](https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.09566) found no clear counterpart, constrained diffuse synchrotron emission and identified an extended X-ray source with uncertain association. Chandra now provides the spatial detail needed to examine that source. The historical ambiguity persists: high-energy gamma rays establish an extreme accelerator, while morphology, magnetic field and particle-population assumptions determine whether electrons or hadrons produce the emission."}, {"id": "paper-2610-30722", "arxiv": "2610.30722", "title": "Search for high-energy neutrinos from Einstein Probe detected Fast X-ray Transients using 14 years of IceCube muon track data", "authors": "Sri Devaki Meduri, Shantanu Desai", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Multimessenger"], "label": "New paper · archival position search", "teaser": "A null search at 27 transient coordinates has a crucial timing limitation: the neutrino data end in 2022, before the 2024–2026 Einstein Probe events.", "path": "2026/10/23/index.html#paper-2610-30722", "reportDate": "23 October 2026", "searchText": "Search for high-energy neutrinos from Einstein Probe detected Fast X-ray Transients using 14 years of IceCube muon track data Sri Devaki Meduri, Shantanu Desai 2610.30722 A null search at 27 transient coordinates has a crucial timing limitation: the neutrino data end in 2022, before the 2024–2026 Einstein Probe events. 2026-10-23 23 October 2026 Neutrinos Multimessenger #### 3. Einstein Probe directions in archival IceCube data: the timing matters\n\n**Sri Devaki Meduri and Shantanu Desai — [arXiv:2610.30722v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30722v1).** Submitted **22 October, 04:43 UTC**. Archival position-based null search.\n\nAn unbinned spatial-and-energy likelihood searches the directions of **27 spectroscopically confirmed fast X-ray transients**. The largest test statistic is **4.77**, at EP240801a, with local **\\(p=0.04\\)**; this is not significant across 27 targets. The strongest reported 90% flux bound is **\\(E^2\\Phi=2.1\\times10^{-10}\\ \\mathrm{GeV\\,cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}\\)** for an \\(E^{-2}\\) spectrum toward EP241021a.\n\n**The decisive limitation is temporal:** IceTracks-DR2 covers **6 April 2008–23 May 2022**, whereas the Einstein Probe events occurred in **2024–2026**. The data therefore cannot test neutrinos emitted during those observed bursts. The result concerns earlier, time-integrated emission at those sky positions, potentially relevant to persistent hosts. It must not be presented as a prompt-burst fluence limit or a direct exclusion of choked jets in the observed transients. [Dataset and analysis](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.30722v1).\n\n##### History\n\nThe [IceCube DR2 release](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.19040) supplies a long, well-characterized track sample for source searches, but its publication in 2026 does not extend its observing period to 2026. Genuine transient tests, exemplified by [IceCube's GRB searches](https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.11410), align neutrino observations with prompt, precursor or delayed emission windows and calibrate the associated trials. Archival positional and time-coincident searches answer different questions. This distinction also blocks a numerical comparison between an average flux in \\(\\mathrm{GeV\\,cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}\\) and a burst fluence in \\(\\mathrm{GeV\\,cm^{-2}}\\) without specifying duration and emission history. A contemporaneous EP analysis remains necessary."}, {"id": "paper-2610-30754", "arxiv": "2610.30754", "title": "Natural Chlorine Enhances Antineutrino Detection in the Deep Ocean", "authors": "Ruike Cao, Iwan Morton-Blake, Donglian Xu et al.", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Instrumentation"], "label": "New paper · detector forecast and bench validation", "teaser": "Natural chlorine supplies a brighter delayed neutron-capture signal, approximately doubling the modeled TRIDENT supernova horizon; ocean-scale performance remains a forecast.", "path": "2026/10/23/index.html#paper-2610-30754", "reportDate": "23 October 2026", "searchText": "Natural Chlorine Enhances Antineutrino Detection in the Deep Ocean Ruike Cao, Iwan Morton-Blake, Donglian Xu et al. 2610.30754 Natural chlorine supplies a brighter delayed neutron-capture signal, approximately doubling the modeled TRIDENT supernova horizon; ocean-scale performance remains a forecast. 2026-10-23 23 October 2026 Neutrinos Instrumentation #### 4. Natural chlorine could extend deep-sea supernova-neutrino reach\n\n**Ruike Cao, Iwan Morton-Blake, Donglian Xu et al. — [arXiv:2610.30754v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30754v1).** Submitted **22 October, 05:22 UTC**. Detector simulation with laboratory validation.\n\nAfter inverse beta decay, \\(\\bar\\nu_e+p\\rightarrow e^++n\\), natural chlorine offers a brighter delayed neutron-capture signature: an approximately **8.6 MeV gamma cascade**, compared with hydrogen's 2.2 MeV photon. Prompt–delayed coincidences help distinguish signal from optical backgrounds.\n\nFor a **10 km³ TRIDENT configuration**, a one-second, nonoscillated 27-solar-mass supernova benchmark gives a simulated **5σ horizon increasing from roughly 50 to beyond 100 kpc**. This is model-dependent sensitivity, not demonstrated operating-detector reach.\n\nA **0.5-ton underground tank** with an AmBe source tests the capture tag: the NaCl configuration gives **3.4σ** evidence and capture time **\\(0.14\\pm0.04\\) ms**. AmBe supplies neutrons and gammas, not a detected antineutrino burst. Time-dependent ocean bioluminescence is not modeled, and source spectra, oscillations and detector performance affect the forecast. [Simulation, background treatment and tank experiment](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.30754v1).\n\n##### History\n\nSalt-assisted neutron detection has a strong precedent: [SNO's salt phase](https://arxiv.org/abs/nucl-ex/0309004) dissolved NaCl in heavy water to improve neutral-current neutron capture and separate the total active solar-neutrino flux. That measured solar flux is a different observable from a supernova detection horizon. Meanwhile, [IceCube's supernova method](https://arxiv.org/abs/1108.0171) detects a collective rise in optical-module rates despite its sparse geometry. Here the interesting combination is seawater's existing chlorine, a multi-photomultiplier optical module and a delayed coincidence tag. Comparing horizon numbers across SNO, IceCube and TRIDENT would require matched supernova spectra, integration windows, significance definitions and backgrounds. The next decisive validation is sustained operation under realistic ocean noise."}, {"id": "paper-2610-30238", "arxiv": "2610.30238", "title": "Probing imperfect axions with nuclear clocks", "authors": "Salvatore Bottaro, Andrea Caputo, Gilad Perez, Yotam Soreq, Alfredo Stanzione", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "New paper · phenomenological sensitivity", "teaser": "Residual CP violation generates a linear thorium-clock response to axions, with sensitivity controlled by EDM constraints, hadronic matching and dark-matter assumptions.", "path": "2026/10/23/index.html#paper-2610-30238", "reportDate": "23 October 2026", "searchText": "Probing imperfect axions with nuclear clocks Salvatore Bottaro, Andrea Caputo, Gilad Perez, Yotam Soreq, Alfredo Stanzione 2610.30238 Residual CP violation generates a linear thorium-clock response to axions, with sensitivity controlled by EDM constraints, hadronic matching and dark-matter assumptions. 2026-10-23 23 October 2026 Dark matter BSM #### 5. Imperfect axions give nuclear clocks a linear signal\n\n**Salvatore Bottaro, Andrea Caputo, Gilad Perez, Yotam Soreq and Alfredo Stanzione — [arXiv:2610.30238v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30238v1).** Submitted **21 October, 18:00 UTC**. Phenomenology and sensitivity projections.\n\nExplicit Peccei–Quinn breaking or additional CP violation can generate a residual angle \\(\\theta_{\\rm eff}\\). The proposed thorium-229 clock response is\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\delta\\nu}{\\nu}=K\\left[2x^*\\theta_{\\rm eff}\\frac{\\delta a}{f_a}+x_\\pi\\frac{\\delta(a^2)}{f_a^2}\\right].\n\\]\n\nHere \\(a\\) is the axion field, \\(f_a\\) its decay constant, \\(K\\sim10^4\\!\\text{–}\\!10^5\\) the nuclear enhancement and \\(x_\\pi\\simeq0.007\\) a hadronic response coefficient. The linear term oscillates at the axion Compton frequency; the quadratic term at twice that frequency.\n\nQuark chromo-electric dipoles also alter the response directly, so \\(x^*\\) is not universally \\(x_\\pi\\). Hadronic uncertainties and electric-dipole-moment constraints matter; enlarged residual angles can require cancellations. Dark-matter projections assume a local axion abundance, while the Earth-sourced static-field proposal does not. Projected \\(10^{-19}\\) clock precision is not achieved sensitivity. [Matching, benchmarks and appendices](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.30238v1).\n\n##### History\n\n[Kim and Perez](https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.12988) developed the quadratic axion-induced variation of hadronic and atomic energies, including nuclear-clock prospects. [Plakkot et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.07065) already examined CP-violating axions, oscillating constants, fifth forces and EDM constraints; linear sensitivity is therefore not an entirely new principle. [Delaunay et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.02932) developed nuclear clocks as probes of new forces. The present contribution connects these strands through the enhanced thorium hadronic response and explicit CP-violating matching. Its strongest forecasts should be compared at fixed axion mass, density, CP-violating operator and EDM assumptions. Otherwise a nominal sensitivity improvement can simply reflect changing the underlying model."}, {"id": "paper-2610-30984", "arxiv": "2610.30984", "title": "At the Highest-Frequency Frontier: Thermal Gravitational Waves from Supernovae", "authors": "Xun-Jie Xu, Yong Xu, Qiqin Yin", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Gravitational waves", "Compact objects"], "label": "New paper · theoretical benchmark", "teaser": "A degenerate-plasma calculation predicts a thermal graviton spectrum peaking near 0.15 GeV, far beyond the sensitivity of proposed detectors.", "path": "2026/10/23/index.html#paper-2610-30984", "reportDate": "23 October 2026", "searchText": "At the Highest-Frequency Frontier: Thermal Gravitational Waves from Supernovae Xun-Jie Xu, Yong Xu, Qiqin Yin 2610.30984 A degenerate-plasma calculation predicts a thermal graviton spectrum peaking near 0.15 GeV, far beyond the sensitivity of proposed detectors. 2026-10-23 23 October 2026 Gravitational waves Compact objects #### 6. Supernova thermal gravitons at an extraordinary frequency\n\n**Xun-Jie Xu, Yong Xu and Qiqin Yin — [arXiv:2610.30984v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30984v1).** Submitted **22 October, 09:32 UTC**. Theoretical emission benchmark.\n\nThe authors calculate thermal graviton production in a hot, degenerate electron–proton plasma, integrating collision terms with Pauli blocking over a benchmark supernova profile. Electron/proton photoproduction, electron bremsstrahlung and electron–positron annihilation contribute.\n\nThe modeled luminosity is **\\(1.27\\times10^{26}\\) W**, with the spectrum peaking near **0.15 GeV**, or **\\(4\\times10^{22}\\) Hz**. Photoproduction supplies about **87% of the power**, whereas bremsstrahlung supplies about **64% of the graviton number**. Relativistic kinematics and electron degeneracy make this more than a temperature rescaling of a solar calculation.\n\nThis is a plasma/profile benchmark, not a measured supernova signal or a demonstrated detector opportunity. The authors state that no proposed high-frequency detector reaches the required sensitivity. Their optimistic magnetic-conversion discussion counts photons incident on the **whole Earth**, not on an instrument. [Collision terms, spectra and detectability](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.30984v1).\n\n##### History\n\n[García-Cely and Ringwald's solar calculation](https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.18297), first posted in 2024 and revised in 2025, assembled the thermal solar spectrum including plasma effects and macroscopic hydrodynamic fluctuations. It already placed the signal far below proposed helioscope sensitivities. The supernova study extends this microscopic framework into relativistic, degenerate matter, where the dominant channel near the peak changes. These thermal gravitons occupy a radically different frequency band from coherent stellar-collapse signals sought by interferometers. A larger emitted luminosity alone does not imply greater detectability: distance, spectral distribution, conversion probability and detector response must all enter the comparison."}, {"id": "paper-2610-28734", "arxiv": "2610.28734", "title": "The (missing) gamma-ray counterparts to EP events", "authors": "Maria Edvige Ravasio et al.", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "New paper · accepted observational census", "teaser": "A systematic first-year Einstein Probe census finds gamma-ray counterparts for 23 of 72 fast X-ray transients and physically constraining nondetections for part of the remainder.", "path": "2026/10/22/index.html#paper-2610-28734", "reportDate": "22 October 2026", "searchText": "The (missing) gamma-ray counterparts to EP events Maria Edvige Ravasio et al. 2610.28734 A systematic first-year Einstein Probe census finds gamma-ray counterparts for 23 of 72 fast X-ray transients and physically constraining nondetections for part of the remainder. 2026-10-22 22 October 2026 Gamma rays Multimessenger #### The (missing) gamma-ray counterparts to EP events\n\n**Maria Edvige Ravasio, Ansh Chopra, Peter G. Jonker, Qinyu Wu, Eric Burns, Biswajit Banerjee, Gor Oganesyan, Stefano Ascenzi, Jesse T. Palmerio, Frederic Daigne, Joshua Wood, Rachel Hamburg, Andrew J. Levan, Jonathan A. Quirola-Vasquez, Javier Sanchez-Sierras, Joyce N. D. van Dalen, Agnes P. C. van Hoof, Hui Sun, Weimin Yuan, Nikhil Sarin, Antonio Martin-Carrillo, Dylan Adrien, Ce Cai, YanQiu Zhang, Samuele Ronchini and Marica Branchesi.** [arXiv:2610.28734v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28734v1), submitted **20 October, 18:00:02 UTC** and newly listed 22 October. **Accepted in Astronomy & Astrophysics.** Systematic observational census with targeted searches and upper limits.\n\nThe physical question is whether Einstein Probe's fast X-ray transients (FXTs) without catalogued gamma-ray triggers are mostly ordinary gamma-ray bursts (GRBs) seen below existing monitor thresholds, intrinsically soft/weak relativistic explosions, or a mixture containing other transient classes. The authors search all **72** first-year EP FXTs in Fermi/GBM and LAT data, with complementary coverage from SVOM/GRM and ECLAIRs, Swift/BAT and Konus-Wind. Visibility is treated explicitly: 15 of the 23 counterpart events and 29 of the 49 nominal nondetections were in Fermi's usable field of view.\n\nThe census finds **23/72 (32%)** gamma-ray counterparts, including **seven subthreshold** matches recovered by targeted analyses, and **49/72 (68%)** with none. For detected events the gamma-to-X-ray flux ratio over the gamma-ray (T_{90}) lies roughly between **1 and 100** in the stated 10–1000 keV and 0.5–4 keV bands; using the whole, usually longer X-ray duration suppresses that ratio. For visible nondetections, simultaneous **3σ** GBM limits constrain the most favorable cases to spectral peaks (E_{\\rm peak}\\lesssim30\\text{–}100\\) keV. Among the 14 nondetections with redshift, inferred isotropic-equivalent luminosity limits span approximately **(10^{48}) to (9\\times10^{51}\\) erg s(^{-1})**, depending on spectrum and event distance. No significant 0.1–1 GeV LAT signal is found for any visible FXT; the resulting gamma-to-X-ray flux-ratio limits are of order **1–10**.\n\nMapping these limits to a launch Lorentz factor is model-dependent. If the classical GRB (E_{\\rm peak})–luminosity–(\\Gamma_0) correlations extend into this population, the lower energetics imply **(\\Gamma_0\\lesssim100)** in the best-constrained cases; this is not a direct kinematic measurement. The paper's synchrotron-self-Compton calculations are likewise exploratory because magnetic field, emission radius, Lorentz factor and electron distribution are poorly constrained. Selection effects are substantial: gamma monitors have nonuniform sky exposure and energy response, and EP selects on soft X-rays. Therefore the observed 32/68 split is not an intrinsic population fraction. The study supports a continuous extension of the GRB population, but does not prove that every gamma-quiet FXT is a GRB. [Full text, sample table and Figures 2–10](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28734v1).\n\n##### History\n\nThe established baseline is that EP's wide-field soft-X-ray sensitivity uncovers bright, rapidly evolving events for which conventional hard-X-ray monitors often supply no trigger. Optical work on the same first-year sample followed **42 of 72** FXTs, found **12 optical counterparts** and identified **11 direct GRB associations**; that study established the importance of afterglows but could not determine the prompt gamma-ray properties of most events. A separate redshift-distribution comparison found no statistically significant difference between EP FXTs and long GRBs, a population-level consistency test rather than an event classifier. Individual cases such as EP240414a already showed that a gamma nondetection can accommodate several physical scenarios.\n\nThis paper changes the baseline by uniformly interrogating the keV–GeV archives and by recovering seven previously subthreshold counterparts, then turning genuine coverage with no counterpart into spectral and luminosity constraints. Earlier GRB correlations motivate the inferred low-(\\Gamma_0) interpretation, but extrapolating them to soft, low-luminosity events remains the leading uncertainty. The principal unresolved question is mixture: a continuous GRB tail, off-axis or choked jets, shock breakout and non-GRB transients can overlap in EP observables. Larger redshift-complete samples and contemporaneous broad-band coverage are needed before the gamma-quiet fraction can be assigned to physical classes."}, {"id": "paper-2610-28951", "arxiv": "2610.28951", "title": "Interpreting the gamma-ray emission from Westerlund 1: hadronic or leptonic?", "authors": "Ben Li, Pasquale Blasi, Elena Amato", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Gamma rays", "Neutrinos"], "label": "New paper · transport modeling", "teaser": "Bohm diffusion preserves the hadronic–leptonic degeneracy, while Kraichnan transport favors a hadronic fit whose gas-density requirement is the central physical uncertainty.", "path": "2026/10/22/index.html#paper-2610-28951", "reportDate": "22 October 2026", "searchText": "Interpreting the gamma-ray emission from Westerlund 1: hadronic or leptonic? Ben Li, Pasquale Blasi, Elena Amato 2610.28951 Bohm diffusion preserves the hadronic–leptonic degeneracy, while Kraichnan transport favors a hadronic fit whose gas-density requirement is the central physical uncertainty. 2026-10-22 22 October 2026 Cosmic rays Gamma rays Neutrinos #### Interpreting the gamma-ray emission from Westerlund 1: hadronic or leptonic?\n\n**Ben Li, Pasquale Blasi and Elena Amato.** [arXiv:2610.28951v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28951v1), submitted **20 October, 22:04:13 UTC** and newly listed 22 October. Preprint marked “Submitted”; 13 pages, six figures. Transport and emission modeling, not a new gamma-ray detection.\n\nThe paper asks whether the extended GeV–TeV source around the massive stellar cluster Westerlund 1 is dominated by pion decay from accelerated protons or inverse-Compton emission from relativistic electrons. Particles are injected at the cluster-wind termination shock; steady transport equations are solved for protons and primary/secondary electrons with advection, losses and either Bohm or Kraichnan diffusion. The resulting (pp), inverse-Compton and synchrotron emission is fitted jointly to Fermi-LAT and H.E.S.S. spectra and morphology, with the eROSITA X-ray nondetection constraining synchrotron radiation.\n\nFor **Bohm diffusion**, both a proton-dominated and an electron-dominated solution reproduce the multiwavelength data, so the usual hadronic/leptonic degeneracy survives. With **Kraichnan diffusion**, which the authors argue is more plausible for cluster turbulence, the leptonic spectrum falls short of the highest-energy gamma-ray points whereas the hadronic solution remains viable. In the leptonic case eROSITA restricts the immediately downstream magnetic field to **no more than a few microgauss**. In a purely hadronic interpretation with at most 10% of the wind power in accelerated protons, the bubble-averaged target density must be **(n\\gtrsim1.2\\ {\nm cm}^{-3})**. Their worked hadronic model takes (n=2\\ {\nm cm}^{-3}), proton efficiency about **0.06** and electron-to-proton injection ratio (K_{ep}\\sim10^{-3}).\n\nThat density requirement is the critical caveat. A smooth shocked wind gives only about **0.02 cm(^{-3})**, and thermal evaporation estimates nearer **0.2 cm(^{-3})** still fall short; cold entrained clumps or other gas phases must supply the effective (pp) target. Conversely, the leptonic solution needs weak post-shock fields and a diffusion prescription capable of retaining sufficiently energetic electrons. The predicted KM3NeT discovery significance of roughly **3.8σ after ten years** for the benchmark hadronic model is a forecast conditional on that density, spectrum and detector response—not a present neutrino signal. Spatially resolved gas, X-ray and future TeV data remain more discriminating than the current broadband fit alone. [Full text, Figures 2–6](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28951v1).\n\n##### History\n\n[H.E.S.S. discovered](https://arxiv.org/abs/1111.2043) HESS J1646−458 as a degree-scale source at about 20σ, with photon index (2.19\\pm0.08_{\\rm stat}\\pm0.20_{\\rm sys}) and integral flux above 0.2 TeV of ((5.2\\pm0.9)\\times10^{-11}\\ {\nm cm}^{-2}{\\rm s}^{-1}). Westerlund 1 was favored as a plausible accelerator, but source multiplicity was not excluded. A later termination-shock model by [Härer et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.10496) preferred a leptonic interpretation and found that hadrons faced confinement and energy-budget difficulties. Meanwhile, [eROSITA](https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12990) found no diffuse synchrotron counterpart and quoted a model-dependent **1σ** magnetic-field upper bound of **7 μG** for a fully leptonic scenario; hadronic, leptonic and hybrid explanations still survived.\n\nThe new work revisits that conflict with coupled proton/electron transport and explicit morphology. Its main advance is not a categorical hadronic identification but the result that the answer changes with the diffusion law: Bohm leaves both branches open, Kraichnan removes the fitted leptonic branch. Proposed neutrino tests are also not yet decisive: a recent Baikal-GVD cluster study reported three events near Westerlund 1 but found the excess compatible with an accidental post-trial fluctuation. The remaining uncertainty is dominated by transport and the unresolved multiphase gas density, so “hadronic favored under Kraichnan” is more accurate than “hadronic proven.”"}, {"id": "paper-2610-29436", "arxiv": "2610.29436", "title": "Searches for coincident Gravitational Waves and High-Energy Neutrinos from the KM3NeT and ANTARES telescopes during O3", "authors": "ANTARES Collaboration and KM3NeT Collaboration", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Multimessenger", "Gravitational waves"], "label": "New paper · collaboration null search", "teaser": "Neutrino-triggered and fully joint subthreshold O3 searches find no significant coincidence and set model-dependent joint-source rate limits.", "path": "2026/10/22/index.html#paper-2610-29436", "reportDate": "22 October 2026", "searchText": "Searches for coincident Gravitational Waves and High-Energy Neutrinos from the KM3NeT and ANTARES telescopes during O3 ANTARES Collaboration and KM3NeT Collaboration 2610.29436 Neutrino-triggered and fully joint subthreshold O3 searches find no significant coincidence and set model-dependent joint-source rate limits. 2026-10-22 22 October 2026 Neutrinos Multimessenger Gravitational waves #### Searches for coincident Gravitational Waves and High-Energy Neutrinos from the KM3NeT and ANTARES telescopes during O3\n\n**ANTARES Collaboration and KM3NeT Collaboration (O. Adriani et al.).** [arXiv:2610.29436v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29436v1), submitted **21 October, 08:59:23 UTC** and newly listed 22 October. Collaboration preprint; no journal reference listed; 41 pages, 15 figures. Null search and conditional rate constraints.\n\nThe analysis tests whether transient engines produced both gravitational waves (GWs) and high-energy neutrinos during LIGO–Virgo's third observing run. It uses two complementary directions. First, four selected ANTARES neutrino candidates trigger minimally modeled coherent GW searches with X-Pipeline in a **±500 s** window over **20–500 Hz**. Second, subthreshold compact-binary GW candidates are combined with ANTARES and six-line KM3NeT/ORCA neutrino candidates using spatial overlap and **±30 s** and **±500 s** coincidence windows.\n\nNo association passes the prespecified threshold. In the ±500 s joint search, **34 ANTARES** and **36 ORCA6** coincidences survive initial selection; the most significant inverse false-alarm rates are only **0.4 yr** and **0.07 yr**, respectively. The ±30 s search retains three and two coincidences, with maxima **0.8 yr** and **0.2 yr**. All are below the required inverse false-alarm rate of 1 yr, and inspection associates the leading cases with terrestrial GW triggers or noise-compatible neutrinos. The four neutrino-triggered searches also show no GW excess. Model-specific **90%** exclusion distances for standard-candle mergers range from **22.6–72.0 Mpc** for binary neutron stars and **49.9–157.6 Mpc** for neutron-star–black-hole systems.\n\nFor a fiducial binary-neutron-star population emitting (E_{\\rm GW}=0.1M_\\odot c^2) and isotropic-equivalent neutrino energy (E_{\\nu,\\rm iso}=10^{51}) erg with the adopted spectrum, the fully joint search gives a **90% confidence** source-rate density upper limit of **(8.8\\times10^2\\ {\nm Gpc}^{-3}{\\rm yr}^{-1})**. This is not a universal bound: it scales with assumed GW and neutrino energies, spectrum, orientation, sky exposure and source class. The triggered-search rate construction additionally assumes all four input neutrinos are astrophysical and adopts beaming factor (f_b=10), deliberately conservative choices that preclude direct comparison with rates conditioned on confirmed astrophysical events. [Full text, Tables 1–4 and Figures 4–15](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.29436v1).\n\n##### History\n\nJoint GW–neutrino searches began with high-profile events such as [GW150914](https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.05411), for which IceCube and ANTARES found no coincident high-energy neutrino. ANTARES later searched individually around public O2 and O3 GW candidates; its [O3 targeted analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.13207) found no excess around 80 GW events and set stacked, spectrum-dependent neutrino-energy bounds, including values of order (4\\times10^{53}\\) erg divided by the neutrino-emitting fraction for binary black holes. Those were GW-triggered fluence constraints, not the same population-rate observable reported here.\n\nThe new analysis adds the reverse search—neutrinos triggering a coherent burst search—and a fully joint subthreshold statistic using both ANTARES and ORCA6. This expands sensitivity to sources too weak to enter either standalone catalogue. It confirms the previous null picture rather than conflicting with it. Numerical “improvement factors” would be misleading because detector configurations, trigger populations, spectra, time windows and population assumptions differ. The open uncertainty is astrophysical: neutrino energies and beaming from mergers, collapsars and disk instabilities span orders of magnitude, while O3 ORCA was still in a six-line construction phase."}, {"id": "paper-2610-29247", "arxiv": "2610.29247", "title": "How general relativity stacks up: Testing gravity by coherently combining gravitational-wave observations", "authors": "Lachlan Passenger, Nir Guttman, Paul D. Lasky, Eric Thrane", "primary_category": "gr-qc", "tags": ["Gravitational waves", "Fundamental physics"], "label": "New paper · population-level null test", "teaser": "A coherent residual-strain stack across 28 high-SNR binary-black-hole mergers remains consistent with general relativity under a deliberately restricted deviation model.", "path": "2026/10/22/index.html#paper-2610-29247", "reportDate": "22 October 2026", "searchText": "How general relativity stacks up: Testing gravity by coherently combining gravitational-wave observations Lachlan Passenger, Nir Guttman, Paul D. Lasky, Eric Thrane 2610.29247 A coherent residual-strain stack across 28 high-SNR binary-black-hole mergers remains consistent with general relativity under a deliberately restricted deviation model. 2026-10-22 22 October 2026 Gravitational waves Fundamental physics #### How general relativity stacks up: Testing gravity by coherently combining gravitational-wave observations\n\n**Lachlan Passenger, Nir Guttman, Paul D. Lasky and Eric Thrane.** [arXiv:2610.29247v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29247v1), submitted **21 October, 05:51:12 UTC** and newly listed 22 October. Preprint; 23 pages, seven figures and one table. Population-level consistency method and null result.\n\nRather than fit one modified-gravity coefficient to each merger, the authors ask whether coherent residual strain appears near merger and varies smoothly with source-frame total mass. They select **28** binary-black-hole signals from GWTC-5 with network signal-to-noise ratio at least 15, analyze Hanford and Livingston data, and concentrate on the dominant (\\ell=|m|=2) mode in the **50–511 Hz** ringdown-sensitive band. Baseline maximum-likelihood waveforms use IMRPhenomXPHM for earlier events and SpinTaylor-based models for newer catalog entries.\n\nPossible deviations in the plus and cross polarizations are modeled as functions of time and total mass with a Gaussian process. The analysis fixes a mass coherence scale **(\\Delta M=10M_\\odot)** and regularization hyperparameters after injection studies, then combines event residuals rather than incoherent per-event (p)-values. Against **97** empirical noise catalogues, the observed population statistic is **(\\ln\\lambda_{\\rm pop}=0.46)** with **(p=0.05)**. The authors interpret this as consistent with GR. Given only 97 background realizations, the tail calibration is coarse, and 0.05 should not be promoted into evidence for a deviation.\n\nThis is a deliberately restricted null test, not a generic bound on modified gravity. It assumes deviations peak near merger, depend smoothly on total mass and primarily enter the quadrupole. A sharp resonance, higher-mode anomaly, propagation effect or parameter dependence other than mass can evade it. The proposed diagnostic—mass-independent residuals suggest waveform systematics whereas smooth mass-dependent residuals could suggest new physics—is useful but heuristic, because waveform errors can also depend on mass and spin. No coupling constant, energy scale or confidence interval on a named theory is reported. [Full text, Figures 2–7](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.29247v1).\n\n##### History\n\nCatalog-level strong-field tests have repeatedly found consistency with GR. The [GWTC-3 tests](https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.06861) combined inspiral–merger–ringdown consistency, parametrized phasing, dispersion, ringdown and residual analyses without a significant departure. The LVK [GWTC-5 tests](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.01912) extend that baseline with seven families of tests and likewise report no compelling deviation. Hierarchical population approaches have also shown that flexible treatment of event-to-event variation can prevent one outlier from dominating a combined result.\n\nThe present method changes what is combined: it reconstructs a coherent strain deviation shared smoothly across a source property rather than multiplying posteriors for a fixed theory coefficient or stacking scalar significances. That can expose a population pattern while retaining time-domain morphology. It does not overturn the catalog consensus; it supplies a new consistency projection with its own blind spots. More noise realizations, spins and mass ratios as covariates, higher modes and independent waveform families are needed before a marginal population statistic could be interpreted physically."}, {"id": "paper-2610-28681", "arxiv": "2610.28681", "title": "Testing the statistical compatibility of data sets with a common prior", "authors": "Pilar Coloma, Michele Maltoni, Thomas Schwetz", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Statistics", "Neutrinos", "Cosmology"], "label": "New paper · statistical method", "teaser": "Two generalized parameter-goodness statistics restore calibrated compatibility tests when nominally separate data sets share an auxiliary prior.", "path": "2026/10/22/index.html#paper-2610-28681", "reportDate": "22 October 2026", "searchText": "Testing the statistical compatibility of data sets with a common prior Pilar Coloma, Michele Maltoni, Thomas Schwetz 2610.28681 Two generalized parameter-goodness statistics restore calibrated compatibility tests when nominally separate data sets share an auxiliary prior. 2026-10-22 22 October 2026 Statistics Neutrinos Cosmology #### Testing the statistical compatibility of data sets with a common prior\n\n**Pilar Coloma, Michele Maltoni and Thomas Schwetz.** [arXiv:2610.28681v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28681v1), submitted **19 October, 18:00:27 UTC** and newly listed 22 October. Methodological preprint with report number IFT-UAM/CSIC-26-135; 26 pages, six figures, one table and accompanying Python code.\n\nThe Parameter Goodness-of-fit (PG) test asks whether statistically independent data sets prefer compatible values of a model's shared parameters, while avoiding dilution by bins that carry little parameter information. A common auxiliary constraint—called a prior here—breaks the independence assumption because it is reused in every fit. The paper derives two corrected statistics, (\\mathrm{PG}_\\star) and (\\mathrm{PG}_+), and their null distributions in the Gaussian approximation.\n\n(\\mathrm{PG}_+) is non-negative and assigns greater weight to tension between the common prior and the data; (\\mathrm{PG}_\\star) more closely isolates mutual data-set tension but can be negative. In general neither follows an ordinary chi-square law: the distribution is a generalized (\\chi^2) obtained from eigenvalues of covariance-matrix combinations. In the strong-prior limit both approach the standard PG statistic for the remaining unconstrained parameters. In the weak-prior limit (\\mathrm{PG}_+) approaches an ordinary chi-square with the combined relevant dimensions, whereas even a simple (\\mathrm{PG}_\\star) case becomes the difference of two chi-square variables.\n\nMonte Carlo tests with **(10^4)** neutrino-oscillation-like pseudoexperiments validate the analytic calibration in high-statistics, single-minimum examples. The code turns supplied covariance information into survival probabilities, but the demonstration is not a measurement of tension in any real experiment. The caveats are explicit: Gaussian likelihoods, interior parameters, a unique local minimum and known covariance matrices. Physical boundaries, degeneracies, multimodal oscillation likelihoods, nonlinear nuisance responses and empirically estimated priors may require dedicated simulation. [Full text and ancillary code](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28681v1).\n\n##### History\n\n[Maltoni and Schwetz introduced the PG test in 2003](https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/0304176) to diagnose whether independent experiments agree in the parameter subspace they jointly constrain. It became standard in neutrino-global-fit tension studies because a global goodness-of-fit can look excellent when many insensitive bins swamp a small incompatible subset. Cosmology developed related diagnostics for correlated probes and shared calibration information, but applying the original PG formula after duplicating one prior can overstate or understate disagreement.\n\nThe new result supplies an analytic correction for exactly that shared-prior geometry and separates two reasonable questions: data-to-data compatibility and joint data-to-prior compatibility. A timely example is atmospheric-neutrino combinations that share flux or cross-section constraints; the independent ordering analysis highlighted on 21 October reported a parameter-goodness (p=0.014), but that specific number cannot be revised without reimplementing its likelihood under the new prescription. The paper therefore changes the statistical toolbox, not any published experimental conclusion. Its practical value will depend on real analyses releasing enough covariance and likelihood information to test the Gaussian approximation."}, {"id": "paper-2610-27105", "arxiv": "2610.27105", "title": "The Electron-Recoil Response Tail and the LZ High-Energy Event Background", "authors": "Triparno Bandyopadhyay, Rajarajeswari Ramasamy, Nishitha TP", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Fundamental physics"], "label": "New paper · independent systematic reconstruction", "teaser": "Constructive response-tail counterexamples expose an unquantified LZ background extrapolation; they neither establish a larger background nor recompute the event significance.", "path": "2026/10/21/index.html#paper-2610-27105", "reportDate": "21 October 2026", "searchText": "The Electron-Recoil Response Tail and the LZ High-Energy Event Background Triparno Bandyopadhyay, Rajarajeswari Ramasamy, Nishitha TP 2610.27105 Constructive response-tail counterexamples expose an unquantified LZ background extrapolation; they neither establish a larger background nor recompute the event significance. 2026-10-21 21 October 2026 Dark matter Fundamental physics #### The Electron-Recoil Response Tail and the LZ High-Energy Event Background\n\n**Triparno Bandyopadhyay, Rajarajeswari Ramasamy, Nishitha TP.** [arXiv:2610.27105v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27105v1), submitted **19 October, 18:05:06 UTC**. Preprint; no journal reference listed. Independent reconstruction, not an LZ Collaboration analysis.\n\nThe paper asks whether LZ's published calibration summary determines the exceedingly small electron-recoil (ER) leakage probability used near its unusual high-energy event. The authors rebuild the continuum ER component from the released NEST configuration. In the event's \\(500<\\mathrm{S1c}<600\\) phd comparison region, LZ quotes **\\(0.0106\\pm0.0008\\) systematic counts from all backgrounds**. The reconstruction finds that the nominal ER term relies on a per-decay low-tail probability near **\\(1.3\\times10^{-7}\\)**, evaluated \\(4.1\\sigma\\) below the mean of the fixed-\\(N_q\\) charge response.\n\nThe central result is an identifiability test. Families of distributions are constructed to reproduce the executable model's mean, variance and standardized skewness exactly, and its 10%, 50% and 90% charge quantiles at about percent accuracy, yet their relevant tail probabilities span more than five orders of magnitude. Within that construction, a **0.14%** maximum quantile shift makes ER leakage 10% of LZ's quoted total background; **0.47%** makes ER alone equal that total; and **1.10%** yields 0.1 expected ER counts. These are mathematical counterexamples, **not** physically preferred liquid-xenon response models, confidence intervals or evidence that the background actually is larger.\n\nThe study also identifies an executable-versus-printed specification issue: taking a printed supplement parameter literally would give about 0.60 ER counts, inconsistent with the reported total, so the authors use a specific repository implementation while stating that they have not established it is the exact collaboration build. A direct response-tail measurement around \\(N_q\\simeq5\\times10^3\\) down to \\(10^{-5}\\), or shape fits with roughly \\(5.4\\times10^2\\) to \\(2.9\\times10^4\\) calibration events per fixed-\\(N_q\\) slice for the illustrated alternatives, could constrain the ambiguity. Tritium and \\(^{14}\\)C calibration cuts stop below S1c=500 phd; \\(^{212}\\)Pb reaches the event region. The paper does **not** propagate a modified ER term through LZ's two-dimensional likelihood or revise the reported 2.6σ global significance. [Full text, Tables 2, 7–8](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27105v1).\n\n##### History\n\nThe baseline is the [LZ extended-energy search](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.02823v1): one event in **2.84 tonne-years**, reconstructed as a \\(248\\pm23\\,(\\mathrm{stat})\\pm23\\,(\\mathrm{sys})\\) keV nuclear recoil, with a maximum local significance of **3.4σ** and **2.6σ global** after a look-elsewhere correction. Those numbers refer to a profile likelihood over multiple signal spectra; they are not simply a Poisson conversion of 0.0106 counts.\n\nEarlier xenon calibration work had already shown that recombination fluctuations control ER-band width and skewness. The [LUX discrimination study](https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.06304) measured ER acceptance below \\(10^{-5}\\) near S1=120 detected photons at 50% NR acceptance, but in a different detector, coordinate and energy regime; it cannot be transplanted as an LZ bound. In argon, [DEAP-3600](https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.08042) directly constrained pulse-shape leakage below \\(1.2\\times10^{-7}\\) at 90% confidence over 16–33 keVee, again using different physics. Its relevance is methodological: rare-event claims require either tail data or a model with propagated tail-shape uncertainty. The new paper exposes a missing public constraint, not a contradiction with internal LZ calibration and not proof that the candidate is an ER."}, {"id": "paper-2610-27236", "arxiv": "2610.27236", "title": "Searching for Dense Perturbers in Cold Dark Matter Simulations", "authors": "Xingyu Zhang, Hai-Bo Yu, Demao Kong, Ethan O. Nadler", "primary_category": "astro-ph.CO", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Cosmology"], "label": "New paper · simulation comparison", "teaser": "More than 5,000 simulated subhalos miss three inferred compact perturbers, but the comparison depends on extrapolated inner profiles and unsettled observational interpretations.", "path": "2026/10/21/index.html#paper-2610-27236", "reportDate": "21 October 2026", "searchText": "Searching for Dense Perturbers in Cold Dark Matter Simulations Xingyu Zhang, Hai-Bo Yu, Demao Kong, Ethan O. Nadler 2610.27236 More than 5,000 simulated subhalos miss three inferred compact perturbers, but the comparison depends on extrapolated inner profiles and unsettled observational interpretations. 2026-10-21 21 October 2026 Dark matter Cosmology #### Searching for Dense Perturbers in Cold Dark Matter Simulations\n\n**Xingyu Zhang, Hai-Bo Yu, Demao Kong, Ethan O. Nadler.** [arXiv:2610.27236v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27236v1), submitted **19 October, 20:02:50 UTC**. Preprint; no journal reference listed. Dark-matter-only simulation comparison.\n\nCan ordinary cold-dark-matter (CDM) assembly produce objects as compact as three recently inferred perturbers? The authors analyze the MW004 zoom simulation, a \\(7.2\\times10^{11}h^{-1}M_\\odot\\) Milky-Way analogue with particle mass \\(4.4\\times10^3h^{-1}M_\\odot\\), selecting **5,080** surviving subhalos above \\(10^6h^{-1}M_\\odot\\). Because the 0.16 kpc force-smoothing scale does not resolve the relevant \\(\\sim10\\) pc cores, they anchor each progenitor's NFW scale parameters at infall and fit a tidally truncated NFW profile, then extrapolate inward. A 436-subhalo LMC analogue provides a host-mass check.\n\nFor the B1938+666-\\(\\mathcal V\\) lens perturber at \\(z\\simeq0.881\\), none of the simulated profiles reaches even the conservative no-point-mass NFW reconstruction. At matched projected mass \\(M_{\\rm cyl}(<90\\,\\mathrm{pc})=(1.1\\text{–}1.2)\\times10^6M_\\odot\\), the simulations accumulate too little mass inside \\(\\sim50\\) pc; the inferred concentration is about **two orders of magnitude** above the simulation maximum at corresponding mass. For GD-1 and the proposed Fornax 6 perturber, simulated \\(10^6\\text{–}10^7M_\\odot\\) halos have \\(M(<10\\,\\mathrm{pc})\\) at least **five times** below the viable observational ranges. Gaussian fits to simulated concentration tails would place the adopted GD-1 and Fornax 6 lower edges at roughly 9σ and 11σ, but those sigma values describe a fitted simulation distribution, not observational discovery significances.\n\nLimitations are decisive: this is primarily one collisionless Milky-Way zoom, with inner structure extrapolated more than an order of magnitude below force resolution; baryons and nonstandard early formation histories are absent; and each observational perturber has substantial model dependence. A time-dependent Galactic potential may mimic the GD-1 gap/spur, Fornax 6 need not sit in a dark halo, and B1938+666-\\(\\mathcal V\\) could be a stripped composite halo plus central black hole. The result is therefore tension with the sampled CDM population, not an exclusion of CDM. [Full text, Figures 1–5 and Appendix A](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27236v1).\n\n##### History\n\nThe established CDM baseline is a broad concentration–mass distribution, with tidal stripping removing outer material while leaving cuspy central structure comparatively resilient. Earlier stellar-stream analyses inferred that GD-1 encountered compact perturbers, but [Banik et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.02662) found the overall \\(10^5\\text{–}10^9M_\\odot\\) inner-halo substructure abundance consistent with CDM and emphasized age and baryonic-perturber degeneracies. A single gap is not a census of a dark population.\n\nStrong-lensing work has sharpened the compactness problem. [Şengül and Dvorkin](https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.10635) measured an effective slope \\(\\gamma=1.96\\pm0.12\\) for the earlier B1938+666 perturber, about a 2σ CDM outlier in their mocks. [Tajalli et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.07944v2) found analogues for one B1938+666 detection near the edge of TNG50's distribution, while also showing that inferred redshift and profile choices change the conclusion. The newer million-solar-mass \\(\\mathcal V\\) object is more extreme; proposed explanations include core-collapsed self-interacting dark matter and an unusually stripped CDM halo with an intermediate-mass black hole. The present simulation search makes the CDM rarity quantitative within its sample, but the remaining uncertainty is shared between unresolved formation tails and the observational reconstructions themselves."}, {"id": "paper-2610-28182", "arxiv": "2610.28182", "title": "Hidden engines in tidal disruption events: multimessenger signatures in a lepto-hadronic framework", "authors": "D. Kantzas, D. A. Langis, I. Liodakis, A. Paggi", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "New paper · accepted modeling study", "teaser": "Source-tailored benchmark models map when TDEs could be visible to CTAO and KM3NeT; expected counts remain below one per source and are not association evidence.", "path": "2026/10/21/index.html#paper-2610-28182", "reportDate": "21 October 2026", "searchText": "Hidden engines in tidal disruption events: multimessenger signatures in a lepto-hadronic framework D. Kantzas, D. A. Langis, I. Liodakis, A. Paggi 2610.28182 Source-tailored benchmark models map when TDEs could be visible to CTAO and KM3NeT; expected counts remain below one per source and are not association evidence. 2026-10-21 21 October 2026 Neutrinos Gamma rays Multimessenger #### Hidden engines in tidal disruption events: multimessenger signatures in a lepto-hadronic framework\n\n**D. Kantzas, D. A. Langis, I. Liodakis, A. Paggi.** [arXiv:2610.28182v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28182v1), submitted **20 October, 13:21:01 UTC**. **Accepted for publication in Astronomy & Astrophysics.** Source-tailored modeling and detectability study.\n\nThe authors ask which particle distributions and radiation environments let tidal-disruption events (TDEs) produce escaping MeV–TeV photons and high-energy neutrinos. A one-zone lepto-hadronic code includes electron and proton acceleration, synchrotron and inverse-Compton radiation, \\(pp\\) and \\(p\\gamma\\) pion production, Bethe–Heitler pairs, cascades and internal absorption. Exploratory families are built separately for AT2019dsg, AT2019ehz and ASASSN-14li, then compared with COSI, Fermi-LAT, CTAO and KM3NeT/ARCA reference sensitivities.\n\nHard proton spectra (\\(p_p\\simeq1.6\\text{–}1.7\\)) or high minimum proton Lorentz factors concentrate power near photomeson energies. For AT2019dsg, representative hadronic cases predict **0.25–0.32 IceCube** and **0.30–0.38 ARCA** muon-neutrino events over the adopted active interval, versus \\(3\\text{–}4\\times10^{-6}\\) in a neutrino-quiet GeV model. AT2019ehz gives **0.05–0.09 IceCube** and **0.06–0.11 ARCA** events in its hadronic cases. ASASSN-14li reaches **0.36–0.45 IceCube** and **0.41–0.51 ARCA** events in three hadronic benchmarks, while its lepton-dominated case is below \\(10^{-3}\\). These are expectation values under selected parameters, all below one event per source; they are not detections or confidence limits.\n\nThe gamma-ray outcome depends strongly on opacity: photon-rich coronæ enhance \\(p\\gamma\\) interactions but can absorb TeV photons and reprocess energy toward X-ray/MeV bands, whereas transparent regions favor escaping GeV–TeV emission. The authors explicitly do not perform a common parameter grid or unique best fit; some reduced-\\(\\chi^2\\) values are poor, source durations and geometries are imposed, and steady one-zone spectra omit time-dependent transport. CTAO/KM3NeT “reach” means crossing a reference sensitivity in a benchmark, not a forecasted joint detection probability. [Full text, Tables 1–5 and Figures 10–13](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28182v1).\n\n##### History\n\nThe observational baseline is suggestive but not secure. [Stein et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.05340v2) reported IceCube-191001A near radio-emitting AT2019dsg about 150 days after optical peak, quoting post-trial chance probabilities of 0.5% for a coincident radio TDE and 0.2% for one at least as bright in bolometric flux. Those probabilities were selection-specific; one event did not establish TDEs as a source class. A subsequent [ANTARES search](https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.15526) found no significant clustering from AT2019dsg or AT2019fdr and set flux/fluence limits.\n\nEarlier models already showed that delayed neutrinos can emerge from X-ray targets with proton confinement, optical/UV targets, IR dust echoes or choked/outflow shocks. [Winter and Lunardini](https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.11538) modeled three candidate associations time-dependently and emphasized that several zones reproduce \\(\\mathcal O(100\\,\\mathrm{day})\\) delays. The new work adds a unified radiation–hadron code and concrete multi-instrument benchmarks for three well-observed TDEs. Its remaining uncertainty is chiefly physical degeneracy: the same sparse spectral energy distribution permits very different baryon loading, compactness, opacity and particle injection."}, {"id": "paper-2610-28633", "arxiv": "2610.28633", "title": "Constraints on Millimeter-wave Extragalactic Transient Populations from 4 Years of SPT-3G data", "authors": "S. Guns et al.", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "New paper · observational null result", "teaser": "No extragalactic event in four SPT-3G seasons constrains benchmark GRB reverse-shock fractions and Earth-pointed all-sky rates at 90% confidence.", "path": "2026/10/21/index.html#paper-2610-28633", "reportDate": "21 October 2026", "searchText": "Constraints on Millimeter-wave Extragalactic Transient Populations from 4 Years of SPT-3G data S. Guns et al. 2610.28633 No extragalactic event in four SPT-3G seasons constrains benchmark GRB reverse-shock fractions and Earth-pointed all-sky rates at 90% confidence. 2026-10-21 21 October 2026 Gamma rays Multimessenger #### Constraints on Millimeter-wave Extragalactic Transient Populations from 4 Years of SPT-3G data\n\n**S. Guns et al.** [arXiv:2610.28633v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28633v1), submitted **20 October, 17:59:56 UTC**. Preprint; 98 authors; no journal reference listed. Observational null result with population-model limits.\n\nSPT-3G searched four 2019–2022 seasons over **1,500 deg²** for hour-to-day millimetre transients, targeting GRB reverse shocks. Difference maps at 95 and 150 GHz are filtered and searched jointly; an initial simultaneous 3σ cut in both bands implies a transient floor near **24 mJy**, and positive events are compared with negative-noise events through a two-band light-curve likelihood. No candidate extragalactic transient survives. Bright stellar flares are foregrounds rather than evidence that the search was insensitive.\n\nInjected population models fold GRB rate, redshift, energy, Lorentz factor, cadence, weather-dependent noise and the actual detection pipeline. Using a Poisson construction with model-dependent thresholds, the paper reports 90% confidence upper limits. For a standard \\(10^{51}\\) erg burst with \\(\\Gamma=100\\), the fraction carrying the modeled reverse shock is **\\(f_{\\rm RS}<0.57\\)** and the corresponding Earth-pointed all-sky reverse-shock rate is **<1,226 yr\\(^{-1}\\)**. For the most detectable \\(10^{52}\\) erg, \\(\\Gamma=50\\) “dirty” model, **\\(f_{\\rm RS}<0.09\\)** and the rate is **<120 yr\\(^{-1}\\)**. Detection efficiencies for those two examples are only 1.6% and 17.3%, respectively.\n\nThese bounds condition on the synthesized light-curve family, luminosity, beaming, cosmic GRB rate and SPT selection. They do not constrain all millimetre transients, long forward shocks or reverse shocks with different microphysics. A null detection is not a statement that individual GRBs lack reverse shocks. [Full text, Table 3 and Figures 4–7](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28633v1).\n\n##### History\n\nThe first untargeted SPTpol millimetre search found one uncertain candidate in one year over 100 deg². Later SPT-3G and ACT searches found Galactic stellar flares but no firm extragalactic population. The pre-existing population forecast by [Eftekhari et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.05494v2) predicted that wide CMB surveys could find tens to hundreds of long-GRB counterparts, dominated on sub-week scales by bright reverse shocks; some SPT-3G benchmarks were near ten detections per year.\n\nThe new injection/recovery calculation improves sensitivity by nearly a factor of two relative to that simplified forecast, yet four seasons still yield zero extragalactic events. The result therefore converts a model expectation into the first direct millimetre-population constraint of this type. It is comparable in outcome to the 2025 ACT single-observation search, which found no \\(>5σ\\) extragalactic event in five years, but survey cadences and detection functions differ, so no simple exposure-scaled improvement factor is warranted."}, {"id": "paper-2610-20276", "arxiv": "2610.20276", "title": "Search for Compact Binary Coalescences with Sub-solar Mass Components in Data from the Second Part of LIGO–Virgo–KAGRA's Fourth Observing Run", "authors": "LIGO Scientific Collaboration, Virgo Collaboration and KAGRA Collaboration", "primary_category": "gr-qc", "tags": ["Gravitational waves", "Dark matter", "Multimessenger"], "label": "Catch-up/cross-list · collaboration null search", "teaser": "Two O4b outliers remain below evidence threshold; combined O4a+b sensitivities tighten model-dependent compact-dark-object bounds.", "path": "2026/10/21/index.html#paper-2610-20276", "reportDate": "21 October 2026", "searchText": "Search for Compact Binary Coalescences with Sub-solar Mass Components in Data from the Second Part of LIGO–Virgo–KAGRA's Fourth Observing Run LIGO Scientific Collaboration, Virgo Collaboration and KAGRA Collaboration 2610.20276 Two O4b outliers remain below evidence threshold; combined O4a+b sensitivities tighten model-dependent compact-dark-object bounds. 2026-10-21 21 October 2026 Gravitational waves Dark matter Multimessenger #### Catch-up/cross-list — Search for Compact Binary Coalescences with Sub-solar Mass Components in O4b\n\n**LIGO Scientific Collaboration, Virgo Collaboration and KAGRA Collaboration.** [arXiv:2610.20276v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20276v1), submitted **14 October 2026**; unchanged v1, appearing as a 21 October cross-list. Preprint; no journal reference listed at the cutoff. Collaboration search.\n\nThree matched-filter pipelines search LIGO–Virgo data from **10 April 2024 15:00 UTC to 28 January 2025 17:00 UTC** for binaries with detector-frame secondary mass \\(0.2\\text{–}1M_\\odot\\), primary mass \\(0.2\\text{–}10M_\\odot\\) and mass ratio 0.1–1. No significant signal is found. The loudest MBTA candidate, S241229bq, has pipeline-specific FAR \\(0.0043\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1}\\), nominal single-search 2.8σ significance and false-alarm probability 0.0027 over O4b, but GstLAL and PyCBC rank the same time as noise-like; 14 historical pipeline/run trials would further reduce its significance. S240422ed is likewise consistent with nonstationary noise and has lost its striking low-latency rank.\n\nCombining O4a and O4b sensitivities, low-spin binary-black-hole merger-rate upper limits span about **50–5,000 Gpc\\(^{-3}\\) yr\\(^{-1}\\)** at 90% confidence across masses; the best mass-bin limit is **47.8 Gpc\\(^{-3}\\) yr\\(^{-1}\\)** in PyCBC. A fixed broad sub-solar neutron-star population gives **249 Gpc\\(^{-3}\\) yr\\(^{-1}\\)**. These are frequentist loudest-event limits with assumed mass/spin distributions, not object-abundance bounds by themselves.\n\nFor monochromatic primordial black holes (PBHs), the mapping uses specified early- and late-binary merger prescriptions. In the conservative early-binary model, \\(f_{\\rm PBH}=\\rho_{\\rm PBH}/\\rho_{\\rm DM}\\) limits range from roughly **0.10–0.18** to **0.018–0.032** over the probed masses; late-binary assumptions only become informative enough to require \\(f_{\\rm PBH}<1\\) above about \\(0.62\\text{–}0.74M_\\odot\\). For a dissipative-dark-matter black-hole population, conditional 90% limits reach **\\(f_{\\rm DBH}=(1.0\\text{–}1.1)\\times10^{-5}\\)** at \\(M_{\\min}\\simeq0.65\\text{–}0.85M_\\odot\\), after marginalizing over mass-function slope and dynamic range. Binary formation and clustering dominate the model uncertainty. [Full text, Tables 1 and Figures 7–12](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.20276v1).\n\n##### History\n\nSub-solar black holes have no established stellar-formation channel, making a secure merger an unusually clean new-physics signature; sub-solar neutron stars are less impossible but require exotic formation or equations of state. Earlier LVK O1–O3 searches found no detections and set mass-dependent rate limits. The collaboration's [O4a search](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.12115) extended sensitivity and introduced explicit sub-solar-neutron-star injections; O4b adds 0.84 years versus 0.76 years in O4a, Virgo participation and pipeline changes, with up to 50% efficiency gains in places.\n\nThe combined O4a+b sensitive spacetime volume is about twice O4a alone, and the dissipative-dark-object fraction limit improves from \\(1.8\\text{–}2.5\\times10^{-5}\\) to \\(1.0\\text{–}1.1\\times10^{-5}\\) under the **same conditional construction**. That factor is comparable. It must not be compared directly with the numerically stronger-looking O4a highest-posterior-density contour, whose nonlocal two-dimensional definition was different. Likewise, PBH bounds from microlensing, CMB accretion and dynamical heating probe abundance without the same binary-formation uncertainty; the gravitational-wave result is most valuable as an independent merger-channel constraint, not automatically the strongest \\(f_{\\rm PBH}\\) bound."}, {"id": "paper-2610-12208", "arxiv": "2610.12208", "title": "Evidence for Normal Neutrino Ordering with Combined Atmospheric Neutrino Experiments", "authors": "Miaochen Jin, Carlos A. Argüelles, Pablo Fernández-Menéndez, Iván Martínez-Soler", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Fundamental physics"], "label": "Catch-up/cross-list · independent public-data combination", "teaser": "A correlated Super-Kamiokande, DeepCore and ORCA6 fit reports 3.3-sigma evidence for normal ordering, with visible inter-experiment tension and public-release approximations.", "path": "2026/10/21/index.html#paper-2610-12208", "reportDate": "21 October 2026", "searchText": "Evidence for Normal Neutrino Ordering with Combined Atmospheric Neutrino Experiments Miaochen Jin, Carlos A. Argüelles, Pablo Fernández-Menéndez, Iván Martínez-Soler 2610.12208 A correlated Super-Kamiokande, DeepCore and ORCA6 fit reports 3.3-sigma evidence for normal ordering, with visible inter-experiment tension and public-release approximations. 2026-10-21 21 October 2026 Neutrinos Fundamental physics #### Catch-up/cross-list — Evidence for Normal Neutrino Ordering with Combined Atmospheric Neutrino Experiments\n\n**Miaochen Jin, Carlos A. Argüelles, Pablo Fernández-Menéndez, Iván Martínez-Soler.** [arXiv:2610.12208v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12208v1), submitted **8 October, 15:58:49 UTC**; unchanged v1, cross-listed into the 21 October hep-ph listing. Preprint; no journal reference listed. Independent analysis of public releases, not a joint collaboration result.\n\nThe unknown ordering is the sign of the atmospheric mass splitting: normal ordering places \\(\\nu_3\\) above \\(\\nu_{1,2}\\), inverted ordering below. The analysis simultaneously fits Super-Kamiokande I–V (**6,511.3 live days; 484.2 kt yr**), IceCube-DeepCore and six-string KM3NeT/ORCA data across 1,580 total bins. Atmospheric-flux and neutrino–water cross-section nuisance parameters are fully correlated across detectors; detector-response nuisances remain independent. The Super-K reconstruction alone uses 930 bins and a newly generated high-statistics Monte Carlo.\n\nAssuming normal ordering and fixed \\(\\sin^2\\theta_{13}=0.022\\), the profiled one-dimensional results are\n\n\\[\n\\Delta m^2_{31}=2.44^{+0.06}_{-0.04}\\times10^{-3}\\;\\mathrm{eV}^2,\\qquad\n\\sin^2\\theta_{23}=0.52^{+0.03}_{-0.04},\\qquad\n\\delta_{CP}=1.30^{+0.24}_{-0.28}\\pi.\n\\]\n\nThe quoted precisions are 2.2% and 6.1% for the first two quantities; \\(\\theta_{23}\\) has no meaningful octant preference (\\(\\Delta\\chi^2=0.3\\)). Correlating the shared nuisances narrows the intervals by 19% and 9% relative to a naïve likelihood sum. The ordering difference is **\\(\\Delta\\chi^2=11.0\\)**. Because the hypotheses are non-nested, the authors calibrate with 1,500 NO and 4,397 IO pseudoexperiments; 2/4,397 IO trials are at least as extreme, reported as **99.95% confidence, 3.3σ**.\n\nImportant diagnostics temper the headline. The combined goodness-of-fit is \\(p=0.011\\), with ORCA6 contributing an upper-tail \\(p=0.011\\); a parameter-goodness test gives \\(p=0.014\\) (2.2σ) tension, concentrated between DeepCore and ORCA6. Finite public-MC statistics soften those goodness-of-fit discrepancies. Correlation choices, reconstructed Super-K response and fixed solar/reactor inputs matter, and a formal joint result with experiment-internal detector models could shift the significance. [Full text, Table 1 and Figures 1, 5, 8](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.12208v1).\n\n##### History\n\nAtmospheric matter effects have long offered ordering sensitivity, but individual water/ice Cherenkov data sets produced hints rather than a determination. The [ORCA6 collaboration result](https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.07015v2) used 433 kt yr with six detection units; the [IceCube 9.3-year DeepCore analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.02163) measured \\(\\Delta m^2_{32}=2.40^{+0.05}_{-0.04}\\times10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{eV}^2\\) and \\(\\sin^2\\theta_{23}=0.54^{+0.04}_{-0.03}\\) under normal ordering, but neither alone supplied this significance.\n\nGlobal fits are a different baseline because they combine reactor and accelerator information. [NuFIT 6.0](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.05380v2) found nearly equal ordering fits without Super-K's atmospheric \\(\\chi^2\\) table and only \\(\\Delta\\chi^2=6.1\\) for normal ordering after including it. The new 3.3σ claim is therefore a substantive independent atmospheric-only combination, not a final resolution: it reuses public data that the collaborations have already analyzed, and its shared-systematic model both creates the gain and supplies a principal vulnerability. Full ORCA, the IceCube Upgrade, SK-Gd and JUNO provide the necessary independent tests."}, {"id": "paper-2610-24068", "arxiv": "2610.24068", "title": "Measurement of the neutrino cross section across the TeV scale using IceCube data", "authors": "Rasmi Hajjar, Mauricio Bustamante, Sergio Palomares-Ruiz", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Fundamental physics"], "label": "New paper · public-data measurement", "teaser": "Five energy bins agree with the Standard Model, but the low-energy calibration prior materially influences the inferred cross sections.", "path": "2026/10/20/index.html#paper-2610-24068", "reportDate": "20 October 2026", "searchText": "Measurement of the neutrino cross section across the TeV scale using IceCube data Rasmi Hajjar, Mauricio Bustamante, Sergio Palomares-Ruiz 2610.24068 Five energy bins agree with the Standard Model, but the low-energy calibration prior materially influences the inferred cross sections. 2026-10-20 20 October 2026 Neutrinos Fundamental physics #### Measurement of the neutrino cross section across the TeV scale using IceCube data\n\n**Rasmi Hajjar, Mauricio Bustamante, Sergio Palomares-Ruiz.** [arXiv:2610.24068v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24068v1), submitted **16 October, 18:00:01 UTC**. Preprint; no journal reference listed. Independent public-data analysis, not an IceCube Collaboration publication.\n\nCan neutrino–nucleon interactions be measured continuously across the TeV regime? A Bayesian energy/zenith fit uses **305,735 upward-going events from 2011–2019**, including Earth propagation and marginalized flux/detector uncertainties. The reported quantity is the unweighted charged-current average \\((\\sigma_\\nu+\\sigma_{\\bar\\nu})/2\\), per free isoscalar nucleon, divided by the CSMS Standard Model prediction:\n\n| Neutrino energy [TeV] | Normalization; 68.27% HPD interval |\n|---|---|\n| 0.15–0.36 | \\(1.11^{+0.12}_{-0.13}\\) |\n| 0.36–1.17 | \\(1.22^{+0.24}_{-0.17}\\) |\n| 1.17–3.79 | \\(1.11^{+0.15}_{-0.12}\\) |\n| 3.79–12.32 | \\(0.92^{+0.15}_{-0.12}\\) |\n| 12.32–40 | \\(1.01^{+0.15}_{-0.13}\\) |\n\nThe neutrino/antineutrino ratio and inelasticity shapes are fixed to theory; neutral-current scaling follows charged-current scaling. A **16% Gaussian prior** anchors the first bin; other normalizations have uniform priors on [0,20]. Removing that anchor raises other bins by **20–30%**. The separate-species fit gives only **1.6σ** evidence for nonzero antineutrino scattering above 360 GeV—not a robust separation. [Full text, Tables 1–2 and Appendix F](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.24068v1).\n\n##### History\n\nThe physical baseline is weak deep-inelastic scattering on quarks; flux uncertainty is entangled with cross section. [IceCube's 2017 Earth-absorption measurement](https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.08119) fitted one scaling across **6.3–980 TeV**, obtaining \\(1.30^{+0.21}_{-0.19}\\) statistical and \\(^{+0.39}_{-0.43}\\) systematic relative to theory. [FASER's collider measurement](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.03186) supplies an independent production environment.\n\nThe directly checked [Super-Kamiokande analysis](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.10221v1) measures a **flux-averaged** \\(\\sigma/E_\\nu=(0.51\\pm0.11)\\times10^{-38}\\,\\mathrm{cm^2\\,GeV^{-1}}\\) over **0.5–5 TeV**, also using accelerator information below its target bin. Its flux-weighted quantity must not be equated blindly with the unweighted average above. The new contribution is energy-resolved coverage, not the first TeV cross-section measurement; different weighting, energy ranges and priors preclude a single clean improvement factor."}, {"id": "paper-2610-24412", "arxiv": "2610.24412", "title": "Models of spectral bumps of primary cosmic rays and multi-messenger tests", "authors": "Qiang Yuan, Chuan Yue, Yi-Zhong Fan", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "New paper · comparative source and transport models", "teaser": "Anisotropy favors a nearby-source contribution within the tested models; source parameters remain benchmarks, not an identification.", "path": "2026/10/20/index.html#paper-2610-24412", "reportDate": "20 October 2026", "searchText": "Models of spectral bumps of primary cosmic rays and multi-messenger tests Qiang Yuan, Chuan Yue, Yi-Zhong Fan 2610.24412 Anisotropy favors a nearby-source contribution within the tested models; source parameters remain benchmarks, not an identification. 2026-10-20 20 October 2026 Cosmic rays Gamma rays Multimessenger #### Models of spectral bumps of primary cosmic rays and multi-messenger tests\n\n**Qiang Yuan, Chuan Yue, Yi-Zhong Fan.** [arXiv:2610.24412v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24412v1), submitted **17 October, 03:25:48 UTC**. Preprint; no journal reference listed. Comparative modeling, not a source discovery.\n\nThe question is whether spectral hardening and softening reflect acceleration, transport or a local source. GALPROP calculations compare all three against primary nuclei, boron, dipole anisotropy and diffuse gamma rays. The nearby-source example uses **250 pc**, **\\(3.4\\times10^5\\) years** and an exponential rigidity cutoff of **30 TV**; these are model choices, not confidence bounds.\n\nOpposing local/background dipoles can reproduce an amplitude minimum and phase reversal near **100 TeV**. However, the source direction is adjusted to the anisotropy data. The tested globally modified spectra produce more **0.1–10 TeV** diffuse gamma emission than the local-bump model, but all underproduce the comparison data. Local magnetic geometry, extra gamma components and imperfect species fits remain unresolved. The text is internally inconsistent about which model fares worst against boron; Figure 5 and the detailed secondary-nuclei discussion are more informative than the concluding shorthand. [Full text, Figures 4–6](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.24412v1).\n\n##### History\n\nRigidity \\(R=pc/(Ze)\\), rather than energy per particle, controls magnetic transport and often acceleration ceilings. A common rigidity cutoff produces charge-ordered spectral features—the Peters-cycle idea—but does not by itself locate the accelerator.\n\n[Yue et al. (2019/2020)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.12857), whose full discussion was checked, already compared source populations and a nearby component for the roughly 10-TV softening, using anisotropy to help distinguish them. Thus the nearby-source interpretation is established literature, not a newly invented explanation. An independently checked alternative, [Malkov and Moskalenko (2021)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8506973/), instead reaccelerates pre-existing primaries **and secondaries** at a local stellar shock. These are physically different mechanisms. Updated composition and gamma-ray comparisons are useful discriminants, but neither a good spectral fit nor the tested dipole construction uniquely identifies Geminga or excludes all alternative transport geometries."}, {"id": "paper-2610-26792", "arxiv": "2610.26792", "title": "First observation of weak magnetism in neutron decay", "authors": "Miriam Camarasa-Domene, Martín González-Alonso, Martín Novoa-Brunet", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Fundamental physics"], "label": "New paper · combined experimental-data inference", "teaser": "A global fit finds the dimensionless weak-magnetism coupling g_M = 4.47 ± 0.87, with a reported 5.1-sigma rejection of zero under its assumptions.", "path": "2026/10/20/index.html#paper-2610-26792", "reportDate": "20 October 2026", "searchText": "First observation of weak magnetism in neutron decay Miriam Camarasa-Domene, Martín González-Alonso, Martín Novoa-Brunet 2610.26792 A global fit finds the dimensionless weak-magnetism coupling g_M = 4.47 ± 0.87, with a reported 5.1-sigma rejection of zero under its assumptions. 2026-10-20 20 October 2026 Fundamental physics #### First observation of weak magnetism in neutron decay\n\n**Miriam Camarasa-Domene, Martín González-Alonso, Martín Novoa-Brunet.** [arXiv:2610.26792v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.26792v1), submitted **19 October, 17:40:03 UTC**. Preprint; no journal reference listed. Combined-data inference.\n\nThe fit separates the recoil-order weak-magnetism coupling from the axial/vector ratio by combining neutron asymmetries, lifetime and superallowed nuclear-decay information. In the authors' convention, **dimensionless \\(g_M\\)** has conserved-vector-current prediction **4.71**. The profiled fit gives\n\n\\[g_M=4.47\\pm0.87,\\]\n\nwith a reported **5.1σ rejection of \\(g_M=0\\)**. Baseline inputs include bottle lifetime **878.36±0.43 s** and the updated \\(^{26m}\\)Al corrected value **\\(\\mathcal{F}t=3070.01\\pm1.90\\) s**. Experimental electron-energy windows enter the asymmetry recoil correction. Tested dataset variations give **5.0–6.1σ**.\n\nThis is not a new dedicated neutron experiment or evidence against the Standard Model. The inference assumes the stated interaction framework and nuclear/radiative corrections; it does not resolve the beam–bottle lifetime discrepancy. Simulated gains from energy-binned asymmetries are forecasts, not additional measured significance. [Full text, Tables 1–2](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.26792v1).\n\n##### History\n\nCVC links the weak vector current to the isovector electromagnetic current; magnetic moments therefore predict the recoil correction. The leading asymmetry alone is degenerate with the axial coupling, explaining why earlier precision measurements commonly inserted the CVC value rather than measuring it freely.\n\nCrucially, [Falkowski et al., JHEP 02 (2024) 091](https://arxiv.org/html/2112.07688v3), already fitted weak magnetism. Its neutron-plus-superallowed fit gives **\\(v^2C_M^+=3.6\\pm1.1\\)**, exceeding 3σ; adding mirror decays gives **3.86±0.87**, exceeding 4σ, but imports CVC information for nuclear-dependent contributions. These Wilson coefficients are not numerically identical to \\(g_M\\): the matching involves the vector normalization. The present claim is a stronger neutron-centered extraction using updated inputs and recoil averaging—not the first empirical indication of weak magnetism in any system. Remaining uncertainty includes the consistency of input datasets and nuclear corrections."}, {"id": "paper-2610-25909", "arxiv": "2610.25909", "title": "Constraints on the Cosmic Expansion Rate Using the DECam Local Volume Exploration Survey and the Fifth Gravitational Wave Transient Catalog", "authors": "Sean Patrick MacBride, Isaac McMahon, Danny Laghi, David Sánchez-Cid, Felipe Andrade-Oliveira, Marcelle Soares-Santos, Maciej Bilicki, Sarah Ferraiuolo, Surhud More, Rachel Gray, Gergely Dálya", "primary_category": "astro-ph.CO", "tags": ["Cosmology", "Multimessenger"], "label": "New paper · standard-siren inference", "teaser": "DELVE galaxy information improves a GW expansion-rate inference, but the posterior cannot resolve the Hubble tension.", "path": "2026/10/20/index.html#paper-2610-25909", "reportDate": "20 October 2026", "searchText": "Constraints on the Cosmic Expansion Rate Using the DECam Local Volume Exploration Survey and the Fifth Gravitational Wave Transient Catalog Sean Patrick MacBride, Isaac McMahon, Danny Laghi, David Sánchez-Cid, Felipe Andrade-Oliveira, Marcelle Soares-Santos, Maciej Bilicki, Sarah Ferraiuolo, Surhud More, Rachel Gray, Gergely Dálya 2610.25909 DELVE galaxy information improves a GW expansion-rate inference, but the posterior cannot resolve the Hubble tension. 2026-10-20 20 October 2026 Cosmology Multimessenger #### Constraints on the Cosmic Expansion Rate Using the DECam Local Volume Exploration Survey and the Fifth Gravitational Wave Transient Catalog\n\n**Sean Patrick MacBride, Isaac McMahon, Danny Laghi, David Sánchez-Cid, Felipe Andrade-Oliveira, Marcelle Soares-Santos, Maciej Bilicki, Sarah Ferraiuolo, Surhud More, Rachel Gray, Gergely Dálya.** [arXiv:2610.25909v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.25909v1), submitted **19 October, 07:12:32 UTC**; **submitted to A&A**, not yet a listed journal publication.\n\nA joint population/cosmology analysis combines GWTC-5 distances with DELVE DR3 candidate-host galaxies, treating incompleteness and photometric-redshift bias. With **\\(15.5<m_r<20\\)**, luminosity weighting and an evolving luminosity function:\n\n\\[H_0=67.4^{+13.0}_{-11.2}\\ \\mathrm{km\\,s^{-1}\\,Mpc^{-1}}\\]\n\nfrom dark sirens, or **\\(70.6^{+8.7}_{-7.0}\\)** in the same units with GW170817. Intervals are **16th–84th posterior percentiles**, not detection significances. The analysis uses FULLPOP-4.0 mass populations and Madau–Dickinson rate evolution. It cannot distinguish the early-/late-Universe expansion-rate determinations. Numerical cosmological-prior limits are not restated in the inspected text; robustness to those limits was not established here. [Full text, Sections 3–5](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.25909v1).\n\n##### History\n\nStandard sirens obtain luminosity distance from gravitational-wave amplitude, but require redshift information. Without a uniquely identified host, galaxy-catalog completeness and population assumptions become part of the inference—not mere bookkeeping.\n\nThe direct predecessor, [McMahon et al., DES Y6 plus GWTC-4](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.04766v2), published in MNRAS and revised **7 July 2026**, reported **\\(71.7^{+22.7}_{-18.3}\\)** from dark sirens and **\\(73.1^{+11.9}_{-8.5}\\)** with GW170817, in \\(\\mathrm{km\\,s^{-1}\\,Mpc^{-1}}\\). It restricted catalog redshifts to **0.05–0.50**. The new work changes both the GW dataset and galaxy treatment, so attributing the entire precision change to increased sky area would be unjustified. Wider coverage is not automatically more informative when photometry is poorer. These are overlapping-data analyses, not independent measurements to multiply together; the broad posterior still cannot adjudicate the Hubble tension."}, {"id": "paper-2610-25214", "arxiv": "2610.25214", "title": "Lorentz-Invariance Violation with neutrinos from gamma-ray bursts using IceCube High-Energy Starting Events 12-year dataset", "authors": "Mathieu Lamoureux, José Manuel Carmona, José Luis Cortés, Ardit Gkioni, Antoine Kouchner, Maykoll A. Reyes", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Gamma rays", "Fundamental physics"], "label": "New paper · low-significance phenomenological search", "teaser": "A 1.9-sigma time-delay preference is not a detection; electron-stability constraints challenge the conventional EFT interpretation.", "path": "2026/10/20/index.html#paper-2610-25214", "reportDate": "20 October 2026", "searchText": "Lorentz-Invariance Violation with neutrinos from gamma-ray bursts using IceCube High-Energy Starting Events 12-year dataset Mathieu Lamoureux, José Manuel Carmona, José Luis Cortés, Ardit Gkioni, Antoine Kouchner, Maykoll A. Reyes 2610.25214 A 1.9-sigma time-delay preference is not a detection; electron-stability constraints challenge the conventional EFT interpretation. 2026-10-20 20 October 2026 Neutrinos Gamma rays Fundamental physics #### Lorentz-Invariance Violation with neutrinos from gamma-ray bursts using IceCube High-Energy Starting Events 12-year dataset\n\n**Mathieu Lamoureux, José Manuel Carmona, José Luis Cortés, Ardit Gkioni, Antoine Kouchner, Maykoll A. Reyes.** [arXiv:2610.25214v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.25214v1), submitted **18 October, 12:15:06 UTC**. Preprint; no journal reference listed.\n\nA likelihood search compares **164 HESE events** with **746 redshift-measured GRBs**, including directional uncertainties, assumed energy resolutions, source-frame emission offsets uniform over **−350 to +150 s**, and superluminal-neutrino decay. GRBs receive equal weights. For linear dispersion, \\(\\eta=M_P/\\Lambda_{\\rm LIV}\\), the fit favors **\\(\\eta=21\\)**, or **\\(\\Lambda_{\\rm LIV}=5.8\\times10^{17}\\) GeV**. Scrambled-data significance is only **\\(p=0.031\\), 1.9σ one-sided**. This is neither a detection nor a lower limit on the LIV scale. Quadratic dispersion shows no preference. The assumed energy response and emission model matter; universal subluminal propagation is discussed as a distinct, nonstandard alternative. [Full text, Sections III–V](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.25214v1).\n\n##### History\n\nA propagation-delay interpretation requires both a real source association and consistent particle kinematics. Earlier GRB/neutrino correlation claims motivate the search, but reusing overlapping events is not independent confirmation.\n\nThe decisive comparison is [Bustamante et al., *Electron stability constrains neutrino time delays*, 1 July 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.01339). Subluminal neutrinos can permit \\(e^-\\to e^-\\nu\\bar\\nu\\), so removing unstable superluminal neutrinos does not eliminate all consistency constraints. For the same **linear, isotropic, flavor-universal coefficient magnitude**, that work quotes **\\(|\\eta_1|\\lesssim1.4\\times10^{-3}\\) at 95% CL** from electrons inferred to have **2.34±0.145 PeV** energy in a leptonic interpretation of the Crab's **1.12±0.09 PeV** gamma ray. The confidence convention propagates the electron-energy uncertainty one-sided; the conservative degradation parameter **\\(\\rho=10\\)** is fixed, not marginalized. Direct **15.5±1.55 TeV** H.E.S.S. electron candidates yield the much weaker **\\(|\\eta_1|\\lesssim6.0\\times10^3\\)** under that prescription.\n\nThus the strong constraint is astrophysically conditional, not a model-independent laboratory exclusion. Nevertheless, the conventional EFT interpretation faces serious tension. A universally modified, non-EFT framework changes the assumptions rather than simply improving the fit. A weak correlation alone cannot establish quantum-gravity propagation."}, {"id": "paper-2610-23393", "arxiv": "2610.23393", "title": "Exploring Cosmic Rays from Gravity-Limited Acceleration at Supermassive Black Holes", "authors": "Elena Manao, Elisa Resconi, Frank M. Rieger, Paolo Padovani, Gia Dvali", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Particle acceleration", "Multimessenger"], "label": "New paper · theoretical ceiling and correlation test", "teaser": "A formal voltage ceiling is distinct from an achievable source energy; the Auger correlation falls to a reported 1.25 sigma after trials.", "path": "2026/10/19/index.html#paper-2610-23393", "reportDate": "19 October 2026", "searchText": "Exploring Cosmic Rays from Gravity-Limited Acceleration at Supermassive Black Holes Elena Manao, Elisa Resconi, Frank M. Rieger, Paolo Padovani, Gia Dvali 2610.23393 A formal voltage ceiling is distinct from an achievable source energy; the Auger correlation falls to a reported 1.25 sigma after trials. 2026-10-19 19 October 2026 Cosmic rays Particle acceleration Multimessenger #### 1. Quiet black holes as UHECR accelerators: an energy ceiling, not a source detection\n\n**Elena Manao, Elisa Resconi, Frank M. Rieger, Paolo Padovani and Gia Dvali — “Exploring Cosmic Rays from Gravity-Limited Acceleration at Supermassive Black Holes.” [arXiv:2610.23393v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23393v1).** Submitted **16 October, 12:51:28 UTC**; **19 October listing**. Accepted for PRL, according to arXiv.\n\nA gravity-based magnetic-energy estimate gives \\(B_{\\max}\\propto M^{-1}\\), hence a formal horizon-scale voltage ceiling\n\n\\[\nE_{\\rm geom}=aZeBR_S\\sim10^{27}Za\\ {\\rm eV},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(a\\) is dimensionless spin and \\(Ze\\) the particle charge. This is **not an achievable-energy prediction**: realistic fields are much smaller, and curvature losses impose another ceiling. The quoted \\(E_{\\rm curv}\\propto M^{1/4}\\) assumes the gravity-limited field scaling and horizon-scale curvature.\n\nAn angular scan correlates **2,635 Auger events above 32 EeV** with **90 galaxy nuclei**, separated into active and quiescent classes. The strongest local excess, reported as **2.79σ**, falls to **1.25σ after trials**. The authors find no significant class preference. [Full text and supplement](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23393v1).\n\nGap survival, magnetic-flux supply and nuclear escape remain model-dependent; the formal ceiling does not establish an accelerator population or explain its flux.\n\n##### History\n\nThe physical issue is whether a source can supply enough **voltage while remaining transparent**, rather than merely confine a high-rigidity particle. [Boldt and Ghosh, “Cosmic rays from remnants of quasars?” (1999)](https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/9902342), already proposed horizon-scale acceleration in dormant nuclei, with induced voltage approximately **\\(4.4\\times10^{20}B_4M_9\\) V**, where \\(B_4=B/(10^4\\,\\mathrm G)\\) and \\(M_9=M/(10^9M_\\odot)\\). Their low-luminosity condition was intended to avoid photon losses and shorting of the accelerator.\n\nThe present work adds a gravitational ceiling and an explicit modern arrival-direction test to that established proposal. Its much larger formal voltage is not a measured improvement over earlier attainable energies: the magnetic-field assumptions differ. Radiatively inefficient accretion must still provide currents and magnetic flux without screening the gap. A nonsignificant correlation also cannot decide whether these nuclei contribute alongside other source classes. The source-power budget, composition-dependent propagation and self-consistent gap dynamics remain unresolved."}, {"id": "paper-2610-23331", "arxiv": "2610.23331", "title": "Reactor Antineutrino Detection with JUNO-TAO: Commissioning Performance and First Results", "authors": "JUNO Collaboration", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Instrumentation"], "label": "New paper · commissioning measurement", "teaser": "Reactor-on/off data demonstrate antineutrino detection; calibration resolution and spectrum precision remain different quantities.", "path": "2026/10/19/index.html#paper-2610-23331", "reportDate": "19 October 2026", "searchText": "Reactor Antineutrino Detection with JUNO-TAO: Commissioning Performance and First Results JUNO Collaboration 2610.23331 Reactor-on/off data demonstrate antineutrino detection; calibration resolution and spectrum precision remain different quantities. 2026-10-19 19 October 2026 Neutrinos Instrumentation #### 2. JUNO-TAO reports its first reactor antineutrino sample\n\n**JUNO Collaboration — “Reactor Antineutrino Detection with JUNO-TAO: Commissioning Performance and First Results.” [arXiv:2610.23331v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23331v1).** Submitted **16 October, 11:32:51 UTC**; **19 October listing**. Preprint; no journal acceptance listed.\n\nTAO operates a **2.8-ton scintillator target, 43.2 m from a reactor core**, at approximately **−50°C**. Physics data taking began **10 February 2026**. Prompt positron–delayed neutron coincidences give candidate rates of **\\(2900\\pm10\\) per day reactor-on** and **\\(419\\pm4\\) per day reactor-off**, using **31.3 and 29.4 days**, respectively. The total selection efficiency is **\\((46.6\\pm5.0)\\%\\)**.\n\nCalibration yields approximately **4,000 photoelectrons/MeV** after dark-noise/crosstalk corrections and **2.8% energy resolution for central \\(^{68}\\)Ge events**. These are source- and position-specific calibration results, not a universal positron resolution law. [Full text, Sections 4–8](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23331v1).\n\nReactor-off subtraction and a forward-folded response validate the detection chain. The spectrum is still primarily a commissioning result: statistics, fiducial-volume efficiency, nonlinear response and energy leakage limit precision. It is neither a mass-ordering measurement nor a new sterile-neutrino exclusion.\n\n##### History\n\n[TAO's 2020 conceptual design report](https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.08745) proposed a high-resolution near-reactor reference spectrum to reduce JUNO's dependence on reactor calculations. Its roughly **4,500 photoelectrons/MeV** and sub-percent resolution over much of the energy range were **design projections**, including energy-dependent effects; they cannot be compared directly with one central gamma calibration point as a simple success/failure ratio. The realized baseline and selections also differ from the original design.\n\nThe need for a measured reference is well established. [Daya Bay's full-sample result (PRL, 2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.00746), using **4.7 million IBD candidates**, measured the total IBD yield as **\\((5.84\\pm0.07)\\times10^{-43}\\) cm²/fission** and found spectral disagreement with the tested reactor models, including the familiar excess around 5 MeV. This yield includes the IBD interaction cross section; it is not simply neutrinos emitted per fission.\n\nTAO's advance is demonstrated operation of the proposed precision technology and a reactor-correlated sample. A fine-structure reference competitive in systematic control requires more exposure and calibrated response covariance; small calibration widths alone do not provide it."}, {"id": "paper-2610-23380", "arxiv": "2610.23380", "title": "Three-dimensional spatial modelling of nuclear de-excitation lines", "authors": "Tomohiko Oka, Thomas Siegert, Tristan Bouchet, Laura Eisenberger, Mika A. Gelowicz, Saurabh Mittal, Rudi Reinhardt, Manuel R. H. W. Skalka, Dimitris Tsatsis, Hiroki Yoneda, Manja Zimmerer", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Gamma rays", "Instrumentation"], "label": "New paper · spatial emission forecast", "teaser": "Three-dimensional line maps favor small Galactic-centre regions, but bright additional cosmic-ray models have a severe power-budget problem.", "path": "2026/10/19/index.html#paper-2610-23380", "reportDate": "19 October 2026", "searchText": "Three-dimensional spatial modelling of nuclear de-excitation lines Tomohiko Oka, Thomas Siegert, Tristan Bouchet, Laura Eisenberger, Mika A. Gelowicz, Saurabh Mittal, Rudi Reinhardt, Manuel R. H. W. Skalka, Dimitris Tsatsis, Hiroki Yoneda, Manja Zimmerer 2610.23380 Three-dimensional line maps favor small Galactic-centre regions, but bright additional cosmic-ray models have a severe power-budget problem. 2026-10-19 19 October 2026 Cosmic rays Gamma rays Instrumentation #### 3. Mapping MeV nuclear lines to test low-energy Galactic cosmic rays\n\n**Tomohiko Oka, Thomas Siegert, Tristan Bouchet, Laura Eisenberger, Mika A. Gelowicz, Saurabh Mittal, Rudi Reinhardt, Manuel R. H. W. Skalka, Dimitris Tsatsis, Hiroki Yoneda and Manja Zimmerer — “Three-dimensional spatial modelling of nuclear de-excitation lines.” [arXiv:2610.23380v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23380v1).** Submitted **16 October, 12:36:28 UTC**; **19 October listing**. Accepted for A&A, according to arXiv.\n\nGALPROP transport plus TALYS nuclear reactions produce sky maps of cosmic-ray-induced lines, especially **4.439 MeV carbon** and **6.129 MeV oxygen**. Additional low-energy ions can enhance the central signal: concentrated models reach approximately **\\(10^{-6}\\) photons cm⁻² s⁻¹** within **2°** of the Galactic centre. Instrument-response/background simulations are still needed to establish detectability. [Full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23380v1).\n\nThe bright models demand **\\(7\\times10^{42}\\)–\\(10^{43}\\) erg/s** of injected cosmic-ray power, versus approximately **\\(10^{41}\\) erg/s** in the conventional Galactic budget. This is a major physical difficulty. The imposed **20% allowance relative to the local baseline proton flux** is a modeling selection, **not a confidence limit**; metallicity, gas and cross sections add uncertainty.\n\n##### History\n\nLow-energy ions efficiently ionize gas but are difficult to reconstruct from local particle data because of transport and heliospheric effects. Their collisions excite nuclei, making gamma lines a complementary probe of the ion population.\n\n[Benhabiles-Mezhoud et al. (2013; 2012 preprint)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1212.1622) already connected cloud ionization to these lines. For their extended inner-Galaxy region they predicted **\\((0.1–2.0)\\times10^{-5}\\) photons cm⁻² s⁻¹** in the 4.4-MeV line, with substantial cosmic-ray and metallicity uncertainty; they found existing SPI sensitivity inadequate. That was a prediction, not a detection or bound on the ion population.\n\nThe new contribution is a three-dimensional spatial treatment and explicit comparison with the spatially varying continuum. Its small central aperture and added cosmic-ray distribution differ from the older integrated calculation, so the fluxes are not an improvement-factor comparison. Moreover, modern ionization-rate reassessments alter the normalization problem. Spatially localized acceleration and transport may be more realistic than a smooth extra population, but must be tested against both line brightness and available power."}, {"id": "paper-2610-23713", "arxiv": "2610.23713", "title": "Foreground dependence of the 20 GeV Galactic halo gamma-ray excess and its proposed connection to the LUX-ZEPLIN event", "authors": "Kevork N. Abazajian, Youyou Li, Oscar Macias", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Dark matter", "Cosmic rays"], "label": "New paper · foreground and morphology tests", "teaser": "Halo-correlated residuals persist in some fits, but model-dependent spectra and inconsistent regional normalizations weaken a dark-matter interpretation.", "path": "2026/10/19/index.html#paper-2610-23713", "reportDate": "19 October 2026", "searchText": "Foreground dependence of the 20 GeV Galactic halo gamma-ray excess and its proposed connection to the LUX-ZEPLIN event Kevork N. Abazajian, Youyou Li, Oscar Macias 2610.23713 Halo-correlated residuals persist in some fits, but model-dependent spectra and inconsistent regional normalizations weaken a dark-matter interpretation. 2026-10-19 19 October 2026 Gamma rays Dark matter Cosmic rays #### 4. The 20 GeV halo residual survives some foreground tests, but its dark-matter interpretation weakens\n\n**Kevork N. Abazajian, Youyou Li and Oscar Macias — “Foreground dependence of the 20 GeV Galactic halo gamma-ray excess and its proposed connection to the LUX-ZEPLIN event.” [arXiv:2610.23713v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23713v1).** Submitted **16 October, 16:34:04 UTC**; **19 October listing**. Preprint; no acceptance listed.\n\nThe authors reanalyze **15 years of Fermi-LAT data** with **281 diffuse configurations**, alternative bubble/Loop-I templates and finer, response-folded bins. Outside the original Fermi-bubble mask, nominal halo-template significances of **4–16σ** become approximately **1–7σ** after enlarged boundaries are excluded. These are **conditional template significances**, not a global dark-matter significance.\n\nIndependent sky regions do not support one common NFW normalization. Simulated foreground mismatches can create substantial halo-correlated emission, although they do not reproduce every observed spectral feature. A **420 GeV \\(W^+W^-\\)** benchmark fits some foreground choices and fails others; the full LZ model/likelihood is not fitted. [Full text, Sections III–VI and validation appendices](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23713v1).\n\nThis weakens attribution of the entire residual to a smooth annihilation halo without excluding a smaller dark-matter contribution.\n\n##### History\n\nFor annihilation, the intensity scales as **\\(\\langle\\sigma v\\rangle J/m_\\chi^2\\)**, with **\\(J=\\int\\rho^2ds\\)**. A template-shaped residual therefore does not determine particle physics without a density model and reliable foreground separation.\n\n[Totani's original analysis (JCAP, 2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07209) favored approximately **0.5–0.8 TeV**, **\\(b\\bar b\\)** annihilation with **\\(\\langle\\sigma v\\rangle\\sim(5–8)\\times10^{-25}\\) cm³/s**. These were conditional best fits, not exclusion limits. [Cholis, Hu and Zhong (7 October 2026 preprint)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.10695) instead modeled bubble substructure as the origin. The new paper supports a major foreground role while finding additional residuals in some fits; differing templates, fit freedoms and data selections matter.\n\n[Galactic-centre constraints](https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.10416) and [dwarf-galaxy analyses](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.25586) independently test annihilation. Comparing them requires the same channel, velocity dependence, density/J-factor treatment and dark-matter abundance; this reanalysis supplies no new universal exclusion curve or improvement factor.\n\nSeparately, [LZ's extended-recoil search](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.02823) reported **one 248 ± 23(stat) ± 23(sys) keV recoil candidate**, observed **16 June 2023**, with **2.6σ global background-only tension**. Its 2026 reporting date is not its event date. A shared mass scale or an acceptable gamma spectral fit does not establish a common origin or combine their significances."}, {"id": "paper-2610-20695", "arxiv": "2610.20695", "title": "The impact of nonlinear Landau damping on cosmic ray streaming and the role of external turbulence", "authors": "Rouven Lemmerz, Mohamad Shalaby, Ellen Zweibel, Christoph Pfrommer, Timon Thomas", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Particle acceleration"], "label": "New paper · simulations", "teaser": "Switchable Landau closures isolate damping in a relaxing cosmic-ray population; external waves and particle sampling change the outcome.", "path": "2026/10/16/index.html#paper-2610-20695", "reportDate": "16 October 2026", "searchText": "The impact of nonlinear Landau damping on cosmic ray streaming and the role of external turbulence Rouven Lemmerz, Mohamad Shalaby, Ellen Zweibel, Christoph Pfrommer, Timon Thomas 2610.20695 Switchable Landau closures isolate damping in a relaxing cosmic-ray population; external waves and particle sampling change the outcome. 2026-10-16 16 October 2026 Cosmic rays Particle acceleration #### 1. Cosmic-ray self-confinement: damping depends on the waves actually present\n\n**Rouven Lemmerz, Mohamad Shalaby, Ellen Zweibel, Christoph Pfrommer and Timon Thomas — “The impact of nonlinear Landau damping on cosmic ray streaming and the role of external turbulence.” [arXiv:2610.20695v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20695v1).** Submitted **14 October, 18:00:00 UTC**; **16 October listing**. Preprint; no journal reference listed.\n\nCan nonlinear Landau damping (NLLD) saturate the streaming instability by itself? One-dimensional fluid–particle-in-cell simulations toggle Landau closures while keeping other inputs fixed. A monoenergetic cosmic-ray shell begins at **\\(v_d=5v_A\\)**, with **\\(v_A=0.01c\\)**, **\\(\\beta_i=\\beta_e=1\\)** and **\\(n_{\\rm CR}/n_{\\rm bg}=5\\times10^{-4}\\)**.\n\nWithout external waves, damping-on/off runs agree and relax toward **\\(v_d\\simeq v_A\\)**. Seeded, counter-propagating large-scale waves make NLLD effective and can instead drive the drift toward the background frame. Reducing particle sampling from **4,080 to 204 cosmic-ray macroparticles per ion skin depth** produces spurious backward waves and an inverse cascade.\n\nThis is a controlled numerical result, not a measured Galactic diffusion coefficient. The shell distribution, reduced mass ratio, non-cascading external modes and absence of thermal-particle trapping in the closure limit generalization. [Full text, Sections III–V](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.20695v1).\n\n##### History\n\nSelf-confinement couples cosmic-ray transport to the waves the particles excite; damping then controls scattering, heating and momentum transfer. [Bai et al. (2019)](https://arxiv.org/html/1902.10219v1) established detailed MHD-PIC tests of streaming growth and pitch-angle scattering, but ideal-MHD backgrounds do not capture NLLD.\n\n[Schroer, Caprioli and Blasi (2024 preprint; PRL 2025)](https://arxiv.org/html/2409.02230v1) found NLLD, heating, an inverse cascade and a steady drift around **\\(3.7v_A\\)** in a continuously replenished hybrid-PIC setup. The new paper challenges the interpretation of inverse-cascade signatures, but it does not reproduce that experiment identically: boundaries, driving, plasma treatment and sampling differ. Its numerical-artifact explanation is a testable hypothesis about the disagreement, not proof that the earlier calculation is invalid. Realistic broad cosmic-ray spectra and cross-code comparisons remain necessary."}, {"id": "paper-2610-21495", "arxiv": "2610.21495", "title": "Identification of ultra-high-energy neutrinos in vertical air showers with surface detectors", "authors": "Jaime Alvarez-Muñiz, Ruben Conceição, Pedro J. Costa, David Dias, Mário Pimenta", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Cosmic rays", "Multimessenger", "Instrumentation"], "label": "New paper · detection-method forecast", "teaser": "An AugerPrime-like detector combination could extend transient follow-up to smaller zenith angles; photon showers remain a degeneracy.", "path": "2026/10/16/index.html#paper-2610-21495", "reportDate": "16 October 2026", "searchText": "Identification of ultra-high-energy neutrinos in vertical air showers with surface detectors Jaime Alvarez-Muñiz, Ruben Conceição, Pedro J. Costa, David Dias, Mário Pimenta 2610.21495 An AugerPrime-like detector combination could extend transient follow-up to smaller zenith angles; photon showers remain a degeneracy. 2026-10-16 16 October 2026 Neutrinos Cosmic rays Multimessenger Instrumentation #### 2. Vertical air showers could extend UHE-neutrino follow-up\n\n**Jaime Alvarez-Muñiz, Ruben Conceição, Pedro J. Costa, David Dias and Mário Pimenta — “Identification of ultra-high-energy neutrinos in vertical air showers with surface detectors.” [arXiv:2610.21495v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21495v1).** Submitted **15 October, 08:32:23 UTC**; **16 October listing**. Preprint; no acceptance listed.\n\nCORSIKA/HERWIG showers and Geant4 detector simulations test whether combined scintillator and water-Cherenkov signals distinguish electromagnetic-rich neutrino showers from protons. The quantitative benchmark is **\\(E_\\nu=10^{18}\\) eV**, **\\(\\theta=30^\\circ\\)**, with **1,500 m** station spacing.\n\nAt proton leakage **\\(\\varepsilon_{\\rm bkg}=2\\times10^{-5}\\)**, motivated by a one-hour, **3°** transient search, the detector-level electron-neutrino effective area is approximately **\\(1.6\\times10^{-2}\\) km²**; ideal electromagnetic/muon separation yields **\\(4.0\\times10^{-2}\\) km²**. These are simulated responses, not observed flux limits.\n\nImportant simplifications include fixed shower cores, dense virtual sampling at 1,000 m instead of reconstructed lateral profiles, and a lowered **1.2 VEM** station threshold. Efficiency across energy and zenith angle remains unvalidated. Photon showers resemble electron-neutrino showers, so this selection identifies neutral-particle candidates rather than uniquely identifying neutrinos. [Full text, Sections II–VI](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.21495v1).\n\n##### History\n\nEstablished Auger searches exploit old, muon-rich inclined cosmic-ray showers versus young, electromagnetic-rich neutrino showers, including Earth-skimming tau decays. The [2019 Auger point-source analysis](https://arxiv.org/html/1906.07419v2) searched **1 January 2004–31 August 2018**, finding no candidates and reporting declination-dependent limits over its UHE response range.\n\nThe new method targets a complementary field of view, conventionally called vertical here for **\\(\\theta\\leq60^\\circ\\)**. Its strongest case is short, directionally constrained follow-up, where backgrounds are reduced externally. An instantaneous single-energy effective area cannot establish an improvement over a published exposure-integrated, flavor-dependent bound. The paper's wider-angle sensitivity extrapolation assumes constant identification efficiency; it is not a demonstrated full detector response."}, {"id": "paper-2610-21110", "arxiv": "2610.21110", "title": "Neutrino production at the extreme vicinity of supermassive black hole in NGC 1068", "authors": "Zhi-Peng Ma, Kai Wang", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Gamma rays", "Multimessenger", "Particle acceleration"], "label": "New paper · conditional source inference", "teaser": "A proton-and-cascade scan favors compact emission, but its nominal sigma exclusions are not covariance-calibrated confidence limits.", "path": "2026/10/16/index.html#paper-2610-21110", "reportDate": "16 October 2026", "searchText": "Neutrino production at the extreme vicinity of supermassive black hole in NGC 1068 Zhi-Peng Ma, Kai Wang 2610.21110 A proton-and-cascade scan favors compact emission, but its nominal sigma exclusions are not covariance-calibrated confidence limits. 2026-10-16 16 October 2026 Neutrinos Gamma rays Multimessenger Particle acceleration #### 3. NGC 1068: compact-source inference with an important statistical qualification\n\n**Zhi-Peng Ma and Kai Wang — “Neutrino production at the extreme vicinity of supermassive black hole in NGC 1068.” [arXiv:2610.21110v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21110v1).** Submitted **15 October, 02:13:36 UTC**; **16 October listing**. Preprint; no journal reference listed.\n\nA steady proton model includes **\\(pp\\)** and **\\(p\\gamma\\)** production, losses and electromagnetic cascades. It samples **10,000** five-parameter configurations and five magnetic-energy ratios, adjusting proton luminosity against neutrino and Fermi-LAT spectra. The radius scan spans **\\(0–15R_s\\)**, where **\\(R_s=2GM/c^2\\)**; escape is fixed initially to **\\(10R/c\\)**.\n\nThe authors quote exclusions of **\\(R\\gtrsim6R_s\\)** at “3σ” and **\\(R\\gtrsim10R_s\\)** at “5σ.” However, their statistic treats **15 points sampled from a power-law confidence band as independent**, without the fitted-spectrum covariance. These nominal significances should not be adopted as calibrated confidence limits.\n\nAlso, **\\(f_{\\rm cov}>0.8\\)** means over 80% of sampled energy points lie inside the band—not 80% of the neutrino flux. The selected models favor **\\(R\\lesssim3R_s\\)** and stochastic acceleration, conditional on this spectral criterion and geometry. Sample percentiles are not marginalized likelihood intervals. [Methods, Tables I–II and appendices](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.21110v1).\n\n##### History\n\nThe underlying puzzle is established: a bright neutrino source with much weaker escaping gamma rays requires opacity and energy reprocessing. The [IceCube analysis used here](https://arxiv.org/html/2510.13403v2) fits an unbroken spectrum; points drawn along its band are not additional independent measurements.\n\n[Das, Murase and Zhang (2026)](https://arxiv.org/html/2604.01222v2) already favored **\\(R\\sim3–10R_s\\)** for photohadronic coronae, while allowing larger regions in some hadronuclear settings. Those are conditional physical constraints, not directly comparable Gaussian radius exclusions. The new scan changes spectral acceptance and parameter sampling; it does not supply a model-independent improvement factor.\n\nComposition is another unvaried assumption. [Esmaeili, Esmaili and Serpico (2026)](https://arxiv.org/html/2606.23812v1) explicitly propagate nuclei and nuclear/electromagnetic cascades in NGC 1068. Their framework is not identical—their illustrated electromagnetic treatment neglects synchrotron losses—but it shows why a proton-only scan does not cover all source physics. A covariance-aware likelihood and explicit near-horizon geometry are needed before interpreting the new radius thresholds as robust exclusions."}, {"id": "paper-2610-22219", "arxiv": "2610.22219", "title": "Gallium Anomaly and Solar Neutrinos", "authors": "Steven R. Elliott, R. G. Hamish Robertson", "primary_category": "nucl-ex", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Oscillations"], "label": "New paper · data synthesis", "teaser": "Solar-flux calibration agrees with radiochemical gallium rates, challenging a universal cross-section or efficiency explanation of the source anomaly.", "path": "2026/10/16/index.html#paper-2610-22219", "reportDate": "16 October 2026", "searchText": "Gallium Anomaly and Solar Neutrinos Steven R. Elliott, R. G. Hamish Robertson 2610.22219 Solar-flux calibration agrees with radiochemical gallium rates, challenging a universal cross-section or efficiency explanation of the source anomaly. 2026-10-16 16 October 2026 Neutrinos Oscillations #### 4. Solar neutrinos test explanations of the gallium anomaly\n\n**Steven R. Elliott and R. G. Hamish Robertson — “Gallium Anomaly and Solar Neutrinos.” [arXiv:2610.22219v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22219v1).** Submitted **15 October, 17:02:46 UTC**; **16 October listing**. Preprint.\n\nCan an incorrect gallium capture cross section or extraction efficiency explain the artificial-source deficit? The authors combine Borexino elastic-scattering measurements, SNO neutral-current data, the solar luminosity constraint, updated capture cross sections and neutrino mixing inputs.\n\nThey predict **\\(67.5^{+1.4}_{-1.1}\\) SNU**, versus the radiochemical solar result **\\(66.1\\pm3.1\\) SNU**; the measured/predicted ratio is **\\(0.980^{+0.050}_{-0.049}\\)**. One SNU is **\\(10^{-36}\\) captures per target atom per second**. These are rate estimates with propagated uncertainties, not sterile-neutrino exclusion contours.\n\nThe agreement challenges a universal approximately 20% response suppression. It does not resolve the artificial-source anomaly. The inference retains solar-density dependence through MSW conversion, a luminosity-constrained pp flux, oscillation inputs and correlated nuclear uncertainties. Extending solar-run efficiency validation to radioactive-source runs is an inference from similar procedures, not a direct remeasurement. [Calculation, Tables II–III and Figure 3](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22219v1).\n\n##### History\n\n[BEST](https://arxiv.org/html/2201.07364v2) measured deficient capture rates in both concentric gallium zones, without establishing the distance dependence needed to identify an oscillation pattern. The anomaly concerns neutrinos from intense chromium/argon sources; it is distinct from the historical solar-neutrino problem.\n\n[Cadeddu et al.](https://arxiv.org/html/2512.20560v3), now published in PRL, proposed that treating leptonic wave functions beyond factorization could lower the capture cross section while preserving the germanium electron-capture lifetime. The new solar comparison supplies an independent empirical consistency test of such explanations. Whether an energy-dependent correction could reconcile both datasets requires a joint calculation with the same nuclear response and covariance. Neither paper alone establishes sterile neutrinos or definitively eliminates all conventional explanations."}, {"id": "paper-2610-22118", "arxiv": "2610.22118", "title": "Are E>B regions essential for particle injection in relativistic magnetic reconnection?", "authors": "Omar French, Fan Guo, Xiaocan Li, Qile Zhang, Yi-Hsin Liu, William Daughton, Hui Li", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Particle acceleration", "Cosmic rays"], "label": "New paper · kinetic simulations", "teaser": "Trajectory work budgets distinguish injection of the whole population from the histories of the eventual highest-energy particles.", "path": "2026/10/16/index.html#paper-2610-22118", "reportDate": "16 October 2026", "searchText": "Are E>B regions essential for particle injection in relativistic magnetic reconnection? Omar French, Fan Guo, Xiaocan Li, Qile Zhang, Yi-Hsin Liu, William Daughton, Hui Li 2610.22118 Trajectory work budgets distinguish injection of the whole population from the histories of the eventual highest-energy particles. 2026-10-16 16 October 2026 Particle acceleration Cosmic rays #### 5. Reconnection injection need not pass through electric-dominated regions\n\n**Omar French, Fan Guo, Xiaocan Li, Qile Zhang, Yi-Hsin Liu, William Daughton and Hui Li — “Are E>B regions essential for particle injection in relativistic magnetic reconnection?” [arXiv:2610.22118v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22118v1).** Submitted **15 October, 15:58:42 UTC**; **16 October listing**. Submitted to MNRAS; not reported accepted.\n\nTwo-dimensional pair-plasma PIC simulations partition each tracer's electric work before **\\(\\gamma_{\\rm inj}=\\sigma/4\\)** into work inside **\\(E>B\\)** regions, post-exit work, and remaining **\\(B>E\\)** work. Here **\\(\\sigma=B_0^2/(4\\pi n_0m_ec^2)\\)** uses the total pair density; **\\(\\sigma=12–800\\)**.\n\nTable 2 gives the magnetized channel the largest contribution for **93%, 70%, 65% and 63%** of injected tracers at **\\(\\sigma=12,50,200,800\\)**, respectively. These exact table entries are preferable to rounded abstract percentages. Many particles reach injection without earlier **\\(E>B\\)** contact.\n\nThis is a trajectory-budget result with negligible guide field and no radiation losses. “Magnetized” here means a field-magnitude classification, not demonstrated adiabatic motion. Three-dimensional, radiative and electron–ion systems need separate tests. [Full analysis, Sections II–V](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22118v1).\n\n##### History\n\nThe injection question asks how thermal particles first enter the nonthermal population, before subsequent high-energy acceleration. [Sironi (2022)](https://arxiv.org/html/2203.04342v1) argued that encounters with nonideal fields were necessary, using energetic-particle histories and altered test-particle trajectories.\n\n[Gupta, Sridhar and Sironi (2025)](https://arxiv.org/html/2501.00979v1) found large pre-injection electric-dominated work fractions for particles selected to end at especially high energies. The new paper instead emphasizes the injection shares of the whole injected population and separately tracks post-injection selection. These statistics answer different questions: a channel can inject a minority yet be overrepresented near the eventual cutoff. Boundary conditions and altered-trajectory experiments also differ. The result directly challenges universal necessity of **\\(E>B\\)** contact in the tested regime; it does not show that every definition of nonideal electric work is unimportant."}, {"id": "paper-2208-01640", "arxiv": "2208.01640", "title": "Searching for Ultra-Light Axions with Twisted Cavity Resonators of Anyon Rotational Symmetry with Bulk Modes of Non-Zero Helicity", "authors": "J. F. Bourhill, E. C. I. Paterson, M. Goryachev, M. E. Tobar", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Dark matter", "BSM", "Instrumentation"], "label": "Substantive correction · v4", "teaser": "Keeping both axion frequency components changes the helicity signal to phase modulation; projected sensitivity requires a demonstrated phase-noise floor.", "path": "2026/10/15/index.html#paper-2208-01640", "reportDate": "15 October 2026", "searchText": "Searching for Ultra-Light Axions with Twisted Cavity Resonators of Anyon Rotational Symmetry with Bulk Modes of Non-Zero Helicity J. F. Bourhill, E. C. I. Paterson, M. Goryachev, M. E. Tobar 2208.01640 Keeping both axion frequency components changes the helicity signal to phase modulation; projected sensitivity requires a demonstrated phase-noise floor. 2026-10-15 15 October 2026 Dark matter BSM Instrumentation #### 1. Axion cavities: a correction to the observable, not a new exclusion\n\n**J. F. Bourhill, E. C. I. Paterson, M. Goryachev and M. E. Tobar — “Searching for Ultra-Light Axions with Twisted Cavity Resonators of Anyon Rotational Symmetry with Bulk Modes of Non-Zero Helicity.” [arXiv:2208.01640v4](https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.01640v4).** First submitted **1 August 2022**; v4 submitted **13 October 2026, 23:33 UTC**, appearing in the **15 October** listing. Original article: *Physical Review D* **108**, 052014 (2023); the authors say an erratum has been submitted.\n\nThe corrected Maxwell/sideband calculation retains both Fourier components of the real axion field. For the complex overlap \\(\\mathcal C=\\int\\mathbf E\\cdot\\mathbf B^*dV\\), its real part drives amplitude modulation; its imaginary, helicity part drives **phase modulation**. The proposed twisted cavities therefore require phase-sensitive readout.\n\nThe **\\(10^{-22}\\)–\\(10^{-14}\\) eV** target is prospective. Sensitivity curves assume a **−160 dBc/Hz residual phase-noise floor**, local axion density **0.45 GeV/cm³**, and signal-to-noise ratio **1**. These are benchmarks, not confidence limits. Room-temperature cavity tests validate resonances; they do not demonstrate the projected dark-matter reach. [Full corrected analysis, Sections III–IV](https://arxiv.org/html/2208.01640v4).\n\n##### History\n\nAxion–photon coupling converts an oscillating dark-matter field into an electromagnetic response. Established searches must calibrate both that response and its noise. For example, [ABRACADABRA-10 cm](https://arxiv.org/html/2102.06722v1) used a toroidal magnet, pickup circuit and SQUID, magnet-off vetoes, and a profiled noise model to obtain observed one-sided 95% coupling limits in the **0.41–8.27 neV** range.\n\nThat mass interval differs from this proposal's target. A projected phase-noise benchmark cannot be ranked against observed laboratory or astrophysical exclusions as an achieved improvement. The substantive correction is which quadrature carries the signal: unchanged numerical curves remain conditional on demonstrating the assumed noise in that quadrature. Oscillator locking, drift and technical phase noise remain decisive uncertainties."}, {"id": "paper-2608-01943", "arxiv": "2608.01943", "title": "Long-Delayed Afterpulse Measurement of JUNO 20-inch Photomultiplier Tubes", "authors": "Xiaojie Luo, Cailian Jiang, Haojie Dong, Yuduo Guan, Gaosong Li, Zhonghua Qin, Zhenning Qu, Junyu Shao, Mingxia Sun, Liangjian Wen, Zeyuan Yu, Boyi Zheng", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Instrumentation"], "label": "Revised-paper catch-up · v2", "teaser": "Laboratory measurements extend delayed PMT response to 20 ms; pulse-count yields and charge ratios must be distinguished.", "path": "2026/10/15/index.html#paper-2608-01943", "reportDate": "15 October 2026", "searchText": "Long-Delayed Afterpulse Measurement of JUNO 20-inch Photomultiplier Tubes Xiaojie Luo, Cailian Jiang, Haojie Dong, Yuduo Guan, Gaosong Li, Zhonghua Qin, Zhenning Qu, Junyu Shao, Mingxia Sun, Liangjian Wen, Zeyuan Yu, Boyi Zheng 2608.01943 Laboratory measurements extend delayed PMT response to 20 ms; pulse-count yields and charge ratios must be distinguished. 2026-10-15 15 October 2026 Neutrinos Instrumentation #### 2. JUNO: small delayed PMT signals persist into millisecond windows\n\n**Xiaojie Luo, Cailian Jiang, Haojie Dong, Yuduo Guan, Gaosong Li, Zhonghua Qin, Zhenning Qu, Junyu Shao, Mingxia Sun, Liangjian Wen, Zeyuan Yu and Boyi Zheng — “Long-Delayed Afterpulse Measurement of JUNO 20-inch Photomultiplier Tubes.” [arXiv:2608.01943v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.01943v2).** First submitted **3 August 2026**; v2 submitted **14 October, 06:52 UTC**; **15 October** listing. Submitted to *JINST*; no acceptance stated.\n\nLED measurements of one dynode and one microchannel-plate PMT combine direct **1.8 ms** records with sliding-window sampling to **20 ms**. After dark-noise subtraction, the integrated **2–20 ms pulse-count yields per primary photoelectron** are **\\((8.1\\pm0.4)\\times10^{-5}\\)** and **\\((6.4\\pm0.4)\\times10^{-4}\\)**, respectively. These are measured yields, not exclusion limits; quoted uncertainties include mean single-photoelectron calibration.\n\nBroad structures occur near **260 μs** in the dynode tube and **90 and 550 μs** in the MCP tube. Gain recovery can hide pulses below threshold, and the microscopic mechanism remains unresolved. Two tested tubes do not establish population-wide response. [Methods, Figures 9–11 and discussion](https://arxiv.org/html/2608.01943v2).\n\n##### History\n\nAfterpulses are correlated detector signals following a real light pulse; ordinary dark counts are largely uncorrelated. Earlier [Zhao et al., arXiv:2207.04995](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2207.04995), tested **150 MCP and 11 dynode PMTs**, principally over **0.5–20 μs**. Their average afterpulse-to-primary **charge ratios** were **5.6% and 13.2%**, respectively.\n\nThose percentages cannot be divided by the new pulse-count yields to infer an improvement: the observables, illumination and integration windows differ. The revised paper supplies later-time response information, rather than proving that afterpulsing has been eliminated. Physically, this matters because post-muon neutron-capture and veto analyses occupy similar delayed windows; translating a bench response into an in-detector energy bias still requires detector-level modeling. No oscillation-parameter shift is measured here."}, {"id": "paper-2512-10951", "arxiv": "2512.10951", "title": "Evidence of galaxy cluster rotation and intrahalo gas motions in the cosmic microwave background", "authors": "Samuel Goldstein, J. Colin Hill", "primary_category": "astro-ph.CO", "tags": ["Cosmology"], "label": "Publication-version catch-up · v2", "teaser": "A weighted Planck–SDSS stack supports a rotation-aligned temperature dipole; the signal does not uniquely measure coherent gas rotation.", "path": "2026/10/15/index.html#paper-2512-10951", "reportDate": "15 October 2026", "searchText": "Evidence of galaxy cluster rotation and intrahalo gas motions in the cosmic microwave background Samuel Goldstein, J. Colin Hill 2512.10951 A weighted Planck–SDSS stack supports a rotation-aligned temperature dipole; the signal does not uniquely measure coherent gas rotation. 2026-10-15 15 October 2026 Cosmology #### 3. Cluster gas motions in the CMB: evidence with a qualified interpretation\n\n**Samuel Goldstein and J. Colin Hill — “Evidence of galaxy cluster rotation and intrahalo gas motions in the cosmic microwave background.” [arXiv:2512.10951v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.10951v2).** First submitted **11 December 2025**; v2 submitted **13 October 2026, 22:13 UTC**; **15 October** listing. Updated to the published *Physical Review Letters* **137**, 161001 version. This is a publication-version catch-up; the headline 3.6σ result was already in v1.\n\nA weighted stack of **25** X-ray-selected clusters combines Planck temperature/polarization with SDSS galaxy velocities. Thermal-SZ removal and E-mode subtraction help isolate a rotation-aligned dipole. Random-position stacks give **3.6σ** evidence. The fitted dimensionless template amplitude is **\\(A_{\\rm rkSZ}=1.05\\pm0.32\\)** with a **68% posterior interval** and uniform prior \\( -5\\leq A\\leq5 \\). The illustrative gas template fixes **1,500 km/s at \\(0.2r_{500c}\\)**; its density profile is also fixed.\n\nA randomized-angle diagnostic gives **2.9σ**, but signal leaks into those realizations, so it is not a separately calibrated replacement significance. Mergers can contribute. Template agreement does not measure a unique gas rotation speed. [Full analysis and robustness tests](https://arxiv.org/html/2512.10951v2).\n\n##### History\n\nThe kinematic SZ effect measures electron-density-weighted line-of-sight velocity through Thomson scattering of CMB photons. Opposite velocities across a cluster can create a temperature dipole; density structure, projection and mergers complicate its interpretation.\n\n[Baxter, Sherwin and Raghunathan (2019)](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1904.04199) used Planck maps and **13 clusters**, with both parametric fits marginalizing electron-distribution parameters and a less model-dependent oriented stack, finding roughly **2σ** evidence. The newer study strengthens this observational case with different selection, weighting and map processing. This is not a simple sensitivity-improvement factor between identical experiments. The physical decomposition into coherent rotation and other intrahalo flows remains uncertain."}, {"id": "paper-2610-14144", "arxiv": "2610.14144", "title": "Planck-Scale Constraints on Quantum Gravity-Induced Neutrino Decoherence with IceCube", "authors": "R. Abbasi et al. — IceCube Collaboration", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Oscillations", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Atmospheric-neutrino data tighten energy-growing decoherence limits; the energy-independent limit is weaker than the earlier IceCube result.", "path": "2026/10/14/index.html#paper-2610-14144", "reportDate": "14 October 2026", "searchText": "Planck-Scale Constraints on Quantum Gravity-Induced Neutrino Decoherence with IceCube R. Abbasi et al. — IceCube Collaboration 2610.14144 Atmospheric-neutrino data tighten energy-growing decoherence limits; the energy-independent limit is weaker than the earlier IceCube result. 2026-10-14 14 October 2026 Neutrinos Oscillations BSM #### 1. IceCube: neutrino decoherence and the Planck scale\n\n**R. Abbasi et al., IceCube Collaboration — [arXiv:2610.14144v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14144v1).** Submitted 9 October; preprint.\n\nIceCube fits 368,071 upward-going muon-neutrino events collected over 10.7 years, spanning reconstructed energies of 0.5–100 TeV. Three-flavor propagation, matter effects, attenuation and experimental uncertainties enter the analysis. No anomalous decoherence is detected.\n\nThe parameterization is \\(\\Gamma(E)=\\Gamma_0(E/1\\,\\mathrm{TeV})^n\\). State selection drives flavor populations toward equipartition; phase perturbation damps coherence without that same relaxation. The **90% confidence upper limits**, in eV, are:\n\n| Energy exponent \\(n\\) | State selection | Phase perturbation |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| 0 | \\(2.36\\times10^{-15}\\) | \\(2.41\\times10^{-15}\\) |\n| 1 | \\(4.58\\times10^{-16}\\) | \\(4.80\\times10^{-16}\\) |\n| 2 | \\(7.26\\times10^{-19}\\) | \\(7.26\\times10^{-19}\\) |\n| 3 | \\(6.05\\times10^{-21}\\) | \\(6.37\\times10^{-21}\\) |\n\nThese test specified open-system models, not quantum gravity as a whole. The strongest leverage comes from energy-growing effects in the high-energy tail. [Full analysis, Tables 1 and 3](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.14144v1).\n\n##### History\n\nDecoherence is a loss of phase information, distinct from ordinary unitary oscillations or experimental averaging. In natural units, \\(1/\\Gamma\\) is a characteristic coherence length. Comparing experiments requires the same damping matrix, exponent, pivot energy and confidence level; a bound quoted at 1 GeV cannot be compared directly with one at 1 TeV when \\(n\\ne0\\).\n\nThe [2024 IceCube analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.00105), using 305,735 events and the same two model classes, set state-selection limits at 1 TeV of \\(1.17\\times10^{-15}\\), \\(6.67\\times10^{-16}\\), \\(9.48\\times10^{-18}\\) and \\(1.77\\times10^{-19}\\) eV for \\(n=0,1,2,3\\). Thus the new limits improve by approximately **1.5, 13 and 29** for \\(n=1,2,3\\), whereas **the energy-independent limit is about twice as weak**. This comparison is calculated from the two tables; “stronger limits” should not be applied indiscriminately.\n\nFor the particular ansatz \\(\\Gamma\\sim E^3/M_{\\rm Pl}^2\\), the new result reaches the order-one Planck-suppressed benchmark. That conclusion depends on the assumed coupling and flavor structure."}, {"id": "paper-2610-18671", "arxiv": "2610.18671", "title": "Multimessenger Constraints on Nuclear Acceleration in Neutrino-Emitting Seyfert Coronae", "authors": "Chengchao Yuan", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Cosmic rays", "Gamma rays", "Multimessenger", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Acceleration-dependent nuclear spectra change the composition allowed by Seyfert neutrino and gamma-ray emission; the selections are conditional on a coronal model.", "path": "2026/10/14/index.html#paper-2610-18671", "reportDate": "14 October 2026", "searchText": "Multimessenger Constraints on Nuclear Acceleration in Neutrino-Emitting Seyfert Coronae Chengchao Yuan 2610.18671 Acceleration-dependent nuclear spectra change the composition allowed by Seyfert neutrino and gamma-ray emission; the selections are conditional on a coronal model. 2026-10-14 14 October 2026 Neutrinos Cosmic rays Gamma rays Multimessenger Compact objects #### 2. Nuclear acceleration in neutrino-emitting Seyfert coronae\n\n**Chengchao Yuan — [arXiv:2610.18671v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18671v1).** Submitted 13 October; preprint.\n\nStochastic-acceleration spectra are combined with nuclear transport and electromagnetic cascades for NGC 1068 and NGC 7469. The key parameter is \\(f_{\\rm nuc}=L_{A>1}/L_{\\rm CR}\\): the fraction of injected cosmic-ray **power** in nuclei, including helium. It is not gas metallicity or the escaping heavy-nucleus fraction.\n\nFor NGC 1068, the benchmark scan over coronal radii of 10–100 gravitational radii permits helium-dominated, solar-mixture injection approaching \\(f_{\\rm nuc}=1\\) over a restricted radius interval; CNO- and iron-rich mixtures reach roughly 0.8. These are **model-dependent flux selections**, not confidence-level composition limits from a global likelihood.\n\nThe spectra inherit an acceleration cutoff: fragmentation largely preserves energy per nucleon and cannot repair an insufficient cutoff. The calculation uses power-normalized effective injection and fixed backgrounds, without secondary reacceleration or full acceleration–cascade feedback. [Methods and parameter scan](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.18671v1).\n\n##### History\n\nThe relevant earlier comparison is [Esmaeili, Esmaili and Serpico, arXiv:2606.23812](https://arxiv.org/html/2606.23812v1). That study prescribed injection spectra at comparable energy per nucleon, normalized the resulting neutrinos to NGC 1068, and followed nuclear and electromagnetic cascades. In its compact benchmark, rapid fragmentation and neutron production reduce Bethe–Heitler losses: iron injection lowers the required cosmic-ray power by about 15–20% and bolometric cascade luminosity by about 35% relative to protons. At a larger source radius, surviving charged fragments instead enhance pair losses and gamma-ray constraints. Its COMPTEL reanalysis also supplied **95% confidence photon-flux limits**, a different statistical object from a selected composition range.\n\nThe studies therefore answer related but different questions: propagation at prescribed injection versus spectra restricted by an acceleration model. Apparent differences in the viability of heavy injection must be checked at matched compactness, photon field, spectrum and normalization. The new calculation advances that comparison; it does not establish a universal upper bound on source metallicity."}, {"id": "paper-2610-16296", "arxiv": "2610.16296", "title": "Direct Observational Constraint on Upstream Magnetic Field Amplification Reveals the Particle Acceleration Limit in a Supernova Remnant", "authors": "Yuya Kawabata, Takaaki Tanaka, Hiromasa Suzuki, Tsuyoshi Inoue", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Gamma rays", "X rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Upstream X-ray hotspots in RX J1713.7−3946 constrain diffusion and magnetic amplification under a hadronic-secondary interpretation.", "path": "2026/10/14/index.html#paper-2610-16296", "reportDate": "14 October 2026", "searchText": "Direct Observational Constraint on Upstream Magnetic Field Amplification Reveals the Particle Acceleration Limit in a Supernova Remnant Yuya Kawabata, Takaaki Tanaka, Hiromasa Suzuki, Tsuyoshi Inoue 2610.16296 Upstream X-ray hotspots in RX J1713.7−3946 constrain diffusion and magnetic amplification under a hadronic-secondary interpretation. 2026-10-14 14 October 2026 Cosmic rays Gamma rays X rays #### 3. RX J1713.7−3946: the upstream field and the current acceleration ceiling\n\n**Yuya Kawabata, Takaaki Tanaka, Hiromasa Suzuki and Tsuyoshi Inoue — [arXiv:2610.16296v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16296v1).** Submitted 12 October; published in [Communications Physics](https://doi.org/10.1038/s42005-026-02906-y).\n\nChandra identifies 195 significant compact X-ray hotspots, including a population extending approximately one parsec beyond the forward shock. Interpreting those upstream hotspots as synchrotron radiation from hadronically produced secondary electrons in dense, magnetized clumps connects their spatial extent to proton diffusion.\n\nUnder the benchmark assumptions,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{B_{\\rm up}}{\\eta}\\simeq14\\,\\mu{\\rm G}\n\\left(\\frac{\\epsilon_X}{1\\,\\mathrm{keV}}\\right)^{1/2}\n\\left(\\frac{B_{\\rm clump}}{1\\,\\mathrm{mG}}\\right)^{-1/2}\n\\left(\\frac{v_{\\rm sh}}{3000\\,\\mathrm{km\\,s^{-1}}}\\right)^{-1}\n\\left(\\frac{\\ell_{\\rm diff}}{1\\,\\mathrm{pc}}\\right)^{-1}.\n\\]\n\nHere \\(\\eta\\ge1\\) measures diffusion relative to Bohm diffusion. The observation constrains **\\(B_{\\rm up}/\\eta\\)**, not an assumption-free magnetic field. Inserting the inferred diffusion coefficient into an age-limited acceleration estimate gives a present ceiling near **100 TeV**, below a PeV. The secondary-electron interpretation remains conditional; primary-electron emission is discussed as an alternative. [Equations 6–7 and discussion](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.16296v1).\n\n##### History\n\n[Uchiyama et al. (2007)](https://doi.org/10.1038/nature06210) interpreted rapid X-ray hotspot variability as evidence for locally amplified, milligauss-scale fields. Those compact downstream environments should not be equated with the field ahead of the shock. Efficient diffusive shock acceleration requires particles to scatter and return across the shock; a large field in isolated emitting clumps alone does not establish the upstream confinement required for PeV acceleration.\n\n[H.E.S.S.'s deeper spatial study](https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.08671) already found gamma-ray emission extending beyond the X-ray shell, consistent with a precursor or escaping particles. The new X-ray population offers a complementary spatial diagnostic of that region. A current acceleration ceiling also does not exclude a higher maximum energy earlier in the remnant's evolution, when its shock was faster. This is a constraint on a particular remnant and epoch, not a general exclusion of supernova remnants as Galactic cosmic-ray sources."}, {"id": "paper-2610-17207", "arxiv": "2610.17207", "title": "Implications for new physics solutions to the LSND and MiniBooNE anomalies after MicroBooNE results", "authors": "Waleed Abdallah, Raj Gandhi, Samiran Roy", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Oscillations", "BSM", "Particle physics"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A pseudoscalar benchmark illustrates an incoherent-scattering explanation; detector efficiencies, fit assumptions, and newer EDM constraints remain important.", "path": "2026/10/14/index.html#paper-2610-17207", "reportDate": "14 October 2026", "searchText": "Implications for new physics solutions to the LSND and MiniBooNE anomalies after MicroBooNE results Waleed Abdallah, Raj Gandhi, Samiran Roy 2610.17207 A pseudoscalar benchmark illustrates an incoherent-scattering explanation; detector efficiencies, fit assumptions, and newer EDM constraints remain important. 2026-10-14 14 October 2026 Neutrinos Oscillations BSM Particle physics #### 4. LSND and MiniBooNE after MicroBooNE: an incoherent-scattering route\n\n**Waleed Abdallah, Raj Gandhi and Samiran Roy — [arXiv:2610.17207v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17207v1).** Submitted 12 October; preprint.\n\nThe paper organizes MicroBooNE's electron, photon and electron–positron searches by which new-physics final states they test. It illustrates a light pseudoscalar mediator producing predominantly incoherent scattering, with a **17 MeV mediator and 114 MeV heavy neutral lepton** benchmark.\n\nThe fits use assumed uncorrelated fractional systematic errors of 13% for MiniBooNE and 15% for LSND. They should therefore be read as a phenomenological viability demonstration, not an experimental joint-likelihood discovery. Predicted MicroBooNE/SBND yields also assume a 12% pair-detection efficiency.\n\nA useful test is an explicit search for low-invariant-mass pairs accompanied by weak hadronic activity. The discussion of Dirac neutrinos concerns model-building and radiative-mass consistency; it is not evidence that neutrinos are Dirac particles. [Fit prescription, Table 1 and constraints](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.17207v1).\n\n##### History\n\nAn electron-like Cherenkov excess need not uniquely identify an electron-neutrino appearance signal. [MicroBooNE's two-beam oscillation analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.07159) excludes the tested single-light-sterile interpretation of the LSND/MiniBooNE anomalies at 95% confidence. Its [dark-sector pair search](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.10900) separately tests neutrino-induced production followed by visible pairs and missing energy. Limits on a coherent vector-mediated topology cannot automatically be transferred to incoherent pseudoscalar scattering with different efficiencies.\n\n**Independent constraint check:** the new paper uses the ACME electron-EDM bound \\(1.1\\times10^{-29}\\,e\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\). [Roussy et al., Science (2023)](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.adg4084) already reported \\(|d_e|<4.1\\times10^{-30}\\,e\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\) at 90% confidence using HfF\\(^+\\). Applying that bound to the paper's quoted \\(d_e\\simeq2.3\\times10^{-26}\\theta_{\\rm CP}\\,e\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\), at its specified mixing benchmark, gives **\\(|\\theta_{\\rm CP}|\\lesssim1.8\\times10^{-4}\\)** rather than approximately \\(5\\times10^{-4}\\). This is a conditional rescaling, assuming the same dominant contribution and no cancellations or additional molecular CP-odd effects; it does not exclude the CP-conserving limit."}, {"id": "paper-2610-14781", "arxiv": "2610.14781", "title": "TDCOSMO 2026: A 3% measurement of the Hubble constant from strong lensing and stellar kinematics", "authors": "TDCOSMO Collaboration — S. Birrer et al.", "primary_category": "astro-ph.CO", "tags": ["Cosmology"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Resolved stellar kinematics sharpen time-delay cosmography while allowing mass-sheet freedom; the baseline precision is 3.3%.", "path": "2026/10/14/index.html#paper-2610-14781", "reportDate": "14 October 2026", "searchText": "TDCOSMO 2026: A 3% measurement of the Hubble constant from strong lensing and stellar kinematics TDCOSMO Collaboration — S. Birrer et al. 2610.14781 Resolved stellar kinematics sharpen time-delay cosmography while allowing mass-sheet freedom; the baseline precision is 3.3%. 2026-10-14 14 October 2026 Cosmology #### 5. TDCOSMO 2026: better kinematics sharpen the Hubble constant\n\n**TDCOSMO Collaboration, S. Birrer et al. — [arXiv:2610.14781v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14781v1).** Submitted 10 October; preprint submitted to Physical Review D.\n\nEleven time-delay lenses, including nine with spatially resolved stellar kinematics, give\n\n\\[\nH_0=71.1^{+2.4}_{-2.3}\\ \\mathrm{km\\,s^{-1}\\,Mpc^{-1}}\n\\]\n\nfor the baseline flat-\\(\\Lambda\\)CDM analysis with the DES-Dovekie supernova constraint on \\(\\Omega_m\\). This is **3.3% precision**; adding external non-time-delay lens samples improves it to approximately 3.0%.\n\nThe blinded analysis retains mass-sheet freedom, uses axisymmetric stellar dynamics and updates line-of-sight modeling. Six systems now have resolved JWST/NIRSpec kinematics. The advance is a stronger internal dynamical calibration of lens masses. The resulting uncertainty still allows considerable overlap with competing cosmological inferences; this measurement alone does not settle the Hubble tension. [Full analysis](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.14781v1).\n\n##### History\n\nTime delays constrain a distance scale only after the lens's gravitational potential is modeled. The mass-sheet transformation can preserve image observables while rescaling inferred time delays and hence \\(H_0\\). Stellar kinematics supply information that helps break this degeneracy, at the price of modeling orbital anisotropy and galaxy structure.\n\n[TDCOSMO IV (2020)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.02941) demonstrated the precision cost of allowing that freedom: the seven time-delay lenses yielded \\(74.5^{+5.6}_{-6.1}\\) in flat \\(\\Lambda\\)CDM. The eight-lens [TDCOSMO 2025 analysis, v4, Table 6](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.03023v4) gave \\(71.6^{+3.9}_{-3.3}\\) when combined with Pantheon+; adding SLACS and SL2S gave \\(74.3^{+3.1}_{-3.7}\\).\n\nThese are not independent measurements to combine naively: lens samples overlap, and the supernova prior and kinematic treatment change. The strongest reading of the new work is improved precision with explicitly retained lens-model freedom. Comparing the paper's rounded “3%” title to a different historical dataset combination would obscure the actual gain."}, {"id": "paper-2610-14058", "arxiv": "2610.14058", "title": "An All-Sky Search for Moving Gamma-Ray Sources with Fermi-LAT", "authors": "Dou Lai, James Unwin", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A ten-year photon-track search finds no significant excess and calibrates constraints on nearby steady moving gamma-ray sources.", "path": "2026/10/14/index.html#paper-2610-14058", "reportDate": "14 October 2026", "searchText": "An All-Sky Search for Moving Gamma-Ray Sources with Fermi-LAT Dou Lai, James Unwin 2610.14058 A ten-year photon-track search finds no significant excess and calibrates constraints on nearby steady moving gamma-ray sources. 2026-10-14 14 October 2026 Gamma rays Dark matter BSM #### 6. Fermi-LAT searches for nearby moving gamma-ray sources\n\n**Dou Lai and James Unwin — [arXiv:2610.14058v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14058v1).** Submitted 6 October; preprint announced in the 14 October listing.\n\nA ten-year, 1–100 GeV search links annual photon clusters into tracks moving at 0.25–0.8 degrees per year. For a transverse speed of 200 km/s, this corresponds to distances of roughly 3,000–9,700 AU.\n\nThere are 577 candidate tracks versus \\(533\\pm27\\) in time-scrambled skies. The empirical \\(p\\)-value is approximately **0.066**, so this is not a detection. The **90% upper limit is 80.8 recovered signal tracks**. After efficiency calibration, the strongest common-flux example permits fewer than 123 steady sources, assuming an \\(E^{-2}\\) spectrum, flux \\(6.5\\times10^{-10}\\,\\mathrm{ph\\,cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}\\), and speed 0.25 degrees/year.\n\nRecovered-track limits, incident source-population limits and primordial-black-hole abundance limits are different quantities. The latter additionally require emission and population assumptions. [Search and injection calibration](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.14058v1).\n\n##### History\n\n[Koushiappas (2006)](https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0606208) proposed gamma-ray proper motion as a discriminator for nearby dark-matter microhalos. The [Fermi-LAT Collaboration's 2018 primordial-black-hole search](https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.00100) already implemented proper-motion detection for 318 unassociated high-latitude catalog sources, obtaining a **99% confidence** local evaporation-rate limit of \\(7.2\\times10^3\\,\\mathrm{pc^{-3}\\,yr^{-1}}\\).\n\nThe new contribution is a photon-level all-sky search for persistent tracks, rather than the first use of gamma-ray proper motion. Its 90% count limit cannot be ranked directly against the earlier 99% evaporation-rate limit: the selections, time evolution, spectral models and measured quantities differ. An evaporating black hole can brighten and harden, whereas a steady-source calibration imposes a different signal template. These distinctions matter before translating the result into a dark-matter constraint."}, {"id": "paper-2610-17217", "arxiv": "2610.17217", "title": "MeV dark matter decay forecasts for the COSI mission", "authors": "Robin Anthony-Petersen et al.", "primary_category": "astro-ph.CO", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Dark matter", "BSM", "Cosmology"], "label": "★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Full-response simulations forecast two-year sensitivity to MeV dark-matter decay; instrumental backgrounds and halo assumptions remain central.", "path": "2026/10/14/index.html#paper-2610-17217", "reportDate": "14 October 2026", "searchText": "MeV dark matter decay forecasts for the COSI mission Robin Anthony-Petersen et al. 2610.17217 Full-response simulations forecast two-year sensitivity to MeV dark-matter decay; instrumental backgrounds and halo assumptions remain central. 2026-10-14 14 October 2026 Gamma rays Dark matter BSM Cosmology #### 7. COSI: a forecast for MeV dark-matter decay\n\n**Robin Anthony-Petersen et al. — [arXiv:2610.17217v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17217v1).** Submitted 12 October; preprint, **forecast**.\n\nAll-sky detector-response simulations estimate COSI's two-year sensitivity to \\(\\chi\\rightarrow\\gamma\\gamma\\) for masses of 0.4–10 MeV. With an assumed Galactic NFW dark-matter halo, the energy-spectrum analyses project **95% one-sided lower limits on the partial lifetime reaching order \\(10^{30}\\) seconds**, principally below a few MeV.\n\nThree-month simulations are scaled to two years using background-limited exposure scaling. Background normalization, instrumental lines and the spectral fitting window affect the result. A separate four-dimensional Compton-data analysis fixes the background and supplies an optimistic performance ceiling.\n\nThe projected lifetime is for the specified photon channel, and is not automatically the total lifetime if other decays exist. No observed exclusion or dark-matter signal is reported. [Model, backgrounds and results](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.17217v1).\n\n##### History\n\nFor two-photon decay, each photon has energy \\(m_\\chi/2\\), and the flux scales with the line-of-sight integral of density divided by \\(m_\\chi\\tau_{\\gamma\\gamma}\\). This differs from annihilation's density-squared dependence. If the particle supplies only a fraction of dark matter, the inferred lifetime limit rescales with that fraction.\n\n[Calore et al.'s 16-year INTEGRAL/SPI analysis](https://arxiv.org/html/2209.06299v5) constrains two-photon decays across approximately 60 keV–16 MeV at 95% confidence, explicitly fitting a dark-matter spatial component. It shows why foreground correlations can strengthen or weaken limits relative to using residuals alone. The corrected version is the relevant reference. [Earlier COSI forecasts](https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.09310) used point-source sensitivities and restricted sky regions; the new work forward-models an extended halo over the full sky.\n\nThe claimed improvement is therefore an instrumental-analysis forecast. A strictly quantitative comparison should first align halo normalization, sky region, photon yield, confidence convention and background treatment. Spectral lines remain a particularly clean target, but instrumental activation lines must be controlled."}, {"id": "paper-2610-13771", "arxiv": "2610.13771", "title": "TeV Gamma-Ray Emission in the DA 495 Region from HAWC Observations of J1952+2924, J1954+2837, and J1958+2848", "authors": "HAWC Collaboration", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Compact objects", "Cosmic rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "HAWC resolves three extended sources near DA 495, reconciling its flux with VERITAS and strengthening model-dependent TeV-halo interpretations.", "path": "2026/10/12/index.html#paper-2610-13771", "reportDate": "12 October 2026", "searchText": "TeV Gamma-Ray Emission in the DA 495 Region from HAWC Observations of J1952+2924, J1954+2837, and J1958+2848 HAWC Collaboration 2610.13771 HAWC resolves three extended sources near DA 495, reconciling its flux with VERITAS and strengthening model-dependent TeV-halo interpretations. 2026-10-12 12 October 2026 Gamma rays Compact objects Cosmic rays #### ★★★ 1. TeV Gamma-Ray Emission in the DA 495 Region from HAWC Observations of J1952+2924, J1954+2837, and J1958+2848\n\n**HAWC Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2610.13771](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13771)\n\n**New measurement**\n\nUsing 3,071 days of fifth-pass HAWC data, the collaboration resolves three extended sources with curved spectra in a confused Galactic-plane field: HAWC J1952+2924, associated with the pulsar-wind nebula DA 495, and J1954+2837 and J1958+2848, both plausible TeV-halo candidates. A fourth component is only a hint and is sensitive to the adopted Galactic diffuse-emission model.\n\nFor DA 495, the new spectrum extrapolates to\n\n\\[\n\\left.\\frac{dN}{dE}\\right|_{1\\,\\mathrm{TeV}}\n=(3.89\\pm1.27_{\\rm stat})\\times10^{-13}\\;\n\\mathrm{TeV^{-1}\\,cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}},\n\\]\n\nconsistent within \\(1\\sigma\\) with VERITAS. A one-zone leptonic interpretation uses \\(B\\sim10\\,\\mu\\mathrm G\\) and a roughly 50-TeV electron cutoff. The two other sources require diffusion coefficients around \\(3.2\\times10^{26}\\) and \\(1.4\\times10^{27}\\,\\mathrm{cm^2\\,s^{-1}}\\) at their fitted cutoff energies, far below the canonical interstellar value. \n\n##### History\n\nThe earlier [VERITAS/Fermi follow-up](https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.10423) confirmed TeV emission from DA 495 but measured a substantially smaller flux than HAWC’s 2HWC catalog; the later [HAWC–VERITAS–NuSTAR study](https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.07327) could not resolve whether the mismatch arose from morphology or source confusion. The dedicated three-source likelihood fit now removes that discrepancy without invoking variability and is therefore a substantive observational advance. \n\nIt also strengthens, but does not establish, the TeV-halo interpretation of J1954+2837 and J1958+2848. Their pulsar associations are positional and model dependent, the tentative fourth source changes with the diffuse template, and the DA 495 distance and age remain uncertain. At the often-used 1-kpc distance the inferred transport can fall below the Bohm limit; this indicates that the geometry or distance assumptions need revision, not exotic diffusion physics."}, {"id": "paper-2610-13865", "arxiv": "2610.13865", "title": "Perpendicular transport in isotropic magnetic turbulence: The role of stochastic drifts", "authors": "F. Pucci, P. Blasi, O. Pezzi, C. Evoli, F. Malara", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Cosmic rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Test-particle simulations over a wider turbulence range and a stochastic-drift extension of guiding-center theory explain rigidity-dependent perpendicular transport.", "path": "2026/10/12/index.html#paper-2610-13865", "reportDate": "12 October 2026", "searchText": "Perpendicular transport in isotropic magnetic turbulence: The role of stochastic drifts F. Pucci, P. Blasi, O. Pezzi, C. Evoli, F. Malara 2610.13865 Test-particle simulations over a wider turbulence range and a stochastic-drift extension of guiding-center theory explain rigidity-dependent perpendicular transport. 2026-10-12 12 October 2026 Cosmic rays #### ★★★ 2. Perpendicular transport in isotropic magnetic turbulence: The role of stochastic drifts\n\n**F. Pucci, P. Blasi, O. Pezzi, C. Evoli, F. Malara**  \n[arXiv:2610.13865](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13865)\n\n**Methodological/theory advance**\n\nThe authors propagate test particles through wavelet-generated, magnetostatic isotropic turbulence spanning six to seven decades in wavenumber—about two orders of magnitude more dynamic range than conventional grid realizations. Parallel diffusion follows \\(D_\\parallel\\propto E^{1/3}\\) at low rigidity and \\(D_\\parallel\\propto E^2\\) in the quasi-ballistic regime. The ratio \\(D_\\perp/D_\\parallel\\) is nearly constant only for \\(r_L\\lesssim10^{-2}L_c\\), then rises with rigidity.\n\nExtending nonlinear guiding-center theory with stochastic curvature/gradient drifts and a telegraph closure reproduces this transition better than standard NLGC/UNLT formulas. \n\n##### History\n\nTest-particle simulations had already found unequal parallel and perpendicular energy scalings, notably in [Dundović et al. 2020](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.102.103016). [Kuhlen, Mertsch and Phan](https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.05882) instead emphasized magnetic-field-line subdiffusion as the origin of the growing ratio. The new work both extends the resolved inertial range and supplies a quantitatively successful alternative mechanism—random guiding-center drifts caused by turbulent field components parallel to the guide field.\n\nIt does not decisively exclude the field-line explanation; the paper explicitly allows both effects to contribute. The calculation uses prescribed, isotropic, magnetostatic turbulence, a fitted closure parameter, and no self-consistent cosmic-ray feedback, so its scaling should not be transplanted directly to anisotropic Galactic turbulence."}, {"id": "paper-2610-12564", "arxiv": "2610.12564", "title": "Seeing in the Dark: Gravitational Waves from Resonant Production of Dark Photon Dark Matter", "authors": "Yanou Cui, Pankaj Saha, Evangelos I. Sfakianakis", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Cosmology", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Lattice simulations follow dark-photon resonance into nonlinear evolution, connecting mixed scalar–vector dark matter to a conditional gravitational-wave forecast.", "path": "2026/10/12/index.html#paper-2610-12564", "reportDate": "12 October 2026", "searchText": "Seeing in the Dark: Gravitational Waves from Resonant Production of Dark Photon Dark Matter Yanou Cui, Pankaj Saha, Evangelos I. Sfakianakis 2610.12564 Lattice simulations follow dark-photon resonance into nonlinear evolution, connecting mixed scalar–vector dark matter to a conditional gravitational-wave forecast. 2026-10-12 12 October 2026 Dark matter Cosmology Multimessenger #### ★★★ 3. Seeing in the Dark: Gravitational Waves from Resonant Production of Dark Photon Dark Matter\n\n**Yanou Cui, Pankaj Saha, Evangelos I. Sfakianakis**  \n[arXiv:2610.12564](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12564)\n\n**Nonlinear calculation and forecast**\n\nAn oscillating Abelian-Higgs condensate can parametrically amplify the longitudinal mode of a dark photon. Dedicated three-dimensional lattice simulations now follow the linear resonance through backreaction, fragmentation, vacuum trapping, scalar–vector rescattering, and gravitational-wave production.\n\nThe final relic is generically a mixture of dark photons and dark-Higgs excitations; the partition is fixed by nonlinear evolution rather than by the linear Floquet exponent. If this sector supplies all dark matter, the gravitational-wave peak and amplitude correlate with the resonance scale. Frequencies from nanohertz to about 100 Hz map, in the studied benchmarks, to dark-photon masses from roughly \\(10^{-18}\\) eV to the sub-µeV range. \n\n##### History\n\n[Dror, Harigaya and Narayan](https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.07195) established the analytic parametric-resonance mechanism but could not determine the post-backreaction abundance, phase-space distribution, or gravitational-wave spectrum. The new lattice calculation changes that status materially: the scalar remnant can be comparable to the vector abundance, and the observable is a correlated multicomponent-dark-matter/GW signal rather than a pure-vector relic.\n\nThis is still a forecast, not a detection claim. Finite lattice dynamic range, approximate post-trapping number-conserving scatterings, the minimal quartic potential, and the assumption that the sector makes all dark matter control the reach; astrophysical constraints on the mixed scalar–vector population are not yet propagated globally."}, {"id": "paper-2610-12577", "arxiv": "2610.12577", "title": "The road to H₀, Ωm, and w with a global neutrino-telescope network", "authors": "Pavel Zhelnin, Gonzalo Herrera, Nicholas Kamp, Carlos A. Argüelles", "primary_category": "astro-ph.CO", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Cosmology", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "An all-sky Seyfert reanalysis and neutrino-network forecast explore joint constraints on the Hubble constant and the neutrino–X-ray luminosity relation.", "path": "2026/10/12/index.html#paper-2610-12577", "reportDate": "12 October 2026", "searchText": "The road to H₀, Ωm, and w with a global neutrino-telescope network Pavel Zhelnin, Gonzalo Herrera, Nicholas Kamp, Carlos A. Argüelles 2610.12577 An all-sky Seyfert reanalysis and neutrino-network forecast explore joint constraints on the Hubble constant and the neutrino–X-ray luminosity relation. 2026-10-12 12 October 2026 Neutrinos Cosmology Multimessenger #### ★★☆ 4. The road to \\(H_0\\), \\(\\Omega_m\\), and \\(w\\) with a global neutrino-telescope network\n\n**Pavel Zhelnin, Gonzalo Herrera, Nicholas Kamp, Carlos A. Argüelles**  \n[arXiv:2610.12577](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12577)\n\n**Current reanalysis plus forecast**\n\nThis study treats the empirical Seyfert neutrino–X-ray luminosity relation, \\(L_\\nu\\propto L_X^\\beta\\), as a distance indicator. Applying it to 14 years of public IceCube data for 26 northern and southern Seyferts, anchored by geometric distances to NGC 1068 and NGC 4945, gives\n\n\\[\nH_0=66^{+44}_{-37}\\;\n\\mathrm{km\\,s^{-1}\\,Mpc^{-1}},\\qquad\n\\beta=0.72^{+0.14}_{-0.16}.\n\\]\n\nA 2030 IceCube+KM3NeT-ARCA+Baikal-GVD forecast with five anchors gives \\(H_0=66^{+15}_{-13}\\) and \\(\\beta=0.72\\pm0.04\\), a nearly 65% reduction in the quoted \\(H_0\\) uncertainty and a projected \\(\\gtrsim5\\sigma\\) rejection of the calorimetric slope \\(\\beta=1\\). A high-redshift “little-red-dot” extension to \\(\\Omega_m\\) and \\(w\\) is explicitly conditional on those objects becoming neutrino sources obeying the same relation. \n\n##### History\n\nThe authors’ [initial neutrino \\(H_0\\) analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00923) used a smaller northern-sky Seyfert sample and one distance anchor. The present real-data fit adds the southern sky and a second anchor but remains a proof of principle: its roughly 60% fractional uncertainty is not cosmologically competitive.\n\nThe principal advance is the all-sky network design and demonstration that better angular reconstruction and multiple anchors can separately constrain \\(H_0\\) and \\(\\beta\\). The forecast assumes that a single luminosity law—with controlled X-ray absorption, variability, source selection, and intrinsic scatter—applies to every Seyfert and future detector. Those systematics may dominate before the advertised statistical precision is reached. The high-redshift \\(\\Omega_m,w\\) exercise is more speculative still."}, {"id": "paper-2610-13633", "arxiv": "2610.13633", "title": "nCTEQ26 nuclear parton distribution functions", "authors": "nCTEQ Collaboration: K. Greve, T. Ježo, A. Kusina, J. Wissmann, M. Klasen, K. Kovařík, C. Léger, J. G. Morfín, F. I. Olness, P. Risse, I. Schienbein, J. Y. Yu", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Particle physics"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A global nuclear-parton fit combines 3,598 measurements with flexible nuclear dependence, high-x corrections, and public uncertainty grids.", "path": "2026/10/12/index.html#paper-2610-13633", "reportDate": "12 October 2026", "searchText": "nCTEQ26 nuclear parton distribution functions nCTEQ Collaboration: K. Greve, T. Ježo, A. Kusina, J. Wissmann, M. Klasen, K. Kovařík, C. Léger, J. G. Morfín, F. I. Olness, P. Risse, I. Schienbein, J. Y. Yu 2610.13633 A global nuclear-parton fit combines 3,598 measurements with flexible nuclear dependence, high-x corrections, and public uncertainty grids. 2026-10-12 12 October 2026 Particle physics #### ★★★ 5. nCTEQ26 nuclear parton distribution functions\n\n**nCTEQ Collaboration: K. Greve, T. Ježo, A. Kusina, J. Wissmann, M. Klasen, K. Kovařík, C. Léger, J. G. Morfín, F. I. Olness, P. Risse, I. Schienbein, J. Y. Yu**  \n[arXiv:2610.13633](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13633)\n\n**Global analysis and public resource**\n\nnCTEQ26 fits 3,598 measurements—neutral- and charged-current DIS, neutrino dimuons, fixed-target Drell–Yan, electroweak bosons, inclusive hadrons, open heavy flavor and quarkonia from RHIC and LHC Runs 1–2—with 36 free parameters at NLO.\n\nA flexible \\(x\\) dependence is combined with quadratic logarithmic nuclear-\\(A\\) dependence, target-mass and deuteron corrections, and the CJ15 proton baseline. The data reach \\(x\\sim10^{-5}\\), while relaxed cuts admit high-\\(x\\) Jefferson Lab information. Hessian grids are released through LHAPDF and HEPForge. \n\n##### History\n\n[nCTEQ15](https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.00792) used 740 points, chiefly fixed-target DIS/Drell–Yan plus RHIC pion data. [EPPS21](https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.12462) and [nNNPDF3.0](https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.12363) had already shown the leverage of LHC dijets, electroweak bosons and heavy flavor on small-\\(x\\) gluons. \n\nnCTEQ26’s advance is therefore not a first observation of shadowing but the consolidation of the nCTEQ program into a much larger, process-diverse fit with explicit high-\\(x\\) corrections and public error sets. Direct precision comparisons across groups remain sensitive to parametrization and tolerance choices: nCTEQ26 uses a Hessian tolerance \\(T=50\\), works at NLO, fixes the proton baseline rather than propagating its full uncertainty, and does not include a general missing-higher-order error. Data-driven quarkonium/open-heavy-flavor constraints may also absorb production-model effects into the fitted nPDFs."}, {"id": "paper-2610-13873", "arxiv": "2610.13873", "title": "Complete four-loop pressure of cold QED", "authors": "Aapeli Kärkkäinen, Pablo Navarrete, Mika Nurmela, Kaapo Seppänen, Samuel Abreu, Maximilian Delto, Aleksi Kurkela, Risto Paatelainen, Aleksi Vuorinen", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Particle physics", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The final finite hard contribution completes the four-loop pressure of cold QED and tests methods needed for dense QCD and neutron-star equations of state.", "path": "2026/10/12/index.html#paper-2610-13873", "reportDate": "12 October 2026", "searchText": "Complete four-loop pressure of cold QED Aapeli Kärkkäinen, Pablo Navarrete, Mika Nurmela, Kaapo Seppänen, Samuel Abreu, Maximilian Delto, Aleksi Kurkela, Risto Paatelainen, Aleksi Vuorinen 2610.13873 The final finite hard contribution completes the four-loop pressure of cold QED and tests methods needed for dense QCD and neutron-star equations of state. 2026-10-12 12 October 2026 Particle physics Compact objects #### ★★★ 6. Complete four-loop pressure of cold QED\n\n**Aapeli Kärkkäinen, Pablo Navarrete, Mika Nurmela, Kaapo Seppänen, Samuel Abreu, Maximilian Delto, Aleksi Kurkela, Risto Paatelainen, Aleksi Vuorinen**  \n[arXiv:2610.13873](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13873)\n\n**First complete calculation**\n\nThe authors complete the next-to-next-to-next-to-leading-order pressure of a zero-temperature ultrarelativistic electron gas. The calculation combines hard, soft and mixed momentum regions; the remaining finite-density four-loop integrals are evaluated with dense loop–tree duality.\n\nIt is the first thermodynamic quantity calculated completely through four loops in a physical gauge theory. The series shows good apparent convergence and weak renormalization-scale dependence even at relatively large couplings, although a Bayesian missing-order analysis gives uncertainties larger than scale variation alone. \n\n##### History\n\nFreedman and McLerran’s 1977 calculation fixed the cold-QED pressure through N2LO. The 2023 result [Degenerate fermionic matter at N3LO: Quantum electrodynamics](https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.11893) determined all logarithmic and factorized pieces but left the finite hard four-loop constant unevaluated. This paper supplies that last contribution and is therefore a genuine completion rather than an incremental coefficient update.\n\nIts largest impact is methodological: it validates tools aimed at the still-incomplete N3LO cold-QCD pressure relevant to neutron-star equations of state. QED itself is weakly coupled, so the direct phenomenological correction to ordinary electron matter is small; furthermore, scale variation alone demonstrably understates truncation uncertainty. The analogous non-Abelian calculation remains substantially harder."}, {"id": "paper-2610-13671", "arxiv": "2610.13671", "title": "ALP interactions with mesons and baryons at O(1/fa): a general field-redefinition-invariant approach", "authors": "Jorge Alda, Stefano Rigolin", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["BSM", "Particle physics"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Field-redefinition-invariant matching extends ALP–hadron interactions to flavor change and the baryon octet, with phenomenological limits at GeV masses.", "path": "2026/10/12/index.html#paper-2610-13671", "reportDate": "12 October 2026", "searchText": "ALP interactions with mesons and baryons at O(1/fa): a general field-redefinition-invariant approach Jorge Alda, Stefano Rigolin 2610.13671 Field-redefinition-invariant matching extends ALP–hadron interactions to flavor change and the baryon octet, with phenomenological limits at GeV masses. 2026-10-12 12 October 2026 BSM Particle physics #### ★★☆ 7. ALP interactions with mesons and baryons at \\(\\mathcal O(1/f_a)\\): a general field-redefinition-invariant approach\n\n**Jorge Alda, Stefano Rigolin**  \n[arXiv:2610.13671](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13671)\n\n**Formalism and phenomenology**\n\nStarting from general CP-conserving quark and gluon couplings, the paper identifies field-redefinition-invariant Wilson combinations and matches them into \\(U(3)\\) chiral perturbation theory. LSZ reduction is used to choose a no-mixing basis, so physical ALP–meson amplitudes depend only on invariant direct couplings.\n\nThe authors derive general vector and axial couplings to the baryon octet, including a second topological-singlet ALP–baryon structure, constrain flavor-changing terms with rare hyperon decays, and show in a UV example how running generates flavor change from diagonal high-scale couplings. In a gluophilic benchmark the summed baryon–antibaryon width can exceed any individual exclusive mesonic width for \\(2\\lesssim m_a/\\mathrm{GeV}\\lesssim3\\). \n\n##### History\n\nEarlier weak-chiral analyses, notably [Bauer et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.13112), enforced consistent mesonic matching, while [Balkin et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.15637) developed a covariant description of ALP–hadron interactions. The new work extends field-basis invariance beyond the anomalous Wess–Zumino–Witten sector and systematically includes both flavor-changing quark couplings and the baryon octet.\n\nNo close prior study was found in the literature checked for the second topological-singlet term used as an independent dynamical ALP–baryon coupling; that is the paper’s sharpest formal novelty. The 2–3 GeV width estimates sit near the edge of controlled chiral reasoning and use phenomenological form factors/duality assumptions. Rare-hyperon bounds and collider comparisons also depend on the ALP decay branching fractions and UV flavor structure."}, {"id": "paper-2610-12595", "arxiv": "2610.12595", "title": "Nailing down the Hubble constant value through time-delay cosmography: A 3% measurement from SN Refsdal", "authors": "S. Schuldt, C. Grillo, S. H. Suyu, A. Acebron, L. Bazzanini, P. Bergamini, G. Di Rosa, A. Halkola, A. Mercurio, P. Rosati", "primary_category": "astro-ph.CO", "tags": ["Cosmology"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Resolved host-galaxy reconstruction halves the SN Refsdal Hubble-constant uncertainty within a fixed lens-model family; wider systematics remain to be assessed.", "path": "2026/10/12/index.html#paper-2610-12595", "reportDate": "12 October 2026", "searchText": "Nailing down the Hubble constant value through time-delay cosmography: A 3% measurement from SN Refsdal S. Schuldt, C. Grillo, S. H. Suyu, A. Acebron, L. Bazzanini, P. Bergamini, G. Di Rosa, A. Halkola, A. Mercurio, P. Rosati 2610.12595 Resolved host-galaxy reconstruction halves the SN Refsdal Hubble-constant uncertainty within a fixed lens-model family; wider systematics remain to be assessed. 2026-10-12 12 October 2026 Cosmology #### ★★★ 8. Nailing down the Hubble constant value through time-delay cosmography: A 3% measurement from SN Refsdal\n\n**S. Schuldt, C. Grillo, S. H. Suyu, A. Acebron, L. Bazzanini, P. Bergamini, G. Di Rosa, A. Halkola, A. Mercurio, P. Rosati**  \n[arXiv:2610.12595](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12595)\n\n**New cosmographic measurement**\n\nThe analysis adds an extended-source reconstruction of 77,000 HST pixels from the SN Refsdal host to the MACS J1149.5+2223 cluster lens and its measured supernova time delays. In flat \\(\\Lambda\\)CDM it obtains\n\n\\[\nH_0=65.0^{+2.0}_{-1.8}\\;\n\\mathrm{km\\,s^{-1}\\,Mpc^{-1}},\\qquad\n\\Omega_m=0.30^{+0.08}_{-0.09}.\n\\]\n\nWith \\(\\Omega_m\\) free, the surface-brightness information halves the \\(H_0\\) uncertainty relative to the same point-image model, reaching 3.0%. Four source/weighting realizations give mutually consistent results. \n\n##### History\n\nThe blinded multi-model [Kelly et al. analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.06367) found \\(H_0=64.8^{+4.4}_{-4.3}\\), while the position-and-delay cluster model of [Grillo et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.10980) obtained \\(65.1^{+3.5}_{-3.4}\\) in a broader cosmological fit. The central value is therefore confirmation, not a shift; the new result is the factor-of-two precision gain obtained by using resolved host-galaxy structure and the first joint \\(H_0,\\Omega_m\\) inference from such an extended lensed-SN reconstruction. \n\nThe 3% figure is conditional on a fixed family of cluster mass parametrizations. The authors vary source reconstruction and weighting but deliberately do not explore the full mass-model space, external convergence, or every line-of-sight systematic. It should therefore not yet be read as a 3% total-error resolution of the Hubble tension."}, {"id": "paper-2610-10685", "arxiv": "2610.10685", "title": "Neutrino flavor conversion alters the lepton-emission self-sustained asymmetry in core-collapse supernovae", "authors": "Noah Roux, Irene Tamborra, Maryna Mesiura", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Multidimensional quantum-kinetic calculations enhance an imposed LESA dipole by 5–10%, with hydrodynamic feedback and full flavor evolution still absent.", "path": "2026/10/09/index.html#paper-2610-10685", "reportDate": "9 October 2026", "searchText": "Neutrino flavor conversion alters the lepton-emission self-sustained asymmetry in core-collapse supernovae Noah Roux, Irene Tamborra, Maryna Mesiura 2610.10685 Multidimensional quantum-kinetic calculations enhance an imposed LESA dipole by 5–10%, with hydrodynamic feedback and full flavor evolution still absent. 2026-10-09 9 October 2026 Neutrinos Compact objects #### ★★★ 1. Neutrino flavor conversion alters the lepton-emission self-sustained asymmetry in core-collapse supernovae\n\n**Noah Roux, Irene Tamborra, Maryna Mesiura**  \n[arXiv:2610.10685](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.10685) — v1 submitted 7 October 2026; announced 9 October\n\nCan quantum flavor evolution feed back on LESA, the dipolar electron-lepton-number emission asymmetry seen in multidimensional core-collapse-supernova simulations? The authors solve two-flavor quantum kinetic equations with advection and collisions in a two-dimensional half-annulus spanning 20–45 km, with energy and polar-angle resolution and two effective azimuthal momentum bins. Their imposed LESA-like background produces collisional instability near decoupling, fast conversion mainly in the electron-lepton-number-depleted hemisphere, and slow instabilities elsewhere. Nonlinear conversion is direction dependent and raises the normalized LESA dipole by about 5–10% beyond 30 km, with little dependence on mass ordering.\n\n##### History\n\nLESA was discovered in three-dimensional hydrodynamic simulations by [Tamborra et al. (2014)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1402.5418), while earlier work on LESA and collective oscillations within a bulb-like treatment found matter suppression ([Chakraborty et al. 2015](https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.0670)). More recent quantum-kinetic studies established that advection, collisions, and fast and slow modes can coexist near decoupling ([Shalgar & Tamborra 2023](https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.04058)), but did not calculate their effect on a global LESA geometry. The substantive novelty is therefore the first multidimensional kinetic calculation of flavor conversion in a LESA-like configuration and the sign and scale of the resulting dipole change.\n\nThis is not yet a self-consistent supernova result. The dipole is imposed on a spherically symmetric 18.6-solar-mass snapshot rather than generated by hydrodynamics; matter feedback is absent; only two flavors and two effective azimuthal bins are retained; and the neutrino self-interaction Hamiltonian is attenuated by a factor 0.025 for tractability. The authors explicitly note that this rescaling can affect slow and collisional modes. The 5–10% enhancement is thus a controlled result for the adopted setup, not a calibrated correction ready for global explosion simulations."}, {"id": "paper-2610-10695", "arxiv": "2610.10695", "title": "The Origin of the Fermi Halo-like Emission: A New Model of the Fermi Bubbles", "authors": "Ilias Cholis, Leo Qiyuan Hu, Yi-Ming Zhong", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Dark matter", "Cosmic rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A wider foreground analysis attributes a bright Fermi halo-like residual to bubble substructure, challenging a specific dark-matter interpretation.", "path": "2026/10/09/index.html#paper-2610-10695", "reportDate": "9 October 2026", "searchText": "The Origin of the Fermi Halo-like Emission: A New Model of the Fermi Bubbles Ilias Cholis, Leo Qiyuan Hu, Yi-Ming Zhong 2610.10695 A wider foreground analysis attributes a bright Fermi halo-like residual to bubble substructure, challenging a specific dark-matter interpretation. 2026-10-09 9 October 2026 Gamma rays Dark matter Cosmic rays #### ★★★ 2. The Origin of the Fermi Halo-like Emission: A New Model of the Fermi Bubbles\n\n**Ilias Cholis, Leo Qiyuan Hu, Yi-Ming Zhong**  \n[arXiv:2610.10695](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.10695) — v1 submitted 7 October 2026; announced 9 October\n\nIs the broad high-latitude, roughly 20-GeV residual reported in Fermi-LAT data evidence for dark-matter annihilation in the Galactic halo? Using 2008–2025 LAT data from 0.3 to 900 GeV, the authors repeat the template analysis across multiple sky regions, source masks, fit parameterizations, and 100 Galactic diffuse-emission models. They confirm that an unmodeled bright component exists, but find that above \\(|b|\\geq20^\\circ\\) its normalization changes across regions in a way inconsistent with a single annihilation template. Updated Fermi-Bubble boundaries plus separate flat-bubble, cocoon, and newly introduced inner-bubble templates absorb the claimed halo-like component. A dark-matter signal as bright as the earlier claim is no longer required.\n\n##### History\n\n[Totani (2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07209) found the residual with six diffuse models and interpreted its morphology and spectrum as compatible with an approximately 0.5-TeV annihilating particle, requiring a cross section of order \\(6\\times10^{-25}\\,\\mathrm{cm^3\\,s^{-1}}\\). The new work does not dispute the photons; it changes their attribution. The broader foreground library and region-by-region normalization test expose a degeneracy with inverse-Compton emission from incompletely modeled Fermi-Bubble substructure. This is a direct, technically substantial challenge to that specific dark-matter interpretation, while leaving the inner-Galaxy excess essentially unchanged.\n\nThe limitation is that the replacement components are still phenomenological spatial templates, not a predictive cosmic-ray transport solution. Bubble, Loop I, inverse-Compton, and diffuse-Galactic components remain correlated, and some fitted morphology changes across diffuse models. The result therefore removes the need for the reported *bright* halo dark-matter component; it does not prove that every weaker high-latitude dark-matter contribution is absent."}, {"id": "paper-2610-11465", "arxiv": "2610.11465", "title": "Observation of single top-quark production in the s-channel in proton–proton collisions at √s = 13 TeV with the ATLAS detector", "authors": "ATLAS Collaboration", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["Particle physics"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "ATLAS observes s-channel single-top production at 5.1 sigma using improved graph-network discrimination in the existing Run-2 sample.", "path": "2026/10/09/index.html#paper-2610-11465", "reportDate": "9 October 2026", "searchText": "Observation of single top-quark production in the s-channel in proton–proton collisions at √s = 13 TeV with the ATLAS detector ATLAS Collaboration 2610.11465 ATLAS observes s-channel single-top production at 5.1 sigma using improved graph-network discrimination in the existing Run-2 sample. 2026-10-09 9 October 2026 Particle physics #### ★★★ 3. Observation of single top-quark production in the \\(s\\)-channel in proton–proton collisions at \\(\\sqrt{s}=13\\) TeV with the ATLAS detector\n\n**ATLAS Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2610.11465](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.11465) — submitted 8 October 2026; announced 9 October\n\nATLAS searches for the highly suppressed electroweak process \\(q\\bar q'\\to W^*\\to t\\bar b\\) in the full \\(140\\,\\mathrm{fb^{-1}}\\) Run-2 sample. Events contain one electron or muon, missing transverse momentum, and exactly two \\(b\\)-tagged jets. A graph neural network separates signal from \\(t\\bar t\\), \\(W+\\)jets, and \\(t\\)-channel single-top backgrounds. The fitted significance is 5.1 standard deviations observed (5.2 expected), and\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{t\\bar b+\\bar t b}=10.8\\pm1.5\\,\\text{(stat.)}\\pm1.6\\,\\text{(syst.) pb},\n\\]\n\nconsistent with the Standard Model.\n\n##### History\n\nThe Tevatron first observed the \\(s\\) channel only by combining CDF and D0, reaching 6.3 sigma ([CDF+D0 2014](https://arxiv.org/abs/1402.5126)). At the LHC, ATLAS obtained 3.2 sigma at 8 TeV ([ATLAS 2016](https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.05980)) and 3.3 sigma with the same 139–140 \\(\\mathrm{fb^{-1}}\\) Run-2 exposure at 13 TeV, measuring \\(8.2^{+3.5}_{-2.9}\\) pb ([ATLAS 2023](https://doi.org/10.1007/JHEP06(2023)191)). The new result crosses the discovery threshold without additional luminosity; the advance is the graph-network discrimination and revised analysis strategy, not an exposure update. It is the first single-experiment observation of this channel.\n\nThe measurement uncertainty remains about 20%, with statistical and systematic terms comparable, so it is a confirmation of the Standard Model rather than a precision constraint on anomalous \\(Wtb\\) couplings. Comparisons with the older analysis also require care because the event modeling and multivariate discriminant changed together."}, {"id": "paper-2610-10689", "arxiv": "2610.10689", "title": "Discovery of a nearby ultra-fast magnetic white dwarf propeller: common pathways to magnetic cataclysmic variables", "authors": "Antonio C. Rodriguez, Kareem El-Badry, R. Michael Rich, Kevin N. Ortiz Ceballos, Ingrid Pelisoli, Casey J. Law, Kaya Mori, Boris Gänsicke, Edo Berger, Liam Connor, Ilkham Galiullin, Ilaria Caiazzo, Matthew Graham, Samuel Whitebook", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Compact objects", "X rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A nearby magnetic white-dwarf propeller combines dynamical and field measurements, while its candidate spin period and population implications remain provisional.", "path": "2026/10/09/index.html#paper-2610-10689", "reportDate": "9 October 2026", "searchText": "Discovery of a nearby ultra-fast magnetic white dwarf propeller: common pathways to magnetic cataclysmic variables Antonio C. Rodriguez, Kareem El-Badry, R. Michael Rich, Kevin N. Ortiz Ceballos, Ingrid Pelisoli, Casey J. Law, Kaya Mori, Boris Gänsicke, Edo Berger, Liam Connor, Ilkham Galiullin, Ilaria Caiazzo, Matthew Graham, Samuel Whitebook 2610.10689 A nearby magnetic white-dwarf propeller combines dynamical and field measurements, while its candidate spin period and population implications remain provisional. 2026-10-09 9 October 2026 Compact objects X rays #### ★★★ 4. Discovery of a nearby ultra-fast magnetic white dwarf propeller: common pathways to magnetic cataclysmic variables\n\n**Antonio C. Rodriguez, Kareem El-Badry, R. Michael Rich, Kevin N. Ortiz Ceballos, Ingrid Pelisoli, Casey J. Law, Kaya Mori, Boris Gänsicke, Edo Berger, Liam Connor, Ilkham Galiullin, Ilaria Caiazzo, Matthew Graham, Samuel Whitebook**  \n[arXiv:2610.10689](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.10689) — v1 submitted 7 October 2026; announced 9 October\n\nA cross-match of eROSITA and Gaia using the “X-ray Main Sequence” identifies 1eRASS J042633.5−250226 (Valeria), 265 pc away. TESS and ZTF show AE Aqr-like flaring, a 6.62-hour orbit, and month-scale state changes; phase-resolved spectroscopy gives \\(K_2=202\\pm7\\,\\mathrm{km\\,s^{-1}}\\) and no accretion disk or stream. Zeeman splitting suggests \\(B_{\\rm WD}=26\\pm3\\) MG, with an alternative solution near 33 MG. High-speed optical data show a candidate 26.8-second pulsation, though the robust statement is only \\(P_{\\rm spin}<60\\) s. For any plausible transfer rate, the inferred magnetospheric radius greatly exceeds corotation, making the propeller classification secure. The authors infer a local birth-rate density near \\(8\\times10^{-16}\\,\\mathrm{pc^{-3}\\,yr^{-1}}\\) and argue that many magnetic cataclysmic variables pass through this phase.\n\n##### History\n\nAE Aquarii was long the sole white-dwarf propeller; LAMOST J0240+1952 became the second ([Garnavich et al. 2021](https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.08377)), with its 24.939-second spin later confirmed in the far ultraviolet ([Pelisoli et al. 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.00990)). Valeria is the third and the nearest. Unlike the second case at discovery, it combines a direct magnetic-field estimate, strong dynamical constraints, and multi-survey variability. The common hot, undermassive donors in all three systems give the first population-level support—still based on only three objects—for a recent thermal-timescale-transfer origin and a pathway toward ordinary magnetic CVs.\n\nThe 26.8-second period is not yet a secure spin: it is intermittent, absent from eROSITA X-rays, and requires independent confirmation. The Zeeman fit has aliases, the source is radio-undetected, and the space-density/birth-rate estimate is dominated by small-number and selection uncertainties. The object itself is robust; the claim that a substantial fraction of magnetic CVs traverse this phase remains provisional."}, {"id": "paper-2610-12205", "arxiv": "2610.12205", "title": "Evidence for orbital eccentricity supports a hierarchical origin for GW231123", "authors": "Baoxiang Wang, Tao Yang, Zhoujian Cao, Bin Hu, Xikai Shan, Rong-Gen Cai", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Compact objects", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "An eccentric waveform analysis moderately favors a hierarchical origin for GW231123, with sensitivity to low-frequency data, waveform models, and priors.", "path": "2026/10/09/index.html#paper-2610-12205", "reportDate": "9 October 2026", "searchText": "Evidence for orbital eccentricity supports a hierarchical origin for GW231123 Baoxiang Wang, Tao Yang, Zhoujian Cao, Bin Hu, Xikai Shan, Rong-Gen Cai 2610.12205 An eccentric waveform analysis moderately favors a hierarchical origin for GW231123, with sensitivity to low-frequency data, waveform models, and priors. 2026-10-09 9 October 2026 Compact objects Multimessenger #### ★★☆ 5. Evidence for orbital eccentricity supports a hierarchical origin for GW231123\n\n**Baoxiang Wang, Tao Yang, Zhoujian Cao, Bin Hu, Xikai Shan, Rong-Gen Cai**  \n[arXiv:2610.12205](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12205) — v1 submitted 8 October 2026; announced 9 October\n\nCould the exceptional masses and near-extremal inferred spins of GW231123 partly reflect a quasi-circular waveform assumption? The authors analyze Hanford data from 11–448 Hz and Livingston from 20–448 Hz with the eccentric, precessing, higher-mode TEOBResumS–Dalí model, including calibration uncertainty. They obtain\n\n\\[\ne_{5.5\\,\\mathrm{Hz}}=0.34^{+0.12}_{-0.23}\\quad(90\\%\\ \\text{credible}),\n\\]\n\nwith a Bayes factor of 10.2 over the quasi-circular version of the same waveform family. Allowing eccentricity lowers the secondary-spin median from about 0.90 to 0.74, close to the expected spin of a comparable-mass merger remnant, while leaving the primary rapidly spinning. Zero-noise injection studies show that discarding the 11–20 Hz Hanford information can hide this eccentricity.\n\n##### History\n\nThe LVK discovery analysis inferred source-frame masses near \\(137\\) and \\(101\\,M_\\odot\\) and high spins under quasi-circular models ([LVK 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.08219)). A subsequent numerical-relativity study argued that eccentricity can masquerade as high spin but found quasi-circular templates still preferred and concluded that better eccentric merger models were needed ([Chandra et al. 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.15150)). The new analysis changes that conclusion by using a precessing eccentric effective-one-body model and lower-frequency Hanford data; at a 20-Hz cutoff it recovers a weak constraint consistent with prior work. If correct, the secondary becomes more naturally second-generation and less suitable as a direct marker of the pair-instability gap's upper edge.\n\nThis is meaningful but not decisive evidence: a Bayes factor of 10 is moderate, the posterior retains support near zero eccentricity, the decisive extra information comes from one detector's glitch-subtracted 11–20 Hz band, and the result is waveform- and prior-dependent for a signal with very few cycles. Independent eccentric waveform families and collaboration-level reanalysis are required before treating the hierarchical interpretation as established."}, {"id": "paper-2610-11782", "arxiv": "2610.11782", "title": "Short-Baseline Near Detector (SBND): Design and Initial Performance", "authors": "SBND Collaboration", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Oscillations"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "SBND reports its as-built detector and first-year performance, establishing operating capability for future oscillation and interaction measurements.", "path": "2026/10/09/index.html#paper-2610-11782", "reportDate": "9 October 2026", "searchText": "Short-Baseline Near Detector (SBND): Design and Initial Performance SBND Collaboration 2610.11782 SBND reports its as-built detector and first-year performance, establishing operating capability for future oscillation and interaction measurements. 2026-10-09 9 October 2026 Neutrinos Oscillations #### ★★☆ 6. Short-Baseline Near Detector (SBND): Design and Initial Performance\n\n**SBND Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2610.11782](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.11782) — v1 submitted 8 October 2026; announced 9 October\n\nThe paper documents the as-built 112-tonne active-mass liquid-argon TPC 110 m from Fermilab's Booster Neutrino Beam and reports its first year of beam performance. Electron lifetimes exceeded 30 ms—ten times the design requirement—for more than 95% of Run 1; 99.3% of TPC channels were live; collection-wire noise averaged 388 electrons; photon yield exceeded 10 photoelectrons/MeV with few-nanosecond timing; and the system achieved 98.6% data-collection efficiency. These conditions have already produced the world's largest neutrino–argon interaction sample.\n\n##### History\n\nFermilab announced SBND's first neutrino interactions in September 2024 ([official announcement](https://news.fnal.gov/2024/09/first-neutrinos-detected-at-fermilab-short-baseline-detector/)), and previous papers described expected photon-system performance ([Machado et al. 2024](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.07514)) and the broader physics program ([SBND 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.00245)). The new article is the first consolidated as-built detector and measured-performance reference. The factor-of-ten purity margin, near-theoretical wire noise, high live fraction, and resolved 19-ns beam bunch structure turn design forecasts into demonstrated operating capability.\n\nThis is an instrumentation milestone, not yet a sterile-neutrino or cross-section measurement. Detector stability does not by itself establish the final flux, reconstruction, and correlated near/far systematic uncertainties that will control SBN oscillation tests. Physics reach still depends on paired ICARUS analyses and validated interaction models."}, {"id": "paper-2610-10692", "arxiv": "2610.10692", "title": "Effective field theory for strong phase transitions", "authors": "Oliver Gould, Farbod-Sayyed Rassouli, Paul M. Saffin", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Cosmology", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A new effective-field-theory hierarchy improves thermodynamic control for strong phase transitions, with realistic nucleation and gravitational-wave forecasts still ahead.", "path": "2026/10/09/index.html#paper-2610-10692", "reportDate": "9 October 2026", "searchText": "Effective field theory for strong phase transitions Oliver Gould, Farbod-Sayyed Rassouli, Paul M. Saffin 2610.10692 A new effective-field-theory hierarchy improves thermodynamic control for strong phase transitions, with realistic nucleation and gravitational-wave forecasts still ahead. 2026-10-09 9 October 2026 Cosmology BSM #### ★★☆ 7. Effective field theory for strong phase transitions\n\n**Oliver Gould, Farbod-Sayyed Rassouli, Paul M. Saffin**  \n[arXiv:2610.10692](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.10692) — v1 submitted 7 October 2026; announced 9 October\n\nStandard high-temperature dimensional reduction becomes unreliable precisely for the strong first-order transitions that would yield the largest gravitational-wave signals. The authors identify a new power counting in which the order parameter remains soft while fields coupled to its large background become hard. Integrating out those modes gives a three-dimensional EFT with non-polynomial field dependence but a controlled derivative expansion. In a two-scalar model they match through NLO, add the first infrared correction at NNLO, demonstrate renormalization-scale cancellation, and find decreasing corrections to the latent heat. They extend the construction to Yukawa and gauge theories and give the useful strong-transition criterion \\(\\lambda\\sim g^4/\\pi^2\\).\n\n##### History\n\nConventional dimensional-reduction EFTs are systematically controlled for weak or intermediate transitions, and earlier uncertainty studies showed that their largest errors often occur at the strongest, most gravitational-wave-visible points ([Croon et al. 2021](https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.10080); [Gould & Tenkanen 2021](https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.04399)). The new paper does not merely calculate another loop order; it defines a different EFT hierarchy and resums the large background-field dependence that invalidates the polynomial expansion. That is a genuine methodological advance toward credible gravitational-wave forecasts.\n\nThe numerical demonstration is still a benchmark two-scalar model. Supercooling can change the hierarchy between critical and nucleation temperatures, gauge and fermion examples are not carried to the same quantitative order, and automation comparable to DRalgo remains future work. The framework improves thermodynamic control; it does not yet supply complete nucleation and gravitational-wave predictions for realistic models."}, {"id": "paper-2610-11881", "arxiv": "2610.11881", "title": "From GeV to PeV: Orbital Modulation and Wind-Regulated Ultrahigh-energy Emission in Cygnus X-3", "authors": "Su-Yu Wan, Ruo-Yu Liu, Xing-Fu Zhang, Dmitriy Khangulyan", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Cosmic rays", "Compact objects", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A joint GeV–PeV model uses orbital modulation to constrain Cygnus X-3 geometry and proposes wind-regulated ultrahigh-energy emission.", "path": "2026/10/09/index.html#paper-2610-11881", "reportDate": "9 October 2026", "searchText": "From GeV to PeV: Orbital Modulation and Wind-Regulated Ultrahigh-energy Emission in Cygnus X-3 Su-Yu Wan, Ruo-Yu Liu, Xing-Fu Zhang, Dmitriy Khangulyan 2610.11881 A joint GeV–PeV model uses orbital modulation to constrain Cygnus X-3 geometry and proposes wind-regulated ultrahigh-energy emission. 2026-10-09 9 October 2026 Gamma rays Cosmic rays Compact objects Multimessenger #### ★★☆ 8. From GeV to PeV: Orbital Modulation and Wind-Regulated Ultrahigh-energy Emission in Cygnus X-3\n\n**Su-Yu Wan, Ruo-Yu Liu, Xing-Fu Zhang, Dmitriy Khangulyan**  \n[arXiv:2610.11881](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.11881) — v1 submitted 8 October 2026; announced 9 October\n\nThe authors build a common binary–jet model for Cygnus X-3's contemporaneous GeV and PeV spectra and orbital light curves. Anisotropic inverse-Compton emission from jet electrons fixes the geometry using high-statistics GeV modulation. Multi-PeV protons then escape ballistically and make TeV–PeV photons through \\(p\\gamma\\) and \\(pp\\) interactions. In the fitted present state, a fast accretion-disk wind displaces the much denser Wolf–Rayet wind from observer-directed proton trajectories, suppressing \\(pp\\) emission. Modest changes in wind orientation or collimation can instead expose the protons to the dense stellar wind and raise the PeV luminosity by orders of magnitude.\n\n##### History\n\nLHAASO established variable emission from 0.06 to 3.7 PeV and reported 3.2-sigma evidence for 4.8-hour orbital modulation ([LHAASO 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.16638)). Immediate models explained the UHE component alone, including a counterjet-dominated geometry, but did not simultaneously reproduce the GeV light curve. The present work's advance is to let the GeV data constrain geometry before interpreting the PeV component; in the authors' test, the earlier UHE geometry fails that joint constraint. The wind-switching mechanism also offers a concrete—though unverified—connection to the much brighter 1980s PeV claims.\n\nThe model assumes ballistic multi-PeV propagation, an effective proton escape height, and idealized thin-shell wind interaction; shocked-layer mixing, clumping, and detailed magnetic transport are neglected. The historical PeV detections remain unconfirmed, so the predicted orders-of-magnitude state dependence is a scenario, not an observed cycle. Contemporaneous X-ray/radio wind diagnostics and more orbital PeV exposure are the decisive tests."}, {"id": "paper-2610-09477", "arxiv": "2610.09477", "title": "Polarised X-ray emission from the accreting millisecond pulsar SAX J1808.4–3658 during an X-ray outburst and a type-I X-ray burst", "authors": "Alessandro Papitto et al.", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["X rays", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "IXPE measures persistent X-ray polarization from SAX J1808.4–3658 and finds tentative burst polarization requiring confirmation.", "path": "2026/10/08/index.html#paper-2610-09477", "reportDate": "8 October 2026", "searchText": "Polarised X-ray emission from the accreting millisecond pulsar SAX J1808.4–3658 during an X-ray outburst and a type-I X-ray burst Alessandro Papitto et al. 2610.09477 IXPE measures persistent X-ray polarization from SAX J1808.4–3658 and finds tentative burst polarization requiring confirmation. 2026-10-08 8 October 2026 X rays Compact objects #### ★★★ 1. Polarised X-ray emission from the accreting millisecond pulsar SAX J1808.4–3658 during an X-ray outburst and a type-I X-ray burst\n\n**Alessandro Papitto et al.**  \n[arXiv:2610.09477](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.09477) — submitted 7 October 2026 — **new measurement**\n\nIXPE observed the 2025 outburst of the 2.5-ms accreting millisecond pulsar SAX J1808.4–3658. During the decay, the phase-averaged 2–8 keV polarization degree was \\(2.9\\pm0.5\\%\\), at position angle \\(46^\\circ\\pm5^\\circ\\); no significant pulse-phase dependence of the Stokes parameters was found. Simple two-spot modeling places the emitting regions close to the spin axis. During the peak of a thermonuclear burst, IXPE obtained a tentative \\(2\\!-\\!6\\) keV polarization degree of \\(15.5\\pm6.1\\%\\), with the angle approximately orthogonal to the persistent emission.\n\n##### History\n\nIXPE’s earlier observation of SRGA J144459.2−604207—the only other accreting millisecond pulsar targeted so far—found \\(2.3\\pm0.4\\%\\) polarization and similarly weak phase dependence ([Papitto et al. 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.00608)). SAX J1808 therefore confirms, in a second source, that mildly relativistic Comptonization above broad, nearly spin-aligned hot spots can yield only a few percent polarization. The genuinely new element is the possible burst polarization and its roughly \\(90^\\circ\\) angle change, which thin-disc reflection alone does not naturally produce. That burst result is only about \\(2.5\\sigma\\), is based on a 15-s interval, and requires confirmation; the spot geometry is also preliminary because the adopted model uses point spots and simplified energy-independent polarization."}, {"id": "paper-2610-09676", "arxiv": "2610.09676", "title": "Gamma-rays from black hole coronae: disentangling the leptonic and hadronic contributions", "authors": "Sébastien Le Bihan, Daniel Grošelj, Martin Lemoine", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Neutrinos", "Multimessenger", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A kinetic coronal model separates electron emission from hadronic cascades, identifying MeV photons as a probe of electron energization.", "path": "2026/10/08/index.html#paper-2610-09676", "reportDate": "8 October 2026", "searchText": "Gamma-rays from black hole coronae: disentangling the leptonic and hadronic contributions Sébastien Le Bihan, Daniel Grošelj, Martin Lemoine 2610.09676 A kinetic coronal model separates electron emission from hadronic cascades, identifying MeV photons as a probe of electron energization. 2026-10-08 8 October 2026 Gamma rays Neutrinos Multimessenger Compact objects #### ★★★ 2. Gamma-rays from black hole coronae: disentangling the leptonic and hadronic contributions\n\n**Sébastien Le Bihan, Daniel Grošelj, Martin Lemoine**  \n[arXiv:2610.09676](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.09676) — submitted 7 October 2026 — **multimessenger modeling advance**\n\nThe authors combine particle distributions motivated by kinetic simulations of magnetized coronal turbulence with the AM3 radiative-transport code, including hadronic electromagnetic cascades. For NGC 4151, a nonthermal electron component dominates the MeV emission and remains COSI-detectable if it carries at least about \\(5\\%\\) of the electron energy; the hadronic cascade generally takes over only toward the sub-GeV band and is below COSI’s sensitivity. The same electron prescription can also reproduce the \\(0.1\\!-\\!1\\) MeV excess of the cosmic X-ray background and the hard-state MeV tail of Cygnus X-1.\n\n##### History\n\nIceCube previously found only a \\(2.9\\sigma\\) post-trial excess toward NGC 4151, not a secure source detection ([IceCube hard-X-ray AGN search](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.06684)). Fermi analyses and coronal models had already shown that sub-GeV photons can accompany neutrino-producing Seyfert coronae ([Murase et al. 2024](https://doi.org/10.3847/2041-8213/ad19c5)), while jet and ultra-fast-outflow alternatives remain viable ([Peretti et al. 2023](https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.03298)). This paper’s advance is to calculate primary-electron and hadronic-cascade components in one kinetic framework and identify the MeV band as a cleaner probe of electron energization than of hadrons. The result does not establish a coronal origin for the putative neutrinos: the proton acceleration efficiency is tuned against gamma-ray bounds, the geometry is effectively one-zone, and the predicted crossover depends on the uncertain nonthermal electron fraction."}, {"id": "paper-2610-09612", "arxiv": "2610.09612", "title": "Remnant-wide mapping of heterogeneous presupernova burning histories in the O-rich ejecta of Cassiopeia A", "authors": "Yui Kuboike, Toshiki Sato, Kai Matsunaga", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["X rays", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Chandra abundance maps trace heterogeneous presupernova burning across Cassiopeia A, with model and projection uncertainties.", "path": "2026/10/08/index.html#paper-2610-09612", "reportDate": "8 October 2026", "searchText": "Remnant-wide mapping of heterogeneous presupernova burning histories in the O-rich ejecta of Cassiopeia A Yui Kuboike, Toshiki Sato, Kai Matsunaga 2610.09612 Chandra abundance maps trace heterogeneous presupernova burning across Cassiopeia A, with model and projection uncertainties. 2026-10-08 8 October 2026 X rays Compact objects #### ★★★ 3. Remnant-wide mapping of heterogeneous presupernova burning histories in the O-rich ejecta of Cassiopeia A\n\n**Yui Kuboike, Toshiki Sato, Kai Matsunaga**  \n[arXiv:2610.09612](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.09612) — submitted 7 October 2026 — **new observational mapping**\n\nUsing deep Chandra data, the authors apply principal-component analysis to O, Ne, Mg, Si, and Fe narrow-band images, then fit spectra from 20 representative regions. They find a continuous compositional sequence from Ne-rich through Ne–Si-rich to O/Si-rich ejecta, with systematic Ne and Mg depletion toward the latter. The O/Si-rich abundances from O through Ca agree simultaneously with the O layers of \\(17\\!-\\!20\\,M_\\odot\\) one-dimensional progenitor models that underwent strong shell-merger burning. Ne-rich and O/Si-rich material are segregated mainly to the north and southeast, respectively.\n\n##### History\n\nEarlier Chandra work established Cas A’s large-scale ejecta asymmetries, and selected regions had already shown Ne/Mg-poor O-rich material compatible with pre-collapse shell merging; recent XRISM spectroscopy added odd-\\(Z\\) nucleosynthetic evidence. The present work changes the evidentiary scale: it maps a remnant-wide continuum of burning histories rather than inferring shell merging from a few knots, and argues that heterogeneous progenitor structure survived the explosion. This is stronger than a generic explosion-asymmetry interpretation, but not unique proof of a shell merger. The comparison uses one-dimensional stellar models, while the inferred spatial history is intrinsically three-dimensional; projection, nonequilibrium-ionization modeling, and post-shock mixing can also reshape abundance maps."}, {"id": "paper-2610-09325", "arxiv": "2610.09325", "title": "Scalar Perturbations and Induced Gravitational Waves from First-Order Phase Transitions in Lattice Simulations", "authors": "Yuan-Jie Li, Jing Liu, Zhuan Ning, Xiang-Xi Zeng, Zong-Kuan Guo", "primary_category": "astro-ph.CO", "tags": ["Cosmology", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Coupled lattice simulations track scalar, radiation, and metric perturbations from bubble walls to the horizon and quantify their gravitational-wave contributions.", "path": "2026/10/08/index.html#paper-2610-09325", "reportDate": "8 October 2026", "searchText": "Scalar Perturbations and Induced Gravitational Waves from First-Order Phase Transitions in Lattice Simulations Yuan-Jie Li, Jing Liu, Zhuan Ning, Xiang-Xi Zeng, Zong-Kuan Guo 2610.09325 Coupled lattice simulations track scalar, radiation, and metric perturbations from bubble walls to the horizon and quantify their gravitational-wave contributions. 2026-10-08 8 October 2026 Cosmology Multimessenger #### ★★★ 4. Scalar Perturbations and Induced Gravitational Waves from First-Order Phase Transitions in Lattice Simulations\n\n**Yuan-Jie Li, Jing Liu, Zhuan Ning, Xiang-Xi Zeng, Zong-Kuan Guo**  \n[arXiv:2610.09325](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.09325) — submitted 7 October 2026 — **methodological advance**\n\nThis is a three-dimensional, multi-bubble lattice calculation of a radiation-era first-order phase transition that evolves the scalar field, scalar metric potentials, and radiation density and velocity perturbations together from the bubble-wall scale through the horizon scale. It yields the curvature spectrum, a non-Gaussian horizon-scale density PDF, and source-resolved gravitational-wave spectra. Radiation and metric-sourced contributions can exceed one third of the direct scalar-field contribution on superhorizon scales and alter the infrared spectral slope. For the simulated strengths and rates, ordinary horizon-scale fluctuations remain well below primordial-black-hole collapse thresholds.\n\n##### History\n\nRecent analyses treated transition-generated curvature perturbations primarily with semi-analytic or separate-universe methods ([Franciolini, Gouttenoire & Jinno 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.01962); [Wang et al. 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.14412)), while earlier lattice studies concentrated on direct scalar/fluid gravitational waves or on density perturbations without this coupled metric treatment. The new calculation closes the wall-to-horizon gap and shows quantitatively that omitting radiation and scalar-metric sources biases the infrared gravitational-wave spectrum. Its domain is deliberately restricted: metric perturbations remain linear, scalar–radiation coupling is only gravitational, and acoustic gravitational waves from direct fluid interactions are absent. Rare, exceptionally delayed regions that might form primordial black holes are not resolved."}, {"id": "paper-2610-09257", "arxiv": "2610.09257", "title": "Toward Simulations of Cold Baryogenesis via Supercooled Phase Transitions", "authors": "Naoya Kitajima, Shota Nakagawa, Yuichiro Nakai, Yaoduo Wang, Junxuan Xu", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Cosmology", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Supercooled-bubble simulations preserve efficient topological production, while the baryon yield remains dependent on imposed rates and a perturbative CP-violating operator.", "path": "2026/10/08/index.html#paper-2610-09257", "reportDate": "8 October 2026", "searchText": "Toward Simulations of Cold Baryogenesis via Supercooled Phase Transitions Naoya Kitajima, Shota Nakagawa, Yuichiro Nakai, Yaoduo Wang, Junxuan Xu 2610.09257 Supercooled-bubble simulations preserve efficient topological production, while the baryon yield remains dependent on imposed rates and a perturbative CP-violating operator. 2026-10-08 8 October 2026 Cosmology BSM #### ★★☆ 5. Toward Simulations of Cold Baryogenesis via Supercooled Phase Transitions\n\n**Naoya Kitajima, Shota Nakagawa, Yuichiro Nakai, Yaoduo Wang, Junxuan Xu**  \n[arXiv:2610.09257](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.09257) — submitted 7 October 2026 — **simulation advance with model-dependent baryon yield**\n\nThree-dimensional real-time lattice simulations follow a transition-driving scalar, the Higgs field, and electroweak gauge fields from bounce-derived bubbles through collision and nonlinear relaxation. Higgs winding and Chern–Simons number are still produced efficiently for strongly non-degenerate vacua, \\(\\epsilon\\sim10^{-3}\\!-\\!10^{-4}\\), where the released vacuum energy can also source a strong gravitational-wave signal. A perturbative higher-dimensional CP-violating operator then gives benchmark baryon yields compatible with the observed asymmetry.\n\n##### History\n\nClassical-lattice cold electroweak baryogenesis was established in tachyonic or externally quenched transitions two decades ago ([Tranberg, Smit & Hindmarsh 2006](https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/0608167)). This work extends that program to nucleated, strongly supercooled bubbles initialized from bounce solutions and shows that large vacuum non-degeneracy need not suppress topological production. The baryon abundance is not yet a prediction of a complete model: \\(\\beta/H\\sim O(10)\\) is imposed rather than thermally derived, the simulated microscopic-to-bubble scale hierarchy is much smaller than the phenomenological one, and the CP-violating operator is inserted perturbatively rather than evolved dynamically. The gravitational-wave/baryogenesis coexistence is therefore demonstrated in principle, not mapped into a robust experimental target."}, {"id": "paper-2610-09834", "arxiv": "2610.09834", "title": "Dynamical pairing in gravitational wave populations", "authors": "Fabio Antonini, Aleksandra Olejak, Isobel Romero-Shaw, Matthew Mould", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Compact objects", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "An ordered-draw model predicts fixed-secondary-mass ridges in dynamically assembled black-hole populations; the observed feature remains tentative.", "path": "2026/10/08/index.html#paper-2610-09834", "reportDate": "8 October 2026", "searchText": "Dynamical pairing in gravitational wave populations Fabio Antonini, Aleksandra Olejak, Isobel Romero-Shaw, Matthew Mould 2610.09834 An ordered-draw model predicts fixed-secondary-mass ridges in dynamically assembled black-hole populations; the observed feature remains tentative. 2026-10-08 8 October 2026 Compact objects Multimessenger #### ★★☆ 6. Dynamical pairing in gravitational wave populations\n\n**Fabio Antonini, Aleksandra Olejak, Isobel Romero-Shaw, Matthew Mould**  \n[arXiv:2610.09834](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.09834) — submitted 7 October 2026 — **population-inference method; tentative data feature**\n\nThe paper derives an “ordered-draw” null model for dynamically assembled black-hole binaries: component masses are independent draws from an effective one-body mass reservoir and are then labeled \\(m_1\\ge m_2\\). Once the primary-mass spectrum is inferred, the conditional \\(p(m_2|m_1)\\) is fixed. Cluster simulations reproduce the resulting fixed-\\(m_2\\) ridges, unlike the approximately fixed-\\(q=m_2/m_1\\) structures expected more naturally from isolated binaries. A nonparametric reconstruction shows a tentative ridge near \\(m_2\\simeq30\\,M_\\odot\\) for \\(m_1\\gtrsim40\\,M_\\odot\\), consistent with—but not evidence for—the dynamical pattern.\n\n##### History\n\nPrevious population analyses parameterized pairing with a mass-ratio power law or tested whether primary and secondary distributions share features ([Fishbach & Holz 2020](https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.12669); [Farah, Fishbach & Holz 2024](https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.05102)). The new contribution is a parameter-free conditional prediction tied to dynamical assembly and a geometric discriminator—fixed secondary-mass ridges—rather than another flexible mixture fit. The \\(30\\,M_\\odot\\) feature is explicitly tentative and sensitive to smoothing, selection effects, and uncertainty in the one-body spectrum. Ordered draws can also fail when hierarchical remnants, multiple metallicities, or generation-dependent pairing correlations are important, so agreement would not uniquely identify a cluster channel."}, {"id": "paper-2610-09626", "arxiv": "2610.09626", "title": "Observation of the electromagnetic Dalitz transition J/ψ → e⁺e⁻ηc", "authors": "BESIII Collaboration", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["Particle physics"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "BESIII observes the electromagnetic Dalitz transition J/ψ → e⁺e⁻ηc and finds a form factor consistent with existing predictions.", "path": "2026/10/08/index.html#paper-2610-09626", "reportDate": "8 October 2026", "searchText": "Observation of the electromagnetic Dalitz transition J/ψ → e⁺e⁻ηc BESIII Collaboration 2610.09626 BESIII observes the electromagnetic Dalitz transition J/ψ → e⁺e⁻ηc and finds a form factor consistent with existing predictions. 2026-10-08 8 October 2026 Particle physics #### ★★☆ 7. Observation of the electromagnetic Dalitz transition \\(J/\\psi\\rightarrow e^+e^-\\eta_c\\)\n\n**BESIII Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2610.09626](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.09626) — submitted 7 October 2026 — **first observation**\n\nWith \\((10.087\\pm0.044)\\times10^9\\) \\(J/\\psi\\) decays, BESIII observes the internal-conversion transition \\(J/\\psi\\to e^+e^-\\eta_c\\) for the first time. Relative to \\(J/\\psi\\to\\gamma\\eta_c\\), the rate is\n\n\\[\nR=(0.65\\pm0.02_{\\rm stat}\\pm0.07_{\\rm syst})\\%,\n\\]\n\ncorresponding to an absolute branching fraction \\((0.92\\pm0.03\\pm0.10\\pm0.09)\\times10^{-4}\\). The extracted timelike transition form factor shows no significant deviation from either vector-meson dominance (\\(0.606\\%\\)) or the lattice-QCD prediction (\\(0.608\\%\\)).\n\n##### History\n\nThe radiative M1 transition \\(J/\\psi\\to\\gamma\\eta_c\\) is well measured, and both vector-meson-dominance and lattice-QCD calculations predicted the Dalitz/radiative ratio before this analysis ([HPQCD 2023](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.108.014513)). The first observation validates those calculations in the low-\\(q^2\\), timelike region and opens a direct form-factor measurement; it does not reveal anomalous charmonium dynamics. The total error is already dominated by systematics, and the small available dilepton-mass range gives limited power to discriminate smooth form-factor models."}, {"id": "paper-2610-08940", "arxiv": "2610.08940", "title": "Predictions for Short Gamma-Ray Burst Detections by COSI", "authors": "Eliza Neights et al.", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Flight-trigger simulations forecast COSI short-GRB detection, localization, and joint gravitational-wave yields, with population and mission uncertainties.", "path": "2026/10/08/index.html#paper-2610-08940", "reportDate": "8 October 2026", "searchText": "Predictions for Short Gamma-Ray Burst Detections by COSI Eliza Neights et al. 2610.08940 Flight-trigger simulations forecast COSI short-GRB detection, localization, and joint gravitational-wave yields, with population and mission uncertainties. 2026-10-08 8 October 2026 Gamma rays Multimessenger #### ★★☆ 8. Predictions for Short Gamma-Ray Burst Detections by COSI\n\n**Eliza Neights et al.**  \n[arXiv:2610.08940](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.08940) — submitted 6 October 2026 — **instrument forecast**\n\nThe authors inject 2,720 Fermi-GBM-derived short-GRB realizations into simulated COSI backgrounds and run an emulation of the actual onboard anticoincidence-shield trigger. Folding the measured trigger efficiency through an intrinsic population gives \\(47\\!-\\!89\\) short-GRB detections per year (90% range), of which about \\(7\\!-\\!10\\) should be localized within \\(2.5^\\circ\\). If COSI operates into the LVK O5 era, the predicted binary-neutron-star GW/GRB yield is \\(0\\!-\\!3\\) joint events per year.\n\n##### History\n\nEarlier COSI/BTO studies forecast broad transient returns and roughly 150 GRBs per year for the lower-energy BTO subsystem ([Gulick et al. 2024](https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.07155)). The present work is more operationally specific: it propagates a short-GRB population through the flight trigger logic and separates trigger, localization, and joint-GW yields. It remains a forecast, not an on-orbit sensitivity measurement. Rates depend on the adopted luminosity function, simulated backgrounds, duty cycle, mission extension into O5, and uncertain future GW-network sensitivity; the \\(0\\!-\\!3\\) interval is therefore more informative than its central value."}, {"id": "paper-2610-08911", "arxiv": "2610.08911", "title": "Sensitivity targets for dark photon searches in charged kaon decays", "authors": "M. Fabbrichesi, E. Gabrielli, K. Müürsepp", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["BSM", "Particle physics"], "label": "★☆☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Charged-kaon decay calculations define massless and massive dark-photon sensitivity targets without a detector-level background or acceptance study.", "path": "2026/10/08/index.html#paper-2610-08911", "reportDate": "8 October 2026", "searchText": "Sensitivity targets for dark photon searches in charged kaon decays M. Fabbrichesi, E. Gabrielli, K. Müürsepp 2610.08911 Charged-kaon decay calculations define massless and massive dark-photon sensitivity targets without a detector-level background or acceptance study. 2026-10-08 8 October 2026 BSM Particle physics #### ★☆☆ 9. Sensitivity targets for dark photon searches in charged kaon decays\n\n**M. Fabbrichesi, E. Gabrielli, K. Müürsepp**  \n[arXiv:2610.08911](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.08911) — submitted 6 October 2026 — **phenomenology forecast**\n\nThe study calculates \\(K^+\\to\\pi^+\\pi^0\\gamma_D\\) and the newly proposed radiative channel \\(K^+\\to\\pi^+\\gamma\\gamma_D\\), with an invisible massless or massive dark photon. A flavor-changing dimension-five magnetic-dipole operator dominates; ordinary kinetic mixing is negligible for these modes. For a massless dark photon, existing \\(K^0\\!-\\!\\bar K^0\\) constraints permit branching fractions up to roughly \\(7\\times10^{-7}\\) and \\(6\\times10^{-10}\\), respectively. For \\(m_{\\gamma_D}\\gtrsim5\\) MeV, \\(K^+\\to\\pi^+X\\) and \\(K^+\\to\\pi^+\\nu\\bar\\nu\\) bounds force the two-pion target below \\(10^{-9}\\); the radiative mode falls more slowly with mass and covers a wider kinematic range.\n\n##### History\n\nThe same framework previously identified \\(K^+\\to\\pi^+\\pi^0\\gamma_D\\) as a promising massless-dark-photon mode ([Fabbrichesi, Gabrielli & Mele 2017](https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.03470)). The new work adds the one-pion radiative channel and treats massive dark photons consistently with current missing-energy constraints. This is useful target-setting, not a new exclusion: there is no detector-level background or acceptance study, and the rates inherit chiral-quark-model matrix elements and an assumed effective flavor-changing operator whose ultraviolet origin is unspecified."}, {"id": "paper-2610-08139", "arxiv": "2610.08139", "title": "The neutrino magnetic moment and supernovae with strong magnetic fields", "authors": "Clément Ehrhardt, Maria Cristina Volpe, Miguel Ángel Aloy, Martin Obergaulinger", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Compact objects", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Supernova simulations connect Majorana transition magnetic moments to JUNO and Hyper-Kamiokande signals, with sensitivity conditional on progenitor and magnetic-field histories.", "path": "2026/10/07/index.html#paper-2610-08139", "reportDate": "7 October 2026", "searchText": "The neutrino magnetic moment and supernovae with strong magnetic fields Clément Ehrhardt, Maria Cristina Volpe, Miguel Ángel Aloy, Martin Obergaulinger 2610.08139 Supernova simulations connect Majorana transition magnetic moments to JUNO and Hyper-Kamiokande signals, with sensitivity conditional on progenitor and magnetic-field histories. 2026-10-07 7 October 2026 Neutrinos Compact objects BSM #### ★★★ 1. The neutrino magnetic moment and supernovae with strong magnetic fields\n\n**Clément Ehrhardt, Maria Cristina Volpe, Miguel Ángel Aloy, Martin Obergaulinger**  \n**Submitted:** 6 October 2026  \n[arXiv:2610.08139](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.08139)\n\nThe authors propagate three-flavor Majorana neutrinos with transition magnetic moments through two detailed three-dimensional core-collapse simulations: a neutrino-driven \\(17\\,M_\\odot\\) explosion leaving a neutron star and a magnetorotational \\(26\\,M_\\odot\\) event ending in black-hole formation. They calculate fluxes and event rates for JUNO and Hyper-Kamiokande. Resonant spin-flavor precession materially alters the neutronization burst and accretion signal for transition moments around \\(10^{-14}\\mu_B\\) in the ordinary model, and can act at \\(10^{-15}\\mu_B\\) during early magnetorotational evolution.\n\n##### History\n\nEarly studies established magnetic-moment-driven spin-flavor conversion in supernova fields using mostly prescribed density and field profiles ([de Gouvêa and Shalgar 2012](https://arxiv.org/abs/1207.0516); [2013](https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.5637)). The authors’ recent precursor treated one \\(13\\,M_\\odot\\) magnetorotational model; this paper extends the calculation to two qualitatively different 3D explosions, including black-hole formation, and maps the flavor evolution into detector counts. The result is therefore a realistic signal-level sensitivity study, not a new bound on \\(\\mu_\\nu\\). Its reach is highly model-dependent: equal transition moments are assumed, neutrino-neutrino refraction is neglected, and only two progenitor and magnetic-field histories are sampled."}, {"id": "paper-2610-08462", "arxiv": "2610.08462", "title": "Predicting High-Energy neutrino emission from X-ray absorption in CSM-interacting SNe", "authors": "Silvia Gagliardini, Simone Dall’Osso, Dafne Guetta", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "X rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Evolving X-ray absorption and temperature reconstruct circumstellar conditions and define source-specific neutrino search windows near supernova shock breakout.", "path": "2026/10/07/index.html#paper-2610-08462", "reportDate": "7 October 2026", "searchText": "Predicting High-Energy neutrino emission from X-ray absorption in CSM-interacting SNe Silvia Gagliardini, Simone Dall’Osso, Dafne Guetta 2610.08462 Evolving X-ray absorption and temperature reconstruct circumstellar conditions and define source-specific neutrino search windows near supernova shock breakout. 2026-10-07 7 October 2026 Neutrinos X rays Multimessenger #### ★★★ 2. Predicting High-Energy neutrino emission from X-ray absorption in CSM-interacting SNe\n\n**Silvia Gagliardini, Simone Dall’Osso, Dafne Guetta**  \n**Submitted:** 6 October 2026  \n[arXiv:2610.08462](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.08462)\n\nThis paper replaces an assumed circumstellar density law with one reconstructed from observations. The time-dependent X-ray absorbing column \\(N_H(t)\\) constrains the material ahead of the shock, while the thermal X-ray temperature supplies the shock speed. Applied to SN 2023ixf and SN 2024ggi, the method reconstructs the shock-breakout epoch and predicts IceCube and KM3NeT/ARCA rates. Hadronic neutrino production is concentrated near breakout, producing a source-specific short search window with better signal-to-background than a generic weeks-long integration.\n\n##### History\n\nPrevious SN 2023ixf calculations inferred the target density from optical or bolometric light curves, or adopted wind profiles. Representative estimates found a fluence below IceCube sensitivity ([Sarmah 2023](https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.08744)) and about \\(0.04\\) IceCube muon events over 50 days in a later model ([Cosentino et al. 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.03699)). The advance is not a larger signal but a data-driven inversion of the evolving X-ray column and temperature, applied consistently to two supernovae, to define both the flux and optimal window. It remains a forecast: spherical symmetry, early X-ray obscuration, acceleration efficiency, magnetic amplification, and the non-unique conversion from fitted \\(N_H\\) to a CSM profile are important systematics."}, {"id": "paper-2610-08474", "arxiv": "2610.08474", "title": "The Potential Contribution of Radiative Supernova Remnants to the Local Cosmic Ray Spectrum", "authors": "P. Cristofari, V. H. M. Phan, S. Recchia, S. Gabici", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Cosmic rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Acceleration and reacceleration during radiative supernova-remnant evolution can lower the instantaneous efficiency needed to sustain Galactic cosmic rays.", "path": "2026/10/07/index.html#paper-2610-08474", "reportDate": "7 October 2026", "searchText": "The Potential Contribution of Radiative Supernova Remnants to the Local Cosmic Ray Spectrum P. Cristofari, V. H. M. Phan, S. Recchia, S. Gabici 2610.08474 Acceleration and reacceleration during radiative supernova-remnant evolution can lower the instantaneous efficiency needed to sustain Galactic cosmic rays. 2026-10-07 7 October 2026 Cosmic rays #### ★★★ 3. The Potential Contribution of Radiative Supernova Remnants to the Local Cosmic Ray Spectrum\n\n**P. Cristofari, V. H. M. Phan, S. Recchia, S. Gabici**  \n**Submitted:** 6 October 2026; accepted in *Astronomy & Astrophysics*  \n[arXiv:2610.08474](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.08474)\n\nThe authors follow diffusive shock acceleration and reacceleration of pre-existing Galactic cosmic rays through both the adiabatic and early radiative stages of representative remnants. If acceleration continues for even part of the radiative phase, the integrated particle energy can exceed the budget required to sustain the Galactic cosmic-ray population. They infer that the instantaneous fraction of ram pressure placed in cosmic rays should generally remain below roughly \\(1\\%\\), rather than the frequently assumed \\(5\\!-\\!10\\%\\). The radiative phase can also reshape the released spectrum up to about \\(10\\) TeV.\n\n##### History\n\nThe canonical SNR paradigm requires an order-ten-percent integrated conversion of supernova kinetic energy into cosmic rays, while earlier population models showed that source dispersion and nonlinear propagation can help reproduce spectral hardening ([Recchia and Gabici 2017](https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.01111)). This paper changes the bookkeeping by retaining acceleration and reacceleration after the Sedov stage: the longer radiative lifetime makes modest instantaneous efficiency sufficient and can otherwise overproduce the spectrum. That is a meaningful correction to standard efficiency estimates, but not evidence that radiative SNRs dominate. The calculation is spherical, confines particles until final release, uses an averaged magnetic field, and does not derive the radiative-shock spectrum self-consistently. Ion-neutral damping could cap acceleration below a TeV."}, {"id": "paper-2610-08201", "arxiv": "2610.08201", "title": "Evolution of neutrino distributions under repeated electron scattering", "authors": "Jens Chluba, Bryce Cyr, Michiru Uwabo-Niibo, Masahide Yamaguchi", "primary_category": "astro-ph.CO", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Cosmology"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "An exact neutrino–electron redistribution kernel and its Fokker–Planck limit quantify spectral relaxation, recoil, and where diffusion approximations fail.", "path": "2026/10/07/index.html#paper-2610-08201", "reportDate": "7 October 2026", "searchText": "Evolution of neutrino distributions under repeated electron scattering Jens Chluba, Bryce Cyr, Michiru Uwabo-Niibo, Masahide Yamaguchi 2610.08201 An exact neutrino–electron redistribution kernel and its Fokker–Planck limit quantify spectral relaxation, recoil, and where diffusion approximations fail. 2026-10-07 7 October 2026 Neutrinos Cosmology #### ★★☆ 4. Evolution of neutrino distributions under repeated electron scattering\n\n**Jens Chluba, Bryce Cyr, Michiru Uwabo-Niibo, Masahide Yamaguchi**  \n**Submitted:** 6 October 2026  \n[arXiv:2610.08201](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.08201)\n\nStarting from the Boltzmann collision term, the authors evolve neutrino spectra scattered repeatedly by an isotropic thermal electron bath. Their one-dimensional redistribution operator retains recoil, relativistic kinematics, and Pauli blocking. Two independent derivations give a Fokker–Planck limit—a fermionic analogue of the Kompaneets equation—and conservative numerical schemes identify where diffusion fails for narrow features. Because the weak cross section grows with energy, the high-energy tail relaxes much faster than the low-energy spectrum.\n\n##### History\n\nA neutrino Kompaneets equation was previously developed for inelastic neutrino–nucleon scattering in supernova matter ([Suwa et al. 2019](https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.05047)), and numerical decoupling calculations already include neutrino–electron collision integrals. The authors’ companion paper reduced the exact isotropic neutrino–electron kernel from five dimensions to two ([arXiv:2607.25436](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.25436)). The new work dynamically implements that kernel, compares it with the Fokker–Planck limit, and derives analytic distortion modes. The gain is methodological rather than a changed cosmological prediction. Homogeneity and isotropy are assumed, while flavor oscillations, neutrino self-interactions, nucleon scattering, and bulk motion are omitted."}, {"id": "paper-2610-07054", "arxiv": "2610.07054", "title": "First observation of the electromagnetic Dalitz decay ψ(3686) → μ⁺μ⁻η′", "authors": "BESIII Collaboration", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["Particle physics"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "BESIII observes the muonic Dalitz decay of ψ(3686) at 6.1 sigma and finds an electron–muon branching-ratio comparison consistent with lepton-flavor universality.", "path": "2026/10/07/index.html#paper-2610-07054", "reportDate": "7 October 2026", "searchText": "First observation of the electromagnetic Dalitz decay ψ(3686) → μ⁺μ⁻η′ BESIII Collaboration 2610.07054 BESIII observes the muonic Dalitz decay of ψ(3686) at 6.1 sigma and finds an electron–muon branching-ratio comparison consistent with lepton-flavor universality. 2026-10-07 7 October 2026 Particle physics #### ★★☆ 5. First observation of the electromagnetic Dalitz decay \\(\\psi(3686)\\rightarrow\\mu^+\\mu^-\\eta'\\)\n\n**BESIII Collaboration**  \n**Submitted:** 5 October 2026  \n[arXiv:2610.07054](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.07054)\n\nUsing \\(2.7124\\times10^9\\) \\(\\psi(3686)\\) decays, BESIII observes the muonic Dalitz channel at \\(6.1\\sigma\\) and measures\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal B[\\psi(3686)\\to\\mu^+\\mu^-\\eta']\n=(4.1\\pm1.0_{\\rm stat}\\pm0.4_{\\rm syst})\\times10^{-7}.\n\\]\n\nCombining it with BESIII’s electron-channel measurement gives \\(\\mathcal B_{\\mu\\mu}/\\mathcal B_{ee}=0.22\\pm0.07\\), consistent with vector-meson dominance and lepton-flavor universality.\n\n##### History\n\nBESIII previously observed \\(\\psi(3686)\\to e^+e^-\\eta'\\) with a branching fraction near \\(1.9\\times10^{-6}\\) ([arXiv:1803.09714](https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.09714)). The new result supplies the first direct electron–muon comparison in this transition and tests the timelike \\(\\psi(3686)\\eta'\\) form factor above the dimuon threshold. It confirms the vector-meson-dominance expectation rather than exposing a discrepancy. The roughly \\(25\\%\\) statistical uncertainty makes the universality test coarse, and substantially more data would be needed to resolve nontrivial \\(q^2\\) dependence."}, {"id": "paper-2610-07331", "arxiv": "2610.07331", "title": "Spectator Dark Matter and the Higgs Portal", "authors": "Stephen E. Henrich, Yann Mambrini, Keith A. Olive", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Cosmology", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "An extremely weak Higgs portal can suppress an inflationary spectator condensate without thermalizing the dark sector, with strong dependence on cosmological assumptions.", "path": "2026/10/07/index.html#paper-2610-07331", "reportDate": "7 October 2026", "searchText": "Spectator Dark Matter and the Higgs Portal Stephen E. Henrich, Yann Mambrini, Keith A. Olive 2610.07331 An extremely weak Higgs portal can suppress an inflationary spectator condensate without thermalizing the dark sector, with strong dependence on cosmological assumptions. 2026-10-07 7 October 2026 Dark matter Cosmology BSM #### ★★☆ 6. Spectator Dark Matter and the Higgs Portal\n\n**Stephen E. Henrich, Yann Mambrini, Keith A. Olive**  \n**Submitted:** 5 October 2026  \n[arXiv:2610.07331](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.07331)\n\nLight scalar spectators acquire inflationary fluctuations that can survive as an excessive dark-matter condensate. An extremely weak Higgs portal, \\(10^{-28}\\lesssim\\lambda_{HS}\\lesssim10^{-9}\\), generates a radiative self-interaction and thermal mass that make the condensate oscillate and redshift early without thermalizing it. The minimal construction favors \\(0.1\\,{\\rm eV}\\lesssim m_S\\lesssim10\\,{\\rm GeV}\\) and low reheating temperatures. If inflation did not reach stochastic equilibrium, isocurvature requires approximately \\(N\\gtrsim10^{10}\\) e-folds.\n\n##### History\n\nSelf-interacting spectator dark matter and its isocurvature constraints were treated previously ([Markkanen et al. 2019](https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.00947)), while coupling a spectator to the inflaton was known to suppress its condensate ([arXiv:2303.07359](https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.07359)). The distinct result is that a portal far below the freeze-in threshold can erase the condensate through radiatively induced self-coupling and the Standard-Model thermal bath without equilibrating the dark sector. It supplies a consistent initial condition for later freeze-in, rather than a new detectable signature. The \\(N\\gtrsim10^{10}\\) nonequilibrium branch is extreme, and the preferred region depends strongly on inflation, reheating, curvature coupling, and thermalization assumptions."}, {"id": "paper-2610-08648", "arxiv": "2610.08648", "title": "Testing Dark Matter Coscattering with the High-Energy LZ Recoil", "authors": "Sreemanti Chakraborti, Soumyajit Datta", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★☆☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A speculative singlet–doublet dark-matter model connects conversion-driven freeze-out to LZ’s isolated high-energy recoil candidate.", "path": "2026/10/07/index.html#paper-2610-08648", "reportDate": "7 October 2026", "searchText": "Testing Dark Matter Coscattering with the High-Energy LZ Recoil Sreemanti Chakraborti, Soumyajit Datta 2610.08648 A speculative singlet–doublet dark-matter model connects conversion-driven freeze-out to LZ’s isolated high-energy recoil candidate. 2026-10-07 7 October 2026 Dark matter BSM #### ★☆☆ 7. Testing Dark Matter Coscattering with the High-Energy LZ Recoil\n\n**Sreemanti Chakraborti, Soumyajit Datta**  \n**Submitted:** 6 October 2026  \n[arXiv:2610.08648](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.08648)\n\nThe authors interpret LZ’s isolated \\(248\\)-keV nuclear-recoil candidate using singlet–doublet fermion dark matter, a light dark photon, and an approximately \\(100\\)-keV inelastic splitting. Slow conversion from the mostly singlet state into the doublet controls freeze-out, while the light mediator enhances the same endothermic transition in xenon. For \\(m_{A'}=250\\) MeV, the LZ-sensitive band covers nearly all of the relic-compatible conversion-driven region in their scan, selecting masses around \\(0.75\\!-\\!1.6\\) TeV.\n\n##### History\n\nCoscattering was introduced as a fourth thermal freeze-out mechanism when conversion, rather than annihilation, is the bottleneck ([D’Agnolo, Pappadopulo and Ruderman 2017](https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.08450)); conversion-driven freeze-out in coannihilating sectors was developed independently at the same time ([Garny et al. 2017](https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.09292)). The novelty is to make the conversion fixing the abundance observable as an inelastic recoil, instead of fitting the LZ event with an unrelated interaction. The interpretation remains speculative because it rests on one candidate near the edge of an extended window. It is also sensitive to backgrounds, the high-velocity halo tail, mediator constraints, nuclear response, and momentum dependence in the conversion Boltzmann equation."}, {"id": "paper-2610-07066", "arxiv": "2610.07066", "title": "Lunar radio emission from dark photon dark matter", "authors": "Ariel Arza", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★☆☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The lunar regolith–vacuum interface is proposed as a natural dark-photon conversion surface, with sensitivity dependent on surface properties and radio backgrounds.", "path": "2026/10/07/index.html#paper-2610-07066", "reportDate": "7 October 2026", "searchText": "Lunar radio emission from dark photon dark matter Ariel Arza 2610.07066 The lunar regolith–vacuum interface is proposed as a natural dark-photon conversion surface, with sensitivity dependent on surface properties and radio backgrounds. 2026-10-07 7 October 2026 Dark matter BSM #### ★☆☆ 8. Lunar radio emission from dark photon dark matter\n\n**Ariel Arza**  \n**Submitted:** 5 October 2026  \n[arXiv:2610.07066](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.07066)\n\nDark-photon dark matter crossing a dielectric discontinuity can convert coherently into ordinary radio emission. This forecast treats the lunar regolith–vacuum boundary as an enormous natural conversion surface and calculates signals observable by ALMA, SKA, FAST, and the Green Bank Telescope.\n\n##### History\n\nLaboratory dish antennas have long exploited hidden-photon conversion at conducting boundaries ([Horns et al. 2012](https://arxiv.org/abs/1212.2970)), and broadband experiments have demonstrated the method experimentally ([BREAD 2024](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.132.131004)). No close prior study was found in the literature checked for using the Moon’s dielectric surface itself as the conversion interface; that remote, passive “planet-sized dish” is the specific novelty. This is a sensitivity forecast, not an observation. Lunar dielectric heterogeneity, roughness, loss, coherence across the visible surface, radio backgrounds, and calibration determine whether the idealized area advantage survives."}, {"id": "paper-2610-05980", "arxiv": "2610.05980", "title": "Search for Diffuse Supernova Neutrino Background of All Flavors with PandaX-4T", "authors": "Yingxin Zhang and the PandaX Collaboration", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Particle physics"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "PandaX-4T sets an independent all-flavor diffuse-supernova-neutrino limit with coherent nuclear scattering; existing LZ bounds remain stronger.", "path": "2026/10/06/index.html#paper-2610-05980", "reportDate": "6 October 2026", "searchText": "Search for Diffuse Supernova Neutrino Background of All Flavors with PandaX-4T Yingxin Zhang and the PandaX Collaboration 2610.05980 PandaX-4T sets an independent all-flavor diffuse-supernova-neutrino limit with coherent nuclear scattering; existing LZ bounds remain stronger. 2026-10-06 6 October 2026 Neutrinos Particle physics #### ★★★ 1. Search for Diffuse Supernova Neutrino Background of All Flavors with PandaX-4T\n\n**Yingxin Zhang and the PandaX Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2610.05980](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.05980) — submitted 5 October 2026  \n*New experimental limit*\n\nPandaX-4T searches for the diffuse supernova neutrino background through coherent elastic neutrino–nucleus scattering, an approximately flavor-blind channel. In \\(1.54\\ {\\rm tonne\\,yr}\\), the surviving events are consistent with background. The 90%-confidence limit on each heavy-lepton flavor above \\(19.3\\) MeV is\n\n\\[\n\\Phi_{\\nu_x}< (2.27-2.43)\\times10^3\\ {\\rm cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}.\n\\]\n\n\n##### History\n\nSuper-Kamiokande previously obtained indirect heavy-flavor limits around \\(10^3\\ {\\rm cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}\\) from neutrino–electron scattering ([Lunardini and Peres 2008](https://doi.org/10.1088/1475-7516/2008/08/033)). More directly comparable, LZ has already reported a CEνNS limit of \\(686-826\\ {\\rm cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}\\) per heavy flavor ([arXiv:2412.15886](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.15886)). PandaX is therefore neither the first public CEνNS DSNB analysis nor the strongest present bound, despite the abstract’s “first search” wording. Its value is an independent full-collaboration demonstration of the neutral-current, all-flavor method. Present sensitivity remains far from realistic DSNB fluxes: the benchmark expectation is only about \\(8\\times10^{-4}\\) event."}, {"id": "paper-2610-05147", "arxiv": "2610.05147", "title": "Limits on the size of electron density fluctuations in the early universe and implications for the Hubble tension", "authors": "Gabriel P. Lynch, Jens Chluba, Richard Battye, Geoffrey Vasil", "primary_category": "astro-ph.CO", "tags": ["Cosmology"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "CMB constraints include both accelerated recombination and modified Thomson transport, weakening electron-inhomogeneity explanations of the Hubble tension.", "path": "2026/10/06/index.html#paper-2610-05147", "reportDate": "6 October 2026", "searchText": "Limits on the size of electron density fluctuations in the early universe and implications for the Hubble tension Gabriel P. Lynch, Jens Chluba, Richard Battye, Geoffrey Vasil 2610.05147 CMB constraints include both accelerated recombination and modified Thomson transport, weakening electron-inhomogeneity explanations of the Hubble tension. 2026-10-06 6 October 2026 Cosmology #### ★★★ 2. Limits on the size of electron density fluctuations in the early universe and implications for the Hubble tension\n\n**Gabriel P. Lynch, Jens Chluba, Richard Battye, Geoffrey Vasil**  \n[arXiv:2610.05147](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.05147) — submitted 4 October 2026  \n*New cosmological constraints and methodological advance*\n\nSmall-scale electron-density inhomogeneities accelerate recombination, but correlated structure also reduces the effective Thomson-scattering rate and increases diffusion damping. Incorporating both effects into a modified Boltzmann code, the authors find no evidence for fluctuations in Planck, ACT DR6 or SPT-3G D1. The variance is below \\(0.21\\) at 95% confidence, tightening to \\(0.033\\) for 2-Mpc coherence.\n\nWith a distance-ladder prior, Planck alone permits\n\n\\[\nH_0=71.1\\pm0.7\\ {\\rm km\\,s^{-1}\\,Mpc^{-1}},\n\\]\n\nbut ACT and SPT break the degeneracy and give \\(69.3\\pm0.5\\ {\\rm km\\,s^{-1}\\,Mpc^{-1}}\\), with no preference over ΛCDM.\n\n##### History\n\nEarlier analyses principally treated accelerated recombination, including primordial-magnetic-field realizations ([Jedamzik and Abel 2013](https://doi.org/10.1088/1475-7516/2013/10/050); [Lee and Ali-Haïmoud 2021](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.104.103509)). The modified-transport term was derived recently ([arXiv:2505.22242](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.22242)); this paper supplies the first joint data constraints including both imprints. It substantially weakens the case that unresolved electron structure alone resolves the Hubble tension. The separate-universe treatment and phenomenological variance/coherence model still require model-dependent translation from a concrete magnetohydrodynamic source."}, {"id": "paper-2610-04782", "arxiv": "2610.04782", "title": "Search for Higgs boson compositeness effects in high-mass dilepton final states at √s = 13 and 13.6 TeV", "authors": "CMS Collaboration", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["BSM", "Particle physics"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "CMS finds no deviation in high-mass dilepton spectra and constrains composite-Higgs scenarios with combined Run-2 and Run-3 data.", "path": "2026/10/06/index.html#paper-2610-04782", "reportDate": "6 October 2026", "searchText": "Search for Higgs boson compositeness effects in high-mass dilepton final states at √s = 13 and 13.6 TeV CMS Collaboration 2610.04782 CMS finds no deviation in high-mass dilepton spectra and constrains composite-Higgs scenarios with combined Run-2 and Run-3 data. 2026-10-06 6 October 2026 BSM Particle physics #### ★★★ 3. Search for Higgs boson compositeness effects in high-mass dilepton final states at \\(\\sqrt{s}=13\\) and \\(13.6\\) TeV\n\n**CMS Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2610.04782](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.04782) — submitted 3 October 2026  \n*Run-2+Run-3 search; journal paper following a preliminary result*\n\nCMS fits high-mass dielectron and dimuon spectra in \\(207\\ {\\rm fb^{-1}}\\), searching for broad vector resonances and nonresonant electroweak effects associated with composite-Higgs scenarios. No deviation is found. The one-parameter 95%-confidence intervals are\n\n\\[\n-3.9\\times10^{-4}<\\mathcal Y<3.5\\times10^{-4},\n\\qquad\n-2.1\\times10^{-4}<\\mathcal W<1.6\\times10^{-4}.\n\\]\n\nLarge resonance widths are treated explicitly.\n\n##### History\n\nCMS’s previous lepton-plus-missing-momentum analysis supplied the first LHC determination of \\(\\mathcal W\\), but had little direct leverage on \\(\\mathcal Y\\) ([arXiv:2202.06075](https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.06075)). The neutral-current spectrum adds the complementary hypercharge direction; combining the two gives CMS’s strongest \\(\\mathcal W,\\mathcal Y\\) limits to date. The paper formalizes the [EXO-25-001 result released preliminarily on 6 July](https://cms-results.web.cern.ch/cms-results/public-results/preliminary-results/EXO-25-001/index.html), so this is not an entirely new announcement. The EFT interpretation requires new states above the fitted mass range; broad-resonance exclusions remain coupling- and width-dependent."}, {"id": "paper-2610-06633", "arxiv": "2610.06633", "title": "Pair echo model of CTAO/LST-1 signal from GRB 221009A", "authors": "Andrii Neronov, Ievgen Vovk, Teneman Keita, Davide Miceli", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A joint LHAASO–LST-1 cascade interpretation of GRB 221009A gives conditional intergalactic-field estimates without establishing a pair-echo detection.", "path": "2026/10/06/index.html#paper-2610-06633", "reportDate": "6 October 2026", "searchText": "Pair echo model of CTAO/LST-1 signal from GRB 221009A Andrii Neronov, Ievgen Vovk, Teneman Keita, Davide Miceli 2610.06633 A joint LHAASO–LST-1 cascade interpretation of GRB 221009A gives conditional intergalactic-field estimates without establishing a pair-echo detection. 2026-10-06 6 October 2026 Gamma rays Multimessenger #### ★★☆ 4. Pair echo model of CTAO/LST-1 signal from GRB 221009A\n\n**Andrii Neronov, Ievgen Vovk, Teneman Keita, Davide Miceli**  \n[arXiv:2610.06633](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.06633) — submitted 5 October 2026  \n*Interpretive model, not a pair-echo detection*\n\nCombining LHAASO’s early VHE spectrum with an LST-1 excess measured 1.33 days later, the authors find that a cascade echo can reproduce the late signal for\n\n\\[\n\\log_{10}(B/{\\rm G})=-16.2\\pm0.7\n\\]\n\nwhen \\(\\lambda_B\\gtrsim20\\) kpc. At shorter coherence length, the inferred field scales as \\(\\sqrt{20\\,{\\rm kpc}/\\lambda_B}\\); short-coherence phase-transition relic fields would lie around \\(10^{-14}-10^{-12}\\) G.\n\n##### History\n\nFermi-LAT non-detections or delayed GeV emission from this burst had already been used to constrain intergalactic fields ([arXiv:2306.05347](https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05347); [arXiv:2306.07672](https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.07672); [arXiv:2306.05970](https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05970)). The advance is the joint LHAASO–LST-1 interpretation at hundreds of GeV and day-scale delay. It is not a magnetic-field measurement: the LST-1 excess was \\(4.1\\sigma\\), later observations were non-detections, and intrinsic afterglow emission cannot be separated from a cascade. The inferred field is conditional on the intrinsic spectrum, background light, jet geometry and cascade model."}, {"id": "paper-2610-04796", "arxiv": "2610.04796", "title": "Jets with Streaks: the Global Dynamics and Structure of Striped Poynting Flux Dominated Jets", "authors": "Ho-Sang Chan, Anna Chashkina, Omer Bromberg, Gregory R. Werner, Hayk Hakobyan", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Global axisymmetric simulations connect alternating disk magnetic flux to striped relativistic jets and magnetic dissipation.", "path": "2026/10/06/index.html#paper-2610-04796", "reportDate": "6 October 2026", "searchText": "Jets with Streaks: the Global Dynamics and Structure of Striped Poynting Flux Dominated Jets Ho-Sang Chan, Anna Chashkina, Omer Bromberg, Gregory R. Werner, Hayk Hakobyan 2610.04796 Global axisymmetric simulations connect alternating disk magnetic flux to striped relativistic jets and magnetic dissipation. 2026-10-06 6 October 2026 Cosmic rays Compact objects #### ★★☆ 5. Jets with Streaks: the Global Dynamics and Structure of Striped Poynting Flux Dominated Jets\n\n**Ho-Sang Chan, Anna Chashkina, Omer Bromberg, Gregory R. Werner, Hayk Hakobyan**  \n[arXiv:2610.04796](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.04796) — submitted 3 October 2026  \n*Global-simulation advance*\n\nAxisymmetric GRMHD simulations follow alternating-polarity magnetic loops from accretion through black-hole jet launching and dissipation out to \\(10^4r_g\\). Coherent loops formed within roughly \\(50r_g\\) launch nested relativistic mini-jets. Opposite polarities form current sheets, dissipating a few tens of percent of the observer-frame Poynting power inside the simulated domain.\n\n##### History\n\nAnalytic striped-jet models prescribed stripe distributions and reconnection laws ([Drenkhahn 2002](https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0112509); [Giannios and Uzdensky 2019](https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/sty3502)), while earlier simulations established that alternating disk flux can launch stripes ([arXiv:2212.06058](https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.06058)). The advance is a launch-to-dissipation causal picture with a structured core/sheath and a physical loop-survival criterion. Quantitative reconnection and particle acceleration remain uncertain because the calculation is 2D ideal GRMHD: dissipation is numerically regulated and kinetic, radiative and three-dimensional effects are absent."}, {"id": "paper-2610-03881", "arxiv": "2610.03881", "title": "The Virgo Cluster as a Case Study for Ancient Cosmic Ray Halos in Radio and X-rays", "authors": "Philip F. Hopkins, Emily M. Silich, Jack Sayers, Sam B. Ponnada, Isabel S. Sands", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "X rays"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A fossil-lepton model links Virgo’s radio halo, soft X-rays and cooling deficit, with uncertainty dominated by the unobserved low-energy electron population.", "path": "2026/10/06/index.html#paper-2610-03881", "reportDate": "6 October 2026", "searchText": "The Virgo Cluster as a Case Study for Ancient Cosmic Ray Halos in Radio and X-rays Philip F. Hopkins, Emily M. Silich, Jack Sayers, Sam B. Ponnada, Isabel S. Sands 2610.03881 A fossil-lepton model links Virgo’s radio halo, soft X-rays and cooling deficit, with uncertainty dominated by the unobserved low-energy electron population. 2026-10-06 6 October 2026 Cosmic rays X rays #### ★★☆ 6. The Virgo Cluster as a Case Study for Ancient Cosmic Ray Halos in Radio and X-rays\n\n**Philip F. Hopkins, Emily M. Silich, Jack Sayers, Sam B. Ponnada, Isabel S. Sands**  \n[arXiv:2610.03881](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.03881) — submitted 2 October 2026  \n*Multiwavelength physical interpretation*\n\nVirgo’s X-ray continuum implies a cooling rate above \\(10\\,M_\\odot\\,{\\rm yr^{-1}}\\), yet little cold gas or star formation appears outside M87’s nucleus. Combining the aged radio halo with X-ray spectra and Faraday limits, the authors argue that fossil cosmic-ray leptons within approximately 40 kpc provide appreciable pressure, heat the gas through Coulomb interactions and inverse-Compton-scatter CMB photons into soft X-rays. This could reduce the true cooling requirement and bias central X-ray metallicities low.\n\n##### History\n\nCosmic-ray heating has long been considered for cool-core clusters, and gamma-ray data constrain M87’s highest-energy particles ([arXiv:0910.3565](https://arxiv.org/abs/0910.3565)). A companion study recently applied a related fossil-lepton framework to Perseus ([arXiv:2605.08349](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.08349)). This paper extends it to an X-ray-weak cool core and links radio aging, Faraday fields, soft X-rays and metallicity bias. It is not a direct cosmic-ray-pressure measurement: the crucial low-energy electron distribution is extrapolated and multiphase-gas or calibration explanations remain viable."}, {"id": "paper-2610-06151", "arxiv": "2610.06151", "title": "Revisiting neutrino-assisted self-interacting dark matter", "authors": "Luqi Wang, Shu-Yuan Guo, Xuewen Liu, Bin Zhu", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Neutrinos", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Mediator-resolved calculations close the neutrino-assisted self-interaction window in two thermal scalar dark-matter models.", "path": "2026/10/06/index.html#paper-2610-06151", "reportDate": "6 October 2026", "searchText": "Revisiting neutrino-assisted self-interacting dark matter Luqi Wang, Shu-Yuan Guo, Xuewen Liu, Bin Zhu 2610.06151 Mediator-resolved calculations close the neutrino-assisted self-interaction window in two thermal scalar dark-matter models. 2026-10-06 6 October 2026 Dark matter Neutrinos BSM #### ★★☆ 7. Revisiting neutrino-assisted self-interacting dark matter\n\n**Luqi Wang, Shu-Yuan Guo, Xuewen Liu, Bin Zhu**  \n[arXiv:2610.06151](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.06151) — submitted 5 October 2026  \n*Negative phenomenological result*\n\nTwo scalar-mediator realizations are tested using mediator-resolved potentials, numerical scattering, thermal freeze-out and current constraints. In the \\(t\\)-channel model, loop-induced neutrino forces are negligible wherever the relic abundance and sizable scattering coexist. In the \\(s\\)-channel case, the largest cross section is about\n\n\\[\n6.2\\times10^{-13}\\ {\\rm cm^2\\,g^{-1}},\n\\]\n\nover eleven orders of magnitude below the usual \\(0.1\\ {\\rm cm^2\\,g^{-1}}\\) SIDM benchmark. Sommerfeld enhancement and early kinetic decoupling can still alter the relic abundance by up to 50%.\n\n##### History\n\nEarlier “neutrino as the dark force” models proposed astrophysically significant neutrino-mediated scattering ([arXiv:2106.08339](https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.08339)), with subsequent work clarifying the short-distance potential and Sommerfeld behavior ([arXiv:2112.03060](https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.03060); [arXiv:2203.05455](https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.05455)). Resolving the mediator, imposing current bounds and enforcing thermal abundance closes the apparent SIDM window in these two attractive scalar completions. The no-go result does not encompass asymmetric or nonthermal dark matter, repulsive forces or different ultraviolet completions."}, {"id": "paper-2610-06159", "arxiv": "2610.06159", "title": "Learning Astrophysical Uncertainties in Dark Matter Direct Detection with Simulation-Based Inference", "authors": "Felix Kahlhoefer, Niklas Reus", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Particle physics"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Detector-level simulation and neural ratio estimation marginalize dark-matter halo uncertainties in a direct-detection proof of concept.", "path": "2026/10/06/index.html#paper-2610-06159", "reportDate": "6 October 2026", "searchText": "Learning Astrophysical Uncertainties in Dark Matter Direct Detection with Simulation-Based Inference Felix Kahlhoefer, Niklas Reus 2610.06159 Detector-level simulation and neural ratio estimation marginalize dark-matter halo uncertainties in a direct-detection proof of concept. 2026-10-06 6 October 2026 Dark matter Particle physics #### ★★☆ 8. Learning Astrophysical Uncertainties in Dark Matter Direct Detection with Simulation-Based Inference\n\n**Felix Kahlhoefer, Niklas Reus**  \n[arXiv:2610.06159](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.06159) — submitted 5 October 2026  \n*Proof-of-concept methodology*\n\nNeural ratio estimators are trained on realistic XENONnT scintillation and ionization simulations. Mixing halo models during training implicitly marginalizes velocity-distribution uncertainties. Mixed training corrects deliberately extreme Standard-Halo-Model misspecification, while realistic SHM, SHM++ and Large-Magellanic-Cloud variants produce only modest low-mass differences. The expected exclusion agrees with XENONnT sensitivity after accounting for Bayesian/frequentist differences.\n\n##### History\n\nSimulation-based inference had previously been demonstrated for direct-detection parameter reconstruction ([arXiv:1810.09930](https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.09930)); XENONnT normally uses a profile likelihood with explicit nuisance parameters ([arXiv:2303.14729](https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.14729)). The advance is the combination of detector-level simulation, amortized likelihood-ratio inference, astrophysical-model mixing and calibration checks. This is not a new experimental limit. The proof of concept uses a simplified flat electronic-recoil background, omits position information and bins observations coarsely; collaboration-grade backgrounds and coverage tests are still required."}, {"id": "paper-2610-04048", "arxiv": "2610.04048", "title": "Stars Would Remember a Recent Gravitational Transition: Testing G-step Resolutions of the Hubble Tension with Red Dwarfs", "authors": "Jeremy Sakstein, Harry Desmond", "primary_category": "astro-ph.CO", "tags": ["Cosmology", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Red-dwarf radii constrain proposed recent steps in Newton’s constant, with sensitivity to one influential binary and systematic radius uncertainties.", "path": "2026/10/06/index.html#paper-2610-04048", "reportDate": "6 October 2026", "searchText": "Stars Would Remember a Recent Gravitational Transition: Testing G-step Resolutions of the Hubble Tension with Red Dwarfs Jeremy Sakstein, Harry Desmond 2610.04048 Red-dwarf radii constrain proposed recent steps in Newton’s constant, with sensitivity to one influential binary and systematic radius uncertainties. 2026-10-06 6 October 2026 Cosmology BSM #### ★★☆ 9. Stars Would Remember a Recent Gravitational Transition: Testing G-step Resolutions of the Hubble Tension with Red Dwarfs\n\n**Jeremy Sakstein, Harry Desmond**  \n[arXiv:2610.04048](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.04048) — submitted 2 October 2026  \n*Astrophysical test of a speculative cosmological proposal*\n\nFully convective red dwarfs have thermal timescales comparable to the proposed 73–130-Myr lookback times of “G-step” Hubble-tension models. Comparing evolved stellar models with eight detached-eclipsing-binary radii, the authors exclude a proposed 4% change in \\(G\\) at 73 Myr at \\(3.1\\sigma\\), and an 11% change at 130 Myr at \\(4.0\\sigma\\).\n\n##### History\n\nEarlier G-step proposals focused on supernova calibration and background cosmology rather than stellar populations with relaxation times spanning the transition. No close prior study was found in the literature checked for this specific stellar-memory test. The paper therefore supplies an independent constraint. It is not a generic limit on time-varying gravity: the sample has eight stars, J2343+29 carries substantial weight, and omitting it reduces the quoted significances to approximately \\(2.45\\sigma\\) and \\(1.90\\sigma\\). A 1% systematic radius floor also weakens the result."}, {"id": "paper-2610-03143", "arxiv": "2610.03143", "title": "First observation of electron antineutrinos from nuclear reactors at Super-Kamiokande", "authors": "K. Abe et al. — Super-Kamiokande Collaboration", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Oscillations", "Particle physics"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Super-Kamiokande observes reactor antineutrinos at approximately six sigma with gadolinium neutron tagging, enabling an internal comparison with its solar-neutrino measurements.", "path": "2026/10/05/index.html#paper-2610-03143", "reportDate": "5 October 2026", "searchText": "First observation of electron antineutrinos from nuclear reactors at Super-Kamiokande K. Abe et al. — Super-Kamiokande Collaboration 2610.03143 Super-Kamiokande observes reactor antineutrinos at approximately six sigma with gadolinium neutron tagging, enabling an internal comparison with its solar-neutrino measurements. 2026-10-05 5 October 2026 Neutrinos Oscillations Particle physics #### ★★★ 1. First observation of electron antineutrinos from nuclear reactors at Super-Kamiokande\n\n**K. Abe et al. — Super-Kamiokande Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2610.03143](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.03143)  \n**New experimental result**\n\nUsing **411.52 live days** from two gadolinium-loaded operating phases, Super-Kamiokande observes reactor antineutrinos through inverse beta decay with delayed neutron tagging. The prompt-energy spectrum and time-dependent rates favor an oscillated reactor contribution over the no-reactor hypothesis at approximately **\\(6\\sigma\\)**.\n\nThe reactor-only fit gives\n\n\\[\n\\sin^2\\theta_{12}=0.500\\pm0.155,\\qquad\n\\Delta m_{21}^{2}\n=\\left(9.08^{+0.55}_{-0.54}\\right)\\times10^{-5}\\ {\\rm eV^2}.\n\\]\n\nA joint comparison with Super-K’s solar-neutrino measurement yields a parameter-goodness-of-fit discrepancy of **\\(1.9\\sigma\\)**—not significant evidence for different neutrino and antineutrino oscillation parameters. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.03143v1))\n\n##### History\n\nThis is the first reactor-antineutrino observation **in Super-Kamiokande**, not the first in a water Cherenkov detector. [SNO+ previously reported \\(3.5\\sigma\\) evidence using pure water](https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.14154), with its nearest reactors approximately 240 km away. The new result establishes the practical reactor sensitivity of SK-Gd and enables a solar–reactor comparison within the same detector. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.14154))\n\nThe principal advance is therefore experimental capability and an internally comparable dataset. The reactor mixing-angle uncertainty remains substantial, and the analysis depends on accidental-background estimation, neutron-tagging efficiency, and reactor-spectrum modeling. The quoted solar–reactor mismatch does not support a CPT-violation claim. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.03143v1))"}, {"id": "paper-v4641-sgr", "arxiv": "2610.02966", "title": "XMM-Newton Resolves Parsec-scale X-ray Jets in the PeVatron Microquasar V4641 Sgr", "authors": "Naomi Tsuji, Caterina Tresoldi, Kaya Mori, Hiromasa Suzuki, Matthew Lundy, Dmitry Khangulyan, et al.", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["X rays", "Gamma rays", "Compact objects", "Cosmic rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "XMM-Newton resolves extended nonthermal X-ray structures around V4641 Sgr, favoring localized electron acceleration while leaving a hadronic gamma-ray component possible.", "path": "2026/10/05/index.html#paper-v4641-sgr", "reportDate": "5 October 2026", "searchText": "XMM-Newton Resolves Parsec-scale X-ray Jets in the PeVatron Microquasar V4641 Sgr Naomi Tsuji, Caterina Tresoldi, Kaya Mori, Hiromasa Suzuki, Matthew Lundy, Dmitry Khangulyan, et al. 2610.02966 XMM-Newton resolves extended nonthermal X-ray structures around V4641 Sgr, favoring localized electron acceleration while leaving a hadronic gamma-ray component possible. 2026-10-05 5 October 2026 X rays Gamma rays Compact objects Cosmic rays #### ★★★ 2. V4641 Sgr: two complementary detections of extended X-ray emission\n\n**XMM-Newton Resolves Parsec-scale X-ray Jets in the PeVatron Microquasar V4641 Sgr**  \n**Naomi Tsuji, Caterina Tresoldi, Kaya Mori, Hiromasa Suzuki, Matthew Lundy, Dmitry Khangulyan, et al.**  \n[arXiv:2610.02966](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02966)\n\n**Candidate X-ray Counterparts of Ultra-High-Energy Emission of the Galactic Microquasar V4641 Sgr Revealed by Einstein Probe**  \n**Yi-Heng Chi, Shao-Qiang Xi, Ping Zhou, Hua Feng, Ruo-Yu Liu, Songzhan Chen, et al.**  \n[arXiv:2610.02962](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02962)  \n**New observations; emission mechanism remains non-unique**\n\nThe XMM-Newton study resolves northern and southern X-ray structures extending tens of parsecs from the binary, including knots approximately **20 pc** away. Their spectra are consistent with nonthermal emission, with a representative photon index\n\n\\[\n\\Gamma_X=1.9\\pm0.1_{\\rm stat}\\pm0.5_{\\rm sys}.\n\\]\n\nA joint synchrotron/inverse-Compton interpretation favors magnetic fields around **\\(3\\!-\\!5\\,\\mu{\\rm G}\\)**. The compact X-ray structures suggest localized acceleration at jet termination or recollimation regions. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02966v1))\n\nEinstein Probe independently identifies diffuse knots approximately **20–40 pc** from the source. Their softer X-ray spectra, \\(\\Gamma_X\\sim2\\!-\\!3\\), and different extraction regions give approximately **\\(1\\!-\\!2\\,\\mu{\\rm G}\\)** in one-zone leptonic fits. These values should not be averaged with the XMM result: they come from different regions and modeling assumptions. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02962v1))\n\n##### History\n\n[HAWC’s 2024 discovery](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.16117) established exceptionally hard, extended gamma-ray emission reaching approximately 200 TeV, but could not determine whether electrons or nuclei dominated. A subsequent [hadronic propagation model by Neronov, Oikonomou and Semikoz](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.111.103025) explained the large-scale gamma-ray structure through cosmic rays escaping along Galactic magnetic-field lines and predicted a potential neutrino counterpart. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.16117))\n\nThe new X-ray measurements provide the missing spatially resolved evidence for energetic electrons and constrain their magnetic environment. They strengthen leptonic interpretations but **do not establish that all the gamma rays are leptonic**, particularly where gamma-ray emission extends beyond the X-ray knots. Conversely, calling the system a PeVatron does not by itself prove acceleration of PeV protons. Distinguishing localized synchrotron-emitting electrons from a more extended hadronic component now requires joint, spatially resolved modeling rather than only a broadband spectral fit. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02966v1))"}, {"id": "paper-v4641-sgr", "arxiv": "2610.02962", "title": "Candidate X-ray Counterparts of Ultra-High-Energy Emission of the Galactic Microquasar V4641 Sgr Revealed by Einstein Probe", "authors": "Yi-Heng Chi, Shao-Qiang Xi, Ping Zhou, Hua Feng, Ruo-Yu Liu, Songzhan Chen, et al.", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["X rays", "Gamma rays", "Compact objects", "Cosmic rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Einstein Probe independently finds diffuse X-ray knots around V4641 Sgr; its spatial regions and magnetic-field estimates complement the XMM-Newton study.", "path": "2026/10/05/index.html#paper-v4641-sgr", "reportDate": "5 October 2026", "searchText": "Candidate X-ray Counterparts of Ultra-High-Energy Emission of the Galactic Microquasar V4641 Sgr Revealed by Einstein Probe Yi-Heng Chi, Shao-Qiang Xi, Ping Zhou, Hua Feng, Ruo-Yu Liu, Songzhan Chen, et al. 2610.02962 Einstein Probe independently finds diffuse X-ray knots around V4641 Sgr; its spatial regions and magnetic-field estimates complement the XMM-Newton study. 2026-10-05 5 October 2026 X rays Gamma rays Compact objects Cosmic rays #### ★★★ 2. V4641 Sgr: two complementary detections of extended X-ray emission\n\n**XMM-Newton Resolves Parsec-scale X-ray Jets in the PeVatron Microquasar V4641 Sgr**  \n**Naomi Tsuji, Caterina Tresoldi, Kaya Mori, Hiromasa Suzuki, Matthew Lundy, Dmitry Khangulyan, et al.**  \n[arXiv:2610.02966](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02966)\n\n**Candidate X-ray Counterparts of Ultra-High-Energy Emission of the Galactic Microquasar V4641 Sgr Revealed by Einstein Probe**  \n**Yi-Heng Chi, Shao-Qiang Xi, Ping Zhou, Hua Feng, Ruo-Yu Liu, Songzhan Chen, et al.**  \n[arXiv:2610.02962](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02962)  \n**New observations; emission mechanism remains non-unique**\n\nThe XMM-Newton study resolves northern and southern X-ray structures extending tens of parsecs from the binary, including knots approximately **20 pc** away. Their spectra are consistent with nonthermal emission, with a representative photon index\n\n\\[\n\\Gamma_X=1.9\\pm0.1_{\\rm stat}\\pm0.5_{\\rm sys}.\n\\]\n\nA joint synchrotron/inverse-Compton interpretation favors magnetic fields around **\\(3\\!-\\!5\\,\\mu{\\rm G}\\)**. The compact X-ray structures suggest localized acceleration at jet termination or recollimation regions. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02966v1))\n\nEinstein Probe independently identifies diffuse knots approximately **20–40 pc** from the source. Their softer X-ray spectra, \\(\\Gamma_X\\sim2\\!-\\!3\\), and different extraction regions give approximately **\\(1\\!-\\!2\\,\\mu{\\rm G}\\)** in one-zone leptonic fits. These values should not be averaged with the XMM result: they come from different regions and modeling assumptions. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02962v1))\n\n##### History\n\n[HAWC’s 2024 discovery](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.16117) established exceptionally hard, extended gamma-ray emission reaching approximately 200 TeV, but could not determine whether electrons or nuclei dominated. A subsequent [hadronic propagation model by Neronov, Oikonomou and Semikoz](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.111.103025) explained the large-scale gamma-ray structure through cosmic rays escaping along Galactic magnetic-field lines and predicted a potential neutrino counterpart. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.16117))\n\nThe new X-ray measurements provide the missing spatially resolved evidence for energetic electrons and constrain their magnetic environment. They strengthen leptonic interpretations but **do not establish that all the gamma rays are leptonic**, particularly where gamma-ray emission extends beyond the X-ray knots. Conversely, calling the system a PeVatron does not by itself prove acceleration of PeV protons. Distinguishing localized synchrotron-emitting electrons from a more extended hadronic component now requires joint, spatially resolved modeling rather than only a broadband spectral fit. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02966v1))"}, {"id": "paper-2610-03427", "arxiv": "2610.03427", "title": "Guide field effects on particle acceleration in three-dimensional relativistic magnetic reconnection", "authors": "Despina Karavola, Lorenzo Sironi, Maria Petropoulou", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Three-dimensional pair-plasma simulations show that a fast reconnection acceleration channel survives guide fields while particle participation and acceleration efficiency decline.", "path": "2026/10/05/index.html#paper-2610-03427", "reportDate": "5 October 2026", "searchText": "Guide field effects on particle acceleration in three-dimensional relativistic magnetic reconnection Despina Karavola, Lorenzo Sironi, Maria Petropoulou 2610.03427 Three-dimensional pair-plasma simulations show that a fast reconnection acceleration channel survives guide fields while particle participation and acceleration efficiency decline. 2026-10-05 5 October 2026 Cosmic rays Compact objects #### ★★★ 3. Guide field effects on particle acceleration in three-dimensional relativistic magnetic reconnection\n\n**Despina Karavola, Lorenzo Sironi, Maria Petropoulou**  \n[arXiv:2610.03427](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.03427)  \n**New kinetic-simulation result**\n\nThree-dimensional particle-in-cell simulations examine relativistic pair-plasma reconnection at magnetization \\(\\sigma=10\\), varying the guide field from zero to a strength comparable to the reconnecting field:\n\n\\[\n0\\leq B_g/B_0\\leq1.\n\\]\n\nThe rapid acceleration channel unique to three dimensions survives even with a substantial guide field: particles escaping magnetic flux ropes can sample the large-scale upstream reconnection electric field. However, increasing \\(B_g\\) reduces the participating population, steepens the spectrum, lowers the cutoff, and increases directional anisotropy. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.03427))\n\n##### History\n\n[Zhang, Sironi and Giannios (2021)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.00009) identified this three-dimensional escape-and-acceleration mechanism for weak guide fields. Their escaping particles gained energy approximately linearly with time, unlike the slower growth of particles trapped in two-dimensional plasmoids. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.00009))\n\nThe new paper establishes how robust that particular mechanism is when the magnetic geometry becomes less favorable. It does **not** newly establish the general fact that guide fields can soften reconnection spectra. Its more specific contribution is separating the survival of a fast acceleration channel from the declining efficiency with which particles enter it.\n\nThe simulations use a pair plasma, one principal magnetization, and no radiative cooling. Their results cannot yet be translated directly into an ion acceleration efficiency or a universal maximum energy for radiatively bright astrophysical jets. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.03427v1))"}, {"id": "paper-2610-02332", "arxiv": "2610.02332", "title": "Neutrino flavor instabilities intermediate between slow and fast", "authors": "Nicolás Viaux, Lucas Johns", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Oscillations"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A linear-stability study maps the slow–fast flavor-instability crossover and shows why homogeneous modes alone can miss the fastest growth.", "path": "2026/10/05/index.html#paper-2610-02332", "reportDate": "5 October 2026", "searchText": "Neutrino flavor instabilities intermediate between slow and fast Nicolás Viaux, Lucas Johns 2610.02332 A linear-stability study maps the slow–fast flavor-instability crossover and shows why homogeneous modes alone can miss the fastest growth. 2026-10-05 5 October 2026 Neutrinos Oscillations #### ★★☆ 4. Neutrino flavor instabilities intermediate between slow and fast\n\n**Nicolás Viaux, Lucas Johns**  \n[arXiv:2610.02332](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02332)  \n**New linear-stability analysis**\n\nThe usual distinction between “slow” vacuum-assisted and “fast” neutrino-self-interaction-driven flavor instabilities becomes less clear when neither limiting approximation is adequate.\n\nThis paper maps the transition between them while retaining finite vacuum frequency,\n\n\\[\n\\omega=\\frac{\\Delta m^2}{2E},\n\\]\n\nand varying the angular distributions. It tracks both the maximum growth rate and the wavevector of the fastest-growing mode. The dominant mode can move away from spatial homogeneity or switch between branches, so restricting the analysis to \\(k=0\\) can miss the physically fastest instability. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02332v1))\n\n##### History\n\n[Fiorillo and Raffelt’s unified dispersion framework](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.20389) already connected fast, slow, and collisional instabilities by treating vacuum splittings and collision rates as perturbations and classifying the resulting branches. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.20389))\n\nThe present contribution is a more explicit map of the **slow–fast crossover**, including changes in which branch dominates. It reinforces the need to inspect the full dispersion relation rather than assign a mechanism from a growth-rate estimate or a homogeneous calculation alone.\n\nThese are linear instabilities of prescribed neutrino backgrounds. Growth rates do not determine the eventual flavor mixture, conversion depth, or observable supernova spectrum. Nonlinear saturation and coupling to transport remain separate problems. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02332v1))"}, {"id": "paper-2610-02420", "arxiv": "2610.02420", "title": "High Energy Neutrinos, Gravitational Waves, and Dark Matter from a Cosmological First Order Phase Transition", "authors": "James M. Cline, Savas Stoica, Yong Xu", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Dark matter", "Multimessenger", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Filtered dark matter connects a cosmological phase transition with neutrinos and gravitational waves, but the KM3NeT-motivated benchmark has an inaccessible gravitational-wave counterpart.", "path": "2026/10/05/index.html#paper-2610-02420", "reportDate": "5 October 2026", "searchText": "High Energy Neutrinos, Gravitational Waves, and Dark Matter from a Cosmological First Order Phase Transition James M. Cline, Savas Stoica, Yong Xu 2610.02420 Filtered dark matter connects a cosmological phase transition with neutrinos and gravitational waves, but the KM3NeT-motivated benchmark has an inaccessible gravitational-wave counterpart. 2026-10-05 5 October 2026 Neutrinos Dark matter Multimessenger BSM #### ★★☆ 5. High Energy Neutrinos, Gravitational Waves, and Dark Matter from a Cosmological First Order Phase Transition\n\n**James M. Cline, Savas Stoica, Yong Xu**  \n[arXiv:2610.02420](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02420)  \n**Multimessenger model and forecast**\n\nDark-matter particles acquire a large mass inside expanding phase-transition bubbles. Only the energetic fraction crosses the bubble wall, setting the relic abundance; subsequent dark-matter decay produces high-energy neutrinos, while the transition produces gravitational waves.\n\nFor\n\n\\[\nm_{\\rm DM}\\simeq4.4\\times10^8\\ {\\rm GeV},\n\\]\n\nthe authors obtain benchmarks compatible with the abundance and neutrino energies associated with KM3-230213A. **The corresponding gravitational-wave peak is near \\(10^4\\) Hz and below planned detector sensitivities.** Lower-mass benchmarks, \\(m_{\\rm DM}\\lesssim10^6\\) GeV, move the gravitational-wave signal into potentially accessible frequency ranges. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02420v1))\n\n##### History\n\n[Baker, Kopp and Long](https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.02830) introduced filtered dark matter as a production mechanism allowing masses well above the conventional thermal-relic range. The basic filtering process and its ability to suppress the surviving abundance are therefore established ideas. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.02830))\n\nThe new calculation determines the bubble-wall velocity using improved hydrodynamics rather than leaving it freely adjustable, strengthening the connection between relic abundance and the gravitational-wave prediction. That is the substantive advance.\n\nThe KM3NeT-motivated benchmark should not be presented as predicting an observable two-messenger coincidence: its gravitational-wave counterpart is inaccessible in the stated setup. The neutrino interpretation also remains a model-dependent explanation of an individual event, not evidence that the phase transition occurred. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02420v1))"}, {"id": "paper-2610-03692", "arxiv": "2610.03692", "title": "Higgsino dark matter compatible with the LUX-ZEPLIN high-energy nuclear-recoil event and IceCube constraints", "authors": "Katherine Freese, Dionysios P. Theodosopoulos", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Neutrinos", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A Higgsino reinterpretation identifies the restrictive assumptions needed to reconcile an isolated LZ recoil with solar-neutrino limits and distinguishes capture from thermalization and equilibrium.", "path": "2026/10/05/index.html#paper-2610-03692", "reportDate": "5 October 2026", "searchText": "Higgsino dark matter compatible with the LUX-ZEPLIN high-energy nuclear-recoil event and IceCube constraints Katherine Freese, Dionysios P. Theodosopoulos 2610.03692 A Higgsino reinterpretation identifies the restrictive assumptions needed to reconcile an isolated LZ recoil with solar-neutrino limits and distinguishes capture from thermalization and equilibrium. 2026-10-05 5 October 2026 Dark matter Neutrinos BSM #### ★★☆ 6. Higgsino dark matter compatible with the LUX-ZEPLIN high-energy nuclear-recoil event and IceCube constraints\n\n**Katherine Freese, Dionysios P. Theodosopoulos**  \n[arXiv:2610.03692](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.03692)  \n**Reassessment of an existing exclusion**\n\nThe authors revisit whether inelastic Higgsino scattering can explain LZ’s isolated high-energy recoil while avoiding solar-neutrino limits. They vary the high-speed halo population, post-capture cooling, capture–annihilation equilibrium, and treatment of LZ’s empty high-energy sideband.\n\nThe **thermal \\(1.1\\)-TeV Higgsino remains only marginally compatible** when an LMC-induced high-speed tail is included **and both elastic solar cooling and the sideband constraint are neglected**. Heavier, nonthermally produced Higgsinos admit larger allowed regions. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.03692v1))\n\n##### History\n\n[Pospelov and Ramani’s September analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.02775) obtained \\(\\delta>566\\) keV for the approximately 1.08-TeV Higgsino, excluding its interpretation of the recoil under their solar-capture assumptions. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.02775))\n\nThe new paper does not simply invalidate that result. It identifies which assumptions permit a surviving region and extends the mass range beyond the thermal benchmark. Its most important lesson is that **capture, thermalization, and capture–annihilation equilibrium are distinct conditions**.\n\nThe high-mass conclusions are less secure experimentally: above 10 TeV, the analysis holds IceCube’s reconstructed annihilation-rate limit fixed at its 10-TeV value. That is a benchmark extrapolation, not a published high-mass detector likelihood. Neither this reinterpretation nor the original recoil establishes dark matter. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.03692v1))"}, {"id": "paper-2610-03455", "arxiv": "2610.03455", "title": "Big Bang Nucleosynthesis Constraint on B-mesogenesis", "authors": "James M. Cline, Jonas Frerick, Sebastian A. Naja, Yong Xu", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["BSM", "Particle physics", "Dark matter"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Hadron-driven neutron–proton conversion imposes an approximately 9.5–10 MeV reheating floor in the studied B-mesogenesis scenarios.", "path": "2026/10/05/index.html#paper-2610-03455", "reportDate": "5 October 2026", "searchText": "Big Bang Nucleosynthesis Constraint on B-mesogenesis James M. Cline, Jonas Frerick, Sebastian A. Naja, Yong Xu 2610.03455 Hadron-driven neutron–proton conversion imposes an approximately 9.5–10 MeV reheating floor in the studied B-mesogenesis scenarios. 2026-10-05 5 October 2026 BSM Particle physics Dark matter #### ★★★ 7. Big Bang Nucleosynthesis Constraint on \\(B\\)-mesogenesis\n\n**James M. Cline, Jonas Frerick, Sebastian A. Naja, Yong Xu**  \n[arXiv:2610.03455](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.03455)  \n**New quantitative cosmological constraint**\n\nIn \\(B\\)-mesogenesis, a late-decaying particle reheats the Universe and produces bottom quarks; meson oscillations and exotic decays then generate the baryon asymmetry. This study follows the altered expansion, neutrino heating, and injected hadrons through nucleosynthesis.\n\nThe dominant effect is **hadron-induced neutron–proton interconversion**, not destruction of already-formed nuclei. For parent masses \\(M_\\Phi\\lesssim100\\) GeV, the approximate three-sigma requirement is\n\n\\[\nT_{\\rm rh}\\gtrsim9.5\\!-\\!10\\ {\\rm MeV},\n\\]\n\ncorresponding to \\(\\tau_\\Phi\\lesssim0.02\\) s. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.03455v1))\n\n##### History\n\n[Elor, Escudero and Nelson’s original proposal](https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.00880) connected baryogenesis and dark matter through neutral-\\(B\\) oscillations and visible–dark-sector decays, making low reheating temperatures central to the mechanism. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.00880))\n\nThe present paper supplies the missing process-specific answer to how low that temperature can be. A generic low-reheating bound is insufficient because the decay products directly modify \\(n/p\\), even when the expansion and neutrino temperatures alone would seem acceptable.\n\nThis closes the lowest-temperature portion of the parameter space rather than excluding \\(B\\)-mesogenesis. The bound depends most strongly on hadronic interconversion rates and the adopted decay history; the authors propagate estimated rate uncertainties, which weaken it modestly. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.03455v1))"}, {"id": "paper-2610-03718", "arxiv": "2610.03718", "title": "Dark matter bound-states made easy", "authors": "Simone Biondini, Stefan Vogl", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Compact bound-state and Sommerfeld approximations reproduce tested Abelian relic-density calculations within a few percent, with limits on excited-state simplifications.", "path": "2026/10/05/index.html#paper-2610-03718", "reportDate": "5 October 2026", "searchText": "Dark matter bound-states made easy Simone Biondini, Stefan Vogl 2610.03718 Compact bound-state and Sommerfeld approximations reproduce tested Abelian relic-density calculations within a few percent, with limits on excited-state simplifications. 2026-10-05 5 October 2026 Dark matter BSM #### ★★☆ 8. Dark matter bound-states made easy\n\n**Simone Biondini, Stefan Vogl**  \n[arXiv:2610.03718](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.03718)  \n**Computational-method improvement**\n\nThe paper develops compact approximations for freeze-out calculations including Sommerfeld enhancement and bound-state formation, dissociation, annihilation, and selected excited-state transitions.\n\nIn the Abelian model tested, smooth approximations reproduce the full relic-density calculation at the **few-percent level**. Including excited-state transitions can shift the abundance by approximately **15%** relative to a ground-state-only treatment; the corresponding approximate calculation stays within about **5%** of the full result.\n\nA semi-analytic post-freeze-out integration further reduces the numerical workload. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.03718v1))\n\n##### History\n\n[Von Harling and Petraki (2014)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1407.7874) already demonstrated that bound-state formation can materially change thermal relic abundances and supplied a fit for the coupling needed to obtain the observed density. The physical importance of bound states is therefore not new. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/1407.7874))\n\nThe advance is a more systematic, reusable approximation to the rates and thermal evolution, with explicit accuracy tests against the full calculation.\n\nA particularly useful caution is that the frequently invoked efficient-transition limit does **not** consistently simplify all excited-state processes in this setup. The prescription is not a universal replacement for coupled bound-state evolution, especially for non-Abelian models, complicated level networks, or percent-level precision targets. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.03718v1))"}, {"id": "paper-2610-02667", "arxiv": "2610.02667", "title": "Distinguishing the origin of cosmic birefringence: dark energy, dark matter, and neutrino asymmetry", "authors": "Lu Yin, Eiichiro Komatsu", "primary_category": "astro-ph.CO", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Neutrinos", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The angular dependence of CMB polarization rotation offers a forecasted test of axion dark energy, axion dark matter, and neutrino asymmetry; no new detection is claimed.", "path": "2026/10/05/index.html#paper-2610-02667", "reportDate": "5 October 2026", "searchText": "Distinguishing the origin of cosmic birefringence: dark energy, dark matter, and neutrino asymmetry Lu Yin, Eiichiro Komatsu 2610.02667 The angular dependence of CMB polarization rotation offers a forecasted test of axion dark energy, axion dark matter, and neutrino asymmetry; no new detection is claimed. 2026-10-05 5 October 2026 Dark matter Neutrinos BSM #### ★★☆ 9. Distinguishing the origin of cosmic birefringence: dark energy, dark matter, and neutrino asymmetry\n\n**Lu Yin, Eiichiro Komatsu**  \n[arXiv:2610.02667](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02667)  \n**New discrimination strategy; no new detection**\n\nDifferent mechanisms can rotate CMB polarization by a similar mean angle while producing different angular-scale dependence in the \\(EB\\) spectrum.\n\nThe authors compare axionlike dark energy, axionlike dark matter, and a neutrino-asymmetry-induced interaction. In the models studied, neutrino asymmetry and axion dark matter suppress the large-angle signal at approximately\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\lesssim20,\n\\]\n\nwhereas the dark-energy case does not. Differences during recombination supply additional discrimination at smaller angular scales. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02667v1))\n\n##### History\n\n[Nakatsuka, Namikawa and Komatsu (2022)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.08560) already proposed using reionization- and recombination-era polarization as a tomographic test distinguishing dark energy from a more rapidly evolving axion field. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.08560))\n\nThe new contribution adds the neutrino-asymmetry mechanism and works out where its predicted spectrum differs from both axion scenarios. It extends an existing tomographic idea rather than introducing it.\n\nThese are model-discrimination forecasts, not evidence that any proposed source is responsible for the reported birefringence hints. Practical inference still requires foreground treatment, polarization calibration, and sufficiently precise large-angle measurements; similar mean rotation angles alone cannot identify the underlying physics. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02667v1))"}, {"id": "paper-2610-01877", "arxiv": "2610.01877", "title": "Recycling serendipitous observations of XRISM for dark matter searches", "authors": "Lidiia Zadorozhna, Denys Malyshev, Oleg Ruchayskiy", "tags": ["Dark matter", "X rays", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Stacking low-background XRISM observations in the Galactic halo frame strengthens decay-line limits; a 3.53-keV residual remains below three-sigma global significance.", "path": "2026/10/02/index.html#paper-2610-01877", "reportDate": "2 October 2026", "searchText": "Recycling serendipitous observations of XRISM for dark matter searches Lidiia Zadorozhna, Denys Malyshev, Oleg Ruchayskiy 2610.01877 Stacking low-background XRISM observations in the Galactic halo frame strengthens decay-line limits; a 3.53-keV residual remains below three-sigma global significance. 2026-10-02 2 October 2026 Dark matter X rays BSM #### ★★★ 1. Recycling serendipitous observations of XRISM for dark matter searches\n\n**Lidiia Zadorozhna, Denys Malyshev, Oleg Ruchayskiy**  \n[arXiv:2610.01877](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.01877)\n\nThe authors combine 60 low-background public XRISM/Resolve observations, totaling 2.917 Ms, to search for narrow dark-matter decay lines from the Milky Way halo. Each spectrum is corrected for the observer’s velocity relative to the Galactic halo before stacking.\n\nThe resulting limits are, on average, approximately 70% stronger than XRISM’s recent cluster-stack constraints. More intriguingly, a residual appears at\n\n\\[\nE=3.5311~{\\rm keV}.\n\\]\n\nIts significance rises from \\(2.5\\sigma\\) in an ordinary stack to \\(3.0\\sigma\\) locally after halo-frame correction, but falls to only \\(2.0\\!-\\!2.5\\sigma\\) after the look-elsewhere effect. The excess corresponds to approximately \\(26\\pm10\\) photons over roughly 52 background counts in a 5-eV bin. Interpreted as sterile-neutrino decay, its best-fit mixing is\n\n\\[\n\\sin^2(2\\theta)=(2.7\\pm1.0)\\times10^{-10}.\n\\]\n\n##### History\n\nBulbul et al. originally reported a cluster-stack line near 3.55–3.57 keV, while Boyarsky et al. found a compatible feature in Perseus and M31. ([Bulbul et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/1402.2301); [Boyarsky et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/1402.4119)) XRISM’s subsequent 3.75-Ms stack of ten clusters found no line and improved on Hitomi’s decay-rate bound by a factor of 3–4, although it was still insufficient to test the original Bulbul flux fully. ([XRISM cluster-stack search](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.24560))\n\nThe present work materially improves the experimental strategy: low-continuum blank-field observations and halo-frame alignment can outperform a longer cluster exposure. The 3.53-keV residual is nevertheless not evidence for sterile-neutrino dark matter. Its global significance is below \\(3\\sigma\\), it contains only a few dozen excess counts, and the inferred mixing is already in roughly \\(2\\sigma\\) tension with existing exclusions. An independent, pre-specified analysis of additional fields—and explicit tests of detector-line and field-selection systematics—is essential."}, {"id": "paper-2610-01308", "arxiv": "2610.01308", "title": "Search for charged-lepton-flavour violating eμtq interactions at √s = 13 TeV with the ATLAS detector", "authors": "ATLAS Collaboration", "tags": ["Particle physics", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "ATLAS finds no charged-lepton-flavour violation in top production or decay and sets improved limits on individual effective operators.", "path": "2026/10/02/index.html#paper-2610-01308", "reportDate": "2 October 2026", "searchText": "Search for charged-lepton-flavour violating eμtq interactions at √s = 13 TeV with the ATLAS detector ATLAS Collaboration 2610.01308 ATLAS finds no charged-lepton-flavour violation in top production or decay and sets improved limits on individual effective operators. 2026-10-02 2 October 2026 Particle physics BSM #### ★★★ 2. Search for charged-lepton-flavour-violating \\(e\\mu tq\\) interactions with ATLAS\n\n**ATLAS Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2610.01308](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.01308)\n\nATLAS searches for contact interactions containing an electron, muon, top quark and up- or charm-type quark, using the full \\(140~{\\rm fb^{-1}}\\) Run-2 dataset. The three-lepton analysis covers both anomalous single-top production and flavour-violating top decay inside \\(t\\bar t\\) events.\n\nNo excess is observed. One-operator-at-a-time bounds on six EFT coefficients range from\n\n\\[\n0.018\\ {\\rm TeV^{-2}}\\quad{\\rm to}\\quad0.32\\ {\\rm TeV^{-2}},\n\\]\n\nwith corresponding branching-fraction limits spanning approximately\n\n\\[\n{\\cal B}(t\\rightarrow e\\mu u)<6\\times10^{-9}\n\\]\n\nto\n\n\\[\n{\\cal B}(t\\rightarrow e\\mu c)<2.7\\times10^{-7},\n\\]\n\ndepending on Lorentz and flavour structure.\n\n##### History\n\nATLAS’s earlier 79.8-\\({\\rm fb^{-1}}\\) decay-only search obtained a combined limit near \\({\\cal B}(t\\rightarrow\\ell\\ell' q)<1.86\\times10^{-5}\\). ([Earlier ATLAS search](https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.09048)) The quoted branching-fraction limits are numerically smaller by factors ranging from roughly 70 to several thousand, although the comparison depends on the flavour and operator assumptions. That gain is not attributable merely to luminosity: the analysis adds the often more sensitive anomalous-production channel and resolves individual EFT structures.\n\nThe limits assume one nonzero coefficient at a time. Correlations, cancellations and mediator-induced relations between operators can weaken their direct ultraviolet interpretation, while the contact-interaction description must be checked if the underlying mediator is light enough to be produced at the LHC."}, {"id": "paper-2610-01825", "arxiv": "2610.01825", "title": "Near Threshold Kaluza–Klein Graviton Decays and the Dark Dimension", "authors": "Kevin Langhoff", "tags": ["Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Near-threshold d-wave suppression limits graviton cascades in the minimal flat dark-dimension model, independently corroborating a related theoretical analysis.", "path": "2026/10/02/index.html#paper-2610-01825", "reportDate": "2 October 2026", "searchText": "Near Threshold Kaluza–Klein Graviton Decays and the Dark Dimension Kevin Langhoff 2610.01825 Near-threshold d-wave suppression limits graviton cascades in the minimal flat dark-dimension model, independently corroborating a related theoretical analysis. 2026-10-02 2 October 2026 Dark matter BSM #### ★★★ 3. Near Threshold Kaluza–Klein Graviton Decays and the Dark Dimension\n\n**Kevin Langhoff**  \n[arXiv:2610.01825](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.01825)\n\nThe paper revisits decays within a nearly degenerate Kaluza–Klein graviton tower. Angular-momentum constraints make the near-threshold decay purely \\(d\\)-wave, yielding\n\n\\[\n\\Gamma\\propto p^5\n\\]\n\nrather than the \\(s\\)-wave-like \\(\\Gamma\\propto p\\) scaling used in some dark-dimension cosmologies.\n\nApplied to a flat dark dimension, the suppression prevents the proposed graviton cascade from efficiently creating a sufficiently cold, long-lived dark-matter population. Irreducible freeze-in also produces MeV gamma rays, while BBN and CMB requirements force the compactification radius to be about three orders of magnitude smaller than the nominal \\(R\\sim\\Lambda^{-1/4}\\) dark-dimension scale.\n\n##### History\n\nThe dark-dimension dark-matter proposal relied on cascades ending in approximately \\(1\\!-\\!100\\)-keV KK gravitons. ([Dark Dimension Gravitons as Dark Matter](https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.09249)) The new threshold suppression directly removes the decay efficiency needed for that construction. However, a near-contemporaneous independent analysis, [*A Universal KK Graviton Cascade Rate*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36234), derives the same \\(p^5\\) behavior and associated amplitude suppression. Thus the physical correction is important, but it should not be attributed to this paper alone.\n\nThe exclusion concerns the minimal flat-dimensional realization and its assumed production history. Warped geometries, additional light states or nonstandard cosmological histories are not generically ruled out."}, {"id": "paper-2610-00965", "arxiv": "2610.00965", "title": "High-energy neutrinos from shocked circumnuclear material around optically-bright and infrared-only tidal disruption events", "authors": "Mainak Mukhopadhyay, Shigeo S. Kimura, Tatsuya Matsumoto", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Multimessenger", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Radiatively compressed shocks in obscured tidal disruption events could supply part of the diffuse neutrino flux, with large uncertainties in event rates and cosmic-ray loading.", "path": "2026/10/02/index.html#paper-2610-00965", "reportDate": "2 October 2026", "searchText": "High-energy neutrinos from shocked circumnuclear material around optically-bright and infrared-only tidal disruption events Mainak Mukhopadhyay, Shigeo S. Kimura, Tatsuya Matsumoto 2610.00965 Radiatively compressed shocks in obscured tidal disruption events could supply part of the diffuse neutrino flux, with large uncertainties in event rates and cosmic-ray loading. 2026-10-02 2 October 2026 Neutrinos Multimessenger Compact objects #### ★★☆ 4. High-energy neutrinos from shocked circumnuclear material around optically bright and infrared-only tidal disruption events\n\n**Mainak Mukhopadhyay, Shigeo S. Kimura, Tatsuya Matsumoto**  \n[arXiv:2610.00965](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.00965)\n\nA semi-analytic calculation follows cosmic-ray acceleration and transport in shocks driven into circumnuclear gas by tidal-disruption-event outflows. Radiative cooling and post-shock compression are included in calculating the \\(pp\\) efficiency and neutrino output.\n\nOrdinary optically selected TDEs are generally inefficient neutrino emitters. Dust-obscured, infrared-only events can approach hadronic calorimetry and, given the still-uncertain event rate, may supply as much as roughly 10% of the diffuse neutrino intensity around 100 TeV. Detecting the predicted stacked signal would probably require a future detector with an effective volume near \\(30~{\\rm km^3}\\) and several years of observations of nearby events.\n\n##### History\n\nEarlier source-specific outflow–cloud calculations for AT2019dsg predicted only about \\(4\\times10^{-3}\\) PeV events under already favorable assumptions. ([AT2019dsg outflow–cloud model](https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.01748)) IceCube’s recent search over 89 catalogued TDEs found neither a significant individual source nor a stacked excess. ([IceCube TDE search](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.00657))\n\nThe present work does not contradict that null result: most IceCube targets were optically or X-ray selected, whereas the predicted efficient population is heavily obscured and potentially missing from those catalogues. The real advance is the self-consistent population treatment of infrared-only events, including radiative compression. The 10% contribution is an upper-end estimate controlled by poorly measured event rates, gas covering factors and cosmic-ray loading; individual-event detections remain unlikely."}, {"id": "paper-2610-00995", "arxiv": "2610.00995", "title": "Search for dark matter produced in association with a low-multiplicity jet in proton-proton collisions at √s = 13 TeV", "authors": "CMS Collaboration", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Particle physics", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "CMS opens a search for missing momentum accompanied by a narrow low-multiplicity jet, setting model-dependent mediator limits without a signal excess.", "path": "2026/10/02/index.html#paper-2610-00995", "reportDate": "2 October 2026", "searchText": "Search for dark matter produced in association with a low-multiplicity jet in proton-proton collisions at √s = 13 TeV CMS Collaboration 2610.00995 CMS opens a search for missing momentum accompanied by a narrow low-multiplicity jet, setting model-dependent mediator limits without a signal excess. 2026-10-02 2 October 2026 Dark matter Particle physics BSM #### ★★☆ 5. Search for dark matter produced with a low-multiplicity jet using CMS\n\n**CMS Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2610.00995](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.00995)\n\nCMS searches for a dark-matter pair produced through a heavy vector or axial-vector mediator, with one dark particle radiating a light leptophobic \\(Z'\\). A \\(0.3\\!-\\!3\\)-GeV \\(Z'\\) decaying into two pions or kaons produces an unusually narrow, low-track-multiplicity “pencil jet,” rather than a conventional QCD jet.\n\nThe full \\(138~{\\rm fb^{-1}}\\) Run-2 sample is analyzed using hadronic-\\(\\tau\\) reconstruction followed by machine-learning classification. No excess is found. For \\(m_\\chi=150\\) GeV, \\(m_{Z'}=1\\) GeV, \\(g_q=0.25\\) and \\(g_\\chi=1\\), heavy mediators below approximately 4.25 TeV are excluded; at \\(m_\\chi=550\\) GeV, the reach is about 3.5 TeV.\n\n##### History\n\nBai, Bourbeau and Lin proposed the mono-\\(Z'\\)-jet signature in 2015, but it had not previously received a dedicated collider search. ([Mono-Z′-jet proposal](https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.01395)) This is therefore an experimental opening of a qualitatively new channel, not simply a stronger monojet bound.\n\nThe impressive mediator reach is conditional on a very light leptophobic boson, prompt hadronic decay and the specified benchmark couplings. It cannot be compared directly with conventional monojet exclusions, which test different radiation and acceptance assumptions. Modeling extremely narrow hadronic jets and calibrating a classifier inherited partly from \\(\\tau\\) reconstruction are important experimental systematics."}, {"id": "paper-2610-02107", "arxiv": "2610.02107", "title": "The Scalar MSW Effect: Compact analytical formulas for neutrino oscillations with large matter effects, including μ–μ and τ–τ dominant matter potentials", "authors": "Sandhya Choubey, Andreas Lund", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Oscillations", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Analytic diagonalization exposes scalar matter resonances and maps vacuum to effective mixing parameters, without adding a new experimental constraint.", "path": "2026/10/02/index.html#paper-2610-02107", "reportDate": "2 October 2026", "searchText": "The Scalar MSW Effect: Compact analytical formulas for neutrino oscillations with large matter effects, including μ–μ and τ–τ dominant matter potentials Sandhya Choubey, Andreas Lund 2610.02107 Analytic diagonalization exposes scalar matter resonances and maps vacuum to effective mixing parameters, without adding a new experimental constraint. 2026-10-02 2 October 2026 Neutrinos Oscillations BSM #### ★★☆ 6. Scalar MSW resonance and approximate diagonalization with scalar nonstandard interactions\n\n**Sandhya Choubey, Andreas Lund**  \n[arXiv:2610.02107](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02107)\n\nThe authors extend Jacobi-style approximate diagonalization of the three-flavour neutrino Hamiltonian to scalar nonstandard interactions, which modify the mass matrix rather than adding an ordinary vector matter potential.\n\nThey identify an energy-independent scalar analogue of the MSW resonance for each diagonal scalar coupling and derive compact mappings between vacuum and in-matter mixing parameters. The electron-flavour case admits particularly accurate formulae; the \\(\\mu\\mu\\) and \\(\\tau\\tau\\) cases require an expansion in the charged-current matter potential plus controlled corrections.\n\n##### History\n\nScalar matter effects in neutrino oscillations were introduced explicitly by Ge and Parke and have since been constrained with solar and long-baseline data. ([Ge and Parke](https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.08376)) The new paper does not introduce the interaction or improve an experimental constraint. Its contribution is to expose the resonance structure analytically and provide fast parameter mappings that can replace repeated numerical diagonalization in fits.\n\nThe “energy-independent resonance” is a property of the effective scalar mass contribution; observability still depends on density profiles, flavour structure and degeneracies with vacuum masses. Off-diagonal couplings and simultaneous multi-parameter fits remain more complicated than the presented single-coupling cases."}, {"id": "paper-2610-00455", "arxiv": "2610.00455", "title": "Three New Likely Spider Millisecond Pulsar Binaries and Optical Kinematic Tracers of Intrabinary Shocks", "authors": "Jay Strader, Rebecca Kyer, Laura Chomiuk, Elias Aydi, Peter Craig, Thomas M. Do, Isabella Molina, Teresa Panurach, Subhroja Roy, Kirill V. Sokolovsky, Samuel J. Swihart, Ryan Urquhart", "tags": ["Compact objects", "Gamma rays"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Three candidate spider pulsar binaries include a system whose optical line kinematics favor an intrabinary shock wrapping around the neutron star.", "path": "2026/10/02/index.html#paper-2610-00455", "reportDate": "2 October 2026", "searchText": "Three New Likely Spider Millisecond Pulsar Binaries and Optical Kinematic Tracers of Intrabinary Shocks Jay Strader, Rebecca Kyer, Laura Chomiuk, Elias Aydi, Peter Craig, Thomas M. Do, Isabella Molina, Teresa Panurach, Subhroja Roy, Kirill V. Sokolovsky, Samuel J. Swihart, Ryan Urquhart 2610.00455 Three candidate spider pulsar binaries include a system whose optical line kinematics favor an intrabinary shock wrapping around the neutron star. 2026-10-02 2 October 2026 Compact objects Gamma rays #### ★★☆ 7. Three New Likely Spider Millisecond Pulsar Binaries and Optical Kinematic Tracers of Intrabinary Shocks\n\n**Jay Strader, Rebecca Kyer, Laura Chomiuk, Elias Aydi, Peter Craig, Thomas M. Do, Isabella Molina, Teresa Panurach, Subhroja Roy, Kirill V. Sokolovsky, Samuel J. Swihart, Ryan Urquhart**  \n[arXiv:2610.00455](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.00455)\n\nMultiwavelength follow-up of unassociated Fermi sources identifies two strong redback candidates with orbital periods of \\(0.15\\!-\\!0.30\\) days and companion mass functions around \\(0.6\\!-\\!0.9\\,M_\\odot\\), plus a less secure 30.5-hour candidate.\n\nOne system, J1020, shows persistent broad H\\(\\alpha\\) emission with FWHM near \\(2100~{\\rm km\\,s^{-1}}\\). Its phase-dependent velocity is offset from the companion but consistent with material near the neutron star, providing the first kinematic evidence for an intrabinary shock wrapping around the pulsar rather than the secondary.\n\n##### History\n\nPhase-resolved H\\(\\alpha\\) tomography of PSR J1048+2339 previously located emission near the companion and inner Lagrange point. ([PSR J1048+2339 shock tomography](https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.06317)) J1020 therefore supplies a qualitatively different geometry and observationally supports the theoretically expected reversal of shock orientation when the companion wind dominates.\n\nNone of the three systems yet has detected radio or gamma-ray pulsations, so their neutron-star identifications remain probabilistic. The third association is particularly tentative, and interpreting H\\(\\alpha\\) velocities as direct spatial tracers requires assumptions about acceleration and radiative transfer in the shock."}, {"id": "paper-2610-00508", "arxiv": "2610.00508", "title": "X-ray Through Radio Observations and Modeling of the Soft X-ray Flash GRB 250419A", "authors": "Genevieve Schroeder et al.", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "X rays", "Compact objects"], "label": "★☆☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Broadband observations favor a low-energy relativistic jet for a soft X-ray flash, with degeneracy between energy stratification and viewing geometry.", "path": "2026/10/02/index.html#paper-2610-00508", "reportDate": "2 October 2026", "searchText": "X-ray Through Radio Observations and Modeling of the Soft X-ray Flash GRB 250419A Genevieve Schroeder et al. 2610.00508 Broadband observations favor a low-energy relativistic jet for a soft X-ray flash, with degeneracy between energy stratification and viewing geometry. 2026-10-02 2 October 2026 Gamma rays X rays Compact objects #### ★☆☆ 8. X-ray through radio observations and modeling of the soft X-ray flash GRB 250419A\n\n**Genevieve Schroeder et al.**  \n[arXiv:2610.00508](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.00508)\n\nSVOM measured a prompt spectral peak below 4 keV. Broadband follow-up found an achromatic X-ray/optical break and evidence for two radio components. The authors model the event as a low-energy relativistic jet with\n\n\\[\nE_{\\rm jet}\\sim10^{49}-10^{50}\\ {\\rm erg},\n\\]\n\nsupplemented by radial energy stratification, modest off-axis viewing, or both. The result favors at least some X-ray flashes being the low-energy tail of the classical GRB population.\n\n##### History\n\nNearby soft events such as GRB 060218 and GRB 100316D have long admitted competing relativistic-jet and shock-breakout interpretations. The new event adds an unusually broad radio-to-X-ray dataset supporting a genuine jet, but does not settle the population question. Radial energy injection and off-axis geometry remain partly interchangeable in the fit, and a single burst cannot establish whether all X-ray flashes share the same origin."}, {"id": "paper-2609-40237", "arxiv": "2609.40237", "title": "First double-differential cross-section measurements in proton multiplicity and kinematics for mesonless νμ charged-current interactions on argon using the MicroBooNE detector", "authors": "MicroBooNE Collaboration", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Particle physics"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "MicroBooNE resolves argon interaction cross sections through the fourth outgoing proton, exposing correlated generator discrepancies relevant to neutrino-energy reconstruction.", "path": "2026/10/01/index.html#paper-2609-40237", "reportDate": "1 October 2026", "searchText": "First double-differential cross-section measurements in proton multiplicity and kinematics for mesonless νμ charged-current interactions on argon using the MicroBooNE detector MicroBooNE Collaboration 2609.40237 MicroBooNE resolves argon interaction cross sections through the fourth outgoing proton, exposing correlated generator discrepancies relevant to neutrino-energy reconstruction. 2026-10-01 1 October 2026 Neutrinos Particle physics #### ★★★ 1. First double-differential cross-section measurements in proton multiplicity and kinematics for mesonless \\(\\nu_\\mu\\) charged-current interactions on argon\n\n**MicroBooNE Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2609.40237](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.40237)\n\nUsing MicroBooNE’s complete \\(1.30\\times10^{21}\\)-proton-on-target Booster Neutrino Beam exposure, the collaboration measures mesonless charged-current cross sections jointly as functions of proton multiplicity and kinematics. Observables include the momenta and muon–proton opening angles of the first through fourth protons and the total available hadronic kinetic energy.\n\nNo tested event generator adequately describes the complete correlated dataset. The largest discrepancies appear in proton multiplicities, backward muon–proton configurations and subleading-proton kinematics—precisely the regions most sensitive to intranuclear rescattering and nucleon ejection.\n\n##### History\n\nMicroBooNE’s earlier CC\\(0\\pi\\) analyses established argon cross sections with final-state protons, including a 359-bin double-differential dataset, but primarily constrained inclusive or leading-particle kinematics ([arXiv:2403.19574](https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.19574)). The new result resolves the hadronic cascade by proton rank through the fourth proton and exposes generator failures that leading-proton observables can conceal.\n\nThis is therefore a genuinely new constraint on final-state-interaction modeling, important for neutrino-energy reconstruction in liquid-argon oscillation experiments. It does not isolate a unique microscopic defect: nuclear ground-state modeling, multinucleon interactions and intranuclear transport contribute correlated uncertainties, and generator tuning to these data will need validation against independent targets and beam energies."}, {"id": "paper-2609-38682", "arxiv": "2609.38682", "title": "New Constraints on r-process Nucleosynthesis in Neutron Star Mergers from GW170817 Late-Phase Spectra", "authors": "Salma Rahmouni, Masaomi Tanaka, Daiji Kato, Gediminas Gaigalas, Kenta Hotokezaka", "tags": ["Multimessenger", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Late spectra of GW170817 constrain several heavy-element abundances in its inner ejecta, with lanthanum and cerium estimates sensitive to atomic and radiation-field uncertainties.", "path": "2026/10/01/index.html#paper-2609-38682", "reportDate": "1 October 2026", "searchText": "New Constraints on r-process Nucleosynthesis in Neutron Star Mergers from GW170817 Late-Phase Spectra Salma Rahmouni, Masaomi Tanaka, Daiji Kato, Gediminas Gaigalas, Kenta Hotokezaka 2609.38682 Late spectra of GW170817 constrain several heavy-element abundances in its inner ejecta, with lanthanum and cerium estimates sensitive to atomic and radiation-field uncertainties. 2026-10-01 1 October 2026 Multimessenger Compact objects #### ★★★ 2. New Constraints on \\(r\\)-process Nucleosynthesis in Neutron Star Mergers from GW170817 Late-Phase Spectra\n\n**Salma Rahmouni, Masaomi Tanaka, Daiji Kato, Gediminas Gaigalas, Kenta Hotokezaka**  \n[arXiv:2609.38682](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.38682)\n\nAn analytic nebular model follows allowed and forbidden emission from collisionally excited ions in AT2017gfo. It identifies La III and Ce III as the principal contributors near \\(1.4\\) and \\(1.6\\,\\mu{\\rm m}\\), respectively, and confirms Te III at \\(2.1\\,\\mu{\\rm m}\\).\n\nThe inferred mass fractions are\n\n\\[\nX({\\rm La})\\simeq0.025-0.05,\\quad\nX({\\rm Ce})\\simeq0.05-0.10,\\quad\nX({\\rm Te})\\simeq0.04-0.08,\n\\]\n\nwith \\(X({\\rm Kr})\\lesssim0.03\\) and \\(X({\\rm Sb})\\lesssim0.003\\). The implied inner ejecta have a suppressed first \\(r\\)-process peak, enhanced heavy elements relative to the solar pattern and a lanthanide fraction \\(X_{\\rm LN}\\simeq0.03-0.06\\).\n\n##### History\n\nThe \\(2.1\\,\\mu{\\rm m}\\) feature had already been attributed to [Te III] using a solar heavy-\\(r\\)-process abundance pattern ([Hotokezaka et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.00988)); JWST subsequently detected a similar feature in the GRB 230307A kilonova. ([JWST kilonova spectroscopy](https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.02098)) The new advance is the simultaneous identification and abundance inference for several heavy species, including constraints from absent lines, rather than another qualitative Te identification.\n\nThe La and Ce numbers remain tentative because they depend strongly on the poorly known radiation field, ionization balance and atomic collision data. The analytic treatment also cannot reproduce the full geometric and time-dependent radiative-transfer problem. The heavy-element enhancement is consequently evidence about the inner ejecta, not yet a model-independent total yield for GW170817."}, {"id": "paper-2609-38590", "arxiv": "2609.38590", "title": "Chandra X-ray Spectral Evidence of the Dual AGN Nature of the Gravitational lens MG B2016+112 at z=3.273 with 175 pc separation", "authors": "Daniel A. Schwartz, Julia M. Sisk-Reynes, Anna Barnacka", "tags": ["X rays", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Separate absorbed X-ray spectra support two active nuclei at a projected separation of 175 pc in a strongly lensed high-redshift system.", "path": "2026/10/01/index.html#paper-2609-38590", "reportDate": "1 October 2026", "searchText": "Chandra X-ray Spectral Evidence of the Dual AGN Nature of the Gravitational lens MG B2016+112 at z=3.273 with 175 pc separation Daniel A. Schwartz, Julia M. Sisk-Reynes, Anna Barnacka 2609.38590 Separate absorbed X-ray spectra support two active nuclei at a projected separation of 175 pc in a strongly lensed high-redshift system. 2026-10-01 1 October 2026 X rays Compact objects #### ★★★ 3. Chandra X-ray Spectral Evidence of the Dual AGN Nature of MG B2016+112 at \\(z=3.273\\) with 175-pc separation\n\n**Daniel A. Schwartz, Julia M. Sisk-Reynes, Anna Barnacka**  \n[arXiv:2609.38590](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.38590)\n\nA new 175-ks Chandra observation uses extreme gravitational magnification to obtain separate spectra for two compact X-ray emitters in MG B2016+112. The highly magnified component inside the lens caustic shows intrinsic absorption\n\n\\[\nN_{\\rm H}>4\\times10^{22}\\ {\\rm cm^{-2}}\n\\]\n\nat \\(3\\sigma\\), identifying it as an obscured AGN alongside the previously known type-II nucleus. The projected source-plane separation is approximately 175 pc, making this the tightest confirmed high-redshift AGN pair reported so far.\n\n##### History\n\nEarlier VLBI proper-motion measurements and archival Chandra imaging had resolved two emitters and favored a dual-AGN interpretation, but could not exclude one AGN plus a highly magnified parsec-scale jet. ([VLBI study](https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.11756); [archival Chandra analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.08537)) The distinct absorbed X-ray spectrum is the decisive new discriminator; this is more than a deeper-image confirmation.\n\nThe system is nevertheless not a dynamically verified binary: the 175-pc distance is projected and lens-model-dependent, and this X-ray analysis does not measure an orbit or dynamically determine the two black-hole masses. Its immediate significance is as a rare compact merger phase and potential precursor population for massive-black-hole gravitational-wave sources."}, {"id": "paper-2609-38373", "arxiv": "2609.38373", "title": "NEXUS: Discovery of a Luminous Young Transient at z=5", "authors": "Ming-Yang Zhuang, Yue Shen, Lei Hu, Justin D. R. Pierel, Zhiwei Pan, Zachary Stone, Adam J. Burgasser, Xiaohui Fan, Jenny E. Greene, Junyao Li, Feige Wang", "tags": ["Compact objects"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "JWST captures a luminous young transient in an unlensed field at redshift five; an unusual core-collapse supernova is favored but its subtype remains unconfirmed.", "path": "2026/10/01/index.html#paper-2609-38373", "reportDate": "1 October 2026", "searchText": "NEXUS: Discovery of a Luminous Young Transient at z=5 Ming-Yang Zhuang, Yue Shen, Lei Hu, Justin D. R. Pierel, Zhiwei Pan, Zachary Stone, Adam J. Burgasser, Xiaohui Fan, Jenny E. Greene, Junyao Li, Feige Wang 2609.38373 JWST captures a luminous young transient in an unlensed field at redshift five; an unusual core-collapse supernova is favored but its subtype remains unconfirmed. 2026-10-01 1 October 2026 Compact objects #### ★★★ 4. NEXUS: Discovery of a Luminous Young Transient at \\(z=5\\)\n\n**Ming-Yang Zhuang, Yue Shen, Lei Hu, Justin D. R. Pierel, Zhiwei Pan, Zachary Stone, Adam J. Burgasser, Xiaohui Fan, Jenny E. Greene, Junyao Li, Feige Wang**  \n[arXiv:2609.38373](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.38373)\n\nJWST/NEXUS discovered AT 2026abck in a galaxy at \\(z=5.017\\pm0.001\\). It brightened by more than 1.4 magnitudes in at most 12 rest-frame days and was probably caught only 4–10 days after explosion.\n\nA blackbody fit gives \\(T\\simeq1.7\\times10^4\\) K, \\(R\\simeq7\\times10^{14}\\) cm and \\(L_{\\rm bol}\\simeq3\\times10^{43}\\ {\\rm erg\\,s^{-1}}\\). Its off-nuclear position and blue continuum favor a core-collapse supernova, although it is hotter and more luminous than ordinary early Type-II events. It is the first robust \\(z\\gtrsim5\\) transient found in an unlensed field.\n\n##### History\n\nSN Eos at \\(z=5.133\\) remains the stronger object-level classification because it was multiply imaged, strongly lensed and spectroscopically confirmed as a metal-poor Type-II supernova ([arXiv:2601.04156](https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.04156)). AT 2026abck changes the survey context: no lens magnification was required, and the high cadence captured the UV-bright rise in a host roughly \\(10^{3.3}\\) times more massive than that of Eos.\n\nThe event itself lacks a spectroscopic subtype and a mature light curve. A superluminous supernova, interaction-powered event or other unusual core-collapse transient is not yet excluded, so “core-collapse supernova” should be read as the favored photometric interpretation."}, {"id": "paper-2609-38316", "arxiv": "2609.38316", "title": "Constraints on Complex Electroweak WIMPs from Solar Capture", "authors": "Juan P. Luengas, Maxim Pospelov, Harikrishnan Ramani", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Neutrinos", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Solar capture and IceCube limits constrain inelastic electroweak multiplets, with implications for the LZ recoil candidate that depend on relic abundance and the halo velocity distribution.", "path": "2026/10/01/index.html#paper-2609-38316", "reportDate": "1 October 2026", "searchText": "Constraints on Complex Electroweak WIMPs from Solar Capture Juan P. Luengas, Maxim Pospelov, Harikrishnan Ramani 2609.38316 Solar capture and IceCube limits constrain inelastic electroweak multiplets, with implications for the LZ recoil candidate that depend on relic abundance and the halo velocity distribution. 2026-10-01 1 October 2026 Dark matter Neutrinos BSM #### ★★☆ 5. Constraints on Complex Electroweak WIMPs from Solar Capture\n\n**Juan P. Luengas, Maxim Pospelov, Harikrishnan Ramani**  \n[arXiv:2609.38316](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.38316)\n\nThe authors calculate capture, energy loss and annihilation in the Sun for scalar and fermionic electroweak dark-matter multiplets with sub-MeV inelastic splittings. For higher representations, IceCube’s non-observation of solar annihilation neutrinos requires approximately\n\n\\[\n\\delta\\gtrsim350~{\\rm keV}\n\\]\n\nover masses extending to \\(100\\) TeV. Under thermal freeze-out and the Standard Halo Model, this excludes most such multiplets as explanations of LZ’s single 248-keV recoil candidate. An LMC-induced high-velocity tail can, however, move the LZ-preferred splitting above the solar limit for several representations.\n\n##### History\n\nA study published in September 2026 applied the same solar-acceleration mechanism specifically to the 1.08-TeV Higgsino and obtained the stronger doublet-specific bound \\(\\delta>566\\) keV ([arXiv:2609.02775](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.02775)). The present paper’s substantive advance is the generalization to scalar and fermionic higher multiplets, revealing an approximately representation-independent \\(350\\)-keV floor.\n\nThe conclusion about the LZ event is conditional. LZ reported one candidate, not a dark-matter discovery, and the mapping to a preferred splitting depends on thermal abundance and the poorly known high-speed halo tail. Nonstandard production, a subdominant WIMP abundance or modified local phase space can weaken the stated exclusion."}, {"id": "paper-2609-38233", "arxiv": "2609.38233", "title": "XENONnT pp-Dominated Solar Neutrinos Constrain the ν₁ Pseudo-Dirac Splitting", "authors": "Gio Leone", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Oscillations", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "An independent reanalysis of XENONnT solar-neutrino recoils limits a pseudo-Dirac mass splitting without finding a preference for active–sterile oscillations.", "path": "2026/10/01/index.html#paper-2609-38233", "reportDate": "1 October 2026", "searchText": "XENONnT pp-Dominated Solar Neutrinos Constrain the ν₁ Pseudo-Dirac Splitting Gio Leone 2609.38233 An independent reanalysis of XENONnT solar-neutrino recoils limits a pseudo-Dirac mass splitting without finding a preference for active–sterile oscillations. 2026-10-01 1 October 2026 Neutrinos Oscillations BSM #### ★★☆ 6. XENONnT \\(pp\\)-Dominated Solar Neutrinos Constrain the \\(\\nu_1\\) Pseudo-Dirac Splitting\n\n**Gio Leone**  \n[arXiv:2609.38233](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.38233)\n\nThe published XENONnT low-energy electron-recoil spectrum is reanalyzed for active–sterile oscillations caused by a pseudo-Dirac splitting of the first neutrino mass eigenstate. The calculation includes the \\(pp\\) and \\(^{7}\\)Be recoil spectra and finds no preference for a nonzero splitting.\n\nThe resulting two-sigma limit is\n\n\\[\n\\delta m_1^2 \\lesssim 5.9\\times10^{-13}\\ {\\rm eV^2}.\n\\]\n\nThis reaches the regime previously associated mainly with future low-energy solar-neutrino detectors.\n\n##### History\n\nSolar data had generally limited pseudo-Dirac splittings at roughly the \\(10^{-11}\\ {\\rm eV^2}\\) scale. Dedicated forecasts found that a 300-ton-year DARWIN exposure could reach \\(10^{-13}\\ {\\rm eV^2}\\), while a \\(pp\\)-inclusive JUNO analysis might reach \\(2.9\\times10^{-13}\\ {\\rm eV^2}\\). ([DARWIN forecast](https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.02421); [JUNO forecast](https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.05418)) The new paper is the first actual xenon-\\(pp\\) constraint in this range, although it does not yet equal those full-exposure projections.\n\nThis is an independent phenomenological likelihood built from published spectra, not an official XENONnT oscillation analysis. Correlated background, detector-response and solar-flux nuisance parameters may therefore be treated less completely than in a collaboration likelihood."}, {"id": "paper-2609-38305", "arxiv": "2609.38305", "title": "Close Pulsar Pairs See Dark Matter Substructure through the Gravitational-Wave Background", "authors": "Abhiram Cherukupalli, Vincent S. H. Lee, Kim V. Berghaus, Kathryn M. Zurek", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Multimessenger", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Differencing nearby pulsars could suppress the common gravitational-wave background and reveal dark substructure, subject to untested cluster and timing assumptions.", "path": "2026/10/01/index.html#paper-2609-38305", "reportDate": "1 October 2026", "searchText": "Close Pulsar Pairs See Dark Matter Substructure through the Gravitational-Wave Background Abhiram Cherukupalli, Vincent S. H. Lee, Kim V. Berghaus, Kathryn M. Zurek 2609.38305 Differencing nearby pulsars could suppress the common gravitational-wave background and reveal dark substructure, subject to untested cluster and timing assumptions. 2026-10-01 1 October 2026 Dark matter Multimessenger Compact objects #### ★★☆ 7. Close Pulsar Pairs See Dark Matter Substructure through the Gravitational-Wave Background\n\n**Abhiram Cherukupalli, Vincent S. H. Lee, Kim V. Berghaus, Kathryn M. Zurek**  \n[arXiv:2609.38305](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.38305)\n\nThe proposal uses sub-parsec pulsar pairs as a differential detector. A nanohertz gravitational-wave background produces nearly common timing residuals and cancels when the pair is differenced, whereas the gravitational perturbation from a nearby dark subhalo remains differential.\n\nFor separations near \\(0.1\\) pc, the projected sensitivity to approximately \\(10^{-6}M_\\odot\\) subhalos improves by more than an order of magnitude relative to an otherwise identical array of widely separated pulsars. Globular clusters naturally supply candidate pairs.\n\n##### History\n\nThe same authors recently showed that the measured stochastic gravitational-wave background degrades ordinary PTA sensitivity to substructure by one to three orders of magnitude ([arXiv:2607.03533](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.03533)). The new paper supplies a geometrical cancellation strategy for that specific limitation rather than merely updating a forecast.\n\nIts practicality remains unproven. Globular clusters may have lost much of their primordial dark-matter content, and their stellar accelerations, encounters, intracluster plasma and timing-model correlations can all generate differential signals. The forecast also assumes suitable, precisely timed pulsar pairs that do not yet constitute a demonstrated observing array."}, {"id": "paper-2609-38314", "arxiv": "2609.38314", "title": "How collective modes terminate in the dispersion relation of neutrino plasmas", "authors": "Damiano F. G. Fiorillo", "tags": ["Neutrinos"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A linear-dispersion analysis separates symmetry-dependent endpoints of collective neutrino modes from generic branch-cut termination when velocity dispersion is included.", "path": "2026/10/01/index.html#paper-2609-38314", "reportDate": "1 October 2026", "searchText": "How collective modes terminate in the dispersion relation of neutrino plasmas Damiano F. G. Fiorillo 2609.38314 A linear-dispersion analysis separates symmetry-dependent endpoints of collective neutrino modes from generic branch-cut termination when velocity dispersion is included. 2026-10-01 1 October 2026 Neutrinos #### ★★☆ 8. How collective modes terminate in the dispersion relation of neutrino plasmas\n\n**Damiano F. G. Fiorillo**  \n[arXiv:2609.38314](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.38314)\n\nThe paper studies how nonzero neutrino masses and the associated velocity dispersion modify singular endpoints of collective flavor-wave branches. Masses remove the frequently seen symmetry-axis degeneracy of fast-limit modes, although degeneracy lines may remain in the full three-dimensional wavevector space. Abrupt termination at a branch cut of the flavor dielectric function is instead generic.\n\nA useful analytic result is that a Lorentzian neutrino-frequency distribution is exactly equivalent to a monochromatic distribution with an effective collision rate, allowing more general spectra to be approximated by sums of simpler components.\n\n##### History\n\nRecent work unified fast, slow and collisionally driven instabilities within a common neutrino-plasma dispersion framework but generally treated mass or collision effects perturbatively around simpler distributions ([arXiv:2505.20389](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.20389)). The present result clarifies which apparent mode endpoints are symmetry artifacts and which survive realistic velocity dispersion.\n\nThis is a rigorous linear-dispersion result, not a calculation of nonlinear flavor conversion in a supernova or merger. Inhomogeneity, time-dependent transport, angular structure and backreaction may determine whether any of these modes significantly alter an observable neutrino signal."}, {"id": "paper-2609-35974", "arxiv": "2609.35974", "title": "JWST lensed quasar dark matter survey V: Hints of self-interacting dark matter from 29 quadruply imaged quasars", "authors": "D. Gilman et al.", "tags": ["Dark matter"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Flux ratios in 29 lensed quasars favor tested populations containing core-collapsed dark-matter halos, with evidence sensitive to globular-cluster and subhalo assumptions.", "path": "2026/09/30/index.html#paper-2609-35974", "reportDate": "30 September 2026", "searchText": "JWST lensed quasar dark matter survey V: Hints of self-interacting dark matter from 29 quadruply imaged quasars D. Gilman et al. 2609.35974 Flux ratios in 29 lensed quasars favor tested populations containing core-collapsed dark-matter halos, with evidence sensitive to globular-cluster and subhalo assumptions. 2026-09-30 30 September 2026 Dark matter #### ★★★ 1. JWST lensed quasar dark matter survey V: Hints of self-interacting dark matter from 29 quadruply imaged quasars\n\n**D. Gilman et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.35974](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.35974)\n\nThe authors use flux-ratio perturbations in 29 quadruply imaged quasars to infer the abundance of core-collapsed dark-matter halos and subhalos between \\(10^6\\) and \\(10^{10.7}\\,M_\\odot\\). Such deeply collapsed objects are a characteristic late-time outcome of some velocity-dependent self-interacting-dark-matter models.\n\nAfter marginalizing over globular clusters, subhalo abundance, lens macromodels and collapsed-halo structure, the data prefer models containing core-collapsed halos. The reported Bayes factors disfavor collisionless CDM relative to the tested SIDM populations by between \\(6{:}1\\) and \\(24{:}1\\). A CDM-only fit requires both more globular clusters than expected and a subhalo abundance above standard \\(N\\)-body predictions.\n\n##### History\n\nEarlier simulations showed that quasar flux ratios should be unusually sensitive to SIDM core collapse, forecasting roughly \\(20{:}1\\) odds against CDM for a favorable 50-lens sample. ([SIDM lensing forecasts](https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.05259)) The JWST survey has already used essentially the same sample to set competitive warm-dark-matter limits—\\(m_{\\rm WDM}\\gtrsim5.6\\!-\\!6.4\\) keV depending on the substructure prior—but those analyses tested suppression of halo abundance, not the enhanced central densities generated by collapse. ([JWST warm-dark-matter analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.07765))\n\nThis is therefore the first substantial population-level observational hint for SIDM core collapse from strong lenses, rather than another forecast. It is not yet evidence for a unique particle interaction: the odds vary by a factor of four under plausible assumptions about globular clusters and SIDM subhalos, and the inference depends on extrapolating collapsed-halo profiles below scales directly resolved by simulations. Independent lens samples and the promised particle-physics interpretation are essential before treating this as a challenge to CDM rather than a lens-population modeling tension.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-35968", "arxiv": "2609.35968", "title": "Constraining dark matter using 20-year INTEGRAL/IBIS observations II: Sub-GeV dark matter", "authors": "Jordan Koechler, Pedro De la Torre Luque", "tags": ["Dark matter", "X rays", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A joint spectral and spatial analysis of twenty years of INTEGRAL/IBIS data strengthens sub-GeV dark-matter limits while retaining dependence on astrophysical background and propagation models.", "path": "2026/09/30/index.html#paper-2609-35968", "reportDate": "30 September 2026", "searchText": "Constraining dark matter using 20-year INTEGRAL/IBIS observations II: Sub-GeV dark matter Jordan Koechler, Pedro De la Torre Luque 2609.35968 A joint spectral and spatial analysis of twenty years of INTEGRAL/IBIS data strengthens sub-GeV dark-matter limits while retaining dependence on astrophysical background and propagation models. 2026-09-30 30 September 2026 Dark matter X rays BSM #### ★★★ 2. Constraining dark matter using 20-year INTEGRAL/IBIS observations II: Sub-GeV dark matter\n\n**Jordan Koechler, Pedro De la Torre Luque**  \n[arXiv:2609.35968](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.35968)\n\nTwenty years of diffuse INTEGRAL/IBIS hard-X-ray data are analyzed jointly in energy and Galactic longitude. The background model combines inverse-Compton emission from cosmic-ray electrons with unresolved accreting white dwarfs; secondary inverse-Compton photons from dark-matter-produced electrons and positrons are propagated consistently.\n\nFor \\(1\\) MeV–\\(5\\) GeV dark matter annihilating or decaying to \\(e^+e^-\\), \\(\\mu^+\\mu^-\\), or \\(\\pi^+\\pi^-\\), the analysis obtains limits of approximately\n\n\\[\n10^{-28}\\lesssim \\langle\\sigma v\\rangle/\n({\\rm cm^3\\,s^{-1}})\\lesssim10^{-26},\n\\qquad\n\\tau\\gtrsim10^{25}\\ {\\rm s}\n\\]\n\nat masses of a few MeV, with improvements reaching two orders of magnitude over previous X-ray bounds. The results are also translated into dark-photon and scalar-Higgs portal parameters.\n\n##### History\n\nThe foundational INTEGRAL analysis already showed that secondary inverse-Compton emission greatly strengthens limits above roughly 20 MeV. ([Earlier INTEGRAL analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.11493)) A subsequent combination of INTEGRAL, XMM-Newton, NuSTAR and Suzaku produced leading bounds over parts of the \\(0.1\\!-\\!1\\) GeV range. ([Combined X-ray constraints](https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.08854)) The present gain comes from twenty years of IBIS exposure and, more importantly, fitting spectral and longitudinal morphology simultaneously instead of treating the diffuse intensity as a one-dimensional bound.\n\nThe improvement is substantial, but not purely exposure-driven or detector-systematics-limited. It relies on the adopted cosmic-ray propagation model, unresolved-white-dwarf template, Galactic radiation fields and dark-matter density profile. These astrophysical nuisance choices should be compared directly with the earlier multi-instrument analysis before the strongest limits are used in global portal-model fits.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-36632", "arxiv": "2609.36632", "title": "Combined constraints on the diffuse flux of cosmic neutrinos between 10¹⁶ and 10²⁶ eV", "authors": "S. V. Troitsky", "tags": ["Neutrinos"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A common exposure and likelihood framework combines diffuse ultrahigh-energy neutrino searches across ten decades in energy, with machine-readable tables for testing model spectra.", "path": "2026/09/30/index.html#paper-2609-36632", "reportDate": "30 September 2026", "searchText": "Combined constraints on the diffuse flux of cosmic neutrinos between 10¹⁶ and 10²⁶ eV S. V. Troitsky 2609.36632 A common exposure and likelihood framework combines diffuse ultrahigh-energy neutrino searches across ten decades in energy, with machine-readable tables for testing model spectra. 2026-09-30 30 September 2026 Neutrinos #### ★★★ 3. Combined constraints on the diffuse flux of cosmic neutrinos between \\(10^{16}\\) and \\(10^{26}\\) eV\n\n**S. V. Troitsky**  \n[arXiv:2609.36632](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36632)\n\nPublished searches from optical Cherenkov telescopes, air-shower arrays, in-ice and balloon radio detectors, and lunar radio experiments use mutually incompatible flavor, binning and confidence-limit conventions. Troitsky reconstructs their energy-dependent exposures, converts them to a common all-flavor \\(\\nu+\\bar\\nu\\) convention, and combines event counts and backgrounds in a Poisson likelihood.\n\nThe output is a reproducible set of 90%-confidence, one-decade quasi-differential limits over ten orders of magnitude in energy. Model spectra—including cosmogenic neutrinos and selected exotic scenarios—can be folded directly through the combined exposure; machine-readable exposure and limit tables are supplied.\n\n##### History\n\nIceCube and Auger previously published strong quasi-differential constraints, but as experiment-specific results with different energy intervals and statistical definitions. ([IceCube limits](https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.01820); [Auger limits](https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.07422)) Broader “unified neutrino spectrum” reviews compiled fluxes through the PeV scale rather than constructing a likelihood combination of ultrahigh-energy null searches. ([Grand Unified Neutrino Spectrum review](https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.11878))\n\nThe advance is consequently methodological and highly useful, not a new neutrino event or detector result. It appears to be the first reproducible exposure-level combination spanning the full \\(10^{16}\\!-\\!10^{26}\\) eV range. It is nevertheless an external reconstruction from published summaries, not a collaboration-sanctioned joint likelihood; incomplete covariance information, changing cross-section assumptions and detector-specific systematics cannot all be propagated exactly.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-35956", "arxiv": "2609.35956", "title": "Discovery of the Infrared Counterpart of the Rapid Burster with JWST", "authors": "Malina M. Desai, Kevin B. Burdge, Cristina Pallanca, Saul Rappaport, Francesco R. Ferraro, Barbara Lanzoni, Mason Ng, Paul Draghis, Emma T. Chickles, Ilaria Caiazzo, Deepto Chakrabarty", "tags": ["Compact objects", "X rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "JWST identifies the Rapid Burster's infrared counterpart through strong active-state variability, enabling follow-up studies of its donor and accretion flow.", "path": "2026/09/30/index.html#paper-2609-35956", "reportDate": "30 September 2026", "searchText": "Discovery of the Infrared Counterpart of the Rapid Burster with JWST Malina M. Desai, Kevin B. Burdge, Cristina Pallanca, Saul Rappaport, Francesco R. Ferraro, Barbara Lanzoni, Mason Ng, Paul Draghis, Emma T. Chickles, Ilaria Caiazzo, Deepto Chakrabarty 2609.35956 JWST identifies the Rapid Burster's infrared counterpart through strong active-state variability, enabling follow-up studies of its donor and accretion flow. 2026-09-30 30 September 2026 Compact objects X rays #### ★★★ 4. Discovery of the Infrared Counterpart of the Rapid Burster with JWST\n\n**Malina M. Desai, Kevin B. Burdge, Cristina Pallanca, Saul Rappaport, Francesco R. Ferraro, Barbara Lanzoni, Mason Ng, Paul Draghis, Emma T. Chickles, Ilaria Caiazzo, Deepto Chakrabarty**  \n[arXiv:2609.35956](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.35956)\n\nJWST/NIRCam observations obtained while MXB 1730−335 was X-ray active identify an unambiguous infrared counterpart to the Rapid Burster—the only known neutron star producing both thermonuclear Type-I and accretion-driven Type-II bursts.\n\nThe source fades by approximately 2.5 magnitudes in F200W and shows strong variability on minute timescales. The identification provides a practical target for infrared spectroscopy and coordinated X-ray/IR observations of the donor and accretion flow.\n\n##### History\n\nChandra, HST and ground-based infrared imaging had narrowed the field but could not isolate a uniquely variable object in Liller 1’s extremely crowded and extinguished core. ([Earlier counterpart search](https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0106140)) A 2025 HST/Gemini study proposed a variable optical candidate, explicitly leaving its association unconfirmed. ([2025 optical candidate](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10640)) JWST’s active-state variability converts that candidate-level situation into a compelling identification.\n\nThe result is an observational discovery rather than yet a determination of the binary parameters. The donor’s spectral type, orbital period and the relation between infrared variability and individual Type-II bursts remain unmeasured.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-37928", "arxiv": "2609.37928", "title": "Joint Mass-Radius and Magnetic-Geometry Inference of PSR J0740+6620", "authors": "Chun Huang, Tuomo Salmi, Alexander Y. Chen", "tags": ["Compact objects", "X rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Magnetically motivated pulse-profile models yield a radius consistent with previous NICER results while exposing an unresolved problem in polar-cap heating.", "path": "2026/09/30/index.html#paper-2609-37928", "reportDate": "30 September 2026", "searchText": "Joint Mass-Radius and Magnetic-Geometry Inference of PSR J0740+6620 Chun Huang, Tuomo Salmi, Alexander Y. Chen 2609.37928 Magnetically motivated pulse-profile models yield a radius consistent with previous NICER results while exposing an unresolved problem in polar-cap heating. 2026-09-30 30 September 2026 Compact objects X rays #### ★★★ 5. Joint Mass-Radius and Magnetic-Geometry Inference of PSR J0740+6620\n\n**Chun Huang, Tuomo Salmi, Alexander Y. Chen**  \n[arXiv:2609.37928](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.37928)\n\nInstead of fitting phenomenological emitting spots, the authors connect the NICER/XMM pulse profile of the massive pulsar J0740+6620 to two low-dimensional magnetospheric geometries: a centered dipole–quadrupole and a shifted dipole.\n\nThe shifted dipole is favored by\n\n\\[\n\\Delta\\ln Z=5.65,\n\\]\n\ncorresponding to a Bayes factor near 285 under the chosen priors. It gives\n\n\\[\nM=2.059^{+0.068}_{-0.069}M_\\odot,\\qquad\nR=13.28^{+1.47}_{-1.07}\\ {\\rm km}.\n\\]\n\nBoth tested configurations require an X-ray footprint considerably smaller than a standard force-free polar cap; exploratory photon-conversion calculations do not reproduce that compactness.\n\n##### History\n\nThe two independent 2024 NICER analyses obtained \\(R=12.49^{+1.28}_{-0.88}\\) km and \\(12.92^{+2.09}_{-1.13}\\) km using flexible hotspot parameterizations. ([Salmi et al. (2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.14466); [Dittmann et al. (2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.14467)) The new radius is fully consistent with both, so this does not materially alter neutron-star equation-of-state constraints. The important result is that a restricted, magnetically motivated hotspot model preserves the radius inference while making a testable statement about polar-cap heating.\n\nOnly two idealized field families were compared, and the preferred model itself fails to explain the required footprint through the simple heating prescription. The large Bayes factor should therefore be read as preference within this narrow model space, not as a definitive magnetic-field reconstruction.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-37335", "arxiv": "2609.37335", "title": "First Measurement of the Superscaling Variable as a Function of Visible Kinematics in Charged-Current Quasi-Elastic Neutrino–Nucleus Scattering", "authors": "MINERvA Collaboration", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Particle physics"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "MINERvA measures a visible-kinematics superscaling variable that tests nuclear missing-energy biases relevant to neutrino-energy reconstruction.", "path": "2026/09/30/index.html#paper-2609-37335", "reportDate": "30 September 2026", "searchText": "First Measurement of the Superscaling Variable as a Function of Visible Kinematics in Charged-Current Quasi-Elastic Neutrino–Nucleus Scattering MINERvA Collaboration 2609.37335 MINERvA measures a visible-kinematics superscaling variable that tests nuclear missing-energy biases relevant to neutrino-energy reconstruction. 2026-09-30 30 September 2026 Neutrinos Particle physics #### ★★☆ 6. First Measurement of the Superscaling Variable as a Function of Visible Kinematics in Charged-Current Quasi-Elastic Neutrino–Nucleus Scattering\n\n**MINERvA Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2609.37335](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.37335)\n\nMINERvA measures a visible-kinematics version of the quasielastic superscaling variable, \\(\\psi'_{\\rm vis}\\), in \\(\\nu_\\mu\\)-carbon CCQE-like events. Because its peak is externally calibrated, shifts directly expose nuclear missing energy that would bias reconstructed neutrino energy.\n\nFive nuclear-interaction models are compared through forward folding. Their predictions diverge most strongly—and generally fit least well—at low proton recoil energy and low muon transverse momentum. The relativistic mean-field model gives the best peak position among those tested.\n\n##### History\n\nSuperscaling and RMF/SuSAv2 models were developed using electron scattering and had already been confronted with inclusive neutrino cross sections. ([SuSAv2 model](https://arxiv.org/abs/1407.8346)) The new contribution is an experimentally unfolded, visible final-state variable designed specifically to diagnose missing-energy biases rather than another inclusive-rate comparison.\n\nThat makes it directly useful for oscillation-energy systematics, although extrapolation from carbon and MINERvA’s few-GeV flux to argon experiments still requires generator-level validation. The conclusion that RMF performs best is restricted to the measured projections and the five tested implementations.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-37422", "arxiv": "2609.37422", "title": "Search for dark matter annihilation in the Sun using the full ANTARES dataset", "authors": "ANTARES Collaboration", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Dark matter"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The full ANTARES dataset yields no solar dark-matter neutrino excess and improves earlier WIMP–proton limits by factors of about two to three.", "path": "2026/09/30/index.html#paper-2609-37422", "reportDate": "30 September 2026", "searchText": "Search for dark matter annihilation in the Sun using the full ANTARES dataset ANTARES Collaboration 2609.37422 The full ANTARES dataset yields no solar dark-matter neutrino excess and improves earlier WIMP–proton limits by factors of about two to three. 2026-09-30 30 September 2026 Neutrinos Dark matter #### ★★☆ 7. Search for dark matter annihilation in the Sun using the full ANTARES dataset\n\n**ANTARES Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2609.37422](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.37422)\n\nThe final 2007–2022 ANTARES exposure is searched for neutrinos from WIMP annihilation in the Sun. No excess is observed. Spin-dependent and spin-independent WIMP–proton limits are reported for masses from 35 GeV to 10 TeV, assuming 100% branching to \\(b\\bar b\\), \\(\\tau^+\\tau^-\\), or \\(W^+W^-\\).\n\nThe limits improve previous ANTARES results by factors of roughly two to three.\n\n##### History\n\nThe earlier analysis used only 2007–2012 data and covered 50 GeV–5 TeV. ([Earlier ANTARES analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.02228)) The expanded mass range and factor-of-few sensitivity gain make this the definitive ANTARES solar-WIMP result, but it is an exposure-driven legacy improvement rather than a qualitatively new search technique or globally new WIMP regime.\n\nTranslation from a neutrino-flux limit to a scattering cross section assumes capture–annihilation equilibrium in the Sun, a Galactic velocity distribution and single-channel annihilation. Those assumptions can fail in models with suppressed annihilation or mixed final states.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-37087", "arxiv": "2609.37087", "title": "Trace isotopes set the neutron horizon of liquid argon", "authors": "Yasser Maghrbi", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Particle physics"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Trace argon isotopes dominate neutron opacity near a 40Ar interference minimum, affecting simulated neutron-capture calibration geometry in liquid-argon detectors.", "path": "2026/09/30/index.html#paper-2609-37087", "reportDate": "30 September 2026", "searchText": "Trace isotopes set the neutron horizon of liquid argon Yasser Maghrbi 2609.37087 Trace argon isotopes dominate neutron opacity near a 40Ar interference minimum, affecting simulated neutron-capture calibration geometry in liquid-argon detectors. 2026-09-30 30 September 2026 Neutrinos Particle physics #### ★★☆ 8. Trace isotopes set the neutron horizon of liquid argon\n\n**Yasser Maghrbi**  \n[arXiv:2609.37087](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.37087)\n\nNear 57 keV, destructive nuclear interference makes \\(^{40}\\)Ar nearly transparent to neutrons. The \\(0.4\\%\\) natural abundance of \\(^{36}\\)Ar and \\(^{38}\\)Ar consequently supplies more than 90% of the total opacity, amplifying their effect by a factor of about 230.\n\nPublished transmission data exclude the \\(^{36}\\)Ar evaluation used by default in FLUKA and instead constrain its cross section to approximately \\(2\\!-\\!11\\) b. In a DUNE-scale simulation, adopting data-consistent evaluations shifts the fiducial fraction of \\(^{41}\\)Ar calibration captures by 9–13%, although the total yield changes by only 2%.\n\n##### History\n\nNeutron-transport studies normally treat natural argon as effectively \\(^{40}\\)Ar; no earlier work appears to have isolated this trace-isotope dominance in the interference window and propagated it to DUNE calibration geometry. The result is novel and potentially operationally important, but rests on existing transmission datasets rather than a new dedicated isotope measurement. A modern \\(^{36}\\)Ar measurement around 57 keV is the clean way to resolve the discrepancy among nuclear-data libraries."}, {"id": "paper-2609-32649", "arxiv": "2609.32649", "title": "Global Spline Fit: A unified data-driven view of the cosmic-ray spectrum and mass composition from GeV to the highest energies", "authors": "Anatoli Fedynitch, Kozo Fujisue, Hans Dembinski, Ralph Engel", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Neutrinos"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A unified spline fit combines cosmic-ray spectrum and composition measurements from GeV energies to above 100 EeV, with updated all-nucleon fluxes relevant to atmospheric backgrounds.", "path": "2026/09/29/index.html#paper-2609-32649", "reportDate": "29 September 2026", "searchText": "Global Spline Fit: A unified data-driven view of the cosmic-ray spectrum and mass composition from GeV to the highest energies Anatoli Fedynitch, Kozo Fujisue, Hans Dembinski, Ralph Engel 2609.32649 A unified spline fit combines cosmic-ray spectrum and composition measurements from GeV energies to above 100 EeV, with updated all-nucleon fluxes relevant to atmospheric backgrounds. 2026-09-29 29 September 2026 Cosmic rays Neutrinos #### ★★★ 1. Global Spline Fit: A unified data-driven view of the cosmic-ray spectrum and mass composition from GeV to the highest energies\n\n**Anatoli Fedynitch, Kozo Fujisue, Hans Dembinski, Ralph Engel**  \n[arXiv:2609.32649](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.32649)\n\nThe authors combine elemental AMS-02, CALET and DAMPE spectra, LHAASO knee-region measurements, and Auger fluorescence composition into a single nonparametric fit from GeV energies to above \\(10^{20}\\) eV. Four leading mass groups are represented by cubic splines; experimental energy scales, solar modulation and correlated uncertainties are fitted simultaneously.\n\nApproximately one thousand measurements are described with \\(\\chi^2/{\\rm ndf}\\simeq1.3\\), falling to 0.83 after localized dataset tensions are down-weighted. The derived all-nucleon flux differs from widely used parametrizations by \\(20\\!-\\!50\\%\\) over four decades in energy—larger than the remaining fit uncertainty and therefore potentially important for atmospheric-neutrino and muon calculations.\n\n##### History\n\nThe authors’ [2017 Global Spline Fit](https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.11432) pioneered the same data-driven combination and explicit energy-scale alignment. The present work is a major modernization: it incorporates the precision spaceborne spectra that exposed multiple spectral breaks, LHAASO’s element-separated knee measurements, Auger’s updated composition, solar modulation, and a more useful covariance/reduced representation.\n\nThe practical advance is stronger than merely updating a plot: the large change in the inferred nucleon flux means atmospheric-background calculations based on older H3a/GST-like parameterizations should be revisited. It is still a phenomenological world average, not a source or transport model. Air-shower composition retains hadronic-interaction dependence, four broad mass groups lose elemental information, and the improvement obtained by de-weighting discrepant data shows that full experimental consistency has not literally been achieved.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-34155", "arxiv": "2609.34155", "title": "Comment on “Fermi-LAT Galactic Center Excess Morphology of Dark Matter in Simulations of the Milky Way Galaxy”", "authors": "Kevork N. Abazajian, Jason Kumar, Oscar Macias", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Gamma rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A correction to simulated dark-matter annihilation templates challenges a claimed morphological degeneracy with the Galactic stellar bulge, without establishing the origin of the GeV excess.", "path": "2026/09/29/index.html#paper-2609-34155", "reportDate": "29 September 2026", "searchText": "Comment on “Fermi-LAT Galactic Center Excess Morphology of Dark Matter in Simulations of the Milky Way Galaxy” Kevork N. Abazajian, Jason Kumar, Oscar Macias 2609.34155 A correction to simulated dark-matter annihilation templates challenges a claimed morphological degeneracy with the Galactic stellar bulge, without establishing the origin of the GeV excess. 2026-09-29 29 September 2026 Dark matter Gamma rays #### ★★★ 2. Comment on “Fermi-LAT Galactic Center Excess Morphology of Dark Matter in Simulations of the Milky Way Galaxy”\n\n**Kevork N. Abazajian, Jason Kumar, Oscar Macias**  \n[arXiv:2609.34155](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.34155)\n\nThis Comment identifies a consequential error in the dark-matter templates of Muru et al.: their “quadratic dark matter” map used\n\n\\[\n\\left[\\int \\rho\\,s^2\\,ds\\right]^2\n\\]\n\nrather than the annihilation \\(J\\)-factor,\n\n\\[\nJ=\\int \\rho^2\\,ds.\n\\]\n\nBecause squaring an already projected positive map preserves its isophote geometry, the published similarity between dark-matter and old-stellar-population contours was largely built into the erroneous construction. Recalculation with the proper line-of-sight integral does not support the claimed morphological degeneracy.\n\n##### History\n\nThe original [Muru et al. PRL](https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.06314) argued that realistic, merger-disturbed Milky-Way dark-matter halos could look essentially indistinguishable from the boxy stellar bulge, weakening morphology as a discriminator for the GeV excess. The Comment attacks the central observable rather than a secondary numerical detail; unless the original authors supply a different corrected analysis, their main conclusion should not be used.\n\nThe correction does not establish that the excess is dark matter, nor does it resolve diffuse-emission and point-source systematics. It restores only the narrower conclusion that a correctly calculated annihilation morphology remains meaningfully distinguishable from a projected stellar template in these simulations.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-35697", "arxiv": "2609.35697", "title": "JWST unveils a dust-obscured supernova associated with a gamma-ray burst", "authors": "B. Schneider et al.", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "JWST spectroscopy reveals a dust-obscured supernova associated with GRB 240825A, identifying a massive-star progenitor despite earlier optical nondetections.", "path": "2026/09/29/index.html#paper-2609-35697", "reportDate": "29 September 2026", "searchText": "JWST unveils a dust-obscured supernova associated with a gamma-ray burst B. Schneider et al. 2609.35697 JWST spectroscopy reveals a dust-obscured supernova associated with GRB 240825A, identifying a massive-star progenitor despite earlier optical nondetections. 2026-09-29 29 September 2026 Gamma rays Compact objects #### ★★★ 3. JWST unveils a dust-obscured supernova associated with a gamma-ray burst\n\n**B. Schneider et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.35697](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.35697)\n\nGRB 240825A, at \\(z=0.659\\), had conflicting prompt-emission classifications and no supernova in deep ground-based observations. JWST/NIRSpec spectroscopy obtained 40.1 rest-frame days after the burst is decomposed pixel by pixel into host and transient components.\n\nA point source at the burst position shows broad features resembling SN 1998bw. The sightline has \\(A_V=1.37\\pm0.08\\) mag; the supernova supplies \\(10\\!-\\!15\\%\\) of the NIRSpec flux but would have contributed only about 1% of a contemporaneous ground-based optical image. After extinction correction its luminosity is comparable to SN 1998bw, securely identifying a massive-star progenitor.\n\n##### History\n\nA previous multiwavelength study found hybrid merger/collapsar prompt properties and only an optical limit, \\(m_r>26.1\\), while already suggesting that dust could conceal an ordinary broad-lined Type Ic supernova. JWST converts that inference into a spectroscopic detection. Relative to the foundational GRB 980425/SN 1998bw association, the novelty is not another bright nearby GRB-SN but recovery of one beneath a bright, dusty and unusually metal-rich host.\n\nThis strengthens the warning that some apparently “supernova-less” or duration-ambiguous bursts are obscured collapsars. The inferred luminosity remains sensitive to host subtraction, extinction law and the adopted SN template; a single event cannot establish how common this failure mode is.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-32306", "arxiv": "2609.32306", "title": "Constraints on Supermassive Black Hole Binaries from the Lack of Resolvable Sources in the NANOGrav 15-yr Dataset", "authors": "NANOGrav Collaboration; Emiko C. Gardiner et al.", "tags": ["Compact objects"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Combining the NANOGrav background with the absence of resolved continuous-wave sources favors more numerous, lower-mass supermassive-black-hole binaries within the tested population models.", "path": "2026/09/29/index.html#paper-2609-32306", "reportDate": "29 September 2026", "searchText": "Constraints on Supermassive Black Hole Binaries from the Lack of Resolvable Sources in the NANOGrav 15-yr Dataset NANOGrav Collaboration; Emiko C. Gardiner et al. 2609.32306 Combining the NANOGrav background with the absence of resolved continuous-wave sources favors more numerous, lower-mass supermassive-black-hole binaries within the tested population models. 2026-09-29 29 September 2026 Compact objects #### ★★★ 4. Constraints on Supermassive Black Hole Binaries from the Lack of Resolvable Sources in the NANOGrav 15-yr Dataset\n\n**NANOGrav Collaboration; Emiko C. Gardiner et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.32306](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.32306)\n\nSemi-analytic supermassive-black-hole-binary populations are confronted jointly with NANOGrav’s stochastic-background spectrum and its nondetection of a convincing continuous wave. The absence of resolved systems disfavors backgrounds dominated by a few ultramassive nearby binaries and instead favors many lower-mass contributors.\n\nCompared with the background-only inference, the joint posterior moves toward higher galaxy-merger rates, smaller scatter in the black-hole–bulge relation, shorter binary lifetimes and steeper environmental hardening. The highest-S/N expected sources cluster around chirp mass \\(10^{9.6}M_\\odot\\), luminosity distance \\(1.3\\) Gpc and frequency \\(6\\) nHz.\n\n##### History\n\nNANOGrav’s 15-year analyses established evidence for a nanohertz background and separately found only weak individual-source candidates near 4 and 170 nHz. The 4-nHz feature lost much of its significance when the correlated background was modeled, while the higher-frequency candidate lacked a plausible host. ([Continuous-wave search](https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.16222))\n\nThis is the first population analysis to use that *absence* quantitatively alongside the detected background, breaking part of the number-versus-mass degeneracy. The result is conditional on semi-analytic prescriptions for galaxy pairing, black-hole scaling relations and sub-parsec hardening. It is not yet a measurement of any one environmental parameter, and the low-frequency candidate should not be interpreted as a resolved binary.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-33990", "arxiv": "2609.33990", "title": "First measurement of the antihydrogen production cross section through charge exchange of low-energy antiprotons with orthopositronium", "authors": "GBAR Collaboration", "tags": ["Particle physics"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "GBAR measures antihydrogen production cross sections through antiproton–positronium charge exchange at two beam energies, calibrating a reaction needed for future experiments.", "path": "2026/09/29/index.html#paper-2609-33990", "reportDate": "29 September 2026", "searchText": "First measurement of the antihydrogen production cross section through charge exchange of low-energy antiprotons with orthopositronium GBAR Collaboration 2609.33990 GBAR measures antihydrogen production cross sections through antiproton–positronium charge exchange at two beam energies, calibrating a reaction needed for future experiments. 2026-09-29 29 September 2026 Particle physics #### ★★★ 5. First measurement of the antihydrogen production cross section through charge exchange of low-energy antiprotons with orthopositronium\n\n**GBAR Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2609.33990](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.33990)\n\nGBAR measures \\(\\bar p+\\mathrm{Ps}\\rightarrow\\bar H+e^-\\) at two antiproton energies:\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(6.2\\,{\\rm keV})\n =\\left(14.1\\pm1.3_{\\rm stat}^{+2.2}_{-1.4}{}_{\\rm sys}\\right)\n \\times10^{-16}\\ {\\rm cm^2},\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(4.15\\,{\\rm keV})\n =\\left(8.7\\pm2.4_{\\rm stat}^{+1.5}_{-0.09}{}_{\\rm sys}\\right)\n \\times10^{-16}\\ {\\rm cm^2}.\n\\]\n\nBoth agree with recent three-body calculations. This is the first direct charge-exchange cross-section measurement made with an antiproton beam.\n\n##### History\n\nGBAR previously reported the first antihydrogen beam produced through this channel, at only \\(3\\!-\\!4\\sigma\\), without extracting a cross section. ([2023 production result](https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.15801)) The new result converts proof of production into a calibrated atomic-collision measurement and validates the reaction-rate calculations used to design GBAR’s two-step \\(\\bar H^+\\) source.\n\nIt is enabling metrology rather than a gravity or CPT test: only two kinetic energies are measured, the second positron-capture step remains rate-limiting, and no conclusion about antihydrogen free fall follows directly.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-31833", "arxiv": "2609.31833", "title": "Neutrino Direct Simulation Monte Carlo: Accurate modeling of out-of-equilibrium decaying cosmological relics", "authors": "Kensuke Akita, Miguel Escudero, Oleksii Ihnatenko, Maksym Ovchynnikov", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The neutrino DSMC framework couples decaying relics, nonthermal neutrino spectra and Big Bang nucleosynthesis, with validation against independent transport solvers.", "path": "2026/09/29/index.html#paper-2609-31833", "reportDate": "29 September 2026", "searchText": "Neutrino Direct Simulation Monte Carlo: Accurate modeling of out-of-equilibrium decaying cosmological relics Kensuke Akita, Miguel Escudero, Oleksii Ihnatenko, Maksym Ovchynnikov 2609.31833 The neutrino DSMC framework couples decaying relics, nonthermal neutrino spectra and Big Bang nucleosynthesis, with validation against independent transport solvers. 2026-09-29 29 September 2026 Neutrinos BSM #### ★★☆ 6. Neutrino Direct Simulation Monte Carlo: Accurate modeling of out-of-equilibrium decaying cosmological relics\n\n**Kensuke Akita, Miguel Escudero, Oleksii Ihnatenko, Maksym Ovchynnikov**  \n[arXiv:2609.31833](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.31833)\n\nThe \\(\\nu\\)DSMC framework is extended to evolve a decaying relic, nonthermal neutrino spectra, the electromagnetic plasma, cosmic expansion and Big Bang nucleosynthesis together. It includes inverse decays, flavor conversion, quantum statistics and pion production by energetic neutrinos.\n\nBenchmarking against independent quantum-kinetic and Boltzmann solvers shows excellent agreement, with a computational advantage when spectra become strongly nonthermal. Momentum-averaged treatments can predict even the wrong sign of \\(\\Delta N_{\\rm eff}\\) and substantially incorrect light-element yields.\n\n##### History\n\nThe original \\(\\nu\\)DSMC work introduced Monte Carlo neutrino transport for new-physics cosmologies, while the subsequent precision implementation reproduced standard decoupling at \\(N_{\\rm eff}=3.0439\\pm0.0006\\). ([Method paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.07378); [precision validation](https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.08379))\n\nThe present paper supplies the missing end-to-end relic-decay and BBN machinery, making it a useful computational advance. It does not yet provide a universal revised exclusion plot: constraints remain model-dependent, and nuclear-rate, hadronic-decay and flavor-coherence uncertainties must still be propagated separately.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-33364", "arxiv": "2609.33364", "title": "Evidence for an extended Galactic proton component beyond the cosmic-ray knee", "authors": "Jieshuang Wang", "tags": ["Cosmic rays"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Fits across the cosmic-ray spectrum favor a Galactic proton contribution beyond 100 PeV, while source-population and transport interpretations remain degenerate.", "path": "2026/09/29/index.html#paper-2609-33364", "reportDate": "29 September 2026", "searchText": "Evidence for an extended Galactic proton component beyond the cosmic-ray knee Jieshuang Wang 2609.33364 Fits across the cosmic-ray spectrum favor a Galactic proton contribution beyond 100 PeV, while source-population and transport interpretations remain degenerate. 2026-09-29 29 September 2026 Cosmic rays #### ★★☆ 7. Evidence for an extended Galactic proton component beyond the cosmic-ray knee\n\n**Jieshuang Wang**  \n[arXiv:2609.33364](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.33364)\n\nFits to proton and helium spectra from \\(0.1\\) to \\(1000\\) PeV disfavor interpreting the few-PeV knee as a single terminal proton cutoff. When the fit is anchored down to tens of GeV, a proton contribution extending beyond \\(100\\) PeV is required, either as a continuous component or as a distinct component with a break near the knee.\n\nA transport break can produce the latter behavior without requiring ordinary supernova remnants to accelerate protons continuously to \\(100\\) PeV; extreme Galactic accelerators such as super-Eddington X-ray binaries are discussed as possible contributors.\n\n##### History\n\nLHAASO directly established a proton spectrum that hardens toward 3 PeV and then softens sharply, while its helium spectrum hardens near 1.1 PeV and softens around 7 PeV. Those measurements already suggested multiple Galactic components rather than a featureless rigidity cutoff. ([Protons](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.14447); [helium](https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.05013))\n\nThe new paper extends this into a full-spectrum consistency argument and makes the \\(>100\\)-PeV proton tail explicit. It is not a new composition measurement, however. Its significance depends on combining experiments with different energy scales and hadronic models, and the data do not distinguish a new source population from energy-dependent transport.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-31836", "arxiv": "2609.31836", "title": "Discovery of minute-scale polarization-angle rotation in a supermassive-black-hole jet", "authors": "Aristeidis Polychronakis, Ioannis Liodakis, Dmitry Blinov, Anastasia Glykopoulou, Sebastian Kiehlmann, Karan Pal, Georgios F. Paraschos, Lena Debbrecht, Swati Ravi, Herman L. Marshall", "tags": ["Compact objects"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Minute-scale optical polarization rotation in PKS 1510−089 coincides with a new radio-jet component, supporting a possible interaction between moving and standing shocks.", "path": "2026/09/29/index.html#paper-2609-31836", "reportDate": "29 September 2026", "searchText": "Discovery of minute-scale polarization-angle rotation in a supermassive-black-hole jet Aristeidis Polychronakis, Ioannis Liodakis, Dmitry Blinov, Anastasia Glykopoulou, Sebastian Kiehlmann, Karan Pal, Georgios F. Paraschos, Lena Debbrecht, Swati Ravi, Herman L. Marshall 2609.31836 Minute-scale optical polarization rotation in PKS 1510−089 coincides with a new radio-jet component, supporting a possible interaction between moving and standing shocks. 2026-09-29 29 September 2026 Compact objects #### ★★☆ 8. Discovery of minute-scale polarization-angle rotation in a supermassive-black-hole jet\n\n**Aristeidis Polychronakis, Ioannis Liodakis, Dmitry Blinov, Anastasia Glykopoulou, Sebastian Kiehlmann, Karan Pal, Georgios F. Paraschos, Lena Debbrecht, Swati Ravi, Herman L. Marshall**  \n[arXiv:2609.31836](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.31836)\n\nOptical monitoring of PKS 1510−089 finds a coherent \\(136^\\circ\\) polarization-angle rotation in about 80 minutes, corresponding to roughly \\(2450^\\circ\\) per day. Contemporaneous 43-GHz VLBA imaging shows a new component leaving the radio core with apparent speed \\(17.8c\\) and inferred Doppler factor near 42.\n\nThe authors favor interaction between a moving shock and a standing-shock complex. The stable, low polarization degree and absence of detected periodicity or a strong broadband flare are argued against simple turbulent-walk, kink and reconnection interpretations.\n\n##### History\n\nPreviously catalogued coherent rotations normally lasted days; the earlier speed record was approximately \\(360^\\circ\\) per day. A 2014 rotation in 3C 454.3 had similar structure and a component ejection but lasted about twelve hours. Correcting for the different Doppler factors brings its intrinsic timescale within roughly \\(2\\sigma\\) of the present event.\n\nThe new observation is the first resolved intra-night rotation, not an entirely new physical mechanism. The shock interpretation is plausible but circumstantial: the VLBA cadence does not time the interaction to 80-minute precision, and Fermi-LAT’s unfavorable viewing geometry prevents a meaningful test for a simultaneous short gamma-ray flare."}, {"id": "paper-2609-31457", "arxiv": "2609.31457", "title": "4FHL: The Fourth Catalog of Hard Fermi-LAT Sources", "authors": "Fermi-LAT Collaboration", "tags": ["Gamma rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The 4FHL catalog uses 16 years of Fermi-LAT observations to identify 673 sources between 50 GeV and 2 TeV, improving sensitivity and localization over 2FHL.", "path": "2026/09/28/index.html#paper-2609-31457", "reportDate": "28 September 2026", "searchText": "4FHL: The Fourth Catalog of Hard Fermi-LAT Sources Fermi-LAT Collaboration 2609.31457 The 4FHL catalog uses 16 years of Fermi-LAT observations to identify 673 sources between 50 GeV and 2 TeV, improving sensitivity and localization over 2FHL. 2026-09-28 28 September 2026 Gamma rays #### ★★★ 1. 4FHL: The Fourth Catalog of Hard Fermi-LAT Sources\n\n**Fermi-LAT Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2609.31457](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.31457)\n\nUsing 16 years of LAT data, the collaboration constructs the deepest all-sky catalog yet in the \\(50~{\\rm GeV}-2~{\\rm TeV}\\) band. The catalog contains **673 sources**:\n\n- 84.7% have extragalactic associations, predominantly BL Lac objects;\n- 36 associated sources lie at \\(z>1\\);\n- approximately 10% are Galactic, chiefly pulsar-wind nebulae and supernova remnants;\n- about 5% remain unassociated or uncertain;\n- 20 have no counterparts in previous LAT catalogs.\n\nSensitivity and localization are each improved by approximately a factor of two relative to the previous catalog covering the same energy range.\n\n##### History\n\nThe 2015 [2FHL catalog](https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.04449) used 80 months of Pass-8 data and contained 360 sources above 50 GeV. 4FHL nearly doubles that sample while preserving the same energy threshold, making this a major catalog-level advance rather than a routine duration update. The intervening 3FHL catalog contained more objects but extended down to 10 GeV, so it is not the appropriate direct sensitivity comparison.\n\nThe larger sample should materially improve blazar luminosity-function, extragalactic-background and CTA-target studies. Its principal selection limitation is the 16-year time average: steady hard-spectrum sources are favored over brief flares. At high redshift, EBL absorption also makes the 36 \\(z>1\\) objects a spectrally selected subset rather than an unbiased blazar population.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-30366", "arxiv": "2609.30366", "title": "Hybrid Partial Dressing: Correct Effective Potentials at All Temperatures", "authors": "Raphaël Berthiaume, David Curtin, Michael Luke, Andrija Rasovic, Jyotirmoy Roy", "tags": ["BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Hybrid Partial Dressing combines all-temperature scalar-potential resummation with two-loop exactness and agreement with dimensional reduction in its controlled regime.", "path": "2026/09/28/index.html#paper-2609-30366", "reportDate": "28 September 2026", "searchText": "Hybrid Partial Dressing: Correct Effective Potentials at All Temperatures Raphaël Berthiaume, David Curtin, Michael Luke, Andrija Rasovic, Jyotirmoy Roy 2609.30366 Hybrid Partial Dressing combines all-temperature scalar-potential resummation with two-loop exactness and agreement with dimensional reduction in its controlled regime. 2026-09-28 28 September 2026 BSM #### ★★★ 2. Hybrid Partial Dressing: Correct Effective Potentials at All Temperatures\n\n**Raphaël Berthiaume, David Curtin, Michael Luke, Andrija Rasovic, Jyotirmoy Roy**  \n[arXiv:2609.30366](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.30366)\n\nThe authors introduce Hybrid Partial Dressing, a resummation prescription for finite-temperature scalar effective potentials. It is designed to remain valid outside the high-temperature expansion while being exactly correct through two loops and avoiding the overlapping-momentum problem of conventional partial dressing.\n\nIn multi-scalar \\(\\mathbb Z_2\\)-breaking examples, HPD reproduces two-loop dimensional reduction in the high-temperature regime and continues smoothly into temperatures where dimensional reduction becomes unreliable. Renormalization-group improvement is incorporated, and the resulting potentials are used to calculate first-order-transition gravitational-wave spectra.\n\n##### History\n\nConventional Parwani and Arnold–Espinosa daisy prescriptions are easy to use but can miscount diagrams or fail when thermal and zero-temperature masses are comparable. The authors’ earlier [Partial Dressing framework](https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.00466) removed the high-temperature restriction, while [Optimized Partial Dressing](https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.08218) improved numerical convergence and substantially altered some gravitational-wave forecasts. HPD’s advance is to combine all-temperature applicability with explicit two-loop exactness and agreement with dimensional reduction where the latter is controlled.\n\nThis potentially removes an important theoretical systematic from BSM phase-transition scans. The present demonstrations are nevertheless scalar theories. Gauge dependence, gauge-boson resummation and realistic multi-field tunneling calculations remain to be established before HPD can be treated as a universal replacement for existing electroweak-transition pipelines.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-30378", "arxiv": "2609.30378", "title": "Mass and spin properties of black-hole mergers reveal formation in triples", "authors": "Jakob Stegmann, Aleksandra Olejak", "tags": ["Compact objects"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Population synthesis of hierarchical stellar triples reproduces several observed black-hole merger mass and spin distributions, while formation-channel degeneracies remain.", "path": "2026/09/28/index.html#paper-2609-30378", "reportDate": "28 September 2026", "searchText": "Mass and spin properties of black-hole mergers reveal formation in triples Jakob Stegmann, Aleksandra Olejak 2609.30378 Population synthesis of hierarchical stellar triples reproduces several observed black-hole merger mass and spin distributions, while formation-channel degeneracies remain. 2026-09-28 28 September 2026 Compact objects #### ★★☆ 3. Mass and spin properties of black-hole mergers reveal formation in triples\n\n**Jakob Stegmann, Aleksandra Olejak**  \n[arXiv:2609.30378](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.30378)\n\nPopulation-synthesis and secular-dynamics calculations follow massive hierarchical triples through stellar evolution and tertiary-induced black-hole mergers. The resulting population simultaneously reproduces several structures inferred from current gravitational-wave data:\n\n\\[\nm_1\\simeq10\\,M_\\odot\n\\]\n\nas the dominant primary-mass peak, a preference for nearly equal masses, and broad, asymmetric distributions of the effective and precessing-spin parameters \\(\\chi_{\\rm eff}\\) and \\(\\chi_p\\). The model predicts a sharp decline near \\(m_1\\simeq30\\,M_\\odot\\), above which another formation channel may dominate.\n\n##### History\n\nField-triple calculations previously established that Lidov–Kozai excitation can produce mergers with misaligned spins and \\(\\chi_{\\rm eff}\\) concentrated near zero ([Antonini et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.06614); [Fragione and Kocsis](https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.00407)). Separate work found that triples can produce distinctive mass-ratio distributions, but did not claim agreement with the complete observed population. The authors’ own recent hierarchical inference argued statistically that a large triple contribution is favored ([Stegmann et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.15873)).\n\nThe new paper supplies the missing forward-model demonstration: one channel can reproduce the low-mass peak, mass ratios and two spin observables together. That is substantive, but “reveal formation in triples” remains stronger than the evidence warrants. Triple initial conditions, natal kicks, common-envelope evolution and stellar-spin transport are uncertain, while selection effects and mixtures of isolated-binary, cluster and triple channels can imitate parts of the same distributions.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-31611", "arxiv": "2609.31611", "title": "A Multidimensional General-Relativistic Boltzmann Solver for Neutrino Transport", "authors": "Arthur Offermans, Harry Ho-Yin Ng, Patrick Chi-Kit Cheong, Anthony Mezzacappa, Tjonnie Guang Feng Li", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A six-dimensional Boltzmann neutrino solver is integrated with Gmunu, using interaction-kernel factorization to reduce the cost of implicit collision calculations.", "path": "2026/09/28/index.html#paper-2609-31611", "reportDate": "28 September 2026", "searchText": "A Multidimensional General-Relativistic Boltzmann Solver for Neutrino Transport Arthur Offermans, Harry Ho-Yin Ng, Patrick Chi-Kit Cheong, Anthony Mezzacappa, Tjonnie Guang Feng Li 2609.31611 A six-dimensional Boltzmann neutrino solver is integrated with Gmunu, using interaction-kernel factorization to reduce the cost of implicit collision calculations. 2026-09-28 28 September 2026 Neutrinos Compact objects #### ★★☆ 4. A Multidimensional General-Relativistic Boltzmann Solver for Neutrino Transport\n\n**Arthur Offermans, Harry Ho-Yin Ng, Patrick Chi-Kit Cheong, Anthony Mezzacappa, Tjonnie Guang Feng Li**  \n[arXiv:2609.31611](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.31611)\n\nThe full six-dimensional Boltzmann equation for neutrinos is incorporated into the general-relativistic MHD code `Gmunu`, using finite-volume discretization in spatial, energy and angular coordinates.\n\nA factorization based on the Legendre expansion of interaction kernels reduces the cost of the stiff implicit collision solve. In a one-dimensional core-collapse snapshot with 14 angular bins, it is approximately **300 times faster** than full-matrix LU decomposition. Tests show quadratic numerical convergence and roughly 1% energy conservation with 20 energy bins. Compared with M1 transport, free-streaming luminosities can differ by about 10%, although mean energies and fluid profiles at bounce agree reasonably well.\n\n##### History\n\nFully general-relativistic multidimensional Boltzmann solvers already exist, notably the discrete-ordinate implementation of [Akaho et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.10780), while three-dimensional supernova calculations with six-dimensional transport have been demonstrated in Newtonian or approximate-GR settings ([Iwakami et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.02091)). The novelty is therefore not the first multidimensional Boltzmann calculation, but the flexible integration with a GRMHD framework and the large reduction in the species- and energy-coupled collision cost.\n\nThe validation remains mostly one-dimensional and near core bounce. The decisive test will be whether the optimization makes long-duration multidimensional GRMHD simulations practical while retaining sufficient angular and energy resolution; the quoted 300-fold gain is relative to an especially expensive dense-matrix baseline.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-30616", "arxiv": "2609.30616", "title": "Tracing the Host-Galaxy Environments of FRBs with a New Sample of Localized Sources Detected with the DSA-110", "authors": "Frank M. Verdi, Vikram Ravi, Myles B. Sherman, Jakob T. Faber, Stella Koch Ocker, Kritti Sharma, Casey J. Law, Gregg Hallinan, Kaitlyn Shin, Liam Connor", "tags": ["Compact objects"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A homogeneous 43-burst DSA-110 sample links host dispersion, rotation measures and scattering to dense, magnetized host-galaxy environments.", "path": "2026/09/28/index.html#paper-2609-30616", "reportDate": "28 September 2026", "searchText": "Tracing the Host-Galaxy Environments of FRBs with a New Sample of Localized Sources Detected with the DSA-110 Frank M. Verdi, Vikram Ravi, Myles B. Sherman, Jakob T. Faber, Stella Koch Ocker, Kritti Sharma, Casey J. Law, Gregg Hallinan, Kaitlyn Shin, Liam Connor 2609.30616 A homogeneous 43-burst DSA-110 sample links host dispersion, rotation measures and scattering to dense, magnetized host-galaxy environments. 2026-09-28 28 September 2026 Compact objects #### ★★☆ 5. Tracing the Host-Galaxy Environments of FRBs with a New Sample of Localized Sources Detected with the DSA-110\n\n**Frank M. Verdi, Vikram Ravi, Myles B. Sherman, Jakob T. Faber, Stella Koch Ocker, Kritti Sharma, Casey J. Law, Gregg Hallinan, Kaitlyn Shin, Liam Connor**  \n[arXiv:2609.30616](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.30616)\n\nThe paper presents 24 localized DSA-110 FRBs with spectroscopic host redshifts, including four near or beyond \\(z=1\\), and combines them with earlier detections to form a homogeneous 43-burst sample.\n\nAfter subtracting Milky-Way and intergalactic contributions, host dispersion and rotation measures are significantly correlated. Scattered bursts also approximately follow the scattering–DM relation measured for Galactic pulsars. The joint evidence indicates that much of the excess DM, RM and temporal broadening originates in dense, magnetized host-galaxy plasma, plausibly H II regions associated with young magnetars.\n\n##### History\n\nThe first uniform DSA-110 sample contained 11 localized FRBs, while an earlier polarimetric analysis of ten DSA events already indicated appreciable host RM contributions ([Law et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03344); [Sherman et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.06816)). The new 43-object sample turns those hints into a joint DM–RM–scattering environmental result and extends the redshift baseline beyond unity.\n\nThe conclusion favors young, core-collapse magnetars but does not uniquely identify them: compact binaries or older neutron stars passing through dense gas could produce similar propagation measures. Host inclination, uncertain halo/intergalactic DM subtraction and DSA’s fluence and scattering selection functions must be propagated before inferring the intrinsic progenitor fraction.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-30376", "arxiv": "2609.30376", "title": "Saturation Mechanism of Cosmic Ray Streaming Instabilities with a Shell Distribution", "authors": "Mohamad Shalaby, Rouven Lemmerz, Christoph Pfrommer", "tags": ["Cosmic rays"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Kinetic and fluid-PIC simulations identify gyro-phase bunching as the saturation mechanism of a cosmic-ray streaming instability in the studied shell-distribution regime.", "path": "2026/09/28/index.html#paper-2609-30376", "reportDate": "28 September 2026", "searchText": "Saturation Mechanism of Cosmic Ray Streaming Instabilities with a Shell Distribution Mohamad Shalaby, Rouven Lemmerz, Christoph Pfrommer 2609.30376 Kinetic and fluid-PIC simulations identify gyro-phase bunching as the saturation mechanism of a cosmic-ray streaming instability in the studied shell-distribution regime. 2026-09-28 28 September 2026 Cosmic rays #### ★★☆ 6. Saturation Mechanism of Cosmic Ray Streaming Instabilities with a Shell Distribution\n\n**Mohamad Shalaby, Rouven Lemmerz, Christoph Pfrommer**  \n[arXiv:2609.30376](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.30376)\n\nFully kinetic PIC and fluid-PIC simulations show that a gyroresonant cosmic-ray streaming instability saturates through **gyro-phase bunching around the excited Alfvén waves**. Simulations with and without Landau closures reach the same saturation amplitude and fully isotropize the cosmic rays in the wave frame.\n\nAt the simulated parameters,\n\n\\[\nv_A=0.01c,\\qquad n_{\\rm CR}/n_i=0.01,\n\\]\n\nnonlinear Landau damping is therefore not responsible for either saturation or isotropization. Increasing the domain size by a factor of ten leaves the saturation amplitude unchanged, supporting a local wave–particle mechanism.\n\n##### History\n\nEarlier PIC and MHD-PIC studies established quasilinear growth, nonlinear pitch-angle scattering and eventual isotropization, but generally characterized saturation through wave growth, damping and redistribution of the CR anisotropy ([Holcomb and Spitkovsky](https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.01951); [Bai et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.10219)). The new result identifies a more specific microscopic mechanism and explicitly falsifies nonlinear Landau damping as the controlling process in this regime.\n\nThe extrapolation caveat is unusually important: the adopted CR density is many orders of magnitude above typical diffuse-ISM values, and \\(v_A\\) is also comparatively large. The result challenges a common closure assumption in galaxy simulations but does not yet demonstrate that gyro-phase bunching controls saturation under realistic Galactic conditions.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-31340", "arxiv": "2609.31340", "title": "Natural Saturation of Sterile-Neutrino Dark-Matter Resonant Production by a High Lepton Flavor Asymmetry", "authors": "Dmitry Gorbunov, Dmitry Kalashnikov", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Neutrinos", "Oscillations", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Active-neutrino flavor equilibration can interrupt delayed resonant sterile-neutrino production, causing the dark-matter abundance to saturate at high initial lepton asymmetry.", "path": "2026/09/28/index.html#paper-2609-31340", "reportDate": "28 September 2026", "searchText": "Natural Saturation of Sterile-Neutrino Dark-Matter Resonant Production by a High Lepton Flavor Asymmetry Dmitry Gorbunov, Dmitry Kalashnikov 2609.31340 Active-neutrino flavor equilibration can interrupt delayed resonant sterile-neutrino production, causing the dark-matter abundance to saturate at high initial lepton asymmetry. 2026-09-28 28 September 2026 Dark matter Neutrinos Oscillations BSM #### ★★☆ 7. Natural Saturation of Sterile-Neutrino Dark-Matter Resonant Production by a High Lepton Flavor Asymmetry\n\n**Dmitry Gorbunov, Dmitry Kalashnikov**  \n[arXiv:2609.31340](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.31340)\n\nAt very large primordial flavor asymmetry, the active–sterile resonance occurs later and at lower temperature. The authors show that this can overlap with the onset of active-neutrino flavor oscillations, which redistribute or erase the asymmetry driving resonant sterile-neutrino production.\n\nConsequently, the final abundance saturates: increasing the initial asymmetry no longer proportionally increases sterile-dark-matter production. The surviving resonant-production region spans approximately two orders of magnitude in the mixing parameter \\(\\sin^2 2\\theta\\).\n\n##### History\n\nThe Shi–Fuller mechanism has long relied on a lepton asymmetry to resonantly produce colder sterile dark matter. Recent work reopened much larger asymmetries consistent with BBN and used them to enlarge the viable production region ([Vogel and Abazajian](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.18752)). The present paper adds a countervailing effect that was not included in that simple monotonic picture: sufficiently delayed production encounters active-flavor equilibration and ceases to benefit from a larger initial asymmetry.\n\nThe conclusion is sensitive to flavor-dependent initial conditions and the treatment of quantum kinetics through the QCD epoch. Its discussion of pion condensation and an associated gravitational-wave transition is more speculative and should be separated from the better-motivated abundance-saturation result."}, {"id": "paper-2609-29911", "arxiv": "2609.29911", "title": "Plasma Effects Suppress Mixing-Induced Collisional Freeze-In", "authors": "Shao-Ping Li, Josef Pradler", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A flavor-covariant quantum-kinetic calculation finds strong in-medium suppression of sterile-neutrino production by electroweak gauge-boson decays in the Standard Model plasma.", "path": "2026/09/25/index.html#paper-2609-29911", "reportDate": "25 September 2026", "searchText": "Plasma Effects Suppress Mixing-Induced Collisional Freeze-In Shao-Ping Li, Josef Pradler 2609.29911 A flavor-covariant quantum-kinetic calculation finds strong in-medium suppression of sterile-neutrino production by electroweak gauge-boson decays in the Standard Model plasma. 2026-09-25 25 September 2026 Neutrinos Dark matter BSM #### ★★★ 1. Plasma Effects Suppress Mixing-Induced Collisional Freeze-In\n\n**Shao-Ping Li, Josef Pradler**  \n[arXiv:2609.29911](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.29911)\n\nThe paper examines whether electroweak gauge-boson decays can produce keV–MeV sterile-neutrino dark matter through its small vacuum mixing with active neutrinos. A flavor-covariant Kadanoff–Baym calculation shows that the collision rate is controlled by the **in-medium**, rather than vacuum, mixing angle:\n\n\\[\n\\sin^2 2\\theta_{\\rm eff}=\n\\frac{m_N^4\\sin^2 2\\theta}\n{(m_N^2\\cos2\\theta-\\Pi_\\nu)^2+m_N^4\\sin^22\\theta}.\n\\]\n\nFor a 10-keV sterile neutrino, the predicted abundance is approximately \\(10^{20}\\) times smaller than in the semiclassical calculation; across the studied range the suppression is \\(10^{12}-10^{24}\\). Production from the Standard Model plasma therefore cannot supply all the dark matter.\n\n##### History\n\n[Datta, Roshan and Sil](https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.02030) previously used vacuum mixing in a Boltzmann collision term and found viable gauge-boson freeze-in. Conversely, [Lello, Boyanovsky and Pisarski](https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.07647) found strong medium suppression from finite-temperature Dirac equations. The new paper resolves that disagreement: the plasma rotates the propagation eigenstates, a multiplicative effect that adding thermal masses only to phase space misses. This is a substantial closure of a claimed production window, not a small rate correction. The conclusion applies to minimal Standard-Model-plasma production; resonant production from a lepton asymmetry, decays of additional heavy particles and other nonthermal channels remain viable. Narrow-width and quasiparticle approximations also warrant further checking near possible resonances.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-28668", "arxiv": "2609.28668", "title": "Composition-dependent acceleration of UHE Cosmic Rays in AGN electric fields", "authors": "E. Koutsoumpou, I. Contopoulos, A. Nathanail, A. Loules, D. Kazanas", "tags": ["Cosmic rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A voltage-sampling model connects AGN current-sheet acceleration with Auger composition measurements, favoring proton-poor injection in tested nearby-source scenarios.", "path": "2026/09/25/index.html#paper-2609-28668", "reportDate": "25 September 2026", "searchText": "Composition-dependent acceleration of UHE Cosmic Rays in AGN electric fields E. Koutsoumpou, I. Contopoulos, A. Nathanail, A. Loules, D. Kazanas 2609.28668 A voltage-sampling model connects AGN current-sheet acceleration with Auger composition measurements, favoring proton-poor injection in tested nearby-source scenarios. 2026-09-25 25 September 2026 Cosmic rays #### ★★★ 2. Composition-dependent acceleration of UHE Cosmic Rays in AGN electric fields\n\n**E. Koutsoumpou, I. Contopoulos, A. Nathanail, A. Loules, D. Kazanas**  \n[arXiv:2609.28668](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.28668)\n\nThe authors connect particle acceleration in near-horizon current sheets of magnetically arrested AGN disks directly to Auger composition observables. Nuclei sample a log-normal distribution of effective voltage fractions \\(\\eta\\), acquire energy proportional to \\(Z\\eta\\mathcal V_{\\max}\\), and are propagated through photodisintegration, photopion, Bethe–Heitler and adiabatic losses.\n\nBayesian fits to the 2025 Auger deep-learning \\(X_{\\max}\\) moments favor a proton-poor injection at source distances of 10–30 Mpc. At 30 Mpc,\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{\\ln\\eta}=0.59^{+0.13}_{-0.10},\n\\]\n\ncorresponding to a factor \\(\\sim1.8\\) dispersion in sampled voltage. A template containing about 53% hydrogen is disfavored by \\(\\Delta\\chi^2=18-26\\); both tested templates fit poorly at 100 Mpc.\n\n##### History\n\nGRMHD simulations had already identified reconnection layers and large electric potentials in magnetically arrested disks ([Ripperda et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.15115); [Nathanail et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.03689)), while Auger source fits had established that hard, relatively heavy injection can reproduce the spectrum and composition ([Auger combined fit](https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.07155)). The new contribution is the explicit mapping from stochastic voltage sampling to both the mean and width of the latest [Auger \\(X_{\\max}\\) distributions](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.111.022003). The preferred composition is not independently derived—the successful injection template is itself Auger-inspired—and the log-normal voltage distribution is phenomenological. A joint fit of spectrum, \\(X_{\\max}\\), anisotropy and source population would be needed before interpreting the distance preference as evidence for nearby AGN.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-29761", "arxiv": "2609.29761", "title": "Multi-messenger and multi-band signal from first-order phase transitions in proto-neutron stars", "authors": "Christian Ecker, Mauro Giliberti, Luciano Rezzolla", "tags": ["Multimessenger", "Neutrinos", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A large equation-of-state ensemble correlates kHz and MHz gravitational-wave signals with a delayed neutrino burst from phase transitions in proto-neutron stars.", "path": "2026/09/25/index.html#paper-2609-29761", "reportDate": "25 September 2026", "searchText": "Multi-messenger and multi-band signal from first-order phase transitions in proto-neutron stars Christian Ecker, Mauro Giliberti, Luciano Rezzolla 2609.29761 A large equation-of-state ensemble correlates kHz and MHz gravitational-wave signals with a delayed neutrino burst from phase transitions in proto-neutron stars. 2026-09-25 25 September 2026 Multimessenger Neutrinos Compact objects #### ★★★ 3. Multi-messenger and multi-band signal from first-order phase transitions in proto-neutron stars\n\n**Christian Ecker, Mauro Giliberti, Luciano Rezzolla**  \n[arXiv:2609.29761](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.29761)\n\nA first-order hadron–quark transition in a proto-neutron star should produce three correlated signals: a kHz gravitational-wave burst from global stellar contraction and oscillation, MHz acoustic gravitational waves from colliding quark bubbles, and a delayed second neutrino burst.\n\nThe authors evolve accreting stars through approximately \\(5\\times10^4\\) model-agnostic equations of state. A 5% measurement of the kHz mode would reduce the logarithmic uncertainty in the predicted MHz frequency interval by about 8.7%. Their weighted population gives a median delay of roughly 2.1 seconds between the ordinary collapse neutrino burst and the transition-driven second burst.\n\n##### History\n\nPhase-transition-induced kHz emission and second neutrino bursts were studied separately in collapse simulations ([Abdikamalov et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.1700); [Sagert et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/0809.4225)), while bubble-collision MHz emission has recently been proposed for compact-star transitions ([Blas et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03171)). This is the first systematic attempt to correlate all three signals across a large equation-of-state ensemble, and apparently the first population estimate of the neutrino delay. The forecast is not yet a realistic detectability calculation: the stellar evolution uses cold equations of state, the kHz amplitude assumes maximally efficient mode excitation, and the MHz calculation adapts a simplified sound-shell treatment developed for cosmological transitions. MHz detection itself remains highly speculative.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-28629", "arxiv": "2609.28629", "title": "Probing the Metallicity Dependence of Fast Radio Burst Progenitors with CHIME/FRB Outrigger Dwarf Host Galaxies", "authors": "Y. Dong et al.", "tags": ["Compact objects"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A systematic CHIME/FRB Outrigger search identifies dwarf hosts and investigates metallicity differences between repeating and nonrepeating fast radio bursts.", "path": "2026/09/25/index.html#paper-2609-28629", "reportDate": "25 September 2026", "searchText": "Probing the Metallicity Dependence of Fast Radio Burst Progenitors with CHIME/FRB Outrigger Dwarf Host Galaxies Y. Dong et al. 2609.28629 A systematic CHIME/FRB Outrigger search identifies dwarf hosts and investigates metallicity differences between repeating and nonrepeating fast radio bursts. 2026-09-25 25 September 2026 Compact objects #### ★★☆ 4. Probing the Metallicity Dependence of Fast Radio Burst Progenitors with CHIME/FRB Outrigger Dwarf Host Galaxies\n\n**Y. Dong et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.28629](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.28629)\n\nAmong 78 accurately localized CHIME/FRB Outrigger events, the authors identify eight dwarf-galaxy candidates and spectroscopically confirm five new dwarf hosts. Combining these with the literature suggests a metallicity-dependent split:\n\n- nonrepeaters preferentially occupy relatively metal-rich dwarfs, generally \\(12+\\log({\\rm O/H})\\gtrsim8.35\\);\n- repeaters preferentially occupy lower-metallicity hosts;\n- nearly every repeater with a persistent radio source lies below approximately 8.15.\n\nNo correlation exceeding \\(3\\sigma\\) is found between metallicity and host dispersion, rotation measure or scattering.\n\n##### History\n\nEarlier demographic studies established that FRB hosts span a wide galaxy population ([Gordon et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.05465)), while the DSA sample suggested an overall preference for massive, metal-rich star-forming galaxies ([Sharma et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.16964)). Individual nonrepeaters in dwarfs were already known ([Bhandari et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.16790)). The advance is a systematic, uniformly selected dwarf-host search with direct gas-phase metallicities, revealing that the global high-metallicity preference may conceal different repeater and nonrepeater channels. The evidence remains sample-limited—particularly for repeaters—and “nonrepeating” is an exposure-dependent classification. Strong-line metallicity calibrations, heterogeneous literature follow-up and optical host-selection effects can shift the proposed thresholds.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-28651", "arxiv": "2609.28651", "title": "Dynamically Stable Magnetic Fields in Relativistic Neutron Stars: A Diverse Landscape of GRMHD Equilibria", "authors": "Mai Li, Vasileios Paschalidis, Antonios Tsokaros, Maria Mutz, Koji Uryu", "tags": ["Compact objects"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Relativistic MHD simulations find diverse stable mixed magnetic-field configurations in neutron stars that retain memory of their initial current geometry.", "path": "2026/09/25/index.html#paper-2609-28651", "reportDate": "25 September 2026", "searchText": "Dynamically Stable Magnetic Fields in Relativistic Neutron Stars: A Diverse Landscape of GRMHD Equilibria Mai Li, Vasileios Paschalidis, Antonios Tsokaros, Maria Mutz, Koji Uryu 2609.28651 Relativistic MHD simulations find diverse stable mixed magnetic-field configurations in neutron stars that retain memory of their initial current geometry. 2026-09-25 25 September 2026 Compact objects #### ★★☆ 5. Dynamically Stable Magnetic Fields in Relativistic Neutron Stars: A Diverse Landscape of GRMHD Equilibria\n\n**Mai Li, Vasileios Paschalidis, Antonios Tsokaros, Maria Mutz, Koji Uryu**  \n[arXiv:2609.28651](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.28651)\n\nFully relativistic MHD simulations evolve slowly rotating neutron stars with several self-consistent mixed poloidal–toroidal initial fields for tens of Alfvén times. The relaxed fields are neither unique nor universally dipolar: they retain memory of the initial current geometry and contain substantial higher multipoles.\n\nA more limited regularity survives—the angle-averaged toroidal-to-poloidal field-amplitude ratio is typically 20–40% through much of the stellar interior.\n\n##### History\n\nPurely poloidal and toroidal configurations were long known to be unstable, and previous calculations often suggested relaxation toward a broadly “twisted-torus” geometry ([Ciolfi and Rezzolla](https://arxiv.org/abs/1105.3971); [Sur et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.11858)). By starting from multiple self-consistent current structures and decomposing the relaxed fields into vector spherical harmonics, the new simulations demonstrate that the twisted-torus picture is not a unique dynamical attractor. This materially changes the interpretation of magnetar internal-field models. Stability is established only in ideal GRMHD over dynamical times: resistive evolution, Hall drift, crust formation, rapid rotation and realistic stratification could erase or reorganize the retained multipoles.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-29162", "arxiv": "2609.29162", "title": "Binary-pulsar Timing: A 3D Solar-System Accelerometer and Unknown-Source Probe", "authors": "Zheng-Long Wang, Zi-Qing Xia, Bo Zhang, Yi-Zhong Fan", "tags": ["Compact objects"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Timing measurements from 26 binary pulsars constrain the three-dimensional acceleration of the Solar-System barycenter and the presence of nearby unseen objects.", "path": "2026/09/25/index.html#paper-2609-29162", "reportDate": "25 September 2026", "searchText": "Binary-pulsar Timing: A 3D Solar-System Accelerometer and Unknown-Source Probe Zheng-Long Wang, Zi-Qing Xia, Bo Zhang, Yi-Zhong Fan 2609.29162 Timing measurements from 26 binary pulsars constrain the three-dimensional acceleration of the Solar-System barycenter and the presence of nearby unseen objects. 2026-09-25 25 September 2026 Compact objects #### ★★☆ 6. Binary-pulsar Timing: A 3D Solar-System Accelerometer and Unknown-Source Probe\n\n**Zheng-Long Wang, Zi-Qing Xia, Bo Zhang, Yi-Zhong Fan**  \n[arXiv:2609.29162](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.29162)\n\nOrbital-period derivatives from 26 detached binary pulsars are combined to reconstruct the full three-dimensional acceleration of the Solar-System barycenter. No anomalous acceleration is detected. Directional 95%-confidence limits are \\(0.198\\), \\(0.262\\) and \\(0.693~\\mu{\\rm as\\,yr^{-1}}\\) over 50%, 75% and the full sampled sky.\n\nIncluding both the observer- and pulsar-side response excludes a \\(10\\,M_\\odot\\) unseen object within 0.39, 0.34 and 0.21 pc over the corresponding sky fractions.\n\n##### History\n\n[Zakamska and Tremaine](https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0506548) introduced astronomical-clock limits on peculiar Solar-System acceleration, and later timing plus VLBI of PSR J0437−4715 improved the single-direction constraint. The new network reconstruction is the first three-dimensional binary-pulsar measurement and improves historical pulsar limits by factors of 12–28; it is also tighter than a matched Gaia EDR3 comparison over most directions. The mass bounds improve on a rescaled INPOP19a benchmark beyond roughly 0.2 pc, but that comparison is not confidence-matched. Full timing covariances are unavailable for much of the heterogeneous sample, only six or seven pulsars dominate the precision, and the method tests quasi-static perturbers rather than rapid flybys.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-28953", "arxiv": "2609.28953", "title": "Constraining the Quadratic-mode Amplitude Coupling in GW250114", "authors": "Yuxin Yang, Changfu Shi, Yi-Ming Hu", "tags": ["Compact objects"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A GW250114 ringdown analysis measures a quadratic-mode amplitude-coupling coefficient and compares it with numerical-relativity and perturbative predictions.", "path": "2026/09/25/index.html#paper-2609-28953", "reportDate": "25 September 2026", "searchText": "Constraining the Quadratic-mode Amplitude Coupling in GW250114 Yuxin Yang, Changfu Shi, Yi-Ming Hu 2609.28953 A GW250114 ringdown analysis measures a quadratic-mode amplitude-coupling coefficient and compares it with numerical-relativity and perturbative predictions. 2026-09-25 25 September 2026 Compact objects #### ★★☆ 7. Constraining the Quadratic-mode Amplitude Coupling in GW250114\n\n**Yuxin Yang, Changfu Shi, Yi-Ming Hu**  \n[arXiv:2609.28953](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.28953)\n\nThe GW250114 ringdown is fitted with the linear \\(220\\) mode, its first overtone \\(221\\), and the quadratic \\(220\\times220\\) mode. The analysis directly infers the nonlinear amplitude-coupling coefficient, which second-order perturbation theory predicts should depend on the remnant spin.\n\nThe measurement agrees with both numerical-relativity fits and second-order black-hole perturbation calculations; none differs from the posterior by more than \\(1.3\\sigma\\). Present data cannot discriminate between the competing spin-dependent predictions.\n\n##### History\n\nEarlier analyses reported evidence that a quadratic mode improves the GW250114 ringdown fit ([Yang, Shi and Hu](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.16903)) and, using more restrictive merger-informed priors, claimed approximately \\(3\\sigma\\) evidence for several nonlinear modes ([Wang et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.05734)). The new paper advances from asking whether a quadratic component is present to measuring its mode-specific coupling coefficient. Agreement with theory is encouraging but not yet a precision nonlinear-gravity test: the result remains sensitive to ringdown start time, the assumed mode set and correlations with the \\(221\\) overtone, and it cannot distinguish numerical-relativity from perturbative predictions."}, {"id": "paper-2609-28457", "arxiv": "2609.28457", "title": "Sterile Neutrino Dark Matter Cries for GeV Heavy Neutral Leptons", "authors": "Marco Drewes, Yannis Georis, Juraj Klarić, Mikhail Shaposhnikov, Inar Timiryasov", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A quantum-kinetic parameter scan of the νMSM links baryogenesis and sterile-neutrino dark matter to experimentally testable heavy neutral leptons below approximately 50 GeV.", "path": "2026/09/24/index.html#paper-2609-28457", "reportDate": "24 September 2026", "searchText": "Sterile Neutrino Dark Matter Cries for GeV Heavy Neutral Leptons Marco Drewes, Yannis Georis, Juraj Klarić, Mikhail Shaposhnikov, Inar Timiryasov 2609.28457 A quantum-kinetic parameter scan of the νMSM links baryogenesis and sterile-neutrino dark matter to experimentally testable heavy neutral leptons below approximately 50 GeV. 2026-09-24 24 September 2026 Neutrinos Dark matter BSM #### ★★★ 1. Sterile Neutrino Dark Matter Cries for GeV Heavy Neutral Leptons\n\n**Marco Drewes, Yannis Georis, Juraj Klarić, Mikhail Shaposhnikov, Inar Timiryasov**  \n[arXiv:2609.28457](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.28457)\n\nThe authors perform the first modern parameter scan of the \\(\\nu\\)MSM that consistently evolves the quantum kinetics of its three right-handed neutrinos through baryogenesis, late-time lepton-asymmetry production and resonant sterile-neutrino dark-matter production.\n\nFor a benchmark \\(7\\)-keV dark-matter state, producing the required late lepton asymmetry confines the heavier quasi-degenerate pair to\n\n\\[\nM_{2,3}\\lesssim 50~{\\rm GeV},\n\\]\n\nwith mixing angles above the minimal seesaw floor throughout the meson-decay range. A newly identified self-amplified MSW “ignition” mechanism reopens masses below the \\(B\\)-meson threshold. SHiP should cover the complete allowed region below approximately \\(2\\) GeV and a significant part up to \\(5\\) GeV; LHC and future lepton-collider searches cover higher masses.\n\n##### History\n\nThe original comprehensive \\(\\nu\\)MSM scans found regions simultaneously compatible with neutrino masses, baryogenesis and resonantly produced dark matter, but used kinetic treatments later shown to be incomplete. ([Canetti et al. 2013](https://arxiv.org/abs/1204.3902)) Subsequent calculations improved thermal rates and resonance dynamics without producing a new global scan. ([Ghiglieri and Laine 2019](https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.08814), [2020](https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.10766)) This paper closes that technical gap and turns the all-in-one \\(\\nu\\)MSM explanation into a bounded, experimentally testable target rather than a broadly open scenario. Important residual uncertainties arise below \\(M\\simeq500\\) MeV, where hadronic HNL decays become difficult to treat, and above \\(50\\) GeV, where the employed Fermi-theory description breaks down. The quantitative region also depends on the assumed sterile-dark-matter mass and its astrophysical constraints.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-26965", "arxiv": "2609.26965", "title": "Tension between MiniBooNE and MicroBooNE within a 3+1 sterile-neutrino framework using simulation-based inference", "authors": "Julia P. Woodward, Austin Schneider, Joshua Villarreal, John Hardin, Janet Conrad", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Oscillations", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "An independent, trial-calibrated reconstruction finds a residual 2.7σ preference for 3+1 oscillations alongside at least 2.5σ tension between MiniBooNE and MicroBooNE.", "path": "2026/09/24/index.html#paper-2609-26965", "reportDate": "24 September 2026", "searchText": "Tension between MiniBooNE and MicroBooNE within a 3+1 sterile-neutrino framework using simulation-based inference Julia P. Woodward, Austin Schneider, Joshua Villarreal, John Hardin, Janet Conrad 2609.26965 An independent, trial-calibrated reconstruction finds a residual 2.7σ preference for 3+1 oscillations alongside at least 2.5σ tension between MiniBooNE and MicroBooNE. 2026-09-24 24 September 2026 Neutrinos Oscillations BSM #### ★★★ 2. Tension between MiniBooNE and MicroBooNE within a \\(3+1\\) sterile-neutrino framework using simulation-based inference\n\n**Julia P. Woodward, Austin Schneider, Joshua Villarreal, John Hardin, Janet Conrad**  \n[arXiv:2609.26965](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.26965)\n\nA common simulation and statistical framework is constructed for MiniBooNE and the MicroBooNE BNB and NuMI samples. Unlike the published analyses, post-hoc data-driven flux-correction factors are omitted consistently from both experiments.\n\nTrial-calibrated simulation-based inference gives a residual preference for \\(3+1\\) oscillations of\n\n\\[\n2.7\\sigma\n\\]\n\nafter MicroBooNE is included. The parameter-goodness-of-fit test nevertheless finds at least \\(2.5\\sigma\\) tension between MiniBooNE and MicroBooNE within the minimal model.\n\n##### History\n\nMiniBooNE reported a \\(4.8\\sigma\\) electron-like excess and a roughly \\(4.6\\sigma\\) oscillation preference, whereas MicroBooNE’s inclusive electron-neutrino searches found no corresponding excess. ([MiniBooNE final sample](https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.16883), [MicroBooNE Wire-Cell search](https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.13978)) A previous combined fit still admitted parts of \\(3+1\\) parameter space, but relied on asymptotic likelihood statistics and less complete MicroBooNE information. ([Previous combined fit](https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.01724)) The new result supplies the most carefully trial-calibrated direct consistency test to date and shows that neither “MicroBooNE completely eliminates MiniBooNE” nor “the combined data prefer sterile neutrinos” adequately summarizes the situation: the residual preference is modest, and the two datasets are themselves mutually uncomfortable in \\(3+1\\).\n\nThis is an independent reconstruction, not a collaboration likelihood. MicroBooNE has not released event-level Monte Carlo containing all true and reconstructed variables, forcing the authors to approximate detector efficiencies and smearing. The quoted tension therefore should not be treated as a final experimental combination.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-26888", "arxiv": "2609.26888", "title": "The universe as a superconductor: How to search for a photon mass", "authors": "Andrei Gruzinov, Anson Hook, Junwu Huang", "tags": ["Particle physics", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "An Abelian Higgs realization of photon mass is studied across magnetic environments, distinguishing symmetry-restored, Meissner and vortex phases and their applicable constraints.", "path": "2026/09/24/index.html#paper-2609-26888", "reportDate": "24 September 2026", "searchText": "The universe as a superconductor: How to search for a photon mass Andrei Gruzinov, Anson Hook, Junwu Huang 2609.26888 An Abelian Higgs realization of photon mass is studied across magnetic environments, distinguishing symmetry-restored, Meissner and vortex phases and their applicable constraints. 2026-09-24 24 September 2026 Particle physics BSM #### ★★★ 3. The universe as a superconductor: How to search for a photon mass\n\n**Andrei Gruzinov, Anson Hook, Junwu Huang**  \n[arXiv:2609.26888](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.26888)\n\nConventional photon-mass limits normally assume an explicit Proca mass. Here the mass instead arises through an Abelian Higgs field, making electromagnetism analogous to a superconductor with Meissner, vortex and symmetry-restored phases.\n\nBecause ambient magnetic fields can restore the \\(U(1)\\) symmetry, a photon may be massive in vacuum but effectively massless on Earth or inside galaxies. For a Type-I realization, the authors find that conventional limits can formally permit a vacuum mass as large as\n\n\\[\nm_\\gamma\\sim 0.3~{\\rm MeV}.\n\\]\n\nFor Type-II models, a generalized magnetohydrodynamic calculation makes Galactic-dynamo operation the principal constraint. The authors identify fast-radio-burst dispersion, frequency-dependent cosmic opacity, magnetically shielded laboratory volumes and space-based Coulomb-law tests as complementary probes.\n\n##### History\n\nIt has been known for two decades that vortices in a Higgs realization can invalidate magnetic-energy bounds that are extremely strong for Proca electromagnetism. ([Adelberger, Dvali and Gruzinov](https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/0306245); [photon-mass review](https://arxiv.org/abs/0809.1003)) The new paper’s advance is a systematic three-parameter phase diagram, including symmetry restoration, vortex formation, plasma currents and Galactic dynamo evolution. It therefore clarifies which existing bounds apply in which physical environment and proposes searches targeted specifically at the remaining regions.\n\nThe \\(0.3\\)-MeV statement is not a generic relaxation of the photon-mass limit: it requires an extraordinarily light millicharged Higgs, extremely small couplings and a very flat potential. The paper excludes the most severe order-by-order tunings but still occupies an exotic theoretical regime. No established Proca bound is contradicted.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-26897", "arxiv": "2609.26897", "title": "A coherent search for new Galactic magnetars using Fermi/GBM and follow-up Swift/BAT", "authors": "Ariel Perera, Barak Zackay, Tejaswi Venumadhav", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "X rays", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A coherent search of Fermi-GBM data identifies 1,720 magnetar-like bursts and seven candidate source clusters, with Swift-BAT providing narrower localizations for three candidates.", "path": "2026/09/24/index.html#paper-2609-26897", "reportDate": "24 September 2026", "searchText": "A coherent search for new Galactic magnetars using Fermi/GBM and follow-up Swift/BAT Ariel Perera, Barak Zackay, Tejaswi Venumadhav 2609.26897 A coherent search of Fermi-GBM data identifies 1,720 magnetar-like bursts and seven candidate source clusters, with Swift-BAT providing narrower localizations for three candidates. 2026-09-24 24 September 2026 Gamma rays X rays Compact objects #### ★★★ 4. A coherent search for new Galactic magnetars using Fermi/GBM and follow-up Swift/BAT\n\n**Ariel Perera, Barak Zackay, Tejaswi Venumadhav**  \n[arXiv:2609.26897](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.26897)\n\nA coherent Poisson matched-filter search is applied to continuous Fermi/GBM data from 2013–2025. Archival Swift/BAT imaging and spatiotemporal clustering are then used to associate the broadly localized GBM events with individual sources.\n\nThe analysis identifies 1,720 magnetar-like bursts:\n\n- 1,170 are associated with 11 known magnetars;\n- 550 remain unassociated;\n- seven spatially distinct clusters are proposed as candidate new magnetars;\n- three candidates possess BAT localizations sufficiently narrow for targeted X-ray follow-up.\n\nThe associated sample extends below \\(10^{-8}\\ {\\rm erg\\,cm^{-2}}\\) in fluence.\n\n##### History\n\nThe recent independent 17-year GBM catalog associated 735 bursts over the overlapping observing span. ([Godwin et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.02809)) The coherent search raises that number to 1,170 and combines GBM sensitivity with BAT’s arcminute localization, making the source-association methodology—not merely the longer exposure—the key improvement. The catalog and posterior samples are public.\n\nThe seven objects remain candidates. Four have localization regions too broad for efficient pointed observations, while even the BAT-localized candidates require persistent X-ray emission, pulsations or repeated localized activity for confirmation. The search completeness as a function of fluence is not established, so the low-fluence turnover cannot yet be used for an intrinsic luminosity-function measurement.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-27057", "arxiv": "2609.27057", "title": "Disk truncation triggers relativistic jet launching of a highly accreting supermassive black hole", "authors": "Hirofumi Noda, Kazuhiro Hada, Hiroto Maruyama, Taishu Kayanoki, Kenji Toma, Taiki Kawamuro, Yoshiyuki Inoue, Tomohisa Kawashima, Soyeon Yeo, Ilje Cho, Keiichi Asada, Yasushi Fukazawa, Yoshihiro Ueda", "tags": ["X rays", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "XRISM spectroscopy and contemporaneous radio imaging constrain a truncated disk and compact jet base in the highly accreting radio galaxy 3C 120.", "path": "2026/09/24/index.html#paper-2609-27057", "reportDate": "24 September 2026", "searchText": "Disk truncation triggers relativistic jet launching of a highly accreting supermassive black hole Hirofumi Noda, Kazuhiro Hada, Hiroto Maruyama, Taishu Kayanoki, Kenji Toma, Taiki Kawamuro, Yoshiyuki Inoue, Tomohisa Kawashima, Soyeon Yeo, Ilje Cho, Keiichi Asada, Yasushi Fukazawa, Yoshihiro Ueda 2609.27057 XRISM spectroscopy and contemporaneous radio imaging constrain a truncated disk and compact jet base in the highly accreting radio galaxy 3C 120. 2026-09-24 24 September 2026 X rays Compact objects #### ★★★ 5. Disk truncation triggers relativistic jet launching of a highly accreting supermassive black hole\n\n**Hirofumi Noda, Kazuhiro Hada, Hiroto Maruyama, Taishu Kayanoki, Kenji Toma, Taiki Kawamuro, Yoshiyuki Inoue, Tomohisa Kawashima, Soyeon Yeo, Ilje Cho, Keiichi Asada, Yasushi Fukazawa, Yoshihiro Ueda**  \n[arXiv:2609.27057](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.27057)\n\nCoordinated XRISM/Resolve spectroscopy and GMVA, VLBA and EAVN imaging probe the accretion flow and jet of the broad-line radio galaxy 3C 120, which accretes at roughly \\(0.1\\!-\\!0.2\\) of Eddington.\n\nThe resolved relativistic Fe-\\(K\\alpha\\) profile gives\n\n\\[\nR_{\\rm in}=17.4^{+6.3}_{-6.5}\\,R_g,\n\\]\n\nindicating a truncated cold disk with a hot, geometrically thick inner flow. Extrapolation of the VLBI collimation profile places the jet base within approximately \\(10R_g\\). The resulting geometry can advect large-scale magnetic flux and support Blandford–Znajek jet production despite the high accretion rate.\n\n##### History\n\nLong-term monitoring previously showed that X-ray dips in 3C 120 precede the ejection of superluminal radio knots, establishing a phenomenological disk–jet connection. ([Chatterjee et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/0909.2051)) CCD spectroscopy had also suggested disk truncation, but multiple reflection, absorption and jet-continuum models remained comparably acceptable. ([Lohfink et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/1305.4937)) XRISM separates the broad disk line from narrower distant components, providing the most robust truncation-radius measurement for this source, while contemporaneous VLBI supplies an independent jet-scale constraint.\n\nThe observations establish compatible geometry, not direct causality: the VLBI size near the horizon is an extrapolation, and the data do not prove that the inner flow is magnetically arrested or that the jet is powered uniquely by Blandford–Znajek extraction.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-28460", "arxiv": "2609.28460", "title": "First pre-peak ultraviolet spectrum of a tidal disruption event", "authors": "Erica Hammerstein et al.", "tags": ["Compact objects"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The first pre-peak UV spectrum of a tidal disruption event reveals an early fast outflow and a continuum whose far-UV contribution is missed by optical blackbody fits.", "path": "2026/09/24/index.html#paper-2609-28460", "reportDate": "24 September 2026", "searchText": "First pre-peak ultraviolet spectrum of a tidal disruption event Erica Hammerstein et al. 2609.28460 The first pre-peak UV spectrum of a tidal disruption event reveals an early fast outflow and a continuum whose far-UV contribution is missed by optical blackbody fits. 2026-09-24 24 September 2026 Compact objects #### ★★☆ 6. First pre-peak ultraviolet spectrum of a tidal disruption event\n\n**Erica Hammerstein et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.28460](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.28460)\n\nHST/STIS obtained a UV spectrum of TDE 2025aarm approximately twenty days before its optical maximum—the first such pre-peak UV spectrum of a tidal disruption event.\n\nBroad far- and near-UV absorption features are blueshifted by approximately\n\n\\[\nv_{\\rm out}\\simeq10^4~{\\rm km\\,s^{-1}},\n\\]\n\ndemonstrating that a fast outflow was already present well before peak light. The far-UV-to-infrared continuum is substantially bluer than a single blackbody. Fitting only near-UV and optical photometry would underestimate the inferred bolometric luminosity by roughly a factor of nine for this event.\n\n##### History\n\nBroad UV absorption and comparably fast outflows were already observed after or near maximum in iPTF15af and AT 2019qiz. ([iPTF15af](https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07446), [AT 2019qiz](https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.01593)) The new result is the pre-maximum timing, which places strong outflow formation during the early circularization or disk-assembly phase. The continuum also provides direct evidence that optical blackbody fits can miss considerable far-UV/EUV power.\n\nThe factor-of-nine correction is object-specific and depends on extrapolating across the unobserved EUV; it should not yet be applied to the optical TDE population. Only a sample of prompt UV spectra can determine whether this is typical.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-26878", "arxiv": "2609.26878", "title": "Compact-object mergers and micro-tidal disruption events in dense stellar environments", "authors": "Konstantinos Kritos, Francesco Iacovelli, Rosalba Perna, Emanuele Berti", "tags": ["Compact objects", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Cluster simulations jointly track compact-object mergers, stellar disruptions, spin evolution and accretion-induced collapse, linking their cosmic rates and observable populations.", "path": "2026/09/24/index.html#paper-2609-26878", "reportDate": "24 September 2026", "searchText": "Compact-object mergers and micro-tidal disruption events in dense stellar environments Konstantinos Kritos, Francesco Iacovelli, Rosalba Perna, Emanuele Berti 2609.26878 Cluster simulations jointly track compact-object mergers, stellar disruptions, spin evolution and accretion-induced collapse, linking their cosmic rates and observable populations. 2026-09-24 24 September 2026 Compact objects Multimessenger #### ★★☆ 7. Compact-object mergers and micro-tidal disruption events in dense stellar environments\n\n**Konstantinos Kritos, Francesco Iacovelli, Rosalba Perna, Emanuele Berti**  \n[arXiv:2609.26878](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.26878)\n\nUsing the rapid cluster code Rapster, the authors simulate \\(10^5\\) clusters for each of ten physical variations and jointly follow compact-object mergers, stellar disruptions by neutron stars or stellar-mass black holes, compact-object spin evolution and accretion-induced collapse.\n\nThe black-hole merger rate peaks at \\(z\\simeq2\\!-\\!3\\) at several tens of \\({\\rm Gpc^{-3}\\,yr^{-1}}\\). The integrated number of micro-TDEs exceeds the merger number by a factor of approximately \\(2\\!-\\!5\\). About 88–90% of disruptions in these simulations arise from single compact-object–star encounters. Accretion can spin up low-mass black holes and push neutron stars above their maximum mass, producing black holes in the \\(2.2\\!-\\!5\\,M_\\odot\\) lower mass gap.\n\n##### History\n\nThe original analytical estimate predicted a local globular-cluster micro-TDE rate of a few \\({\\rm Gpc^{-3}\\,yr^{-1}}\\). ([Perets et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.07698)) Later direct cluster simulations obtained a similar local black-hole–star disruption rate near \\(3\\ {\\rm Gpc^{-3}\\,yr^{-1}}\\). ([Kremer et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.06353)) The present globular-cluster value is about \\(0.04\\ {\\rm Gpc^{-3}\\,yr^{-1}}\\), roughly 25 times below the original analytical estimate, principally because mass segregation strongly suppresses neutron-star encounters. Its new contribution is a unified cosmic calculation linking disruption rates, merger observables, compact-object spins and accretion-induced collapse.\n\nThe channel fractions are not robust population predictions: primordial binaries and triples are omitted, even though other simulations find them responsible for most micro-TDEs. The authors correctly describe their disruption rates as conservative lower limits.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-27496", "arxiv": "2609.27496", "title": "Origin of the high-energy spectral cutoff in gamma-ray pulsar halos", "authors": "Qi-Ling Chen, Ming-Ming Kang, Kun Fang, Hong-Bo Hu, Yi-Qing Guo, Chao-Wen Yang", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Cosmic rays", "X rays"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Geminga’s halo cutoff is modeled with either a maximum electron injection energy or energy-dependent diffusion that carries emission beyond the observation aperture.", "path": "2026/09/24/index.html#paper-2609-27496", "reportDate": "24 September 2026", "searchText": "Origin of the high-energy spectral cutoff in gamma-ray pulsar halos Qi-Ling Chen, Ming-Ming Kang, Kun Fang, Hong-Bo Hu, Yi-Qing Guo, Chao-Wen Yang 2609.27496 Geminga’s halo cutoff is modeled with either a maximum electron injection energy or energy-dependent diffusion that carries emission beyond the observation aperture. 2026-09-24 24 September 2026 Gamma rays Cosmic rays X rays #### ★★☆ 8. Origin of the high-energy spectral cutoff in gamma-ray pulsar halos\n\n**Qi-Ling Chen, Ming-Ming Kang, Kun Fang, Hong-Bo Hu, Yi-Qing Guo, Chao-Wen Yang**  \n[arXiv:2609.27496](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.27496)\n\nThe tens-of-TeV cutoff in Geminga’s gamma-ray halo is modeled under two physically different hypotheses:\n\n1. the PWN termination shock reaches a finite maximum electron energy;\n2. the diffusion coefficient rises sharply above a characteristic energy, allowing high-energy electrons and their inverse-Compton emission to escape beyond the HAWC analysis aperture.\n\nBoth scenarios reproduce the spectrum and radial profiles. They predict different correlations between cutoff energy and pulsar properties and different hard-X-ray cutoffs for the central PWN.\n\n##### History\n\nHAWC’s precision Geminga and Monogem analysis established the cutoff and strongly suppressed local diffusion but did not determine whether the spectral feature originates at injection or during propagation. ([HAWC measurement](https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4357/ad738e)) The important point here is that the measured cutoff need not directly measure the PWN accelerator limit: energy-dependent aperture leakage can generate a similar spectrum. This is a useful reinterpretation rather than a new observational detection.\n\nThe broken diffusion coefficient is phenomenological and is not derived from a self-consistent turbulence calculation. Wide-field energy-resolved morphology and PWN hard-X-ray measurements are needed to discriminate the models."}, {"id": "paper-2609-25681", "arxiv": "2609.25681", "title": "Search for the ¹⁶O(ppp) → ¹³C π⁺π⁺e⁺ decay mode in Super-Kamiokande using machine learning techniques", "authors": "Super-Kamiokande Collaboration", "tags": ["BSM", "Particle physics"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Super-Kamiokande searches for a baryon-number-violating three-proton decay using a convolutional neural network and sets a 90%-confidence lifetime limit of 4.2 × 10³² years.", "path": "2026/09/23/index.html#paper-2609-25681", "reportDate": "23 September 2026", "searchText": "Search for the ¹⁶O(ppp) → ¹³C π⁺π⁺e⁺ decay mode in Super-Kamiokande using machine learning techniques Super-Kamiokande Collaboration 2609.25681 Super-Kamiokande searches for a baryon-number-violating three-proton decay using a convolutional neural network and sets a 90%-confidence lifetime limit of 4.2 × 10³² years. 2026-09-23 23 September 2026 BSM Particle physics #### ★★★ 1. Search for the \\(^{16}\\mathrm O(ppp)\\rightarrow{}^{13}\\mathrm C\\,\\pi^+\\pi^+e^+\\) decay mode in Super-Kamiokande using machine learning techniques\n\n**Super-Kamiokande Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2609.25681](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.25681)\n\nSuper-Kamiokande searches for a baryon-number-violating \\(\\Delta B=3\\) decay of three bound protons. An attention-based convolutional neural network classifies the complex multi-ring topology—the first CNN-based nucleon-decay analysis by Super-K.\n\nIn \\(0.401\\) megaton-years of SK-I–V data, one event survives, consistent with an expected background of 1.33 events. The resulting 90%-confidence lower limit is\n\n\\[\n\\tau/ {\\rm Br}>4.2\\times10^{32}\\ {\\rm yr}.\n\\]\n\n##### History\n\nPrevious direct searches by GERDA and the Majorana Demonstrator obtained limits of approximately \\(4.7\\times10^{25}\\) and \\(1.6\\times10^{26}\\) years, respectively. The new result therefore improves the direct bound by more than six orders of magnitude and, for the first time, reaches the \\(\\sim10^{33}\\)-year range motivated by some anomaly-free \\(Z_6\\) baryon-parity models. This is a new sensitivity regime rather than an incremental exposure update. Its interpretation remains specific to this nuclear final state; efficiency and background estimates depend on intranuclear pion transport, nuclear de-excitation, and atmospheric-neutrino simulations.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-25990", "arxiv": "2609.25990", "title": "Einstein Probe discovery of the magnetar EP J223759.5+531421", "authors": "Nanda Rea et al.", "tags": ["X rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Coherent pulsations, bursts and broadband follow-up identify the Einstein Probe transient EP J223759.5+531421 as a new Galactic magnetar.", "path": "2026/09/23/index.html#paper-2609-25990", "reportDate": "23 September 2026", "searchText": "Einstein Probe discovery of the magnetar EP J223759.5+531421 Nanda Rea et al. 2609.25990 Coherent pulsations, bursts and broadband follow-up identify the Einstein Probe transient EP J223759.5+531421 as a new Galactic magnetar. 2026-09-23 23 September 2026 X rays #### ★★★ 2. Einstein Probe discovery of the magnetar EP J223759.5+531421\n\n**Nanda Rea et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.25990](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.25990)\n\nEinstein Probe discovered a new Galactic X-ray transient on 28 June 2026. Follow-up by XMM-Newton, NuSTAR, SVOM and IXPE revealed short magnetar-like bursts and coherent pulsations with\n\n\\[\nP\\simeq6\\ {\\rm s},\\qquad\n\\dot P\\simeq2.8\\times10^{-12}\\ {\\rm s\\,s^{-1}}.\n\\]\n\nUnder vacuum dipole braking, these imply \\(B_{\\rm dip}\\simeq2.6\\times10^{14}\\) G and a characteristic age near 34 kyr. Its \\(0.5-30\\) keV luminosity declined from roughly \\(1.2\\times10^{35}\\) to \\(5.7\\times10^{34}\\ {\\rm erg\\,s^{-1}}\\) during the first month, assuming a distance of 3.3 kpc. No radio or near-infrared counterpart was found.\n\n##### History\n\nThe period, period derivative, bursts, hard X-ray tail and thermal spectrum together elevate EP J223759.5+531421 from a candidate to a securely identified magnetar. It adds a new object to the population summarized in the [McGill magnetar catalogue](https://arxiv.org/abs/1309.4167), with unusually comprehensive observations immediately after discovery. Its position about 0.26 kpc below the Galactic plane is difficult to reconcile with the nominal characteristic age if it was born close to the midplane. That tension is suggestive but not yet anomalous physics: the adopted distance is uncertain, and magnetar torque variability makes \\(\\tau_c=P/(2\\dot P)\\) an unreliable chronological age.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-25958", "arxiv": "2609.25958", "title": "IXPE observation of the new magnetar source EP J223759.5+531421", "authors": "Roberto Taverna et al.", "tags": ["X rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "IXPE measurements resolve strong phase- and energy-dependent polarization in EP J223759.5+531421, constraining its viewing geometry and surface-emission models.", "path": "2026/09/23/index.html#paper-2609-25958", "reportDate": "23 September 2026", "searchText": "IXPE observation of the new magnetar source EP J223759.5+531421 Roberto Taverna et al. 2609.25958 IXPE measurements resolve strong phase- and energy-dependent polarization in EP J223759.5+531421, constraining its viewing geometry and surface-emission models. 2026-09-23 23 September 2026 X rays #### ★★★ 3. IXPE observation of the new magnetar source EP J223759.5+531421\n\n**Roberto Taverna et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.25958](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.25958)\n\nIXPE began observing the newly discovered magnetar approximately eight days after its outburst. The phase-integrated polarization degree is about 7%, detected above \\(4\\sigma\\), but varies strongly with rotational phase and energy. It reaches approximately 63% in the \\(4-7.5\\) keV band near the secondary pulse.\n\nThe polarization angle swings through almost \\(180^\\circ\\). A rotating-vector fit gives approximate angles of \\(28^\\circ\\) between the line of sight and spin axis and \\(109^\\circ\\) between the magnetic and spin axes. The authors interpret the cooler component as emission from condensed surface regions and the hotter component as radiation processed through a magnetized atmosphere.\n\n##### History\n\nIXPE previously found much stronger energy-integrated polarization in 1RXS J170849.0−400910, rising from roughly 20% at low energies to about 80% above 6 keV ([previous IXPE result](https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.12919)). The new observation extends this program to a magnetar caught immediately after discovery and provides unusually rapid phase-resolved constraints on its viewing geometry. The proposed mixture of condensed-surface and atmospheric emission confirms and refines interpretations already developed for 1RXS J170849 and 4U 0142+61, rather than introducing an entirely new emission mechanism. The 63% value occurs in a restricted phase-energy bin with limited counts, and the geometric inference depends on an idealized dipolar rotating-vector model.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-25950", "arxiv": "2609.25950", "title": "Big Bang nucleosynthesis confronts domain walls and first-order phase transitions as pulsar-timing-array explanations", "authors": "Fatemeh Elahi, Diego Fortini, Yann Gouttenoire, Nicholas Leister, Pedro Schwaller", "tags": ["BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Inhomogeneous reheating from domain walls and first-order phase transitions is mapped into deuterium constraints on early-Universe explanations of pulsar-timing-array signals.", "path": "2026/09/23/index.html#paper-2609-25950", "reportDate": "23 September 2026", "searchText": "Big Bang nucleosynthesis confronts domain walls and first-order phase transitions as pulsar-timing-array explanations Fatemeh Elahi, Diego Fortini, Yann Gouttenoire, Nicholas Leister, Pedro Schwaller 2609.25950 Inhomogeneous reheating from domain walls and first-order phase transitions is mapped into deuterium constraints on early-Universe explanations of pulsar-timing-array signals. 2026-09-23 23 September 2026 BSM #### ★★★ 4. Big Bang nucleosynthesis confronts domain walls and first-order phase transitions as pulsar-timing-array explanations\n\n**Fatemeh Elahi, Diego Fortini, Yann Gouttenoire, Nicholas Leister, Pedro Schwaller**  \n[arXiv:2609.25950](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.25950)\n\nDomain-wall annihilation or a first-order phase transition can reheat the visible plasma inhomogeneously, producing baryon-to-photon fluctuations that survive until nucleosynthesis. Because deuterium production responds nonlinearly to these fluctuations, the spatially averaged abundance differs from homogeneous-BBN predictions.\n\nUsing PRIMAT nuclear rates, fluctuations with RMS amplitude\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\langle S_B^2\\rangle}\\simeq0.12-0.29\n\\]\n\ncan reduce the existing mild deuterium discrepancy, whereas larger amplitudes overproduce deuterium. PArthENoPE already agrees with the observed abundance without inhomogeneities and instead gives an approximate upper limit of 0.19. For fully visible reheating, the authors infer \\(\\alpha_{\\rm DW}\\lesssim0.25\\) for domain walls and exclude particularly strong, slow transitions in parts of pulsar-timing-array parameter space.\n\n##### History\n\nThe [NANOGrav 15-year analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.16213) established a common-spectrum signal compatible with a stochastic gravitational-wave background, prompting many phase-transition and domain-wall interpretations. Earlier inhomogeneous-BBN studies established that deuterium is sensitive to baryon fluctuations, but this paper explicitly maps fluctuations from those two gravitational-wave sources into BBN and overlays the result on PTA posteriors. The constraint may be the strongest current BBN test of these particular early-Universe PTA explanations. Its main limitation is unusually consequential: switching between PRIMAT and PArthENoPE changes a nominally preferred fluctuation interval into only an upper bound. Visible branching fraction, diffusion, source morphology and thermalization assumptions also enter directly.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-25217", "arxiv": "2609.25217", "title": "Limitations of quantum tomography in Higgs-boson decays to four leptons", "authors": "Morgan Del Gratta, Fabio Maltoni, Davide Pagani, Giovanni Pelliccioli", "tags": ["Particle physics"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Off-shell photon interference and radiative corrections can obstruct a full two-Z density-matrix interpretation of Higgs four-lepton decays; a reduced angular construction remains well behaved.", "path": "2026/09/23/index.html#paper-2609-25217", "reportDate": "23 September 2026", "searchText": "Limitations of quantum tomography in Higgs-boson decays to four leptons Morgan Del Gratta, Fabio Maltoni, Davide Pagani, Giovanni Pelliccioli 2609.25217 Off-shell photon interference and radiative corrections can obstruct a full two-Z density-matrix interpretation of Higgs four-lepton decays; a reduced angular construction remains well behaved. 2026-09-23 23 September 2026 Particle physics #### ★★★ 5. Limitations of quantum tomography in Higgs-boson decays to four leptons\n\n**Morgan Del Gratta, Fabio Maltoni, Davide Pagani, Giovanni Pelliccioli**  \n[arXiv:2609.25217](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.25217)\n\nThe paper examines whether the angular distribution in\n\n\\[\nH\\rightarrow ZZ^\\ast\\rightarrow4\\ell\n\\]\n\ncan consistently be interpreted as quantum-state tomography of two spin-1 particles beyond leading order.\n\nAt NLO, the conventionally reconstructed two-qutrit density matrix can acquire negative eigenvalues even after radiatively corrected decay analyzers are used. The failure originates from coherent \\(Z^\\ast\\) and \\(\\gamma^\\ast\\) contributions to the off-shell dilepton system, which do not share a universal production-times-decay factorization. A neutrino control channel restores positivity, while an SMEFT \\(HZ\\gamma\\) contribution can generate the same problem already at tree level. A reduced rank-two angular sector remains well behaved and can still supply a lower bound on concurrence.\n\n##### History\n\nThis is an important qualification to the recent ATLAS and CMS evidence for \\(ZZ^\\ast\\) entanglement ([CERN summary](https://home.cern/strong-evidence-for-quantum-entanglement-between-z-bosons-found-by-atlas-and-cms/), [ATLAS result](https://doi.org/10.1103/y1nh-1b82)). It does **not** show that the measured angular correlations or the entanglement conclusion are false. Instead, it demonstrates that interpreting the complete four-lepton distribution as a physical two-\\(Z\\) density matrix becomes theoretically ambiguous once off-shell photon interference and radiative corrections matter. The robust rank-two construction is therefore the paper’s practical advance. Detector acceptance and unfolding may introduce additional complications beyond the parton-level issue studied here.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-25672", "arxiv": "2609.25672", "title": "A multi-mission detection framework for high-energy transients: application to a 13-year Fermi/GBM and Swift/BAT sample", "authors": "Ariel Perera, Barak Zackay, Tejaswi Venumadhav", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A joint probability statistic combines Fermi-GBM and Swift-BAT trigger information to rank high-energy transient candidates across their overlapping 2013–2025 observations.", "path": "2026/09/23/index.html#paper-2609-25672", "reportDate": "23 September 2026", "searchText": "A multi-mission detection framework for high-energy transients: application to a 13-year Fermi/GBM and Swift/BAT sample Ariel Perera, Barak Zackay, Tejaswi Venumadhav 2609.25672 A joint probability statistic combines Fermi-GBM and Swift-BAT trigger information to rank high-energy transient candidates across their overlapping 2013–2025 observations. 2026-09-23 23 September 2026 Gamma rays Multimessenger #### ★★★ 6. A multi-mission detection framework for high-energy transients: application to a 13-year Fermi/GBM and Swift/BAT sample\n\n**Ariel Perera, Barak Zackay, Tejaswi Venumadhav**  \n[arXiv:2609.25672](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.25672)\n\nThe authors construct a joint probability statistic from Fermi-GBM and Swift-BAT trigger information and apply it to their overlapping 2013–2025 data. Instead of using one instrument merely to validate candidates from the other, the foreground and background distributions are modeled jointly.\n\nDepending on the assumed transient class and favorable spacecraft geometry, the accessible volume can increase by as much as 60% for short-GRB-like events. Candidates with high joint probability have an estimated contamination fraction near 4%.\n\n##### History\n\nPrevious GBM targeted searches and BAT/GBM coincidence studies recovered subthreshold events by conditioning on an external trigger—famously important for weak counterparts such as [GRB 170817A](https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.05833). The present work advances this into a statistically calibrated multi-instrument ranking rather than a post-hoc confirmation test. The quoted 60% volume gain is an optimistic upper envelope: the current implementation is still seeded by a GBM trigger threshold, and it does not fully marginalize over realistic field-of-view, orientation, energy-response and duty-cycle effects. A fully symmetric low-level search would be the decisive next step.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-26574", "arxiv": "2609.26574", "title": "Self-consistent modeling of energy-dependent synchrotron polarization in blazars with application to Mrk 421", "authors": "Frederike Apel, Motseothata Tisang, Victor Barbosa Martins, Xavier Rodrigues, Markus Böttcher, Anna Franckowiak", "tags": ["X rays"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Polarized AM3 transport models reproduce the broad energy-dependent polarization of Mrk 421 with one helical emission region and pitch-angle-dependent cooling.", "path": "2026/09/23/index.html#paper-2609-26574", "reportDate": "23 September 2026", "searchText": "Self-consistent modeling of energy-dependent synchrotron polarization in blazars with application to Mrk 421 Frederike Apel, Motseothata Tisang, Victor Barbosa Martins, Xavier Rodrigues, Markus Böttcher, Anna Franckowiak 2609.26574 Polarized AM3 transport models reproduce the broad energy-dependent polarization of Mrk 421 with one helical emission region and pitch-angle-dependent cooling. 2026-09-23 23 September 2026 X rays #### ★★☆ 7. Self-consistent modeling of energy-dependent synchrotron polarization in blazars with application to Mrk 421\n\n**Frederike Apel, Motseothata Tisang, Victor Barbosa Martins, Xavier Rodrigues, Markus Böttcher, Anna Franckowiak**  \n[arXiv:2609.26574](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.26574)\n\nThe AM3 transport framework is extended to follow synchrotron polarization, pitch-angle-dependent cooling and multiple magnetic-field structures self-consistently. A single-zone helical-field model reproduces the broad polarization behavior of Mrk 421 during May 2022: approximately 15% X-ray polarization versus about 3% in the optical and infrared.\n\nIn the shock-in-jet calculation, the X-ray polarization angle rotates by roughly \\(70^\\circ\\) over five hours while the optical angle stays nearly fixed. The result demonstrates that chromatic polarization need not imply spatially separated emission zones.\n\n##### History\n\nThe contemporaneous [MAGIC/IXPE campaign](https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.10732) interpreted the X-ray polarization exceeding the optical and radio values by factors of two to five as evidence for an energy-stratified region near a shock. This paper supplies an explicit counterexample: pitch-angle-dependent cooling in one co-spatial helical region can produce similar stratification. Time-dependent helical-field models existed previously, but the self-consistent polarized transport is a real methodological improvement. The fit is not unique, modest polarization residuals remain, and the magnetic geometry is prescribed rather than generated dynamically; the data therefore do not yet distinguish this scenario from multi-zone models.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-25202", "arxiv": "2609.25202", "title": "Cosmic birefringence and axiogenesis", "authors": "Raymond T. Co, Lawrence J. Hall, Keisuke Harigaya, Taegyu Lee", "tags": ["BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A rotating axion-like field is proposed to connect baryogenesis with tentative CMB birefringence, predicting a positive rotation angle and a nonstandard pre-BBN expansion history.", "path": "2026/09/23/index.html#paper-2609-25202", "reportDate": "23 September 2026", "searchText": "Cosmic birefringence and axiogenesis Raymond T. Co, Lawrence J. Hall, Keisuke Harigaya, Taegyu Lee 2609.25202 A rotating axion-like field is proposed to connect baryogenesis with tentative CMB birefringence, predicting a positive rotation angle and a nonstandard pre-BBN expansion history. 2026-09-23 23 September 2026 BSM #### ★★☆ 8. Cosmic birefringence and axiogenesis\n\n**Raymond T. Co, Lawrence J. Hall, Keisuke Harigaya, Taegyu Lee**  \n[arXiv:2609.25202](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.25202)\n\nA rotating axion-like field is proposed as a common origin for the baryon asymmetry through axiogenesis and the tentative isotropic CMB-polarization rotation near \\(0.3^\\circ\\).\n\nThe minimal construction predicts a positive birefringence angle and an early matter-dominated era followed by kination, ending at temperatures of approximately \\(3-50\\) MeV. Producing the required conserved axion charge is difficult in a single-field realization, so the authors construct a multi-field clockwork model that exponentially enhances the angular momentum.\n\n##### History\n\nPlanck analyses previously reported a possible isotropic rotation of \\(\\beta=0.35\\pm0.14^\\circ\\) ([Minami and Komatsu](https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.11254)), while [axiogenesis](https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.02080) was developed independently as a baryogenesis mechanism driven by axion rotation. This paper’s new contribution is a single dynamical origin for both effects, with a sign prediction and a constrained pre-BBN expansion history. It should not yet be read as evidence for an axion: the birefringence measurement remains sensitive to polarization calibration and Galactic foregrounds, while the necessary charge requires an elaborate multi-field sector. The late kination phase also lies close enough to BBN to require careful thermal-history tests.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-26611", "arxiv": "2609.26611", "title": "Dark-matter relay for ultra-high-energy cosmic rays", "authors": "Mikhail Sekretov", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★☆☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A speculative two-step mechanism transfers ultrahigh-energy cosmic-ray energy to dark matter for long-distance propagation, followed by reconversion into a baryon near the Milky Way.", "path": "2026/09/23/index.html#paper-2609-26611", "reportDate": "23 September 2026", "searchText": "Dark-matter relay for ultra-high-energy cosmic rays Mikhail Sekretov 2609.26611 A speculative two-step mechanism transfers ultrahigh-energy cosmic-ray energy to dark matter for long-distance propagation, followed by reconversion into a baryon near the Milky Way. 2026-09-23 23 September 2026 Cosmic rays Dark matter BSM #### ★☆☆ 9. Dark-matter relay for ultra-high-energy cosmic rays\n\n**Mikhail Sekretov**  \n[arXiv:2609.26611](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.26611)\n\nThe paper proposes that an ultrahigh-energy nucleon near an active galaxy transfers energy to a light neutral dark-matter particle. The boosted particle then crosses cosmological distances without GZK attenuation and scatters back into a baryon near the Milky Way.\n\nFor a benchmark \\(m_\\chi\\sim25\\) MeV dark-photon model, the required high-energy \\(\\chi N\\) cross section is of order \\(10^{-25}\\ {\\rm cm^2}\\). The probability of reconversion is estimated at roughly \\(5\\times10^{-5}\\) through the local Galactic disk and potentially \\(10^{-2}\\) along dark-matter-rich Galactic-center directions.\n\n##### History\n\nCosmic-ray-accelerated and boosted dark matter have been studied previously, as have dark-matter interactions that cool AGN cosmic rays ([related recent study](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11906)). The two-step “relay” mechanism appears to be new. It remains a feasibility argument rather than a predictive UHECR model: there is no end-to-end spectrum, composition or event-rate calculation, and the motivating BL Lac correlations are themselves statistically controversial. The large deep-inelastic cross section must also survive accelerator, cosmological and boosted-dark-matter constraints, while secondary gamma rays and neutrinos may provide stronger limits."}, {"id": "paper-2609-22638", "arxiv": "2609.22638", "title": "New bounds on light scalars from the tip of the red giant branch", "authors": "Natnael Debru, Audrey Fung, Saniya Heeba, Hugo Schérer, Katelin Schutz, Aaron C. Vincent", "tags": ["BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Self-consistent MESA stellar evolution and red-giant-branch measurements from 27 globular clusters constrain electron-coupled and Higgs-mixed light scalars.", "path": "2026/09/22/index.html#paper-2609-22638", "reportDate": "22 September 2026", "searchText": "New bounds on light scalars from the tip of the red giant branch Natnael Debru, Audrey Fung, Saniya Heeba, Hugo Schérer, Katelin Schutz, Aaron C. Vincent 2609.22638 Self-consistent MESA stellar evolution and red-giant-branch measurements from 27 globular clusters constrain electron-coupled and Higgs-mixed light scalars. 2026-09-22 22 September 2026 BSM #### ★★★ 1. New bounds on light scalars from the tip of the red giant branch\n\n**Natnael Debru, Audrey Fung, Saniya Heeba, Hugo Schérer, Katelin Schutz, Aaron C. Vincent**  \n[arXiv:2609.22638](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.22638)\n\nLight scalars coupled to electrons can be resonantly produced through longitudinal-plasmon conversion in degenerate red-giant cores. The resulting energy loss delays helium ignition and brightens the tip of the red giant branch.\n\nRather than evaluating the exotic emissivity at fixed benchmark core conditions, the authors incorporate it into MESA and evolve the stellar structure self-consistently. Comparing with the bolometric TRGB magnitudes of 27 Milky Way globular clusters gives\n\n\\[\n\\alpha_{\\phi ee}\\equiv\\frac{g_e^2}{4\\pi}\n   <1.6\\times10^{-34}\n\\]\n\nfor sub-keV scalar masses. For a Higgs-mixed scalar this becomes\n\n\\[\n\\sin\\theta<2.2\\times10^{-11},\n\\]\n\nthe strongest reported bound over approximately \\(1\\,{\\rm eV}-10\\,{\\rm keV}\\).\n\n##### History\n\n[Hardy and Lasenby](https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.05852) established that plasma mixing makes resonant longitudinal-plasmon conversion dominate low-mass scalar emission, but their red-giant estimate used representative core conditions rather than a full evolutionary calculation. Subsequent work concentrated largely on revised bremsstrahlung rates, including [Balaji et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.01669) and [Yamamoto and Yoshioka](https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.03123). The present calculation combines the resonant rate with stellar backreaction and a multi-cluster likelihood, strengthening the previous TRGB scalar bound by nearly two orders of magnitude. The remaining caveat is the dependence on stellar-evolution inputs and globular-cluster calibration, although the paper explicitly varies helium abundance, metallicity, mass loss and mixing length.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-22480", "arxiv": "2609.22480", "title": "Importance of the Resonant Cosmic-Ray Streaming Instability Upstream of Collisionless Shocks", "authors": "Bricker Ostler, Benedikt Schroer, Damiano Caprioli", "tags": ["Cosmic rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Kinetic calculations and hybrid-PIC simulations show that resonant streaming can dominate magnetic amplification driven by maximum-energy cosmic rays upstream of shocks.", "path": "2026/09/22/index.html#paper-2609-22480", "reportDate": "22 September 2026", "searchText": "Importance of the Resonant Cosmic-Ray Streaming Instability Upstream of Collisionless Shocks Bricker Ostler, Benedikt Schroer, Damiano Caprioli 2609.22480 Kinetic calculations and hybrid-PIC simulations show that resonant streaming can dominate magnetic amplification driven by maximum-energy cosmic rays upstream of shocks. 2026-09-22 22 September 2026 Cosmic rays #### ★★★ 2. Importance of the Resonant Cosmic-Ray Streaming Instability Upstream of Collisionless Shocks\n\n**Bricker Ostler, Benedikt Schroer, Damiano Caprioli**  \n[arXiv:2609.22480](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.22480)\n\nEscaping maximum-energy cosmic rays form a relativistic, forward-beamed population upstream of collisionless shocks. Solving the kinetic dispersion relation for cold and finite-width beams, the authors find that the resonant streaming mode can grow faster than the usually assumed non-resonant Bell mode.\n\nAt sufficiently slow shocks or large pitch-angle spread, the non-resonant mode can even become stable while the resonant mode survives. Relativistic hybrid-PIC simulations reproduce the linear result and yield nonlinear saturation around\n\n\\[\n\\delta B/B_0\\sim1.\n\\]\n\nBecause the growing wavelength is resonant with the escaping particles themselves, it directly supplies fluctuations capable of confining the highest-energy cosmic rays.\n\n##### History\n\nSince [Bell’s 2004 analysis](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1365-2966.2004.08097.x), upstream magnetic amplification has generally been organized around the fast non-resonant current-driven mode. Later kinetic and hybrid studies showed simultaneous resonant and non-resonant activity, for example [Haggerty, Caprioli and Zweibel](https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.06346), but did not establish the resonant mode as the dominant instability specifically for the narrow, maximum-energy escaping population. This paper changes that ordering and shows that neglecting resonance can underestimate amplification at precisely the gyroradius required for confinement. Its nonlinear simulations treat an idealized homogeneous beam rather than a self-consistent shock precursor, so the impact on maximum SNR energy still needs to be demonstrated in shock simulations.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-22577", "arxiv": "2609.22577", "title": "Resolving the Galactic Center Region’s Gamma-Ray Emission and Searching for its Neutrino Counterpart with HAWC and IceCube", "authors": "S. Yun-Cárcamo, R. Babu, for the HAWC and IceCube collaborations", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Neutrinos", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "HAWC separates point-like and diffuse Galactic-center emission; an IceCube search with the corresponding hadronic template finds no neutrino excess.", "path": "2026/09/22/index.html#paper-2609-22577", "reportDate": "22 September 2026", "searchText": "Resolving the Galactic Center Region’s Gamma-Ray Emission and Searching for its Neutrino Counterpart with HAWC and IceCube S. Yun-Cárcamo, R. Babu, for the HAWC and IceCube collaborations 2609.22577 HAWC separates point-like and diffuse Galactic-center emission; an IceCube search with the corresponding hadronic template finds no neutrino excess. 2026-09-22 22 September 2026 Gamma rays Neutrinos Multimessenger #### ★★★ 3. Resolving the Galactic Center Region’s Gamma-Ray Emission and Searching for its Neutrino Counterpart with HAWC and IceCube\n\n**S. Yun-Cárcamo, R. Babu, for the HAWC and IceCube collaborations**  \n[arXiv:2609.22577](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.22577)\n\nUsing approximately 2860 days of HAWC data and improved event reconstruction, the analysis resolves the Galactic-center emission into a point-like component, HAWC J1745−2857, and Galactic diffuse emission. The point-source spectrum remains an unbroken power law beyond \\(100\\) TeV.\n\nThe diffuse contribution is approximately 35% of the point-source flux at the Galactic center. The resulting gamma-ray morphology is converted into a hadronic neutrino template and searched for using 12 years of IceCube muon-track data. No excess is found:\n\n\\[\np=0.49,\n\\]\n\nand the 90% upper limit is approximately twelve times the neutrino flux predicted from the nominal hadronic gamma-ray model.\n\n##### History\n\nHAWC’s earlier analysis detected \\(>100\\)-TeV Galactic-center emission but modeled it essentially as one point source. ([Previous HAWC result](https://doi.org/10.3847/2041-8213/ad772e)) The new analysis retains that hard, cutoff-free component while explicitly separating diffuse ridge emission. IceCube had already established diffuse Galactic-plane neutrinos at population level ([Science 380, adc9818](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.adc9818)), but this is the first IceCube limit tied to HAWC’s two-component Galactic-center morphology. Numerically, however, the limit remains a factor of twelve above the transparent \\(pp\\) prediction: it neither confirms nor excludes the hadronic interpretation. The principal advance is therefore the spatially consistent multimessenger template, not a constraining neutrino result.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-22581", "arxiv": "2609.22581", "title": "Hunting Thermal Relics in the DESI DR1 Lyα Forest", "authors": "Emanuelly Silva, Artur Ladeira, Rafael C. Nunes, Eleonora Di Valentino", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "DESI Lyman-α forest information, combined with CMB and BAO data, constrains extra radiation and thermal sterile-neutrino relics with nonstandard temperatures.", "path": "2026/09/22/index.html#paper-2609-22581", "reportDate": "22 September 2026", "searchText": "Hunting Thermal Relics in the DESI DR1 Lyα Forest Emanuelly Silva, Artur Ladeira, Rafael C. Nunes, Eleonora Di Valentino 2609.22581 DESI Lyman-α forest information, combined with CMB and BAO data, constrains extra radiation and thermal sterile-neutrino relics with nonstandard temperatures. 2026-09-22 22 September 2026 Neutrinos Dark matter BSM #### ★★★ 4. Hunting Thermal Relics in the DESI DR1 Ly\\(\\alpha\\) Forest\n\n**Emanuelly Silva, Artur Ladeira, Rafael C. Nunes, Eleonora Di Valentino**  \n[arXiv:2609.22581](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.22581)\n\nThe authors combine Planck 2018, DESI DR2 BAO and DESI DR1 one-dimensional Lyman-\\(\\alpha\\) forest power-spectrum information to constrain extra radiation and thermal sterile neutrinos whose temperature may differ from that of active neutrinos.\n\nThey validate DESI’s two-parameter \\(P_{\\rm 1D}\\) compression for this nonstandard model, finding residual cosmological dependence below 0.15%. No extra radiation or sterile component is preferred. The combined limits are\n\n\\[\nN_{\\rm eff}<3.41,\n\\qquad\nm_s^{\\rm eff}<0.061~{\\rm eV}\n\\]\n\nat 95% credibility. Lyman-\\(\\alpha\\) information improves the \\(m_s^{\\rm eff}\\) limit by roughly a factor of seven relative to CMB alone.\n\n##### History\n\nThe DESI DR1 Lyman-\\(\\alpha\\) cosmology analysis introduced the compressed small-scale-power likelihood but tested mostly standard cosmological extensions ([Chaves-Montero et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21432)). Different-temperature sterile relics had recently been constrained with CMB and large-scale structure without this Lyman-\\(\\alpha\\) information ([Ladeira et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.21518)). The new contribution is both the validation of the compression for a colder sterile distribution and the resulting \\(0.061\\)-eV effective-mass bound. \\(m_s^{\\rm eff}\\) is an abundance-weighted cosmological parameter, not the physical sterile mass; a colder, dilute population can still contain substantially heavier particles. The limit also inherits the astrophysical and hydrodynamical assumptions used to compress the forest likelihood.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-22806", "arxiv": "2609.22806", "title": "Gamma-Ray Echo as a Probe of Supernova Neutrino Emission Anisotropy and Long-Baseline Effects", "authors": "Garv Chauhan, Cecilia Lunardini, Yago Porto", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A supernova’s delayed 511-keV gamma-ray echo could be compared with its directly measured neutrino fluence to probe emission anisotropy and propagation effects.", "path": "2026/09/22/index.html#paper-2609-22806", "reportDate": "22 September 2026", "searchText": "Gamma-Ray Echo as a Probe of Supernova Neutrino Emission Anisotropy and Long-Baseline Effects Garv Chauhan, Cecilia Lunardini, Yago Porto 2609.22806 A supernova’s delayed 511-keV gamma-ray echo could be compared with its directly measured neutrino fluence to probe emission anisotropy and propagation effects. 2026-09-22 22 September 2026 Neutrinos Gamma rays Multimessenger #### ★★★ 5. Gamma-Ray Echo as a Probe of Supernova Neutrino Emission Anisotropy and Long-Baseline Effects\n\n**Garv Chauhan, Cecilia Lunardini, Yago Porto**  \n[arXiv:2609.22806](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.22806)\n\nElectron antineutrinos from a core-collapse supernova undergo inverse beta decay in the progenitor’s outer hydrogen envelope. The resulting positrons thermalize and produce a delayed 511-keV annihilation signal—the gamma-ray echo.\n\nThe authors compare the \\(\\bar\\nu_e\\) fluence inferred from this echo with the fluence measured directly at Earth. Because the envelope and Earth view the source along different effective directions and baselines, discrepancies can probe intrinsic neutrino-emission anisotropy or propagation effects such as invisible neutrino decay. For a nearby supernova, tens-of-percent effects could be tested with a 511-keV effective area of order\n\n\\[\nA_{\\rm eff}\\sim10^4~{\\rm cm^2}.\n\\]\n\n##### History\n\n[Lunardini et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.13197) introduced the modern quantitative gamma-ray-echo calculation for an approximately isotropic neutrino burst. The new paper generalizes it to dipolar and quadrupolar emission and, more originally, formulates the stellar envelope and terrestrial detector as an astrophysical near–far experiment. There is no previous quantitative sensitivity study of this particular echo/direct-neutrino ratio. The limitation is instrumental: the required narrow-band MeV effective area exceeds that of current instruments, and intrinsic anisotropy is partly degenerate with exotic propagation. The latter would require time, energy or directional structure to disentangle.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-24950", "arxiv": "2609.24950", "title": "Search for a top-philic heavy resonance in association with top quarks", "authors": "ATLAS Collaboration", "tags": ["Particle physics", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "An ATLAS multilepton search combining Run-2 and Run-3 data finds no significant excess and constrains a heavy vector resonance that couples preferentially to top quarks.", "path": "2026/09/22/index.html#paper-2609-24950", "reportDate": "22 September 2026", "searchText": "Search for a top-philic heavy resonance in association with top quarks ATLAS Collaboration 2609.24950 An ATLAS multilepton search combining Run-2 and Run-3 data finds no significant excess and constrains a heavy vector resonance that couples preferentially to top quarks. 2026-09-22 22 September 2026 Particle physics BSM #### ★★★ 6. Search for a top-philic heavy resonance in association with top quarks\n\n**ATLAS Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2609.24950](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.24950)\n\nATLAS searches for a color-singlet vector \\(Z'\\) that couples preferentially to top quarks, using the complete \\(140~{\\rm fb^{-1}}\\) Run-2 sample plus \\(56~{\\rm fb^{-1}}\\) from Run 3.\n\nEvents with two same-sign leptons or at least three leptons are classified using a mass-parameterized transformer network. No significant excess is found. For a purely left-handed benchmark with \\(c_t=1\\),\n\n\\[\nm_{Z'}<1.15~{\\rm TeV}\n\\]\n\nis excluded at 95% confidence, compared with an expected reach of \\(1.25\\) TeV. For \\(c_t=3\\), the observed exclusion reaches \\(2.0\\) TeV.\n\n##### History\n\nATLAS’s earlier full-Run-2 single-lepton search constrained cross sections but could not exclude any mass for the \\(c_t=1\\) benchmark. ([Previous ATLAS search](https://doi.org/10.1140/epjc/s10052-023-12318-9)) The new multilepton analysis improves the cross-section sensitivity by approximately a factor of fifteen; the Run-3 addition alone supplies about 30% more sensitivity at \\(1.5\\) TeV. This is therefore a substantial experimental improvement, not merely a luminosity update. The observed reach is modestly weaker than expected, and the quoted mass exclusions apply to a simplified resonance with specified coupling and chirality rather than generic top-compositeness models.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-24085", "arxiv": "2609.24085", "title": "Constraints on Axion-like Dark Matter from Cosmic Birefringence with a Polarization Array of Repeating Fast Radio Bursts", "authors": "Xiaohui Liu, Zi-Yan Yuwen, Yun-Long Zhang, Zerui Liu, Shiqian Zhao, Shuai Feng, Wei-Yang Wang, Xuelei Chen", "tags": ["Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A joint polarization analysis of three repeating fast radio bursts finds no axion-induced oscillation and constrains ultralight axion-like dark matter.", "path": "2026/09/22/index.html#paper-2609-24085", "reportDate": "22 September 2026", "searchText": "Constraints on Axion-like Dark Matter from Cosmic Birefringence with a Polarization Array of Repeating Fast Radio Bursts Xiaohui Liu, Zi-Yan Yuwen, Yun-Long Zhang, Zerui Liu, Shiqian Zhao, Shuai Feng, Wei-Yang Wang, Xuelei Chen 2609.24085 A joint polarization analysis of three repeating fast radio bursts finds no axion-induced oscillation and constrains ultralight axion-like dark matter. 2026-09-22 22 September 2026 Dark matter BSM #### ★★☆ 7. Constraints on Axion-like Dark Matter from Cosmic Birefringence with a Polarization Array of Repeating Fast Radio Bursts\n\n**Xiaohui Liu, Zi-Yan Yuwen, Yun-Long Zhang, Zerui Liu, Shiqian Zhao, Shuai Feng, Wei-Yang Wang, Xuelei Chen**  \n[arXiv:2609.24085](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.24085)\n\nA coherently oscillating axion field produces periodic rotations of linear polarization. The authors analyze three highly active repeating FRBs and jointly treat their independent source terms and correlated Earth term in a hierarchical Bayesian model that includes intrinsic burst-to-burst polarization-angle scatter.\n\nNo oscillation is found. Across\n\n\\[\n10^{-23}\\lesssim m_a/{\\rm eV}\\lesssim10^{-18},\n\\]\n\nthe polarization-array limits range from roughly \\(10^{-12.6}\\) to \\(10^{-7.9}\\ {\\rm GeV^{-1}}\\).\n\n##### History\n\nPulsar-polarization arrays already exploit the same correlated Earth term ([Liu, Lou and Ren](https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.10615); [PPTA limits](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.02229)), while a single-FRB analysis was performed by [Wang et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.00473). This is the first multi-source repeating-FRB polarization array. Combining the three sources improves the low-mass limit by factors of about \\(2-10\\) relative to the best individual FRB, primarily by combining different observing baselines. At present, however, auto-correlations dominate; the shared Earth term adds little, and the bounds remain weaker than several pulsar and other astrophysical constraints. With only three sources and a maximum baseline of about 472 days, this is a proof of concept.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-24735", "arxiv": "2609.24735", "title": "Particle Acceleration in Stratified 2D MRI Turbulence", "authors": "Astor Sandoval, Mario Riquelme, Jorge Cuadra", "tags": ["Cosmic rays"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Particle-in-cell simulations trace acceleration in stratified magnetorotational turbulence through reconnection injection and successive first- and second-order Fermi stages.", "path": "2026/09/22/index.html#paper-2609-24735", "reportDate": "22 September 2026", "searchText": "Particle Acceleration in Stratified 2D MRI Turbulence Astor Sandoval, Mario Riquelme, Jorge Cuadra 2609.24735 Particle-in-cell simulations trace acceleration in stratified magnetorotational turbulence through reconnection injection and successive first- and second-order Fermi stages. 2026-09-22 22 September 2026 Cosmic rays #### ★★☆ 8. Particle Acceleration in Stratified 2D MRI Turbulence\n\n**Astor Sandoval, Mario Riquelme, Jorge Cuadra**  \n[arXiv:2609.24735](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.24735)\n\nParticle-in-cell simulations identify three stages of acceleration in collisionless magnetorotational turbulence:\n\n1. reconnection electric fields inject particles during plasmoid splitting;\n2. converging separating plasmoids produce rapid first-order-Fermi acceleration;\n3. repeated plasmoid encounters produce slower second-order-Fermi acceleration, with a subdominant shear contribution.\n\nThis explains why the maximum energy scales as\n\n\\[\nE_{\\max}\\propto\\frac{\\omega_{c,0}}{\\Omega_0},\n\\]\n\nwhile the late stochastic phase increases energies by only a factor of a few.\n\n##### History\n\nThe authors’ earlier stratified simulations discovered the \\(E_{\\max}\\propto\\omega_c/\\Omega\\) scaling without isolating its physical origin ([Sandoval et al. 2024](https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.12348)). Other kinetic MRI simulations established persistent nonthermal acceleration in unstratified boxes ([Bacchini et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.01399)). The new work explains the scaling through rapid plasmoid-driven first-order acceleration rather than ordinary slow stochastic energization. The main caveat is substantial: the simulations remain two-dimensional and use a pair plasma. Three-dimensional topology, electron–ion energy partition and radiative losses may change both injection and saturation.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-24887", "arxiv": "2609.24887", "title": "A New Probe of Strongly-Interacting Dark Sectors using Neutrino Telescopes", "authors": "Jose A. Macias Cruz, Christopher V. Cappiello, P. S. Bhupal Dev, Suchita Kulkarni", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Dark vector-meson resonances could imprint absorption features in ultrahigh-energy neutrino spectra through interactions with the cosmic neutrino background.", "path": "2026/09/22/index.html#paper-2609-24887", "reportDate": "22 September 2026", "searchText": "A New Probe of Strongly-Interacting Dark Sectors using Neutrino Telescopes Jose A. Macias Cruz, Christopher V. Cappiello, P. S. Bhupal Dev, Suchita Kulkarni 2609.24887 Dark vector-meson resonances could imprint absorption features in ultrahigh-energy neutrino spectra through interactions with the cosmic neutrino background. 2026-09-22 22 September 2026 Neutrinos Dark matter BSM #### ★★☆ 9. A New Probe of Strongly-Interacting Dark Sectors using Neutrino Telescopes\n\n**Jose A. Macias Cruz, Christopher V. Cappiello, P. S. Bhupal Dev, Suchita Kulkarni**  \n[arXiv:2609.24887](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.24887)\n\nHigh-energy cosmic neutrinos can resonantly annihilate on the cosmic neutrino background through dark vector mesons. The resulting conversion into dark pions produces an absorption feature whose position reflects the dark-hadron spectrum.\n\nUnlike the Standard-Model \\(\\rho\\) resonance, the dark vector can possess a much larger branching ratio into neutrinos, allowing an observable dip without requiring a large local relic-neutrino overdensity. Forecasts indicate sensitivity with future detectors, particularly IceCube-Gen2 Radio.\n\n##### History\n\nResonant C\\(\\nu\\)B absorption is an old idea, originally involving the \\(Z\\) resonance, and has been extensively adapted to light secret-neutrino mediators ([Ioka and Murase](https://arxiv.org/abs/1404.2279); [Esteban et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.13568)). Standard QCD-vector-meson absorption was also considered, but its tiny neutrino branching ratio makes detection impractical without a large relic-neutrino overdensity. The new element is to use the resonance tower of a confining dark sector as a spectroscopic observable. This remains a forecast: sensitivity depends on the uncertain ultrahigh-energy neutrino flux, the absolute neutrino mass, detector energy resolution and the assumed dark-neutrino portal."}, {"id": "paper-2609-21921", "arxiv": "2609.21921", "title": "Observation of the doubly charmed baryon Ωcc⁺", "authors": "LHCb Collaboration", "tags": ["Particle physics"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "LHCb observes the doubly charmed strange baryon Ωcc⁺ in upgraded-detector data, with a global significance of 8.7σ.", "path": "2026/09/21/index.html#paper-2609-21921", "reportDate": "21 September 2026", "searchText": "Observation of the doubly charmed baryon Ωcc⁺ LHCb Collaboration 2609.21921 LHCb observes the doubly charmed strange baryon Ωcc⁺ in upgraded-detector data, with a global significance of 8.7σ. 2026-09-21 21 September 2026 Particle physics #### ★★★ 1. Observation of the doubly charmed baryon \\(\\Omega_{cc}^{+}\\)\n\n**LHCb Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2609.21921](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.21921)\n\nLHCb reports the first observation of the doubly charmed strange baryon \\(\\Omega_{cc}^{+}\\), reconstructed through\n\n\\[\n\\Omega_{cc}^{+}\\rightarrow\\Omega_c^0\\pi^+,\n\\qquad\n\\Omega_c^0\\rightarrow pK^-K^-\\pi^+ .\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(6.3~\\mathrm{fb^{-1}}\\) of 13.6-TeV proton-proton data collected with the upgraded detector in 2024, the analysis finds a peaking structure with a global significance of \\(8.7\\sigma\\). Its measured mass is\n\n\\[\nm_{\\Omega_{cc}^{+}}\n =3725.9\\pm1.0_{\\rm stat}\\pm0.2_{\\rm syst}\n \\pm0.4_{\\rm lifetime}\\pm0.6_{\\rm external}\n \\ {\\rm MeV}/c^2.\n\\]\n\nIts weak decay establishes that the state is a ground-state doubly charmed baryon rather than a short-lived excited resonance.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-20949", "arxiv": "2609.20949", "title": "First measurement of differential cross sections in kinematic-imbalance variables with electron-neutrino interactions using MicroBooNE", "authors": "MicroBooNE Collaboration", "tags": ["Neutrinos"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "MicroBooNE measures electron-neutrino–argon cross sections in transverse kinematic-imbalance variables, testing nuclear-interaction and intranuclear-transport models.", "path": "2026/09/21/index.html#paper-2609-20949", "reportDate": "21 September 2026", "searchText": "First measurement of differential cross sections in kinematic-imbalance variables with electron-neutrino interactions using MicroBooNE MicroBooNE Collaboration 2609.20949 MicroBooNE measures electron-neutrino–argon cross sections in transverse kinematic-imbalance variables, testing nuclear-interaction and intranuclear-transport models. 2026-09-21 21 September 2026 Neutrinos #### ★★★ 2. First measurement of differential cross sections in kinematic-imbalance variables with electron-neutrino interactions using MicroBooNE\n\n**MicroBooNE Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2609.20949](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.20949)\n\nMicroBooNE measures flux-integrated \\(\\nu_e\\)-argon cross sections in transverse kinematic-imbalance variables using its complete Booster Neutrino Beam exposure of approximately\n\n\\[\n1.3\\times10^{21}\\ {\\rm protons\\ on\\ target}.\n\\]\n\nThese observables separate simple quasielastic scattering from Fermi motion, multinucleon interactions and final-state rescattering more effectively than inclusive lepton kinematics. Comparisons with commonly used neutrino-event generators reveal discrepancies in parts of phase space expected to be relatively insensitive to final-state interactions, pointing to deficiencies in the primary nuclear-interaction model as well as in intranuclear transport.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-20944", "arxiv": "2609.20944", "title": "Systematic uncertainties and their impact on gamma-ray searches for dark matter in dwarf galaxies", "authors": "Toni Bertólez-Martínez, Dan Hooper, Arifa Khatee Zathul", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Gamma rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A study of dwarf-galaxy J-factor systematics quantifies how additional uncertainties and spatial extension can weaken stacked gamma-ray annihilation limits.", "path": "2026/09/21/index.html#paper-2609-20944", "reportDate": "21 September 2026", "searchText": "Systematic uncertainties and their impact on gamma-ray searches for dark matter in dwarf galaxies Toni Bertólez-Martínez, Dan Hooper, Arifa Khatee Zathul 2609.20944 A study of dwarf-galaxy J-factor systematics quantifies how additional uncertainties and spatial extension can weaken stacked gamma-ray annihilation limits. 2026-09-21 21 September 2026 Dark matter Gamma rays #### ★★★ 3. Systematic uncertainties and their impact on gamma-ray searches for dark matter in dwarf galaxies\n\n**Toni Bertólez-Martínez, Dan Hooper, Arifa Khatee Zathul**  \n[arXiv:2609.20944](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.20944)\n\nThe paper examines whether quoted dwarf-spheroidal \\(J\\)-factor uncertainties adequately describe uncertainties from stellar membership, unresolved binaries, equilibrium and spherical-symmetry assumptions, and the adopted stellar and dark-matter density profiles.\n\nThe spread among published determinations is substantially larger than many nominal statistical error bars. The authors argue that an additional uncertainty of at least\n\n\\[\n\\Delta\\log_{10}J\\sim0.5\n\\]\n\nis plausible. In simulated stacked Fermi-LAT analyses of 27 dwarfs, adding systematic uncertainties of \\(0.5\\) and \\(1.0\\) dex weakens median annihilation limits by factors of approximately \\(1.4\\) and \\(3.2\\), respectively. Treating spatially extended halos as point sources can further overstate the sensitivity.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-21118", "arxiv": "2609.21118", "title": "Energy partition in relativistic electron–positron–ion reconnection", "authors": "Nilay Mancini, Luca Comisso, Lorenzo Sironi", "tags": ["Multimessenger", "Gamma rays", "Neutrinos"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Analytic particle-orbit arguments and particle-in-cell simulations determine how magnetic-reconnection energy is divided between leptons and ions in mixed pair-ion plasmas.", "path": "2026/09/21/index.html#paper-2609-21118", "reportDate": "21 September 2026", "searchText": "Energy partition in relativistic electron–positron–ion reconnection Nilay Mancini, Luca Comisso, Lorenzo Sironi 2609.21118 Analytic particle-orbit arguments and particle-in-cell simulations determine how magnetic-reconnection energy is divided between leptons and ions in mixed pair-ion plasmas. 2026-09-21 21 September 2026 Multimessenger Gamma rays Neutrinos #### ★★★ 4. Energy partition in relativistic electron–positron–ion reconnection\n\n**Nilay Mancini, Luca Comisso, Lorenzo Sironi**  \n[arXiv:2609.21118](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.21118)\n\nThe authors combine analytic particle-orbit arguments with particle-in-cell simulations to determine how magnetic-reconnection energy is divided between leptons and ions in mixed pair-ion plasmas.\n\nThey find that the mean lepton energy scales approximately with the effective electron magnetization, while the ion energy in the nonrelativistic-escape regime behaves as\n\n\\[\n\\langle\\gamma_i-1\\rangle\n \\propto \\mu^{1/3}\\left(\\sigma_e^\\ast\\right)^{2/3},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mu=m_i/m_e\\). Ion and lepton energies become comparable only when ion escape is itself ultrarelativistic. In pair-dominated systems, the ion energy fraction can consequently lie orders of magnitude below simple equipartition assumptions.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-21947", "arxiv": "2609.21947", "title": "Test of lepton-flavor universality with B̄ → D(*)τ⁻ν̄τ decays at Belle II", "authors": "Belle II Collaboration", "tags": ["Particle physics", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Belle II simultaneously measures R(D) and R(D*) with hadronic tagging; the combined result agrees with the Standard Model at 1.5σ.", "path": "2026/09/21/index.html#paper-2609-21947", "reportDate": "21 September 2026", "searchText": "Test of lepton-flavor universality with B̄ → D(*)τ⁻ν̄τ decays at Belle II Belle II Collaboration 2609.21947 Belle II simultaneously measures R(D) and R(D*) with hadronic tagging; the combined result agrees with the Standard Model at 1.5σ. 2026-09-21 21 September 2026 Particle physics BSM #### ★★★ 5. Test of lepton-flavor universality with \\(\\bar B\\rightarrow D^{(*)}\\tau^-\\bar\\nu_\\tau\\) decays at Belle II\n\n**Belle II Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2609.21947](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.21947)\n\nBelle II performs a simultaneous hadronic-tag measurement using \\(387\\) million \\(\\Upsilon(4S)\\) decays and obtains\n\n\\[\nR(D^\\ast)=0.242\\pm0.019_{\\rm stat}\\pm0.016_{\\rm syst},\n\\]\n\n\\[\nR(D)=0.439\\pm0.055_{\\rm stat}\\pm0.046_{\\rm syst}.\n\\]\n\nThe two measurements are negatively correlated. Their combination agrees with the Standard Model at \\(1.5\\sigma\\), while the updated global average differs by approximately \\(1.3\\sigma\\).\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-20936", "arxiv": "2609.20936", "title": "AutoTherm: Automated thermal field-theory rates for cosmology", "authors": "Killian Bouzoud, Jacopo Ghiglieri, Greg Jackson", "tags": ["BSM", "Dark matter", "Neutrinos"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "AutoTherm automates ultrarelativistic thermal production rates, combining matrix elements, thermal phase-space integration and hard-thermal-loop resummation.", "path": "2026/09/21/index.html#paper-2609-20936", "reportDate": "21 September 2026", "searchText": "AutoTherm: Automated thermal field-theory rates for cosmology Killian Bouzoud, Jacopo Ghiglieri, Greg Jackson 2609.20936 AutoTherm automates ultrarelativistic thermal production rates, combining matrix elements, thermal phase-space integration and hard-thermal-loop resummation. 2026-09-21 21 September 2026 BSM Dark matter Neutrinos #### ★★★ 6. AutoTherm: Automated thermal field-theory rates for cosmology\n\n**Killian Bouzoud, Jacopo Ghiglieri, Greg Jackson**  \n[arXiv:2609.20936](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.20936)\n\nAutoTherm is a Wolfram/Python pipeline that converts particle-physics models into ultrarelativistic thermal production rates. It automates \\(2\\leftrightarrow2\\) matrix elements, thermal phase-space integration and hard-thermal-loop resummation for massless \\(t\\)-channel mediators.\n\nThree formally leading-order-equivalent prescriptions are provided to expose sensitivity to the hard-soft separation. Validation cases include right-handed neutrinos, gravitons, gravitinos, axions and dark photons; the comparisons reproduce most known results while identifying corrections or unexpectedly large scheme dependence in several cases.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-21823", "arxiv": "2609.21823", "title": "LUX-ZEPLIN’s Stairway to Heaνen: limits on elastic dark-matter scattering from solar capture", "authors": "Debajit Bose, Akash Kumar Saha, Nirmal Raj, Tarak Nath Maity, Ranjan Laha", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Neutrinos"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Solar capture and neutrino limits constrain elastic effective-operator interpretations of LZ’s isolated 248-keV recoil candidate.", "path": "2026/09/21/index.html#paper-2609-21823", "reportDate": "21 September 2026", "searchText": "LUX-ZEPLIN’s Stairway to Heaνen: limits on elastic dark-matter scattering from solar capture Debajit Bose, Akash Kumar Saha, Nirmal Raj, Tarak Nath Maity, Ranjan Laha 2609.21823 Solar capture and neutrino limits constrain elastic effective-operator interpretations of LZ’s isolated 248-keV recoil candidate. 2026-09-21 21 September 2026 Dark matter Neutrinos #### ★★☆ 7. LUX-ZEPLIN’s Stairway to Hea\\(\\nu\\)en: limits on elastic dark-matter scattering from solar capture\n\n**Debajit Bose, Akash Kumar Saha, Nirmal Raj, Tarak Nath Maity, Ranjan Laha**  \n[arXiv:2609.21823](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.21823)\n\nThe authors reconstruct nonrelativistic effective-operator interpretations of LZ’s isolated \\(248\\)-keV recoil candidate and calculate the corresponding capture and annihilation of dark matter in the Sun.\n\nFor annihilation into \\(W^+W^-\\), \\(b\\bar b\\), \\(\\tau^+\\tau^-\\) or neutrino pairs, Super-Kamiokande and IceCube exclude substantial mass ranges in which elastic scattering could reproduce the LZ event. Direct annihilation to neutrinos generally provides the strongest constraint.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-21991", "arxiv": "2609.21991", "title": "Searching for axions from atmospheric kaon decays", "authors": "Tousif Raza", "tags": ["BSM", "Cosmic rays", "Neutrinos"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Atmospheric charged-kaon decays provide a possible source of axion-like particles that decay to photons inside large underground neutrino detectors.", "path": "2026/09/21/index.html#paper-2609-21991", "reportDate": "21 September 2026", "searchText": "Searching for axions from atmospheric kaon decays Tousif Raza 2609.21991 Atmospheric charged-kaon decays provide a possible source of axion-like particles that decay to photons inside large underground neutrino detectors. 2026-09-21 21 September 2026 BSM Cosmic rays Neutrinos #### ★★☆ 8. Searching for axions from atmospheric kaon decays\n\n**Tousif Raza**  \n[arXiv:2609.21991](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.21991)\n\nCosmic-ray air showers produce charged kaons that could undergo\n\n\\[\nK^+\\rightarrow\\pi^+a.\n\\]\n\nA sufficiently long-lived axion-like particle can reach an underground detector before decaying to two photons. The paper uses MCEq with atmospheric-density and hadronic-interaction models to predict the angular and energy distributions, then derives constraints from published Super-Kamiokande and IceCube data for masses extending to approximately \\(350\\) MeV.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-21444", "arxiv": "2609.21444", "title": "The high-velocity dark-matter halo and the LZ high-recoil event", "authors": "Ciaran A. J. O’Hare", "tags": ["Dark matter"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Milky-Way analogues from TNG50 and FIRE/Latte are used to study the high-speed dark-matter population required for the LZ recoil candidate.", "path": "2026/09/21/index.html#paper-2609-21444", "reportDate": "21 September 2026", "searchText": "The high-velocity dark-matter halo and the LZ high-recoil event Ciaran A. J. O’Hare 2609.21444 Milky-Way analogues from TNG50 and FIRE/Latte are used to study the high-speed dark-matter population required for the LZ recoil candidate. 2026-09-21 21 September 2026 Dark matter #### ★★☆ 9. The high-velocity dark-matter halo and the LZ high-recoil event\n\n**Ciaran A. J. O’Hare**  \n[arXiv:2609.21444](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.21444)\n\nThis study uses 98 Milky-Way analogues from TNG50 and three high-resolution FIRE/Latte halos to determine whether realistic halo dynamics materially alter the high-speed dark-matter population required for the LZ recoil candidate.\n\nFor the event date, the inferred maximum laboratory-frame speed is\n\n\\[\nv_{\\max}=792.7^{+82.9}_{-95.6}\\ {\\rm km\\,s^{-1}}\n\\]\n\nat 95% containment, close to the conventional Standard Halo Model estimate. Simulated halos generally co-rotate with their stellar disks, but their negatively skewed velocity distributions retain substantial counter-rotating high-speed tails. At thresholds above roughly \\(700~{\\rm km\\,s^{-1}}\\), annual modulation can approach unity in some halos."}, {"id": "paper-2609-19477", "arxiv": "2609.19477", "title": "Lepto-hadronic modeling of blazars associated with well-reconstructed high-energy neutrino events", "authors": "Leonard Pfeiffer, Margot Boughelilba, Simone Garrappa, Sara Buson, Massimiliano Lincetto, Annette Bremer", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The authors examine six blazars that are the sole gamma-ray counterparts inside the reconstructed directions of well-localized IceCAT-1 neutrino events, also accounting for the improved localizations anticipated from IceCAT-2.", "path": "2026/09/18/index.html#paper-2609-19477", "reportDate": "18 September 2026", "searchText": "Lepto-hadronic modeling of blazars associated with well-reconstructed high-energy neutrino events Leonard Pfeiffer, Margot Boughelilba, Simone Garrappa, Sara Buson, Massimiliano Lincetto, Annette Bremer 2609.19477 The authors examine six blazars that are the sole gamma-ray counterparts inside the reconstructed directions of well-localized IceCAT-1 neutrino events, also accounting for the improved localizations anticipated from IceCAT-2. 2026-09-18 18 September 2026 Neutrinos Gamma rays Multimessenger #### ★★★ 1. Lepto-hadronic modeling of blazars associated with well-reconstructed high-energy neutrino events\n\n**Leonard Pfeiffer, Margot Boughelilba, Simone Garrappa, Sara Buson, Massimiliano Lincetto, Annette Bremer**  \n[arXiv:2609.19477](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.19477)\n\nThe authors examine six blazars that are the sole gamma-ray counterparts inside the reconstructed directions of well-localized IceCAT-1 neutrino events, also accounting for the improved localizations anticipated from IceCAT-2.\n\nMultifrequency spectral-energy distributions are fitted using compact one-zone lepto-hadronic models. The preferred emission regions lie near the broad-line region, where external photons can substantially enhance the photohadronic interaction rate. Most fitted gamma-ray spectra remain leptonic-dominated, with hadronic cascades subdominant; one object permits hadronically dominated gamma-ray emission.\n\nThe maximum predicted IceCube rate is approximately\n\n\\[\nR_\\nu\\sim5\\times10^{-2}\\ {\\rm yr^{-1}}.\n\\]\n\nThis can be statistically compatible with one detected event for several sources, but it does not establish a physical association. The modeled objects would contribute negligibly to the diffuse astrophysical-neutrino flux.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-19174", "arxiv": "2609.19174", "title": "Testing Higgs-Coupled Minimal Dark Matter with Solar Neutrinos after the LZ High-Recoil Event", "authors": "Mattia Di Mauro", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Neutrinos", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This paper subjects Higgs-coupled minimal dark-matter interpretations of the isolated 248-keV LZ recoil candidate to an independent solar-neutrino test.", "path": "2026/09/18/index.html#paper-2609-19174", "reportDate": "18 September 2026", "searchText": "Testing Higgs-Coupled Minimal Dark Matter with Solar Neutrinos after the LZ High-Recoil Event Mattia Di Mauro 2609.19174 This paper subjects Higgs-coupled minimal dark-matter interpretations of the isolated 248-keV LZ recoil candidate to an independent solar-neutrino test. 2026-09-18 18 September 2026 Dark matter Neutrinos BSM #### ★★★ 2. Testing Higgs-Coupled Minimal Dark Matter with Solar Neutrinos after the LZ High-Recoil Event\n\n**Mattia Di Mauro**  \n[arXiv:2609.19174](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.19174)\n\nThis paper subjects Higgs-coupled minimal dark-matter interpretations of the isolated **248-keV LZ recoil candidate** to an independent solar-neutrino test.\n\nThe electroweak interaction responsible for endothermic xenon scattering also permits capture in the Sun. The calculation includes exact inelastic kinematics, realistic isotope mixtures, nuclear form factors, thermal nuclear motion near the capture threshold, orbital cooling through loop-induced elastic scattering, and an explicit test of capture–annihilation equilibrium.\n\nFor two thermal benchmarks within IceCube’s published mass grid, the predicted solar annihilation rates exceed the corresponding \\(W^+W^-\\) IceCube response by factors of approximately\n\n\\[\n2.2\\times10^3\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n91.\n\\]\n\nA 22-TeV benchmark remains strongly disfavored under a conservative extrapolation beyond IceCube’s published 10-TeV grid. The 48-TeV solution is closer to the sensitivity boundary, while the conclusions for 82 and 130 TeV become increasingly response-dependent.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-19259", "arxiv": "2609.19259", "title": "On the Clustering Bias of Unresolved Gamma-Ray Sources", "authors": "Bhashin A. Thakore, Marco Regis, Michela Negro, Stefano Camera, Daniel Gruen, Nicolao Fornengo, Aaron J. Roodman", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Dark matter"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Cross-correlations between the unresolved gamma-ray background and weak gravitational lensing depend not only on source luminosities and redshifts, but also on how strongly the unresolved sources trace the matter distribution.", "path": "2026/09/18/index.html#paper-2609-19259", "reportDate": "18 September 2026", "searchText": "On the Clustering Bias of Unresolved Gamma-Ray Sources Bhashin A. Thakore, Marco Regis, Michela Negro, Stefano Camera, Daniel Gruen, Nicolao Fornengo, Aaron J. Roodman 2609.19259 Cross-correlations between the unresolved gamma-ray background and weak gravitational lensing depend not only on source luminosities and redshifts, but also on how strongly the unresolved sources trace the matter distribution. 2026-09-18 18 September 2026 Gamma rays Dark matter #### ★★★ 3. On the Clustering Bias of Unresolved Gamma-Ray Sources\n\n**Bhashin A. Thakore, Marco Regis, Michela Negro, Stefano Camera, Daniel Gruen, Nicolao Fornengo, Aaron J. Roodman**  \n[arXiv:2609.19259](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.19259)\n\nCross-correlations between the unresolved gamma-ray background and weak gravitational lensing depend not only on source luminosities and redshifts, but also on how strongly the unresolved sources trace the matter distribution.\n\nThe authors connect gamma-ray luminosity to host-halo mass and calculate the effective redshift-dependent linear bias. For a reference unresolved-blazar population, the cross-correlation is dominated around \\(z\\simeq1\\) by sources with\n\n\\[\n\\langle b\\rangle\\gtrsim2,\n\\qquad\n\\langle M_{\\rm halo}\\rangle\\sim10^{13}M_\\odot.\n\\]\n\nReplacing the blazar population with a misaligned-AGN model gives similar effective bias values. The inferred clustering is therefore comparatively insensitive to the detailed gamma-ray source prescription.\n\nThis is relevant when using UGRB–large-scale-structure correlations to constrain unresolved astrophysical populations or exotic components such as annihilating dark matter: treating the source bias too simplistically can shift the inferred component amplitudes.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-20787", "arxiv": "2609.20787", "title": "A systematic search for long GRBs without supernovae: global properties and rate of events", "authors": "Yu-Han Yang, Eleonora Troja, Nicolas Monsalves, Massine El Kabir, Narjes Shahamat Dehsorkh, Hira Waseem", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Using two decades of Swift data, the authors systematically search for nearby long-duration GRBs lacking an accompanying bright supernova.", "path": "2026/09/18/index.html#paper-2609-20787", "reportDate": "18 September 2026", "searchText": "A systematic search for long GRBs without supernovae: global properties and rate of events Yu-Han Yang, Eleonora Troja, Nicolas Monsalves, Massine El Kabir, Narjes Shahamat Dehsorkh, Hira Waseem 2609.20787 Using two decades of Swift data, the authors systematically search for nearby long-duration GRBs lacking an accompanying bright supernova. 2026-09-18 18 September 2026 Multimessenger #### ★★★ 4. A systematic search for long GRBs without supernovae: global properties and rate of events\n\n**Yu-Han Yang, Eleonora Troja, Nicolas Monsalves, Massine El Kabir, Narjes Shahamat Dehsorkh, Hira Waseem**  \n[arXiv:2609.20787](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.20787)\n\nUsing two decades of Swift data, the authors systematically search for nearby,\n\n\\[\nz\\lesssim0.35,\n\\]\n\nlong-duration GRBs lacking an accompanying bright supernova. The selected population is heterogeneous: some events lie in star-forming or dusty environments, while others are associated with relatively quiescent galaxies.\n\nRequiring meaningful distance information and sufficiently deep supernova limits more than doubles the previous sample of credible SN-less long-GRB candidates. The inferred unbeamed volumetric rate is\n\n\\[\nR=0.5\\pm0.2\\ {\\rm Gpc^{-3}\\,yr^{-1}},\n\\]\n\ncomparable to the apparent rates of short GRBs and luminous conventional long GRBs.\n\nCompact-object mergers can reproduce this rate for plausible beaming corrections, but assigning the entire population to any single merger channel already restricts the allowed parameter space. The present sample cannot establish whether SN-less long GRBs form a distinct progenitor class.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-19948", "arxiv": "2609.19948", "title": "Constraints on the Higgs boson total width from on-shell signal-background interference in H → γγ", "authors": "ATLAS Collaboration", "tags": ["BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "ATLAS uses interference between resonant Higgs production and the continuum diphoton background to constrain the Higgs total width with an on-shell measurement.", "path": "2026/09/18/index.html#paper-2609-19948", "reportDate": "18 September 2026", "searchText": "Constraints on the Higgs boson total width from on-shell signal-background interference in H → γγ ATLAS Collaboration 2609.19948 ATLAS uses interference between resonant Higgs production and the continuum diphoton background to constrain the Higgs total width with an on-shell measurement. 2026-09-18 18 September 2026 BSM #### ★★★ 5. Constraints on the Higgs boson total width from on-shell signal-background interference in \\(H\\rightarrow\\gamma\\gamma\\)\n\n**ATLAS Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2609.19948](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.19948)\n\nATLAS introduces a direct on-shell approach to the Higgs total width. The method uses interference between the resonant\n\n\\[\npp\\rightarrow H\\rightarrow\\gamma\\gamma\n\\]\n\namplitude and the continuum diphoton background. This interference slightly distorts the diphoton invariant-mass distribution in a width-dependent manner.\n\nUsing the full \\(140~{\\rm fb^{-1}}\\) Run-2 dataset, ATLAS obtains\n\n\\[\n\\Gamma_H<307~{\\rm MeV}\n\\qquad(95\\%~{\\rm CL}),\n\\]\n\ncompared with an expected limit of \\(258\\) MeV and the Standard-Model prediction\n\n\\[\n\\Gamma_H^{\\rm SM}\\simeq4.1~{\\rm MeV}.\n\\]\n\nThe numerical constraint is much weaker than indirect off-shell limits, but it does not require assumptions connecting the on-shell and off-shell Higgs couplings.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-19271", "arxiv": "2609.19271", "title": "Probing Scalar-Induced First-Order Phase Transitions with the Diffuse Supernova Neutrino Background", "authors": "Sudipta Das, Shamik Niyogi, Manibrata Sen", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The paper considers a light scalar coupled to neutrinos and thermalized inside proto-neutron stars. A scalar-driven first-order phase transition would generate two additional neutrino components:", "path": "2026/09/18/index.html#paper-2609-19271", "reportDate": "18 September 2026", "searchText": "Probing Scalar-Induced First-Order Phase Transitions with the Diffuse Supernova Neutrino Background Sudipta Das, Shamik Niyogi, Manibrata Sen 2609.19271 The paper considers a light scalar coupled to neutrinos and thermalized inside proto-neutron stars. A scalar-driven first-order phase transition would generate two additional neutrino components: 2026-09-18 18 September 2026 Neutrinos BSM #### ★★☆ 6. Probing Scalar-Induced First-Order Phase Transitions with the Diffuse Supernova Neutrino Background\n\n**Sudipta Das, Shamik Niyogi, Manibrata Sen**  \n[arXiv:2609.19271](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.19271)\n\nThe paper considers a light scalar coupled to neutrinos and thermalized inside proto-neutron stars. A scalar-driven first-order phase transition would generate two additional neutrino components:\n\n- a prompt contribution from thermally produced scalars;\n- a delayed, spectrally narrower component from coherent scalar excitations produced during the transition.\n\nAfter integrating over the cosmic core-collapse history, the authors calculate the resulting DSNB spectra for Super-Kamiokande, Hyper-Kamiokande and DUNE. The constraints depend strongly on neutrino mass ordering and absolute mass scale. Existing Super-K data already probe part of the parameter space for inverted ordering with quasi-degenerate masses.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-19922", "arxiv": "2609.19922", "title": "Investigating the influence of full-kinematics charged-current processes in binary neutron-star mergers", "authors": "Ramon Jaeger, Henrique Gieg, Tim Dietrich", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Binary-neutron-star simulations commonly calculate charged-current neutrino reactions using an elastic approximation. This work compares that treatment with NuLib rates and a full-kinematics calculation within M1 neutrino transport.", "path": "2026/09/18/index.html#paper-2609-19922", "reportDate": "18 September 2026", "searchText": "Investigating the influence of full-kinematics charged-current processes in binary neutron-star mergers Ramon Jaeger, Henrique Gieg, Tim Dietrich 2609.19922 Binary-neutron-star simulations commonly calculate charged-current neutrino reactions using an elastic approximation. This work compares that treatment with NuLib rates and a full-kinematics calculation within M1 neutrino transport. 2026-09-18 18 September 2026 Neutrinos Multimessenger #### ★★☆ 7. Investigating the influence of full-kinematics charged-current processes in binary neutron-star mergers\n\n**Ramon Jaeger, Henrique Gieg, Tim Dietrich**  \n[arXiv:2609.19922](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.19922)\n\nBinary-neutron-star simulations commonly calculate charged-current neutrino reactions using an elastic approximation. This work compares that treatment with NuLib rates and a full-kinematics calculation within M1 neutrino transport.\n\nThe elastic approximation slightly overestimates dynamical ejecta and early neutrino luminosities while underproducing high-electron-fraction ejecta. However, NuLib and full-kinematics simulations agree within current numerical uncertainties. Their post-merger gravitational-wave spectra are indistinguishable, and most nucleosynthetic yields remain consistent.\n\nThe main exception occurs around\n\n\\[\nA\\simeq90-125,\n\\]\n\nwhere the elastic approximation predicts larger abundances. Differences also appear in remnant-disk properties and could become more consequential in longer secular simulations.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-19984", "arxiv": "2609.19984", "title": "A 32-day quasi-periodic modulation in the post-peak light curve of SN 2018bsz", "authors": "Aiswarya Sankar K. et al.", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A multiband reanalysis of the nearby hydrogen-poor superluminous supernova SN 2018bsz finds phase-coherent post-maximum residual variability across ten optical and near-infrared bands.", "path": "2026/09/18/index.html#paper-2609-19984", "reportDate": "18 September 2026", "searchText": "A 32-day quasi-periodic modulation in the post-peak light curve of SN 2018bsz Aiswarya Sankar K. et al. 2609.19984 A multiband reanalysis of the nearby hydrogen-poor superluminous supernova SN 2018bsz finds phase-coherent post-maximum residual variability across ten optical and near-infrared bands. 2026-09-18 18 September 2026 Multimessenger #### ★★☆ 8. A 32-day quasi-periodic modulation in the post-peak light curve of SN 2018bsz\n\n**Aiswarya Sankar K. et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.19984](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.19984)\n\nA multiband reanalysis of the nearby hydrogen-poor superluminous supernova SN 2018bsz finds phase-coherent post-maximum residual variability across ten optical and near-infrared bands.\n\nThe joint rest-frame period is\n\n\\[\nP=31.61\\pm0.03~{\\rm days},\n\\]\n\nalthough only about three cycles are observed, so the signal is appropriately classified as quasi-periodic. Its amplitude rises toward shorter wavelengths. Tests using alternative detrending choices, comparison stars and the lunar cycle disfavor straightforward analysis or observing artifacts.\n\nPossible explanations include Lense–Thirring precession of a central engine, interaction with circumstellar material structured before the explosion, or interaction with a surviving binary companion. Current data do not discriminate among them.\n\n---\n\nAn additional particle-phenomenology study is **“SHiP as a (post-)discovery machine: identifying the diphoton signals’ origin”** by Climescu, Mikkelsen and Ovchynnikov. It finds that as few as **2–4 reconstructed diphoton decays** could distinguish pure hypercharge-coupled from pure \\(SU(2)_L\\)-coupled ALPs for masses between approximately \\(0.2\\) and \\(1\\) GeV. [arXiv:2609.19269](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.19269)"}, {"id": "paper-2609-18383", "arxiv": "2609.18383", "title": "On the importance of cosmic-ray background in the Atomki anomaly", "authors": "Hicham Benmansour et al.", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The authors construct detailed Geant4 models of the five- and six-arm \\(e^+e^-\\) spectrometers used in the Atomki searches for the proposed \\(17\\) MeV “X17” boson.", "path": "2026/09/17/index.html#paper-2609-18383", "reportDate": "17 September 2026", "searchText": "On the importance of cosmic-ray background in the Atomki anomaly Hicham Benmansour et al. 2609.18383 The authors construct detailed Geant4 models of the five- and six-arm \\(e^+e^-\\) spectrometers used in the Atomki searches for the proposed \\(17\\) MeV “X17” boson. 2026-09-17 17 September 2026 Cosmic rays BSM #### ★★★ 1. On the importance of cosmic-ray background in the Atomki anomaly\n\n**Hicham Benmansour et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.18383](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18383)\n\nThe authors construct detailed Geant4 models of the five- and six-arm \\(e^+e^-\\) spectrometers used in the Atomki searches for the proposed \\(17\\) MeV “X17” boson.\n\nCosmic-ray muons producing two-arm coincidences generate peaks whose locations are controlled by the detectors’ discrete azimuthal geometry:\n\n\\[\n\\theta_{e^+e^-}\\sim140^\\circ\n\\]\n\nin the \\(^{8}\\mathrm{Be}\\) energy window and approximately \\(120^\\circ\\) in the \\(^{4}\\mathrm{He}\\) window. These structures resemble the reported Atomki anomalies not only in angle, but also in their energy-window and pair-asymmetry dependence.\n\nFor the five-arm apparatus, normalization to representative running conditions gives a cosmic-to-internal-pair-conversion ratio above unity in the signal region. The calculation does not prove that cosmic rays explain the anomaly, because the conclusion ultimately depends on the actual beam-off data and event-selection procedure. It does, however, establish cosmic backgrounds as a potentially leading contribution that must be demonstrated experimentally to be under control.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-18226", "arxiv": "2609.18226", "title": "Detectability of 100 TeV Gamma Rays from PeV Cosmic Rays Accelerated in the Early Phase of a Supernova Shock Wave", "authors": "Tomotaka Nishikawa, Tsuyoshi Inoue, Alexandre Marcowith", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Ordinary supernova remnants hundreds of years old often appear unable to accelerate particles to the cosmic-ray knee. This work instead examines the first tens of days after a Type II-P explosion, when the shock crosses a dense…", "path": "2026/09/17/index.html#paper-2609-18226", "reportDate": "17 September 2026", "searchText": "Detectability of 100 TeV Gamma Rays from PeV Cosmic Rays Accelerated in the Early Phase of a Supernova Shock Wave Tomotaka Nishikawa, Tsuyoshi Inoue, Alexandre Marcowith 2609.18226 Ordinary supernova remnants hundreds of years old often appear unable to accelerate particles to the cosmic-ray knee. This work instead examines the first tens of days after a Type II-P explosion, when the shock crosses a dense… 2026-09-17 17 September 2026 Cosmic rays Gamma rays Multimessenger #### ★★★ 2. Detectability of 100 TeV Gamma Rays from PeV Cosmic Rays Accelerated in the Early Phase of a Supernova Shock Wave\n\n**Tomotaka Nishikawa, Tsuyoshi Inoue, Alexandre Marcowith**  \n[arXiv:2609.18226](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18226)\n\nOrdinary supernova remnants hundreds of years old often appear unable to accelerate particles to the cosmic-ray knee. This work instead examines the first tens of days after a Type II-P explosion, when the shock crosses a dense red-supergiant wind.\n\nUsing cosmic-ray distributions from kinetic-MHD simulations that reach proton energies near \\(3\\) PeV, the authors calculate the time-dependent hadronic gamma-ray signal, including \\(\\gamma\\gamma\\) attenuation on the supernova photospheric radiation field and diffuse cosmic backgrounds.\n\nThe photospheric field suppresses the intrinsic \\(1\\!-\\!100\\) TeV flux by roughly one order of magnitude, but does not erase it. For\n\n\\[\n\\dot M_{\\rm RSG}\\sim10^{-3}\\,M_\\odot\\,{\\rm yr}^{-1},\n\\qquad\nR_{\\rm CSM}\\sim3\\times10^{15}\\ {\\rm cm},\n\\]\n\nthey estimate that a 50-hour CTA observation could detect emission around \\(100\\) TeV from distances up to approximately \\(4\\) Mpc. Reducing the circumstellar extent to \\(2\\times10^{15}\\) cm lowers the horizon to roughly \\(3\\) Mpc.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-18247", "arxiv": "2609.18247", "title": "Probing Feebly Interacting Particles with 511 keV Line from Circumstellar Medium of Supernovae", "authors": "Yu Cheng, Chui-Fan Kong, Yen-Hsun Lin, Meng-Ru Wu, Seokhoon Yun", "tags": ["BSM", "Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This paper proposes a new electromagnetic precursor of core-collapse supernovae. Feebly interacting particles produced in the core can escape the star and decay as", "path": "2026/09/17/index.html#paper-2609-18247", "reportDate": "17 September 2026", "searchText": "Probing Feebly Interacting Particles with 511 keV Line from Circumstellar Medium of Supernovae Yu Cheng, Chui-Fan Kong, Yen-Hsun Lin, Meng-Ru Wu, Seokhoon Yun 2609.18247 This paper proposes a new electromagnetic precursor of core-collapse supernovae. Feebly interacting particles produced in the core can escape the star and decay as 2026-09-17 17 September 2026 BSM Gamma rays Multimessenger #### ★★★ 3. Probing Feebly Interacting Particles with 511 keV Line from Circumstellar Medium of Supernovae\n\n**Yu Cheng, Chui-Fan Kong, Yen-Hsun Lin, Meng-Ru Wu, Seokhoon Yun**  \n[arXiv:2609.18247](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18247)\n\nThis paper proposes a new electromagnetic precursor of core-collapse supernovae. Feebly interacting particles produced in the core can escape the star and decay as\n\n\\[\nX\\rightarrow e^+e^- .\n\\]\n\nIf the decay occurs in the circumstellar medium, the positrons slow down and annihilate before the outgoing shock arrives, generating a transient \\(511\\) keV line.\n\nUsing MeV-scale dark photons as a benchmark and circumstellar profiles motivated by SN 2023ixf and SN 2024ggi, the authors find that a Galactic explosion at \\(10\\) kpc could allow COSI—and a future AMEGO-like mission—to probe parameter space beyond existing supernova limits.\n\nThe same deposited energy can heat the circumstellar gas and destroy dust, producing a rapid infrared-to-optical/UV transition. A coincident \\(511\\) keV line and dust-sublimation signature would therefore be substantially harder to mimic than either signal alone.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-17705", "arxiv": "2609.17705", "title": "Assessment of Super- and Hyper-Kamiokande Sensitivity to the DSNB", "authors": "Pablo Blanco-Mas, André de Gouvêa, Iván Martínez-Soler", "tags": ["Neutrinos"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The authors reconstruct the current Super-Kamiokande limits on the diffuse supernova neutrino background and forecast ten-year sensitivities for Super-Kamiokande and Hyper-Kamiokande.", "path": "2026/09/17/index.html#paper-2609-17705", "reportDate": "17 September 2026", "searchText": "Assessment of Super- and Hyper-Kamiokande Sensitivity to the DSNB Pablo Blanco-Mas, André de Gouvêa, Iván Martínez-Soler 2609.17705 The authors reconstruct the current Super-Kamiokande limits on the diffuse supernova neutrino background and forecast ten-year sensitivities for Super-Kamiokande and Hyper-Kamiokande. 2026-09-17 17 September 2026 Neutrinos #### ★★★ 4. Assessment of Super- and Hyper-Kamiokande Sensitivity to the DSNB\n\n**Pablo Blanco-Mas, André de Gouvêa, Iván Martínez-Soler**  \n[arXiv:2609.17705](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17705)\n\nThe authors reconstruct the current Super-Kamiokande limits on the diffuse supernova neutrino background and forecast ten-year sensitivities for Super-Kamiokande and Hyper-Kamiokande.\n\nThe technically important component is their treatment of atmospheric-neutrino backgrounds. Oscillations and propagation modify the flavor and energy distributions entering the DSNB window; approximating these backgrounds too crudely can bias both the discovery significance and the reconstructed DSNB flux.\n\nTheir conclusion is that additional Super-K exposure produces only modest sensitivity gains, whereas Hyper-K has the potential for a statistically significant DSNB detection after approximately ten years.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-18353", "arxiv": "2609.18353", "title": "The unidentified PeV source LHAASO J2108+5157: A microquasar remnant?", "authors": "Josep Martí et al.", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Gamma rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "LHAASO J2108+5157 emits up to hundreds of TeV but has no obvious conventional counterpart. Its compact GeV emission and hard TeV–PeV component are separated by stringent intermediate-energy limits from VERITAS and LST-1.", "path": "2026/09/17/index.html#paper-2609-18353", "reportDate": "17 September 2026", "searchText": "The unidentified PeV source LHAASO J2108+5157: A microquasar remnant? Josep Martí et al. 2609.18353 LHAASO J2108+5157 emits up to hundreds of TeV but has no obvious conventional counterpart. Its compact GeV emission and hard TeV–PeV component are separated by stringent intermediate-energy limits from VERITAS and LST-1. 2026-09-17 17 September 2026 Cosmic rays Gamma rays #### ★★★ 5. The unidentified PeV source LHAASO J2108+5157: A microquasar remnant?\n\n**Josep Martí et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.18353](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18353)\n\nLHAASO J2108+5157 emits up to hundreds of TeV but has no obvious conventional counterpart. Its compact GeV emission and hard TeV–PeV component are separated by stringent intermediate-energy limits from VERITAS and LST-1.\n\nThe proposed explanation is a past microquasar outburst injecting one population of relativistic protons. Energy-dependent diffusion then separates that population:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{lower-energy protons}&\\rightarrow\n\\text{remain near dense gas}\\rightarrow\\text{GeV emission},\\\\\n\\text{higher-energy protons}&\\rightarrow\n\\text{reach more distant gas}\\rightarrow\\text{TeV--PeV emission}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe same transport model accounts for the compact–extended morphological difference and the absence of a bright present-day radio or X-ray source.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-17665", "arxiv": "2609.17665", "title": "Unifying spatial correlation with hierarchical population inference to constrain the AGN channel for binary black hole mergers", "authors": "Lucas Pouw, Lorenzo Speri, Niccolò Veronesi, Elena Maria Rossi", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Previous tests of binary-black-hole formation in AGN disks generally used either correlations between GW localizations and AGN catalogues or the inferred masses and spins of the binaries. This paper combines both sources of information…", "path": "2026/09/17/index.html#paper-2609-17665", "reportDate": "17 September 2026", "searchText": "Unifying spatial correlation with hierarchical population inference to constrain the AGN channel for binary black hole mergers Lucas Pouw, Lorenzo Speri, Niccolò Veronesi, Elena Maria Rossi 2609.17665 Previous tests of binary-black-hole formation in AGN disks generally used either correlations between GW localizations and AGN catalogues or the inferred masses and spins of the binaries. This paper combines both sources of information… 2026-09-17 17 September 2026 Multimessenger #### ★★★ 6. Unifying spatial correlation with hierarchical population inference to constrain the AGN channel for binary black hole mergers\n\n**Lucas Pouw, Lorenzo Speri, Niccolò Veronesi, Elena Maria Rossi**  \n[arXiv:2609.17665](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17665)\n\nPrevious tests of binary-black-hole formation in AGN disks generally used either correlations between GW localizations and AGN catalogues or the inferred masses and spins of the binaries. This paper combines both sources of information in one hierarchical Bayesian likelihood.\n\nApplied to GWTC-5.0 and the Quaia AGN catalogue, the analysis does not exclude an AGN contribution. For unobscured AGN above three luminosity thresholds,\n\n\\[\nL_{\\rm bol}\\geq\n\\{10^{44.5},10^{45.5},10^{46.5}\\}\\ {\\rm erg\\,s^{-1}},\n\\]\n\nthe corresponding 95% credible upper limits on the AGN-hosted merger fraction are approximately\n\n\\[\nf_{\\rm AGN}<\\{0.52,0.41,0.61\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe current bounds are therefore moderate, but the joint framework should become considerably more informative as GW localization, population statistics and AGN catalogues improve.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-17661", "arxiv": "2609.17661", "title": "1D PIC Simulations of Resonant Scattering-Driven Pair Cascades in Magnetar Magnetospheres", "authors": "Jens F. Mahlmann et al.", "tags": ["Gamma rays"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "These particle-in-cell simulations model resonant inverse-Compton scattering, pair creation, particle acceleration and radiative drag self-consistently along twisted magnetar field lines.", "path": "2026/09/17/index.html#paper-2609-17661", "reportDate": "17 September 2026", "searchText": "1D PIC Simulations of Resonant Scattering-Driven Pair Cascades in Magnetar Magnetospheres Jens F. Mahlmann et al. 2609.17661 These particle-in-cell simulations model resonant inverse-Compton scattering, pair creation, particle acceleration and radiative drag self-consistently along twisted magnetar field lines. 2026-09-17 17 September 2026 Gamma rays #### ★★☆ 7. 1D PIC Simulations of Resonant Scattering-Driven Pair Cascades in Magnetar Magnetospheres\n\n**Jens F. Mahlmann et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.17661](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17661)\n\nThese particle-in-cell simulations model resonant inverse-Compton scattering, pair creation, particle acceleration and radiative drag self-consistently along twisted magnetar field lines.\n\nDespite strong drag opposing both electron and positron flows, the circuit reorganizes into a single accelerating gap near the stellar surface in one hemisphere. The resulting outflow becomes heavily pair-loaded, while ions extracted from the surface help transfer momentum across the equatorial region through streaming instabilities.\n\nA notable prediction is that confinement of the gap to one hemisphere should produce asymmetric hard-X-ray emission that may be visible in phase-resolved spectra.\n\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-17672", "arxiv": "2609.17672", "title": "WIMPs with Enhanced Annihilation from a Feebly Interacting Unstable Partner", "authors": "Chance Hoskinson, Pearl Sandick, Barmak Shams Es Haghi", "tags": ["Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The authors extend thermal WIMP cosmology by adding a heavier, feebly interacting state populated through inverse decays while WIMPs remain in equilibrium.", "path": "2026/09/17/index.html#paper-2609-17672", "reportDate": "17 September 2026", "searchText": "WIMPs with Enhanced Annihilation from a Feebly Interacting Unstable Partner Chance Hoskinson, Pearl Sandick, Barmak Shams Es Haghi 2609.17672 The authors extend thermal WIMP cosmology by adding a heavier, feebly interacting state populated through inverse decays while WIMPs remain in equilibrium. 2026-09-17 17 September 2026 Dark matter BSM #### ★★☆ 8. WIMPs with Enhanced Annihilation from a Feebly Interacting Unstable Partner\n\n**Chance Hoskinson, Pearl Sandick, Barmak Shams Es Haghi**  \n[arXiv:2609.17672](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17672)\n\nThe authors extend thermal WIMP cosmology by adding a heavier, feebly interacting state populated through inverse decays while WIMPs remain in equilibrium.\n\nIf the partner decays after WIMP freeze-out, it injects a nonthermal WIMP population. Obtaining the observed relic density then requires re-annihilation and an annihilation cross section potentially up to\n\n\\[\n\\langle\\sigma v\\rangle\\sim10^3\n\\langle\\sigma v\\rangle_{\\rm canonical}.\n\\]\n\nThis creates a relic-density mechanism that remains WIMP-like but predicts much stronger present-day indirect-detection signals. A light-higgsino realization is exhibited in the NMSSM.\n\n\n---\n\nThe paper on lensing-induced neutrino decoherence, [arXiv:2609.18202](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18202), presents a novel idea, but its observational utility depends critically on whether the relevant astrophysical neutrino wave packets retain sufficient coherence before encountering the proposed stochastic lensing structure."}, {"id": "paper-2609-17275", "arxiv": "2609.17275", "title": "A fast radio burst from a non-targeted low-frequency survey", "authors": "Cristian Di Pietrantonio et al.", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Using an imaging-based search of Murchison Widefield Array observations, the authors report the first blind—or non-targeted—FRB detection below 300 MHz. The only previously confirmed FRB at such frequencies came from targeted monitoring…", "path": "2026/09/16/index.html#paper-2609-17275", "reportDate": "16 September 2026", "searchText": "A fast radio burst from a non-targeted low-frequency survey Cristian Di Pietrantonio et al. 2609.17275 Using an imaging-based search of Murchison Widefield Array observations, the authors report the first blind—or non-targeted—FRB detection below 300 MHz. The only previously confirmed FRB at such frequencies came from targeted monitoring… 2026-09-16 16 September 2026 Multimessenger #### ★★★ 1. A fast radio burst from a non-targeted low-frequency survey\n\n**Cristian Di Pietrantonio et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.17275](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17275)\n\nUsing an imaging-based search of Murchison Widefield Array observations, the authors report the **first blind—or non-targeted—FRB detection below 300 MHz**. The only previously confirmed FRB at such frequencies came from targeted monitoring of the already-known repeater FRB 20180916B.\n\nThis matters physically because plasma scattering and free–free absorption should become much stronger toward low frequencies. The inferred event rate is nevertheless compatible with rates measured above 400 MHz, disfavoring explanations in which severe low-frequency suppression is generic across the FRB population. It also demonstrates that wide-field imaging searches can handle the tens-of-seconds dispersion delays that make conventional low-frequency transient searches computationally difficult.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-17315", "arxiv": "2609.17315", "title": "Caught by its own light: Wimpzillas, the LHAASO knee and the diffuse γ-ray sky", "authors": "D. Akl, A. J. Iovino, D. Kantzas, A. V. Macciò", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Gamma rays", "Dark matter"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The authors test whether decays of superheavy dark matter in the Galactic halo could explain both the proton hardening at approximately", "path": "2026/09/16/index.html#paper-2609-17315", "reportDate": "16 September 2026", "searchText": "Caught by its own light: Wimpzillas, the LHAASO knee and the diffuse γ-ray sky D. Akl, A. J. Iovino, D. Kantzas, A. V. Macciò 2609.17315 The authors test whether decays of superheavy dark matter in the Galactic halo could explain both the proton hardening at approximately 2026-09-16 16 September 2026 Cosmic rays Gamma rays Dark matter #### ★★★ 2. Caught by its own light: Wimpzillas, the LHAASO knee and the diffuse γ-ray sky\n\n**D. Akl, A. J. Iovino, D. Kantzas, A. V. Macciò**  \n[arXiv:2609.17315](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17315)\n\nThe authors test whether decays of superheavy dark matter in the Galactic halo could explain both the proton hardening at approximately\n\n\\[\nE\\simeq0.34~{\\rm PeV}\n\\]\n\nand the knee near\n\n\\[\nE\\simeq3.3~{\\rm PeV}\n\\]\n\nreported by LHAASO. A decaying relic with\n\n\\[\nm_\\chi\\simeq5\\times10^7~{\\rm GeV}\n\\]\n\nprovides a good fit to the proton spectrum across all three hadronic-interaction-model reconstructions considered.\n\nThe same hadronic cascade inevitably produces photons, however. Those photons exceed the measured cosmic-ray photon fraction by roughly **two orders of magnitude** and the LHAASO diffuse Galactic gamma-ray emission by about **one order of magnitude**.\n\nThe result is a particularly clean example of a multimessenger consistency test:\n\n\\[\n\\text{fit to CR nucleons}\n\\quad\\not\\Rightarrow\\quad\n\\text{viable model once }\\gamma\\text{-rays are included}.\n\\]\n\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-16425", "arxiv": "2609.16425", "title": "Right Energy, Wrong Profile: Why the 43 GeV Cluster Line Is Unlikely to Be Dark Matter", "authors": "Stefano Profumo", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Dark matter"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This reanalysis revisits the reported narrow Fermi-LAT feature around 43 GeV from the Virgo, Fornax and Ophiuchus clusters.", "path": "2026/09/16/index.html#paper-2609-16425", "reportDate": "16 September 2026", "searchText": "Right Energy, Wrong Profile: Why the 43 GeV Cluster Line Is Unlikely to Be Dark Matter Stefano Profumo 2609.16425 This reanalysis revisits the reported narrow Fermi-LAT feature around 43 GeV from the Virgo, Fornax and Ophiuchus clusters. 2026-09-16 16 September 2026 Gamma rays Dark matter #### ★★★ 3. Right Energy, Wrong Profile: Why the 43 GeV Cluster Line Is Unlikely to Be Dark Matter\n\n**Stefano Profumo**  \n[arXiv:2609.16425](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16425)\n\nThis reanalysis revisits the reported narrow Fermi-LAT feature around 43 GeV from the Virgo, Fornax and Ophiuchus clusters.\n\nThe excess remains present under several instrumental and data-splitting checks, but its inferred morphology is unexpectedly broad: uniform surface brightness or strongly substructure-enhanced emission is preferred over a smooth NFW annihilation template or central point source. Accounting for the restricted energy and morphology scan gives a conditional global significance of only about\n\n\\[\n2.7\\sigma,\n\\]\n\nwith the maximum near \\(44.5\\) GeV. Because the three-cluster sample had already been selected, this is not an unconditional discovery significance.\n\nA dark-matter interpretation would require an extreme subhalo boost and a large \\(\\gamma\\gamma\\) branching fraction, leading to an annihilation cross section already in tension with stronger Galactic-halo line searches. The favored explanation is therefore a statistical fluctuation, possibly assisted by diffuse-background or unresolved-source mismodelling.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-16529", "arxiv": "2609.16529", "title": "Dark Matter Inelastic Scattering with Nuclei for Direct Detection", "authors": "Shao-Feng Ge, Oleg Titov, Yakun Wang", "tags": ["Dark matter"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The paper calculates elastic and inelastic WIMP response functions for 129Xe and 131Xe using relativistic configuration-interaction density-functional theory rather than the nuclear shell model normally adopted in xenon analyses.", "path": "2026/09/16/index.html#paper-2609-16529", "reportDate": "16 September 2026", "searchText": "Dark Matter Inelastic Scattering with Nuclei for Direct Detection Shao-Feng Ge, Oleg Titov, Yakun Wang 2609.16529 The paper calculates elastic and inelastic WIMP response functions for 129Xe and 131Xe using relativistic configuration-interaction density-functional theory rather than the nuclear shell model normally adopted in xenon analyses. 2026-09-16 16 September 2026 Dark matter #### ★★★ 4. Dark Matter Inelastic Scattering with Nuclei for Direct Detection\n\n**Shao-Feng Ge, Oleg Titov, Yakun Wang**  \n[arXiv:2609.16529](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16529)\n\nThe paper calculates elastic and inelastic WIMP response functions for \\(^{129}\\mathrm{Xe}\\) and \\(^{131}\\mathrm{Xe}\\) using relativistic configuration-interaction density-functional theory rather than the nuclear shell model normally adopted in xenon analyses.\n\nAfter performing the multipole expansion and imposing angular-momentum, parity and time-reversal selection rules, the authors find that nuclear excitation cannot always be treated as a small correction. Depending on the effective DM operator:\n\n- the inelastic rate can be comparable to the elastic rate;\n- for some operators it can dominate by as much as three orders of magnitude;\n- transitions to higher excited states can be as important as the first excited state.\n\nThe resulting response functions differ significantly from shell-model calculations in some channels, potentially changing the interpretation of xenon direct-detection limits and high-energy recoil events.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-16139", "arxiv": "2609.16139", "title": "Multiple-Mediator-Affected Ultra-High-Energy Neutrino Attenuation: Hints for 5D U(1)Lμ−Lτ in IceCube", "authors": "Atri Bhattacharya, Ayushi Kaushik, Kenji Nishiwaki", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The authors attempt to reproduce the softening of the IceCube spectrum above tens of TeV through scattering of astrophysical neutrinos on the cosmic neutrino background.", "path": "2026/09/16/index.html#paper-2609-16139", "reportDate": "16 September 2026", "searchText": "Multiple-Mediator-Affected Ultra-High-Energy Neutrino Attenuation: Hints for 5D U(1)Lμ−Lτ in IceCube Atri Bhattacharya, Ayushi Kaushik, Kenji Nishiwaki 2609.16139 The authors attempt to reproduce the softening of the IceCube spectrum above tens of TeV through scattering of astrophysical neutrinos on the cosmic neutrino background. 2026-09-16 16 September 2026 Neutrinos BSM #### ★★☆ 5. Multiple-Mediator-Affected Ultra-High-Energy Neutrino Attenuation: Hints for 5D \\(U(1)_{L_\\mu-L_\\tau}\\) in IceCube\n\n**Atri Bhattacharya, Ayushi Kaushik, Kenji Nishiwaki**  \n[arXiv:2609.16139](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16139)\n\nThe authors attempt to reproduce the softening of the IceCube spectrum above tens of TeV through scattering of astrophysical neutrinos on the cosmic neutrino background.\n\nInstead of one \\(L_\\mu-L_\\tau\\) mediator, a compact fifth dimension generates a Kaluza–Klein tower. This produces multiple nearby \\(s\\)-channel resonances, supplemented by \\(t\\)- and \\(u\\)-channel scattering. Repeated interactions can consequently create broad, energy-dependent attenuation rather than the isolated absorption dip typical of a single mediator.\n\nThe mechanism can soften an injected single power law, but its interpretation is highly model-dependent: it depends on the compactification scale, coupling, mediator spectrum, neutrino masses and assumed unattenuated source population. “Hints” in the title should therefore not be read as statistical evidence for an extra dimension.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-16547", "arxiv": "2609.16547", "title": "Probing multi-state dark matter via optical absorption lines in DESI spectra", "authors": "Anoma Ganguly, Shadab Alam", "tags": ["Dark matter"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "For two-state dark matter with a magnetic-dipole transition, halo DM can resonantly absorb photons with", "path": "2026/09/16/index.html#paper-2609-16547", "reportDate": "16 September 2026", "searchText": "Probing multi-state dark matter via optical absorption lines in DESI spectra Anoma Ganguly, Shadab Alam 2609.16547 For two-state dark matter with a magnetic-dipole transition, halo DM can resonantly absorb photons with 2026-09-16 16 September 2026 Dark matter #### ★★☆ 6. Probing multi-state dark matter via optical absorption lines in DESI spectra\n\n**Anoma Ganguly, Shadab Alam**  \n[arXiv:2609.16547](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16547)\n\nFor two-state dark matter with a magnetic-dipole transition, halo DM can resonantly absorb photons with\n\n\\[\nE_\\gamma=\\Delta E,\n\\]\n\nproducing a narrow line in the spectra of background galaxies or quasars.\n\nThe authors stack **145,087 DESI Early Data Release spectra** whose sightlines pass through foreground GAMA galaxy groups. No absorption line is found, and they derive \\(2\\sigma\\) limits on the transition cross section.\n\nThe strongest sensitivity occurs for splittings around \\(3\\!-\\!5\\) eV and DM masses of a few MeV, including parameter space poorly covered by direct detection and conventional BBN/CMB limits. The full DESI sample could improve the sensitivity by more than an order of magnitude.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-16826", "arxiv": "2609.16826", "title": "Testing a Pre-Supernova Contribution to 44Ti in Cassiopeia A", "authors": "Toshiki Sato et al.", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The usual interpretation treats the 44Ti mapped by NuSTAR in Cas A as a tracer of asymmetric explosive burning. This paper tests whether a significant fraction could instead have been synthesized before core collapse in an oxygen–carbon…", "path": "2026/09/16/index.html#paper-2609-16826", "reportDate": "16 September 2026", "searchText": "Testing a Pre-Supernova Contribution to 44Ti in Cassiopeia A Toshiki Sato et al. 2609.16826 The usual interpretation treats the 44Ti mapped by NuSTAR in Cas A as a tracer of asymmetric explosive burning. This paper tests whether a significant fraction could instead have been synthesized before core collapse in an oxygen–carbon… 2026-09-16 16 September 2026 Multimessenger #### ★★☆ 7. Testing a Pre-Supernova Contribution to \\(^{44}\\)Ti in Cassiopeia A\n\n**Toshiki Sato et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.16826](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16826)\n\nThe usual interpretation treats the \\(^{44}\\mathrm{Ti}\\) mapped by NuSTAR in Cas A as a tracer of asymmetric explosive burning. This paper tests whether a significant fraction could instead have been synthesized before core collapse in an oxygen–carbon shell merger.\n\nThe low- and intermediate-velocity \\(^{44}\\mathrm{Ti}\\) components correlate more strongly with oxygen-layer tracers than with shocked iron-rich material. If that association is pre-supernova in origin, it could account for as much as\n\n\\[\nM_{^{44}\\mathrm{Ti}}\\sim7\\times10^{-5}M_\\odot,\n\\]\n\nroughly half the observed inventory. Producing this amount requires stronger ingestion and convection than standard one-dimensional stellar-evolution models predict. XRISM measurements of stable Fe-group abundance ratios provide an independent restriction on how much \\(^{44}\\mathrm{Ti}\\) can come from explosive burning.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-17507", "arxiv": "2609.17507", "title": "Constraints on the central engine of merger-driven long gamma-ray bursts", "authors": "Muskan Yadav et al.", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The paper examines the emerging class of long-duration GRBs apparently produced by compact-object mergers rather than massive-star collapse.", "path": "2026/09/16/index.html#paper-2609-17507", "reportDate": "16 September 2026", "searchText": "Constraints on the central engine of merger-driven long gamma-ray bursts Muskan Yadav et al. 2609.17507 The paper examines the emerging class of long-duration GRBs apparently produced by compact-object mergers rather than massive-star collapse. 2026-09-16 16 September 2026 Gamma rays Multimessenger #### ★★☆ 8. Constraints on the central engine of merger-driven long gamma-ray bursts\n\n**Muskan Yadav et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.17507](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17507)\n\nThe paper examines the emerging class of long-duration GRBs apparently produced by compact-object mergers rather than massive-star collapse.\n\nATCA observations of seven nearby candidates at \\(2.1\\) GHz, taken \\(520\\!-\\!6900\\) days after the bursts, yield no late-time counterparts, with \\(3\\sigma\\) limits of \\(33\\!-\\!72~\\mu{\\rm Jy}\\). These limits do not yet exclude energetic magnetar-driven ejecta when the circumburst density is low.\n\nCombining the radio nondetections with fallback-powered prompt-emission modelling sharply restricts the allowed magnetar spin, magnetic field and jet baryon loading. A magnetar engine remains possible, but only in a comparatively narrow region of parameter space.\n\n---\n\nThe main neutrino item is the extra-dimensional attenuation model above; it is theoretical and exploratory."}, {"id": "paper-2609-14955", "arxiv": "2609.14955", "title": "Measurement of Cosmic-Ray Density in the Spiral Arms toward Galactic Anticenter", "authors": "Jia-hao Liu, Bing Liu, Rui-zhi Yang", "tags": ["Cosmic rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Using almost 17 years of Fermi-LAT data, they isolate diffuse gamma-ray emission associated with the Local, Perseus and Outer spiral arms over 105∘<l<145∘. All three components show a clear pion bump, so the inferred emissivity is…", "path": "2026/09/15/index.html#paper-2609-14955", "reportDate": "15 September 2026", "searchText": "Measurement of Cosmic-Ray Density in the Spiral Arms toward Galactic Anticenter Jia-hao Liu, Bing Liu, Rui-zhi Yang 2609.14955 Using almost 17 years of Fermi-LAT data, they isolate diffuse gamma-ray emission associated with the Local, Perseus and Outer spiral arms over 105∘<l<145∘. All three components show a clear pion bump, so the inferred emissivity is… 2026-09-15 15 September 2026 Cosmic rays #### ★★★ Measurement of Cosmic-Ray Density in the Spiral Arms toward Galactic Anticenter\n\n**Jia-hao Liu, Bing Liu, Rui-zhi Yang**\n[arXiv:2609.14955](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.14955)\n\nUsing almost **17 years of Fermi-LAT data**, they isolate diffuse gamma-ray emission associated with the Local, Perseus and Outer spiral arms over \\(105^\\circ<l<145^\\circ\\). All three components show a clear **pion bump**, so the inferred emissivity is directly tracing the underlying CR-proton population rather than relying on a purely phenomenological gamma-ray spectrum.\n\nThey obtain proton spectral indices approximately\n\\[\n\\Gamma_p\\simeq\n2.75\\quad{\\rm Local},\\qquad\n2.55\\quad{\\rm Perseus},\\qquad\n2.80\\quad{\\rm Outer},\n\\]\nwith CR energy densities above \\(10\\) GeV of order\n\\[\nu_{\\rm CR}\\sim0.2~{\\rm eV\\,cm^{-3}}\n\\]\nin all three arms. The important tension is that the outer-Galaxy CR population comes out **denser and harder than standard propagation models usually predict** as Galactocentric radius increases. Possible explanations include a larger CR halo, slower radial decline of the magnetic field, or local acceleration—especially in the Perseus arm.\n\n**Significance:** it is a clean observational handle on the radial dependence of Galactic CR transport, with obvious implications for diffuse gamma rays and Galactic-neutrino modelling.\n\n**Classification:** **genuinely useful observational CR result.**"}, {"id": "paper-2609-13363", "arxiv": "2609.13363", "title": "The Loud Tail of the Supermassive Black Hole Binary Population: Multimessenger Candidates and Prospects for SKAO", "authors": "Juhan Raidal, Ville Vaskonen, Juan Urrutia, Hardi Veermäe", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "They start from SMBH-binary population models fitted to the NANOGrav 15-year stochastic-background result and ask which already-known electromagnetic SMBH-binary candidates should populate the rare high-strain tail strongly enough to…", "path": "2026/09/15/index.html#paper-2609-13363", "reportDate": "15 September 2026", "searchText": "The Loud Tail of the Supermassive Black Hole Binary Population: Multimessenger Candidates and Prospects for SKAO Juhan Raidal, Ville Vaskonen, Juan Urrutia, Hardi Veermäe 2609.13363 They start from SMBH-binary population models fitted to the NANOGrav 15-year stochastic-background result and ask which already-known electromagnetic SMBH-binary candidates should populate the rare high-strain tail strongly enough to… 2026-09-15 15 September 2026 Multimessenger #### ★★★ The Loud Tail of the Supermassive Black Hole Binary Population: Multimessenger Candidates and Prospects for SKAO\n\n**Juhan Raidal, Ville Vaskonen, Juan Urrutia, Hardi Veermäe**\n[arXiv:2609.13363](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.13363)\n\nThey start from SMBH-binary population models fitted to the **NANOGrav 15-year stochastic-background result** and ask which already-known electromagnetic SMBH-binary candidates should populate the rare high-strain tail strongly enough to become individually resolvable PTA sources.\n\nTwo objects stand out: **3C 66B and Mrk 501**. In their population realization these systems have a good chance of rising above the unresolved stochastic background. The result is especially interesting because individual detections would discriminate between binary-evolution scenarios: strong environmental hardening changes the abundance of resolvable systems relative to a purely GW-driven inspiral population.\n\nFor the SKA era, after including confusion noise from the unresolved SMBH population, they forecast **tens of individually detectable binaries**, with 3C 66B particularly robust.\n\n**Significance:** this is genuine **multimessenger source-population physics**—connecting radio/AGN binary candidates to PTA gravitational waves rather than treating the GW background only statistically.\n\n**Classification:** **new multimessenger population forecast; conceptually strong.**"}, {"id": "paper-2609-14446", "arxiv": "2609.14446", "title": "Probe of Solar Neutrino Magnetic Moments through Spin-Flavor Precession: Resonance Structure and Antineutrino Appearance", "authors": "Pouya Bakhti, Sudip Jana, Chui-Fan Kong, Seodong Shin, Seokhoon Yun", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Oscillations"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "They perform the spin-flavor-precession problem with the full \\[ 6\\times6 \\] neutrino/antineutrino Hamiltonian and realistic GS98 and AGSS09 solar profiles.", "path": "2026/09/15/index.html#paper-2609-14446", "reportDate": "15 September 2026", "searchText": "Probe of Solar Neutrino Magnetic Moments through Spin-Flavor Precession: Resonance Structure and Antineutrino Appearance Pouya Bakhti, Sudip Jana, Chui-Fan Kong, Seodong Shin, Seokhoon Yun 2609.14446 They perform the spin-flavor-precession problem with the full \\[ 6\\times6 \\] neutrino/antineutrino Hamiltonian and realistic GS98 and AGSS09 solar profiles. 2026-09-15 15 September 2026 Neutrinos Oscillations #### ★★★ Probe of Solar Neutrino Magnetic Moments through Spin-Flavor Precession: Resonance Structure and Antineutrino Appearance\n\n**Pouya Bakhti, Sudip Jana, Chui-Fan Kong, Seodong Shin, Seokhoon Yun**\n[arXiv:2609.14446](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.14446)\n\nThey perform the spin-flavor-precession problem with the full\n\\[\n6\\times6\n\\]\nneutrino/antineutrino Hamiltonian and realistic GS98 and AGSS09 solar profiles.\n\nFor **Majorana neutrinos**, a transition magnetic moment can generate\n\\[\n\\nu_\\alpha\\rightarrow\\bar\\nu_\\beta ,\n\\]\nwhereas for Dirac neutrinos the flipped state is sterile. A useful technical result is that in normal ordering they find **no finite-density Majorana level crossing** for \\(1\\!-\\!20\\) MeV solar neutrinos. A Dirac resonance appears above about \\(12\\) MeV, but it involves only a small \\(\\nu_e\\) component, so the resonant disappearance signal is limited.\n\nThe more interesting signature is therefore **nonresonant Majorana conversion producing solar \\(\\bar\\nu_e\\)**. For a future 3-kton Jinping detector operating for \\(5\\!-\\!10\\) years they estimate sensitivity to\n\\[\nP(\\nu_e\\rightarrow\\bar\\nu_e)\n\\sim(0.85-1.3)\\times10^{-5}.\n\\]\nAssuming optimistic transverse core fields \\(B_\\perp\\sim7\\!-\\!10\\) MG, this corresponds in their \\(\\mu_{12}\\)-only example to\n\\[\n|\\mu_{12}|\\sim(2.3-4.1)\\times10^{-13}\\mu_B .\n\\]\n\n**Significance:** it gives an explicit three-flavor treatment of the distinction between **Dirac disappearance and Majorana antineutrino appearance**, which is much more informative than a simple magnetic-moment bound.\n\n**Classification:** **good neutrino-phenomenology paper; projected rather than observed result.**"}, {"id": "paper-2609-13821", "arxiv": "2609.13821", "title": "Consistent treatment of flavour-specific neutrino self-interactions in cosmology", "authors": "Gabriela Barenboim, Marco Drewes, Isabel M. Oldengott", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Oscillations"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This paper addresses a subtlety that is often glossed over in cosmological constraints on secret neutrino interactions: if the new interaction acts only on a particular flavor, neutrino oscillations/mixing mean that one cannot simply…", "path": "2026/09/15/index.html#paper-2609-13821", "reportDate": "15 September 2026", "searchText": "Consistent treatment of flavour-specific neutrino self-interactions in cosmology Gabriela Barenboim, Marco Drewes, Isabel M. Oldengott 2609.13821 This paper addresses a subtlety that is often glossed over in cosmological constraints on secret neutrino interactions: if the new interaction acts only on a particular flavor, neutrino oscillations/mixing mean that one cannot simply… 2026-09-15 15 September 2026 Neutrinos Oscillations #### ★★★ Consistent treatment of flavour-specific neutrino self-interactions in cosmology\n\n**Gabriela Barenboim, Marco Drewes, Isabel M. Oldengott**\n[arXiv:2609.13821](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.13821)\n\nThis paper addresses a subtlety that is often glossed over in cosmological constraints on secret neutrino interactions: if the new interaction acts only on a particular **flavor**, neutrino oscillations/mixing mean that one cannot simply evolve three independent flavor fluids.\n\nStarting from the neutrino **quantum kinetic equations**, they derive the Boltzmann hierarchy in the neutrino-mass basis and determine when a simpler combined-fluid approximation is valid.\n\nTheir practical result is neat. Existing CMB limits on an effective neutrino self-coupling should be weakened by approximately\n\\[\n\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\nwhen two flavors interact and by approximately\n\\[\n\\sqrt{6}\n\\]\nwhen only one flavor interacts. The \\(\\nu_e\\)-only case is the one where the fluid approximation becomes somewhat less reliable.\n\n**Significance:** this is one of those papers that can quietly change how constraints are recast across a large class of neutrino-BSM models; the correction is not orders of magnitude, but it is conceptually important.\n\n**Classification:** **methodological improvement to cosmological neutrino constraints.**"}, {"id": "paper-2609-15970", "arxiv": "2609.15970", "title": "Less isn't more: Cosmological bounds on the neutrino masses are robust to changes in the neutrino abundance", "authors": "Sebastian J. Figueroa, Alexander C. Sobotka, Adrienne L. Erickcek", "tags": ["Neutrinos"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This asks a timely question: can the increasingly aggressive cosmological limit on \\[ \\sum m_\\nu \\] be evaded by changing the relic neutrino abundance through a nonstandard post-BBN thermal history?", "path": "2026/09/15/index.html#paper-2609-15970", "reportDate": "15 September 2026", "searchText": "Less isn't more: Cosmological bounds on the neutrino masses are robust to changes in the neutrino abundance Sebastian J. Figueroa, Alexander C. Sobotka, Adrienne L. Erickcek 2609.15970 This asks a timely question: can the increasingly aggressive cosmological limit on \\[ \\sum m_\\nu \\] be evaded by changing the relic neutrino abundance through a nonstandard post-BBN thermal history? 2026-09-15 15 September 2026 Neutrinos #### ★★★ Less isn't more: Cosmological bounds on the neutrino masses are robust to changes in the neutrino abundance\n\n**Sebastian J. Figueroa, Alexander C. Sobotka, Adrienne L. Erickcek**\n[arXiv:2609.15970](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.15970)\n\nThis asks a timely question: can the increasingly aggressive cosmological limit on\n\\[\n\\sum m_\\nu\n\\]\nbe evaded by changing the relic neutrino abundance through a nonstandard post-BBN thermal history?\n\nThey explicitly model late particle decays injecting photons and/or dark radiation, and then perform MCMC analyses using **Planck primary CMB, Planck+ACT lensing and DESI BAO**. Surprisingly, reducing the neutrino abundance does **not** substantially weaken the mass bound.\n\nFor degenerate masses they quote\n\\[\n\\sum m_\\nu <0.0691~{\\rm eV}\n\\]\nfor the standard thermal history,\n\\[\n<0.0652~{\\rm eV}\n\\]\nfor photon-only injection, and\n\\[\n<0.0710~{\\rm eV}\n\\]\nwhen dark radiation is also produced. Even allowing the neutrino/photon temperature ratio to vary phenomenologically gives only\n\\[\n\\sum m_\\nu<0.0724~{\\rm eV}\n\\]\nat 95% credibility.\n\nThe underlying reason is interesting: \\(T_\\nu\\) and \\(\\sum m_\\nu\\) come out **positively correlated** once the full cosmological dataset is used.\n\n**Significance:** the emerging tension between cosmological neutrino-mass bounds and laboratory oscillation mass splittings cannot apparently be dismissed simply by saying “perhaps the relic neutrino density is smaller.”\n\n**Classification:** **important robustness check.**"}, {"id": "paper-2609-14508", "arxiv": "2609.14508", "title": "First Light of Neutron Star Mergers: Off-axis Cocoon Cooling X-ray Emission from Short Gamma-Ray Burst Jets", "authors": "Jian-He Zheng, Wenbin Lu", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The problem is straightforward: short-GRB jets are strongly beamed, so most neutron-star mergers will not show prompt gamma rays to an off-axis observer. The authors investigate whether the jet cocoon provides a less-beamed early…", "path": "2026/09/15/index.html#paper-2609-14508", "reportDate": "15 September 2026", "searchText": "First Light of Neutron Star Mergers: Off-axis Cocoon Cooling X-ray Emission from Short Gamma-Ray Burst Jets Jian-He Zheng, Wenbin Lu 2609.14508 The problem is straightforward: short-GRB jets are strongly beamed, so most neutron-star mergers will not show prompt gamma rays to an off-axis observer. The authors investigate whether the jet cocoon provides a less-beamed early… 2026-09-15 15 September 2026 Gamma rays Multimessenger #### ★★☆ First Light of Neutron Star Mergers: Off-axis Cocoon Cooling X-ray Emission from Short Gamma-Ray Burst Jets\n\n**Jian-He Zheng, Wenbin Lu**\n[arXiv:2609.14508](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.14508)\n\nThe problem is straightforward: short-GRB jets are strongly beamed, so most neutron-star mergers will not show prompt gamma rays to an off-axis observer. The authors investigate whether the **jet cocoon** provides a less-beamed early counterpart.\n\nHydrodynamic simulations plus radiative post-processing give a mildly relativistic cocoon producing soft quasi-thermal X-rays with\n\\[\nL_X\\sim10^{46}-10^{48}\\ {\\rm erg\\,s^{-1}},\n\\qquad\nkT\\sim0.1-1~{\\rm keV},\n\\]\nlasting a few to roughly ten seconds.\n\nFor viewing angles around \\(10^\\circ\\), Einstein Probe could see the signal to \\(z\\sim0.3\\). For a GW170817-like nearby event, detectability may extend to roughly \\(45^\\circ\\). Their fiducial Einstein Probe rate is about\n\\[\n0.5~{\\rm yr}^{-1}.\n\\]\n\n**Significance:** a genuinely useful possible **early X-ray trigger for off-axis GW mergers**, preceding the kilonova and conventional afterglow.\n\n**Classification:** **multimessenger prediction, not yet observational confirmation.**"}, {"id": "paper-2609-14551", "arxiv": "2609.14551", "title": "First results on the search for the Galactic Center Excess in the sub-GeV band with the emulsion telescope in GRAINE 2023", "authors": "Yuya Nakamura et al.", "tags": ["Gamma rays"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "GRAINE is interesting because the balloon-borne emulsion gamma-ray telescope reaches angular resolutions of about \\[ 1^\\circ\\ {\\rm at}\\ 100~{\\rm MeV}, \\qquad 0.1^\\circ\\ {\\rm at}\\ 1~{\\rm GeV}, \\] making the difficult sub-GeV…", "path": "2026/09/15/index.html#paper-2609-14551", "reportDate": "15 September 2026", "searchText": "First results on the search for the Galactic Center Excess in the sub-GeV band with the emulsion telescope in GRAINE 2023 Yuya Nakamura et al. 2609.14551 GRAINE is interesting because the balloon-borne emulsion gamma-ray telescope reaches angular resolutions of about \\[ 1^\\circ\\ {\\rm at}\\ 100~{\\rm MeV}, \\qquad 0.1^\\circ\\ {\\rm at}\\ 1~{\\rm GeV}, \\] making the difficult sub-GeV… 2026-09-15 15 September 2026 Gamma rays #### ★★☆ First results on the search for the Galactic Center Excess in the sub-GeV band with the emulsion telescope in GRAINE 2023\n\n**Yuya Nakamura et al.**\n[arXiv:2609.14551](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.14551)\n\nGRAINE is interesting because the balloon-borne emulsion gamma-ray telescope reaches angular resolutions of about\n\\[\n1^\\circ\\ {\\rm at}\\ 100~{\\rm MeV},\n\\qquad\n0.1^\\circ\\ {\\rm at}\\ 1~{\\rm GeV},\n\\]\nmaking the difficult sub-GeV Galactic-center region less confusion-limited than Fermi in some respects.\n\nRather than looking at the familiar \\(\\sim2\\) GeV GCE peak, they deliberately examine\n\\[\n75-300~{\\rm MeV},\n\\]\nwhere predicted spectra from millisecond-pulsar and DM-annihilation interpretations diverge more strongly.\n\nThey see **no significant excess** and obtain a \\(2\\sigma\\) upper limit\n\\[\nE^2\\Phi_{\\gamma}<1.70\\times10^{-7}\n\\ {\\rm GeV\\,cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}\n\\]\nfor a \\(1^\\circ\\)-radius Galactic-center ROI. Current statistics still allow both the DM and MSP interpretations.\n\n**Significance:** the important part is not the reported limit itself, which is still weak, but the ability to obtain **high-angular-resolution sub-GeV imaging** precisely where the GCE models become spectrally distinguishable.\n\n**Classification:** **first instrumental result; interesting but not yet constraining enough to settle the GCE.**"}, {"id": "paper-2609-15985", "arxiv": "2609.15985", "title": "Inferring dark matter masses and interactions from high recoil energy events in LUX-ZEPLIN", "authors": "Stefano Palmisano, Michele Tammaro, Andrea Tesi", "tags": ["Dark matter"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The analysis constrains dark-matter masses and interactions compatible with the LZ high-recoil candidate with limited model assumptions.", "path": "2026/09/15/index.html#paper-2609-15985", "reportDate": "15 September 2026", "searchText": "Inferring dark matter masses and interactions from high recoil energy events in LUX-ZEPLIN Stefano Palmisano, Michele Tammaro, Andrea Tesi 2609.15985 The analysis constrains dark-matter masses and interactions compatible with the LZ high-recoil candidate with limited model assumptions. 2026-09-15 15 September 2026 Dark matter #### ★★☆ Inferring dark matter masses and interactions from high recoil energy events in LUX-ZEPLIN\n\n**Stefano Palmisano, Michele Tammaro, Andrea Tesi**\n[arXiv:2609.15985](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.15985)\n\nThere is now a flood of papers trying to model-build around the LZ \\(248\\) keV recoil candidate. This one is more useful than most because it is relatively **model-independent**.\n\nThey infer the posterior directly in the space of DM masses, mass splittings and effective nuclear interactions, retaining exact two-body kinematics. The single high-recoil event plus the lack of lower-energy events drives the solution into disconnected kinematic regimes.\n\nGeneric interactions favor a finely tuned **endothermic heavy-WIMP region**, while exothermic scattering permits masses ranging from sub-GeV to heavy DM with large splittings. Purely elastic interpretations survive only with momentum suppression and/or spin-dependent operators.\n\n**Significance:** the inference characterizes what the LZ candidate implies without committing to a specific \\(U(1)'\\), SUSY or freeze-in construction.\n\n**Classification:** **useful inference paper, but the underlying LZ anomaly is still weak; not evidence for DM.**\n\nAn additional methodological paper is **“A Unified Numerical Study of Axion Stars: From the Nonrelativistic Regime to General Relativity”** by Arabameri, Arias, Colipí-Marchant and Schiappacasse. They benchmark Schrödinger–Poisson, relativistic EFT and full Einstein–Klein–Gordon calculations against one another and show quantitatively where the usual nonrelativistic axion-star description breaks down. This is a reference and methodological study."}, {"id": "paper-2609-12055", "arxiv": "2609.12055", "title": "Time-Integrated Searches for Sub-TeV Neutrino Sources with IceCube-DeepCore", "authors": "IceCube Collaboration — R. Abbasi et al.", "tags": ["Neutrinos"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "IceCube has developed a dedicated sub-TeV point-source analysis using 11.1 years of DeepCore data, extending source searches down into an energy range that ordinary IceCube point-source analyses do not exploit efficiently. The…", "path": "2026/09/14/index.html#paper-2609-12055", "reportDate": "14 September 2026", "searchText": "Time-Integrated Searches for Sub-TeV Neutrino Sources with IceCube-DeepCore IceCube Collaboration — R. Abbasi et al. 2609.12055 IceCube has developed a dedicated sub-TeV point-source analysis using 11.1 years of DeepCore data, extending source searches down into an energy range that ordinary IceCube point-source analyses do not exploit efficiently. The… 2026-09-14 14 September 2026 Neutrinos #### ★★★ 1. **Time-Integrated Searches for Sub-TeV Neutrino Sources with IceCube-DeepCore**\n\n**IceCube Collaboration — R. Abbasi et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.12055](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12055)\n\nIceCube has developed a dedicated **sub-TeV point-source analysis using 11.1 years of DeepCore data**, extending source searches down into an energy range that ordinary IceCube point-source analyses do not exploit efficiently. The motivation is especially strong for sources with soft spectra.\n\nThey perform three searches: X-ray-bright AGN selected with Swift/BAT, including **NGC 1068**; Galactic Fermi-LAT sources whose spectra look compatible with \\(\\pi^0\\) decay; and an all-sky point-source search. They also combine individual source \\(p\\)-values with a binomial test to look for weak population-level emission. No statistically significant sub-TeV signal appears in any of them.\n\nFor NGC 1068, assuming\n\n\\[\n\\frac{d\\Phi_\\nu}{dE}\\propto E^{-3.4},\n\\]\n\nthey obtain\n\n\\[\n\\left.\\Phi_{\\nu+\\bar\\nu}\\right|_{1\\,{\\rm TeV}}\n<\n9.5\\times10^{-11}\n\\ {\\rm TeV^{-1}\\,cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}\n\\]\n\nper flavor at 90% C.L., probing approximately **30–400 GeV**. Importantly, that upper limit is still about a factor of two *above* the extrapolation of IceCube's higher-energy NGC 1068 measurement, so it does **not yet rule out** a simple continuation of the measured soft component.\n\n**Significance:** this provides an observational input for multimessenger modelling of NGC 1068. It begins constraining what the neutrino spectrum can do below the existing IceCube measurement and therefore interfaces naturally with MeV–GeV electromagnetic constraints and source-opacity/cascade models.\n\n**Assessment:** **genuinely new experimental result.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-12174", "arxiv": "2609.12174", "title": "Hidden structures in the UHE sky: unveiling potential sources via spectral signatures", "authors": "Mateus Zeferino Rennó, Cainã de Oliveira", "tags": ["Cosmic rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "An energy-weighted directional statistic searches for spectral variations in UHECR arrival maps, complementing conventional event-count anisotropy searches.", "path": "2026/09/14/index.html#paper-2609-12174", "reportDate": "14 September 2026", "searchText": "Hidden structures in the UHE sky: unveiling potential sources via spectral signatures Mateus Zeferino Rennó, Cainã de Oliveira 2609.12174 An energy-weighted directional statistic searches for spectral variations in UHECR arrival maps, complementing conventional event-count anisotropy searches. 2026-09-14 14 September 2026 Cosmic rays #### ★★★ 2. **Hidden structures in the UHE sky: unveiling potential sources via spectral signatures**\n\n**Mateus Zeferino Rennó, Cainã de Oliveira**  \n[arXiv:2609.12174](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12174)\n\nMost UHECR source searches ask whether some angular region contains **more particles than expected**,\n\n\\[\nN(\\hat n)>N_{\\rm iso}(\\hat n).\n\\]\n\nBut a real source contribution could alter the *local energy distribution* without producing an obvious flux excess, particularly after magnetic smearing.\n\nThe authors construct a directional functional \\(\\mu_E\\) of the energy spectrum specifically designed to separate\n\n\\[\n\\text{flux normalization}\n\\qquad\\text{from}\\qquad\n\\text{spectral shape}.\n\\]\n\nThey then generalize the **Li–Ma significance** formalism to such energy-weighted observables and test the statistic in Monte Carlo. Hard-spectrum patches that are essentially invisible in conventional event-count maps become detectable with \\(\\mu_E\\).\n\nThat idea is interesting because magnetic deflections can smear angular overdensities while still allowing a direction-dependent spectrum to survive.\n\n**Significance:** it is complementary to conventional UHECR anisotropy statistics and particularly relevant to the general question of how much source information survives GMF/EGMF smearing. Comparison with composition-sensitive and entropy/information-based approaches could help establish the statistic's complementary value.\n\n**Assessment:** **new methodology; potentially useful rather than yet experimentally decisive.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-12044", "arxiv": "2609.12044", "title": "Constraining dark matter using 20-year INTEGRAL/IBIS observations I: Primordial black holes", "authors": "Jordan Koechler, Pedro De la Torre Luque", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A joint spectral and morphological analysis of 20 years of INTEGRAL/IBIS data constrains primordial black holes through inverse-Compton hard X-rays.", "path": "2026/09/14/index.html#paper-2609-12044", "reportDate": "14 September 2026", "searchText": "Constraining dark matter using 20-year INTEGRAL/IBIS observations I: Primordial black holes Jordan Koechler, Pedro De la Torre Luque 2609.12044 A joint spectral and morphological analysis of 20 years of INTEGRAL/IBIS data constrains primordial black holes through inverse-Compton hard X-rays. 2026-09-14 14 September 2026 Dark matter Multimessenger #### ★★★ 3. **Constraining dark matter using 20-year INTEGRAL/IBIS observations I: Primordial black holes**\n\n**Jordan Koechler, Pedro De la Torre Luque**  \n[arXiv:2609.12044](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12044)\n\nLow-mass PBHs emit \\(e^\\pm\\) through Hawking evaporation. Those electrons and positrons subsequently inverse-Compton scatter the Galactic radiation field,\n\n\\[\ne^\\pm+\\gamma_{\\rm ISRF}\\rightarrow e^\\pm+\\gamma ,\n\\]\n\ncreating diffuse hard X-rays.\n\nThe authors use **20 years of INTEGRAL/IBIS observations** and, importantly, do not rely only on the energy spectrum. They perform a combined **spectral + morphological analysis**, modelling the conventional Galactic hard-X-ray sky with cosmic-ray-electron IC emission plus unresolved accreting white dwarfs and adding the PBH-induced IC morphology.\n\nThey obtain competitive constraints on the PBH DM fraction for monochromatic, extended and rotating-PBH mass functions. Their dominant theoretical uncertainty is not Hawking physics itself but the Galactic transport of the relatively low-energy \\(e^\\pm\\) responsible for the IC emission.\n\nThat is an important methodological point:\n\n\\[\n\\text{particle-physics injection}\n\\longrightarrow e^\\pm\\ {\\rm propagation}\n\\longrightarrow {\\rm IC}\n\\longrightarrow {\\rm diffuse\\ morphology}.\n\\]\n\n**Significance:** besides the PBH result itself, the spectral+morphological separation of signal from Galactic diffuse emission is highly relevant to any MeV/hard-X-ray multimessenger analysis.\n\n**Assessment:** **genuinely new search strategy and observational constraint.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-12821", "arxiv": "2609.12821", "title": "Supernova cooling from neutrinophilic dark matter", "authors": "Yugen Lin", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This paper considers light DM whose dominant coupling is to neutrinos and asks whether it can be produced inside a core-collapse SN through neutrino–neutrino reactions.", "path": "2026/09/14/index.html#paper-2609-12821", "reportDate": "14 September 2026", "searchText": "Supernova cooling from neutrinophilic dark matter Yugen Lin 2609.12821 This paper considers light DM whose dominant coupling is to neutrinos and asks whether it can be produced inside a core-collapse SN through neutrino–neutrino reactions. 2026-09-14 14 September 2026 Neutrinos Dark matter BSM #### ★★★ 4. **Supernova cooling from neutrinophilic dark matter**\n\n**Yugen Lin**  \n[arXiv:2609.12821](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12821)\n\nThis paper considers light DM whose dominant coupling is to neutrinos and asks whether it can be produced inside a core-collapse SN through neutrino–neutrino reactions.\n\nBecause the proto-neutron-star core contains an enormous neutrino density, even extremely weak interactions can efficiently populate a new light sector. If the produced DM escapes, it adds a new cooling channel.\n\nUsing modern supernova simulation profiles, the paper derives bounds on effective \\(\\nu\\)-DM interactions for sub-GeV DM. The striking numerical claim is that for\n\n\\[\nm_\\chi\\lesssim {\\cal O}(100)\\ {\\rm MeV},\n\\]\n\nthe SN cooling limits on the reference DM–neutrino cross section can improve existing indirect-detection limits by **more than ten orders of magnitude**.\n\nThe physics is essentially the usual SN energy-loss argument, but with the very large thermal neutrino bath acting as the production environment rather than nucleons/electrons.\n\n**Significance:** it connects supernova neutrino transport directly to light-dark-sector phenomenology and illustrates how dramatically SN1987A-like arguments can outperform terrestrial/indirect searches for genuinely neutrinophilic states.\n\n**Assessment:** **new BSM constraint; quite interesting.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-12942", "arxiv": "2609.12942", "title": "Probing Neutrinophilic Axion-Like Particles in Tritium Beta Decay", "authors": "Yi Chung, Florian Goertz, Maya Hager, Joscha Lauer", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Here the new particle is a keV-scale ALP coupling preferentially to neutrinos. Such a state modifies the ordinary tritium spectrum measured by KATRIN and potentially by TRISTAN.", "path": "2026/09/14/index.html#paper-2609-12942", "reportDate": "14 September 2026", "searchText": "Probing Neutrinophilic Axion-Like Particles in Tritium Beta Decay Yi Chung, Florian Goertz, Maya Hager, Joscha Lauer 2609.12942 Here the new particle is a keV-scale ALP coupling preferentially to neutrinos. Such a state modifies the ordinary tritium spectrum measured by KATRIN and potentially by TRISTAN. 2026-09-14 14 September 2026 Neutrinos Dark matter BSM #### ★★☆ 5. **Probing Neutrinophilic Axion-Like Particles in Tritium Beta Decay**\n\n**Yi Chung, Florian Goertz, Maya Hager, Joscha Lauer**  \n[arXiv:2609.12942](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12942)\n\nHere the new particle is a keV-scale ALP coupling preferentially to neutrinos. Such a state modifies the ordinary tritium spectrum measured by KATRIN and potentially by **TRISTAN**.\n\nThe authors calculate the spectral distortions for both lepton-number-conserving and lepton-number-violating interactions and derive updated laboratory constraints. Ordinarily cosmology wins over beta-decay experiments, but they emphasize an important assumption behind that comparison: the particle and its interactions must exist in essentially the same form in the early Universe.\n\nThey therefore construct an explicit pseudo-Nambu–Goldstone realization with a nontrivial symmetry-breaking thermal history in which the usual cosmological constraints can be evaded. In that case,\n\n\\[\n{\\rm KATRIN/TRISTAN}\n\\]\n\ncan become the leading probe.\n\n**Significance:** good example of the often-overlooked distinction between a powerful cosmological constraint and a genuinely model-independent laboratory constraint.\n\n**Assessment:** **interesting neutrino/ALP phenomenology.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-12542", "arxiv": "2609.12542", "title": "Leptogenesis Determined By Low Energy Parameters", "authors": "Xiao-Gang He, Zhong-Lv Huang, Raymond R. Volkas, Yu-Qi Xiao", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This attacks the usual problem with type-I seesaw leptogenesis: the heavy-neutrino sector normally contains enough freedom that low-energy neutrino observables provide little information about baryogenesis.", "path": "2026/09/14/index.html#paper-2609-12542", "reportDate": "14 September 2026", "searchText": "Leptogenesis Determined By Low Energy Parameters Xiao-Gang He, Zhong-Lv Huang, Raymond R. Volkas, Yu-Qi Xiao 2609.12542 This attacks the usual problem with type-I seesaw leptogenesis: the heavy-neutrino sector normally contains enough freedom that low-energy neutrino observables provide little information about baryogenesis. 2026-09-14 14 September 2026 Neutrinos BSM #### ★★☆ 6. **Leptogenesis Determined By Low Energy Parameters**\n\n**Xiao-Gang He, Zhong-Lv Huang, Raymond R. Volkas, Yu-Qi Xiao**  \n[arXiv:2609.12542](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12542)\n\nThis attacks the usual problem with type-I seesaw leptogenesis: the heavy-neutrino sector normally contains enough freedom that low-energy neutrino observables provide little information about baryogenesis.\n\nThey instead impose three predictive relations,\n\n\\[\nM_D=M_u,\\qquad M_D=M_d,\\qquad \\text{or}\\qquad M_D=M_\\ell,\n\\]\n\nso the seesaw equation allows the heavy Majorana matrix to be reconstructed from low-energy parameters.\n\nThey evolve the flavored asymmetries using **density-matrix Boltzmann equations**. Viable thermal leptogenesis occurs for normal ordering with nonzero Majorana phases in the down-quark-like and charged-lepton-like constructions. Successful points favor a close heavy-neutrino pair,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|M_i-M_j|}{M_i}<10^{-3},\n\\]\n\nalthough the authors stress that it is not the conventional resonant-leptogenesis limit.\n\nThe interesting experimental payoff is \\(0\\nu\\beta\\beta\\): some successful leptogenesis regions predict\n\n\\[\nm_{\\beta\\beta}\n\\]\n\nwithin reach of future **sub-10-meV** experiments such as LEGEND-1000, nEXO and CUPID-1T.\n\n**Significance:** this is exactly the kind of seesaw construction where cosmological baryogenesis and measurable low-energy neutrino observables cease to be almost completely disconnected.\n\n**Assessment:** **good neutrino-pheno paper; model-dependent but predictive.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-12775", "arxiv": "2609.12775", "title": "Constraints on Galactic Dark Photons from a DALI Prototype", "authors": "Javier De Miguel et al.", "tags": ["Dark matter"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A straightforward but clean experimental result.", "path": "2026/09/14/index.html#paper-2609-12775", "reportDate": "14 September 2026", "searchText": "Constraints on Galactic Dark Photons from a DALI Prototype Javier De Miguel et al. 2609.12775 A straightforward but clean experimental result. 2026-09-14 14 September 2026 Dark matter #### ★★☆ 7. **Constraints on Galactic Dark Photons from a DALI Prototype**\n\n**Javier De Miguel et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.12775](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12775)\n\nA straightforward but clean experimental result.\n\nThe DALI haloscope prototype analyzed **36 hours** of data around\n\n\\[\n\\nu\\simeq6.9~{\\rm GHz},\n\\]\n\ncorresponding to a dark-photon mass near\n\n\\[\nm_{A'}=28.54~\\mu{\\rm eV}.\n\\]\n\nNo statistically significant excess is observed, giving approximately\n\n\\[\n\\chi\\lesssim6.8\\times10^{-14},\n\\]\n\nwhich the authors report as the strongest laboratory exclusion in that narrow frequency range.\n\n**Significance:** not transformative, but a real experimental improvement in ultralight-vector DM parameter space rather than another forecast paper.\n\n**Assessment:** **incremental but solid experimental result.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-12120", "arxiv": "2609.12120", "title": "Probing Lorentz Invariance Violation in Cosmogenic Neutrino Propagation with KM3-230213A", "authors": "Rodrigo Sasse, Rodrigo Guedes Lang, Rita de Cássia dos Anjos", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Cosmogenic-neutrino propagation calculations test Lorentz-invariance violation in interpretations of KM3-230213A.", "path": "2026/09/14/index.html#paper-2609-12120", "reportDate": "14 September 2026", "searchText": "Probing Lorentz Invariance Violation in Cosmogenic Neutrino Propagation with KM3-230213A Rodrigo Sasse, Rodrigo Guedes Lang, Rita de Cássia dos Anjos 2609.12120 Cosmogenic-neutrino propagation calculations test Lorentz-invariance violation in interpretations of KM3-230213A. 2026-09-14 14 September 2026 Neutrinos BSM #### ★★☆ 8. **Probing Lorentz Invariance Violation in Cosmogenic Neutrino Propagation with KM3-230213A**\n\n**Rodrigo Sasse, Rodrigo Guedes Lang, Rita de Cássia dos Anjos**  \n[arXiv:2609.12120](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12120)\n\nThey generate standard cosmogenic neutrino spectra using **CRPropa 3.2**, then introduce superluminal neutrino LIV producing neutrino splitting. The basic spectral effect is\n\n\\[\n{\\rm LIV}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{suppression at the highest energies},\\\\\n\\text{pile-up/enhancement at PeV--EeV energies}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nUsing KM3-230213A as a benchmark, they find sensitivity to dimensionful LIV coefficients roughly in the range\n\n\\[\n10^{-24}\\lesssim\n\\delta_{\\nu,1}/{\\rm eV^{-1}}\n\\lesssim10^{-22}.\n\\]\n\nLarger values can generate too many neutrinos in energy intervals already constrained by IceCube and Auger.\n\nThe important caveat is explicitly acknowledged by the authors: **one KM3NeT event does not permit a formal LIV constraint**, and the inferred sensitivity depends strongly on the assumed UHECR source population.\n\n**Significance:** the study connects UHECR propagation to cosmogenic-neutrino phenomenology, but its physical conclusion remains exploratory rather than a formal bound.\n\n**Assessment:** **interesting/speculative phenomenology.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10931", "arxiv": "2609.10931", "title": "IceCube neutrino point-source searches in the direction of the KM3NeT ultra-high-energy event", "authors": "IceCube Collaboration — R. Abbasi et al.", "tags": ["Neutrinos"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "IceCube uses 15 years of data to search the direction of KM3-230213A, the extraordinary KM3NeT event with reconstructed energy around 200 PeV, under three temporal hypotheses: a steady source, a flare coincident with the KM3NeT event,…", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-10T00:36:30Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-11", "original_report_date": "2026-09-12", "path": "2026/09/11/index.html#paper-2609-10931", "reportDate": "11 September 2026", "searchText": "IceCube neutrino point-source searches in the direction of the KM3NeT ultra-high-energy event IceCube Collaboration — R. Abbasi et al. 2609.10931 IceCube uses 15 years of data to search the direction of KM3-230213A, the extraordinary KM3NeT event with reconstructed energy around 200 PeV, under three temporal hypotheses: a steady source, a flare coincident with the KM3NeT event,… 2026-09-11 11 September 2026 Neutrinos #### ★★★ 1. IceCube neutrino point-source searches in the direction of the KM3NeT ultra-high-energy event\n\n**IceCube Collaboration — R. Abbasi et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.10931](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10931)\n\nIceCube uses **15 years of data** to search the direction of KM3-230213A, the extraordinary KM3NeT event with reconstructed energy around \\(200\\) PeV, under three temporal hypotheses: a steady source, a flare coincident with the KM3NeT event, and a transient occurring at an arbitrary time. They find **no evidence for neutrino emission in any search** and derive point-source flux limits. The important conclusion is that if KM3-230213A came from an astrophysical point source, the simplest interpretation in which that source produces an \\(E^{-2}\\)-like flux at the normalization inferred from the single KM3NeT event is now **strongly constrained**, both for steady and transient scenarios.\n\n**Significance:** this significantly sharpens the astrophysical interpretation problem surrounding the KM3NeT event. The combination of one extraordinarily energetic KM3NeT neutrino and a long IceCube null exposure is becoming an interesting consistency test rather than merely an “extreme-energy neutrino” story.\n\n**Classification:** **genuinely new experimental constraint.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-11833", "arxiv": "2609.11833", "title": "Solar Neutrino Constraints on Inelastic Dark Matter Scattering in Light of Recent LUX-ZEPLIN Observations", "authors": "Thong T. Q. Nguyen, Tim Linden, Dan Hooper", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Dark matter"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The recent LZ high-recoil candidate has encouraged DM models in which the recoil spectrum is strongly momentum-dependent or the scattering is inelastic, allowing a large high-energy recoil without producing the many low-energy events…", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-10T17:25:40Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-11", "original_report_date": "2026-09-12", "path": "2026/09/11/index.html#paper-2609-11833", "reportDate": "11 September 2026", "searchText": "Solar Neutrino Constraints on Inelastic Dark Matter Scattering in Light of Recent LUX-ZEPLIN Observations Thong T. Q. Nguyen, Tim Linden, Dan Hooper 2609.11833 The recent LZ high-recoil candidate has encouraged DM models in which the recoil spectrum is strongly momentum-dependent or the scattering is inelastic, allowing a large high-energy recoil without producing the many low-energy events… 2026-09-11 11 September 2026 Neutrinos Dark matter #### ★★★ 2. Solar Neutrino Constraints on Inelastic Dark Matter Scattering in Light of Recent LUX-ZEPLIN Observations\n\n**Thong T. Q. Nguyen, Tim Linden, Dan Hooper**  \n[arXiv:2609.11833](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.11833)\n\nThe recent LZ high-recoil candidate has encouraged DM models in which the recoil spectrum is strongly momentum-dependent or the scattering is inelastic, allowing a large high-energy recoil without producing the many low-energy events expected from conventional elastic WIMPs. Nguyen, Linden and Hooper point out an independent astroparticle constraint: such particles can be captured in the Sun, annihilate there, and generate **high-energy solar neutrinos**. IceCube sees no corresponding excess.\n\nTheir conclusion is that IceCube solar-neutrino observations **strongly constrain a substantial part of the inelastic-DM parameter space relevant to the LZ event**. This tests the recoil interpretation through an independent messenger.\n\nFor perspective, LZ itself reports only **2.6σ local significance**, approximately a \\(0.5\\%\\) background probability, and explicitly does **not** claim a DM discovery.\n\n**Significance:** a multimessenger connection between direct detection, solar capture and neutrino-telescope observations.\n\n**Classification:** **new phenomenological constraint on interpretations of the LZ candidate.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10668", "arxiv": "2609.10668", "title": "The Dark Dimension and Majorana Neutrinos", "authors": "Arturo de Giorgi, Dhruv Pasari", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Oscillations", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The setup is the “Dark Dimension” scenario motivated by swampland arguments: an extra spatial dimension with characteristic size", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-09T18:00:00Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-11", "original_report_date": "2026-09-13", "path": "2026/09/11/index.html#paper-2609-10668", "reportDate": "11 September 2026", "searchText": "The Dark Dimension and Majorana Neutrinos Arturo de Giorgi, Dhruv Pasari 2609.10668 The setup is the “Dark Dimension” scenario motivated by swampland arguments: an extra spatial dimension with characteristic size 2026-09-11 11 September 2026 Neutrinos Oscillations BSM #### ★★★ 3. The Dark Dimension and Majorana Neutrinos\n\n**Arturo de Giorgi, Dhruv Pasari**  \n[arXiv:2609.10668](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10668)\n\nThe setup is the “Dark Dimension” scenario motivated by swampland arguments: an extra spatial dimension with characteristic size\n\n\\[\nR \\sim 0.1-10~\\mu{\\rm m},\n\\]\n\nwith Majorana fermions propagating in the bulk and masses in roughly the \\(1-10\\) keV regime. The authors explicitly diagonalize the resulting neutrino/Kaluza–Klein system and then confront it with three rather different observables:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Daya Bay oscillations},\\qquad\n\\text{KATRIN } \\beta\\text{-decay},\\qquad\n\\text{KamLAND-Zen } 0\\nu\\beta\\beta .\n\\]\n\nThe technically interesting point is the neutrinoless-double-beta-decay calculation. They derive a closed expression for the effective Majorana mass including the KK tower and find that the tower can **screen the \\(0\\nu\\beta\\beta\\) amplitude** once the characteristic nuclear momentum exceeds the compactification and Majorana scales. Thus adding a tower of Majorana states does *not* simply add positive contributions to \\(m_{\\beta\\beta}\\); destructive structure emerges from the complete spectrum.\n\nWithin their preferred Dark-Dimension parameter window, **KATRIN becomes the strongest terrestrial constraint**, pushing the relevant Yukawa coupling to approximately\n\n\\[\ny\\lesssim {\\cal O}(10^{-3}),\n\\]\n\nwhile oscillation experiments and \\(0\\nu\\beta\\beta\\) probe complementary regions of the Majorana-mass parameter space.\n\n**Significance:** it is an unusually clean example where oscillations, direct kinematic neutrino-mass measurements and \\(0\\nu\\beta\\beta\\) constrain the *same explicit BSM neutrino construction*. The KK screening of \\(0\\nu\\beta\\beta\\) is a distinctive feature of the calculation.\n\n**Classification:** **new neutrino/BSM phenomenology; high-interest theory paper.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10784", "arxiv": "2609.10784", "title": "Low-Mass Magnetic Monopoles in the Galaxy: Simulations and Comparison with Ultra-High-Energy Cosmic-Ray Data", "authors": "Ala’a AL-Zetoun, Igor Ostrovskiy, Peiwen Mi, Daniele Perri", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The authors implement Galactic propagation of low-mass magnetic monopoles in a custom CRPropa-based simulation with a realistic Galactic magnetic field. Because magnetic monopoles are accelerated along magnetic-field structures rather…", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-09T19:37:43Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-11", "original_report_date": "2026-09-12", "path": "2026/09/11/index.html#paper-2609-10784", "reportDate": "11 September 2026", "searchText": "Low-Mass Magnetic Monopoles in the Galaxy: Simulations and Comparison with Ultra-High-Energy Cosmic-Ray Data Ala’a AL-Zetoun, Igor Ostrovskiy, Peiwen Mi, Daniele Perri 2609.10784 The authors implement Galactic propagation of low-mass magnetic monopoles in a custom CRPropa-based simulation with a realistic Galactic magnetic field. Because magnetic monopoles are accelerated along magnetic-field structures rather… 2026-09-11 11 September 2026 Cosmic rays BSM #### ★★☆ 4. Low-Mass Magnetic Monopoles in the Galaxy: Simulations and Comparison with Ultra-High-Energy Cosmic-Ray Data\n\n**Ala’a AL-Zetoun, Igor Ostrovskiy, Peiwen Mi, Daniele Perri**  \n[arXiv:2609.10784](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10784)\n\nThe authors implement Galactic propagation of low-mass magnetic monopoles in a custom **CRPropa-based simulation with a realistic Galactic magnetic field**. Because magnetic monopoles are accelerated *along* magnetic-field structures rather than deflected in the ordinary \\(q\\,\\mathbf v\\times\\mathbf B\\) sense, their trajectories develop what the authors call **Galactic magnetic funnels**, producing strongly anisotropic arrival directions. They then compare the predicted directional/energy distributions with Pierre Auger data.\n\nThe data generally favor **zero monopole contribution**, and the profile-likelihood analysis yields a \\(90\\%\\) C.L. integral flux limit below roughly\n\n\\[\n2.1\\times10^{-23}\\ {\\rm cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}\\,sr^{-1}}.\n\\]\n\n**Significance:** beyond monopole phenomenology, the study develops methods for modelling a highly anisotropic exotic charged population propagating through the GMF.\n\n**Classification:** **new simulation + Auger constraint; niche but technically interesting.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10690", "arxiv": "2609.10690", "title": "EFT Approaches to Sommerfeld Enhancement and Bound States in Singular Potentials", "authors": "Arindam Bhattacharya, Rashmish K. Mishra, Tracy R. Slatyer", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The issue is that singular long-range potentials make the usual Schrödinger boundary-condition treatment of Sommerfeld enhancement and bound-state formation ambiguous. They attack the problem both with velocity power counting in…", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-09T18:00:05Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-11", "original_report_date": "2026-09-12", "path": "2026/09/11/index.html#paper-2609-10690", "reportDate": "11 September 2026", "searchText": "EFT Approaches to Sommerfeld Enhancement and Bound States in Singular Potentials Arindam Bhattacharya, Rashmish K. Mishra, Tracy R. Slatyer 2609.10690 The issue is that singular long-range potentials make the usual Schrödinger boundary-condition treatment of Sommerfeld enhancement and bound-state formation ambiguous. They attack the problem both with velocity power counting in… 2026-09-11 11 September 2026 Multimessenger #### ★★☆ 5. EFT Approaches to Sommerfeld Enhancement and Bound States in Singular Potentials\n\n**Arindam Bhattacharya, Rashmish K. Mishra, Tracy R. Slatyer**  \n[arXiv:2609.10690](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10690)\n\nThe issue is that singular long-range potentials make the usual Schrödinger boundary-condition treatment of **Sommerfeld enhancement and bound-state formation** ambiguous. They attack the problem both with velocity power counting in nonrelativistic EFT and by explicitly regularizing the potential at short distance.\n\nFor a pseudoscalar mediator coupled to massive Dirac DM, an important result is that a **weakly coupled UV-complete theory need not generate Sommerfeld enhancement at all**. Conversely, derivative/higher-dimensional interactions can produce enhancement and bound states at weak coupling when there is a sufficiently large hierarchy between the EFT cutoff and \\(m_\\chi\\). In that case the enhancement retains genuine UV sensitivity.\n\n**Significance:** it is a useful warning against blindly inserting a singular potential into the Schrödinger equation and interpreting the resulting large enhancement as robust DM phenomenology.\n\n**Classification:** **conceptual/methodological advance rather than a new observational result.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10674", "arxiv": "2609.10674", "title": "Re-examining the sensitivity of JWST to decaying axion dark matter", "authors": "Caleb Gemmell, Christopher Dessert, Andrea Caputo, Joshua W. Foster", "tags": ["Dark matter"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Different JWST searches for the infrared line from a→γγ had been claiming coupling sensitivities differing by roughly an order of magnitude. The authors redo the data reduction and use flexible…", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-09T18:00:00Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-11", "original_report_date": "2026-09-12", "path": "2026/09/11/index.html#paper-2609-10674", "reportDate": "11 September 2026", "searchText": "Re-examining the sensitivity of JWST to decaying axion dark matter Caleb Gemmell, Christopher Dessert, Andrea Caputo, Joshua W. Foster 2609.10674 Different JWST searches for the infrared line from a→γγ had been claiming coupling sensitivities differing by roughly an order of magnitude. The authors redo the data reduction and use flexible… 2026-09-11 11 September 2026 Dark matter #### ★★☆ 6. Re-examining the sensitivity of JWST to decaying axion dark matter\n\n**Caleb Gemmell, Christopher Dessert, Andrea Caputo, Joshua W. Foster**  \n[arXiv:2609.10674](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10674)\n\nDifferent JWST searches for the infrared line from \\(a\\rightarrow\\gamma\\gamma\\) had been claiming coupling sensitivities differing by roughly **an order of magnitude**. The authors redo the data reduction and use flexible non-parametric inference, concluding that some of the strongest previously claimed sensitivities are simply not achievable with the datasets used.\n\nTheir robust analysis nevertheless excludes QCD-axion DM over approximately\n\n\\[\nm_a\\simeq 0.5-2.5\\ {\\rm eV},\n\\]\n\nand gives complementary ALP constraints at \\(0.1-0.5\\) eV. Importantly, they argue that the present analyses are approaching a **systematics floor**, so simply accumulating more exposure will not give the naive statistical improvement unless JWST instrumental modelling and reduction pipelines improve.\n\n**Significance:** not “new physics discovered,” but exactly the kind of systematic reassessment that determines whether a spectacular exclusion plot is actually trustworthy.\n\n**Classification:** **important re-analysis/systematics paper.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10694", "arxiv": "2609.10694", "title": "Strong Black Hole Natal Kicks in Magnetized Accretion-Powered Explosions", "authors": "Sean E. Li, Ore Gottlieb, Brian D. Metzger", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Using 3D general-relativistic MHD simulations extending to ~30 s after collapse, they show that rapidly rotating, strongly magnetized collapsars can give newly formed black holes natal kicks of", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-09T18:00:06Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-11", "original_report_date": "2026-09-13", "path": "2026/09/11/index.html#paper-2609-10694", "reportDate": "11 September 2026", "searchText": "Strong Black Hole Natal Kicks in Magnetized Accretion-Powered Explosions Sean E. Li, Ore Gottlieb, Brian D. Metzger 2609.10694 Using 3D general-relativistic MHD simulations extending to ~30 s after collapse, they show that rapidly rotating, strongly magnetized collapsars can give newly formed black holes natal kicks of 2026-09-11 11 September 2026 Multimessenger #### ★★☆ 7. Strong Black Hole Natal Kicks in Magnetized Accretion-Powered Explosions\n\n**Sean E. Li, Ore Gottlieb, Brian D. Metzger**  \n[arXiv:2609.10694](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10694)\n\nUsing **3D general-relativistic MHD simulations extending to \\(\\sim30\\) s after collapse**, they show that rapidly rotating, strongly magnetized collapsars can give newly formed black holes natal kicks of\n\n\\[\nv_{\\rm kick}\\sim10^2-10^3~{\\rm km\\,s^{-1}},\n\\]\n\nsubstantially larger than the weak BH kicks often assumed.\n\nThree effects contribute:\n\n\\[\n\\text{asymmetric electromagnetic outflow},\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{magnetic-flux eruptions},\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n\\text{gravitational pull from asymmetric ejecta}.\n\\]\n\nFaster-spinning BHs tend to receive larger and more spin-aligned kicks. Particularly strong asymmetries occur when the system undergoes a delayed transition into a **magnetically arrested disk (MAD)** state.\n\nThe astrophysical connection is that the strong coherent magnetic flux required to generate these kicks is also precisely the ingredient invoked for relativistic **GRB jets and jet-driven hypernovae**. Thus strong BH kicks might be an intrinsic consequence of some collapsar engines rather than an independent phenomenon.\n\nConsequences include BH retention in clusters, binary disruption, spin-orbit misalignment and formation channels of high-spin BHs in or near the pair-instability mass gap.\n\n**Significance:** this connects GRB-engine MHD directly to the compact-object populations measured by gravitational waves.\n\n**Classification:** **genuinely new numerical result; strong high-energy-astrophysics paper.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10736", "arxiv": "2609.10736", "title": "Teglon: A Pixel-Level Pipeline for Galaxy-Informed Gravitational-Wave Follow-up Planning and Efficiency Analysis", "authors": "D. A. Coulter, P. Darc, C. D. Kilpatrick, R. J. Foley, P. McGill, S. D. Wyatt, R. Hausen", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This is not a physics discovery but potentially useful multimessenger infrastructure.", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-09T18:31:56Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-11", "original_report_date": "2026-09-13", "path": "2026/09/11/index.html#paper-2609-10736", "reportDate": "11 September 2026", "searchText": "Teglon: A Pixel-Level Pipeline for Galaxy-Informed Gravitational-Wave Follow-up Planning and Efficiency Analysis D. A. Coulter, P. Darc, C. D. Kilpatrick, R. J. Foley, P. McGill, S. D. Wyatt, R. Hausen 2609.10736 This is not a physics discovery but potentially useful multimessenger infrastructure. 2026-09-11 11 September 2026 Multimessenger #### ★★☆ 8. Teglon: A Pixel-Level Pipeline for Galaxy-Informed Gravitational-Wave Follow-up Planning and Efficiency Analysis\n\n**D. A. Coulter, P. Darc, C. D. Kilpatrick, R. J. Foley, P. McGill, S. D. Wyatt, R. Hausen**  \n[arXiv:2609.10736](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10736)\n\nTeglon takes a three-dimensional GW localization posterior and combines it with a **3D galaxy-completeness model**, producing a revised probability map informed by the known galaxy distribution. It can then ingest an arbitrary telescope footprint and continuously interpolate between pure galaxy targeting and ordinary sky tiling.\n\nThe clever extension is integration with **Redback**, allowing pixel-by-pixel detection efficiencies to be computed for transient models. The pipeline evaluates more than\n\n\\[\nP(\\mathrm{source\\ is\\ here}),\n\\]\n\nbut effectively\n\n\\[\nP(\\mathrm{detect}\\mid\\mathrm{source\\ here},\\,\n\\mathrm{instrument},\\,t,\\,\\mathrm{transient\\ model}).\n\\]\n\nThis supports an assessment of the detection efficiency of a follow-up campaign.\n\nThey report particularly substantial gains for telescopes with fields of view \\(\\lesssim1~{\\rm deg}^2\\), and Teglon has already been used through O3/O4 and is being prepared for O5.\n\n**Significance:** the tool quantitatively connects GW posterior volumes to gamma-ray, X-ray and optical counterpart searches.\n\n**Classification:** **methodological/tool paper, not a new astrophysical result.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-11885", "arxiv": "2609.11885", "title": "Hierarchical Population Inference with Normalizing Flows for Binary Black Holes", "authors": "Leonardo Iampieri, Simone Mastrogiovanni", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Instead of assuming a low-dimensional parametric BBH mass model, they represent the source population", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-10T17:49:31Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-11", "original_report_date": "2026-09-13", "path": "2026/09/11/index.html#paper-2609-11885", "reportDate": "11 September 2026", "searchText": "Hierarchical Population Inference with Normalizing Flows for Binary Black Holes Leonardo Iampieri, Simone Mastrogiovanni 2609.11885 Instead of assuming a low-dimensional parametric BBH mass model, they represent the source population 2026-09-11 11 September 2026 Multimessenger #### ★★☆ 9. Hierarchical Population Inference with Normalizing Flows for Binary Black Holes\n\n**Leonardo Iampieri, Simone Mastrogiovanni**  \n[arXiv:2609.11885](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.11885)\n\nInstead of assuming a low-dimensional parametric BBH mass model, they represent the source population\n\n\\[\np(z,m_1,m_2)\n\\]\n\nwith a **normalizing flow embedded directly inside the hierarchical GW likelihood**. Event posterior samples propagate individual-event uncertainty, while injection campaigns encode detector selection effects.\n\nRestricting the inference to the region sufficiently sampled by injections can itself create an apparent correlation between mass and redshift. They therefore introduce a mutual-information diagnostic comparing the inferred distribution with a redshift-independent reference subject to the *same support restriction*.\n\nApplied to **GWTC-5.0**, the reconstruction independently recovers population features near\n\n\\[\nm_1\\sim10\\,M_\\odot,\\qquad 35\\,M_\\odot,\n\\]\n\nbut finds **no significant evidence that the intrinsic primary-mass distribution evolves with redshift**.\n\n**Significance:** mainly methodology—the warning about spurious correlations induced by selection/support boundaries is applicable well beyond gravitational-wave inference.\n\n**Classification:** **strong methodological work; result itself is broadly consistent with existing LVK population conclusions.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10671", "arxiv": "2609.10671", "title": "Ultralight Axial Dark Matter", "authors": "Anson Hook, Junwu Huang, Kevin Zhou", "tags": ["Dark matter"], "label": "★☆☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "They explore ultralight DM as an axial vector field rather than the usual scalar/axion or vector dark photon. Fermionic couplings generate spin torques that can be parametrically enhanced relative to axion DM. They also construct an…", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-09T18:00:00Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-11", "original_report_date": "2026-09-12", "path": "2026/09/11/index.html#paper-2609-10671", "reportDate": "11 September 2026", "searchText": "Ultralight Axial Dark Matter Anson Hook, Junwu Huang, Kevin Zhou 2609.10671 They explore ultralight DM as an axial vector field rather than the usual scalar/axion or vector dark photon. Fermionic couplings generate spin torques that can be parametrically enhanced relative to axion DM. They also construct an… 2026-09-11 11 September 2026 Dark matter #### ★☆☆ 10. Ultralight Axial Dark Matter\n\n**Anson Hook, Junwu Huang, Kevin Zhou**  \n[arXiv:2609.10671](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10671)\n\nThey explore ultralight DM as an **axial vector field** rather than the usual scalar/axion or vector dark photon. Fermionic couplings generate spin torques that can be parametrically enhanced relative to axion DM. They also construct an analogue of the axion-photon coupling, though its consistent realization requires a photon mass, and discuss signatures involving polarimetry, superconducting cavities, electric fields and the CMB. The massive axial vector description is dual to a massive two-form/Kalb–Ramond field.\n\n**Significance:** an expansion of the ultralight-DM model space with unusual experimental signatures.\n\n**Classification:** **new theoretical framework/model-building.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-11511", "arxiv": "2609.11511", "title": "Cosmological Evolution of Primordial Black Holes: UV/IR Decoupling and the KM3NeT 220 PeV Neutrino Anomaly", "authors": "Debdatta Dey, Tausif Parvez, S. Shankaranarayanan", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Dark matter", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★☆☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The proposal is that KM3-230213A came from the terminal Hawking evaporation of a primordial black hole. The authors embed the PBH in a McVittie cosmological spacetime: early-time cosmological accretion and expansion modify the PBH…", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-10T13:18:16Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-11", "original_report_date": "2026-09-12", "path": "2026/09/11/index.html#paper-2609-11511", "reportDate": "11 September 2026", "searchText": "Cosmological Evolution of Primordial Black Holes: UV/IR Decoupling and the KM3NeT 220 PeV Neutrino Anomaly Debdatta Dey, Tausif Parvez, S. Shankaranarayanan 2609.11511 The proposal is that KM3-230213A came from the terminal Hawking evaporation of a primordial black hole. The authors embed the PBH in a McVittie cosmological spacetime: early-time cosmological accretion and expansion modify the PBH… 2026-09-11 11 September 2026 Neutrinos Dark matter Multimessenger #### ★☆☆ 11. Cosmological Evolution of Primordial Black Holes: UV/IR Decoupling and the KM3NeT 220 PeV Neutrino Anomaly\n\n**Debdatta Dey, Tausif Parvez, S. Shankaranarayanan**  \n[arXiv:2609.11511](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.11511)\n\nThe proposal is that KM3-230213A came from the **terminal Hawking evaporation of a primordial black hole**. The authors embed the PBH in a McVittie cosmological spacetime: early-time cosmological accretion and expansion modify the PBH lifetime and initial mass required to evaporate at the present epoch, while the final \\(z\\simeq0\\) phase becomes essentially Schwarzschild. They argue that this can produce a large local \\(\\sim220\\) PeV neutrino signal while avoiding excessive diffuse early-time emission.\n\nThis theoretical interpretation is **highly model-dependent**, and the IceCube point-source search further constrains source interpretations of KM3-230213A.\n\n**Significance:** the paper explores an exotic origin for the KM3NeT event, with viability dependent on the underlying PBH and source assumptions.\n\n**Classification:** **speculative interpretation.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10176", "arxiv": "2609.10176", "title": "Extended gamma-ray emission in the vicinity of Westerlund 1 and Kes 41 seen by Fermi-LAT", "authors": "L. Tibaldo, R. Bernet, L. Härer, M. Lemoine-Goumard, L. Mohrmann, G. Peron, B. Reville, T. Vieu", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Fermi-LAT observations reveal three extended gamma-ray components near Westerlund 1 and Kes 41, with interpretations involving local cosmic rays or diffuse-background modelling.", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-09T13:44:10Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-10", "original_report_date": "2026-09-12", "path": "2026/09/10/index.html#paper-2609-10176", "reportDate": "10 September 2026", "searchText": "Extended gamma-ray emission in the vicinity of Westerlund 1 and Kes 41 seen by Fermi-LAT L. Tibaldo, R. Bernet, L. Härer, M. Lemoine-Goumard, L. Mohrmann, G. Peron, B. Reville, T. Vieu 2609.10176 Fermi-LAT observations reveal three extended gamma-ray components near Westerlund 1 and Kes 41, with interpretations involving local cosmic rays or diffuse-background modelling. 2026-09-10 10 September 2026 Gamma rays Multimessenger #### ★★★ 1. Extended gamma-ray emission in the vicinity of Westerlund 1 and Kes 41 seen by Fermi-LAT\n\n**L. Tibaldo, R. Bernet, L. Härer, M. Lemoine-Goumard, L. Mohrmann, G. Peron, B. Reville, T. Vieu**  \n[arXiv:2609.10176](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10176)\n\nUsing **more than 16 years of Fermi-LAT data above \\(0.8\\) GeV**, the authors identify **three new extended, soft-spectrum gamma-ray components** in the Westerlund 1/Kes 41 region. One is associated with the natal cloud of Kes 41 and is statistically preferred over the previously used point-source description. Two others overlap neutral gas within roughly \\(100\\) pc of the edge of the Westerlund 1 superbubble.\n\nThere is an important caveat: they cannot yet cleanly distinguish **real locally injected CR populations** from residual mismodelling of the Galactic interstellar emission. If the components are physical, the Kes 41 emission requires \\(\\lesssim5\\%\\) of the SNR energy budget in accelerated particles, while the two Wd1 components correspond to a gamma-ray luminosity of roughly \\(10^{-4}\\) of the cluster-wind mechanical power.\n\n**Significance:** this is precisely the sort of intermediate-scale CR-transport phenomenology that can alter how one interprets TeV–PeV emission and neutrino expectations from stellar-cluster environments.\n\n**Classification:** **new observational result; interpretation not yet unique.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10636", "arxiv": "2609.10636", "title": "Nonthermal Solar Stalling of an Inelastic Scalar Signal in Xenon", "authors": "XinXin Qi, Hao Sun", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A light-mediator inelastic-scalar model tests whether nonthermal solar capture can reconcile the LZ recoil candidate with solar-neutrino constraints.", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-09T10:43:05Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-10", "original_report_date": "2026-09-13", "path": "2026/09/10/index.html#paper-2609-10636", "reportDate": "10 September 2026", "searchText": "Nonthermal Solar Stalling of an Inelastic Scalar Signal in Xenon XinXin Qi, Hao Sun 2609.10636 A light-mediator inelastic-scalar model tests whether nonthermal solar capture can reconcile the LZ recoil candidate with solar-neutrino constraints. 2026-09-10 10 September 2026 Multimessenger #### ★★★ 2. Nonthermal Solar Stalling of an Inelastic Scalar Signal in Xenon\n\n**XinXin Qi, Hao Sun**  \n[arXiv:2609.10636](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10636)\n\nThis light-mediator scalar model tests how nonthermal solar capture affects the interpretation of the LZ recoil candidate.\n\nThe authors construct a concrete anomaly-free\n\n\\[\nU(1)_{B-3L_\\tau}\n\\]\n\nmodel containing an \\(840\\) GeV complex scalar DM particle undergoing an endothermic transition with splitting\n\n\\[\n\\delta\\simeq 320~{\\rm keV},\n\\]\n\nmediated by a \\(160\\) MeV vector. Their benchmark gives about \\(0.96\\) expected accepted events in their reconstruction of the high-energy LZ search, i.e. roughly the scale required to interpret the single \\(248\\) keV LZ recoil candidate.\n\nThe calculation follows the evolution of DM after solar capture.\n\nInstead of assuming rapid thermalization into a compact central DM distribution, they evolve the captured particles in energy, angular momentum and internal state, including thermal nuclear motion, finite scattering rates and excited-state decay. Because the scattering is endothermic, the particles eventually reach orbital configurations for which further up-scattering is kinematically forbidden.\n\nThey find a nonthermal population that effectively **stalls around**\n\n\\[\nr_{\\rm eff}\\simeq0.194-0.198\\,R_\\odot,\n\\]\n\nrather than thermalizing in the solar core. The resulting annihilation rate is only\n\n\\[\n\\Gamma_{\\rm ann}\\sim1.3\\times10^{15}\\ {\\rm s^{-1}},\n\\]\n\nwhich in their benchmark is more than three orders of magnitude below their IceCube-based neutrino proxy.\n\n> Solar-neutrino constraints on *electroweak* inelastic DM cannot automatically be carried over to every inelastic-DM realization.\n\nThis paper provides an explicit light-mediator scalar counterexample.\n\n**Significance:** the lesson is broader than the LZ anomaly. Whenever one maps DM capture to a neutrino signal, the usual capture–thermalization–annihilation equilibrium assumption can fail badly if the scattering has nontrivial kinematic thresholds.\n\n**Classification:** **new model-specific phenomenology; particularly valuable as a counterpoint to broad solar-neutrino exclusion arguments.**\n\n---"}];
